window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"week1-antimicrobials-antibiotics.html","t":"Antimicrobials & Antibiotics (ABX)","secs":[{"s":"Antimicrobials & Antibiotics (ABX)","a":"","k":["Week 1","Antimicrobial Basics","Choosing & Starting an Antimicrobial","Patient Factors & Dosing","Antimicrobial Resistance & Stewardship","Cell Wall Synthesis Inhibitors","Protein & DNA/RNA Synthesis Inhibitors","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions"]},{"s":"Antimicrobial Basics▾","a":"antimicrobial-basics","k":["Antimicrobial Basics▾","Bactericidal — Kills the bacteria outright.","Bacteriostatic — Only inhibits bacterial growth/reproduction — doesn't kill it.","Broad Spectrum — Effective against many organism types, gram-positive and gram-negative — a \"shotgun approach.\" Used when the organism isn't known yet or hasn't cultured out.","Narrow Spectrum — Effective against only a few species — a \"BB gun approach.\" Requires knowing the organism and what it's sensitive to.","Selective Toxicity — Toxic to the target bacterial cell while sparing the patient's own normal cells nearby.","Innate Resistance — The organism was never susceptible to begin with — it's always been that way.","Acquired Resistance — The organism mutated, or picked up resistance genes from another organism, after antimicrobial exposure.","Superinfection — A new infection that develops during treatment for a different one — e.g., candidiasis or C. diff while on antibiotics for something else.","Five Ways an Antimicrobial Can Work","Inhibit cell wall synthesis.","Increase cell wall permeability.","Inhibit protein synthesis (stops the bacteria from functioning, without necessarily killing it).","Inhibit DNA/RNA synthesis, or alter DNA function.","Disrupt a specific metabolic or biochemical reaction (e.g., folic acid synthesis).","Prophylactic antibiotics are sometimes given to prevent infection in higher-risk groups: certain surgeries (knee replacement, cardiac, abdominal), patients with endocarditis or a history of it, and immunocompromised patients — especially th…"]},{"s":"Choosing & Starting an Antimicrobial▾","a":"choosing-starting-an-antimicrobial","k":["Choosing & Starting an Antimicrobial▾","Get Cultures First — Whenever Possible","The goal is always to get cultures and a sensitivity result before starting an antimicrobial. Giving the drug first can prevent the organism from growing on culture, making it harder to identify.","A culture order is a stat priority — go get it, even if that means delegating it to another nurse.","Exceptions: a critically ill or rapidly declining patient, meningitis (start something that crosses the blood-brain barrier), or severe sepsis — these can't wait on culture results.","Blood cultures: draw two sets, one aerobic bottle and one anaerobic bottle. Always try for a peripheral stick rather than a central line or port — surface bacteria on those devices commonly contaminate the sample. Clean the skin and the bot…","Community- vs. Hospital-Acquired — Community-acquired infections are typically easier to treat and more often viral. Hospital-acquired infections are more often bacterial and more often resistant.","Susceptibility Pattern — What the organism is actually sensitive to, per culture results.","Site of Infection — Can the drug penetrate that specific site — bone, lung, kidney, CNS? Some drugs concentrate in specific tissues better than others.","Suspected Organism — Matching the drug to the likely organism is the number one way to limit resistance.","Reading a Culture","Disk diffusion test: antibiotic disks are placed on a plate smeared with the culture. A clear zone (no bacterial growth) around a disk means that drug is effective against that organism.","Minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC): the lowest concentration of a drug that stops bacterial growth — doesn't necessarily kill it.","Minimum bactericidal concentration (MBC): the lowest concentration that decreases the bacterial colony by 99.9%. Not shown directly on a culture report — it's reported simply as susceptible or resistant."]},{"s":"Patient Factors & Dosing▾","a":"patient-factors-dosing","k":["Patient Factors & Dosing▾","Allergy vs. Adverse Effect","Nausea and vomiting are typically an adverse effect, not a true allergy.","A rash or hives can indicate a true allergy, but doesn't necessarily mean anaphylaxis.","Angioedema or anaphylaxis is a true, life-threatening allergy.","Cross-reactivity: a patient allergic to penicillin may also react to cephalosporins — more likely if the penicillin reaction was anaphylactic rather than just a rash. Sulfa allergy is its own, separate common category.","Allergies can develop at any age, including new penicillin or latex allergies later in life.","Age Extremes — Very young and very old patients typically need lower concentrations — their bodies are less able to handle standard doses.","Renal Function — Impaired kidneys reduce clearance of nephrotoxic drugs — may not tolerate standard dosing.","Hepatic Function — Impaired liver reduces clearance of hepatotoxic drugs.","Site of Infection — An abscess — isolated and walled off — takes longer to treat than a UTI, which a drug can reach more easily.","Host Defenses — Dose and length of treatment also depend on how well the patient's own immune system is fighting the infection.","PEAK after, TROUGH before: draw a peak level about 30 minutes after an IV infusion finishes (peaks are only drawn for IV drugs). Draw a trough level about 30 minutes before the next dose is due."]},{"s":"Antimicrobial Resistance & Stewardship▾","a":"antimicrobial-resistance-stewardship","k":["Antimicrobial Resistance & Stewardship▾","Why Hospital Patients Are At Higher Risk","Decreased immune response from underlying illness.","Invasive devices break the normal skin barrier: IV lines (central line–associated bloodstream infection), catheters (catheter-associated UTI), G-tubes (abdominal infection).","Post-op risk: atelectasis (collapsed alveoli) increases pneumonia risk; wound dehiscence or evisceration carries a high infection risk. Catheters are also common post-operatively, raising UTI risk.","MRSA — Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus — resistant to methicillin specifically.","VRE — Vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus. Can contaminate surfaces like chairs and clothing, spreading to other patients or family members.","CRE — Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae — a whole bacterial class resistant to all carbapenems. See the danger callout under Carbapenems below.","MDRO — Multidrug-resistant organism — resistant to multiple drugs across multiple classes.","Gown, after routine patient care — 37%","Gown, after morning care — 65%","Gown, after caring for a patient colonized with MRSA/VRE — 40%","White coat, after examining a patient — 69% (VRE/MRSA)","Bare hands, after that same exam — 27%","C. diff spores can survive 5+ months (reports of years) on surfaces. And on antibiotic overprescribing: for an uncomplicated upper respiratory infection, an antibiotic helps only about 1 in 4,000 times — but causes a reaction (rash, allergy…","Why Antibiotics Fail","Wrong drug chosen for the organism.","Started too late.","Dose too low, or not continued long enough — the infection can appear to improve, then return worse.","The drug can't physically reach the infection site (e.g., doesn't cross the blood-brain barrier for a CNS infection)."]},{"s":"Cell Wall Synthesis Inhibitors▾","a":"cell-wall-synthesis-inhibitors","k":["Cell Wall Synthesis Inhibitors▾","All of the drugs in this section work by damaging the bacterial cell wall — weakening it so fluid rushes in, the cell swells, and it bursts (cell lysis). Many are beta-lactam drugs, named for a specific ring structure; bacteria fight back b…","\"-bactam\" or \"clavulanic acid\" in a drug name means a beta-lactamase inhibitor is riding along with the actual antibiotic — sulbactam, tazobactam, clavulanic acid, and avibactam are never given alone. Examples: amoxicillin-clavulanate (Augm…","Natural — Penicillin G, penicillin V — Gram-positive coverage; IV; ~30 min half-life (longer with kidney impairment). Allergy to one penicillin means allergy to the whole class.","Penicillinase-Resistant — Nafcillin — Drug of choice when the organism secretes penicillinase (common with staph infections); IV only.","Aminopenicillins — Ampicillin, amoxicillin — One of the first broad-spectrum penicillins. Amoxicillin is preferred orally (often paired with clavulanic acid) and common for pediatric ENT/GI/skin infections; ampicillin resistance is rising.","Extended-Spectrum — Piperacillin + tazobactam (Zosyn) — Broadest-spectrum penicillin, common in the ICU; covers pseudomonas. Watch platelet function and renal status.","1st — Cefazolin (IV), cephalexin/Keflex (PO) — Gram-positive/staph coverage — not enterococcus or strep. Common for skin infections, UTIs, and surgical prophylaxis (cefazolin). Minimal CNS penetration.","2nd — Cefuroxime, cefotetan, cefoxitin — More gram-negative activity while keeping some gram-positive coverage. Cefotetan/cefoxitin-type drugs work on intestinal/anaerobic bacteria. Not for pseudomonas or CNS infections.","3rd — Ceftriaxone, ceftazidime — Ceftriaxone: once-daily dosing, crosses the blood-brain barrier (meningitis) — use caution with liver dysfunction. Ceftazidime covers pseudomonas.","4th — Cefepime — Broad spectrum — gram-negative and gram-positive, crosses the blood-brain barrier, covers pseudomonas. Often the first choice for a very sick patient with an unclear source.","5th — Ceftaroline — Newest, broadest generation; covers multidrug-resistant staph, but not enterobacter, pseudomonas, ESBL, or Klebsiella. IV only, renally dosed.","Cross-reactivity with a penicillin allergy is rare, about 1–4% — but still avoided when the penicillin reaction was anaphylactic. Cephalosporins are pregnancy category B (considered safe). Common adverse effects across all generations: mild…","Imipenem-Cilastatin — Broadest-spectrum antibiotic class. Cilastatin blocks a kidney enzyme (dehydropeptidase) that would otherwise break imipenem down too fast; crosses the blood-brain barrier.","Meropenem — Slightly less coverage than imipenem, but doesn't need cilastatin — used more often when possible for its lower seizure risk. Can cause GI upset.","Ertapenem and doripenem also exist but aren't emphasized here. All carbapenems are IV only, infused over 60 minutes, and saved as a last resort to limit resistance.","Carbapenems & CRE: carbapenems carry a real risk of drug-induced seizures — watch closely in patients with a seizure history. When bacteria become carbapenem-resistant (CRE), it's a public health emergency: carbapenems are the broadest-spec…","Vancomycin","A glycopeptide antibiotic — kills the gram-positive cell wall in a different way than the beta-lactams (not autolysis). Good for MRSA and other drug-resistant gram-positive infections.","Oral vancomycin only works locally in the gut — used for C. diff/pseudomembranous colitis, not systemic infections.","Toxic effects: nephrotoxic (monitor renal levels on IV vancomycin), can cause reversible hearing loss and thrombocytopenia (monitor platelets). Extra caution alongside other nephrotoxic drugs (e.g., IV contrast) or neuromuscular blockers.","Red Man syndrome: flushing, itching, rash — sometimes tachycardia/hypotension — from infusing it too fast. Fix by slowing the infusion, not stopping the drug; can premedicate with Benadryl.","Peak/trough monitoring: trough about 30 minutes before the next dose; peak about 30 minutes after the infusion finishes."]},{"s":"Protein & DNA/RNA Synthesis Inhibitors▾","a":"protein-dna-rna-synthesis-inhibitors","k":["Protein & DNA/RNA Synthesis Inhibitors▾","These drugs don't touch the cell wall — instead they interfere with transcription or translation, so the bacteria can't build proteins or copy its DNA. That either kills the cell or stops it from reproducing.","Class name patterns: \"-mycin/-micin\" = aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin). \"-floxacin\" = fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin). \"-cycline\" = tetracyclines (tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline).","Aminoglycosides — Gentamicin, Tobramycin","Potent, especially against gram-negative organisms; used for complicated or resistant infections — UTIs, pyelonephritis, endocarditis, pneumonia, osteomyelitis.","Two major toxicities to monitor: nephrotoxicity (usually reversible — monitor creatinine) and ototoxicity (can be permanent — ask about ringing or buzzing in the ears).","Peak and trough levels are drawn; dosing is adjusted for renal function.","Often combined with a beta-lactam or vancomycin for a synergistic effect — give the beta-lactamase inhibitor first so the aminoglycoside can get into the bacterial cell.","Gentamicin plus a neuromuscular blocker (paralytic) can cause profound respiratory distress. Also watch for CNS effects (confusion, disorientation, numbness/tingling) and permanent cochlear damage.","Lincosamides — Clindamycin","Bactericidal or bacteriostatic depending on concentration; binds ribosomes to stop protein synthesis.","Used for anaerobic infections — bone, GU, intra-abdominal, aspiration pneumonia. All Enterobacteriaceae are resistant to it.","PO or IV; monitored with peak/trough levels; same neuromuscular-blocker/respiratory-distress caution as gentamicin.","Notable risk: C. diff → pseudomembranous colitis. Watch for diarrhea, bloody stool, or severe GI distress.","Macrolides are bacteriostatic in general (bactericidal at high concentrations) and inhibit protein synthesis via ribosomes, like the aminoglycosides. Used for STIs (especially gonorrhea), respiratory and skin infections, and some unusual or…","Erythromycin — Significant GI upset (nicknamed a \"yuck drug\") — that same effect is actually useful for diabetic gastroparesis, since it increases gastric motility. Doesn't cross the blood-brain barrier; painful IV; poor oral absorption, do…","Azithromycin (Z-Pak) — Less GI upset than erythromycin; better tissue penetration and longer duration allow once-daily dosing (2 tablets day one, then 1 daily). Take on an empty stomach — food decreases absorption.","Tetracycline — PO only; take fasting (75–77% absorbed — food reduces this further). Concentrates in bone, liver, spleen, and teeth.","Doxycycline — Common for STIs, acne, and other skin infections.","Minocycline — Used for meningitis and rheumatoid arthritis; there's an extended-release form for acne.","All tetracyclines are bacteriostatic, inhibit protein synthesis by binding ribosomes, and are broad spectrum despite growing resistance. They still work on some unusual organisms: Rickettsia (Rocky Mountain spotted fever), chlamydia, tricho…","Tetracyclines & pregnancy/pediatrics: never give to pregnant or nursing women, or to children under 8. They cause enamel hypoplasia — permanent discoloration of developing teeth.","Ciprofloxacin (Cipro) — Used for complicated UTIs, some STIs, respiratory infections, gonorrhea, and anthrax. Can be given PO, IV, or topically; poor CNS penetration — avoid for meningitis.","Levofloxacin (Levaquin) — Most widely used fluoroquinolone; once daily, 100% oral bioavailability. Good for sinusitis, chronic bronchitis flares, community-acquired pneumonia. Can prolong the QT interval and lower the seizure threshold; wat…","Fluoroquinolones work by altering bacterial DNA (via the enzymes gyrase and topoisomerase) rather than inhibiting protein synthesis — effective against both rapid- and slow-growing organisms, with a prolonged post-antibiotic effect. They co…","Sulfonamides — Sulfamethoxazole-Trimethoprim (Bactrim)","Bacteriostatic; blocks folic acid synthesis.","First-line for uncomplicated UTIs and respiratory infections; also used for salmonella and shigellosis.","Often given to patients with HIV, who have a higher rate of adverse reactions.","Contains a sulfa component — contraindicated with a sulfa allergy. Reactions typically start with fever, then a skin rash.","Metronidazole (Flagyl)","Works against both bacterial and protozoal infections: amebiasis, giardiasis, trichomoniasis, intra-abdominal infections, H. pylori, Crohn's-related infections.","The go-to drug (with oral vancomycin) for C. diff/antibiotic-associated diarrhea. Only works against anaerobic bacteria.","Works by inhibiting DNA synthesis, similar to the fluoroquinolones.","Other adverse effects seen across most antibiotics: nausea/vomiting, xerostomia (dry mouth), vaginal candidiasis (yeast infection).","Metronidazole (Flagyl) & alcohol: no alcohol — none for 24 hours before starting and at least 36 hours after the last dose. Mixing the two creates a toxic metabolite (a disulfiram-like reaction)."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","NCLEX-style practice questions covering this page's highest-yield content. Choose your answer for each, then submit to see your score and rationales — the same 20 questions also live in the Build Your Own Exam question bank.","A nurse is preparing to draw blood cultures for a patient with a new fever and an existing central venous catheter. Which action best reduces the risk of a false-positive culture result?","A Draw the sample from a peripheral vein rather than the central line","B Draw both culture bottles from the central line to save time","C Draw only one culture bottle instead of two separate bottles","D Wait to draw the culture until after the first antibiotic dose is given","A nurse is caring for a patient in the emergency department with suspected bacterial meningitis. The provider orders IV antibiotics to be given immediately, before culture results return. Which of the following best explains this decision?","A Meningitis cultures cannot grow properly once bacteria have colonized the CNS","B Meningitis is one of the situations where treatment cannot wait for culture results","C Meningitis antibiotics are only effective if started within one hour of symptom onset","D Meningitis cultures do not require separate aerobic and anaerobic bottles","A student nurse states, 'A bacteriostatic drug directly kills the bacteria, while a bactericidal drug only slows its growth.' The instructor should correct this statement by explaining that...","A bactericidal drugs slow bacterial growth without killing the organism","B bacteriostatic drugs work by disrupting the bacterial cell wall until it lyses","C bactericidal drugs kill the organism outright, unlike bacteriostatic drugs","D both terms actually describe drugs that block folic acid synthesis pathways","A patient presents with a severe infection, and the organism has not yet been identified. The provider starts an antibiotic empirically that is effective against many organism types. Which statement best reflects the rationale for this choi…","A Broad-spectrum drugs are narrow-spectrum drugs given at a higher concentration","B Narrow-spectrum drugs are preferred whenever the causative organism is unknown","C Broad-spectrum antibiotics remove the need for a culture and sensitivity test","D Broad-spectrum antibiotics cover many organism types when the organism is not yet known","A nurse needs to schedule peak and trough lab draws for a patient receiving IV gentamicin. Which timing is correct?","A Draw the trough about 30 minutes before the next dose and the peak about 30 minutes after the infusion finishes","B Draw the peak about 30 minutes before the next dose and the trough about 30 minutes after the infusion finishes","C Draw both the peak and trough immediately before the next dose is due","D Draw the peak level about 30 minutes after the oral tablet is swallowed","A patient receiving IV vancomycin develops flushing and itching of the neck and upper body during the infusion, with a stable blood pressure. What is the most appropriate nursing action?","A Stop the infusion immediately and notify the provider of a suspected anaphylactic reaction","B Slow the infusion rate, since this reaction is consistent with Red Man syndrome","C Administer a stat dose of oral vancomycin to reduce systemic absorption","D Continue the infusion at the same rate and document it as an expected effect","A patient has been on IV vancomycin for a resistant gram-positive infection for one week. Which combination of assessments best reflects the toxicities the nurse should monitor for?","A Liver function, due to this drug's hepatotoxic risk","B QT interval and seizure threshold","C Renal function, hearing changes, and platelet counts","D Joint pain and photosensitivity reactions","A patient with a documented history of anaphylaxis to penicillin is being considered for a cephalosporin to treat a serious infection. What is the most important consideration for the nurse to communicate to the provider?","A Cross-reactivity is common, so cephalosporins should be avoided after any penicillin reaction","B Cephalosporins are contraindicated in pregnancy, so an alternative should be selected","C A sulfa-based antibiotic would be a safer alternative given this allergy history","D Cross-reactivity is more likely because this reaction was anaphylactic rather than a mild rash","A nurse reviews a new order for piperacillin-tazobactam (Zosyn) and recalls that tazobactam is included to address antibiotic resistance. What is tazobactam's role in this combination?","A It inhibits the beta-lactamase enzyme that would otherwise inactivate the antibiotic","B It blocks a kidney enzyme that would break the antibiotic down too quickly","C It provides additional gram-negative coverage as a second antibiotic","D It is given separately from the antibiotic to prevent nephrotoxicity","A patient has a staphylococcal skin infection, and cultures show the organism produces penicillinase. Which penicillin is the most appropriate choice?","A Amoxicillin, since it is preferred for oral dosing","B Nafcillin, since it resists breakdown by penicillinase","C Penicillin G, since it has broad gram-positive coverage","D Piperacillin-tazobactam, since it covers pseudomonas","A nurse is caring for an ICU patient started on IV piperacillin-tazobactam for a pseudomonas infection. Which lab values should the nurse monitor closely while the patient is on this medication?","A Liver enzymes and INR","B Hearing tests and creatinine","C Platelet function and renal status","D QT interval and white blood cell count","A provider needs to select a cephalosporin for a patient with bacterial meningitis. Which characteristic makes ceftriaxone an appropriate choice?","A It has the strongest activity against pseudomonas among the cephalosporins","B It is dosed multiple times daily to maintain CNS drug levels","C It has minimal CNS penetration and is paired with a carbapenem","D It crosses the blood-brain barrier and is dosed once daily","A nurse is administering IV meropenem to a patient with a history of a seizure disorder. What is the priority nursing consideration?","A Monitor closely for seizures, a known risk with this drug class","B Infuse the dose over 24 hours to reduce cardiotoxicity","C Request a switch to imipenem-cilastatin, since it carries a lower seizure risk than meropenem","D Pair the dose with cilastatin to prevent seizure activity","A nurse attends an infection-control in-service about carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae (CRE). Which statement about CRE should the nurse identify as accurate?","A CRE infections are resistant to vancomycin but respond well to carbapenems","B CRE is a public health emergency because carbapenems are the broadest-spectrum option left","C CRE spreads mainly through contaminated white coats rather than direct contact","D CRE has a mortality rate similar to routine hospital-acquired UTIs","A patient receiving IV gentamicin is scheduled for a procedure requiring a neuromuscular blocking agent. What is the nurse's priority concern?","A Increased risk of Red Man syndrome from the drug combination","B Increased risk of a disulfiram-like reaction","C Increased risk of profound respiratory distress","D Increased risk of a photosensitivity reaction","A patient is prescribed IV gentamicin together with a beta-lactamase inhibitor combination drug for a synergistic effect. In what order should the nurse plan to administer these medications?","A Administer both medications at the same time through the same IV line","B Administer the gentamicin first so it reaches peak levels sooner","C Administer the two drugs 24 hours apart to avoid combined nephrotoxicity","D Administer the beta-lactamase inhibitor combination first, then the gentamicin","A patient started on clindamycin for an intra-abdominal anaerobic infection reports new diarrhea with visible blood. What should the nurse suspect?","A Pseudomembranous colitis related to C. diff overgrowth","B An expected adverse effect that needs no further evaluation","C A true cross-allergy reaction to penicillin","D Red Man syndrome from infusing the drug too quickly","A nurse is providing discharge teaching to a patient starting metronidazole for a C. diff infection who mentions plans to have wine with dinner that evening. What is the most important teaching point?","A Alcohol is safe as long as it is consumed at least 2 hours after the dose","B Alcohol should be avoided for at least 36 hours after the last dose","C Alcohol only interacts with the IV form of this medication","D Alcohol increases the risk of a photosensitivity reaction with this medication","A nurse is caring for a patient with HIV who has been started on sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim (Bactrim). Which finding should prompt the nurse to hold the next dose and notify the provider?","A Mild nausea after the first dose","B Dry mouth and mild nausea","C New fever followed by a skin rash","D Diarrhea that resolved after one loose stool","A nurse is providing teaching to a patient newly started on doxycycline for acne. Which statements should the nurse include? Select all that apply.","A \"This medication should not be used during pregnancy because it can cause permanent tooth discoloration in the developing fetus.\"","B \"Take this medication with a full meal to increase absorption.\"","C \"Use sunscreen and protective clothing because this drug can cause photosensitivity.\"","D \"This medication kills bacteria by causing the cell wall to rupture.\"","E \"This medication is approved for use in children under 8 years old without restriction.\"","F \"Report any new or worsening diarrhea or unusual bruising while taking this medication.\""]}]},{"f":"week1-antivirals.html","t":"Antivirals","secs":[{"s":"Antivirals","a":"","k":["Week 1","Antiviral Pharmacology Basics","Herpes Simplex & Varicella-Zoster — Acyclovir, Valacyclovir & Famciclovir","Influenza — Oseltamivir (Tamiflu)","Cytomegalovirus — Ganciclovir","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions"]},{"s":"Antiviral Pharmacology Basics▾","a":"antiviral-pharmacology-basics","k":["Antiviral Pharmacology Basics▾","This lecture covers non-HIV antivirals. HIV gets its own antiviral content on the HIV & Antiretroviral Therapy page. Antivirals are a relatively new drug class — the first ones came out in the 1990s — and there are far fewer of them than th…","Why There Are So Few Antivirals","A virus can only replicate once it's inside a living host cell — so for an antiviral to work, it has to get inside the patient's own cells too.","Viruses don't have a cell wall of their own (unlike bacteria, which is what most antibiotics target). A virus lives by inserting its RNA or DNA into a healthy human cell and replicating from inside it.","Because the virus lives inside our own cells, a drug that kills the virus risks killing healthy cells along with it — a much harder balance than antibiotics have, since bacterial cells are a separate target.","Most antivirals only work during the replication phase, and signs/symptoms often don't show up until the virus has already finished replicating — so there's a narrow window where the drug can actually do anything.","Antiviral names end in \"-vir\": acyclovir, valacyclovir, famciclovir, ganciclovir, oseltamivir — all covered on this page.","General mechanism: antivirals work by inhibiting viral replication, which then allows the body's own immune system to clear the virus. Antivirals are only effective against a handful of viruses.","Herpes Simplex / Varicella-Zoster — Oral herpes, genital herpes, shingles (herpes zoster), chickenpox/varicella — Acyclovir, valacyclovir, famciclovir — this page","Influenza A (some B) — The flu — Oseltamivir (Tamiflu) — this page","Cytomegalovirus (CMV) — Concerning only in immunocompromised patients — Ganciclovir — this page","RSV — A common virus in children — Named in lecture, no specific drug given","Hepatitis — Viral hepatitis — Covered in the hepatitis lecture","HIV — — — See HIV & ART"]},{"s":"Herpes Simplex & Varicella-Zoster — Acyclovir, Valacyclovir & Famciclovir▾","a":"herpes-simplex-varicella-zoster-acyclovir-valacyclovir-famciclovir","k":["Herpes Simplex & Varicella-Zoster — Acyclovir, Valacyclovir & Famciclovir▾","All three drugs work the same way and are in the same class. The lecture focused on acyclovir specifically — valacyclovir and famciclovir are alternatives in the same family.","Acyclovir — Three Mechanisms of Action","Interferes with viral nucleic acid synthesis — stops viral DNA and RNA synthesis.","Prevents the virus from binding to the cell, so it can't get in and can't replicate further.","Turns on the body's own immune system to help kill the virus.","Indications — Oral herpes, genital herpes, shingles (herpes zoster), chickenpox/varicella — all caused by the same virus family.","Routes — Oral tablet, liquid, topical ointment, and IV.","Not a Cure — Decreases the severity and frequency of outbreaks, reduces viral shedding, and decreases the duration of illness — but doesn't eliminate the virus. It stays dormant in the body.","Treatment Pattern — Used for both the initial infection and recurrent outbreaks — often prescribed with refills, since outbreaks can reoccur and need repeat courses.","Side Effects — GI distress, renal impairment, seizures. Use cautiously in patients with ITP (idiopathic thrombocytopenia) — a low starting platelet count.","IV acyclovir: can cause tissue necrosis — make sure the IV line is very patent, or use a central line.","Epidemiology asides the instructor flagged as not heavily tested: about 1% of the population has genital herpes, and it's about twice as common in women as in men. HSV-1 generally occurs above the waist, HSV-2 below the waist. Herpes zoster…","There is a shingles vaccine (Shingrix) — a live vaccine given to people age 50 and older to help prevent shingles. It reduces the risk of shingles and decreases the frequency and severity of outbreaks if it does occur."]},{"s":"Influenza — Oseltamivir (Tamiflu)▾","a":"influenza-oseltamivir-tamiflu","k":["Influenza — Oseltamivir (Tamiflu)▾","Target — Best against influenza A; does have some action against influenza B. Also approved for the swine flu.","Mechanism — Inhibits neuraminidase, an enzyme flu viruses create to replicate — stopping viral replication.","Timing — Must be started before or within 48 hours of symptom onset.","Use Pattern — Primarily used for prophylaxis after exposure rather than treating active disease — typically reserved for elderly and immunocompromised patients after exposure, to avoid driving flu resistance to the drug.","Route — PO only.","Side Effects — Nausea, vomiting, seizures, renal impairment.","Once a patient is sick enough to be hospitalized for the flu, oseltamivir may still be tried, but if it's outside that 48-hour window it's unlikely to help — the virus has usually already finished replicating by the time someone is that sic…"]},{"s":"Cytomegalovirus — Ganciclovir▾","a":"cytomegalovirus-ganciclovir","k":["Cytomegalovirus — Ganciclovir▾","Treats — CMV (cytomegalovirus).","Population — Only a concern in immunocompromised patients — transplant recipients and immunosuppressed/AIDS patients. Not a concern for the general population.","Not a Cure — CMV can't be cured. Affected patients have recurrent infections.","CMV Retinitis — CMV infection in the eye — treated with an intraocular (into-the-eye) injection of ganciclovir.","Other Presentation — In other immunocompromised patients, CMV can present as a respiratory infection.","Mechanism — Inhibits viral DNA polymerase, causing chain termination — stops viral replication.","Routes — IV or PO. Usually started IV for a few weeks, then transitioned to an oral tablet.","PO Handling — Don't crush or split the tablet — it can irritate the skin. If it does get on bare skin, wash with soap and water.","Ganciclovir — four black-box warnings: hematologic toxicity (can cause pancytopenia — decreased red cells, white cells, and platelets; monitor labs), fertility impairment (in both men and women of childbearing age), fetal toxicity and birth…","Ganciclovir + imipenem: avoid combining — increased seizure risk. Imipenem's carbapenem seizure risk is covered on the ABX page","Ganciclovir is cleared through the kidneys — use caution with other nephrotoxic drugs and watch renal function."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","NCLEX-style practice questions covering this page's highest-yield content. Choose your answer for each, then submit to see your score and rationales — the same 19 questions also live in the Build Your Own Exam question bank.","A nursing student asks why there are so few antiviral medications compared to antibiotics. Which explanation by the instructor is most accurate?","A Because a virus replicates inside human cells, so a drug that stops it risks harming the patient's own cells too","B Because viruses have a thick, rigid protective cell wall that essentially no antiviral drug developed so far is able to penetrate","C Because symptoms typically appear well before viral replication actually begins, leaving little time to act","D Because antiviral drugs can only be given by the IV route, which severely limits their outpatient use","A patient asks how antiviral medications actually get rid of a virus. What is the nurse's best explanation of the general mechanism?","A The antiviral directly kills viral particles circulating in the bloodstream","B The antiviral inhibits viral replication, which allows the body's own immune system to clear the virus","C The antiviral causes chain termination of the virus's own DNA polymerase enzyme, which kills the infected cell and the virus outright","D The antiviral prevents the virus from binding to host cells, which cures the infection","The nurse is reviewing which antiviral drugs treat which viral infections. Based on the lecture content, which pairing is correct?","A Ganciclovir is used to treat influenza during the contagious period","B Oseltamivir is the antiviral used to treat cytomegalovirus infections","C Acyclovir is used to treat herpes zoster, also known as shingles","D Famciclovir is the specific antiviral used to treat RSV infections","A nursing student lists acyclovir's mechanisms of action. Which of the following is NOT one of acyclovir's three mechanisms?","A Interferes with viral nucleic acid synthesis, stopping viral DNA and RNA synthesis","B Prevents the virus from binding to the host cell so it cannot replicate further","C Turns on the body's own immune system to help kill the virus","D Causes chain termination of the viral DNA polymerase","A patient with genital herpes asks the nurse if acyclovir will cure the infection permanently. What is the nurse's best response?","A Acyclovir reduces outbreak severity and frequency, but the virus remains dormant, not eliminated","B Acyclovir clears the virus completely from the body after one full course of treatment","C Acyclovir is only effective while an outbreak is actively happening and provides no benefit at any other time","D Acyclovir works well for a first infection but has no effect on recurrent outbreaks","A patient is receiving IV acyclovir for a severe herpes infection. Which nursing action is priority to prevent a serious complication of this route?","A Infuse the medication rapidly to reduce total infusion time","B Ensure the IV line is very patent, or use a central line","C Withhold the medication if the patient reports mild GI upset","D Monitor platelet counts closely due to the patient's ITP history","A nurse reviews the history of a patient about to start acyclovir and notes idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP). What is the nurse's best action?","A Hold the medication permanently, since acyclovir is strictly contraindicated in every patient who has a history of ITP","B Switch the administration route to topical only, to avoid any systemic effects on platelets","C Give the medication with caution, since ITP means the patient already has a low starting platelet count","D Change the prescription to oseltamivir instead, since it carries no hematologic risk at all","A patient newly diagnosed with genital herpes asks why the acyclovir prescription includes refills. What is the nurse's best explanation?","A The refills are a pharmacy error, since acyclovir is only used once","B Refills are provided because acyclovir is typically taken continuously for the rest of the patient's life as ongoing suppressive therapy","C Refills allow the dose to be gradually increased until the virus is eliminated","D Because outbreaks can reoccur, acyclovir is often prescribed with refills for repeat treatment courses","A 55-year-old patient asks the nurse about preventing shingles. What should the nurse include when teaching about the Shingrix vaccine?","A Shingrix is a live vaccine that can reduce shingles risk and lessen outbreak severity if it does occur","B Shingrix is an inactivated vaccine that is only recommended starting at age 65 or older","C Shingrix is a vaccine that fully eliminates the herpes zoster virus from the body once it has been administered","D Shingrix is a vaccine given only to patients who are already severely immunocompromised","A patient calls the clinic five days after flu symptom onset asking about starting oseltamivir. What should the nurse explain about its effectiveness at this point?","A Oseltamivir will still fully treat the infection, since it works at any point during the illness","B Oseltamivir likely won't help much now, since it must start within 48 hours of symptom onset","C Oseltamivir works best only after the patient has already been hospitalized for the flu","D Oseltamivir should be started now, specifically to help prevent renal impairment","A nurse explains why oseltamivir is typically reserved for elderly and immunocompromised patients after flu exposure rather than given routinely for active flu symptoms. What is the primary rationale?","A Oseltamivir is too costly for routine use in otherwise healthy patients","B Oseltamivir only comes in an IV formulation, which limits outpatient use","C Reserving its use helps avoid driving viral resistance to the drug","D Oseltamivir is not approved for influenza A, only influenza B","A nursing student is studying oseltamivir's mechanism of action. Which statement is correct?","A It inhibits the viral DNA polymerase enzyme directly, causing termination of the growing viral DNA chain","B It interferes with viral nucleic acid synthesis directly","C It prevents the virus from binding to the host cell","D It inhibits neuraminidase, an enzyme the flu virus needs to replicate","A patient with CMV retinitis is prescribed ganciclovir. Which route should the nurse anticipate for this specific presentation?","A Intraocular injection directly into the eye","B Oral tablet only, to avoid systemic absorption","C IV infusion through a central line only","D Topical ointment applied to the affected eye","A nurse is teaching a community class about CMV. Which statement best reflects the population at risk described in the lecture?","A CMV is a significant concern for the general healthy population, and routine screening is recommended for all patients regardless of immune status","B CMV is primarily a concern in immunocompromised patients, such as transplant recipients or those with AIDS","C CMV mainly affects patients receiving prophylactic oseltamivir after flu exposure","D CMV is curable with a full course of ganciclovir, unlike other viral infections","Which mechanism of action is correctly matched to ganciclovir?","A Inhibits neuraminidase to stop viral replication","B Prevents the virus from binding to host cells","C Inhibits viral DNA polymerase, causing chain termination","D Turns on and strengthens the body's own immune system response in order to help kill the virus directly","A patient is prescribed oral ganciclovir tablets after completing an IV course. What is the most important instruction the nurse should give about administration?","A Take the tablet only on an empty stomach to increase absorption significantly","B Crush the tablet completely and mix it thoroughly into a small amount of food or a beverage before administering","C Store the tablet in the refrigerator at all times to prevent degradation","D Do not crush or split the tablet; wash bare skin with soap and water if contacted","A patient on ganciclovir develops a new infection requiring antibiotic therapy. Which medication should the nurse flag as increasing seizure risk if combined with ganciclovir?","A Imipenem","B A nephrotoxic drug such as an aminoglycoside","C Oseltamivir","D Acyclovir","A patient receiving ganciclovir is also started on a nephrotoxic medication for an unrelated condition. What is the nurse's priority action?","A Discontinue ganciclovir immediately, since nephrotoxic medications are contraindicated with it","B Monitor renal function closely, since ganciclovir is cleared through the kidneys","C Switch the patient to IV acyclovir instead, since it carries no renal risk","D Hold both medications until a hematology consult addresses pancytopenia risk","A nurse is teaching a patient about to start ganciclovir. Which of the following are among the black-box warnings for this medication? Select all that apply.","A Hematologic toxicity, including pancytopenia","B Impaired fertility in men and women of childbearing age","C Fetal toxicity and birth defects","D Possible carcinogenicity based on animal studies","E Tissue necrosis at the IV insertion site","F Renal impairment requiring dose adjustment"]}]},{"f":"week1-hiv.html","t":"HIV & Antiretroviral Therapy (ART)","secs":[{"s":"HIV & Antiretroviral Therapy (ART)","a":"","k":["Week 1","HIV & AIDS — the Basics","How HIV Attacks the Immune System","Stages of HIV Infection","AIDS-Defining Criteria","Oral Manifestations & HIV-Associated Dementia","Transmission & Risk","Antiretroviral Therapy (ART)","PrEP & PEP","Self-Test Flashcards","Practice Questions"]},{"s":"HIV & AIDS — the Basics▾","a":"hiv-aids-the-basics","k":["HIV & AIDS — the Basics▾","HIV — Human Immunodeficiency Virus — a retrovirus that invades the body and destroys CD4 T cells.","Retrovirus — A virus that carries only RNA (no DNA) and uses reverse transcriptase to make a DNA copy of itself inside a host cell.","HIV-1 — The type seen in U.S. practice. Discovered first; still the most prevalent type worldwide.","HIV-2 — A mutated, less pathogenic version — confined largely to West Africa, not commonly seen in the U.S.","AIDS — Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome, caused by HIV. Everyone with AIDS has HIV, but not everyone with HIV has AIDS — it develops from an untreated HIV infection or from severe immune dysfunction that builds up over years.","Who's Most Affected in the US","About 1.1 million people are newly infected annually in the U.S., and that number has been trending up.","South Africa has the highest HIV prevalence in the world — it's believed to be where the virus originated.","76% of adults and adolescents with HIV in the U.S. are men.","Black men have the highest rates of new HIV infections; Black men who have sex with men account for the most new and existing infections. (\"Men who have sex with men,\" or MSM, is the clinical term used here — it describes risk by behavior r…","New cases are also increasing among women — risk assessment shouldn't discount any group, especially in school health or other preventive-care settings."]},{"s":"How HIV Attacks the Immune System▾","a":"how-hiv-attacks-the-immune-system","k":["How HIV Attacks the Immune System▾","The HIV Life Cycle","The virus binds to the CD4 receptor on a host cell — mainly T lymphocytes, though it can also attack monocytes and macrophages.","It fuses with the cell membrane and enters the cell.","Inside, it releases reverse transcriptase, which makes a DNA copy of the virus's own RNA.","That new viral DNA is inserted into the host cell's own genetic material by another enzyme, HIV integrase.","The infected cell's machinery is hijacked to direct the synthesis and assembly of more virus.","New viral copies are released to go infect more CD4 cells, and the cycle repeats.","CD4 T cells are responsible for cell-mediated immunity — the body's long-term immune \"memory.\" Once HIV has taken over enough of them, that memory function is gone and the immune system collapses, leaving the person vulnerable to opportunis…","Because the viral DNA becomes part of the host cell's own DNA, a person is infected for life — HIV cannot be fully cleared, only suppressed to very low levels. The body does form anti-HIV antibodies, which is how infection is detected on a …"]},{"s":"Stages of HIV Infection▾","a":"stages-of-hiv-infection","k":["Stages of HIV Infection▾","Acute (Early) Infection — Rapid viral replication turns CD4 cells into \"HIV factories.\" About 40–60% of infected people don't notice or can't identify symptoms, since they're nonspecific. The person is infectious from day one — even before …","Clinical Latency (Chronic) — Viral levels stabilize as the body suppresses the \"factories\"; some healthy CD4 cells remain. Asymptomatic or only mildly symptomatic, with or without treatment. Untreated, this stage lasts about 3–12 years; wit…","Symptomatic HIV / AIDS — CD4 count continues a persistent decline over time until the bone marrow can no longer keep up, eventually reaching AIDS-defining criteria.","CD4 count and viral load move inversely: a higher CD4 count means a healthier immune system, a lower viral load means less circulating virus, and as one drops the other tends to rise. In a treated patient, viral load can stay very low or un…","The three stages above are the disease-progression picture. The CDC also uses a more granular surveillance staging system, based directly on CD4 count/percentage or an AIDS-defining condition, from the course slides:","Stage 0 — Early HIV infection — —","Stage 1 — ≥500 cells/mm³ — ≥26 — No AIDS-defining condition","Stage 2 — 200–499 cells/mm³ — 14–25 — No AIDS-defining condition","Stage 3 — <200 cells/mm³ — <14 — or documentation of an AIDS-defining condition","Stage Unknown — No data — No data — and no information on AIDS-defining conditions","CD4 percentage is used only when no CD4 count is available. (2014 CDC case definition for HIV infection among adolescents and adults.)","Symptoms during acute infection (or as the disease progresses) look a lot like the flu or COVID: fever, sore throat, swollen lymph nodes, rash, muscle aches, night sweats, mouth sores, chills, and fatigue. Because they're so nonspecific, te…"]},{"s":"AIDS-Defining Criteria▾","a":"aids-defining-criteria","k":["AIDS-Defining Criteria▾","A diagnosis becomes AIDS, rather than just HIV, either way: a CD4 count under 200 — regardless of symptoms — or the presence of an AIDS-defining condition, regardless of CD4 count.","AIDS-Defining Conditions to Memorize","Kaposi sarcoma","Wasting syndrome","Invasive (pervasive) candidiasis","Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP)","Tuberculosis (TB)","Invasive cervical cancer","HIV-related encephalopathy / AIDS dementia complex"]},{"s":"Oral Manifestations & HIV-Associated Dementia▾","a":"oral-manifestations-hiv-associated-dementia","k":["Oral Manifestations & HIV-Associated Dementia▾","Oral Manifestations — Fungal, viral, bacterial, or cancerous oral problems appear as CD4 counts begin to drop — an early sign the immune system is struggling and a possible cue to reassess the treatment regimen. Includes oral hairy leukopla…","HIV-Associated Dementia — Also called AIDS dementia complex — cognitive symptoms of dementia. Historically affected about 40–60% of patients; far less common now with modern treatment. Thought to occur because HIV crosses the blood-brain ba…"]},{"s":"Transmission & Risk▾","a":"transmission-risk","k":["Transmission & Risk▾","Anal or Vaginal Sex, No Condom — High risk if one partner is HIV-positive.","Oral Sex — Very low to no risk.","Perinatal (Mother to Baby) — About 25–30% if the mother is untreated; less than 2% if she's treated.","Shared Injection Equipment — High risk — IV drug use, dirty needles.","Blood Transfusion / Organ Transplant — Possible, but rare now given current screening.","Not Spread By","Sharing a toilet seat","Hugging","Kissing or spit","Other casual, non-fluid contact","Highest-risk group: men who have sex with men. Other major risk factors include injection drug use, unprotected heterosexual contact, perinatal exposure, and blood exposure. Transmission risk climbs with more frequent or longer contact, and…","Face/Skin Splash With Body Fluid — Essentially zero risk, even if blood is present.","Mucous Membrane or Skin Contact — Very low risk, even with blood involved.","Needle Stick — Still under 1%.","Regardless of how low the risk, any exposure still requires completing exposure paperwork/reporting, and post-exposure prophylaxis may be offered depending on the risk level."]},{"s":"Antiretroviral Therapy (ART)▾","a":"antiretroviral-therapy-art","k":["Antiretroviral Therapy (ART)▾","The instructor was explicit here: individual drug names aren't required for the drug matrix, but exam questions will cover the generalizations and principles below — this is a fast-moving treatment area.","Fusion Inhibitors — Block the virus from entering the cell.","CCR5 Antagonists — Also block viral entry, via the CCR5 co-receptor protein.","NRTIs — Nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors — inhibit reverse transcriptase.","NNRTIs — Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors — also inhibit reverse transcriptase, working similarly to NRTIs.","Protease Inhibitors — Inhibit HIV protease.","Integrase Inhibitors — Inhibit HIV integrase.","Drug class matches the step it blocks: fusion inhibitors and CCR5 antagonists stop the virus from getting in; NRTIs/NNRTIs, protease inhibitors, and integrase inhibitors each block one of the enzymes from the life cycle above (reverse trans…","A typical ART regimen is three drugs: two NRTIs plus a third agent from a different class.","NRTI Mechanism — Inhibits reverse transcriptase, blocking HIV's ability to incorporate its RNA into the host cell's DNA.","NRTI Adverse Effects — Peripheral neuropathy, pancreatitis, lipoatrophy, and hepatic steatosis (liver effects).","NRTIs — black-box warning: lactic acidosis. Lactate levels are monitored, especially during the first couple of months after starting treatment.","ART overall doesn't have many drug-drug interactions, which is a plus — but a three-drug regimen is still a lot for a patient to manage.","Treatment Principles","Start therapy as soon as possible after diagnosis.","The goal is to decrease viral load to an undetectable level, which gives the immune system a chance to recover and produce healthy, uninfected CD4 cells.","Effectiveness is monitored with CD4 count and viral load.","An undetectable viral load means effectively no risk of transmitting HIV to others — but the person is still considered HIV-positive."]},{"s":"PrEP & PEP▾","a":"prep-pep","k":["PrEP & PEP▾","PrEP (Pre-Exposure Prophylaxis) — Ongoing antiretroviral medication (typically a combination pill) taken by an HIV-negative person in an ongoing relationship with an HIV-positive partner, to prevent acquiring HIV. Reduces transmission risk …","PEP (Post-Exposure Prophylaxis) — Antiretroviral therapy started after a known or suspected high-risk exposure (a needle stick, unprotected sex with someone found to be HIV-positive, a high-risk healthcare exposure). Given for 28 days, with…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]},{"s":"Practice Questions▾","a":"practice-questions","k":["Practice Questions▾","NCLEX-style practice questions covering this page's highest-yield content. Choose your answer for each, then submit to see your score and rationales — the same 20 questions also live in the Build Your Own Exam question bank.","A nursing student is reviewing the HIV life cycle. Which event occurs first when HIV encounters a susceptible host cell?","A The virus binds to the CD4 receptor on the host cell surface.","B Reverse transcriptase converts viral RNA into a DNA copy.","C HIV integrase inserts viral DNA into the host cell's genome.","D New viral copies are released to infect other CD4 cells.","A patient asks the nurse to explain why HIV is called a 'retrovirus.' Which explanation is accurate based on the virus's life cycle?","A It integrates directly into host DNA without using any enzymes.","B It carries RNA only and uses reverse transcriptase to copy itself into DNA.","C It carries both RNA and DNA, allowing replication in any cell type.","D It uses HIV integrase to convert its RNA into a protein coat.","During patient teaching about the HIV life cycle, a nurse explains the role of HIV integrase. Which statement correctly describes this enzyme's action?","A It binds the virus to the CD4 receptor on the host cell.","B It converts the virus's RNA into a DNA copy inside the host cell.","C It inserts newly made viral DNA into the host cell's own genetic material.","D It assembles and releases new viral copies from the host cell.","A patient with advanced, untreated HIV asks why they keep developing infections they've had before and successfully fought off in the past. Which explanation reflects the pathophysiology described in the lecture?","A HIV destroyed the antibodies the body previously produced against those infections.","B HIV's bone marrow effects immediately stopped all white blood cell production.","C Rising viral load directly causes old infections to reactivate on its own.","D HIV destroyed enough CD4 T cells that the immune system's long-term memory is lost.","A newly diagnosed patient asks the nurse if HIV can ever be cured with treatment. Which response is accurate based on the pathophysiology of the virus?","A No — HIV's DNA integrates into the host cell's genome, so infection is lifelong.","B No — HIV antibodies attack and destroy the patient's own CD4 cells.","C Yes — an undetectable viral load means the virus has been completely eliminated.","D Yes — ART repairs reverse transcriptase so it can no longer function.","A patient in the acute (early) stage of HIV infection asks whether they could have transmitted the virus before noticing any symptoms. What is the nurse's best response?","A \"No, a person only becomes infectious once symptoms of acute infection appear.\"","B \"Yes, a person is infectious from day one, even before symptoms appear.\"","C \"No, infectiousness begins only after seroconversion produces detectable antibodies.\"","D \"Yes, but only once the CD4 count drops below 200 cells/mm³.\"","A patient recently diagnosed with HIV asks how long they could remain in the clinical latency (chronic) stage without treatment. Based on the material presented, what should the nurse explain?","A It typically lasts only a few weeks before progressing to symptomatic disease.","B It cannot be predicted because CD4 counts remain unchanged during this stage.","C Untreated, it typically lasts about 3 to 12 years before progressing.","D With treatment, it always progresses to AIDS within 3 to 12 years.","A patient on ART has a viral load report showing a decreasing trend over the past six months. Based on the inverse relationship described between CD4 count and viral load, what should the nurse expect to see in the CD4 count?","A The CD4 count should decrease in proportion to the viral load.","B The CD4 count should remain completely unaffected by viral load changes.","C The CD4 count should drop below the AIDS-defining threshold of 200.","D The CD4 count should tend to rise as the viral load falls.","A patient's labs show a CD4 count of 150 cells/mm³ with no AIDS-defining condition present. According to the CDC surveillance staging system, which stage should the nurse document?","A Stage 3","B Stage 2","C Stage 1","D Stage 0","A patient has a CD4 count of 550 cells/mm³ but has just been diagnosed with Kaposi sarcoma. How should the nurse classify this patient's diagnosis?","A HIV infection only, because the CD4 count is above the AIDS-defining threshold.","B AIDS, because the presence of an AIDS-defining condition qualifies regardless of CD4 count.","C Clinical latency, because the CD4 count indicates a stable immune system.","D Acute infection, because Kaposi sarcoma is an early symptom of seroconversion.","A nurse is reviewing a patient's chart for conditions that meet AIDS-defining criteria. Select all conditions from the list below that are classified as AIDS-defining conditions.","A Kaposi sarcoma","B Oral hairy leukoplakia","C Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP)","D Tuberculosis (TB)","E Periodontal disease","F Wasting syndrome","A nurse is teaching a class about HIV-associated dementia (AIDS dementia complex). Which statement should be included?","A It occurs because antiretroviral therapy directly damages neurons.","B It affects the majority of patients today, similar to rates seen before modern treatment.","C It occurs because HIV crosses the blood-brain barrier and is an AIDS-defining illness.","D It resolves completely once viral load becomes undetectable.","A dental hygienist notes oral hairy leukoplakia on the tongue of a patient with HIV. What should this finding prompt the nurse to consider?","A The patient has already met AIDS-defining diagnostic criteria based on this finding.","B The patient's viral load has dropped to an undetectable level.","C The patient has developed Pneumocystis pneumonia, an AIDS-defining condition.","D The patient's CD4 count may be dropping, prompting regimen reassessment.","A pregnant patient with HIV asks about the risk of transmitting the virus to her baby if she takes antiretroviral therapy throughout pregnancy. What should the nurse explain?","A Treatment lowers the risk to less than 2%, versus 25-30% untreated.","B The risk remains at 25-30% regardless of treatment status.","C Treatment eliminates all transmission risk, reducing it to 0%.","D The risk increases with treatment due to drug exposure to the fetus.","A nurse experiences a needle stick injury while caring for a patient with HIV. What should the nurse understand about this exposure?","A The risk is essentially zero, so no reporting or follow-up is required.","B The risk is still under 1%, but exposure paperwork must be completed regardless.","C The risk exceeds that of unprotected anal or vaginal sex with an HIV-positive partner.","D The risk is impossible to estimate because needle sticks were not studied.","A patient newly diagnosed with HIV expresses fear that they could transmit the virus to family members through shared meals or hugging. Which teaching point addresses this concern accurately?","A HIV is spread through casual contact such as hugging, requiring home precautions.","B HIV can be transmitted through saliva during kissing, so close contact should be limited.","C HIV is not spread by casual, non-fluid contact such as hugging or sharing a toilet seat.","D HIV can be transmitted through sharing a toilet seat if the patient has open sores.","A patient is prescribed a medication classified as a CCR5 antagonist. The nurse understands this drug works at which step of the HIV life cycle?","A Blocking HIV protease from processing viral proteins.","B Blocking HIV integrase from inserting viral DNA into the host genome.","C Blocking reverse transcriptase from copying viral RNA into DNA.","D Blocking the virus from entering the cell via the CCR5 co-receptor protein.","A nurse is providing teaching about NRTIs for a patient newly starting ART. The patient asks which adverse effect requires the closest monitoring, especially during the first couple months of treatment. Which response is correct?","A \"Lactic acidosis carries a black-box warning, so your lactate levels will be monitored closely.\"","B \"Peripheral neuropathy carries a black-box warning, so your reflexes will be monitored closely.\"","C \"Pancreatitis carries a black-box warning, so your amylase and lipase will be monitored closely.\"","D \"Hepatic steatosis carries a black-box warning, so your liver enzymes will be monitored closely.\"","A nurse is reviewing a new prescription for a patient starting ART for the first time. A typical initial regimen should include which combination of drug classes?","A One NRTI plus one NNRTI only.","B Two NRTIs plus a third agent from another class.","C Three drugs from the same class to maximize effectiveness.","D One fusion inhibitor plus one CCR5 antagonist only.","A patient on ART asks whether they still need to disclose their HIV status to sexual partners now that their viral load is undetectable. What should the nurse explain?","A \"No, an undetectable viral load means you are no longer HIV-positive.\"","B \"Yes, because an undetectable viral load still carries the same transmission risk as an untreated infection.\"","C \"An undetectable viral load means there's effectively no risk of transmitting HIV, but you're still HIV-positive.\"","D \"An undetectable viral load means your CD4 count has returned to normal and treatment can be stopped.\""]}]},{"f":"week1-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 1","The Three Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","How the Three Topics Cluster — The \"When\" Framework","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new — it's a map of Week 1's three topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. A central concept (the importance of timing and recognizing which…"]},{"s":"The Three Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-three-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Three Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Antimicrobials & Antibiotics (ABX) — How bacteria are classified by cell wall type, what antibiotics target each structure, how resistance develops, and when to treat vs. culture first.","2 — Antivirals — Why antivirals are rare compared to antibiotics, how the viral replication cycle creates a narrow treatment window, and specific drugs for herpes, influenza, and CMV.","3 — HIV & Antiretroviral Therapy (ART) — HIV's structure and replication, the CD4 count as an immune marker, HAART as combination therapy, and why adherence and resistance management are critical."]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content — each idea is fully taught in exactly one place. But they resurface as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why.","When treatment cannot wait for culture results — Antimicrobials & Antibiotics — Antivirals (oseltamivir has a 48-hour window, so empiric start is justified)","Why some drugs penetrate certain body compartments poorly — Antimicrobials & Antibiotics — Antivirals (CMV retinitis requires intraocular injection because IV drugs don't reach the eye), HIV (CNS penetration matters for preventing neurocogn…","Renal clearance and the need for dose adjustment — Antimicrobials & Antibiotics — Antivirals (ganciclovir is cleared through the kidneys), HIV ART (several drugs require renal dose adjustment)","Resistance development and how it starts — Antimicrobials & Antibiotics — HIV ART (resistant strains emerge within days if adherence lapses; combination therapy delays this), Antivirals (influenza resistance is why oseltamivir is reserved f…","Cell-based vs. host-based mechanisms of action — Antivirals — ABX (targeting bacterial cell walls) vs. antivirals (working inside host cells); HIV ART (targeting viral proteins, not host proteins)","Black-box warnings and high-alert drugs — All three topics have drugs with serious toxicity profiles — Ganciclovir (four black-box warnings), certain ART drugs (hepatotoxicity, teratogenicity), aminoglycosides (ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity)"]},{"s":"How the Three Topics Cluster — The \"When\" Framework▾","a":"how-the-three-topics-cluster-the-when-framework","k":["How the Three Topics Cluster — The \"When\" Framework▾","Week 1 is really teaching you to match drugs to timing and indication. The three topics show this progression:","\"I have symptoms NOW — bacteria or virus?\" — Antimicrobials & Antibiotics: Get a culture first if safe to delay. If not (meningitis, sepsis), treat empirically based on the most likely organism. Distinguish bacterial from viral by the clini…","\"It's a virus — which one, and how fast must I act?\" — Antivirals: Recognize that antivirals work during replication only. Oseltamivir has a 48-hour window; herpes drugs have a wider window. CMV is special — only in immunocompromised patien…","\"It's HIV — now we're planning years, not days.\" — HIV & ART: A single drug fails; combination therapy (HAART) is standard. CD4 count tells you how immune-depleted the patient is. Adherence is life-or-death — missing doses drives resistance…"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","Every danger callout across the three pages, condensed to a phrase. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation — click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","Antimicrobials & Antibiotics — Fluoroquinolones can rupture tendons — especially in older patients, those on corticosteroids, and with quinolone use in the past 3 months. Watch for tendon pain and report it immediately.","Antimicrobials & Antibiotics — Aminoglycosides are nephrotoxic and ototoxic — check SCr and trough levels before each dose; hearing loss can be permanent; use once-daily dosing when possible to reduce toxicity.","Antimicrobials & Antibiotics — Carbapenems (imipenem, meropenem) lower seizure threshold — especially risky in patients with renal impairment, CNS infection, or history of seizures. Never combine with ganciclovir.","Antivirals — IV acyclovir causes tissue necrosis if it extravasates — ensure the IV line is very patent, or use a central line; watch for pain, swelling, or blanching at the site.","Antivirals — Oseltamivir MUST start within 48 hours of symptom onset — after that window, the virus has usually finished replicating and the drug won't help.","Antivirals — Ganciclovir has FOUR black-box warnings: hematologic toxicity (monitor labs for pancytopenia), fertility impairment (both men and women), fetal toxicity/birth defects, and possible carcinogenicity. Avoid combining with imipenem…","HIV & ART — Missing doses of ART drives resistance — resistant strains can emerge within DAYS. Adherence is the difference between viral suppression and treatment failure. Missed doses early in therapy are more dangerous than missed doses l…","HIV & ART — IRIS (Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome) can occur within weeks of starting ART — the immune system suddenly becomes strong enough to mount an inflammatory response to opportunistic infections. Can be life-threatening …","Patterns you'll notice across these rules","\"Know the organism first, or start empirically with the broadest option.\" ABX teach this first — culture if you can wait, treat empirically if you can't. Antivirals narrow this to specific viruses; HIV requires the broadest combination (HAA…","\"Timing windows matter.\" Meningitis can't wait for culture (ABX). Oseltamivir has a 48-hour window (Antivirals). ART adherence is measured in days/hours — resistance emerges fast (HIV/ART).","\"The same drug can cause multiple serious toxicities.\" Aminoglycosides are both nephrotoxic and ototoxic. Ganciclovir has four black-box warnings. Several ART drugs carry hepatotoxicity, rash, and lipodystrophy warnings together.","\"Drug interactions can be deadly.\" Imipenem + ganciclovir = seizures. Many ART drugs interact with each other or with other medications; the combination matters more than any single drug.","\"Extravasation, poor penetration, and renal clearance come up across all three topics.\" They're mechanisms that determine whether the drug reaches the right place and at the right dose — central to why some drugs work and others fail."]}]},{"f":"week2-uri.html","t":"Upper Respiratory Infections (URI)","secs":[{"s":"Upper Respiratory Infections (URI)","a":"","k":["Week 2","Sputum & Airway Basics","Bronchitis & Influenza","Histamine, Allergic Rhinitis & Sinusitis","Pharyngitis, Tonsillitis & Laryngitis/Croup","Epiglottitis & Bronchospasm","Antihistamines (H1 Blockers)","Decongestants, Cough Suppressants & Expectorants/Mucolytics","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Sputum & Airway Basics▾","a":"sputum-airway-basics","k":["Sputum & Airway Basics▾","Sputum is mucus secreted from the respiratory tract and coughed up. Cilia normally move mucus — and whatever particles it traps — out of the airway through coughing. Sputum can be cultured to check for infection, but its color alone isn't d…"]},{"s":"Bronchitis & Influenza▾","a":"bronchitis-influenza","k":["Bronchitis & Influenza▾","Acute (Simple) Bronchitis","Inflammation of the bronchi and bronchioles — not down at the alveolar level, so there's no airflow obstruction.","About 80% viral; can be bacterial.","Usually mild and self-limiting; lasts about 3–4 weeks.","Symptoms: sore throat, runny nose, muscle aches, cough (~3 weeks), possible wheezing (not always present), productive cough, possible swollen lymph nodes.","Diagnosed by symptoms and physical exam; a CBC with diff is sometimes used if there's concern about progression (age, comorbidities).","Treatment: antibiotics if bacterial is suspected; otherwise supportive care — hydration, a cough suppressant, an expectorant.","Chronic Bronchitis","Bronchitis symptoms for at least 3 months of the year, for at least 2 consecutive years.","Usually related to cigarette smoking.","Unlike acute bronchitis, it does involve airflow obstruction.","Presents as exacerbations when it's active.","Chronic bronchitis is covered in full — pathophysiology, complications, and management — on the Obstructive Airway Disorders page","Influenza — Viral; types A and B are most common, and the virus can mutate. The vaccine composition changes yearly based on predictions of which strains are likely — not always accurate, but still worthwhile.","Vaccine Benefit — Doesn't necessarily prevent infection, but reduces the risk of severe illness and death by about 36%.","Onset — Rapid — fever, chills, body aches, feeling awful all at once.","Serious Complication — Secondary bacterial pneumonia (the flu virus damages the lungs' defenses, letting bacteria move in) — a major reason the flu vaccine is emphasized in elderly and comorbid patients."]},{"s":"Histamine, Allergic Rhinitis & Sinusitis▾","a":"histamine-allergic-rhinitis-sinusitis","k":["Histamine, Allergic Rhinitis & Sinusitis▾","What Histamine Does","Stored in mast cells (skin and soft tissue) and basophils (blood).","Skin: hives and itching.","Blood vessels: vasodilation (redness); if released systemically, can cause hypotension.","Airways: bronchoconstriction — shortness of breath.","Also affects sleep-wake cycles and increases stomach acid secretion.","Allergic Rhinitis — Inflammation and swelling of the nasal mucous membranes from an IgE-mediated histamine release, triggered by environmental allergens. Affects the upper and lower airways and the eyes. Symptoms: sneezing, runny nose, itch…","Sinusitis — Infection/inflammation of the facial sinuses — the cavity gets obstructed by fluid and edema, which lets bacteria grow. Often accompanies other URIs; more likely with nasal polyps, a deviated septum, or an allergic reaction. Vir…"]},{"s":"Pharyngitis, Tonsillitis & Laryngitis/Croup▾","a":"pharyngitis-tonsillitis-laryngitis-croup","k":["Pharyngitis, Tonsillitis & Laryngitis/Croup▾","Pharyngitis — Inflammation of the palate, tonsils, and uvula (back of the throat). The key question is whether it's bacterial (strep) or viral — bacterial pharyngitis often shows white spots/exudate on the throat and tonsils, while viral ty…","Tonsillitis — The tonsils become so swollen they're nearly touching — a very painful sore throat with difficulty swallowing. Recurrent or severe cases sometimes lead to tonsil removal.","Laryngitis — Inflammation of the vocal cords/larynx.","Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup) — Inflammation of the larynx, trachea, and bronchi together — produces the classic \"barking\" cough."]},{"s":"Epiglottitis & Bronchospasm▾","a":"epiglottitis-bronchospasm","k":["Epiglottitis & Bronchospasm▾","Epiglottitis: rare but potentially life-threatening — a swollen epiglottis can block the windpipe without timely treatment. Most common in children but can occur in adults. Rapid onset of fever, pain, and difficulty swallowing, plus droolin…","Bronchospasm: sudden constriction of the bronchial muscles that makes breathing difficult. Can be triggered by a history of asthma or by an active URI — patients being ventilated, recovering from anesthesia, or with a history of airway issu…","Bronchodilator and anticholinergic drug detail — mechanisms, dosing, and side effects — is covered in full on the Obstructive Airway Disorders page"]},{"s":"Antihistamines (H1 Blockers)▾","a":"antihistamines-h1-blockers","k":["Antihistamines (H1 Blockers)▾","Histamine has two receptor types: H1 receptors mediate smooth muscle contraction and capillary dilation — the target of antihistamines. H2 receptors mediate heart rate and gastric acid secretion — the target of H2 blockers, covered separate…","First-Generation (Sedating)","Diphenhydramine (Benadryl) is the main one — the one that belongs on the drug matrix. Also meclizine, promethazine (motion sickness/nausea), and dimenhydrinate (Dramamine, motion sickness) — these three don't need to go on the drug matrix.","Used for mild allergic reactions, motion sickness, and insomnia; can also treat/prevent severe anaphylactic reactions.","Given PO (common OTC) or IV.","Significant CNS depression — drowsiness, dizziness (the \"Benadryl hangover\"). Can cause paradoxical hyperactivity, especially in children.","Mild anticholinergic effect — dries up secretions (helps the runny nose) but can also cause constipation and urinary retention.","Second-Generation (Non-Sedating)","Loratadine, fexofenadine, cetirizine — all of these belong on the drug matrix.","Same indications as first-generation, but fewer side effects and much less drowsiness — can be taken in the morning.","Still some CNS effect possible at higher doses.","Antihistamine Cautions","Closed-angle glaucoma, cardiac disease, kidney disease/uncontrolled hypertension, peptic ulcer disease, seizures, BPH, and pregnancy — use carefully given the anticholinergic effect and the potential to raise blood pressure.","Nursing: monitor for dizziness with ambulation, monitor for urinary retention, and avoid driving or other tasks that need mental alertness — consider dosing a sedating antihistamine at night.","What actually needs to go on the drug matrix from this lecture: diphenhydramine, all three second-generation antihistamines (loratadine, fexofenadine, cetirizine), both decongestants (phenylephrine, pseudoephedrine), and both cough suppress…"]},{"s":"Decongestants, Cough Suppressants & Expectorants/Mucolytics▾","a":"decongestants-cough-suppressants-expectorants-mucolytics","k":["Decongestants, Cough Suppressants & Expectorants/Mucolytics▾","Decongestants — Phenylephrine and pseudoephedrine — sympathomimetics that activate alpha receptors, causing vasoconstriction that shrinks nasal blood vessels and opens the nasal passages. Side effects come from that sympathetic/CNS stimulat…","Cough Suppressants (Antitussives) — Dextromethorphan and benzonatate (Tessalon Perles) — both suppress the cough reflex directly in the brain. Coughing is usually a beneficial, protective response, so these are reserved for when it isn't — …","Expectorants — Guaifenesin (Mucinex) reduces the surface tension of secretions to make mucus easier to cough up — it doesn't actually decrease mucus production, and its effectiveness is debated. Encourage hydration alongside it. Minimal sid…","Mucolytics — Acetylcysteine decreases mucus viscosity, used in chronic pulmonary conditions such as cystic fibrosis (it's also the antidote for acetaminophen overdose — a different indication). Has a strong, rotten-egg smell. Carries a bron…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-obstructive-airway-disorders.html","t":"Obstructive Airway Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Obstructive Airway Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 2","Obstructive Disease Basics","Asthma","Chronic Bronchitis","Emphysema & the COPD Umbrella","Bronchodilators — Beta Agonists","Bronchodilators — Anticholinergics & Xanthine Derivatives","Anti-Inflammatory & Add-On Therapies","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Obstructive Disease Basics▾","a":"obstructive-disease-basics","k":["Obstructive Disease Basics▾","Why Air Gets Trapped","Narrowed airways make obstruction worse on expiration — air gets trapped in the lungs because it can't get out.","This increases the work of breathing and slows lung emptying, measured with FEV1 (forced expiratory volume in 1 second) on pulmonary function tests (PFTs).","All obstructive diseases cause a V/Q (ventilation-perfusion) mismatch — air and blood in the lungs aren't matched up well, so blood isn't oxygenated properly, leading to hypoxemia.","Trapped air also means less effective ventilation, so CO2 builds up — hypoventilation and hypercapnia.","Assessed with an ABG or VBG, exhaled CO2/capnography, and SpO2.","Dyspnea and wheezing (from inflamed airways) are hallmark findings across all three obstructive diseases. Hyperinflation from air trapping shows up on a chest X-ray."]},{"s":"Asthma▾","a":"asthma","k":["Asthma▾","Definition — Chronic inflammation of the bronchial airways — not the alveoli. That's what separates asthma from chronic bronchitis and emphysema.","Airflow Obstruction — Bronchial hyperresponsiveness causes bronchoconstriction and variable airflow obstruction — but unlike bronchitis and emphysema, it's mostly reversible.","Risk Factors — Usually a childhood-onset disease; strongly linked to allergies and a significant familial component (100+ implicated genes). Also: heavy allergen exposure, urban living, indoor/outdoor air pollution, tobacco smoke exposure, …","Common Triggers — Exercise/exertion is the most common trigger, plus secondhand smoke, climate, dust mites, pet dander, and pollen.","Asthma Pathophysiology, Step by Step","Exposure to a trigger/antigen activates an intense immune response — mast cells, eosinophils, neutrophils, basophils, T-helper cells, and B lymphocytes are all recruited.","This causes bronchial airway inflammation.","Inflammation leads to mucus hypersecretion, smooth muscle constriction (narrowing the airway), and swelling.","Result: wheezing, cough, shortness of breath, chest tightness, and an increased work of breathing.","There's an early and a late asthmatic response. The early response is immediate — inflammatory mediators cause vasodilation, capillary permeability, edema, bronchoconstriction, and mucus. A late response can follow about 48 hours later, as …","Repeated, untreated exacerbations lead to airway remodeling — irreversible long-term damage that becomes chronic asthma. Bronchoconstriction is what causes the acute dyspnea, but inflammation is what drives the long-term severity and remode…","Diagnosis — History (childhood allergies, wheezing episodes, exercise intolerance) plus PFTs — the gold standard. Look for a decreased expiratory flow rate and decreased FEV1. Often mistaken for heart failure, cystic fibrosis, or vocal cord…","Classic Symptoms — Wheezing, dyspnea, cough, chest tightness.","Severe Attack — Accessory muscle use, distant or decreased/absent breath sounds, diaphoresis, inability to speak — can progress to a silent chest and respiratory failure. Asthma attacks can be fatal.","Asthma Management Overview","Avoid known triggers.","Monitor with a peak flow meter (tracks FEV1 at home) and an asthma action plan built around green/yellow/red zones based on the patient's personal best.","PO corticosteroids are the mainstay for exacerbations; a short-acting beta agonist inhaler covers milder forms.","More severe disease adds anti-inflammatory therapy — inhaled corticosteroids, long-acting beta agonists, or leukotriene antagonists (full drug detail in the Pharmacology sections below).","Immunotherapy — allergy shots or monoclonal antibody therapy — is an option too.","Status asthmaticus: an unrelenting asthma attack — a life-threatening emergency. The patient develops a silent chest (no air movement at all), and an ABG/VBG shows CO2 greater than 70. Often requires IV epinephrine."]},{"s":"Chronic Bronchitis▾","a":"chronic-bronchitis","k":["Chronic Bronchitis▾","Definition — Hypersecretion of mucus with a chronic productive cough, present for at least 3 months of the year for at least 2 consecutive years — typically worse in winter. Timing is what makes the diagnosis.","Cause — About 90% of patients with chronic bronchitis smoke cigarettes. Smoking and vaping together carry a 6x greater risk.","Airflow Obstruction — Present — unlike acute/simple bronchitis. This is what classifies chronic bronchitis as a form of COPD.","Hallmark Sign — A persistent, productive cough — purulent if a respiratory infection is also present. These patients are more prone to respiratory infections because the mucus becomes a breeding ground.","Diagnosis — History, physical exam, chest imaging, and PFTs — decreased expiratory volume from the airflow obstruction.","Reversibility — By the time most patients seek treatment, the airway changes are usually irreversible.","Chronic Bronchitis Pathophysiology, Step by Step","Inhaled irritants cause airway inflammation and white blood cell infiltration of the bronchial walls.","This drives mucus production, inflammation, and bronchial edema.","Goblet cells respond by getting both bigger and more numerous (hyperplasia) — producing more thick, tenacious mucus.","Smoking damages ciliary function, so that mucus can't be cleared effectively.","The accumulation leads to chronic bronchospasm and, over time, pulmonary fibrosis.","Late in the disease, this can progress to pulmonary hypertension — causing syncope, dyspnea, and fatigue — and eventually right-sided heart failure, also called cor pulmonale.","Smoking cessation can halt progression, but it doesn't reverse or cure the damage already done. Management includes bronchodilators, mucus thinners/expectorants, prophylactic antibiotics during certain times of year, chest physiotherapy, an…"]},{"s":"Emphysema & the COPD Umbrella▾","a":"emphysema-the-copd-umbrella","k":["Emphysema & the COPD Umbrella▾","Definition — Permanent enlargement of the airspaces distal to the terminal bronchioles, with destruction of the alveolar walls.","Cause of Obstruction — Inflammation and loss of elastic recoil — not mucus production, which is what makes chronic bronchitis different.","Mechanism — An imbalance between proteases/antiproteases, plus oxidative stress and apoptosis, breaks down elastin and destroys alveoli — creating blebs/bullae that can't perform gas exchange.","Major Risk Factors — Smoking is the primary cause, plus secondhand smoke, air pollution, and frequent childhood respiratory infections.","Genetic Form — Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency — under 2% of cases. Suspect this in a patient with emphysema who has never smoked.","Diagnosis — PFTs (decreased FEV1); chest X-ray (hyperinflated lungs); ABG (respiratory acidosis — high CO2, low pH); alpha-1 antitrypsin level if there's no smoking history.","Classic Presentation","Gradually increasing dyspnea on exertion, eventually progressing to dyspnea at rest, with a prolonged expiratory phase. Often oxygen-dependent.","Malnutrition and muscle wasting — breathing becomes the body's whole priority, so other needs get pushed aside.","Barrel chest (a roughly 1:1 AP diameter) from chronic hyperinflation.","Pursed-lip breathing — provides positive pressure that helps keep airways open.","Quiet, diminished breath sounds are the hallmark finding.","\"Blue Bloater\" — Chronic Bronchitis","Tends to be more overweight","More cyanotic","More edema","More wheezing","May have an elevated hemoglobin","More hypoxic overall","\"Pink Puffer\" — Emphysema","Tends to be more malnourished/thin","Quiet, diminished breath sounds","Pursed-lip breathing","Barrel chest","More hypercapnic and dyspneic overall","COPD (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease) is the umbrella term for chronic bronchitis and emphysema together — both cause trouble getting air out.","Management: smoking cessation, bronchodilators, anti-inflammatory agents, home oxygen, and breathing/relaxation techniques. Infection prevention matters a lot here — early broad-spectrum antibiotics are often started at the first sign of in…"]},{"s":"Bronchodilators — Beta Agonists▾","a":"bronchodilators-beta-agonists","k":["Bronchodilators — Beta Agonists▾","Two big pharmacology classes treat these three diseases: bronchodilators (relax bronchial smooth muscle) and anti-inflammatories (reduce bronchial inflammation). Either class can be used across asthma, chronic bronchitis, or emphysema, thou…","Short-Acting (SABA)","Albuterol, levalbuterol.","Rescue drugs — onset within minutes, lasting about 4–6 hours; given every 4–6 hours.","First-line treatment for an acute asthma attack; treats acute bronchospasm, wheezing, chest tightness, and shortness of breath from asthma, chronic bronchitis, or emphysema.","Using more than one canister a month signals inadequate asthma control and may mean adding anti-inflammatory therapy (there are about 200 puffs per inhaler).","Also used prophylactically before exercise for exercise-induced asthma — but not recommended for regular daily use.","Long-Acting (LABA)","Salmeterol, formoterol.","Preventer/maintenance drugs — will not help an acute attack. Lasts 12–24 hours, usually dosed twice daily (often by DPI).","Always given combined with an inhaled corticosteroid — never alone.","Historically linked to increased asthma-related deaths, more so in Black and African American patients — used to carry a black-box warning, later removed after further study.","Used for moderate-to-severe asthma or worsening COPD.","Selective (Beta-2) — Albuterol, salmeterol — act only on beta-2 receptors in the lungs. This is what's preferred for pulmonary conditions, since it limits systemic effects.","Non-Selective — Epinephrine, metaproterenol — also stimulate beta-1 (cardiac effects: increased HR and BP) and alpha receptors (vasoconstriction, decreased mucosal edema/secretions). More systemic side effects overall.","Beta Agonist Cautions & Side Effects","Use cautiously with uncontrolled hypertension, cardiac rhythm problems, or high stroke risk.","Avoid combining with MAOIs or other sympathomimetics (ephedrine, pseudoephedrine) — risk of hypertension.","Can raise blood glucose — patients may need more insulin during frequent or exacerbation-driven use.","Common side effects: insomnia, restlessness, anorexia, increased heart rate (PVCs/PACs), tremors, headache.","Overdose risk: paradoxical bronchospasm — can be reversed with a beta blocker if truly needed, used cautiously.","Delivery is mostly inhaled to minimize systemic effects. MDI (metered dose inhaler) is not breath-activated and takes a slow, deep inhale technique; DPI (dry powder inhaler) is breath-activated and generally easier for children or patients …","Beta agonist names end in \"-erol\"/\"-terol\": albuterol, levalbuterol (short-acting); salmeterol, formoterol (long-acting). Short vs. long acting matters — mixing up rescue and preventer drugs is a common exam trap."]},{"s":"Bronchodilators — Anticholinergics & Xanthine Derivatives▾","a":"bronchodilators-anticholinergics-xanthine-derivatives","k":["Bronchodilators — Anticholinergics & Xanthine Derivatives▾","Anticholinergics — Ipratropium is the main one. Blocking acetylcholine inhibits the parasympathetic nervous system, shifting toward sympathetic tone — bronchodilation, less mucus production. A prophylactic (daily) drug, not a rescue drug. O…","Xanthine Derivatives (Methylxanthines) — Theophylline, aminophylline. Inhibit phosphodiesterase, raising cAMP — which relaxes smooth muscle and blocks IgE-mediated release of allergic mediators, producing bronchodilation. Second-line, tried…","Classic anticholinergic side effects: \"dry as a bone, hot as a hare, blind as a bat, red as a beet, mad as a hatter\" — decreased secretions, increased temperature, blurred vision, flushing, and confusion.","Toxicity Signs — Insomnia, headache, tachycardia, dysrhythmias, seizures — distinct from the common side effects of nausea, vomiting, and decreased appetite.","Contraindications — Dysrhythmias, seizure disorders, hyperthyroidism, peptic ulcers.","Monitoring — Narrow therapeutic index — serum theophylline levels are monitored closely. Toxicity is treated with activated charcoal.","Smoking Interaction — Smoking decreases theophylline absorption. If a patient on theophylline quits smoking, the dose may need to be decreased and levels monitored more closely.","Other Cautions — Avoid caffeine (additive effect). Xanthine derivatives have many drug interactions overall — important to communicate clearly about who is taking one."]},{"s":"Anti-Inflammatory & Add-On Therapies▾","a":"anti-inflammatory-add-on-therapies","k":["Anti-Inflammatory & Add-On Therapies▾","Leukotriene Receptor Antagonists — Montelukast (Singulair, age 12 months+), zafirlukast (age 5+). Block leukotriene receptors on immune cells in the lungs, preventing inflammation, bronchoconstriction, and mucus production. Given PO (chewab…","Inhaled Corticosteroids — Beclomethasone, budesonide, fluticasone. Fewer systemic side effects than PO corticosteroids (which cover acute exacerbations instead). Not a rescue drug — for prevention and long-term maintenance; takes several we…","Mast Cell Stabilizers — Cromolyn is the only one. Stabilizes mast cell membranes and prevents the release of bronchoconstrictive, inflammatory substances. Used for prevention — taken 15–20 minutes before a known trigger. Not a rescue drug, …","Newer Add-On Therapies","Omalizumab — a monoclonal antibody that binds IgE, limiting the release of allergic mediators and reducing airway hyperresponsiveness. Asthma-specific; always an add-on, never given alone. Given by injection.","Roflumilast — a selective PDE4 inhibitor with potent anti-inflammatory effects in the lungs. Used for COPD exacerbation prevention, not acute treatment; given orally; works best for chronic bronchitis with a history of frequent exacerbation…","Omalizumab: carries a real risk of anaphylaxis — monitor closely for hypersensitivity reactions, especially with the first doses.","Rescue vs. Long-Term Control, Summarized","Long-term control (preventers): anticholinergics, xanthine derivatives, inhaled corticosteroids, leukotriene receptor antagonists, mast cell stabilizers, long-acting beta agonists.","Rescue (quick relief): short-acting beta agonists — albuterol. If a patient suddenly can't breathe and is wheezing, albuterol is the only right answer."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-pneumonia.html","t":"Pneumonia","secs":[{"s":"Pneumonia","a":"","k":["Week 2","Pneumonia Basics & Risk Factors","Classification — CAP, HAP, VAP & HCAP","How Pneumonia Develops & Presents","Diagnosis","Bacterial & Aspiration Pneumonia","Viral & Atypical Pneumonia","Treatment & Prevention","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Pneumonia Basics & Risk Factors▾","a":"pneumonia-basics-risk-factors","k":["Pneumonia Basics & Risk Factors▾","Pneumonia is an infection in the lower respiratory tract — the pathogen has gotten past the upper airway's defenses (cilia, mucus, the cough reflex) and set up below them. It can be bacterial, viral, fungal, protozoal, or parasitic. It's tr…","Risk Factors","Age extremes — children under 5 and adults over 70–80.","Compromised immunity — long-term steroid therapy, organ transplant, HIV.","Underlying lung disease — COPD, pulmonary hypertension.","Alcohol use — the risk here is from aspiration, not a liver effect.","Altered level of consciousness or impaired swallowing — both raise aspiration risk.","Nursing home residency and immobility.","Hospitalization, especially with endotracheal intubation.","Thoracic or abdominal surgery, or anesthesia.","Influenza — the single most common overall cause of pneumonia.","Smoking — impairs ciliary clearance, a risk factor for every respiratory condition."]},{"s":"Classification — CAP, HAP, VAP & HCAP▾","a":"classification-cap-hap-vap-hcap","k":["Classification — CAP, HAP, VAP & HCAP▾","CAP — Community-acquired pneumonia — develops outside the hospital. One of the most common reasons for hospitalization, and generally easier to treat than HAP.","HAP — Hospital-acquired pneumonia — develops 48+ hours after hospital admission. Worse outcomes than CAP, often associated with ICU care.","VAP — Ventilator-associated pneumonia — a subtype of HAP in intubated patients. More virulent, more contagious, and more deadly.","HCAP — Healthcare-associated pneumonia — technically grouped with CAP. Occurs in patients with frequent healthcare contact (nursing home residents, chronic hospitalizations, dialysis or chemo patients) without being truly hospital-acquired."]},{"s":"How Pneumonia Develops & Presents▾","a":"how-pneumonia-develops-presents","k":["How Pneumonia Develops & Presents▾","Pneumonia Pathophysiology, Step by Step","A pathogen reaches the lower airway — most commonly through aspiration of oropharyngeal secretions, or through inhaled droplets from someone else's cough.","This triggers an inflammatory reaction and pulmonary vasodilation.","Infection spreads into the alveoli; goblet cells are stimulated and secrete mucus.","Mucus accumulates between the alveoli and pulmonary capillaries, so the alveoli can't open and close properly.","Gas exchange becomes impaired, ciliary clearance fails, and the immune system activates.","Often Preceded By — An upper respiratory infection.","Common Symptoms — Fever, chills, cough (productive or dry, depending on cause), malaise, pleuritic chest pain (more pain with breathing).","Severe Symptoms — Dyspnea, hemoptysis.","Bacterial Cough — Typically productive and purulent — sputum may be green, rusty-colored, or described as \"currant jelly.\"","Viral Cough — Typically non-productive and scant.","Respiratory distress vs. respiratory failure: distress means the patient is still maintaining oxygenation, but only with an increased work of breathing — accessory muscle use, frequent breaks, an increased heart rate. Failure means they can…"]},{"s":"Diagnosis▾","a":"diagnosis","k":["Diagnosis▾","Physical Assessment — Cough, fever, chills, wet/rhonchorous breath sounds, pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, exercise intolerance. Over a consolidated area, expect dullness to percussion (normally resonant), inspiratory crackles, increased tac…","Chest X-Ray — Shows an infiltrate or area of consolidation.","CBC — An elevated white blood cell count suggests a bacterial cause.","Sputum Culture & Sensitivity — The gold standard — identifies the specific pathogen and which antibiotics will work against it."]},{"s":"Bacterial & Aspiration Pneumonia▾","a":"bacterial-aspiration-pneumonia","k":["Bacterial & Aspiration Pneumonia▾","Staphylococcus aureus — Gram-positive; usually enters via the bloodstream/IV route. The most common gram-positive cause of hospital-acquired pneumonia, often as MRSA.","Streptococcus pneumoniae — Gram-positive; the most common organism in bacterial community-acquired pneumonia (\"pneumococcal pneumonia\"). Sputum is often brown or rusty-tinged.","Gram-Negative Organisms — Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella pneumoniae — tend to cause more severe illness and are harder to treat. Often linked to central-line infections or IV drug use, and associated with hospital-acquired pneumonia…","MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus) is defined in full on the ABX page's resistance & stewardship section","Aspiration Pneumonia","Aspirated GI contents trigger an inflammatory reaction; the severity depends on the pH of the aspirate — more acidic content causes a worse inflammatory response.","One way to help prevent severe aspiration pneumonia is a PPI, which lowers gastric acidity.","Risk factors: altered level of consciousness, alcohol use, immobility, NG tubes, decreased gag reflex, decreased gastric emptying.","Aspiration can be silent — a cough isn't required. High-risk patients need careful feeding observation and may need a dysphagia evaluation."]},{"s":"Viral & Atypical Pneumonia▾","a":"viral-atypical-pneumonia","k":["Viral & Atypical Pneumonia▾","Viral Pneumonia — Influenza is the most common cause overall and the most common cause of CAP; other viruses include adenovirus, RSV, and parainfluenza. Viruses impair the lungs' immune defenses, raising the risk of a secondary bacterial pn…","Pneumocystis Pneumonia (PCP) — A yeast-like fungus; associated with immunosuppression, most common in HIV.","Mycoplasma pneumoniae — \"Walking pneumonia\" — mild, with a persistent cough, headache, and earache. Has properties of both bacteria and viruses.","Legionella (Legionnaires' Disease) — Gram-negative; spread through water systems — old air conditioners, misted produce, hot tubs. Can be very deadly.","Aspergillus — Fungal; comes from old building walls, construction sites, stored grains, dead leaves, or compost. Can be severe, especially in immunocompromised patients.","Legionella pneumonia is a reportable disease. A confirmed case has to be reported to the health department so the water source can be identified, cleaned, or fixed — these patients also tend to get sicker than with other types of pneumonia."]},{"s":"Treatment & Prevention▾","a":"treatment-prevention","k":["Treatment & Prevention▾","Bacterial pneumonia is treated with antibiotics. Viral pneumonia gets supportive care unless a secondary bacterial infection develops. General supportive measures for any pneumonia: adequate ventilation and oxygenation, hydration, good pulm…","PCV13 — Protects against 13 strains of pneumococcal pneumonia.","PPSV23 — Covers an additional 23 types.","Both are generally given to elderly and immunocompromised patients."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-tuberculosis.html","t":"Tuberculosis (TB)","secs":[{"s":"Tuberculosis (TB)","a":"","k":["Week 2","TB Basics & Transmission","Latent vs. Active TB","Screening & Diagnosis","First-Line TB Drugs — Isoniazid & Rifampin","First-Line TB Drugs — Ethambutol, Pyrazinamide & Streptomycin","Drug-Resistant TB","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"TB Basics & Transmission▾","a":"tb-basics-transmission","k":["TB Basics & Transmission▾","Causative Organism — Mycobacterium tuberculosis is the most common cause; M. leprae causes leprosy, a lesser public-health concern than the TB strains.","Bacteria Type — An aerobic, rod-shaped bacillus — it needs a lot of oxygen to grow and proliferate, which is why TB typically affects the lungs first (it can also grow in bone or brain).","Granulomas — Walled-off areas containing macrophages and lymphocytes, with a hard outer casing and a cheese-like (caseous) center made of broken-down tissue. Sometimes called tubercles.","Growth Rate — Very slow-growing — which makes TB harder to treat, since most drugs work by targeting actively proliferating organisms.","Transmission","Spread human-to-human (by far the most common route), though also from cattle or birds.","Travels in airborne droplets, expelled by an infected person's cough or sneeze and inhaled by a new host.","From there it spreads to the lungs by traveling through the blood and lymphatic system."]},{"s":"Latent vs. Active TB▾","a":"latent-vs-active-tb","k":["Latent vs. Active TB▾","Latent TB","A person with a normal, working immune system exposed to TB usually just kills off the bacteria — no disease results.","In latent TB, the bacteria survive but stay walled off inside granulomas, dormant — no clinical signs or symptoms.","Can remain latent for life.","Active TB","Develops when the immune system can no longer contain the bacteria and it reactivates.","Reactivation triggers: HIV/immunocompromise, immunosuppressive medications (e.g., for rheumatoid arthritis or another autoimmune disease), poor nutritional status, renal failure.","Symptoms usually develop gradually — often not noticed until the disease is fairly advanced.","Early, Nonspecific Symptoms — Fatigue, weight loss, lethargy, anorexia, a low-grade fever (classically worse in the afternoon), and a productive cough that develops slowly over months.","More TB-Specific — Night sweats. Generalized anxiety can also develop as the disease progresses.","Advanced, Untreated — Dyspnea, chest pain, hemoptysis.","Extrapulmonary TB — If the bacteria implant somewhere outside the lungs: neurologic symptoms/meningitis (brain), bone pain (bone), or urinary problems (kidney)."]},{"s":"Screening & Diagnosis▾","a":"screening-diagnosis","k":["Screening & Diagnosis▾","Interferon-Gamma Release Assay — A blood draw — used for high-risk populations (for example, a nurse working in a high-risk area).","TB Skin Test — Used for non-high-risk, general-population screening (for example, a healthcare student).","Chest X-Ray — The next step after a positive screening test — a positive screen alone doesn't confirm active TB, so the chest X-ray looks for granulomas.","Sputum Stain & Culture — Looks directly for mycobacteria — confirms the diagnosis.","Patients are considered contagious until their sputum no longer grows mycobacteria. The exact timing of that is Med-Surg content, not needed for this course."]},{"s":"First-Line TB Drugs — Isoniazid & Rifampin▾","a":"first-line-tb-drugs-isoniazid-rifampin","k":["First-Line TB Drugs — Isoniazid & Rifampin▾","TB treatment uses first-line drugs (the primary regimen) and second-line drugs (reserved for drug-resistant cases — not covered by name in this course). Drug-susceptibility testing guides therapy, but while results are pending, patients are…","Isoniazid (INH) — The most widely used TB medication. Disrupts mycobacterial cell wall synthesis. Given PO. Side effects: peripheral neuropathy (teach and monitor for it, similar to diabetic neuropathy), hepatotoxicity (metabolized by the l…","Rifampin — Also first-line; used for other mycobacterial infections too (meningitis, HIV-related infections, leprosy). Works by inhibiting protein synthesis. Metabolized by the liver — can cause hepatitis, so liver function is monitored; ca…","Isoniazid — black-box warning: increased risk of liver injury and hepatitis."]},{"s":"First-Line TB Drugs — Ethambutol, Pyrazinamide & Streptomycin▾","a":"first-line-tb-drugs-ethambutol-pyrazinamide-streptomycin","k":["First-Line TB Drugs — Ethambutol, Pyrazinamide & Streptomycin▾","Ethambutol — First-line, bacteriostatic. Suppresses RNA synthesis, which in turn suppresses protein synthesis. Side effect: retrobulbar neuritis — inflammation of the optic nerve at the back of the eye. Watch for visual changes; these patie…","Pyrazinamide (PZA) — Bacteriostatic or bactericidal depending on concentration. Always used in combination with other TB drugs, never alone. Thought to inhibit lipid and nucleic acid synthesis. Metabolized by the liver — hepatotoxicity risk…","Streptomycin — The first drug ever available for TB — an aminoglycoside. Interferes with normal protein synthesis, producing faulty proteins in the bacteria. Side effects: ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, and blood dyscrasias (affecting bleedin…","Aminoglycoside toxicity (nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity) is covered as a drug-class fact on the ABX page","All five of these — isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide, and streptomycin — belong on the drug matrix and are fair game by name on the exam."]},{"s":"Drug-Resistant TB▾","a":"drug-resistant-tb","k":["Drug-Resistant TB▾","MDR-TB (Multi-Drug-Resistant TB)","A growing concern both nationally and globally — a significant issue in HIV/AIDS communities and other at-risk populations.","At-risk groups: people who are homeless, malnourished, using substances, living with cancer, immunosuppressed, or living in crowded or poor-sanitation housing.","In the U.S., Asian and Hispanic immigrant populations are particularly high-risk, often having contracted resistant strains in countries with less consistent access to medications.","Because TB spreads through airborne transmission, a resistant strain moving through a community is a serious public health concern.","Resistant cases move to second-line drugs, which are effective but not as effective as first-line therapy."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-anemia.html","t":"Anemia","secs":[{"s":"Anemia","a":"","k":["Week 2","Anemia Basics & Classifying by Cell Morphology","Clinical Manifestations of Anemia","Nutritional Deficiency Anemias — Iron, B12 & Folate","Anemia of Chronic Kidney Disease & Aplastic Anemia","Hemolytic Anemias & Acute Blood Loss — Sickle Cell, Thalassemia & Hemorrhage","Anemia Pharmacology — Iron, B12 & Folate Replacement","Anemia Pharmacology — Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents (ESAs)","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Anemia Basics & Classifying by Cell Morphology▾","a":"anemia-basics-classifying-by-cell-morphology","k":["Anemia Basics & Classifying by Cell Morphology▾","What Anemia Is","Anemia is a lack of red blood cells (RBCs) — either a reduced total number of RBCs, or a decrease in the quality/quantity of hemoglobin.","Causes fall into a few buckets: impaired RBC production, acute or chronic blood loss, erythrocyte (RBC) destruction, or a combination.","Hemoglobin provides the oxygen-carrying capacity of an RBC — each chain has a heme unit with iron at its center, and hemoglobin reversibly binds oxygen and CO2 for transport. Functioning hemoglobin is required for oxygen delivery to the tis…","The bus analogy: the red blood cell is the bus, hemoglobin are the seats, iron is what the seats are made of, and oxygen is the passengers. No iron means no seats, so oxygen can't ride along — a problem with the iron, the hemoglobin, or the…","Regardless of cause, every anemia means less oxygen reaches the tissues — that's what drives the clinical manifestations.","Hemoglobin — not hematocrit — is the best indicator of anemia. Hematocrit is much more affected by a patient's fluid volume status, while hemoglobin is not.","Normocytic, Normochromic — Normal-sized cells, normal hemoglobin color/function. Points to blood loss, sickle cell disease, or aplastic anemia.","Microcytic, Hypochromic — Small (micro), pale (hypo) cells with reduced hemoglobin. This is iron deficiency anemia — without iron, hemoglobin can't be made correctly.","Macrocytic — Large, abnormally shaped cells, but normal hemoglobin color. Measured by MCV (mean corpuscular volume) — increased in macrocytic anemia. Points to a B12 or folate deficiency: without either, RBC DNA synthesis is impaired, produ…","Size + color tells you the type: micro + pale = iron deficiency. Macro + normal color = B12/folate deficiency (megaloblastic). Normal size + normal color = blood loss, sickle cell, or aplastic anemia."]},{"s":"Clinical Manifestations of Anemia▾","a":"clinical-manifestations-of-anemia","k":["Clinical Manifestations of Anemia▾","Mild — Often no symptoms at all — the body compensates well.","Mild to Moderate — Fatigue, weakness, mild tachycardia (the heart beats faster to move more oxygen), and possible shortness of breath.","Moderate to Severe — Tachycardia and tachypnea, low blood pressure, pallor, faintness, and cardiovascular symptoms with exertion. In very severe cases: bone pain (from the marrow working overtime), chest pain, heart failure, and an increase…","Why These Symptoms Happen","Weakness/fatigue: muscles and metabolism aren't getting enough oxygen to work normally.","Pallor: the body redistributes blood away from the skin toward organs that need it more (kidneys, lungs, gut, brain, heart).","Tachycardia/palpitations: a compensatory response — the heart beats faster to try to deliver more blood to the tissues, though this doesn't fix the underlying problem for long.","Bone pain: a chronic-anemia symptom, from the bone marrow working hard to produce more RBCs.","Severity also depends on how fast blood is lost, not just the hemoglobin level. Losing 2 liters instantly (trauma) causes severe symptoms even if the resulting hemoglobin matches a slow GI bleed that's gone unnoticed for months — the body h…"]},{"s":"Nutritional Deficiency Anemias — Iron, B12 & Folate▾","a":"nutritional-deficiency-anemias-iron-b12-folate","k":["Nutritional Deficiency Anemias — Iron, B12 & Folate▾","Iron Deficiency Anemia — The most common anemia in the US. Microcytic, hypochromic. Highest risk: toddlers, adolescent girls, and women of childbearing age (especially pregnancy and menstruation). Causes: decreased dietary intake, impaired …","Iron Deficiency — Specific Findings — Glossitis (smooth, sore tongue), koilonychia (spoon nails), and pica (craving non-food items — ice chips, hard candy, or in extreme cases dirt or paper). Iron deficiency in childhood can cause cognitive…","B12 Deficiency Anemia — Macrocytic/megaloblastic — pale, large RBCs with impaired DNA synthesis. Most common cause: atrophic gastritis (often autoimmune), which destroys the stomach's parietal cells and stops production of intrinsic factor,…","B12 Deficiency — Specific Findings — Neurologic manifestations on top of the usual anemia symptoms — neuropathy and ataxia (gait/balance problems). Neurologic symptoms are the key differentiator that points specifically to B12 over other de…","Folate (B9) Deficiency Anemia — Also macrocytic/megaloblastic — folate is needed for RBC DNA production just like B12. Most common in pregnancy (needs increase greatly) and in malnourished populations, including alcoholism/liver disease (fo…","Folic acid can correct the anemia of a B12 deficiency without fixing the underlying B12 problem — masking it while the neurologic damage keeps progressing. Whenever folic acid is given, confirm the patient's B12 status is also adequate."]},{"s":"Anemia of Chronic Kidney Disease & Aplastic Anemia▾","a":"anemia-of-chronic-kidney-disease-aplastic-anemia","k":["Anemia of Chronic Kidney Disease & Aplastic Anemia▾","Anemia of Chronic Kidney Disease — The kidneys produce erythropoietin, which signals the bone marrow to make RBCs. Failing kidneys can't release enough erythropoietin, so RBC production drops — a chronic anemia that worsens as kidney functi…","Aplastic Anemia — A rare but dangerous primary bone marrow failure (\"hematopoietic failure\") — the prefix \"a-\" means \"all.\" Most commonly autoimmune (the bone marrow's stem cells are attacked); can also be congenital. Causes pancytopenia — …","Aplastic Anemia — Causes — Idiopathic; high-dose toxic exposures (chemotherapy, radiation, benzene, insecticides); bone marrow cancer; or an autoimmune reaction triggered by an infection like viral hepatitis or mononucleosis.","Aplastic Anemia — Why It's Dangerous — Beyond the usual anemia symptoms, low WBCs mean a high infection risk, and low platelets mean a high bleeding risk — all three problems at once.","Aplastic Anemia — Treatment — Whole blood transfusion (not just RBCs — replaces RBCs, WBCs, and platelets together), bone marrow transplant, immune suppression if autoimmune, or corticosteroids. Drugs that stimulate erythropoiesis are also …"]},{"s":"Hemolytic Anemias & Acute Blood Loss — Sickle Cell, Thalassemia & Hemorrhage▾","a":"hemolytic-anemias-acute-blood-loss-sickle-cell-thalassemia-hemorrhage","k":["Hemolytic Anemias & Acute Blood Loss — Sickle Cell, Thalassemia & Hemorrhage▾","Acquired Hemolytic Anemia — Premature destruction of RBCs by an external agent — an autoimmune attack, a blood type incompatibility, a drug reaction, a hereditary condition present at birth, or a physical agent like a severe burn. Immune co…","Hemolytic Anemia — Findings — Low hemoglobin with an increased reticulocyte count (immature RBCs — the bone marrow is releasing them before they're fully mature to try to keep up). Mild jaundice and hemoglobin in the urine, from the byprodu…","Sickle Cell Disease — An autosomal recessive genetic disorder, most common in the Black/African American population (especially those of Sub-Saharan African descent) — about 7–13%. Abnormal hemoglobin S distorts RBCs into a sickle shape; th…","Sickle Cell — Findings — Usual anemia symptoms plus swelling of the hands and feet (from occluded vessels) and fever, especially during a crisis.","Sickle Cell — Treatment — Oxygen, hydration, and pain management are the priorities, plus infection prevention (infection can trigger a crisis). Hydroxyurea increases the presence of fetal (non-sickle) hemoglobin. Blood transfusions treat t…","Thalassemia — An autosomal recessive genetic condition — patients lack the proteins that build hemoglobin (missing alpha or beta globin), producing microcytic, hypochromic RBCs. Beta thalassemia traces to Greek, Italian, or Jewish ancestry;…","Thalassemia — Findings & Treatment — Delayed growth, fatigue, dyspnea, swollen spleen/liver, bone deformities, and jaundice (from the shortened lifespan of these misshapen cells). Treated with regular blood transfusions, and possibly bone m…","Acute Blood Loss Anemia — From hemorrhage — trauma, or an internal bleed. Manifestations depend on the speed of blood loss: a fast, gross bleed causes severe symptoms quickly (the body has no time to compensate), while a slow, occult bleed …","Sickle cell crisis: triggered by anything that increases oxygen demand or lowers oxygen — dehydration, stress, high altitude, fever, and extreme temperatures. Causes painful vaso-occlusion from ischemia and necrosis in clogged vessels; seve…"]},{"s":"Anemia Pharmacology — Iron, B12 & Folate Replacement▾","a":"anemia-pharmacology-iron-b12-folate-replacement","k":["Anemia Pharmacology — Iron, B12 & Folate Replacement▾","Ferrous Sulfate (PO) — Oral iron supplement — drug matrix medication. Treats/prevents iron deficiency anemia. Only about 20% of ferrous sulfate is elemental iron by weight, so the dose looks larger than the actual iron delivered. Best absor…","Ferrous Sulfate — Teaching — Take with vitamin C (helps absorption) — never with antacids or calcium (decreases absorption). Expect dark green/black stool — this is a normal, expected finding, not a sign of GI bleeding. Liquid forms can sta…","Iron Dextran (IV/IM) — IV/parenteral iron supplement — drug matrix medication, for patients with more severe deficiency or who can't tolerate oral iron. Given IM using the Z-track method (can stain skin). Not given at home.","Deferoxamine — The chelating agent used to treat iron toxicity/overdose — binds iron into an insoluble complex that's excreted in the stool.","Cyanocobalamin (B12) — B12 replacement. Given PO, subQ, IM, or intranasal — never IV. High-dose oral therapy is now known to work as well as injections for most patients, though injections are still used, especially when a stomach-absorptio…","Folic Acid — Folate replacement — prescription strength 1–5 mg, or at least 400 mcg/day over the counter. Well tolerated, with essentially no significant side effects. Routinely given in pregnancy via prenatal vitamins.","Iron dextran: the dextran component carries a risk of fatal anaphylactic reaction. Always give a test dose first and have epinephrine available before the first dose."]},{"s":"Anemia Pharmacology — Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents (ESAs)▾","a":"anemia-pharmacology-erythropoiesis-stimulating-agents-esas","k":["Anemia Pharmacology — Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents (ESAs)▾","Epoetin Alfa (Epogen) — An erythropoiesis-stimulating agent (ESA) — stimulates the bone marrow to produce RBCs, raising hemoglobin and reticulocyte count. Used primarily for anemia of chronic kidney disease. Given IV or subQ. Requires adequ…","Darbepoetin Alfa — A longer-acting ESA, also given IV.","ESA Precautions — Contraindicated in uncontrolled hypertension. Do not give if it has been shaken or frozen; protect from light; do not dilute or mix with other medications.","ESA black-box warning: never give if hemoglobin is greater than 10 g/dL — overcorrection risks stroke, heart attack, increased cancer progression, injection-site problems, and a polycythemic, hypercoagulable state. Always check the hemoglob…","How an overshot hemoglobin from ESA therapy leads to a true polycythemic state is covered in full on the Polycythemia page"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-polycythemia.html","t":"Polycythemia","secs":[{"s":"Polycythemia","a":"","k":["Week 2","Polycythemia Basics — Relative vs. Absolute","Relative Polycythemia","Primary Polycythemia (Polycythemia Vera)","Secondary Polycythemia","Complications Shared by Primary & Secondary Polycythemia","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Polycythemia Basics — Relative vs. Absolute▾","a":"polycythemia-basics-relative-vs-absolute","k":["Polycythemia Basics — Relative vs. Absolute▾","\"Poly-\" means many — polycythemia means having too many red blood cells. There are two big buckets: relative polycythemia (a false elevation) and absolute polycythemia, which splits into primary and secondary.","Relative — A false elevation from hemoconcentration — plasma volume is low (usually severe dehydration), so RBCs look falsely elevated on a percentage basis. There isn't actually an excess of red blood cells.","Primary (Absolute) — Polycythemia vera — a true bone marrow stem cell disorder causing overproduction of red blood cells.","Secondary (Absolute) — A true increase in red blood cells as a compensatory response to chronic tissue hypoxia.","Risk Factors for Absolute Polycythemia","Chronic hypoxia — most commonly COPD (chronic bronchitis or emphysema).","Living at high altitude — can progress to chronic mountain sickness.","Long-term cigarette smoking.","Familial/genetic predisposition.","Long-term carbon monoxide exposure — tunnel workers, coal miners, other underground workers, garage attendants, and people in heavily polluted urban areas."]},{"s":"Relative Polycythemia▾","a":"relative-polycythemia","k":["Relative Polycythemia▾","Cause — An isolated decrease in plasma volume, most often from severe dehydration.","Effect — A false elevation of hemoglobin, hematocrit, and RBC percentage — the numbers look high only because the plasma they're measured against is low.","Treatment — Correct the underlying cause of the hemoconcentration (e.g., rehydration). Once the fluid deficit is corrected, the polycythemia resolves — there's nothing else to treat."]},{"s":"Primary Polycythemia (Polycythemia Vera)▾","a":"primary-polycythemia-polycythemia-vera","k":["Primary Polycythemia (Polycythemia Vera)▾","Definition — A rare, slow-growing blood cancer/stem cell disorder — a single mutated stem cell overproduces red blood cells, and often white blood cells and platelets too (everything except lymphocytes), since it affects the bone marrow bro…","Epidemiology — Typically occurs in people over 60; about twice as common in men.","Pathophysiology — A chronic myeloproliferative disorder — the excess red blood cells thicken the blood.","Polycythemia Vera — Manifestations","Headache, fatigue, weight loss, dyspnea.","Hypertension and clotting problems — the excess red blood cells collide and clump in the vessels.","A ruddy (plethoric) color.","Intense, painful itching (aquagenic pruritus), especially worse with heat or water — patients are particularly bothered by warm water."]},{"s":"Secondary Polycythemia▾","a":"secondary-polycythemia","k":["Secondary Polycythemia▾","Definition — An adaptive, compensatory response to chronic tissue hypoxia — not a bone marrow disorder itself.","Mechanism — The body senses it's hypoxic; the kidneys secrete erythropoietin, and the bone marrow responds by making more red blood cells to try to carry more oxygen.","Most Common Causes — COPD and restrictive lung disease (poor gas exchange despite adequate red blood cells) and high altitude.","COPD pathophysiology (chronic bronchitis and emphysema) is covered in full on the Obstructive Airway Disorders page","Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (like epoetin alfa) can also cause an iatrogenic polycythemic state if hemoglobin is overcorrected — full drug detail on the Anemia page"]},{"s":"Complications Shared by Primary & Secondary Polycythemia▾","a":"complications-shared-by-primary-secondary-polycythemia","k":["Complications Shared by Primary & Secondary Polycythemia▾","Increased Blood Viscosity & Volume — Hypertension, headache, and inability to concentrate. Cyanosis of the lips, nails, and mucous membranes can occur but is less common than the other findings.","Decreased Blood Flow — Excess red blood cells collide and clump, slowing flow — like trying to push too much water through a straw. Raises the risk of DVT (deep vein thrombosis), angina, cerebral insufficiency, and TIAs (transient ischemic …","Hypermetabolism — Night sweats and weight loss.","Other — Itching and pain in the fingers and toes.","The biggest concern with any absolute polycythemia (primary or secondary): stroke and heart attack from clotting. Too many red blood cells collide and clump in the vessels, blocking blood flow to the brain and heart."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 2","Four Clusters, Not Six Separate Topics","The Six Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new — it's a map of Week 2's six topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. Three central concepts (hypoxemia from reduced oxygen delivery or p…"]},{"s":"Four Clusters, Not Six Separate Topics▾","a":"four-clusters-not-six-separate-topics","k":["Four Clusters, Not Six Separate Topics▾","Six pages is a lot to hold in your head at once. All of them fall into one of four groups — knowing the group can matter more than memorizing all six titles.","Red Blood Cell Problems — Anemia; Polycythemia — Oxygen-carrying capacity gone wrong — too few RBCs or hemoglobin (anemia) or too many (polycythemia). Both cause tissue hypoxemia or hypercoagulability from different directions.","Airway Obstruction — Obstructive Airway Disorders (Asthma, COPD, Emphysema, Chronic Bronchitis) — Airways are narrowed; air gets trapped on expiration; V/Q mismatch causes hypoxemia and hypercapnia. Inflammation and mucus production drive a…","Infection in the Lungs — Pneumonia; Tuberculosis; URI — An infectious agent invades the lower or upper respiratory tract. All three cause inflammation, fluid accumulation, V/Q mismatch, and hypoxemia. TB is unique because it's chronic and r…","The Bridge Between Blood and Lungs — How anemia affects oxygen delivery to tissues; how lung disease affects oxygen loading onto RBCs — RBC problems (anemia) mean fewer oxygen-carrying vehicles; lung problems mean oxygen can't get INTO thos…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, working through the clusters in this order touches every recurring theme once before moving on, instead of jumping between unrelated systems."]},{"s":"The Six Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-six-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Six Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Anemia — Reduced RBC number or hemoglobin decreases oxygen-carrying capacity; types include iron/B12/folate deficiency, aplastic anemia, hemolytic anemia, and sickle cell disease.","2 — Polycythemia — Too many RBCs (opposite of anemia) causes blood thickening, increased viscosity, and thrombotic risk — either primary (polycythemia vera, a bone marrow cancer) or secondary (compensatory response to chronic hypoxemia).","3 — Obstructive Airway Disorders — Asthma, chronic bronchitis, and emphysema all narrow airways and trap air on expiration; inflammation and V/Q mismatch drive hypoxemia and hypercapnia.","4 — Pneumonia — Bacterial, viral, or fungal infection in the alveoli causes fluid accumulation, inflammation, V/Q mismatch, and hypoxemia; severity depends on the organism and the patient's immune status.","5 — Tuberculosis — A chronic mycobacterial infection (Mycobacterium tuberculosis) that primarily affects the lungs; requires months of multi-drug therapy to prevent drug resistance.","6 — URI (Upper Respiratory Infection) — Viral infection of the nose, sinuses, and throat; mostly self-limited, but can lead to complications like sinusitis, otitis media, or lower respiratory tract extension."]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content — each idea is fully taught in exactly one place. But they resurface as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why.","Hypoxemia as the common endpoint — Anemia — Every other topic — obstructive airway disorders cause hypoxemia from V/Q mismatch, pneumonia from fluid-filled alveoli, TB from tissue destruction, URI when it extends to lower airways, and polyc…","Pallor as a clinical sign — Anemia — Severe pneumonia (from shock/hypoperfusion), TB (from chronic malnutrition), URI in a child (general illness appearance).","Fatigue and weakness as symptoms — Anemia — Chronic lung disease (COPD from years of hypoxemia), TB (from months of infection), polycythemia (from blood viscosity and hyperviscosity syndrome).","V/Q (ventilation-perfusion) mismatch — Obstructive Airway Disorders — Pneumonia (infected/fluid-filled alveoli can't be ventilated), TB (destroyed tissue creates dead space), URI when it involves lower airway obstruction.","Inflammation as the driver of symptoms — All three infection topics (Pneumonia, TB, URI) — Also appears in obstructive airway disorders (inflammation drives bronchoconstriction in asthma), anemia (hemolytic anemia involves immune inflammati…","Infection as a trigger for acute decompensation — Obstructive Airway Disorders — In anemia (infection triggers aplastic crisis), in polycythemia (infection increases thrombotic risk), in TB (secondary infection during treatment complicates …","ABG (arterial blood gas) abnormalities — Obstructive Airway Disorders — Pneumonia (typically hypoxemia, normal or low CO2 early, then rising CO2 if severe), TB (hypoxemia from tissue damage), URI with lower airway involvement (respiratory a…","Chest X-ray findings and interpretation — All lung topics (Obstructive Airway Disorders, Pneumonia, TB, URI) — Also relevant in anemia (may show findings of sickle cell crisis or chronic disease) and polycythemia (plethoric appearance, incr…","Antibiotic selection and resistance — Pneumonia — TB (multi-drug resistant TB is a growing problem; therapy requires specific combinations), URI (mostly viral, so antibiotics don't help but are often prescribed anyway).","Vaccination as prevention — Pneumonia — TB (BCG vaccine in endemic areas), URI (influenza vaccine reduces severity)."]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","Every danger callout across the six pages, condensed to a phrase. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation — click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","Anemia — Sickle cell crisis is triggered by anything that raises oxygen demand or lowers oxygen — dehydration, stress, high altitude, fever, extreme temperatures. Vaso-occlusion causes severe pain and can be life-threatening. Recognize and …","Anemia — Iron dextran carries a fatal anaphylaxis risk from the dextran component — always give a test dose first with epinephrine immediately available. Never give without a test dose.","Anemia — ESAs (epoetin alfa, darbepoetin) have a BLACK-BOX WARNING: never give if hemoglobin is GREATER than 10 g/dL — risk of stroke, MI, thromboembolism, and polycythemic state from overcorrection.","Polycythemia — Polycythemia vera patients are at high thrombotic risk from thick, viscous blood — monitor closely for stroke, MI, DVT/PE. Phlebotomy and anticoagulation are standard management.","Obstructive Airway Disorders — Status asthmaticus is a life-threatening emergency — a SILENT CHEST (no air movement) with CO2 >70 signals imminent respiratory failure. Prepare for intubation and IV epinephrine.","Obstructive Airway Disorders — In COPD patients, high-flow oxygen can suppress the respiratory drive — use lowest effective FiO2 and monitor closely. CO2 retention is the risk; titrate carefully.","Pneumonia — Community-acquired pneumonia can progress rapidly to sepsis — recognize early signs (fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension) and start antibiotics and fluid resuscitation immediately.","Pneumonia — Aspiration pneumonia risk in patients with altered mental status, dysphagia, or recent anesthesia — NPO status, head-of-bed elevation, and careful feeding tube placement can prevent it.","Tuberculosis — TB requires LONG-TERM multi-drug therapy to prevent resistance — missed doses or early stopping allow resistant strains to emerge. Adherence is critical; directly observed therapy (DOT) is often used.","Tuberculosis — TB is reportable and requires contact investigation — close contacts need testing and possibly prophylactic therapy even if asymptomatic.","URI — Most URIs are viral and don't respond to antibiotics — inappropriate antibiotic use drives resistance. Supportive care is standard; antibiotics only for suspected bacterial superinfection (sinusitis, otitis media with specific finding…","Patterns you'll notice across these rules","\"Hypoxemia is the common endpoint — recognize it early.\" Whether from anemia (reduced O2 carriers), lung disease (poor gas exchange), or infection (inflammation/fluid), tissue hypoxemia is the threat. ABG/VBG and SpO2 monitoring matter acro…","\"Inflammation drives symptoms and complications.\" Asthma (bronchoconstriction), pneumonia (fluid), TB (tissue destruction), URI (nasal congestion), anemia (hemolysis), and polycythemia (viscosity) — inflammation is a thread connecting all s…","\"Treatment can be as dangerous as the disease.\" High-flow oxygen suppresses respirations in COPD; ESAs overcorrect anemia to stroke risk; TB drugs have interactions; iron dextran causes anaphylaxis. Know the risk as well as the benefit.","\"Adherence and follow-through save lives.\" TB therapy requires months; COPD patients need home oxygen and monitoring; anemia needs repeat labs to guide treatment adjustments. Short-term thinking (stopping TB drugs early, skipping doses) has…","\"The 'silent' finding is often the most dangerous.\" A silent chest in asthma signals respiratory failure; silent hypoxemia in polycythemia precedes thrombosis; TB can be silent (latent) for years before reactivating."]}]},{"f":"week3-renal-disorders.html","t":"Renal Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Renal Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 2","Pyelonephritis (Upper UTI / Kidney Infection)","Urinary Obstruction","Nephrolithiasis (Kidney Stones)","Urologic Cancers","Glomerular Disorders","Self-Test Flashcards","This page covers five renal conditions in the order the lecture teaches them: pyelonephritis, urinary obstruction, nephrolithiasis (kidney stones), urologic cancers, and the glomerular disorders (glomerulonephritis, nephrotic syndrome, and …"]},{"s":"Pyelonephritis (Upper UTI / Kidney Infection)▾","a":"pyelonephritis-upper-uti-kidney-infection","k":["Pyelonephritis (Upper UTI / Kidney Infection)▾","Definition — Inflammation of one or both kidneys, focused on the upper urinary tract. Usually ascending — a lower UTI climbs up the urinary tract — but can also come from a systemic bloodstream infection reaching the kidney.","Pathophysiology — E. coli is the most common pathogen. The kidney becomes swollen and fills with infectious exudate; if severe, this can compress the renal artery. The kidney can become necrotic or form abscesses.","Acute vs. Chronic — Usually acute. Untreated or recurrent pyelonephritis can become chronic — a cause of chronic kidney disease and a risk for sepsis down the road.","Risk Factors — Pregnancy, recurrent lower UTIs, and an antibiotic-resistant strain (a lower UTI treated with an antibiotic it's resistant to stays untreated and can ascend).","Onset — Gradual — \"do I have a UTI, do I not.\" — Sudden — wakes up or develops within an afternoon.","How They Look — Otherwise well. — Look and feel systemically sick — this is a systemic infection.","Key Findings — Dysuria, frequency, urgency. — Fever, chills, CVA (costovertebral angle) tenderness over the back — plus the same frequency/urgency/dysuria, since the lower tract is usually still involved. Hematuria and nausea/vomiting/anore…","Treatment","Community-acquired, low resistance risk: Bactrim (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) first-line, or ciprofloxacin. Nitrofurantoin (Macrobid) is used clinically but isn't on the drug matrix.","Uncomplicated community cases get a short course; recurrent UTIs get a more conventional 7–14 day course.","More severe cases may need inpatient IV antibiotics. A urinalysis and culture identify the organism and its sensitivity — patients typically start on a broad-spectrum antibiotic and get narrowed once results are back.","Pregnancy complicates treatment — Bactrim and ciprofloxacin are avoided, which narrows the antibiotic options right when pregnant patients are already at higher risk for pyelonephritis.","Urosepsis: in elderly or already-sick patients, a urinary tract infection (upper or lower) can progress to sepsis — a severe systemic response with a high mortality rate."]},{"s":"Urinary Obstruction▾","a":"urinary-obstruction","k":["Urinary Obstruction▾","A blockage can occur anywhere along the urinary tract — the renal pelvis, the ureter, or the bladder/urethra — and whatever is downstream of the blockage backs up, the same way a clogged pipe backs up.","Renal Pelvis — Kidney stones (renal calculi) — most common cause at this level.","Ureter — Kidney stones again, but also pregnancy (an enlarging fetus can compress the ureter) or tumors.","Bladder / Urethra — Stones rarely reach this far — more likely bladder cancer, a prostate problem, a stricture, or a neurogenic bladder (not a true obstruction, but related to a spinal or neuromuscular injury).","Complication 1 — Infection","When urine can't flow freely, infection is much more likely — this is why recurrent UTIs or recurrent pyelonephritis are a common presenting picture for an underlying obstruction.","An enlarged prostate is a classic example: urine can't fully empty, leaving residual urine that raises infection risk.","Complication 2 — Back-Pressure","The kidneys keep making urine even when it can't get out. This raises pressure upstream of the blockage, causing hydroureter or hydronephrosis (the kidney or ureter distending with retained urine) — and can progress to a postrenal acute kid…","This is typically treated as an emergency depending on the patient's urine output and kidney function, and usually needs a surgical fix for the blockage itself.","TRICK: Manifestations of an obstruction come down to three questions: where is the blockage (the renal pelvis has room, so less pain; the ureter is narrow, so more pain), how fast did it happen (fast onset is far less tolerable than a slow-…"]},{"s":"Nephrolithiasis (Kidney Stones)▾","a":"nephrolithiasis-kidney-stones","k":["Nephrolithiasis (Kidney Stones)▾","What's Happening","Kidney stones (renal calculi) are the most common cause of urinary obstruction — crumbs or clumps of crystals ranging from tiny to as large as a golf ball, smooth or jagged depending on composition.","Urine is a solution of water (solvent) and particles (solutes). When a particular solute becomes supersaturated, crystals begin forming — always at the level of the kidney/nephron, never in the bladder or ureter.","Crystal formation is enhanced by pH changes (seen with frequent UTIs), excess salt concentration in the urine, dehydration, bone disease, gout, renal disease, and urinary stasis (a very immobile, sedentary lifestyle — not just a desk job).","Male sex (women's risk is catching up, but men are still slightly higher)","Younger age — this condition actually skews toward 20s–30s","Caucasian race","Family history (a parent or grandparent with kidney stones)","Congenital defects or strictures anywhere in the urinary system","Hot weather — more dehydration, more concentrated (higher-solute) urine","Obesity and immobility/sedentary lifestyle","Calcium (Oxalate or Phosphate) — By far the most common — 70 to 80% of all stones. Mostly idiopathic, though family history matters; diet (high sodium, high oxalate, high protein) and high blood/urine calcium or oxalate levels can contribut…","Struvite (\"Staghorn\") — Related to urinary tract infections.","Uric Acid — Related to gout — a patient with gout who develops a kidney stone most likely has a uric acid stone.","Cystine — Rare, less than 1% of stones — not something you need to know in depth.","Renal Colic — What These Patients Look Like","Flank pain that radiates to the lower abdomen or groin — spasmodic, coming in waves of about 20 to 60 minutes as the ureter tries to move the stone along.","Sharp pain, because the stone is scraping the walls of the ureter.","Nausea and vomiting are common because of the pain; difficulty urinating can occur.","Fever only occurs if an infection is also present (from urine backup) — a fever alongside a kidney stone points toward pyelonephritis as well.","Hematuria, pyuria, and diaphoresis can also be seen.","Pain Control — An NSAID (IV Toradol/ketorolac) for milder pain; most patients need an opioid, usually morphine, for acute pain.","Hydration — IV fluids, especially if the patient can't keep fluids down from the pain — dilutes the urine to keep crystals from reforming and helps move the stone through.","Prevention (Recurrent Stones) — Calcium stones: thiazide diuretics. Struvite stones: treat the underlying infection with antibiotics plus fluids. Uric acid stones: treat the gout, usually with allopurinol."]},{"s":"Urologic Cancers▾","a":"urologic-cancers","k":["Urologic Cancers▾","Kidney Cancer","85% of kidney cancer is renal cell carcinoma — a rare cancer overall (kidney cancers are under 5% of all cancers).","#1 risk factor is smoking, by far; also obesity, older age, male sex, and genetic/family history.","No early manifestations — this is why it usually isn't diagnosed until it has metastasized (sometimes found incidentally on an unrelated abdominal CT). Once metastasized, five-year survival is only about 10%.","Late manifestations: CVA tenderness, cola-colored hematuria, a palpable abdominal mass. Metastasizes to bone or lung — dyspnea, cough, and bone pain are often how it's finally caught.","Resistant to chemo. If it hasn't metastasized, the kidney is simply removed (the other kidney compensates); if it has, chemo and radiation are needed.","Bladder Cancer","The 4th most common cancer in men — typically urothelial carcinoma (cancer of the bladder lining).","#1 risk factor is smoking again; also male sex, occupational toxin exposure (rubber factories, paint), and low fluid intake (concentrates toxins against the bladder lining for longer).","Early: hematuria — urine may look pink or reddish, or the blood may only be visible on a UA. Later: UTI-like symptoms (frequency, urgency, dysuria).","Stage 1 is treated with intravesical (inside-the-bladder) chemotherapy; advanced disease needs systemic chemo.","What It Is — A live vaccine instilled directly into the bladder (via an in-and-out catheter) for early-stage bladder cancer. It stimulates an inflammatory response so the patient's own immune system learns to recognize and attack cancer cel…","Administration — Given weekly for six to twelve weeks. Held in the bladder for 2 hours; the patient changes position every 15 minutes so the medication contacts all portions of the bladder.","Adverse Effects — Bladder irritation and UTI-type symptoms are common, even after treatment ends.","Nursing Care — Disinfect (bleach) the patient's urine for 6 hours after treatment to kill the live bacteria before disposal.","BCG is a live vaccine: it must not be given to a patient with a compromised immune system — someone with HIV/AIDS or otherwise immunosuppressed can develop a systemic infection from the weakened live bacteria."]},{"s":"Glomerular Disorders▾","a":"glomerular-disorders","k":["Glomerular Disorders▾","Where the Damage Occurs","Damage happens within the glomerulus, a delicate network of capillaries inside Bowman's capsule. An afferent arteriole brings blood in and an efferent arteriole takes it out; the pressure difference between them pushes toxins, waste, and ch…","The glomerular capillary membrane has three layers: the endothelium, the glomerular basement membrane (GBM — the layer where most of the problems below actually occur), and the podocyte layer (specialized epithelial cells that start urine p…","The kidneys use about 20% of cardiac output relative to only about 0.5% of body mass — this is why damage at the glomerular level causes such widespread illness.","Glomerulonephritis","Definition — Inflammation within the glomeruli from any cause — infection, post-infection, or a systemic inflammatory condition. Can be focal (one area) or diffuse (both kidneys, all areas). The third leading cause of kidney failure in the …","Immune Mechanism — Primarily immune-mediated, through two hypersensitivity patterns: antibodies attacking the GBM directly (about 5% of cases — anti-GBM antibodies), or the more common pattern of circulating antibody-antigen complexes depos…","Common Triggers — Post-strep infection is the most common trigger (bacterial, viral, and parasitic infections can also trigger it); IgA buildup in the kidney (Berger's disease); and systemic autoimmune diseases — lupus, Goodpasture syndrome…","The Type II vs. Type III hypersensitivity reactions behind glomerulonephritis are covered in full on GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity","Goodpasture Syndrome — A Special Case","The glomerular basement membrane is structurally very similar to the basement membrane of the lungs. In Goodpasture syndrome, anti-GBM antibodies attack both — patients often present with hemoptysis (coughing up blood) alongside renal probl…","HARP: acute glomerulonephritis is characterized by Hematuria, Azotemia (waste product buildup), Retention of sodium and water (generalized edema, increased blood pressure), and Proteinuria (protein spilling into the urine as the damaged GBM…","Nephrotic Syndrome","Definition — The glomerulus becomes too permeable to plasma proteins — measured as more than 3 g of protein lost in a 24-hour urine collection. A common cause is glomerulonephritis, but uncontrolled diabetes damaging the GBM can also cause …","Why the Protein Loss Matters — Albumin is normally too large to pass the GBM and provides oncotic (pulling) pressure to hold fluid inside the vessels. Once it spills into the urine (proteinuria) and drops in the blood, fluid shifts out of t…","Hypercoagulability — Antithrombin III and plasminogen (a precursor to plasmin, which breaks down clots) are also lost in the urine — without them, the blood becomes hypercoagulable, raising the risk of DVT and PE.","Hyperlipidemia — The liver responds to the ongoing protein loss by increasing lipid production, so these patients also develop elevated lipid levels.","Hypertension — The kidneys sense reduced circulating volume (since fluid has third-spaced out of the vessels) and activate the renin-angiotensin system to compensate, raising blood pressure.","Glomerulopathy (Diabetic & Hypertensive)","Definition — Damage specific to the glomerulus from chronic, uncontrolled diabetes or hypertension — distinct from the inflammatory process of glomerulonephritis.","Diabetic Mechanism — Chronically elevated glucose thickens the GBM over time, so it filters particles and fluid less effectively.","Hypertensive Mechanism — Chronic high pressure on the blood vessels causes sclerosis (scarring) of the glomerulus, decreasing perfusion within the kidney.","Presentation — Looks like acute glomerulonephritis — hematuria, oliguria (less than 400 mL/day), fluid retention, hypertension that's harder to control, a BUN:creatinine ratio above 20:1, proteinuria, and low blood protein.","The insulin-deficiency cascade and diabetes basics behind diabetic glomerulopathy are covered in full on Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome","Hypertension classification and long-term complications are covered in full on Hypertension & Hyperlipidemia","A well-known real-world example: singer Selena Gomez has lupus, and it was lupus attacking her kidneys — causing glomerulonephritis — that led to her kidney transplant."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-aki-ckd.html","t":"Acute Kidney Injury & Chronic Kidney Disease","secs":[{"s":"Acute Kidney Injury & Chronic Kidney Disease","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 3","The Kidneys' Key Jobs","GFR — The Indicator of Kidney Function","Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)","Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)","Pharmacotherapy for CKD","Self-Test Flashcards","Pyelonephritis, glomerulonephritis, and the other conditions on Renal Disorders are some of the underlying causes referenced throughout this page"]},{"s":"The Kidneys' Key Jobs▾","a":"the-kidneys-key-jobs","k":["The Kidneys' Key Jobs▾","Why Kidney Injury Affects So Much of the Body","Maintain fluid and electrolyte homeostasis — this underlies blood pressure regulation and third-spacing.","Rid the body of water-soluble waste via urine (fat-soluble waste leaves via stool instead) — including many water-soluble drugs.","Produce erythropoietin, which stimulates red blood cell production in the bone marrow.","Activate vitamin D, necessary for calcium absorption and bone health.","Produce renin, the first step of the renin-angiotensin system that helps regulate blood pressure.","Renal blood flow is about 1 liter per minute — roughly 20% of cardiac output — carried by an organ that's only about 0.5% of body mass. The kidneys are extremely dependent on perfusion, which is exactly why they're so vulnerable to sudden i…","TRICK: Every manifestation on this page traces back to one of these five jobs failing. Can't manage fluid/lytes → edema and electrolyte imbalances. Can't rid the body of waste → azotemia/uremia. Can't make erythropoietin → anemia. Can't act…"]},{"s":"GFR — The Indicator of Kidney Function▾","a":"gfr-the-indicator-of-kidney-function","k":["GFR — The Indicator of Kidney Function▾","Greater than 90 — Normal.","About 25% of normal (~25–30 mL/hr) — Renal insufficiency — the body can compensate for a while, but the longer this state continues, the more likely it progresses to a chronic disease state.","You don't need to memorize every GFR cutoff, but do know that greater than 90 is normal and roughly 25% of that marks insufficiency — GFR is the shared indicator used for both AKI and CKD staging below."]},{"s":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)▾","a":"acute-kidney-injury-aki","k":["Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)▾","What's Happening","Usually the result of an ischemic injury from volume loss — the intravascular space loses volume and the kidneys stop getting the perfusion they need (remember: 1 liter/minute through an organ that's 0.5% of body mass).","Common causes: acute blood loss, third-spacing (as in sepsis), or toxin/drug exposure (including an overdose state).","The body's inflammatory response to the injury is meant to help, but often makes things worse — it increases vascular changes and kidney cell death.","AKI can happen within hours and is potentially reversible — the kidneys can heal over time depending on how severe and how prolonged the injury was.","Prerenal (most common) — Before blood reaches the kidney — a volume problem. — Blood loss during surgery, dehydration.","Intrarenal — Direct injury to the kidney itself — acute tubular necrosis (ATN). — Nephrotoxic drugs/chemicals (e.g., an overdose), kidney disease such as acute glomerulonephritis, or vascular disease (diabetes, hypertension).","Postrenal (least common) — An obstruction downstream of the kidney. — A blockage such as a tumor (see the urinary obstruction section on the Renal Disorders page).","TRICK: Think of it like a traffic jam relative to the kidney. Prerenal = the block is before the kidney (not enough blood arrives). Intrarenal = the damage is inside the kidney itself. Postrenal = the block is after the kidney (urine can't …","Oliguria — less than 400 mL in 24 hours (roughly less than 30 mL/hour). Onset typically begins one day after a hypotensive event (surgery, blood loss, dehydration) and lasts one to three weeks.","Fluid volume excess, since filtration isn't happening properly.","Metabolic acidosis — the kidneys are heavily involved in acid-base balance.","Hyponatremia or hyperkalemia — electrolyte management is impaired.","Azotemia/uremia — waste products accumulate in the blood instead of being filtered out.","Neurologic changes, especially once severe.","Treatment Principles","Address the cause: replace volume for a prerenal problem; look for an antidote or flush the system for an intrarenal toxin/overdose problem.","Treat the manifestations as they appear — correct electrolyte imbalances, manage fluid shifts, and avoid any additional nephrotoxic drugs or medications.","Monitor labs closely and watch for progression toward chronic kidney disease."]},{"s":"Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)▾","a":"chronic-kidney-disease-ckd","k":["Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)▾","Staging & Progression","CKD can be caused by AKI, but more often develops from ongoing chronic conditions. Staging is based on GFR, the same indicator used for AKI. Stages 1–2 are usually asymptomatic; hypertension appears with moderate GFR decline; the rest of th…","Stage 5 is considered uremic — uremia is the presence of urea (an excess amino acid/protein metabolism end-product) in the blood, essentially meaning the body can no longer make and excrete its own urine.","There is no drug that reverses CKD. Management for mild-to-moderate stages focuses on cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk reduction — controlling whatever chronic condition is driving it (diabetes, hypertension, or an unresolved AKI).","Diabetes — ~50%","Hypertension — ~30%","Other (glomerulonephritis, unresolved AKI, etc.) — ~20%","The best treatment for CKD is prevention — controlling the diabetes and hypertension that cause the large majority of cases. Other risk factors: a family history of CKD, other vascular disease (CAD, hyperlipidemia, atherosclerosis), age ove…","The Cyclical Pathogenesis","Whatever reduces blood flow to kidney cells starts a self-worsening cycle. Two findings are the biggest indicators and drivers of worsening CKD: proteinuria (a leaky, damaged GBM lets protein through, and that protein accumulating in the in…","This is why blood pressure control and monitoring the urine for protein are such consistent priorities in CKD care.","Manifestations by Body System","Fluid & Electrolyte — Edema, third-spacing, hyperkalemia (a major concern in both CKD and AKI), hyperphosphatemia, hypermagnesemia, metabolic acidosis.","Neurologic — Fatigue, headaches, sleep disturbances, and — in severe cases — encephalopathy and mental status changes (azotemia crossing the blood-brain barrier). Anxiety and depression are also common, though less emphasized.","Cardiovascular — Hypertension (from angiotensin II), which can progress to heart failure and coronary artery disease; also pericarditis and peripheral artery disease.","Pulmonary — Pulmonary edema from third-spacing — an ominous sign that often marks the point dialysis needs to start. This is the biggest immediate safety concern (fluid overload in the lungs).","GI — Anorexia, nausea/vomiting, and it can contribute to gastritis or GI bleeding.","Integumentary — Itching, redness, and dry, scaly skin — toxins building up in the skin can even produce a coating called uremic frost.","Hematologic — Chronic anemia from lost erythropoietin production — hemoglobin can fall as low as 5–6 g/dL, but because the decline is so slow, patients often have fewer symptoms than someone who lost the same amount of blood acutely.","Musculoskeletal — Renal osteodystrophy — weakened, more fracture-prone bones from the loss of activated vitamin D (think of hip fractures in older CKD patients)."]},{"s":"Pharmacotherapy for CKD▾","a":"pharmacotherapy-for-ckd","k":["Pharmacotherapy for CKD▾","There is no drug that fixes the kidneys or reverses CKD — treatment slows progression and manages complications. Blood pressure goal: systolic under 140, diastolic under 90 (not lowered too aggressively, since CKD kidneys are used to a high…","Volume Overload — A diuretic (usually furosemide) plus a lower-sodium diet (around 2 g/day).","Hyperkalemia — A diuretic to lower potassium; other agents such as Kayexalate; dialysis at more severe stages also corrects this.","Metabolic Acidosis — Sodium bicarbonate — an alkalinizing agent.","Hyperphosphatemia / Renal Osteodystrophy — Calcium carbonate (binds phosphate — calcium and phosphate move inversely, like a teeter-totter) and calcitriol (activated vitamin D, since the kidney can no longer activate it itself).","Anemia — Erythropoietin (know its black-box warning — see the danger callout below).","Sodium Bicarbonate — Oral, alkalinizing agent — lowers pH and raises bicarb levels. Goal bicarb 18–20; started when bicarb is under 15. Slows CKD progression, helps prevent bone loss, improves nutritional status. Can cause bloating; otherwi…","Calcium Carbonate (Tums) — Not a true phosphate binder, but binds phosphate to lower it and reduce mortality. Take with meals. Can cause hypercalcemia — monitor both calcium and phosphate levels.","Calcitriol — An activated form of vitamin D (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D) — stimulates intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate to treat renal osteodystrophy. Adverse effects: hypercalcemia and hyperphosphatemia (watch for nausea and upse…","Erythropoietin black-box warning: know it and use it appropriately — overcorrecting hemoglobin raises the risk of stroke, MI, and thromboembolism (see the cross-reference below).","The full erythropoiesis-stimulating agent black-box warning (never give if hemoglobin is greater than 10 g/dL) is covered on Anemia","Renal Dosing — Why It Matters","The kidneys are the primary filter for most medications. When kidney function drops significantly, drug elimination slows and doses/frequency often need adjusting downward — this is called renal dosing.","Common examples: digoxin, diabetic medications (glyburide, metformin), vancomycin (its own dedicated renal dosing protocol), and opioids.","Opioids in severe CKD: a patient with severe, poorly-filtering CKD will retain opioids far longer than normal, risking significant respiratory depression. This is the classic \"most concerning medication\" answer for a CKD patient in pain."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-male-reproductive-disorders.html","t":"Male Reproductive Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Male Reproductive Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 4","Testicular Cancer","Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)","Prostate Cancer","Erectile Dysfunction (ED)","Quick Comparison — The Four Conditions","Self-Test Flashcards","This page covers four male reproductive conditions in the order the lecture teaches them: testicular cancer, benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), prostate cancer, and erectile dysfunction. The two prostate conditions are presented back to ba…"]},{"s":"Testicular Cancer▾","a":"testicular-cancer","k":["Testicular Cancer▾","How Common — Rare overall — about 1% of all cancers in men. But it is the most common solid tumor in young men, typically diagnosed between ages 15 and 34.","Biggest Risk Factor — Cryptorchidism (an undescended testicle) — even if surgically corrected in childhood, the risk stays elevated for life.","Tumor Origin — The vast majority arise from germ cells (the cells that would normally become sperm) inside the testicle.","Presentation — A painless mass or lump in the testicle is the classic finding — patients often find it themselves. Because it's painless, it's easy to ignore or delay reporting.","Prognosis — Survival rates are very high, even in later-stage disease, because testicular cancer responds well to treatment — this is a reassurance point the lecture makes explicitly.","Seminoma","A germ cell tumor type.","Grows more slowly.","Generally more radiosensitive and has a somewhat more favorable prognosis than nonseminoma.","Nonseminoma","The other germ cell tumor type.","Tends to grow and spread more quickly than seminoma.","Still highly treatable — the overall high-survival point still applies.","Trick: think \"seminoma is the mellow one\" — seminoma grows slower and stays more contained, while nonseminoma is the faster, more aggressive of the two germ cell types."]},{"s":"Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)▾","a":"benign-prostatic-hyperplasia-bph","k":["Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)▾","Prostate's Job — A gland that sits below the bladder and surrounds the urethra; it contributes fluid to semen.","What BPH Is — A non-malignant (benign) overgrowth of the prostate's epithelial and smooth muscle tissue. It is not cancer and does not turn into cancer.","Proposed Mechanisms — Two theories the lecture presents: a hormone imbalance (shifting androgen/estrogen ratio with age) and accumulation of DHT (dihydrotestosterone) in prostate tissue, which drives cell overgrowth.","Symptoms — Because the enlarged prostate squeezes the urethra, symptoms mimic a lower urinary tract problem: hesitancy, weak stream, frequency, urgency, nocturia, and a feeling of incomplete emptying.","Complication — Obstructive nephropathy — if the obstruction is severe and prolonged enough, urine can back up all the way to the kidneys and cause kidney damage.","Obstruction backing pressure up to the kidney follows the same by-level logic covered on Renal Disorders' Urinary Obstruction section","Watchful Waiting — For mild symptoms — monitor rather than treat immediately.","5-Alpha Reductase Inhibitors — Finasteride and dutasteride. Block the enzyme that converts testosterone to DHT, which shrinks the prostate over time. Effects take months to show.","Alpha-1 Blockers — Tamsulosin (Flomax) is the prototype. Relax smooth muscle in the prostate and bladder neck, working much faster than the 5-alpha reductase inhibitors — but they don't shrink the gland itself.","Combination Therapy — Using a 5-alpha reductase inhibitor and an alpha-1 blocker together for moderate-to-severe symptoms — one shrinks the gland long-term, the other relieves symptoms right away.","Trick to tell the two drug classes apart: \"-osteride shrinks it, -osin relaxes it fast.\" Finasteride/dutasteride (-osteride) are the slow shrinkers; tamsulosin (-osin) is the fast relaxer."]},{"s":"Prostate Cancer▾","a":"prostate-cancer","k":["Prostate Cancer▾","How Common — The most common cancer diagnosed in men in the U.S.","Risk Factors — Increasing age, race (higher incidence and mortality in Black men), and family history.","Presentation — Mostly asymptomatic in early stages — that's a key difference from BPH, which causes symptoms right away because of where it grows. Prostate cancer often isn't found until it has metastasized, classically to bone.","PSA Screening — Controversial. PSA (prostate-specific antigen) can be elevated by cancer, but also by BPH and prostatitis, so it isn't cancer-specific. Screening can catch cancer early, but it also drives overdiagnosis — biopsies and treatm…","Gleason Score — A grading system based on how abnormal prostate tissue looks under the microscope — higher scores mean more aggressive, less differentiated cancer.","Malignant? — No — Yes","Symptoms — Early — obstructs the urethra directly — Usually none until advanced/metastatic","Typical Age — Older men, very common with age — Risk rises with age; often older men too","Screening Tool — Not cancer-specific, but PSA can be mildly elevated — PSA — controversial for the overdiagnosis reasons above"]},{"s":"Erectile Dysfunction (ED)▾","a":"erectile-dysfunction-ed","k":["Erectile Dysfunction (ED)▾","Primary (Organic) Causes — A physical problem with blood flow, nerves, or hormones — vascular disease, diabetes, low testosterone, certain medications, pelvic surgery/injury.","Secondary (Psychogenic) Causes — Psychological — performance anxiety, depression, relationship stress. Can occur even when the physical machinery is normal.","Normal Erection Physiology — Sexual stimulation triggers nitric oxide (NO) release, which raises cyclic GMP (cGMP) inside penile smooth muscle. Rising cGMP relaxes that smooth muscle, allowing blood to flow in and fill the erectile tissue.","PDE5 Inhibitors — Sildenafil (Viagra) is the prototype. PDE5 (phosphodiesterase-5) is the enzyme that normally breaks down cGMP; blocking it lets cGMP stay elevated longer, sustaining the erection. Timing: taken before sexual activity, not …","Nitrate contraindication: PDE5 inhibitors must never be combined with nitrates (e.g., nitroglycerin). Both pathways lower blood pressure through the same nitric oxide/cGMP mechanism — combining them can cause a severe, life-threatening drop…","Priapism: a prolonged, painful erection unrelated to sexual stimulation, lasting more than 4 hours — a true urologic emergency. Untreated, it can cause permanent tissue damage and lasting erectile dysfunction. Call for immediate medical att…"]},{"s":"Quick Comparison — The Four Conditions▾","a":"quick-comparison-the-four-conditions","k":["Quick Comparison — The Four Conditions▾","A study trick for keeping these four straight: sort them by whether they're malignant and by their hallmark symptom.","Testicular Cancer — Yes — Painless testicular mass, young men","BPH — No — Obstructive urinary symptoms, older men","Prostate Cancer — Yes — Usually silent until metastatic (bone)","Erectile Dysfunction — N/A — Organic (vascular/nerve/hormone) vs. psychogenic cause"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-visual-sensory-disorders.html","t":"Visual & Sensory Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Visual & Sensory Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 5","Cataracts","Retinopathy","Retinal Detachment","Macular Degeneration","Glaucoma","Meniere's Disease","Quick Comparison — Sudden vs. Gradual, Painful vs. Painless","Self-Test Flashcards","This page covers six conditions in the order the lecture teaches them: cataracts, retinopathy (diabetic and hypertensive), retinal detachment, macular degeneration, glaucoma (open-angle and closed-angle, plus its pharmacology), and Meniere'…"]},{"s":"Cataracts▾","a":"cataracts","k":["Cataracts▾","What It Is — Clouding of the lens, strongly associated with aging.","Presentation — Painless, gradual blurry vision that worsens slowly over time.","Treatment — Surgical only — there is no medication that reverses lens clouding. Surgery removes the clouded lens and replaces it with an artificial one."]},{"s":"Retinopathy▾","a":"retinopathy","k":["Retinopathy▾","Diabetic Retinopathy","Non-proliferative — the earlier stage; small vessel changes (microaneurysms, leaking) in the retina.","Proliferative — the more advanced stage; the retina responds to poor blood flow by growing new, fragile blood vessels that bleed easily and threaten vision more seriously.","Hypertensive Retinopathy","Damage to retinal blood vessels from chronic high blood pressure.","Reversible with good blood pressure control, unlike most of the other conditions on this page.","Trick: retinopathy is the one category on this page that's actually reversible if you fix the root cause — get blood pressure and blood glucose under control and the vessel damage can improve, unlike cataracts, retinal detachment, or macula…"]},{"s":"Retinal Detachment▾","a":"retinal-detachment","k":["Retinal Detachment▾","Presentation — Sudden, painless, and unilateral (one eye). Classic symptoms: floaters, flashing lights, and a curtain or shadow moving across the visual field.","Risk Factors — Myopia (nearsightedness), older age, and trauma to the eye.","Retinal detachment is an emergency: the \"curtain\" description should immediately raise suspicion — delayed treatment risks permanent vision loss."]},{"s":"Macular Degeneration▾","a":"macular-degeneration","k":["Macular Degeneration▾","How Common — About 90% of cases — more common form — About 10% of cases","Mechanism — Gradual thinning/breakdown of the macula over time — Abnormal new blood vessels grow under the macula and leak","Risk Factors","UV light exposure over a lifetime.","Hyperopia (farsightedness).","Smoking.","Macular degeneration is irreversible — management focuses on slowing progression and preserving remaining central vision, not restoring what's lost."]},{"s":"Glaucoma▾","a":"glaucoma","k":["Glaucoma▾","Onset — Gradual, progressive — Sudden — a true emergency","Mechanism — Dysfunction of the trabecular meshwork (the eye's normal drainage pathway) — fluid drains too slowly, pressure builds up slowly over time — The iris blocks the drainage angle outright, causing a rapid, dangerous spike in eye pre…","Trigger — No single trigger — a chronic process — Can be precipitated by anticholinergic drugs, which dilate the pupil and can push the iris into the drainage angle","Trick: \"open\" is the slow leak, \"closed\" is the sudden clog. Open-angle sneaks up gradually because the drain is just sluggish; closed-angle is an emergency because the drain gets physically blocked all at once.","Acute closed-angle glaucoma is an emergency: the sudden pressure spike can cause permanent vision loss within hours — it needs immediate treatment to lower intraocular pressure, not a routine eye-clinic follow-up.","Glaucoma Pharmacology","Beta Blockers (timolol, betaxolol) — Reduce the amount of aqueous humor (the eye's internal fluid) produced. The instructor's own memorable phrase for this class: \"chill out drugs\" — they calm down fluid production the way beta blockers cal…","Prostaglandin Analogs (latanoprost) — Increase outflow of aqueous humor through an alternate drainage pathway.","Alpha-2 Agonists (brimonidine) — Both decrease aqueous humor production and increase its outflow.","Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors (dorzolamide) — Decrease aqueous humor production by inhibiting the enzyme carbonic anhydrase in the eye.","Nasolacrimal Occlusion — Administration Technique","After instilling eye drops, press gently on the inner corner of the eye (over the tear duct) for about a minute.","This prevents the drop from draining into the nasolacrimal duct and being absorbed systemically — reducing systemic side effects (this matters especially for the beta blocker eye drops, since systemic beta-blockade is possible if too much i…"]},{"s":"Meniere's Disease▾","a":"meniere-s-disease","k":["Meniere's Disease▾","Mechanism — Endolymphatic hydrops — an abnormal buildup of endolymph (inner ear fluid) that disrupts normal inner ear function.","Presentation — Episodic attacks of vertigo, hearing loss, tinnitus, and a feeling of fullness/pressure in the affected ear.","Management — Primarily dietary/lifestyle — trigger management (commonly reducing sodium and caffeine). There is no direct pharmacologic cure; treatment focuses on managing symptoms and triggers during episodes."]},{"s":"Quick Comparison — Sudden vs. Gradual, Painful vs. Painless▾","a":"quick-comparison-sudden-vs-gradual-painful-vs-painless","k":["Quick Comparison — Sudden vs. Gradual, Painful vs. Painless▾","A study trick for this whole topic: sort every condition by how fast it comes on and whether it hurts. The emergencies are the sudden, painless ones — that combination is easy to underestimate because there's no pain to prompt urgency.","Cataracts — Gradual — No — Often bilateral over time","Diabetic/Hypertensive Retinopathy — Gradual — No — Usually bilateral","Retinal Detachment — Sudden — No — Unilateral — emergency","Macular Degeneration — Gradual — No — Can be bilateral","Open-Angle Glaucoma — Gradual — No — Can be bilateral","Closed-Angle Glaucoma — Sudden — Yes — often with eye pain/headache — Usually unilateral — emergency","Meniere's Disease — Episodic/sudden attacks — No (pressure/fullness, not pain) — Usually unilateral"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week3-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 3","Three Clusters, Not Four Separate Topics","The Four Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new — it's a map of Week 3's four topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. Two of the four pages form a direct progression (the urinary tract…"]},{"s":"Three Clusters, Not Four Separate Topics▾","a":"three-clusters-not-four-separate-topics","k":["Three Clusters, Not Four Separate Topics▾","The Urinary Tract, Healthy to Failing — Renal Disorders → Acute Kidney Injury & Chronic Kidney Disease — These two build on each other directly: Renal Disorders covers infection, obstruction, stones, cancer, and glomerular disease at the or…","Bridging Back Into the Urinary Tract — Male Reproductive Disorders — Three of its four conditions (testicular cancer, prostate cancer, erectile dysfunction) are self-contained. But BPH's obstructive-nephropathy complication is the same by-l…","A Separate Sensory System — Visual & Sensory Disorders — Not connected to the urinary/reproductive topics mechanically — it's grouped into Week 3 as its own sensory-systems unit. Internally, though, it has the same \"sudden and painless is s…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, work through Renal Disorders before AKI & CKD — the second page assumes you already know how infection, obstruction, stones, and glomerular disease can independently damage the kidney — then …"]},{"s":"The Four Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-four-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Four Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Renal Disorders — Five conditions at the organ level: pyelonephritis (an ascending or bloodstream kidney infection that can progress to urosepsis), urinary obstruction (by level, speed, and internal-vs-external cause), nephrolithiasis (…","2 — Acute Kidney Injury & Chronic Kidney Disease — AKI is classified by where the problem sits relative to the kidney (prerenal, intrarenal, postrenal) and is usually reversible. CKD is staged by GFR, driven by a self-perpetuating proteinur…","3 — Male Reproductive Disorders — Testicular cancer (young men, painless mass, high survival), BPH (benign prostate overgrowth causing obstructive urinary symptoms), prostate cancer (usually silent until metastatic, controversial PSA screen…","4 — Visual & Sensory Disorders — Six conditions sorted by speed and pain: gradual/painless (cataracts, retinopathy, macular degeneration, open-angle glaucoma) versus sudden (retinal detachment — painless but urgent; closed-angle glaucoma — …"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content — each idea is fully taught in exactly one place. But they resurface as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why.","Obstruction by level, causing back-pressure damage — Renal Disorders (Urinary Obstruction) — Male Reproductive Disorders' BPH complication (obstructive nephropathy — the prostate is simply another cause of a bladder/urethra-level blockage),…","The kidneys' five key jobs — Acute Kidney Injury & Chronic Kidney Disease — Referenced implicitly whenever Renal Disorders' conditions cause lasting kidney damage — chronic pyelonephritis and glomerulopathy are both named as CKD causes with…","Diabetes and hypertension causing chronic end-organ damage — Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome and Hypertension & Hyperlipidemia (Pathopharm Review) — Renal Disorders' glomerulopathy section (diabetic/hypertensive glomerular damage) and Visual …","Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent (ESA) black-box warning — Anemia (Week 2) — Acute Kidney Injury & Chronic Kidney Disease's anemia-of-CKD pharmacotherapy section, cross-referenced rather than re-taught since CKD-driven anemia is treated wit…","Type II/III hypersensitivity mechanics — GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity (Pathopharm Review) — Renal Disorders' glomerulonephritis section, which depends on the same immune mechanisms without re-explaining them."]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","Every danger callout across the four pages, condensed to a phrase. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation — click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","Renal Disorders — Urosepsis — a UTI (upper or lower) can progress to sepsis in elderly or already-sick patients. BCG intravesical therapy is contraindicated in immunocompromised patients — it's a live attenuated organism and can cause disse…","AKI & CKD — Erythropoietin's black-box warning — overcorrecting hemoglobin above 10 g/dL raises stroke/MI/thromboembolism risk. Opioids are retained far longer than normal in severe CKD, raising respiratory depression risk.","Male Reproductive Disorders — PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil) can never be combined with nitrates — both drop blood pressure through the same pathway. Priapism (an erection lasting more than 4 hours) is a true emergency that can cause permanen…","Visual & Sensory Disorders — Retinal detachment is a sudden, painless emergency — the \"curtain\" description should raise immediate suspicion. Acute closed-angle glaucoma is a sudden, painful emergency that can cause permanent vision loss wi…","Patterns you'll notice across these rules","\"Sudden and painless is still an emergency.\" Retinal detachment causes no pain at all, and AKI's oliguria can be easy to write off as \"just a little dehydrated\" — neither presentation's calmness means the situation is calm.","\"Trace every finding back to a job that stopped happening.\" The kidneys' five jobs work the same way the liver's five jobs do elsewhere in this course: pick the abnormal lab or symptom, ask which specific job is responsible, and the \"why\" f…","\"One blockage causes disease from two different directions.\" A kidney stone and an enlarged prostate are mechanically the same problem — a blockage backing pressure up the urinary tract — just at different levels and from different organs.","\"Combining two drugs that do the same thing through the same pathway is the danger, not either drug alone.\" PDE5 inhibitors and nitrates both lower blood pressure via nitric oxide/cGMP; ACE inhibitors and ARBs (CKD) are never combined for t…","\"Screening isn't automatically good.\" PSA screening for prostate cancer is the clearest example this week — it can catch real cancer early, but it can also drive biopsies and treatment for disease that may never have caused harm."]}]},{"f":"week3-comprehensive-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 3 · Topic 1","Part 1 — Renal Disorders","Part 2 — Acute Kidney Injury & Chronic Kidney Disease","Part 3 — Male Reproductive Disorders","Part 4 — Visual & Sensory Disorders","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 3 — renal disorders, acute kidney injury & chronic kidney disease, male reproductive disorders, and visual & sensory disorders — all in one place. It intentionally repe…"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Renal Disorders▾","a":"part-1-renal-disorders","k":["Part 1 — Renal Disorders▾","Pyelonephritis (Upper UTI / Kidney Infection)","Definition — Inflammation of one or both kidneys, focused on the upper urinary tract. Usually ascending — a lower UTI climbs up the urinary tract — but can also come from a systemic bloodstream infection reaching the kidney.","Pathophysiology — E. coli is the most common pathogen. The kidney becomes swollen and fills with infectious exudate; if severe, this can compress the renal artery. The kidney can become necrotic or form abscesses.","Acute vs. Chronic — Usually acute. Untreated or recurrent pyelonephritis can become chronic — a cause of chronic kidney disease and a risk for sepsis down the road.","Risk Factors — Pregnancy, recurrent lower UTIs, and an antibiotic-resistant strain (a lower UTI treated with an antibiotic it's resistant to stays untreated and can ascend).","Onset — Gradual — \"do I have a UTI, do I not.\" — Sudden — wakes up or develops within an afternoon.","How They Look — Otherwise well. — Look and feel systemically sick — this is a systemic infection.","Key Findings — Dysuria, frequency, urgency. — Fever, chills, CVA (costovertebral angle) tenderness over the back — plus the same frequency/urgency/dysuria, since the lower tract is usually still involved. Hematuria and nausea/vomiting/anore…","Treatment","Community-acquired, low resistance risk: Bactrim (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) first-line, or ciprofloxacin. Nitrofurantoin (Macrobid) is used clinically but isn't on the drug matrix.","Uncomplicated community cases get a short course; recurrent UTIs get a more conventional 7–14 day course.","More severe cases may need inpatient IV antibiotics. A urinalysis and culture identify the organism and its sensitivity.","Pregnancy complicates treatment — Bactrim and ciprofloxacin are avoided, narrowing options right when pregnant patients are already at higher risk for pyelonephritis.","Urosepsis: in elderly or already-sick patients, a UTI (upper or lower) can progress to sepsis — a severe systemic response with a high mortality rate.","Urinary Obstruction","A blockage can occur anywhere along the urinary tract — the renal pelvis, the ureter, or the bladder/urethra — and whatever is downstream of the blockage backs up, the same way a clogged pipe backs up.","Renal Pelvis — Kidney stones (renal calculi) — most common cause at this level.","Ureter — A stone traveling down, or external compression (e.g., a tumor).","Bladder / Urethra — BPH is the classic cause in men (see Part 3).","Infection Risk","Stagnant urine behind an obstruction is a breeding ground for bacteria.","Raises the risk of UTI and pyelonephritis.","Back-Pressure Risk","Hydronephrosis (kidney swelling) and hydroureter (ureter swelling) from backed-up urine.","Prolonged pressure can permanently damage kidney tissue.","Trick — 3 things that determine how obstruction shows up: (1) site — where along the tract; (2) speed of onset — sudden vs. gradual; (3) internal vs. external — a stone inside the lumen vs. something pressing from outside.","Nephrolithiasis (Kidney Stones)","Mechanism","Supersaturation of urine with stone-forming substances leads to crystal formation.","Stones always form at the kidney level, even if they cause symptoms lower down as they travel.","Low Fluid Intake — Concentrates urine, promoting supersaturation.","Diet — High sodium, oxalate, or animal protein intake raises risk depending on stone type.","Family History / Metabolic Conditions — Predisposes to specific stone types.","Recurrent UTIs — Certain bacteria promote struvite stone formation.","Calcium — Most common — about 70–80% of all kidney stones.","Struvite (Staghorn) — Associated with recurrent UTIs from urease-producing bacteria; can grow to fill the renal pelvis (the \"staghorn\" shape).","Uric Acid — Associated with high-purine diets and conditions like gout.","Cystine — Rare — less than 1% of stones; a genetic/metabolic cause.","Renal Colic & Treatment","Classic presentation: sudden, severe flank pain that can radiate to the groin, often with nausea/vomiting and hematuria.","Treatment goals: pain control, hydration to help pass the stone, and prevention tailored to the specific stone type (e.g., dietary changes, urine alkalinization/acidification depending on stone chemistry).","Urologic Cancers","Kidney Cancer","About 85% are renal cell carcinoma.","Smoking is the #1 risk factor.","No early manifestations — often found incidentally.","Late findings: CVA tenderness, hematuria, a palpable mass.","Metastasizes to bone and lung.","Treatment: nephrectomy, or chemo/radiation for advanced/metastatic disease.","Bladder Cancer","4th most common cancer in men; urothelial carcinoma is the main type.","Risk factors: smoking, occupational toxin exposure, low fluid intake.","Early: painless hematuria. Later: UTI-like symptoms (frequency, urgency, dysuria).","Mechanism — Intravesical (instilled directly into the bladder) immunotherapy that stimulates a local immune response against tumor cells.","Administration — Instilled into the bladder via catheter and held for a period of time before voiding.","Adverse Effects — Bladder irritation, flu-like symptoms, hematuria.","Nursing Care — Patient education on handling urine after treatment (it contains live attenuated bacteria) and monitoring for systemic BCG infection.","BCG is contraindicated in immunocompromised patients — because it uses a live attenuated organism, it can cause a disseminated infection in someone who can't contain it.","Glomerular Disorders","Anatomy — The glomerulus and its basement membrane (GBM) are the kidney's filtering unit.","Glomerulonephritis — Inflammation of the glomerulus, often from a Type II or Type III hypersensitivity reaction.","Common Triggers — Post-streptococcal infection, IgA nephropathy (Berger's disease), lupus, Goodpasture syndrome (anti-GBM antibodies attacking the glomerular basement membrane directly).","Chronic Form — Can progress to end-stage renal disease (ESRD) if untreated or recurrent.","The Type II vs. Type III hypersensitivity mechanisms behind glomerulonephritis are covered in full on GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity","HARP — the acute presentation of glomerulonephritis: Hematuria, Azotemia (rising BUN/creatinine), Red cell casts, Proteinuria.","Nephrotic Syndrome — Defined by more than 3g of protein in urine per 24 hours. Albumin loss causes edema. Loss of antithrombin III and plasminogen causes hypercoagulability. Also causes hyperlipidemia and RAAS-driven hypertension.","Glomerulopathy — Chronic damage from diabetes (thickens the GBM) or hypertension (causes glomerular sclerosis/scarring) — distinct from the inflammatory process of glomerulonephritis, though it can present similarly (acute-looking findings …","Diabetic microvascular damage is covered in full on Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome","Hypertension's long-term organ damage is covered in full on Hypertension & Hyperlipidemia"]},{"s":"Part 2 — Acute Kidney Injury & Chronic Kidney Disease▾","a":"part-2-acute-kidney-injury-chronic-kidney-disease","k":["Part 2 — Acute Kidney Injury & Chronic Kidney Disease▾","The Kidneys' Five Key Jobs","Fluid and electrolyte homeostasis.","Elimination of water-soluble waste.","Producing erythropoietin (drives RBC production).","Activating vitamin D (needed for calcium absorption).","Producing renin (blood pressure regulation).","Trick: almost every CKD manifestation traces back to one of these five jobs failing — anemia (no erythropoietin), bone disease (no activated vitamin D), hypertension (renin dysregulation), fluid overload and electrolyte chaos (homeostasis j…","Greater than 90 — Normal kidney function.","About 25% of normal (roughly 25–30 mL/hr) — Marks renal insufficiency — the shared staging indicator for both AKI and CKD.","Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)","AKI is fundamentally an ischemic/volume-loss problem — the kidney isn't getting adequate blood flow or is directly injured, and it happens quickly.","Prerenal — A volume problem before the kidney (hypotension, dehydration, hemorrhage, heart failure) — the most common classification.","Intrarenal — Direct damage inside the kidney itself, e.g. acute tubular necrosis (ATN) from prolonged ischemia or nephrotoxins.","Postrenal — An obstruction after the kidney (e.g., BPH, a stone) — the least common classification.","Trick: think of AKI's three classifications like a traffic jam — prerenal is \"not enough cars getting to the intersection\" (volume), intrarenal is \"the intersection itself is broken\" (direct damage), postrenal is \"something's blocking the r…","Oliguria — Less than 400 mL of urine in 24 hours (about less than 30 mL/hour), typically beginning about one day after a hypotensive event.","Treatment Principles","Identify and correct the underlying cause (restore volume for prerenal, relieve obstruction for postrenal, stop nephrotoxic agents for intrarenal).","Supportive care while the kidney heals — AKI is usually reversible, though prevention is still the goal since severity and duration affect recovery.","Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)","Staging & Prevention","Staged by GFR — progressively lower GFR means a higher (worse) stage.","Uremia (buildup of nitrogenous waste with systemic effects) appears at stage 5, the most severe stage.","Management is prevention-focused — slowing progression matters more than any single \"fix,\" since CKD is generally not reversible.","Diabetes — ~50%","Hypertension — ~30%","Other Causes — ~20%","Cyclical Pathogenesis","Proteinuria and increased angiotensin II drive ongoing glomerular damage.","That damage worsens proteinuria and angiotensin II activity further — a self-perpetuating cycle that is the biggest driver of CKD progression.","Neurologic — Confusion, lethargy, peripheral neuropathy as uremic toxins accumulate.","Cardiovascular — Hypertension (renin dysregulation), increased cardiovascular disease risk.","Gastrointestinal — Nausea, anorexia, uremic fetor (ammonia-like breath odor).","Pulmonary / Fluid — Fluid overload and pulmonary edema — often the trigger that pushes a patient to start dialysis.","Integumentary — Uremic frost — visible crystallized urea on the skin in very advanced, untreated disease.","Hematologic — Chronic anemia from loss of erythropoietin production.","Musculoskeletal — Renal osteodystrophy — weakened, fracture-prone bones from loss of vitamin D activation.","CKD Pharmacotherapy","Blood Pressure — Goal systolic under 140 — not too aggressive, since overly tight control can compromise kidney perfusion. ACE inhibitors and ARBs are used, but never combined together.","Cholesterol — Statins; goal cholesterol under 200.","Volume Overload — A diuretic (usually furosemide) plus a lower-sodium diet (around 2 g/day).","Hyperkalemia — A diuretic to lower potassium; other agents such as Kayexalate; dialysis at more severe stages also corrects this.","Metabolic Acidosis — Sodium bicarbonate — an alkalinizing agent, goal bicarb 18–20.","Hyperphosphatemia / Renal Osteodystrophy — Calcium carbonate (binds phosphate — calcium and phosphate move inversely, like a teeter-totter) and calcitriol (activated vitamin D, since the kidney can no longer activate it itself).","Anemia — Erythropoietin — has a black-box warning (see below).","Erythropoietin black-box warning: overcorrecting hemoglobin (giving it when hemoglobin is already above 10 g/dL) raises the risk of stroke, MI, and thromboembolism.","The full erythropoiesis-stimulating agent black-box warning is covered on Anemia","Renal Dosing","The kidneys are the primary filter for most medications — when kidney function drops, drug elimination slows and doses/frequency often need adjusting downward.","Common examples: digoxin, diabetic medications (glyburide, metformin), vancomycin (its own dedicated renal dosing protocol), and opioids.","Opioids in severe CKD: a patient with severe, poorly-filtering CKD will retain opioids far longer than normal, risking significant respiratory depression — the classic \"most concerning medication\" answer for a CKD patient in pain."]},{"s":"Part 3 — Male Reproductive Disorders▾","a":"part-3-male-reproductive-disorders","k":["Part 3 — Male Reproductive Disorders▾","Testicular Cancer","How Common — Rare overall (~1% of cancers in men), but the most common solid tumor in young men, ages 15–34.","Biggest Risk Factor — Cryptorchidism (undescended testicle) — risk stays elevated for life even after surgical correction.","Tumor Origin — The vast majority arise from germ cells inside the testicle.","Presentation — A painless mass or lump — patients often find it themselves; painlessness can delay reporting.","Prognosis — Very high survival rates, even in later-stage disease.","Seminoma","Grows more slowly.","More radiosensitive, somewhat more favorable prognosis.","Nonseminoma","Grows and spreads more quickly.","Still highly treatable overall.","Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)","What BPH Is — A non-malignant overgrowth of the prostate's epithelial and smooth muscle tissue — not cancer, does not become cancer.","Proposed Mechanisms — Hormone imbalance (shifting androgen/estrogen ratio with age) and DHT accumulation in prostate tissue.","Symptoms — The enlarged prostate squeezes the urethra: hesitancy, weak stream, frequency, urgency, nocturia, incomplete emptying.","Complication — Obstructive nephropathy — urine can back up all the way to the kidneys if severe/prolonged.","Watchful Waiting — For mild symptoms.","5-Alpha Reductase Inhibitors — Finasteride, dutasteride — block conversion of testosterone to DHT, shrink the prostate over months.","Alpha-1 Blockers — Tamsulosin (Flomax) — relaxes smooth muscle for fast symptom relief, doesn't shrink the gland.","Combination Therapy — Both classes together for moderate-to-severe symptoms.","Trick: \"-osteride shrinks it, -osin relaxes it fast.\" Finasteride/dutasteride (-osteride) shrink slowly; tamsulosin (-osin) relaxes fast.","Prostate Cancer","How Common — The most common cancer diagnosed in men in the U.S.","Risk Factors — Age, race (higher incidence/mortality in Black men), family history.","Presentation — Mostly asymptomatic early — often not found until it metastasizes, classically to bone.","PSA Screening — Controversial — PSA isn't cancer-specific (BPH/prostatitis also raise it), and screening drives overdiagnosis/overtreatment of slow-growing cancers.","Gleason Score — Grading system based on microscopic tissue abnormality — higher score means more aggressive cancer.","Erectile Dysfunction (ED)","Primary (Organic) Causes — Vascular disease, diabetes, low testosterone, medications, pelvic surgery/injury.","Secondary (Psychogenic) Causes — Performance anxiety, depression, relationship stress.","Normal Physiology — Nitric oxide raises cGMP, relaxing penile smooth muscle and allowing blood flow in.","PDE5 Inhibitors — Sildenafil (Viagra) blocks the enzyme that breaks down cGMP, sustaining the erection. Taken before sexual activity. Adverse effects: headache, flushing, visual changes.","Nitrate contraindication: never combine PDE5 inhibitors with nitrates — both lower blood pressure through the same NO/cGMP pathway, risking a severe, life-threatening blood pressure drop.","Priapism: a painful erection lasting more than 4 hours unrelated to stimulation — a true urologic emergency that can cause permanent damage if untreated."]},{"s":"Part 4 — Visual & Sensory Disorders▾","a":"part-4-visual-sensory-disorders","k":["Part 4 — Visual & Sensory Disorders▾","Cataracts","Clouding of the lens, strongly associated with aging.","Painless, gradual blurry vision.","Treatment is surgical only.","Retinopathy","Diabetic","Non-proliferative — earlier, small vessel changes.","Proliferative — advanced, fragile new vessels that bleed easily.","Hypertensive","Vessel damage from chronic high blood pressure.","Reversible with good BP control.","Retinal Detachment","Presentation — Sudden, painless, unilateral. Floaters, flashing lights, a curtain/shadow over the visual field.","Risk Factors — Myopia, older age, trauma.","Why It Matters — An emergency — delayed treatment risks permanent vision loss.","Macular Degeneration","Frequency — ~90% — ~10%","Mechanism — Gradual macular thinning — Abnormal vessels grow/leak under the macula","Risk factors: UV exposure, hyperopia, smoking. Irreversible — management slows progression.","Glaucoma","Onset — Gradual — Sudden — emergency","Mechanism — Trabecular meshwork dysfunction — slow drainage — Iris blocks the drainage angle outright","Trigger — Chronic process, no single trigger — Anticholinergic drugs can precipitate an attack","Beta Blockers (timolol, betaxolol) — Reduce aqueous humor production — the instructor's \"chill out drugs.\"","Prostaglandin Analogs (latanoprost) — Increase outflow through an alternate pathway.","Alpha-2 Agonists (brimonidine) — Decrease production and increase outflow.","Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors (dorzolamide) — Decrease production by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase.","Nasolacrimal occlusion (pressing the inner corner of the eye after drops) reduces systemic absorption and systemic side effects.","Meniere's Disease","Mechanism — Endolymphatic hydrops — abnormal endolymph buildup in the inner ear.","Presentation — Episodic vertigo, hearing loss, tinnitus, fullness in the ear.","Management — Dietary/lifestyle trigger management (sodium, caffeine) — no direct pharmacologic cure."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One more trick for tying the whole week together: three of Week 3's four topics have a genuine emergency buried inside them that's easy to miss because nothing hurts (or, for closed-angle glaucoma, because it hurts but looks like something …","Renal Disorders — Urosepsis from an untreated/progressing UTI or pyelonephritis.","AKI & CKD — Severe hyperkalemia and pulmonary edema from fluid overload.","Male Reproductive — Priapism (>4 hours) and the PDE5-inhibitor/nitrate combination.","Visual & Sensory — Retinal detachment and acute closed-angle glaucoma."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Three cards from each of the four parts above — a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week4-liver-function-jaundice.html","t":"Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice","secs":[{"s":"Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 2","Liver Anatomy & Key Cells","What the Liver Actually Does","Portal Circulation","Liver Function Tests (LFTs)","Jaundice (Icterus)","Recognizing Jaundice at the Bedside","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is the foundation for the rest of Week 4. Viral Hepatitis and Cirrhosis & Liver Failure both build directly on the liver function tests and jaundice mechanics taught here — rather than re-explaining them, those pages link back to …"]},{"s":"Liver Anatomy & Key Cells▾","a":"liver-anatomy-key-cells","k":["Liver Anatomy & Key Cells▾","Size & Location — The largest organ in the body — weighs about 3 pounds and receives 5–7% of total cardiac output. Sits in the right epigastric region, enclosed in a fibrous capsule, divided into left and right lobes.","Lobules — The functional units of the liver. Made up of hepatocytes (liver cells) arranged around a central vein.","Hepatocytes — The liver's main working cells. One of the few cell types in the body that can regenerate — if the remaining liver tissue is healthy, lost liver tissue can regrow.","Kupffer Cells — Line the liver's inner capillaries (sinusoids). Responsible for removing bacteria and toxins from the blood — the liver's built-in blood filter."]},{"s":"What the Liver Actually Does▾","a":"what-the-liver-actually-does","k":["What the Liver Actually Does▾","Understanding these five jobs is what makes the rest of Week 4 make sense — every major finding in hepatitis, cirrhosis, and liver failure is really just \"one of these five jobs stopped happening.\"","The Liver's Five Jobs","Metabolizes and stores fat, carbohydrate, protein, vitamins, and minerals.","Blood volume reservoir — distends and compresses to hold or release blood based on circulating volume.","Blood filter — Kupffer cells purify the blood; the liver also removes bilirubin, a byproduct of hemoglobin breakdown.","Makes clotting factors, including prothrombin and fibrinogen.","Metabolizes and detoxifies drugs.","MNEMONIC: When the liver fails, work backward through its five jobs to predict the labs — no metabolism (glucose/protein problems), no reservoir/filter function (bilirubin and toxins build up), no clotting factors (bleeding), no drug metabo…"]},{"s":"Portal Circulation▾","a":"portal-circulation","k":["Portal Circulation▾","How Blood Reaches the Liver","The hepatic portal vein carries deoxygenated blood to the liver from the stomach, intestines, spleen, and pancreas — the lower digestive organs.","This blood is rich in nutrients absorbed from the intestinal tract, which is exactly why the liver has so much to do with metabolism — digested nutrients go straight to the liver before reaching the rest of the body.","This is also where the first-pass effect comes from — an oral drug passes through the portal circulation and liver before reaching systemic circulation, which is why oral doses are often higher than IV doses of the same drug.","First-pass effect and drug metabolism basics are covered in full on the Pharmacology Foundations page"]},{"s":"Liver Function Tests (LFTs)▾","a":"liver-function-tests-lfts","k":["Liver Function Tests (LFTs)▾","AST, ALT & Alk Phos (\"liver enzymes\") — Released when liver cells are damaged. These are what people usually mean by \"LFTs.\" — Elevated — but the exact number is not a reliable indicator of how severe the disease is. A patient could have en…","Serum Ammonia — The liver normally breaks ammonia down. — Elevated — ammonia is also the key driver of hepatic encephalopathy (covered on the Cirrhosis page).","Total Protein & Albumin — The liver manufactures these proteins. — Decreased — less protein production means less oncotic pressure to hold fluid in the vessels (this is what drives the edema and ascites seen in cirrhosis).","Prothrombin Time (PT) — The liver makes prothrombin, a clotting factor. — Prolonged/elevated — without enough prothrombin, blood takes longer to clot and patients bleed more easily.","Bilirubin is also part of the liver workup, but it gets its own section below since it's the key to understanding jaundice."]},{"s":"Jaundice (Icterus)▾","a":"jaundice-icterus","k":["Jaundice (Icterus)▾","Jaundice and icterus are the same thing — a yellowish discoloration of the skin and deep tissues caused by elevated bilirubin. It usually isn't visible until bilirubin reaches about 2–2.5 mg/dL (normal is roughly 0.6–1 mg/dL).","Conjugated (Direct) Bilirubin — ~30%","Bilirubin the liver has already processed.","Elevated when conjugated bilirubin can't get OUT of the liver — a bile duct obstruction (gallstones).","The liver is doing its job; the bile just can't flow out.","Unconjugated (Indirect) Bilirubin — ~70%","Bilirubin the liver has NOT yet processed.","Elevated with bilirubin overproduction (hemolysis) OR impaired liver function — the liver can't conjugate it in the first place.","Some liver conditions and hepatitis cases elevate both types at once.","Hemolytic Jaundice — Too many red blood cells are being broken down (excess RBCs, internal bleeding, hemolysis). — Unconjugated (indirect) — bilirubin overproduction outpaces the liver's ability to conjugate it.","Hepatocellular Jaundice — The liver itself isn't working — it can't take up or conjugate bilirubin from the blood. — Unconjugated (indirect) — the liver never gets the chance to conjugate it.","Obstructive Jaundice — Not actually a liver problem — bile can't flow OUT of the liver, most often from gallstones. — Conjugated (direct) — the liver already did its job; the bile is just blocked."]},{"s":"Recognizing Jaundice at the Bedside▾","a":"recognizing-jaundice-at-the-bedside","k":["Recognizing Jaundice at the Bedside▾","Clinical Signs","Yellowish hue to skin and deep tissues — rarely as bright as it looks on TV. The sclera of the eyes is the best place to look, especially in a darker-skinned patient.","Palms, soles, and mucous membranes can also show discoloration.","Dark amber urine that foams when shaken — conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble and backs up into the blood, then the urine.","Stool can be normal or clay-colored — bile salts normally give stool its brown color; if bile flow is obstructed, that pigment never reaches the stool.","Pruritus (itching) — backed-up bile salts get deposited in the skin. Sometimes localized to palms/soles, sometimes generalized.","Jaundice is part of hepatitis's icteric phase — full detail on the Viral Hepatitis page","Jaundice and low albumin/PT are also late-stage cirrhosis findings — full detail on the Cirrhosis & Liver Failure page"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week4-viral-hepatitis.html","t":"Viral Hepatitis (A, B & C)","secs":[{"s":"Viral Hepatitis (A, B & C)","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 3","What Viral Hepatitis Is","Pathogenesis","The Three Clinical Phases","Comparing Hepatitis A, B & C","Pharmacotherapy","Self-Test Flashcards","Jaundice mechanics and LFT trends used throughout this page are covered in full on Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice"]},{"s":"What Viral Hepatitis Is▾","a":"what-viral-hepatitis-is","k":["What Viral Hepatitis Is▾","The Basics","A systemic virus that mainly affects the liver — the \"-itis\" means inflammation, so this is inflammation of the liver caused by a virus.","Many strains exist, but this course focuses on hepatitis A, B, and C (HAV, HBV, HCV). Hepatitis D and E exist but are much less common in the U.S. — hepatitis E is notably dangerous in pregnancy, but is not otherwise tested here.","Other viruses (Epstein-Barr, cytomegalovirus) and non-infectious causes (alcohol abuse, certain drugs, chemicals, bacteria) can also inflame the liver, but \"viral hepatitis\" in this course means A, B, or C.","Hepatitis B and C are the most common; hepatitis A outbreaks still occur periodically."]},{"s":"Pathogenesis▾","a":"pathogenesis","k":["Pathogenesis▾","How the Damage Happens","Viral infection triggers an immune response — a release of inflammatory mediators in and around the liver, where the virus lives.","Inflammatory mediators cause infected cells to lyse (rupture), which causes edema and swelling around the dead cells.","That edema and swelling contributes to tissue hypoxia, which — if severe enough — kills healthy hepatocytes (hepatocyte death).","Hepatocyte death can ultimately lead to long-term liver failure or problems.","Many cases of viral hepatitis are asymptomatic. When symptoms do occur, they progress the same way regardless of strain (A, B, or C) — only the transmission route, chronicity risk, and treatment differ between strains."]},{"s":"The Three Clinical Phases▾","a":"the-three-clinical-phases","k":["The Three Clinical Phases▾","Prodromal — Begins about 2 weeks after exposure. — Nonspecific symptoms: fatigue, anorexia, malaise, nausea/vomiting, headache, hyperalgesia (increased sensitivity to pain), cough, low-grade fever. The virus is highly transmissible during t…","Icteric — Begins 1–2 weeks after the prodromal phase; lasts 2–6 weeks. — The patient becomes jaundiced — dark urine, possibly clay-colored stools, an enlarged and tender liver. This is often thought of as the \"active\" phase of illness, even…","Recovery — Jaundice resolves; enzymes normalize over roughly 2–12 weeks after jaundice onset. — Symptoms diminish over about 6–8 weeks total after exposure, though the liver can remain enlarged and tender even as symptoms fade.","Most patients with acute viral hepatitis recover completely and clear the virus — overall mortality is under 1%, higher in elderly patients or those with comorbidities. The concerning outcomes below are what make hepatitis B and C different…","Possible Complications","Chronic hepatitis — the virus never fully clears, causing prolonged liver problems.","Cirrhosis — permanent liver scarring (see the Cirrhosis & Liver Failure page).","Liver cancer — hepatitis B and C are both linked to hepatocellular carcinoma.","Fulminant viral hepatitis — a severe initial case that becomes acute liver failure, which can progress to death."]},{"s":"Comparing Hepatitis A, B & C▾","a":"comparing-hepatitis-a-b-c","k":["Comparing Hepatitis A, B & C▾","Transmission — Fecal-oral (foodborne, poor sanitation); also IV drug use or sexual contact. — IV drug use (dirty needles), sexual contact. — Same as B — IV drug use, unsafe sex; also some mother-to-fetal transmission and rare medical mishap…","Onset — Acute — becomes sick quickly, generally mild symptoms. — Insidious — long incubation, 60–180 days before symptoms may appear. — Insidious, like B — can have more symptoms than B.","Chronic Infection Risk — Does NOT become chronic. — About 10% develop chronic hepatitis B. — About 80% develop chronic hepatitis C — this is why it was such a major problem before effective treatment existed.","Vaccine — Yes — 2 doses, 6 months apart. Recommended for high-risk groups and travelers, not universally required. — Yes — all infants/newborns and healthcare workers required; 3-dose series. — No vaccine exists.","MNEMONIC: Hepatitis A is the \"food and water\" one (fecal-oral, acute, never chronic, has a vaccine). B and C are the \"blood and body fluid\" ones (needles/sex, insidious onset, can go chronic) — the key difference between B and C is how OFTE…"]},{"s":"Pharmacotherapy▾","a":"pharmacotherapy","k":["Pharmacotherapy▾","Hepatitis B (chronic, high-risk patients) — Two drug classes, usually combined: interferons (pegylated interferon alpha / \"Pegasys\") and nucleoside analogs (entecavir, tenofovir). Treatment is reserved for high-risk patients — elevated AST,…","Hepatitis C — Direct-acting antivirals (DAAs) — a newer drug class that can essentially eliminate the virus from the body in most patients. Sometimes combined with interferon or ribavirin. Anyone with a detectable viral load is now treated;…","Acetaminophen (Tylenol) dosing with hepatitis: patients with chronic or active hepatitis may still take Tylenol, but should stay under 2 grams/day (versus the usual 4 g/day limit) since the liver already can't metabolize normally. Avoid Tyl…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week4-cirrhosis-liver-failure.html","t":"Cirrhosis & Liver Failure","secs":[{"s":"Cirrhosis & Liver Failure","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 4","Hepatitis vs. Cirrhosis — Reversible vs. Irreversible","Causes of Cirrhosis","How Fibrosis Actually Develops","Alcoholic Liver Disease — A Spectrum","Manifestations of Late-Stage Cirrhosis","Portal Hypertension","Hepatic Encephalopathy","Acute (Fulminant) Liver Failure vs. Cirrhosis","Drug Therapy for Hepatic Encephalopathy","Self-Test Flashcards","Jaundice mechanics and LFT trends are covered in full on Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice","Hepatitis B and C are two of the causes of cirrhosis below — full detail on Viral Hepatitis"]},{"s":"Hepatitis vs. Cirrhosis — Reversible vs. Irreversible▾","a":"hepatitis-vs-cirrhosis-reversible-vs-irreversible","k":["Hepatitis vs. Cirrhosis — Reversible vs. Irreversible▾","The Key Distinction","Hepatitis = inflammation of the liver. That inflammation is reversible.","Cirrhosis = an irreversible, inflammatory, fibrotic liver disease — the liver becomes literally scarred (fibrotic). Once this stage is reached, the damage is permanent.","Cirrhosis has a high death rate — about 26%.","Typically develops slowly over years; the rate of progression depends on the cause and, for toxin-related cirrhosis (like alcohol), the amount of toxin present. Removing the toxin (e.g., stopping drinking) can slow further progression, but …"]},{"s":"Causes of Cirrhosis▾","a":"causes-of-cirrhosis","k":["Causes of Cirrhosis▾","Hepatitis B and C (chronic infection)","Excessive alcohol intake","Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) / non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH)","Autoimmune conditions","Hereditary metabolic conditions","Idiopathic (unknown cause)"]},{"s":"How Fibrosis Actually Develops▾","a":"how-fibrosis-actually-develops","k":["How Fibrosis Actually Develops▾","The Mechanism","Structural liver injury (from a toxin like alcohol, or a virus like hepatitis) triggers infiltration of white blood cells (leukocytes), which release inflammatory mediators and activate the fibrotic process.","Chaotic fibrosis throughout the liver obstructs biliary channels and blood flow — this is what causes both jaundice and, eventually, portal hypertension.","Hepatocytes can normally regenerate, but in cirrhosis that regeneration is disrupted by ongoing hypoxia and inflammation. Damaged cells try to regrow in a disorganized way, forming abnormal tissue with poor blood flow — which develops more …"]},{"s":"Alcoholic Liver Disease — A Spectrum▾","a":"alcoholic-liver-disease-a-spectrum","k":["Alcoholic Liver Disease — A Spectrum▾","Alcoholic cirrhosis is the single most common type of cirrhosis, though it still only accounts for about 25% of all cirrhosis cases overall. It progresses through three stages:","1. Alcoholic Fatty Liver — Mild, asymptomatic, and reversible if the person stops drinking. Alcohol is converted to acetaldehyde, which alters hepatocyte function and activates hepatic stellate cells (the primary cell involved in liver fibr…","2. Alcoholic Steatohepatitis — A precursor to cirrhosis. Increased hepatic fat storage, inflammation, and hepatocyte degeneration with white blood cell infiltration. This stage is irreversible — anorexia, nausea, jaundice, and edema can app…","3. Alcoholic Cirrhosis — Toxic effects of alcohol metabolism cause ongoing immunologic problems, inflammation, oxidative stress, and cellular damage — resulting in cellular necrosis, fibrosis, and permanent scarring of the liver structure.","Acetaldehyde also inhibits export proteins from the liver, altering vitamin and mineral metabolism and inducing malnutrition — a major reason patients with alcoholic liver disease are often malnourished. Activated Kupffer cells attract neut…"]},{"s":"Manifestations of Late-Stage Cirrhosis▾","a":"manifestations-of-late-stage-cirrhosis","k":["Manifestations of Late-Stage Cirrhosis▾","Cirrhosis is often hard to diagnose early — early signs are vague (nausea, vomiting, poor appetite, flatulence, changed bowel habits, weight loss, a possibly palpable liver). It's usually the late manifestations below that get it diagnosed.","Jaundice — Bilirubin build-up — see the Liver Function & Jaundice page.","Peripheral Edema & Ascites — The liver can't produce enough protein/albumin, so colloid oncotic pressure can't hold fluid in the vessels — fluid third-spaces into tissue (edema) and the peritoneal cavity (ascites). Ascites is also driven by…","Bleeding Tendency — The liver can't produce clotting factors (low albumin, prolonged PT). Portal hypertension also causes small veins to burst, worsening bleeding risk.","Spider Angiomata — Vascular skin lesions specific to cirrhosis and late-stage liver failure — small, spider-like clusters of blood vessels visible on the skin.","Endocrine Changes — Women can develop chronic anovulation/amenorrhea; men can develop hypogonadism, impotence, or infertility. Most common with alcoholic cirrhosis.","Esophageal & Anorectal Varices — Distended veins caused by portal hypertension. If they burst, the patient is actively bleeding with a high risk of mortality.","Hepatic Encephalopathy — The liver can no longer detoxify the blood — see below.","MNEMONIC: Every late-stage cirrhosis finding traces back to one of the liver's five jobs failing (see the Liver Function page) — no albumin/clotting factors → edema/ascites/bleeding; no bilirubin clearance → jaundice; no detox → hepatic enc…"]},{"s":"Portal Hypertension▾","a":"portal-hypertension","k":["Portal Hypertension▾","The Mechanism","Normally, blood from the gut, spleen, and pancreas flows freely through the portal vein into the liver. Fibrosis blocks that free flow — resistant portal blood flow backs everything up.","Think of it like stuffing paper towels into a pipe — water can still get through, but it backs up and causes problems everywhere below the blockage.","Portal hypertension itself is asymptomatic until complications occur: variceal hemorrhage, ascites, translocation of gut bacteria (increasing peritonitis risk), hepatorenal syndrome (liver AND kidney failure together), and even heart failur…","Ascites is driven by a combination of low oncotic pressure, systemic hypotension, vascular underfilling, RAAS activation, and plasma volume expansion — all on top of the portal pressure itself.","Variceal hemorrhage: esophageal or anorectal varices that burst cause active, high-mortality-risk bleeding — a true emergency. There is no drug that reverses portal hypertension or fibrosis; management focuses on preventing and treating thi…"]},{"s":"Hepatic Encephalopathy▾","a":"hepatic-encephalopathy","k":["Hepatic Encephalopathy▾","Cause — The liver can no longer clear toxins from the blood. Ammonia — a neurotoxin normally cleared by the liver — crosses the blood-brain barrier and builds up in the brain.","Frequency — Occurs in about 30–45% of cirrhosis patients.","Diagnosis — Level of consciousness — NOT the ammonia lab value — is the primary driver of diagnosis. Ammonia correlates with and is a marker of the toxin build-up, but hepatic encephalopathy is never diagnosed on an ammonia level alone.","Presentation — Ranges from mild confusion and behavioral changes to coma, unresponsive to pain. Graded by severity."]},{"s":"Acute (Fulminant) Liver Failure vs. Cirrhosis▾","a":"acute-fulminant-liver-failure-vs-cirrhosis","k":["Acute (Fulminant) Liver Failure vs. Cirrhosis▾","Cirrhosis","Slow, insidious onset over years.","Caused by chronic fibrosis (alcohol, hepatitis, NAFLD, etc.).","Chronic, progressive liver failure.","Acute (Fulminant) Liver Failure","Sudden onset — NOT related to fibrosis/cirrhosis.","Most often caused by acetaminophen (Tylenol) overdose; can also follow severe viral hepatitis or metabolic liver disease.","Can occur 5 days to 8 weeks after an acetaminophen overdose, or 6–8 weeks after viral hepatitis.","Edematous hepatocytes with patchy necrosis and inflammatory infiltrates acutely disrupt liver tissue.","The signs and symptoms of acute liver failure and late-stage cirrhosis look the same clinically — the difference is the underlying cellular timeline (sudden vs. chronic) and cause, not the presentation. The antidote for an acetaminophen ove…"]},{"s":"Drug Therapy for Hepatic Encephalopathy▾","a":"drug-therapy-for-hepatic-encephalopathy","k":["Drug Therapy for Hepatic Encephalopathy▾","There is no drug for ascites and no drug that reverses cirrhosis — these two drugs exist specifically to treat hepatic encephalopathy.","Lactulose — First-line. A lactose derivative that isn't digested/absorbed in the stomach or small intestine — it travels to the colon, where bacteria metabolize it into lactic, formic, and acetic acids. These acids create a hyperosmotic, ac…","Rifaximin — Second-line, used if lactulose isn't working. Originally a GI-infection antibiotic; works by inhibiting bacterial RNA synthesis (binds bacterial DNA), which decreases the ammonia-producing gut bacteria. Can be used preventively.…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week4-gallbladder-pancreatitis.html","t":"Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 5","Cholelithiasis (Gallstones)","Biliary Colic & Symptoms of Obstructed Bile Flow","Cholecystitis","Gallbladder Disorders — Pharmacology","Acute Pancreatitis","Clinical Presentation of Acute Pancreatitis","Complications of Acute Pancreatitis","Chronic Pancreatitis","Pancreatitis — Pharmacology","Self-Test Flashcards","These two organ systems are grouped together because they share the same root problem: a gallstone blocking the common bile duct can cause cholecystitis in the gallbladder AND back up pancreatic enzymes into the pancreas at the same time. U…"]},{"s":"Cholelithiasis (Gallstones)▾","a":"cholelithiasis-gallstones","k":["Cholelithiasis (Gallstones)▾","Definition — Stones in the gallbladder — colloquially \"gallstones.\"","Why They Form — Impaired metabolism/breakdown of cholesterol, bilirubin, and bile acids — these components saturate in the gallbladder, sit there, and aggregate into stones.","Stone Types — Cholesterol stones (~70%, the most common); pigmented stones; mixed pigmented stones (black, hard, less common — composed of something other than mainly cholesterol).","Risk Factors","Obesity — a major risk factor.","Being female, middle-aged (most common around 40), and using oral contraceptives.","Rapid weight loss.","Genetic predisposition; higher rates in Indigenous populations (60–70% lifetime risk) than in white Americans (10–15%) — a very common condition in developed countries overall.","Pre-existing pancreatic disease.","MNEMONIC (the instructor's own, dated but memorable): \"Fair, fat, and 40\" — a classic (if old-fashioned) shorthand for the classic gallstone patient profile: female, overweight, and around age 40."]},{"s":"Biliary Colic & Symptoms of Obstructed Bile Flow▾","a":"biliary-colic-symptoms-of-obstructed-bile-flow","k":["Biliary Colic & Symptoms of Obstructed Bile Flow▾","Biliary Colic","Most patients with stones confined to the gallbladder are asymptomatic. Pain starts when one or more stones lodge in the cystic or common duct during a gallbladder contraction.","Epigastric or right-upper-quadrant pain, often radiating to the back — usually severe, intermittent but can be steady.","Classically triggered by fatty food intake, occurring about 30 minutes to a couple of hours after a fatty meal (think: after a fast-food meal).","Other vague symptoms: heartburn, flatulence, epigastric discomfort, food intolerances (especially fats).","Jaundice (Obstructive) — A migrated stone blocks the common bile duct — bile can't flow into the duodenum.","Dark, Foamy Urine — Conjugated bilirubin backs up into the blood; being water-soluble, it spills into the urine.","Clay-Colored Stools — Bilirubin can't reach the intestines to become urobilinogen, the pigment that gives stool its normal brown color.","Steatorrhea — Bile salts can't reach the duodenum, so fat isn't broken down/digested — fatty, undigested stool results.","Pruritus — Backed-up bile salts deposit into the skin, causing itching.","Bleeding Tendency — Without bile, vitamin K (needed to produce clotting factors) can't be absorbed.","Not every patient with cholelithiasis develops these manifestations — they only appear if a stone actually blocks bile from reaching the GI system."]},{"s":"Cholecystitis▾","a":"cholecystitis","k":["Cholecystitis▾","What It Is","Inflammation of the gallbladder — usually caused by a gallstone lodged in the cystic duct, causing the gallbladder to become edematous, hyperemic, and distended, and over time to lose function. Can be acute or chronic.","Pain resembles biliary colic. It's more dangerous than simple cholelithiasis because pressure from the inflamed, distended gallbladder wall decreases blood flow — which can lead to ischemia, necrosis, and perforation if untreated. This is w…","Signs of inflammation: fever, leukocytosis, rebound tenderness, guarding (more specific to cholecystitis than plain cholelithiasis).","Labs: increased bilirubin, increased alkaline phosphatase.","Chronic, ongoing cholecystitis can lead to scarring and eventual degeneration of the gallbladder.","Cholecystitis can progress to ischemia, necrosis, and perforation if the inflammatory pressure against the gallbladder wall isn't relieved — this is why it's treated as a surgical priority."]},{"s":"Gallbladder Disorders — Pharmacology▾","a":"gallbladder-disorders-pharmacology","k":["Gallbladder Disorders — Pharmacology▾","IV Ketorolac (Toradol) — First-line pain control for both cholelithiasis and cholecystitis — an NSAID. Opioids (morphine, hydromorphone/Dilaudid) are used if pain isn't well controlled with this alone.","Antiemetics — Ondansetron (Zofran) or promethazine (Phenergan) for nausea/vomiting.","Anticholinergics — Decrease gallbladder secretion and stop smooth-muscle spasms — reduces the risk of lodging additional stones and causing more issues.","Bile Acid Therapy — Given to help dissolve stones — typically reserved for patients who can't have surgery. Not common.","The definitive treatment for both cholelithiasis and cholecystitis is usually gallbladder removal (cholecystectomy); medications mainly manage symptoms and risk factors in the meantime."]},{"s":"Acute Pancreatitis▾","a":"acute-pancreatitis","k":["Acute Pancreatitis▾","What It Is","Inflammation of the pancreas. Most cases are mild and resolve spontaneously, but about 20% become severe enough to require hospitalization.","Most often caused by obstruction of pancreatic enzyme outflow — usually from a gallstone blocking the common bile duct, which the pancreas also uses to secrete its exocrine chemicals. Also caused by alcohol, certain medications/drugs, and s…","When pancreatic enzymes can't get out, they auto-digest the pancreas itself, causing vascular damage, coagulopathy, necrosis, and fat necrosis.","Trypsin — Edema, necrosis, hemorrhage.","Elastase — Hemorrhage.","Phospholipase A — Fat necrosis within the pancreas.","Kallikrein — Edema and increased vascular permeability (fluid leaks into the pancreas and surrounding space — can cause ascites); also triggers smooth-muscle contraction, which causes pain and can contribute to shock in severe cases.","Lipase — Fat necrosis — its normal job is fat breakdown, so trapped in the pancreas it breaks down pancreatic cells instead."]},{"s":"Clinical Presentation of Acute Pancreatitis▾","a":"clinical-presentation-of-acute-pancreatitis","k":["Clinical Presentation of Acute Pancreatitis▾","What These Patients Look Like","Pain is the hallmark — severe, sudden-onset epigastric or mid-abdominal pain, often radiating to the back. The abdomen is tender with guarding; patients resist palpation. Pain comes from edema-driven distension of the pancreatic ducts/capsu…","Nausea/vomiting, abdominal distension (fluid seeping into the peritoneal cavity), fever, and hypoactive bowel sounds.","In severe cases: hypotension and tachycardia (from vascular permeability/fluid loss), possibly progressing to hypovolemic shock.","Jaundice if a blocked common bile duct is the underlying cause.","Labs: amylase rises with organ damage; glucose can be elevated OR low depending on which pancreatic cells are damaged (monitor closely); elevated WBC count (inflammatory state).","MNEMONIC (the instructor's own): Gray Turner sign (bruising in the flank) and Cullen sign (bruising around the umbilicus/belly button) both indicate hemorrhagic pancreatitis. To remember Cullen = belly button: think of vampires — they don't…"]},{"s":"Complications of Acute Pancreatitis▾","a":"complications-of-acute-pancreatitis","k":["Complications of Acute Pancreatitis▾","Pseudocyst","A fluid-filled cavity that forms OUTSIDE the pancreas, visible on CT.","Contains necrotic products and secretions — creates more inflammation and scarring.","Can sometimes be palpated as an epigastric mass.","If it perforates, it spills necrotic contents into the peritoneal cavity, causing peritonitis — may need surgical intervention.","Pancreatic Abscess","A large fluid-filled cavity INSIDE the pancreas — the result of extensive necrosis (usually with necrotic hemorrhagic pancreatitis).","Can result in further infection or perforation and peritonitis.","Presents with the usual pancreatitis picture plus an abdominal mass, very high fever, and leukocytosis (infection).","Other Complications","Pulmonary: pleural effusion (from vascular permeability/edema) and diaphragmatic irritation, both worsened by patients avoiding deep breaths due to pain.","Hypovolemic shock (from vascular permeability and fluid loss).","Tetany from hypocalcemia — lipase-generated free fatty acids bind calcium and deposit it in the retroperitoneum, dropping serum calcium. Monitor electrolyte status."]},{"s":"Chronic Pancreatitis▾","a":"chronic-pancreatitis","k":["Chronic Pancreatitis▾","Definition — A progressive, fibrotic disease of the pancreas. Unlike acute pancreatitis (which resolves once a gallstone is removed or an offending medication is stopped), chronic pancreatitis does not resolve.","Most Common Cause — Alcohol abuse — alcohol is processed by acinar cells in the pancreas; when these stop working, a toxic metabolite forms that destroys pancreatic tissue and releases inflammatory cytokines, damaging both the acinar cells …","Presentation — Recurring attacks of acute pancreatitis (severe, sudden-onset pain) plus ongoing loss of normal pancreatic function between attacks. Pain is the biggest reported symptom — a chronic pain condition that's very hard to treat.","Other Manifestations — Weight loss (from both pain and fat malabsorption — the enzymes needed to digest fat are missing); diabetes, from loss of the islet cells that manage insulin/glucose.","Diabetes mellitus itself — a disorder of the pancreas's endocrine function — is covered in full on Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome; this page covers the pancreas's exocrine (digestive enzyme) dysfunction"]},{"s":"Pancreatitis — Pharmacology▾","a":"pancreatitis-pharmacology","k":["Pancreatitis — Pharmacology▾","Opioids (Hydromorphone or Morphine) — First-line pain control for acute/chronic pancreatitis.","Dicyclomine — An anticholinergic antispasmodic — reduces secretions and relaxes smooth muscle to decrease duct spasms (also used for cholecystitis, above).","Antacids & H2 Receptor Blockers — Both decrease hydrochloric acid secretion in the stomach, which in turn decreases pancreatic enzyme secretion — reducing the auto-digestion driving the disease.","Pancrelipase — Pancreatic enzyme replacement for CHRONIC pancreatitis — replaces the digestive enzymes patients no longer produce. Must be taken with every single meal and snack. No significant adverse effects, but adherence (taking it ever…","Patients who lose islet cell function from chronic pancreatitis may also need insulin or glucagon, depending on their blood sugar."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week4-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 4","Two Clusters, Not Four Separate Topics","The Four Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new — it's a map of Week 4's four topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. Three of the four pages are really one continuous story about the …"]},{"s":"Two Clusters, Not Four Separate Topics▾","a":"two-clusters-not-four-separate-topics","k":["Two Clusters, Not Four Separate Topics▾","Four pages is easier to hold in your head than six, but they still aren't four unrelated diseases — three of them are one progression, and the fourth is really two organs that fail together.","The Liver, Start to Finish — Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice → Viral Hepatitis → Cirrhosis & Liver Failure — These three build on each other in order: normal liver function and how bilirubin/jaundice work, then a viral cause of liver inf…","One Duct System, Two Organs — Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders — Gallstones are the thread — a stone can block the common bile duct and cause cholecystitis in the gallbladder, or back up pancreatic enzymes and trigger acute pancreatitis, …","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, work through the liver cluster in order first — each page assumes you already know the one before it — then treat gallbladder/pancreas as its own self-contained unit."]},{"s":"The Four Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-four-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Four Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice — The liver does five jobs (metabolize/store, blood reservoir, blood filter/bilirubin removal, make clotting factors, detoxify drugs) — nearly every finding on the rest of this week's pages is one of t…","2 — Viral Hepatitis (A, B & C) — A virus inflames the liver through three predictable phases (prodromal, icteric, recovery); hepatitis A spreads fecal-orally and never goes chronic, while B and C spread through blood/body fluids and go chro…","3 — Cirrhosis & Liver Failure — Once inflammation becomes fibrotic scarring, the damage is permanent — alcoholic liver disease is the clearest example, moving through three stages from reversible fatty liver to irreversible cirrhosis. Porta…","4 — Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders — Gallstones cause both cholecystitis (gallbladder inflammation that can progress to ischemia and perforation) and most cases of acute pancreatitis (trapped enzymes auto-digest the pancreas). Chronic p…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content — each idea is fully taught in exactly one place. But they resurface as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why.","The liver's five jobs — Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice — Cirrhosis's late-stage findings mnemonic ties every symptom (edema, bleeding, jaundice, encephalopathy) straight back to one of these five jobs failing.","Conjugated vs. unconjugated bilirubin / jaundice mechanics — Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice — Viral Hepatitis's icteric phase, Cirrhosis's late-stage jaundice finding, and Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders' obstructive-jaundice symptom…","Bleeding tendency from clotting-factor loss — Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice (prolonged PT) — Cirrhosis's late-stage manifestations (low albumin/PT, worsened by portal hypertension), and Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders' obstructed-bi…","Portal circulation — Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice (the basic pathway and the first-pass effect) — Cirrhosis's portal hypertension section, where fibrosis blocks that same pathway and backs pressure up into the varices, ascites, and ot…","Gallstones (cholelithiasis) — Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders — Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice's obstructive-jaundice classification names a blocked bile duct — usually a gallstone — as its cause, without re-explaining how the stone …","First-pass effect / drug metabolism — Pharmacology Foundations (Pathopharm Review) — Mentioned on Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice's portal circulation section as the reason oral and IV doses differ, without re-teaching the mechanism.","Exocrine vs. endocrine pancreatic dysfunction — Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders (exocrine — digestive enzymes) — Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome (Pathopharm Review) covers the endocrine side (islet cell loss causing diabetes) in full, so i…"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","Every danger callout across the four pages, condensed to a phrase. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation — click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","Viral Hepatitis — Acetaminophen (Tylenol) is still allowed with active or chronic hepatitis, but the daily limit drops to under 2 grams/day (versus the usual 4 g/day) — avoid it entirely in serious, advanced liver disease.","Cirrhosis & Liver Failure — Variceal hemorrhage — a burst esophageal or anorectal varix from portal hypertension — is a true, high-mortality emergency with no drug that reverses the underlying portal hypertension or fibrosis itself.","Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders — Cholecystitis can progress to ischemia, necrosis, and perforation if the inflammatory pressure on the gallbladder wall isn't relieved — this is why it's treated as a surgical priority, not just managed w…","Patterns you'll notice across these rules","\"Reversible until it isn't.\" Hepatitis inflammation, alcoholic fatty liver, and biliary colic are all still reversible stages. Cirrhosis, alcoholic steatohepatitis, and established cholecystitis are not — the whole week is organized around …","\"Trace every finding back to a job that stopped happening.\" The liver's five jobs and the pancreas's enzyme list both work the same way: pick the abnormal lab or symptom, ask which specific job or enzyme is responsible, and the \"why\" falls …","\"One blocked duct causes disease in two directions.\" A gallstone in the common bile duct backs bile up into the liver (jaundice) and pancreatic enzymes up into the pancreas (pancreatitis) at the same time — it's the same mechanical problem …","\"The safe dose changes when the organ is already hurt.\" Acetaminophen with hepatitis and vitamin-K-dependent clotting with obstructed bile flow are both reminders that a liver already working overtime can't handle what it normally could.","\"A normal-sounding lab isn't reassuring on its own.\" LFT numbers don't track severity, ammonia doesn't diagnose hepatic encephalopathy by itself (level of consciousness does), and amylase/lipase rise only after damage is already underway — …"]}]},{"f":"week4-comprehensive-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 4 · Topic 1","Part 1 — Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice","Part 2 — Viral Hepatitis (A, B & C)","Part 3 — Cirrhosis & Liver Failure","Part 4 — Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 4 — liver anatomy/function & jaundice, viral hepatitis, cirrhosis & liver failure, and gallbladder & pancreatic disorders — all in one place. It intentionally repeats t…"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice▾","a":"part-1-liver-anatomy-function-jaundice","k":["Part 1 — Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice▾","Liver Anatomy & Key Cells","Size & Location — The largest organ in the body — about 3 pounds, receiving 5–7% of total cardiac output. Sits in the right epigastric region, enclosed in a fibrous capsule, divided into left and right lobes.","Lobules — The functional units of the liver — hepatocytes arranged around a central vein.","Hepatocytes — The liver's main working cells. One of the few cell types in the body that can regenerate.","Kupffer Cells — Line the liver's inner capillaries (sinusoids) — remove bacteria and toxins from the blood.","What the Liver Actually Does","The Liver's Five Jobs","Metabolizes and stores fat, carbohydrate, protein, vitamins, and minerals.","Blood volume reservoir — distends and compresses to hold or release blood based on circulating volume.","Blood filter — Kupffer cells purify the blood; the liver also removes bilirubin, a byproduct of hemoglobin breakdown.","Makes clotting factors, including prothrombin and fibrinogen.","Metabolizes and detoxifies drugs.","MNEMONIC: When the liver fails, work backward through its five jobs to predict the labs — no metabolism (glucose/protein problems), no reservoir/filter function (bilirubin and toxins build up), no clotting factors (bleeding), no drug metabo…","Portal Circulation","The hepatic portal vein carries deoxygenated blood to the liver from the stomach, intestines, spleen, and pancreas.","This blood is rich in nutrients absorbed from the intestinal tract — exactly why digested nutrients go straight to the liver before reaching the rest of the body.","This is also where the first-pass effect comes from — an oral drug passes through the portal circulation and liver before reaching systemic circulation, which is why oral doses are often higher than IV doses of the same drug.","First-pass effect and drug metabolism basics are covered in full on Pharmacology Foundations","Liver Function Tests (LFTs)","AST, ALT & Alk Phos (\"liver enzymes\") — Released when liver cells are damaged. — Elevated — but the exact number isn't a reliable severity indicator.","Serum Ammonia — The liver normally breaks ammonia down. — Elevated — the key driver of hepatic encephalopathy (Part 3).","Total Protein & Albumin — The liver manufactures these proteins. — Decreased — less oncotic pressure drives edema and ascites.","Prothrombin Time (PT) — The liver makes prothrombin, a clotting factor. — Prolonged — patients bleed more easily.","Jaundice (Icterus)","Jaundice and icterus are the same thing — a yellowish discoloration from elevated bilirubin, usually visible around 2–2.5 mg/dL (normal roughly 0.6–1 mg/dL).","Conjugated (Direct) — ~30%","Already processed by the liver.","Elevated when it can't get OUT (bile duct obstruction, e.g. gallstones).","Unconjugated (Indirect) — ~70%","Not yet processed.","Elevated with overproduction (hemolysis) OR impaired liver function.","Hemolytic Jaundice — Excess RBC breakdown. — Unconjugated","Hepatocellular Jaundice — The liver itself can't take up/conjugate bilirubin. — Unconjugated","Obstructive Jaundice — Bile can't flow OUT — usually gallstones. — Conjugated","Recognizing Jaundice at the Bedside","The sclera of the eyes is the best place to look, especially in a darker-skinned patient.","Dark, foamy urine (conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble); clay-colored stool if bile flow is obstructed.","Pruritus from backed-up bile salts depositing in the skin."]},{"s":"Part 2 — Viral Hepatitis (A, B & C)▾","a":"part-2-viral-hepatitis-a-b-c","k":["Part 2 — Viral Hepatitis (A, B & C)▾","The Basics & Pathogenesis","A systemic virus that mainly inflames the liver. This course focuses on hepatitis A, B, and C (HAV/HBV/HCV).","Viral infection triggers an immune response — inflammatory mediators cause infected cells to lyse, causing edema and swelling, which drives tissue hypoxia and, if severe, hepatocyte death.","Many cases are asymptomatic. When symptoms occur, they progress the same way regardless of strain — only transmission route, chronicity risk, and treatment differ.","Prodromal — ~2 weeks after exposure — Nonspecific symptoms (fatigue, anorexia, malaise, nausea, headache); most transmissible phase.","Icteric — 1–2 weeks after prodromal; lasts 2–6 weeks — Jaundice, dark urine, possibly clay stools, enlarged tender liver.","Recovery — 2–12 weeks after jaundice onset — Symptoms diminish; liver can stay enlarged/tender even as symptoms fade.","Possible Complications","Chronic hepatitis — the virus never fully clears.","Cirrhosis — permanent scarring (Part 3).","Liver cancer — B and C are both linked to hepatocellular carcinoma.","Fulminant viral hepatitis — a severe case progressing to acute liver failure.","Transmission — Fecal-oral — IV drug use, sexual contact — Same as B; also mother-to-fetal","Onset — Acute, mild — Insidious, 60–180 days — Insidious, more symptomatic","Chronic Risk — Never — ~10% — ~80%","Vaccine — Yes (2 doses) — Yes (3-dose series) — None","MNEMONIC: Hepatitis A is the \"food and water\" one (fecal-oral, acute, never chronic, has a vaccine). B and C are \"blood and body fluid\" ones (needles/sex, insidious, can go chronic) — B about 10% chronic, C about 80%.","Hepatitis B (high-risk, chronic) — Interferons + nucleoside analogs (entecavir, tenofovir); prolonged, expensive, high relapse rate — no great treatment exists.","Hepatitis C — Direct-acting antivirals (DAAs) — can essentially eliminate the virus in most patients.","Acetaminophen with hepatitis: stay under 2 g/day (versus the usual 4 g/day) — avoid entirely in serious, advanced liver disease."]},{"s":"Part 3 — Cirrhosis & Liver Failure▾","a":"part-3-cirrhosis-liver-failure","k":["Part 3 — Cirrhosis & Liver Failure▾","Hepatitis vs. Cirrhosis","Hepatitis = inflammation — reversible.","Cirrhosis = irreversible fibrotic scarring, about 26% mortality, develops slowly over years.","Hepatitis B and C","Excessive alcohol intake","NAFLD / NASH","Autoimmune conditions, hereditary metabolic conditions, idiopathic","Fibrosis develops when structural injury (toxin or virus) triggers leukocyte infiltration and inflammatory mediators; chaotic fibrosis obstructs biliary channels and blood flow (jaundice, portal hypertension), and disrupted hepatocyte regen…","1. Alcoholic Fatty Liver — Mild, asymptomatic, reversible if drinking stops.","2. Alcoholic Steatohepatitis — Irreversible — anorexia, nausea, jaundice, edema can appear.","3. Alcoholic Cirrhosis — Necrosis, fibrosis, permanent scarring.","Jaundice — Bilirubin build-up.","Peripheral Edema & Ascites — Low albumin → low oncotic pressure; worsened by portal hypertension.","Bleeding Tendency — No clotting factors; portal hypertension bursts small veins too.","Spider Angiomata — Vascular skin lesions specific to cirrhosis.","Esophageal & Anorectal Varices — Distended veins from portal hypertension — can rupture.","Hepatic Encephalopathy — Loss of detox function — see below.","MNEMONIC: every late-stage cirrhosis finding traces back to one of the liver's five jobs failing — no albumin/clotting → edema/ascites/bleeding; no bilirubin clearance → jaundice; no detox → hepatic encephalopathy; and portal hypertension a…","Portal hypertension: fibrosis blocks free portal blood flow — like paper towels stuffed in a pipe. Asymptomatic until complications: variceal hemorrhage, ascites, gut bacteria translocation/peritonitis risk, hepatorenal syndrome, heart fail…","Variceal hemorrhage: a burst esophageal or anorectal varix is a true, high-mortality emergency — no drug reverses the underlying portal hypertension or fibrosis.","Hepatic Encephalopathy — Ammonia crosses the blood-brain barrier and builds up. Diagnosed by level of consciousness, never by ammonia level alone. Occurs in ~30–45% of cirrhosis patients.","Cirrhosis","Slow, insidious onset over years.","Chronic fibrosis (alcohol, hepatitis, NAFLD).","Acute (Fulminant) Liver Failure","Sudden onset, not related to fibrosis.","Most often acetaminophen overdose; antidote is acetylcysteine.","Lactulose (first-line) — Colonic bacteria metabolize it into acids that draw water in (stooling) and trap ammonia as ammonium for excretion. Monitor potassium.","Rifaximin (second-line) — Reduces ammonia-producing gut bacteria. Watch for C. diff."]},{"s":"Part 4 — Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders▾","a":"part-4-gallbladder-pancreatic-disorders","k":["Part 4 — Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders▾","These two organ systems share a root problem: a gallstone blocking the common bile duct can cause cholecystitis in the gallbladder AND back up pancreatic enzymes into the pancreas at the same time.","Cholelithiasis (Gallstones)","Why They Form — Impaired metabolism of cholesterol, bilirubin, and bile acids — they saturate and aggregate into stones.","Stone Types — Cholesterol (~70%, most common); pigmented; mixed pigmented (black, hard).","MNEMONIC: \"Fair, fat, and 40\" — the classic gallstone patient profile.","Biliary Colic","Epigastric/RUQ pain radiating to the back, classically 30 minutes to a couple hours after a fatty meal.","Most gallstones confined to the gallbladder are asymptomatic — pain starts when a stone lodges in the cystic or common duct.","Jaundice — Stone blocks the common bile duct.","Dark, Foamy Urine — Conjugated bilirubin backs up into blood and urine.","Clay-Colored Stools — No bilirubin reaching the intestines.","Steatorrhea — No bile salts reaching the duodenum — fat malabsorption.","Bleeding Tendency — No vitamin K absorption without bile.","Cholecystitis","Gallbladder inflammation, usually from a lodged stone — edematous, hyperemic, distended gallbladder.","Fever, leukocytosis, rebound tenderness, guarding; increased bilirubin and alk phos.","Cholecystitis can progress to ischemia, necrosis, and perforation if wall pressure isn't relieved — a surgical priority.","IV Ketorolac (Toradol) — First-line pain control (NSAID); opioids added if needed.","Anticholinergics — Decrease gallbladder secretion and smooth-muscle spasm.","Cholecystectomy — Definitive treatment for both cholelithiasis and cholecystitis.","Acute Pancreatitis","Most often caused by a gallstone obstructing the common bile duct, blocking pancreatic enzyme outflow — trapped enzymes auto-digest the pancreas.","Trypsin — Edema, necrosis, hemorrhage.","Elastase — Hemorrhage.","Phospholipase A — Fat necrosis.","Kallikrein — Edema, vascular permeability, pain, shock risk.","Lipase — Fat necrosis.","Clinical Presentation","Severe, sudden-onset epigastric/mid-abdominal pain radiating to the back, with guarding.","Nausea/vomiting, abdominal distension, fever; severe cases can progress to hypovolemic shock.","Amylase rises with organ damage; glucose can go either direction; elevated WBC.","MNEMONIC: Gray Turner sign (flank bruising) and Cullen sign (belly-button bruising) = hemorrhagic pancreatitis. Cullen = belly button: vampires (and \"the Cullens\") don't have belly buttons. Both are severe, uncommon — not required to diagno…","Pseudocyst","Fluid-filled cavity OUTSIDE the pancreas.","Can perforate and cause peritonitis.","Pancreatic Abscess","Infected fluid cavity INSIDE the pancreas.","Very high fever, leukocytosis.","Other complications: pleural effusion, hypovolemic shock, and tetany from hypocalcemia (lipase-generated free fatty acids bind calcium).","Chronic Pancreatitis","Most Common Cause — Alcohol abuse — damages both acinar cells (digestive enzymes) and islet cells (glucose/insulin).","Presentation — Recurring acute attacks plus ongoing pancreatic dysfunction between them; pain is the biggest complaint.","Other Manifestations — Weight loss (fat malabsorption); diabetes (islet cell loss).","Diabetes mellitus itself is covered in full on Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome; this page covers only the pancreas's exocrine dysfunction","Opioids — First-line pain control for acute/chronic pancreatitis.","Dicyclomine — Anticholinergic antispasmodic.","Pancrelipase — Enzyme replacement for chronic pancreatitis — must be taken with every meal/snack."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","A trick for tying the whole week together: three of Week 4's four topics have a single named emergency to watch for, and all three trace back to a structure rupturing or a barrier failing under pressure.","Liver Anatomy, Function & Jaundice — No single named emergency — this page is the foundation the other three build on.","Viral Hepatitis — Fulminant hepatitis progressing to acute liver failure.","Cirrhosis & Liver Failure — Variceal hemorrhage from ruptured esophageal/anorectal varices.","Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders — Cholecystitis progressing to ischemia/necrosis/perforation; hemorrhagic pancreatitis."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Three cards from each of the four parts above — a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week5-esophageal-gastric-disorders.html","t":"Esophageal & Gastric Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Esophageal & Gastric Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 5 · Topic 2","Dysphagia","GERD (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease)","Hiatal Hernia","Gastritis","Acute Gastroenteritis","Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD)","Pharmacology for GERD & Peptic Ulcer Disease","Self-Test Flashcards","These conditions are grouped together because they're all \"upper GI\" disorders — involving the esophagus and stomach — and they share overlapping symptoms (heartburn, epigastric pain, dysphagia) and overlapping pharmacologic treatments."]},{"s":"Dysphagia▾","a":"dysphagia","k":["Dysphagia▾","Dysphagia means difficulty swallowing. It typically affects swallowing solids first, then progresses to also affect liquids.","Mechanical Causes","Stenosis","Strictures","Diverticula","Tumors — physically block the esophagus","Neuromuscular Causes","Stroke — the esophagus loses proper innervation","Achalasia — the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) can't open properly","Patients who have been intubated or have a tracheostomy are also a common population with dysphagia; it can either resolve or progress. LES = lower esophageal sphincter, the sphincter connecting the esophagus to the stomach — this term recu…"]},{"s":"GERD (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease)▾","a":"gerd-gastroesophageal-reflux-disease","k":["GERD (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease)▾","In GERD, the LES doesn't close properly, allowing highly acidic stomach contents to reflux backward into the esophagus, causing heartburn. It occurs from things that either weaken LES closure strength or increase abdominal pressure.","Contributing Factors","Fatty foods","Spicy foods","Tomato-based foods","Citrus","Caffeine","Large amounts of alcohol","Cigarette smoking","Certain sleep positions","Obesity","Pregnancy","Certain pharmacologic agents","Pyrosis — The medical term for heartburn — a burning sensation.","Dyspepsia — Indigestion.","Regurgitation — Feeling like burping up hot, acidic material.","Chest pain — Can occur with reflux.","Dysphagia — Difficulty swallowing.","Pulmonary symptoms — Can occur with severe or chronic reflux.","If severe or chronic, GERD can also cause dental problems (tooth decay, gingivitis), ear problems, and throat problems (hoarseness, chronic sore throat, throat clearing, laryngitis).","Complications of Chronic GERD","Esophageal ulcerations","Scarring and strictures within the esophagus","Barrett's esophagus — the most severe complication; abnormal, metaplastic (precancerous) cell changes develop in the esophageal lining","Barrett's esophagus carries a threefold risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma, with very low survival if it develops — providers monitor for it in long-term GERD patients. There is no real pharmacologic treatment for existing strictures or Barr…"]},{"s":"Hiatal Hernia▾","a":"hiatal-hernia","k":["Hiatal Hernia▾","A hiatal hernia is a defect in the diaphragm that allows part of the stomach to pass upward into the thoracic (chest) cavity.","Sliding Hernia","The peritoneum stays intact, which restrains/limits the size of the hernia","Smaller, more common, and less severe","Paraesophageal (\"Rolling\") Hernia","Part of the stomach pushes through the diaphragm and stays there permanently, above the diaphragm","The peritoneal membrane becomes thin/defective, forming a true sac","More severe than a sliding hernia","A patient can also have a mixed type (sliding + paraesophageal together); the most complex/severe forms are classified as type 3 or type 4 hiatal hernia.","Associated Factors","Aging — the older you get, the more likely","Injury/damage that weakens the diaphragm muscles","Repeatedly putting too much pressure on the muscles around the stomach (chronic severe coughing, vomiting, constipation/straining to have a bowel movement)","Obesity","The cause is not fully understood, but age, obesity, and smoking are the high-risk factors overall.","Presentation","Many patients are asymptomatic.","Others have belching, dysphagia, or chest/epigastric pain that manifests just like GERD — because the LES sits above where it's supposed to be (weaker there), or with a paraesophageal hernia part of the stomach permanently sits above the di…","GERD and hiatal hernia commonly coexist in the same patient.","Treatment","Mostly conservative: small frequent meals, avoid lying down after eating","Avoid things that increase abdominal pressure (tight clothing, abdominal support)","Weight control for those who are obese","Pharmacologic therapy for GERD/esophagitis symptoms","Surgery is sometimes required when conservative treatment isn't well-adhered to or isn't effective"]},{"s":"Gastritis▾","a":"gastritis","k":["Gastritis▾","Gastritis is a non-specific inflammatory condition of the stomach — the \"-itis\" suffix means inflammation, a suffix worth watching for across this week's GI conditions.","Acute Gastritis","Temporary inflammation of the stomach lining only (intestines not affected)","Generally lasts 2 to 10 days","Related to irritating substances, drugs, or infectious agents: alcohol, NSAIDs, H. pylori, stomach viruses","Chronic Gastritis","A progressive disorder with chronic inflammation of the stomach lining","Can last weeks to years","Autoimmune (parietal cells attacked by the immune system) or caused by chronic H. pylori infection","Can lead to peptic ulcer disease, bleeding ulcers, anemia, and increased risk of gastric cancer","NSAIDs block prostaglandin synthesis; prostaglandins normally stimulate protective mucus secretion in the stomach and inhibit gastric acid production, so NSAIDs remove that protection and increase acid.","H. pylori (Helicobacter pylori) — A gram-negative spiral bacterium that thrives in an acidic environment. Initially often asymptomatic, but if it overgrows/overtakes normal gut flora it can cause chronic gastritis and peptic ulcer disease, …","Symptoms (Acute or Chronic)","Vomiting","Epigastric/stomach burning","Upset stomach","Loss of appetite","Severe cases: vomiting blood or black stool","Classic finding: postprandial discomfort — symptoms appear soon after eating"]},{"s":"Acute Gastroenteritis▾","a":"acute-gastroenteritis","k":["Acute Gastroenteritis▾","Acute gastroenteritis involves inflammation of both the stomach and the small intestine — unlike gastritis, which is stomach only.","Causes","Stomach viruses (norovirus, rotavirus) — the classic \"stomach flu\"/food-poisoning picture","Bacterial infections (E. coli, Salmonella, Campylobacter)","Parasitic infections","It usually lasts 1 to 3 days, but can last as long as 10 days.","Symptoms","Watery diarrhea (possibly bloody if bacterial)","Abdominal pain","Nausea and vomiting","Fever","Malaise","The biggest concern is fluid volume deficit/dehydration, since the patient is losing fluid from both ends.","Acute gastroenteritis is usually managed supportively — antibiotics/antivirals aren't typically needed, and antivirals wouldn't work anyway. If symptoms last more than 48–72 hours, the patient should see a provider to prevent dehydration, a…"]},{"s":"Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD)▾","a":"peptic-ulcer-disease-pud","k":["Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD)▾","PUD is a disorder involving ulceration of the upper GI tract: it can occur in the esophagus (esophageal ulcer), the stomach (gastric ulcer), or the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine (a duodenal ulcer, technically a type of pep…","Aggressive Factors","H. pylori infection","NSAID use","Excess gastric acid/pepsin secretion","Smoking","Protective/Defensive Factors","Mucus secretion","Bicarbonate secretion","Adequate blood flow","Prostaglandins","PUD develops when aggressive factors overwhelm the body's defenses.","Risk Factors","H. pylori infection — a major risk factor","Frequent/chronic NSAID or aspirin use","Heavy alcohol use","Family history","Stress does NOT directly cause peptic ulcers — this outdated belief has been re-thought. However, stress can definitely worsen an existing ulcer, because the stress response (HPA axis / sympathetic nervous system activation) increases gastr…","Pathophysiology","Mucosal damage (from acid or NSAIDs) triggers an immune response","Histamine is secreted, which increases acid and pepsin secretion, causing further damage","This causes vasodilation (bringing more white blood cells and blood flow to promote healing), which also causes edema","If blood vessels near the ulcer are destroyed by the acid/pepsin damage, this causes bleeding ulcers","Duodenal ulcer — The most common location for peptic ulcers overall. Can occur at any age but often in earlier adulthood.","Gastric ulcer — Tends to occur later in life (around age 50–70), more associated with chronic risk factors — long-term NSAID use, chronic corticosteroid use, anticoagulant use, or other serious systemic illness.","Gastric Ulcer Pain","Burning, cramping, or gas-like pain in the epigastrium or back","Occurs 1–2 hours after eating, as the stomach empties and the ulcer becomes more irritated","Duodenal Ulcer Pain","Occurs 2–4 hours after eating, once food has passed through to that area","MNEMONIC — HOP (Complications of PUD): Hemorrhage (the stomach and intestines are highly vascularized, so bleeding can be severe), Obstruction (ulcer scar tissue and strictures can bond/connect areas and obstruct the intestines), Perforatio…"]},{"s":"Pharmacology for GERD & Peptic Ulcer Disease▾","a":"pharmacology-for-gerd-peptic-ulcer-disease","k":["Pharmacology for GERD & Peptic Ulcer Disease▾","Treatment works by either decreasing aggressive factors or increasing protective factors.","Decrease Aggressive Factors","H. pylori treatment (antibiotics + gastric acid inhibitor)","H2 receptor blockers","Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs)","Increase Protective Factors","Sucralfate","Antacids","H. pylori treatment — Combination antibiotic therapy plus a gastric acid inhibitor, given over about 10–14 days. A confirmed H. pylori test (a breath test) is always done before treating, to avoid unnecessary antibiotic use/resistance. This…","H2 receptor blockers (H2 antagonists) — Block histamine (H2) receptors in the stomach's parietal cells, reducing gastric acid secretion by about 60–70% and increasing stomach pH. Key drugs: famotidine (most commonly used now) and cimetidine…","Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) — The \"-prazole\" drugs: omeprazole, pantoprazole, esomeprazole. Bind to and irreversibly inhibit the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme (the \"proton pump\"), blocking gastric acid secretion. Work better than H2 blockers. Avail…","Sucralfate — A mucosal protectant — sucrose-based with aluminum hydroxide; forms a sticky, thick protective gel/barrier in the stomach. FDA-approved for duodenal ulcers, and also helps gastric ulcers and chronic gastritis. Given PO only (ta…","Antacids — Neutralize stomach acid by about 50%. Used for PUD and GERD symptoms, stress ulcers, heartburn, and indigestion (though some patients get no relief). There is often an intense rebound effect — heartburn returns rapidly and severe…","H2 Blockers — Safety Notes","Generally very well tolerated","Can cause CNS/alertness changes in elderly patients","Give IV slowly — can cause bradycardia","Cimetidine is a CYP450 inhibitor with significant drug interactions (warfarin, phenytoin, theophylline)","Famotidine has much less of this interaction risk, which is why famotidine is generally preferred over cimetidine","PPIs — Long-Term Use Considerations","Short-term use is relatively safe","Long-term use (years) has been linked to increased risk of pneumonia, bone loss/hip fractures, and stomach cancer","For patients with real PUD or Barrett's esophagus risk, providers often judge the benefits to outweigh these risks and keep patients on PPIs long-term with monitoring","Sucralfate can decrease absorption of other drugs, so other medications should be taken separately, about 2 hours apart. Antacids also cause chelation with other drugs (binding/interfering with their absorption), so they should be separated…","Aluminum-based — Constipation","Calcium-based (e.g., Tums) — Constipation","Magnesium-based — Diarrhea","Combined aluminum + magnesium — More balanced, fewer side effects"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week5-lower-gi-disorders.html","t":"Lower GI Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Lower GI Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 5 · Topic 3","Appendicitis","Peritonitis","Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS)","Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD): Crohn's Disease & Ulcerative Colitis","Diverticular Disease","Pharmacology for Inflammatory Bowel Disease","Self-Test Flashcards","These conditions are grouped together as \"lower GI\" disorders because they all involve the small intestine and below — as opposed to the esophagus and stomach, which are covered on the companion \"Esophageal & Gastric Disorders\" page."]},{"s":"Appendicitis▾","a":"appendicitis","k":["Appendicitis▾","Overview","Inflammation of the appendix (a small appendage organ) that becomes obstructed, leading to inflammation.","The inflammation/obstruction itself isn't the biggest concern — it's the potential complications: the appendix can rupture, become gangrenous, form an abscess, and ultimately cause peritonitis.","Classic pain: right lower quadrant (RLQ) and periumbilical area.","Rebound tenderness (rebound pain): pain is severe after a provider pushes down in the RLQ and then releases — that release pain is rebound pain.","Sudden, complete relief of severe pain is NOT a good sign. If a patient's severe appendicitis pain suddenly and completely dissipates, it can indicate the appendix has ruptured, putting the patient at very high risk for peritonitis.","Manifestations & Diagnosis","Peak incidence age 10–12 years old.","Usually begins as dull, steady pain that progresses quickly over 4–6 hours, localizing to the RLQ.","Fever, nausea, and anorexia are common.","Diagnosed through clinical signs/symptoms, ultrasound (to see if the appendix is inflamed), and sometimes exploratory laparotomy.","Look for rebound pain/tenderness at the RLQ."]},{"s":"Peritonitis▾","a":"peritonitis","k":["Peritonitis▾","Overview","Inflammation of the peritoneum, the serous membrane that lines the abdominal cavity and covers the visceral organs — a protective barrier keeping gut contents contained and keeping what doesn't belong in the gut, out.","One of the biggest concerns with all abdominal conditions.","Pathophysiology","Inflammation leads to fluid shifts known as third spacing, which can lead to deadly complications like hypovolemic shock and sepsis.","Also causes decreased peristalsis (the gut slows down and can't digest food), which can lead to paralytic ileus and intestinal obstruction.","Patients with peritonitis can become very sick very quickly.","Causes","Perforated gastric or duodenal ulcers.","Ruptured gallbladder.","Pancreatitis.","Ruptured spleen.","Ruptured bladder.","Ruptured appendix.","Notice the theme — rupture or perforation, where contents that are supposed to stay inside the gut/organ get outside of it, causing inflammation of the peritoneum.","Presentation","Sudden, severe abdominal pain — the patient can't move, can't talk, has trouble breathing — with tenderness.","The biggest clinical sign is a rigid, board-like abdomen — this is different from a distended abdomen; it's hard to the touch.","Nausea and vomiting, fever, elevated white blood cell count.","Vital sign changes: increased heart rate and decreased blood pressure — from sympathetic nervous system activation due to pain, but mostly related to the fluid shifts peritonitis causes.","Treatment","Difficult to treat — anti-inflammatories can help, but the priority is fixing whatever caused it, which then decreases the inflammation.","Pancreatitis and ruptured gallbladder are two of the most common causes of peritonitis — both are covered in full on Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders (Week 4); this page does not re-teach them"]},{"s":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS)▾","a":"irritable-bowel-syndrome-ibs","k":["Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS)▾","Overview","A chronic condition characterized by alterations in bowel pattern due to changes in intestinal motility (of the small and large intestines).","Usually associated with either chronic/frequent constipation (IBS-C) or chronic/frequent diarrhea (IBS-D).","Symptoms","Abdominal distension, fullness, excessive flatus/bloating.","Intermittent abdominal pain that is exacerbated by stress and relieved by defecation.","Bowel urgency.","Often intolerance to certain foods (sorbitol, lactose, or gluten).","Sometimes non-bloody stool containing excessive mucus.","For a long time, people with gluten intolerance/celiac-type reactions were simply diagnosed with IBS. Providers are getting better at distinguishing a true food allergy/intolerance from IBS, but when no specific cause can be identified and …","Stress Connection","There is a real connection between psychosocial stress and IBS, but it's almost never a primary psychological cause — emotional stress doesn't cause the illness, but it can exacerbate/worsen it (make symptoms more intense).","Conversely, IBS itself is stressful to live with and can cause its own emotional/psychological problems — it works both directions.","Cause","General cause is unknown, but thought to be triggered by stress, food, hormonal changes, GI infections, and (in women) menstrual cycle changes."]},{"s":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD): Crohn's Disease & Ulcerative Colitis▾","a":"inflammatory-bowel-disease-ibd-crohn-s-disease-ulcerative-colitis","k":["Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD): Crohn's Disease & Ulcerative Colitis▾","Overview","IBD is an umbrella term for two conditions: Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis.","Unlike IBS, IBD has better-understood pathophysiology and more well-defined treatment.","A group of life-changing chronic illnesses, both characterized by chronic inflammation of the intestines with periods of exacerbation and remission.","More common in women, Caucasians, people of Jewish descent, and smokers.","Generally thought of as autoimmune conditions activated by an infection.","Crohn's Disease","Affects the lymphoid tissue of the GI tract, which becomes blocked; the surrounding tissue becomes engorged and inflamed.","Causes deep linear fissures and ulcers to develop in patchy patterns along the bowel wall, called skip lesions, which often have a cobblestone appearance.","Transmural — affects all layers of the GI tract wall.","Complications: malnutrition, anemia, scar tissue and obstructions, fistulas (an abnormal connection between two structures that shouldn't be connected), and a much higher risk of cancer.","Presentation: crampy lower abdominal pain, often in the right lower quadrant; watery diarrhea; systemic symptoms (weight loss, fatigue, lack of appetite, fever); malabsorption of nutrients; a palpable abdominal mass, usually RLQ; higher ris…","Granulomas and skip lesions are specific hallmark findings.","Ulcerative Colitis","Inflammation of the mucosa only — NOT transmural — specifically of the rectum and colon (large intestine).","Usually develops in the third decade of life (Crohn's often manifests earlier).","More common in white people of European descent, especially the Ashkenazi Jewish population; occasionally seen in Black/African American patients; very rare in Asian populations.","Inflammation begins in the rectum and extends in a continuous segment up the colon (not patchy/skip-lesion like Crohn's) and may involve the entire colon.","Inflammation leads to large ulcerations, which lead to necrosis of the epithelial tissue lining the colon and cause crypt abscesses; the colon/rectum try to repair the damage with new granulation tissue, but this tissue is fragile and bleed…","Presentation: abdominal pain similar to Crohn's, but bloody diarrhea is very common — a feature not typically seen with Crohn's disease; same systemic symptoms (weight loss, fatigue, lack of appetite, fever).","Complications: hemorrhage (from fragile granulation tissue bleeding), perforation, a much higher risk of colon cancer (colorectal carcinoma) than Crohn's, malnutrition, anemia, strictures, high risk of rectal/colon/anal fissures and abscess…","Toxic megacolon is a rapid, life-threatening dilation of the large intestine — a serious complication of ulcerative colitis.","Both Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis put patients at high risk of VTE/DVT — the exact reason isn't fully understood, but it's a recognized risk factor either way.","Location — Can affect the small intestine and upper large intestine and can involve the rectum. — Primarily the colon and rectum specifically.","Layer of bowel wall affected — Transmural — all layers of the GI tract wall. — Mucosa only — not transmural.","Lesion pattern — Patchy skip lesions with a cobblestone appearance. — Continuous, beginning in the rectum and extending up the colon.","Hallmark diarrhea type — Watery diarrhea. — Bloody diarrhea.","Hallmark pathology finding — Granulomas and skip lesions. — Crypt abscesses and fragile granulation tissue.","Typical population — More common in women, Caucasians, people of Jewish descent, and smokers; often manifests earlier in life. — Third decade of life; white people of European descent, especially the Ashkenazi Jewish population.","Top complications — Malnutrition, anemia, scar tissue/obstructions, fistulas, higher cancer risk. — Hemorrhage, perforation, much higher colon cancer risk, toxic megacolon, liver disease."]},{"s":"Diverticular Disease▾","a":"diverticular-disease","k":["Diverticular Disease▾","Diverticulosis — The development of diverticula — small pouches in the lining of the colon that bulge outward through a weak spot in the colon wall. Can be congenital (present from birth) or acquired. Thought to be caused by a low-fiber die…","Diverticulitis — Occurs when one of the diverticula pouches becomes inflamed — usually because fecal material becomes lodged in the pouch.","Diverticulitis: Presentation","Abdominal pain usually in the left lower quadrant (LLQ) — note this is the opposite side from appendicitis's classic right lower quadrant pain.","Fever, increased white blood cell count.","Abnormal bowel patterns (constipation or diarrhea).","Sometimes passage of a large quantity of frank (bright red) blood.","May resolve spontaneously, but can cause perforation, peritonitis, and obstruction depending on how inflamed/how many diverticula are involved if they rupture."]},{"s":"Pharmacology for Inflammatory Bowel Disease▾","a":"pharmacology-for-inflammatory-bowel-disease","k":["Pharmacology for Inflammatory Bowel Disease▾","Treatment for IBD is still limited — there are really only two drug classes covered here.","5-Aminosalicylic Acid (5-ASA) Class","Sulfasalazine is the key drug to know. It's technically a sulfonamide antibiotic that, within the intestines, converts into two parts: 5-aminosalicylic acid (the active anti-inflammatory component that helps IBD) and sulfapyridine (doesn't …","Used for mild-to-moderate IBD.","Contraindicated in patients with a sulfa allergy (because it converts to sulfapyridine).","Use caution in patients with pre-existing anemia.","Side effects: nausea, fever, rash, headache, and hematologic disorders — this drug isn't ideal for patients with a lot of comorbidities/conditions.","Mesalamine is another drug in the same 5-ASA family that can be given instead.","DMARDs (Disease-Modifying Antirheumatic Drugs)","Increasingly the direction of IBD treatment (outside of diet modification and surgery), since IBD is an autoimmune condition.","Key drug to know: infliximab (brand name Remicade) — a monoclonal antibody that neutralizes TNF-alpha, an inflammatory mediator, essentially turning off/decreasing that inflammatory response.","Because DMARDs suppress the immune system, there's extensive pre-screening required before starting: patients need to be up to date on vaccines, have titers drawn, and be screened for TB/latent TB.","Main risks: increased risk of infection (immunosuppression), increased risk of cancer, and increased risk of heart failure.","Infliximab (and most DMARDs) is given through infusions — sometimes weekly, sometimes a couple times a week, sometimes monthly depending on the regimen.","Associated with infusion reactions and can cause neutropenia.","Requires therapeutic drug monitoring and biomarker monitoring — specifically CRP (C-reactive protein) for infliximab.","Often used in combination with other IBD medications."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week5-gi-pharmacology.html","t":"GI Symptom Pharmacology","secs":[{"s":"GI Symptom Pharmacology","a":"","k":["Week 5 · Topic 4","Antiemetics: Serotonin Blockers","Antiemetics: Antihistamines","Antiemetics: Dopamine Antagonists / Prokinetics","Extrapyramidal Symptoms (EPS)","Antidiarrheal Drug Therapy — A Quick Review","Self-Test Flashcards","These drug classes treat nausea/vomiting and diarrhea as symptoms that show up across many different GI conditions, rather than belonging to any single disease — that's why they get their own page here instead of being folded into one disor…"]},{"s":"Antiemetics: Serotonin Blockers▾","a":"antiemetics-serotonin-blockers","k":["Antiemetics: Serotonin Blockers▾","Ondansetron (Zofran) — A serotonin (5-HT3) receptor blocker. Blocks serotonin receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) in the brain, which affects the afferent vagal nerves in the stomach and small intestine. Can be given PO or IV.…","Adverse Reactions","Mild headache, diarrhea, or dizziness.","Some patients also experience constipation.","In general, ondansetron is very well tolerated.","Serotonin syndrome risk: because ondansetron affects serotonin, be aware of other serotonergic drugs — SSRIs, SNRIs, TCAs, MAOIs, buspirone, and tramadol. A single dose of ondansetron is generally fine, but if a patient is taking ondansetro…"]},{"s":"Antiemetics: Antihistamines▾","a":"antiemetics-antihistamines","k":["Antiemetics: Antihistamines▾","Dimenhydrinate (Dramamine) — H1 receptor blocker. Blocks H1 receptors specifically within the inner ear, which is why it's also commonly used for motion sickness and dizziness — that same mechanism also helps with nausea.","Hydroxyzine (Vistaril) — H1 receptor blocker. Same inner-ear H1 mechanism as dimenhydrinate, used for motion sickness, dizziness, and nausea.","These are H1 receptor blockers — not H2 receptor blockers. That's a common mix-up point worth calling out explicitly: H2 blockers are covered on the Esophageal & Gastric Disorders page for a completely different purpose.","Sedation and Anticholinergic Effects","Most of these drugs cause significant sedation and drowsiness, similar to other H1 blockers like diphenhydramine (Benadryl).","They also have anticholinergic effects.","Because of sedation and anticholinergic effects, use caution in the elderly — fall risk.","MNEMONIC: Anticholinergic toxidrome — \"Mad as a hatter\" (CNS/mental status changes), \"blind as a bat\" (blurred vision), \"dry as a bone\" (dry mucous membranes/secretions), \"red as a beet\" (flushing), \"hot as a hare\" (hyperthermia).","Never give hydroxyzine IV. It can be given IM, but IV administration can cause significant tissue damage, thrombosis, and gangrene."]},{"s":"Antiemetics: Dopamine Antagonists / Prokinetics▾","a":"antiemetics-dopamine-antagonists-prokinetics","k":["Antiemetics: Dopamine Antagonists / Prokinetics▾","Metoclopramide (Reglan) — Blocks dopamine receptors, increases the tone of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), and increases peristalsis in both the stomach and intestines. Can also sedate patients.","Antiemetic Use","Nausea/vomiting associated with chemotherapy, radiation, and opioids/opiates.","Prokinetic Use","GI motility issues in patients with decreased peristalsis — for example, patients with cystic fibrosis.","Patients waking up from a long surgery whose bowel sounds/peristalsis haven't returned yet — metoclopramide can help \"turn that peristalsis back on.\"","There is a rare but severe side effect of metoclopramide: extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS) — covered in the next section. EPS with metoclopramide is more common when it's given in combination with antipsychotic medications, or with regular/pro…"]},{"s":"Extrapyramidal Symptoms (EPS)▾","a":"extrapyramidal-symptoms-eps","k":["Extrapyramidal Symptoms (EPS)▾","EPS are drug-induced movement disorders, most classically associated with antipsychotic medications, but also seen with metoclopramide (especially with regular use or in combination with antipsychotics).","Akathisia — Restlessness — the patient feels a constant desire/urge to move.","Acute dystonia — Involuntary muscle contractions.","Parkinsonism — Symptoms that look and feel like Parkinson's disease — tremors and rigid muscles in the limbs are common symptoms.","Tardive dyskinesia — A late-onset EPS finding that develops with progression/prolonged exposure — repetitive, involuntary facial movements such as tongue twisting, chewing motions, lip smacking, cheek puffing, and grimacing.","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) is the worst-case EPS-related complication — very rare, but very life-threatening. The first signs are rigid muscles and fever, followed by drowsiness and confusion, which can ultimately progress to seiz…"]},{"s":"Antidiarrheal Drug Therapy — A Quick Review▾","a":"antidiarrheal-drug-therapy-a-quick-review","k":["Antidiarrheal Drug Therapy — A Quick Review▾","Mechanism","These drugs work by decreasing intestinal peristalsis, which reduces intestinal fluid output/effluent — essentially slowing the gut down or drawing water out.","Main Concerns","Drowsiness.","Constipation.","Antidiarrheal combination drugs that contain atropine can cause bradycardia — atropine is anticholinergic, and it also has a drying effect on secretions. Use caution with driving/activities requiring alertness on these drugs.","This is a quick pinpoint review — be sure to also review the full constipation and diarrhea medication list from your earlier drug matrix (Block 1 / NUR 325 material), since those drug classes aren't fully re-taught here."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week5-endocrine-disorders.html","t":"Endocrine Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Endocrine Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 5 · Topic 5","Adrenal Cortex Background","Cushing's Syndrome (Cortisol Excess)","Cushing's Syndrome — Clinical Manifestations","Cushing's Syndrome — Treatment & Pharmacology","Addison's Disease (Adrenal Cortex Hyposecretion)","Addison's Disease — Treatment & Pharmacology","Addisonian Crisis","Pheochromocytoma","Pheochromocytoma — Treatment & Pharmacology","Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH) Background","SIADH (Too Much ADH)","SIADH — Treatment","Diabetes Insipidus (Too Little ADH)","Diabetes Insipidus — Treatment","SIADH vs. Diabetes Insipidus — Quick Comparison","Self-Test Flashcards","This page covers the adrenal glands end to end: the adrenal cortex (Cushing's syndrome and Addison's disease — a cortisol excess/deficiency mirror pair) and the adrenal medulla plus ADH disorders (pheochromocytoma, SIADH, and diabetes insip…"]},{"s":"Adrenal Cortex Background▾","a":"adrenal-cortex-background","k":["Adrenal Cortex Background▾","Quick Pituitary Review","The pituitary gland has an anterior and a posterior portion.","The anterior pituitary secretes several hormones, including thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) and adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) — ACTH is the focus of this section.","The posterior pituitary secretes antidiuretic hormone (ADH/vasopressin) and oxytocin — ADH is covered later on this same page.","ACTH acts on the adrenal cortex to stimulate release of adrenal cortical hormones.","The adrenal glands sit on top of the kidneys. Each gland has an inner medulla (secretes epinephrine/norepinephrine — covered later in the Pheochromocytoma section) and an outer cortex. When stimulated by ACTH, the adrenal cortex releases th…","MNEMONIC: The \"3 S's\" of adrenal cortex hormones — Sugar = cortisol (the main glucocorticoid, and the most clinically important of the three), Salt = aldosterone (mineralocorticoid), Sex = androgens (sex steroids).","These hormones are made on demand and are NOT stored in cells, and they regulate the body's response to stress.","Cortisol's Normal Functions (the key to understanding both Cushing's and Addison's below)","Increases glucose availability — raises blood sugar for extra energy.","Maintains vascular tone and blood pressure.","Breaks down fat and protein for energy.","Suppresses the immune and inflammatory response.","Activates the CNS/alertness (\"fight or flight\" readiness)."]},{"s":"Cushing's Syndrome (Cortisol Excess)▾","a":"cushing-s-syndrome-cortisol-excess","k":["Cushing's Syndrome (Cortisol Excess)▾","Cushing's syndrome is a collection of signs and symptoms most commonly associated with hypercortisolism (too much cortisol).","Primary — The adrenal cortex itself is dysfunctional and secretes excessive cortisol directly (e.g., an adrenal tumor).","Secondary — The problem is in the anterior pituitary — something causes the pituitary to produce excess ACTH, which then drives excessive cortisol production from an otherwise normal adrenal gland.","Exogenous — Caused by chronic steroid medication use (e.g., prednisone, dexamethasone). This is the MOST COMMON cause of Cushing syndrome in the United States — steroids are prescribed often, and being on them (especially at higher doses fo…","Even though the adrenal cortex makes three hormone types, cortisol overproduction causes the primary symptoms seen in Cushing's — aldosterone and androgen overproduction also occur but don't drive the main clinical picture the way cortisol …","Why Cushing's Patients Can't Handle a New Stressor (e.g., Surgery)","Because cortisol is chronically elevated in Cushing's, the feedback loop that would normally let cortisol rise further in response to a new stressor doesn't work — cortisol is already maxed out and can't respond to acute triggers the way it…","This means a Cushing's patient undergoing surgery may need supplemental ACTH or cortisol administered pre-operatively.","Because ACTH is chronically suppressed by the elevated cortisol (negative feedback), the adrenal gland can eventually atrophy from disuse, especially with long-standing disease."]},{"s":"Cushing's Syndrome — Clinical Manifestations▾","a":"cushing-s-syndrome-clinical-manifestations","k":["Cushing's Syndrome — Clinical Manifestations▾","Every finding below traces back to one of cortisol's normal functions being pushed into excess.","Glucose effects — Patients become essentially diabetic — glucose intolerant and hyperglycemic (excess cortisol keeps telling the body to raise blood sugar).","Vascular effects — Hypertensive state (cortisol's vasoconstriction/BP-maintaining function chronically \"on\"); also capillary fragility leading to easy skin bruising, because tiny capillaries can't tolerate being chronically \"squeezed\" and s…","Protein/fat breakdown effects — Muscle wasting and muscle weakness (protein constantly broken down); thinning of skin, osteoporosis, and bone pain; a characteristic fat redistribution to the abdomen, shoulders, and face while the limbs beco…","Immune suppression effects — Impaired wound healing, impaired immune response, and higher risk of infection.","CNS effects — Mood swings and inability to sleep (insomnia), from chronic CNS activation.","Additional Classic Physical Findings","Thinning hair in normal hair-bearing areas (pubic region, axilla, scalp), but abnormal hair growth in women where it typically doesn't grow — chin, chest, areolas (hirsutism, also seen with some medications).","Central/truncal obese abdomen with thin extremities.","Gynecomastia (breast development).","\"Buffalo hump\" — a fat pad on the back of the neck.","\"Moon face.\"","Purple stretch marks (striae).","Easy bruising/ecchymosis from fragile capillaries.","Thin, fragile skin and poor bone density.","Elevated blood pressure from sodium/fluid retention (related to the aldosterone excess that accompanies Cushing's)."]},{"s":"Cushing's Syndrome — Treatment & Pharmacology▾","a":"cushing-s-syndrome-treatment-pharmacology","k":["Cushing's Syndrome — Treatment & Pharmacology▾","Treatment is directed at the cause: surgery (and sometimes radiation) for a pituitary tumor (secondary cause) or adrenal tumor (primary cause). For exogenous steroid-induced Cushing's, the treatment is to SLOWLY taper the steroid dose (neve…","Drug therapy is available to help suppress steroid synthesis when surgery/radiation isn't available or as a bridge/adjunct — these are NOT typically the primary/preferred treatment, but they need to be known.","Aminoglutethimide — Blocks the first step in the synthesis of ALL adrenal steroids (glucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids, and androgens all decline). Used only for TEMPORARY therapy to decrease cortisol — for example, while a patient is wait…","Ketoconazole — Actually an antifungal drug (recognizable by the \"-azole\" suffix, a classic antifungal drug-class ending), but it also inhibits glucocorticoid synthesis/blocks adrenal hormone formation, giving it the same use/indication as a…"]},{"s":"Addison's Disease (Adrenal Cortex Hyposecretion)▾","a":"addison-s-disease-adrenal-cortex-hyposecretion","k":["Addison's Disease (Adrenal Cortex Hyposecretion)▾","The opposite of Cushing's: NOT ENOUGH adrenal cortical hormone. By definition, Addison's is a disease of the adrenal cortex that causes hyposecretion of all three adrenal cortical hormones — not enough cortisol, not enough aldosterone (flui…","Idiopathic — Cause unknown.","Autoimmune — The MOST common cause.","Other causes — Any process that damages/destroys the adrenal gland.","Pathophysiology","This is a PRIMARY adrenal cortical insufficiency — the adrenal gland itself is damaged/destroyed and stops secreting cortisol.","Because of the lost negative feedback, the anterior pituitary hypersecretes both ACTH and melanocyte-stimulating hormone (MSH), trying to stimulate cortisol production — but because the adrenal gland itself is the problem, it can't respond …","Manifestations typically don't become evident until about 90% of the adrenal cortex has been destroyed — the disease is often quite advanced by diagnosis, and onset is typically very slow.","General symptoms: poor appetite, weakness, malaise, along with electrolyte imbalances. A classic and distinctive finding is hyperpigmentation/a tanned appearance, caused by the excess MSH being co-secreted along with ACTH, which drives exce…","Aldosterone Loss Effects (Hypoaldosteronism)","Not retaining sodium and water — losing both → hyponatremia and dehydration.","Because sodium and potassium work inversely, these patients also develop hyperkalemia.","Salt cravings, because sodium is so low.","Low blood pressure, low vascular tone, low cardiac output, and low circulating blood volume.","Cortisol Loss Effects","Low energy/glucose availability → hypoglycemia, weakness, fatigue.","High ACTH levels (the body's compensatory attempt).","Hyperpigmentation from MSH co-secretion, as above.","Hormone level — Cortisol excess (aldosterone/androgens also elevated) — Cortisol, aldosterone, and androgens all deficient","Glucose effect — Glucose intolerance, hyperglycemia — Hypoglycemia, weakness, fatigue","BP effect — Hypertension — Low blood pressure, low vascular tone, low cardiac output","Sodium/potassium effect — Sodium and fluid retention (aldosterone excess) — Hyponatremia and hyperkalemia (aldosterone loss)","Skin/body habitus — Moon face, buffalo hump, central obesity with thin limbs, striae, thin fragile skin, easy bruising — Hyperpigmentation/tanned appearance","Treatment approach — Treat the cause (surgery/radiation) or slowly taper exogenous steroids; drug therapy (aminoglutethimide, ketoconazole) as a temporary bridge — Lifelong corticosteroid replacement (hydrocortisone + fludrocortisone)"]},{"s":"Addison's Disease — Treatment & Pharmacology▾","a":"addison-s-disease-treatment-pharmacology","k":["Addison's Disease — Treatment & Pharmacology▾","Adrenal insufficiency requires LIFELONG corticosteroid replacement therapy, regardless of the underlying cause.","Hydrocortisone — Drug of choice — it has BOTH glucocorticoid AND mineralocorticoid activity. Can also use prednisone or dexamethasone. Can be given IM, IV, or PO.","Fludrocortisone — The ONLY mineralocorticoid replacement drug available. Works like aldosterone to help with salt/water balance. Given PO, once daily.","Dosing Strategy","Replacement therapy should try to mimic the body's NORMAL cortisol secretion pattern (cortisol naturally rises overnight and peaks in the early waking hours).","Some clinicians give the entire daily dose at bedtime; others divide it, giving two-thirds in the morning and one-third in the afternoon.","Consistency (taking it every day, the same way) is what matters most.","Dosing is kept as small as possible while still managing symptoms.","Corticosteroid therapy in Addison's disease can NEVER be abruptly stopped — doing so can precipitate an Addisonian crisis, a life-threatening medical emergency.","Patient Teaching: \"Sick Day\" Dosing","Patients must increase their glucocorticoid dose during ANY kind of stress — physiologic, mental, or emotional (infection, surgery, trauma, or significant emotional stress) — because a healthy adrenal gland would normally increase glucocort…","Failing to increase the dose during a stress event can be fatal.","A common teaching guideline is the \"3-by-3 rule\" — take three times the usual dose for three days during a stress event (always in coordination with their care provider).","Patients should always carry an adequate supply of their medication, including BOTH an injectable preparation and an oral preparation.","Patients should wear some form of medical identification (like a medical alert bracelet) to inform emergency personnel of their condition."]},{"s":"Addisonian Crisis▾","a":"addisonian-crisis","k":["Addisonian Crisis▾","An acute insufficiency of serum corticosteroids — a true medical emergency where the body essentially \"crashes\" because it doesn't have enough stress hormone available to meet an increased demand. Also referred to as acute adrenal insuffici…","Triggers","Sudden loss/destruction of adrenal gland function.","Sudden increase in stress on top of a chronic adrenal insufficiency condition.","Sudden cessation of corticosteroid drug therapy.","Any additional stress on the body in a patient with adrenal insufficiency requires more stress hormone than is available — this can be life-threatening if the glucocorticoid dose isn't urgently increased.","There is also a separate, opposite-direction acute emergency called \"severe Cushing syndrome,\" presenting as a massively elevated serum cortisol with severe hypokalemia, often associated with sepsis/opportunistic infection, uncontrolled hyp…"]},{"s":"Pheochromocytoma▾","a":"pheochromocytoma","k":["Pheochromocytoma▾","A rare tumor of the adrenal MEDULLA — the center part of the adrenal gland, which normally secretes catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine) in response to sympathetic nervous system stimulation. A pheochromocytoma causes excessive p…","Pheochromocytoma — Tumor of the adrenal medulla causing excessive catecholamine (epinephrine/norepinephrine) production and release; about 90% benign.","\"-oma\" suffix — A naming pattern where a tumor name ending in \"-oma\" typically indicates the tumor is benign.","Epinephrine — Stimulates BOTH alpha and beta receptors, located in the heart, lungs, and vessels.","Norepinephrine — Stimulates almost entirely alpha receptors.","Hallmark presentation","Hypertension is the hallmark — it can be intermittent or persistent.","Accompanied by bouts of the classic triad: headache, diaphoresis, and tachycardia (also common symptoms of very high blood pressure in general).","Sporadic hypertensive episodes can be triggered by anything that stimulates the sympathetic nervous system: stress, excitement, exercise, or smoking.","Benign does not mean safe: even though the tumor itself is typically benign, the resulting severe hypertension puts the patient at high risk for stroke or cardiac events — these patients need to be treated urgently, with a calm, low-stimulu…"]},{"s":"Pheochromocytoma — Treatment & Pharmacology▾","a":"pheochromocytoma-treatment-pharmacology","k":["Pheochromocytoma — Treatment & Pharmacology▾","Surgical removal of the tumor is the preferred/definitive treatment. Drug therapy can be used to control blood pressure, especially before surgery or for patients whose tumor is inoperable, in which case they may take alpha blockers chronic…","Phenoxybenzamine","An older alpha-blocking drug whose only real modern use is for pheochromocytoma.","Unlike more typical alpha blockers used for blood pressure (like doxazosin/Cardura), phenoxybenzamine is a NONCOMPETITIVE receptor antagonist — meaning its receptor blockade is not reversible.","Normally an irreversible drug effect would be undesirable, but in this specific condition it's exactly what's wanted: long-lasting, irreversible blockage of alpha receptors to keep blood pressure controlled.","Alpha receptors work on smooth muscle in general — blocking them causes smooth muscle relaxation and vessel widening, lowering blood pressure.","Side effects (typical of blood-pressure-lowering drugs generally)","Orthostatic hypotension","Reflex tachycardia","Nasal congestion","Sexual side effects in men"]},{"s":"Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH) Background▾","a":"antidiuretic-hormone-adh-background","k":["Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH) Background▾","ADH (also called vasopressin) is released from the posterior pituitary every day, in response to high serum osmolality (concentrated blood) or hypotension (low blood pressure). Its job is water retention in the kidneys — when the body sense…","MNEMONIC: \"ADH stops the pee\" — a simple way to remember that ADH causes water retention (the opposite of what a diuretic does)."]},{"s":"SIADH (Too Much ADH)▾","a":"siadh-too-much-adh","k":["SIADH (Too Much ADH)▾","SIADH stands for Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone — an abnormal production or sustained secretion of ADH, meaning the body inappropriately holds onto ALL of its water. Characterized by fluid retention, low serum osmolality (di…","Causes","Most common: a malignant tumor — specifically small cell carcinoma / adenocarcinoma of the lung, which can inappropriately secrete ADH on its own.","Central nervous system disorders: head trauma, stroke, brain tumor.","Certain drugs: morphine, SSRIs, some chemotherapy agents.","Miscellaneous conditions: hypothyroidism, infection.","The malignant tumor cause is by far the most common.","When ADH is released (whether from a tumor, infection, or trauma), it tells the kidneys to hold onto all the water, increasing intravascular fluid volume and diluting the vascular system — this causes hyponatremia, decreased serum osmolalit…","Serum osmolality — Low","Serum sodium — Low","Urine osmolality / specific gravity — High (very concentrated urine)","Urine output — Low","Weight — Weight gain (from fluid retention)","Manifestations","Signs of hyponatremia and fluid volume excess: fatigue, confusion, lethargy, altered/dulled sensorium, muscle twitching, convulsions.","GI effects: impaired taste, anorexia, vomiting.","Severity tracks with how low the sodium gets — normal sodium range is 135–145 mEq/L.","In severe cases, patients can develop \"water intoxication\" — when serum sodium drops lower than the sodium concentration inside cells, cells (including brain cells) start pulling in water and swelling, causing further neurologic problems.","Sodium below ~115 mEq/L means severe, irreversible neurologic damage: severe hyponatremia from SIADH can cause water intoxication from cellular swelling, especially dangerous in brain cells."]},{"s":"SIADH — Treatment▾","a":"siadh-treatment","k":["SIADH — Treatment▾","General measures","Directed at the underlying cause: remove the tumor if that's the cause, stop the causative medication if a drug is responsible, or treat/wait out the underlying condition if it's a head trauma or stroke.","Fluid restriction is a core non-drug measure.","Loop diuretics can be given to promote diuresis, but ONLY if the sodium is above 125 mEq/L — giving a diuretic when sodium is already very low risks losing even more sodium.","Salt tablets may also be given.","Pharmacotherapy is generally not first-line.","Demeclocycline (chronic/refractory SIADH)","Used for chronic/refractory SIADH, such as from an unremovable lung tumor.","Actually a tetracycline broad-spectrum antibiotic (originally used as an antifungal, interestingly) that has a side effect of interfering with the kidney's response to ADH, making the kidneys less sensitive to it.","Because it's a tetracycline, it carries typical tetracycline side effects: photosensitivity (patients need to avoid sun exposure), teeth staining, and nephrotoxicity."]},{"s":"Diabetes Insipidus (Too Little ADH)▾","a":"diabetes-insipidus-too-little-adh","k":["Diabetes Insipidus (Too Little ADH)▾","The opposite problem from SIADH: not enough ADH, or the kidneys don't respond properly to the ADH that is present — the patient loses ALL their water (versus SIADH, where the body retains all its water). There are two types, distinguished b…","Neurogenic (central) DI","Damage to the hypothalamus or pituitary gland interferes with the RELEASE of ADH from the brain.","Typically associated with traumatic brain injury, stroke, or a cerebral infection, and often occurs following an injury or surgical intervention.","Onset is SUDDEN.","Usually PERMANENT, because the pituitary has been damaged/destroyed in some way.","Nephrogenic DI","A kidney problem — the kidneys are unable to properly respond to ADH.","Usually related to loss of kidney function (e.g., chronic kidney disease), or caused by certain drugs, notably lithium (used for bipolar disorder).","Onset is SLOW and progressive (versus the sudden onset of neurogenic DI).","Regardless of type, the patient is losing large amounts of essentially pure water.","Serum sodium — Low — Very high","Serum osmolality — Low — Very high (very concentrated)","Urine osmolality / specific gravity — High (very concentrated) — Very low (dilute, like \"straight water\")","Urine output — Low — Very high","Presentation","Massive polyuria — patients can lose as much as 15 liters of urine a day.","Accompanied by extreme thirst (polydipsia) and dehydration, since the body is trying to compensate; patients may drink up to 15 liters of fluid a day trying to keep up.","Causes significant electrolyte imbalances.","Because patients become so intravascularly dry, they can progress to hypovolemic shock, which can be fatal.","Uncontrolled diabetes insipidus can be fatal: severe dehydration from massive, ongoing water loss can progress to fatal hypovolemic shock."]},{"s":"Diabetes Insipidus — Treatment▾","a":"diabetes-insipidus-treatment","k":["Diabetes Insipidus — Treatment▾","Treatment differs by type, which is an important testable distinction.","Neurogenic DI — Desmopressin (DDAVP)","Since the problem is that the brain isn't releasing ADH at all, the treatment is synthetic ADH replacement — desmopressin, also known as DDAVP (not a trade name — a commonly used shortened name for the drug itself).","Essentially straight synthetic ADH replacement.","Can be given via nasal spray (common), PO, IV, or subcutaneously, usually in small doses.","Nasal spray can cause some nasal irritation; overly large doses can cause an overdose/opposite effect (excessive water retention).","Nephrogenic DI — Thiazide diuretic (HCTZ)","Giving more ADH/DDAVP does NOT work for this type, since the kidneys themselves can't respond to ADH whether or not it is present.","Instead, treated, somewhat counterintuitively, with a thiazide diuretic (HCTZ).","Research has shown this paradoxically reduces polyuria and increases urine osmolality in these patients, even though thiazides are normally used to promote diuresis for other conditions.","The exact mechanism for why this works in nephrogenic DI isn't fully understood, but it's an established, testable treatment fact.","Why doesn't DDAVP work for nephrogenic DI? Because the kidneys are the problem — they can't respond to ADH even when it's present, so adding more ADH doesn't help."]},{"s":"SIADH vs. Diabetes Insipidus — Quick Comparison▾","a":"siadh-vs-diabetes-insipidus-quick-comparison","k":["SIADH vs. Diabetes Insipidus — Quick Comparison▾","Serum sodium — Low — Very high","Serum osmolality — Low — Very high","Urine osmolality / concentration — High / concentrated — Low / dilute","Urine output — Low — Very high (up to 15 L/day)","Fluid volume status — Fluid volume excess (water retention, weight gain) — Fluid volume deficit (dehydration, risk of hypovolemic shock)","Treatment — Fluid restriction; loop diuretics only if sodium > 125 mEq/L; salt tablets; demeclocycline for chronic cases — Desmopressin (DDAVP) for neurogenic; thiazide diuretic (HCTZ) for nephrogenic"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week5-thyroid-parathyroid-disorders.html","t":"Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 5 · Topic 6","Thyroid Background & the HPT Axis","Goiter","Hypothyroidism","Hypothyroidism — Treatment","Hyperthyroidism (Thyrotoxicosis)","Hyperthyroidism — Treatment & Thyroid Storm","Parathyroid Disorders","Self-Test Flashcards","Hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism are a mirror pair, much like Cushing's and Addison's disease — one is too little hormone, the other too much, and the manifestations largely run in opposite directions. The parathyroid glands, though physi…"]},{"s":"Thyroid Background & the HPT Axis▾","a":"thyroid-background-the-hpt-axis","k":["Thyroid Background & the HPT Axis▾","The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped gland located in the neck, about 2 inches wide. It secretes two major hormones, T3 and T4, and its overall job is to set the pace of body metabolism.","MNEMONIC: think of thyroid hormone as the \"volume knob\" for the body's metabolism — it can be turned up (hyperthyroidism) or turned down (hypothyroidism).","Triiodothyronine (T3) — The ACTIVE form of thyroid hormone — it's what actually causes thyroid hormone's effects in the body.","Thyroxine (T4) — Circulates in bound and unbound forms; when the body senses it needs more thyroid hormone activity, T4 becomes unbound and is converted into the active T3 form. T4 is the regulator of body metabolism and influences almost e…","Iodine — A necessary component in the synthesis of thyroid hormones — this is why iodine deficiency causes thyroid problems.","The HPT feedback loop","When the hypothalamus senses the body needs more metabolism (\"turn the volume up\"), it releases thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH).","TRH tells the anterior pituitary to produce thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH).","TSH is sensed by the thyroid gland and stimulates it to produce T4 (thyroxine, the largely inactive/prohormone form), which is then converted into T3 (the active form) out in the body.","Once the body senses enough T3 is circulating, it signals the hypothalamus to turn production back down (negative feedback).","Why this matters clinically: measuring TSH, T3, and T4 tells you WHERE the problem is — a problem above the thyroid gland (hypothalamus/pituitary) or a problem at the level of the thyroid gland itself. Thyroid dysfunction is common and can …"]},{"s":"Goiter▾","a":"goiter","k":["Goiter▾","An enlargement of the thyroid gland, which may or may not be accompanied by symptoms of thyroid dysfunction. If there are no symptoms of thyroid disease, it's called a \"nontoxic\" goiter.","Excess TSH stimulation — Seen for example in populations/regions with iodine deficiency — low iodine means the thyroid can't make enough thyroid hormone even though the pituitary keeps increasing TSH trying to compensate; the gland enlarges…","Goitrogens — Foods or medications that promote thyroid gland enlargement, such as lithium (used for bipolar disorder) — this requires close monitoring of thyroid hormone levels in patients taking it."]},{"s":"Hypothyroidism▾","a":"hypothyroidism","k":["Hypothyroidism▾","Not enough thyroid hormone (insufficient T3/T4).","Primary hypothyroidism (~95% of cases)","The most common type — the thyroid gland itself fails to secrete enough T3 and T4.","Labs: INCREASED TSH (the pituitary is trying to compensate) with DECREASED T3 and T4.","Secondary hypothyroidism","The pituitary itself fails to secrete enough TSH, so there isn't enough stimulus telling the thyroid to produce hormone in the first place.","Labs: DECREASED TSH with DECREASED T3 and T4.","Hashimoto's thyroiditis","An autoimmune disorder and the MOST COMMON cause of (primary) hypothyroidism.","Antibodies attack the thyroid gland directly.","Labs show low T3/T4 with high TSH (the pituitary correctly senses the body needs more, but the thyroid can't respond because of the autoimmune damage).","Diagnostic antibody tests: anti-thyroglobulin antibody, and anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody — the anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody is considered the hallmark test for Hashimoto's.","Risk factors","Women older than 50","Caucasian","Pregnancy","Having another autoimmune disorder (such as lupus or rheumatoid arthritis)","Family history","Certain medications: amiodarone and lithium","Prior treatment for hyperthyroidism (such as radiation to the neck or radioactive iodine) that ends up \"working too well\" and pushes the patient into a hypothyroid state instead","Manifestations can be thought of as the metabolic \"volume knob\" being turned DOWN.","Early / nonspecific manifestations","Cold intolerance","Weight gain","Fatigue, lethargy","Memory deficits, poor attention span","Increased cholesterol","Muscle cramping","Constipation","Later / more severe manifestations","Puffy face","Hair loss, brittle nails","Decreased fertility (menstrual disturbances)","Skin yellowing","Very low body temperature","Low heart rate (bradycardia)","Significant weight gain","Decreased level of consciousness","Thick skin","Cardiac complications","Also commonly seen","Hyperlipidemia (often managed with a statin)","Anemia","Decreased kidney filtration","Increased risk of medication toxicity","Hoarse voice","Myxedema coma is a life-threatening complication of severe, prolonged, untreated hypothyroidism, causing loss of brain function. Myxedema is the term for SEVERE hypothyroidism that has gone undiagnosed or untreated for a long period of time…","Primary hypothyroidism diagnosis — High TSH with low T3/T4 (the thyroid gland itself is not working).","Secondary hypothyroidism diagnosis — Low TSH with low T3/T4 (pointing to a pituitary/brain-level problem).","Hashimoto's testing — Anti-thyroid peroxidase and anti-thyroglobulin antibody testing."]},{"s":"Hypothyroidism — Treatment▾","a":"hypothyroidism-treatment","k":["Hypothyroidism — Treatment▾","Levothyroxine (Synthroid)","A synthetic T4 hormone that the body converts to active T3 as needed.","Has a very long half-life, so dosing consistency between doses is important.","Takes time to work — patients typically don't see improved symptoms until about a month after starting.","Too high a dose can cause hyperthyroid symptoms; too low a dose means persistent hypothyroid symptoms.","Administration: taken on an empty stomach, usually about 30 minutes before eating, first thing in the morning (food and other drugs can reduce its absorption) — most patients are taught to keep it at their bedside and take it right when the…","Needed for LIFE (with rare exception, such as if the thyroid gland is surgically removed for another reason).","Important drug interaction: levothyroxine increases the effect of warfarin (an anticoagulant), increasing the risk of bleeding — this combination needs closer monitoring."]},{"s":"Hyperthyroidism (Thyrotoxicosis)▾","a":"hyperthyroidism-thyrotoxicosis","k":["Hyperthyroidism (Thyrotoxicosis)▾","Too much T3/T4 (excessive secretion); historically called thyrotoxicosis.","Hypothyroidism vs. hyperthyroidism at a glance","TSH — Increased (primary) / decreased (secondary) — Decreased (primary)","T3 / T4 — Decreased — Increased","Metabolic effect — Metabolism slowed (\"volume turned down\") — Metabolism sped up (\"volume turned up\")","Key manifestations — Cold intolerance, weight gain, fatigue, bradycardia, constipation — Heat intolerance, weight loss, nervousness/insomnia, tachyarrhythmias","Treatment — Levothyroxine (lifelong) — Antithyroid medication (PTU), radioactive iodine, or thyroidectomy","Classifications","Primary: the thyroid gland itself overproduces T3/T4, even while the pituitary is telling it (via low TSH) that it doesn't need to — the gland simply doesn't \"listen\" to that feedback and keeps overproducing. Graves' disease is the classic …","Secondary: very uncommon — a pituitary problem (the pituitary is secreting too much TSH).","Tertiary: very uncommon — a hypothalamic problem (excess secretion of TRH).","Other causes: a thyroid adenoma, subacute thyroiditis, toxic multinodular goiter, or excessive thyroid hormone replacement (overmedication with levothyroxine).","Risk factors","Family history of Graves' disease","Age over 40, Caucasian","Amiodarone (contains iodine) can cause EITHER hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism — same drug, either direction","Excessive iodine intake","Pregnancy (pregnancy hormones can stimulate TSH receptors)","Graves' disease","An autoimmune condition — thyroid-stimulating antibodies bind to and activate TSH receptors on the thyroid, causing continuous overproduction and enlargement of the gland.","This is a primary condition (the thyroid itself is excessively secreting) with a feedback loop that lowers TSH, but the thyroid doesn't listen to it.","Manifestations can be thought of as the metabolic \"volume knob\" turned UP — essentially the opposite of hypothyroidism.","Manifestations","Nervousness, insomnia (CNS amped up)","Heat sensitivity/intolerance","Weight loss (accelerated metabolism)","Thyroid gland usually enlarged and palpable; a bruit (an abnormal sound from high blood flow) may be heard over the gland","Enhanced sensitivity to catecholamines puts these patients at risk for cardiac arrhythmias (especially atrial fibrillation) and heart failure","Graves' ophthalmopathy","Exophthalmos (bulging eyes) with periorbital edema — an inflammatory eye disorder that develops around the eyes and can cause vision impairment.","Common with Graves' disease specifically; women are more often affected than men.","Teaching point: any patient with a history of hyperthyroidism/Graves' disease needs an eye exam.","Labs — LOW TSH with HIGH T3/T4.","Antibody testing — Anti-thyroglobulin and anti-TSH-receptor/thyroid-stimulating antibodies.","Ultrasound with color Doppler — Shows excessive blood flow over the thyroid, explaining the bruit.","Radioactive iodine uptake scan — In Graves' disease, uptake is INCREASED and DIFFUSELY distributed over the entire gland (not a specific focal area, which would instead point toward a more localized process like a cancerous nodule)."]},{"s":"Hyperthyroidism — Treatment & Thyroid Storm▾","a":"hyperthyroidism-treatment-thyroid-storm","k":["Hyperthyroidism — Treatment & Thyroid Storm▾","Antithyroid medication: PTU","Propylthiouracil (PTU) blocks thyroid hormone synthesis and suppresses the conversion of T4 to T3.","Carries a black box warning for hepatotoxicity (liver toxicity).","Can be used cautiously during the FIRST trimester of pregnancy, but needs to be changed to a different medication for the second and third trimesters because of the hepatotoxicity risk.","Radioactive iodine & surgery","Radioactive iodine treatment is the MORE COMMON treatment — the thyroid gland takes up the radioactive iodine, which suppresses its activity.","Surgery (thyroidectomy) removes the gland — the patient will need lifelong levothyroxine/thyroid hormone replacement afterward, since there's no thyroid gland left to produce it.","Thyroid storm (thyrotoxic crisis) can result in death within 48 hours if not treated. This is the extreme, life-threatening end of the hyperthyroid spectrum — similar in concept to myxedema coma being the extreme end of hypothyroidism. It i…"]},{"s":"Parathyroid Disorders▾","a":"parathyroid-disorders","k":["Parathyroid Disorders▾","The parathyroid glands are four small glands located within/near the actual thyroid tissue. They produce and secrete parathyroid hormone (PTH). PTH is released in response to LOW calcium levels (hypocalcemia) and works to control calcium le…","Hypoparathyroidism","Insufficient PTH secretion, leading to hypocalcemia.","A rare disorder, usually caused by trauma or damage to the parathyroid glands during thyroid surgery (thyroidectomy) — since the parathyroid glands sit right on/near the thyroid, they can be accidentally removed or damaged along with the th…","Any patient who has had their thyroid gland removed should be monitored for resulting hypocalcemia.","Manifestations of the resulting hypocalcemia: muscle cramping, irritability, tetany, and convulsions; also check for Trousseau's sign and Chvostek's sign.","Also see an INCREASE in phosphate levels (since PTH normally increases phosphate excretion by the kidneys — low PTH means the kidneys retain more phosphate); phosphate levels should also be monitored.","Treatment: synthetic parathyroid hormone replacement, normalizing serum calcium and vitamin D levels — this is lifelong treatment.","Hyperparathyroidism","Excess PTH secretion, resulting in hypercalcemia and bone breakdown.","Manifestations: muscle weakness, poor concentration, hypertension, and kidney stones — all related to the excessive circulating calcium levels.","Treatment: diuretics (to help excrete more calcium), calcitonin (helps regulate/lower calcium and phosphate levels in the blood), bisphosphonates (help reduce bone breakdown — the same drug class used for osteoporosis), vitamin D (helps cal…","Both hypo- and hyperparathyroidism are considered rare conditions overall."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week5-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 5","Three Clusters, Not Five Separate Topics","The Five Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new — it's a map of Week 5's five topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. Two pages walk the GI tract from top to bottom (esophagus/stomach,…"]},{"s":"Three Clusters, Not Five Separate Topics▾","a":"three-clusters-not-five-separate-topics","k":["Three Clusters, Not Five Separate Topics▾","Five pages is easier to hold in your head than nine, but they still aren't five unrelated diseases — two are a GI-tract progression, one is a cross-cutting symptom-treatment page, and two are a matched pair of endocrine mirror-image conditi…","The GI Tract, Top to Bottom — Esophageal & Gastric Disorders → Lower GI Disorders — These two move down the GI tube in order: esophagus and stomach first (dysphagia, GERD, hiatal hernia, gastritis, gastroenteritis, PUD), then small intestin…","Treating GI Symptoms, Not GI Diseases — GI Symptom Pharmacology — Nausea/vomiting and diarrhea aren't diseases of their own — they're symptoms of nearly everything in the first cluster (and beyond). This page's antiemetics and antidiarrheal…","Hormone Mirror Pairs — Endocrine Disorders → Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders — Both pages teach an \"excess vs. deficiency\" pair for the same hormone axis — Cushing's/Addison's and SIADH/diabetes insipidus on the first page, hypothyroidism/h…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, work through the two GI pages first (upper, then lower), treat GI Symptom Pharmacology as its own quick-hit drug review, then do the two endocrine pages back to back — the mirror-pair pattern…"]},{"s":"The Five Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-five-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Five Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Esophageal & Gastric Disorders — Upper GI conditions overlap heavily in symptoms (heartburn, epigastric pain, dysphagia) and treatment — H2 blockers and PPIs decrease acid, sucralfate and antacids boost mucosal protection, and the whole…","2 — Lower GI Disorders — Location and pattern are everything: RLQ pain means appendicitis, LLQ pain means diverticulitis, transmural skip lesions mean Crohn's, and continuous mucosal bleeding means ulcerative colitis. Rupture/perforation of…","3 — GI Symptom Pharmacology — Three antiemetic mechanisms (serotonin blockers, H1 antihistamines, dopamine antagonists) each carry their own specific risk — serotonin syndrome, sedation/anticholinergic effects, and EPS/NMS, respectively — p…","4 — Endocrine Disorders — The adrenal cortex (cortisol/aldosterone/androgens) and the ADH axis each get an excess-vs-deficiency pair: Cushing's vs. Addison's, and SIADH vs. diabetes insipidus. The adrenal medulla's pheochromocytoma is the o…","5 — Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders — Hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism are another excess-vs-deficiency mirror pair, this time for thyroid hormone; the parathyroid glands are covered on the same page only because they sit physically near …"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content — each idea is fully taught in exactly one place. But they resurface as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why.","The \"-itis\" suffix means inflammation — Gastritis, Esophageal & Gastric Disorders — Appendicitis, peritonitis, and diverticulitis, all on Lower GI Disorders — plus hepatitis and pancreatitis back in Week 4. Once you notice the suffix, the d…","H1 vs. H2 receptor blockers — a deliberate mix-up point — H2 blockers (famotidine, cimetidine), Esophageal & Gastric Disorders — H1 blockers (dimenhydrinate, hydroxyzine), GI Symptom Pharmacology — the GI Pharm page explicitly flags this as…","Lithium as a recurring iatrogenic culprit — Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, Endocrine Disorders — Goiter (as a goitrogen) and a Hashimoto's risk factor, on Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders — the same drug causing two completely unrelated end…","The excess-vs-deficiency \"mirror pair\" teaching structure — Cushing's vs. Addison's and SIADH vs. diabetes insipidus, both on Endocrine Disorders — Hypothyroidism vs. hyperthyroidism, on Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders — the same \"too much …","Peritonitis's organ-rupture causes — Pancreatitis and ruptured gallbladder, on Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders (Week 4) — Peritonitis, Lower GI Disorders — named as two of the most common causes, without re-teaching them."]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","Every danger callout across the five pages, condensed to a phrase. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation — click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","Esophageal & Gastric Disorders — Barrett's esophagus carries a threefold risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma with no pharmacologic fix once it develops. Acute gastroenteritis's real danger is dehydration from fluid loss at both ends, not the …","Lower GI Disorders — Sudden, complete relief of severe appendicitis pain can mean the appendix has ruptured, not that it's getting better. Toxic megacolon — a rapid, life-threatening colon dilation — is a serious complication of ulcerative …","GI Symptom Pharmacology — Never give hydroxyzine IV — it can cause tissue damage, thrombosis, and gangrene (IM only). Watch for serotonin syndrome when ondansetron is combined with other serotonergic drugs. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (N…","Endocrine Disorders — Corticosteroid therapy in Addison's disease can never be abruptly stopped — doing so can precipitate a life-threatening Addisonian crisis. Pheochromocytoma's severe hypertension risks stroke or cardiac events even thou…","Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders — Myxedema coma (severe, prolonged, untreated hypothyroidism) and thyroid storm (severe hyperthyroidism, death within 48 hours if untreated) are mirror-image emergencies at opposite ends of the same thyroid-h…","Patterns you'll notice across these rules","\"Too much and too little are taught the same way, three times over.\" Cushing's/Addison's, SIADH/diabetes insipidus, and hypothyroidism/hyperthyroidism all use the identical excess-vs-deficiency structure — once you can predict Cushing's fin…","\"A suffix is a shortcut, not a coincidence.\" \"-itis\" means inflammation (gastritis, appendicitis, peritonitis, diverticulitis) and \"-oma\" typically means a benign tumor (pheochromocytoma) — both patterns hold up across this entire week and …","\"Location plus pattern equals diagnosis.\" RLQ vs. LLQ pain, transmural vs. mucosal-only inflammation, and skip lesions vs. continuous involvement are the exact features that separate look-alike lower GI diagnoses from each other.","\"The same drug can cause two unrelated problems.\" Lithium causes both nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and goiter/Hashimoto's risk; amiodarone causes either hypo- or hyperthyroidism — a reminder to check the med list whenever an endocrine pic…","\"Symptom drugs aren't disease drugs.\" GI Symptom Pharmacology's antiemetics and antidiarrheals show up as supporting treatment across nearly every diagnosis in both GI pages — they're organized by symptom instead of by disease for exactly t…"]}]},{"f":"week5-comprehensive-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 5 · Topic 1","Part 1 — Esophageal & Gastric Disorders (Upper GI)","Part 2 — Lower GI Disorders","Part 3 — GI Symptom Pharmacology","Part 4 — Endocrine Disorders (Adrenal Cortex, Adrenal Medulla & ADH)","Part 5 — Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 5 — upper GI (esophageal & gastric) disorders, lower GI disorders, GI symptom pharmacology, endocrine disorders (adrenal cortex, adrenal medulla, and ADH), and thyroid …"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Esophageal & Gastric Disorders (Upper GI)▾","a":"part-1-esophageal-gastric-disorders-upper-gi","k":["Part 1 — Esophageal & Gastric Disorders (Upper GI)▾","These conditions are grouped together because they're all \"upper GI\" disorders — involving the esophagus and stomach — and they share overlapping symptoms (heartburn, epigastric pain, dysphagia) and overlapping pharmacologic treatments.","Dysphagia","Dysphagia means difficulty swallowing. It typically affects swallowing solids first, then progresses to also affect liquids.","Mechanical Causes","Stenosis","Strictures","Diverticula","Tumors — physically block the esophagus","Neuromuscular Causes","Stroke — the esophagus loses proper innervation","Achalasia — the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) can't open properly","Patients who have been intubated or have a tracheostomy are also a common population with dysphagia; it can either resolve or progress.","GERD (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease)","In GERD, the LES doesn't close properly, allowing highly acidic stomach contents to reflux backward into the esophagus, causing heartburn. It occurs from things that either weaken LES closure strength or increase abdominal pressure — fatty/…","Pyrosis — The medical term for heartburn — a burning sensation.","Dyspepsia — Indigestion.","Regurgitation — Feeling like burping up hot, acidic material.","Chest pain / dysphagia / pulmonary symptoms — Can occur with reflux, especially if severe or chronic.","Complications of Chronic GERD","Esophageal ulcerations","Scarring and strictures within the esophagus","Barrett's esophagus — the most severe complication; abnormal, metaplastic (precancerous) cell changes develop in the esophageal lining","Barrett's esophagus carries a threefold risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma, with very low survival if it develops — providers monitor for it in long-term GERD patients. There is no real pharmacologic treatment for existing strictures or Barr…","Hiatal Hernia","A hiatal hernia is a defect in the diaphragm that allows part of the stomach to pass upward into the thoracic (chest) cavity.","Sliding Hernia","The peritoneum stays intact, which restrains/limits the size of the hernia","Smaller, more common, and less severe","Paraesophageal (\"Rolling\") Hernia","Part of the stomach pushes through the diaphragm and stays there permanently, above the diaphragm","The peritoneal membrane becomes thin/defective, forming a true sac","More severe than a sliding hernia","Associated with aging, diaphragm-weakening injury, chronic pressure (coughing, vomiting, straining), and obesity. Many patients are asymptomatic; others have belching, dysphagia, or GERD-like chest/epigastric pain, since GERD and hiatal her…","Gastritis","Gastritis is a non-specific inflammatory condition of the stomach — the \"-itis\" suffix means inflammation, a suffix worth watching for across this week's GI conditions.","Acute Gastritis","Temporary inflammation of the stomach lining only, generally lasting 2–10 days","Related to irritating substances/drugs/infectious agents: alcohol, NSAIDs, H. pylori, stomach viruses","Chronic Gastritis","A progressive disorder lasting weeks to years","Autoimmune (parietal cells attacked) or from chronic H. pylori infection","Can lead to peptic ulcer disease, bleeding ulcers, anemia, and increased gastric cancer risk","NSAIDs block prostaglandin synthesis; prostaglandins normally stimulate protective mucus secretion and inhibit gastric acid production, so NSAIDs remove that protection and increase acid. H. pylori is a gram-negative spiral bacterium that t…","Acute Gastroenteritis","Involves inflammation of both the stomach and the small intestine — unlike gastritis, which is stomach only. Caused by stomach viruses (norovirus, rotavirus — the classic \"stomach flu\"), bacterial infections (E. coli, Salmonella, Campylobac…","The biggest concern is fluid volume deficit/dehydration, since the patient is losing fluid from both ends. Usually managed supportively; if symptoms last more than 48–72 hours, the patient should see a provider, and antibiotics may be neede…","Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD)","PUD is ulceration of the upper GI tract — esophageal, gastric, or duodenal (the first part of the small intestine). It develops when aggressive factors (H. pylori, NSAID use, excess acid/pepsin, smoking) overwhelm protective/defensive facto…","Stress does NOT directly cause peptic ulcers — this outdated belief has been re-thought. However, stress can worsen an existing ulcer, since the stress response increases gastric acid secretion. Pathophysiology: mucosal damage triggers an i…","Gastric Ulcer","Tends to occur later in life (age 50–70), tied to chronic risk factors (long-term NSAID/corticosteroid/anticoagulant use)","Pain occurs 1–2 hours after eating, as the stomach empties","Duodenal Ulcer","The most common location for peptic ulcers overall; can occur at any age, often earlier adulthood","Pain occurs 2–4 hours after eating, once food has passed through","MNEMONIC — HOP (Complications of PUD): Hemorrhage, Obstruction, Perforation.","Pharmacology for GERD & Peptic Ulcer Disease","Treatment works by either decreasing aggressive factors (H. pylori treatment, H2 blockers, PPIs) or increasing protective factors (sucralfate, antacids).","H. pylori treatment — Combination antibiotic therapy plus a gastric acid inhibitor, ~10–14 days, only after a confirmed breath test. Can cost ~$200 with up to 12 pills/day, so adherence is often poor.","H2 receptor blockers — Famotidine (preferred) and cimetidine (older, CYP450 interactions with warfarin/phenytoin/theophylline) reduce gastric acid ~60–70%. Give IV slowly (bradycardia risk); separate from antacids by at least 1 hour.","Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) — The \"-prazole\" drugs (omeprazole, pantoprazole, esomeprazole) irreversibly inhibit the H+/K+ ATPase \"proton pump.\" Work better than H2 blockers; long-term use linked to pneumonia, bone loss/fractures, and sto…","Sucralfate — Mucosal protectant forming a sticky gel barrier; FDA-approved for duodenal ulcers. PO only, before meals; separate other drugs by ~2 hours (decreases their absorption).","Antacids — Neutralize ~50% of stomach acid. Intense rebound heartburn after stopping. Aluminum/calcium bases → constipation; magnesium base → diarrhea. Separate from other drugs by 1–2 hours (chelation)."]},{"s":"Part 2 — Lower GI Disorders▾","a":"part-2-lower-gi-disorders","k":["Part 2 — Lower GI Disorders▾","These conditions are grouped as \"lower GI\" because they involve the small intestine and below, as opposed to the esophagus and stomach covered in Part 1.","Appendicitis","Inflammation of the appendix that becomes obstructed. The obstruction itself isn't the biggest concern — it's the potential complications: rupture, gangrene, abscess, and ultimately peritonitis. Classic pain: right lower quadrant (RLQ) and …","Sudden, complete relief of severe pain is NOT a good sign. It can indicate the appendix has ruptured, putting the patient at very high risk for peritonitis.","Peritonitis","Inflammation of the peritoneum, the serous membrane lining the abdominal cavity and covering the visceral organs. Causes: perforated gastric/duodenal ulcers, ruptured gallbladder, pancreatitis, ruptured spleen/bladder/appendix — notice the …","Pancreatitis and ruptured gallbladder are two of the most common causes of peritonitis — both are covered in full on Gallbladder & Pancreatic Disorders (Week 4); this page does not re-teach them","Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS)","A chronic condition of altered bowel pattern from changes in intestinal motility, associated with chronic/frequent constipation (IBS-C) or diarrhea (IBS-D). Symptoms: abdominal distension/bloating, intermittent pain exacerbated by stress an…","Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD): Crohn's Disease & Ulcerative Colitis","IBD is an umbrella term for Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis — chronic illnesses with periods of exacerbation and remission, more common in women, Caucasians, people of Jewish descent, and smokers, generally thought of as autoimmune c…","Location — Can affect the small intestine and upper large intestine and can involve the rectum. — Primarily the colon and rectum specifically.","Layer affected — Transmural — all layers of the GI tract wall. — Mucosa only — not transmural.","Lesion pattern — Patchy skip lesions with a cobblestone appearance. — Continuous, beginning in the rectum and extending up the colon.","Hallmark diarrhea — Watery diarrhea. — Bloody diarrhea.","Hallmark pathology — Granulomas and skip lesions. — Crypt abscesses and fragile granulation tissue.","Top complications — Malnutrition, anemia, scar tissue/obstructions, fistulas, higher cancer risk. — Hemorrhage, perforation, much higher colon cancer risk, toxic megacolon, liver disease.","Toxic megacolon is a rapid, life-threatening dilation of the large intestine — a serious complication of ulcerative colitis.","Both Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis put patients at high risk of VTE/DVT.","Diverticular Disease","Diverticulosis is the development of diverticula — small pouches bulging outward through a weak spot in the colon wall, thought to be caused by a low-fiber diet leading to chronic constipation. Usually occurs in the descending colon and is …","Pharmacology for Inflammatory Bowel Disease","5-ASA (Sulfasalazine) — Converts in the intestines into 5-aminosalicylic acid (active anti-inflammatory) and sulfapyridine (causes most side effects). Contraindicated with a sulfa allergy. Used for mild-to-moderate IBD; mesalamine is an alt…","DMARDs (Infliximab/Remicade) — A monoclonal antibody that neutralizes TNF-alpha. Requires pre-screening (vaccines up to date, titers, TB screening) since it suppresses the immune system. Risks: infection, cancer, heart failure. Given by inf…"]},{"s":"Part 3 — GI Symptom Pharmacology▾","a":"part-3-gi-symptom-pharmacology","k":["Part 3 — GI Symptom Pharmacology▾","These drug classes treat nausea/vomiting and diarrhea as symptoms that show up across many different GI conditions, rather than belonging to any single disease.","Ondansetron (Zofran) — Serotonin (5-HT3) receptor blocker acting on the chemoreceptor trigger zone; affects afferent vagal nerves. PO or IV. Watch for serotonin syndrome with SSRIs/SNRIs/TCAs/MAOIs/buspirone/tramadol, especially with daily …","Dimenhydrinate & Hydroxyzine — H1 receptor blockers (NOT H2) acting in the inner ear — used for motion sickness, dizziness, and nausea. Cause sedation and anticholinergic effects (elderly fall risk, similar to diphenhydramine/Benadryl). Nev…","Metoclopramide (Reglan) — Blocks dopamine receptors, increases LES tone, increases GI peristalsis. Used as an antiemetic (chemo/radiation/opioid-induced nausea) and a prokinetic (CF, post-op ileus). Rare but severe risk of EPS, more common …","MNEMONIC: Anticholinergic toxidrome — \"Mad as a hatter\" (CNS changes), \"blind as a bat\" (blurred vision), \"dry as a bone\" (dry secretions), \"red as a beet\" (flushing), \"hot as a hare\" (hyperthermia).","Akathisia — Restlessness — a constant urge to move.","Acute dystonia — Involuntary muscle contractions.","Parkinsonism — Tremors and rigid limb muscles, like Parkinson's disease.","Tardive dyskinesia — Late-onset repetitive involuntary facial movements (tongue twisting, chewing, lip smacking, grimacing).","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) is the worst-case EPS complication — rare but life-threatening. First signs: rigid muscles and fever, then drowsiness/confusion, which can progress to seizures.","Antidiarrheal drugs work by decreasing intestinal peristalsis, reducing fluid output. Main concerns: drowsiness and constipation. Atropine-containing combination products can cause bradycardia (anticholinergic, drying effect) — use caution …"]},{"s":"Part 4 — Endocrine Disorders (Adrenal Cortex, Adrenal Medulla & ADH)▾","a":"part-4-endocrine-disorders-adrenal-cortex-adrenal-medulla-adh","k":["Part 4 — Endocrine Disorders (Adrenal Cortex, Adrenal Medulla & ADH)▾","This section covers the adrenal glands end to end: the adrenal cortex (Cushing's syndrome and Addison's disease — a cortisol excess/deficiency mirror pair) and the adrenal medulla plus ADH disorders (pheochromocytoma, SIADH, and diabetes in…","MNEMONIC: The \"3 S's\" of adrenal cortex hormones — Sugar = cortisol, Salt = aldosterone, Sex = androgens.","Cortisol's normal functions: increases glucose availability, maintains vascular tone/BP, breaks down fat/protein for energy, suppresses the immune/inflammatory response, and activates CNS alertness. Nearly every Cushing's and Addison's find…","Cause — Primary (adrenal tumor), secondary (pituitary excess ACTH), or exogenous (chronic steroid use — most common cause in the U.S.) — Idiopathic, or (most common) autoimmune destruction of the adrenal cortex","Glucose effect — Glucose intolerance, hyperglycemia — Hypoglycemia, weakness, fatigue","BP / electrolyte effect — Hypertension; sodium/fluid retention — Low BP/vascular tone/cardiac output; hyponatremia and hyperkalemia","Body habitus — Moon face, buffalo hump, central obesity with thin limbs, striae, easy bruising, thin skin — Hyperpigmentation/tanned appearance (excess ACTH + MSH co-secretion)","Treatment — Treat the cause (surgery/radiation) or slowly taper exogenous steroids; aminoglutethimide or ketoconazole as a temporary bridge — Lifelong hydrocortisone + fludrocortisone; never abruptly stop","Manifestations typically don't become evident in Addison's until about 90% of the adrenal cortex has been destroyed. Sick-day teaching uses the \"3-by-3 rule\" — triple the usual glucocorticoid dose for three days during any stress event. Cor…","Pheochromocytoma","A rare tumor of the adrenal medulla causing excessive catecholamine (epinephrine/norepinephrine) release — about 90% benign (the \"-oma\" suffix typically signals a benign tumor). Hallmark: hypertension (intermittent or persistent) with the c…","Benign does not mean safe: the resulting severe hypertension puts the patient at high risk for stroke or cardiac events.","Surgical removal is definitive treatment. Phenoxybenzamine, a noncompetitive (irreversible) alpha blocker, is given ~10–14 days before surgery to control blood pressure — irreversibility is desirable here since tumor manipulation during sur…","Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH) Disorders","MNEMONIC: \"ADH stops the pee\" — ADH causes water retention, the opposite of a diuretic.","Serum sodium / osmolality — Low / Low — Very high / Very high","Urine osmolality / output — High (concentrated) / Low — Low (dilute) / Very high (up to 15 L/day)","Most common cause — Malignant tumor (small cell lung carcinoma) — Neurogenic: brain/pituitary damage (sudden, usually permanent). Nephrogenic: kidney can't respond to ADH — CKD or lithium (slow, progressive)","Treatment — Fluid restriction; loop diuretics only if sodium >125 mEq/L; demeclocycline for chronic/refractory cases — Neurogenic: desmopressin (DDAVP). Nephrogenic: thiazide diuretic (HCTZ) — DDAVP won't work since the kidneys, not the ADH…"]},{"s":"Part 5 — Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders▾","a":"part-5-thyroid-parathyroid-disorders","k":["Part 5 — Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders▾","Hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism are a mirror pair, much like Cushing's and Addison's disease. The parathyroid glands, though physically embedded in the thyroid, regulate a completely different hormone (calcium via PTH).","MNEMONIC: Thyroid hormone is the \"volume knob\" for body metabolism — turned up (hyperthyroidism) or down (hypothyroidism).","The HPT feedback loop: hypothalamus releases TRH → anterior pituitary releases TSH → thyroid produces T4 (largely inactive/prohormone) → T4 converts to T3 (the active form) in the body. Iodine is a necessary building block for thyroid hormo…","TSH — Increased (primary, ~95% of cases) / decreased (secondary) — Decreased (primary)","T3 / T4 — Decreased — Increased","Most common cause — Hashimoto's thyroiditis (autoimmune) — hallmark test: anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody — Graves' disease (autoimmune — thyroid-stimulating antibodies activate TSH receptors)","Key manifestations — Cold intolerance, weight gain, fatigue, bradycardia, constipation; later: puffy face, hair loss, decreased LOC — Heat intolerance, weight loss, nervousness/insomnia, tachyarrhythmias; Graves' ophthalmopathy (exophthalmo…","Emergency — Myxedema coma — loss of brain function from severe, prolonged, untreated hypothyroidism — Thyroid storm — can cause death within 48 hours if untreated, usually triggered by major stress","Treatment — Levothyroxine (lifelong) — take on an empty stomach, ~30 min before eating; increases warfarin effect — PTU (black-box hepatotoxicity warning), radioactive iodine (more common), or thyroidectomy (needs lifelong levothyroxine aft…","Amiodarone can cause EITHER hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism — same drug, either direction. Hashimoto's risk factors: women over 50, Caucasian, pregnancy, another autoimmune disorder, family history, amiodarone/lithium, or prior hyperthyro…","Parathyroid Disorders","The four parathyroid glands secrete PTH in response to low calcium; PTH promotes vitamin D activation by the kidney to help raise calcium.","Hypoparathyroidism","Usually from thyroidectomy trauma/damage to the parathyroid glands.","Causes hypocalcemia (cramping, tetany, convulsions; check Trousseau's and Chvostek's signs) and increased phosphate (PTH normally increases phosphate excretion).","Treatment: lifelong synthetic PTH replacement, normalized calcium and vitamin D.","Hyperparathyroidism","Excess PTH causes hypercalcemia and bone breakdown: muscle weakness, poor concentration, hypertension, kidney stones.","Treatment: diuretics, calcitonin, bisphosphonates, vitamin D, or surgery if caused by a tumor."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","A trick for tying the whole week together: nearly every topic this week has a named emergency to watch for, and most of them trace back to one hormone or one barrier going too far in one direction.","Esophageal & Gastric Disorders — Barrett's esophagus progressing to esophageal adenocarcinoma; PUD complications (hemorrhage, obstruction, perforation).","Lower GI Disorders — A ruptured appendix or diverticulum causing peritonitis; toxic megacolon in ulcerative colitis.","GI Symptom Pharmacology — Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) from dopamine-blocking antiemetics.","Endocrine Disorders — Addisonian crisis (cortisol deficiency) and its mirror, severe Cushing syndrome (cortisol excess); uncontrolled diabetes insipidus progressing to hypovolemic shock.","Thyroid & Parathyroid Disorders — Myxedema coma (severe hypothyroidism) and its mirror, thyroid storm (severe hyperthyroidism)."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the five parts above — a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week6-ischemic-heart-disease-heart-failure.html","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure","secs":[{"s":"Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure","a":"","k":["Week 6 · Topic 2","Coronary Artery Disease (Ischemic Heart Disease)","Risk Factors for CAD","Angina","Myocardial Infarction (MI)","Heart Failure: Definition & Core Physiology","Cardiomyopathy — the Structural Root of Heart Failure","Causes & Risk Factors of Heart Failure","Left-Sided vs. Right-Sided Heart Failure","HFrEF vs. HFpEF","Chronic vs. Decompensated Heart Failure","Ventricular Remodeling","S3 Gallop","Self-Test Flashcards","Cardiovascular disease breaks down into three broad categories: \"electrical\" problems (dysrhythmias — the conduction system), \"plumbing\" problems (the vessels — ischemic/coronary artery disease), and \"pump\" problems (the heart muscle itself…"]},{"s":"Coronary Artery Disease (Ischemic Heart Disease)▾","a":"coronary-artery-disease-ischemic-heart-disease","k":["Coronary Artery Disease (Ischemic Heart Disease)▾","Coronary artery disease (CAD) is driven by atherosclerosis: plaque builds up inside the coronary arteries, narrowing them and reducing blood flow to the myocardium. \"Plumbing\" problems in the heart aren't only blockage — the coronary arteri…","Endothelial Dysfunction — the Earliest Step","The vessel's inner lining (endothelium) is damaged, which sets up the conditions for plaque to form there.","Normally, when the heart needs more oxygen, coronary arteries dilate to deliver more blood. With endothelial dysfunction, they do the opposite — they vasoconstrict instead of dilating, worsening ischemia on top of whatever plaque is already…","Driven by diabetes, hyperlipidemia, hypertension, and smoking — a major contributor to CAD and heart attacks.","The LAD — the \"Widow Maker\"","The left anterior descending (LAD) artery supplies a large portion of the heart muscle, feeding the left ventricle — the chamber that determines perfusion to the rest of the body.","It's especially dangerous because it typically has no collateral circulation to fall back on — so when it clogs, there's no backup blood supply, which is why a LAD blockage carries the nickname \"widow maker.\"","CAD is extremely common: roughly 1 in 7 deaths in the US is attributed to it, and about half the US population has dyslipidemia, a major driver."]},{"s":"Risk Factors for CAD▾","a":"risk-factors-for-cad","k":["Risk Factors for CAD▾","Non-Modifiable","Age","Family history","Gender — estrogen is cardioprotective before menopause, so risk changes after menopause","Ethnicity — Black, Hispanic, and Native American populations have higher risk, with a documented disparity in CAD-related deaths linked to structural racism","Genetics/genetic variants (SNPs)","Modifiable","Hypertension — roughly 2–3 times the risk","Smoking","Diabetes","Obesity/physical inactivity","Diet — a DASH-style diet is protective","Hyperlipidemia","Depression/stress/anxiety"]},{"s":"Angina▾","a":"angina","k":["Angina▾","Angina generally means chest pain, but it can also show up with a wider associated symptom complex: heartburn, palpitations, weakness, dizziness, anxiety, nausea, cold sweats, or burning sensations in the chest, shoulder, or abdomen. It's s…","Differentiating cardiac from non-cardiac chest pain is a key skill — and within cardiac chest pain, telling stable angina apart from unstable angina (and from a full MI) matters just as much.","Cardiac — Diffuse, hard to pinpoint — described as pressure, tightness, or \"an elephant sitting on my chest.\" Often radiates to the left arm, jaw, or left shoulder, with diaphoresis and pallor.","Non-cardiac — Focal — the patient can point right to it (\"it's right here\").","Stable Angina","Caused by an imbalance between myocardial oxygen supply and demand","Triggered by exertion","Relieved by rest","Typically lasts 2–5 minutes","Treated with nitrates","Unstable Angina","Coronary blood flow is diminished but not completely blocked — a \"partially open water hose\" instead of stable angina's fully-open one","A more dangerous pattern than stable angina","Not reliably relieved by rest","The higher-risk counterpart to stable angina — a step closer to a full MI"]},{"s":"Myocardial Infarction (MI)▾","a":"myocardial-infarction-mi","k":["Myocardial Infarction (MI)▾","MI Symptom Pattern","Pain not relieved by rest — this is what distinguishes MI pain from stable angina","Pain that radiates","Nausea","Vomiting","Dyspnea","Diaphoresis","Women Often Present Atypically","Early CAD/MI research was done mostly in men. Women are more likely to have: hot or burning discomfort/tenderness rather than classic pressure or tightness; pain in a location other than the chest; and nonspecific symptoms like indigestion,…","Call 911 if chest pain is not resolved within about 5 minutes — this window is the concern point for unstable angina or a full MI."]},{"s":"Heart Failure: Definition & Core Physiology▾","a":"heart-failure-definition-core-physiology","k":["Heart Failure: Definition & Core Physiology▾","Using the AHA's framing, heart failure (HF) is fundamentally the heart's inability to pump enough blood to meet the body's needs.","Cardiac output (CO) — CO = heart rate (HR) × stroke volume (SV).","Preload — The fluid volume the heart is working with.","Afterload — The pressure/resistance the heart has to pump against — related to blood pressure.","Contractility — How forcefully the heart squeezes. Preload, afterload, and contractility together determine stroke volume.","The Heart Failure Hemodynamic Chain","Contractility decreases first","Which leads to increased preload — fluid backs up","Which leads to increased afterload","This chain is why HF's hallmark presentation involves fluid volume overload."]},{"s":"Cardiomyopathy — the Structural Root of Heart Failure▾","a":"cardiomyopathy-the-structural-root-of-heart-failure","k":["Cardiomyopathy — the Structural Root of Heart Failure▾","Cardiomyopathy is structural muscle disease of the heart that can lead to heart failure. It's usually idiopathic (no identifiable cause), but can also result from infections, toxins, myocarditis, or inherited disorders. There are three type…","Dilated — Ischemic damage, valve disease, alcohol use, peripartum (pregnancy-related) cardiomyopathy. — Leads to HFrEF (reduced ejection fraction/systolic dysfunction).","Hypertrophic — Related to hypertension. — High risk of sudden cardiac death and arrhythmias.","Restrictive — Least common type; often associated with amyloid deposits. — The ventricle becomes stiff and non-compliant — it can't relax and fill properly; tends to cause right-sided heart failure."]},{"s":"Causes & Risk Factors of Heart Failure▾","a":"causes-risk-factors-of-heart-failure","k":["Causes & Risk Factors of Heart Failure▾","Causes of Heart Failure","Repeated ischemic episodes or prior MI — damaged muscle can't pump as well","Papillary muscle rupture","Chronic hypertension","COPD — specifically leads to right-sided heart failure by straining the right side of the heart against high pulmonary pressures","Dysrhythmias","Valve disorders — especially mitral insufficiency and aortic stenosis","Pulmonary emboli","Cross-reference: dysrhythmias are a cause of heart failure listed above — see Cardiac Dysrhythmias for how they actually arise and disrupt the heart's rhythm","Stable angina by itself is generally NOT considered a risk factor for heart failure — the instructor specifically called this out as something students might mistakenly assume.","Risk Factors for Heart Failure","Hypertension — the single greatest risk factor","Diabetes","Having had an MI — about 22% of men and 46% of women who have an MI go on to develop heart failure","Age over 65 — heart failure is the most common cause of hospitalization in this age group","Black/African-American patients have a higher incidence","A strong genetic/familial link","COPD","Severe anemia","Congenital heart defects","Viral myocarditis","Alcohol, cocaine, or crack abuse","Kidney disease","Obesity","Marijuana — unlike alcohol, cocaine, and crack — is not currently associated with heart failure risk. The instructor called this out specifically so it isn't lumped in with the other substances above."]},{"s":"Left-Sided vs. Right-Sided Heart Failure▾","a":"left-sided-vs-right-sided-heart-failure","k":["Left-Sided vs. Right-Sided Heart Failure▾","Left-Sided Heart Failure","Blood backs up into the lungs — causes pulmonary congestion (pulmonary edema); the LV enlarges trying to compensate","Most common cause: poorly controlled hypertension","Symptoms: cough, crackles, wheezes, frothy or blood-tinged sputum, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea (PND — sudden shortness of breath that wakes a patient at night), orthopnea (shortness of breath when lying flat — patients often sit up in a tr…","Right-Sided Heart Failure","Blood backs up into the venous system — causes systemic (body-wide) congestion; the RV enlarges trying to compensate","Most common cause: COPD (specifically severe COPD)","Symptoms: jugular venous distension (JVD), dependent edema, weight gain, hepatosplenomegaly (enlarged liver/spleen), ascites, and poor appetite/anorexia (both from venous congestion backing up into the GI tract), and fatigue","In practice, the disease usually progresses to affect both sides eventually and patients show a mix of symptoms — but for this class and NCLEX, know how to sort a symptom into left-sided (pulmonary) vs. right-sided (systemic).","MNEMONIC — \"Left = lungs, right = body\": left-sided failure backs up into the lungs (pulmonary); right-sided failure backs up into the rest of the body (systemic)."]},{"s":"HFrEF vs. HFpEF▾","a":"hfref-vs-hfpef","k":["HFrEF vs. HFpEF▾","Normal ejection fraction (EF) is 55–65%. EF is measured by echocardiogram — transthoracic (TTE) or transesophageal (TEE) — and that measurement is how a patient is sorted into HFrEF or HFpEF.","HFrEF (Reduced EF) — \"Systolic Heart Failure\"","Ejection fraction under 40%","Reflects impaired contractile (pumping) function","HFpEF (Preserved EF) — \"Diastolic Heart Failure\"","Ejection fraction of 40–50%","Previously called \"diastolic dysfunction\"","The muscle can still squeeze adequately, but the ventricles can't relax and fill properly","Associated with older age, female sex, diabetes, obesity, and chronic hypertension"]},{"s":"Chronic vs. Decompensated Heart Failure▾","a":"chronic-vs-decompensated-heart-failure","k":["Chronic vs. Decompensated Heart Failure▾","The focus of this class is chronic heart failure — a progressive disease. Within that chronic course, patients can have episodes of decompensated heart failure: new or worsening signs/symptoms, usually driven by volume overload, that send t…"]},{"s":"Ventricular Remodeling▾","a":"ventricular-remodeling","k":["Ventricular Remodeling▾","In heart failure, the body inappropriately releases substances that are meant to help but end up worsening the problem — this is ventricular remodeling, and it progressively worsens heart failure over time.","Substances Inappropriately Released","Angiotensin II","Aldosterone","Endothelin","TNF-alpha","Catecholamines","Effects on the Heart Muscle","Cell death (apoptosis)","Hypertrophy (thickening)","Collagen deposits","Myocardial fibrosis (scarring)","Cross-reference: pharmacologic treatment aimed at interrupting this remodeling process — ACE inhibitors, ARBs, ARNIs, beta blockers, MRAs — is covered in full on Cardiac Pharmacology"]},{"s":"S3 Gallop▾","a":"s3-gallop","k":["S3 Gallop▾","S3 gallop — A low-pitched heart sound heard just after S2, occurring in early diastole. In adults over 40, an S3 gallop is considered an indicator of heart failure."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week6-cardiac-dysrhythmias.html","t":"Cardiac Dysrhythmias","secs":[{"s":"Cardiac Dysrhythmias","a":"","k":["Week 6 · Topic 3","Cardiac Conduction Basics","Three Mechanisms Behind Dysrhythmias","Narrow-QRS (Supraventricular) Rhythms","Wide-QRS (Ventricular) Rhythms","Self-Test Flashcards","This page covers the heart's ELECTRICAL system — the conduction problems and abnormal rhythms that show up on an EKG. Structural and pump problems (coronary artery disease, myocardial infarction, heart failure) are a separate topic and are …","Cross-reference: coronary artery disease, MI, and heart failure are covered in full on the Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure page"]},{"s":"Cardiac Conduction Basics▾","a":"cardiac-conduction-basics","k":["Cardiac Conduction Basics▾","Coordinated heartbeats depend on four core properties of cardiac cells.","Automaticity — The ability to spontaneously generate an electrical impulse.","Excitability — The ability to respond to a stimulus.","Conductivity — The ability to transmit an impulse to neighboring cells.","Contractility — The ability to contract in response to the impulse.","Depolarization & repolarization","Depolarization is the electrical event that triggers systole (contraction).","Repolarization is the cell \"recharging\" back to its resting state.","P wave — Atrial depolarization.","QRS complex — Ventricular depolarization.","T wave — Ventricular repolarization.","Atrial repolarization — Not visible on the EKG — it happens at the same time as ventricular depolarization and is buried inside the QRS complex.","Normal conduction pathway: SA node → AV node → bundle of His → Purkinje fibers. The isoelectric line is the flat baseline of the EKG seen between waveforms.","Normal sinus rhythm — the baseline every rhythm below is compared against","Rate: 60–100 bpm","Rhythm: regular","PR interval: 0.12–0.20 seconds","QRS complex: under 0.12 seconds (narrow)","A 6-second strip is a standard way to estimate heart rate directly off an EKG strip. On the strip, each small box represents 0.04 seconds.","MNEMONIC: read every strip with the same four questions, in order — (1) What's the rate? (2) Is it regular or irregular? (3) Is there a P wave, and does it look normal? (4) Is the QRS narrow or wide? Almost every rhythm on this page falls o…"]},{"s":"Three Mechanisms Behind Dysrhythmias▾","a":"three-mechanisms-behind-dysrhythmias","k":["Three Mechanisms Behind Dysrhythmias▾","Inappropriate automaticity — a cell that shouldn't fire an impulse does so anyway; often from electrolyte imbalances (especially potassium, but also calcium or magnesium) or from a heart attack.","Triggered activity — an extra impulse generated during or just after repolarization; from things like digoxin toxicity, sympathetic nervous system overstimulation, or a genetic cause.","Re-entry — from myocardial ischemia or electrolyte imbalance, where an impulse abnormally re-circulates instead of dying out."]},{"s":"Narrow-QRS (Supraventricular) Rhythms▾","a":"narrow-qrs-supraventricular-rhythms","k":["Narrow-QRS (Supraventricular) Rhythms▾","These rhythms all originate at or above the AV node, and every one of them keeps a narrow QRS (under 0.12 seconds) — the ventricles are still being depolarized through the normal His-Purkinje pathway, even when the P wave and rate are abnor…","Sinus arrhythmia — Normal, fluctuates with respiration — Varies with breathing (speeds up on inhalation, slows on exhalation) — Normal; PR interval normal — Narrow — Normal variant, not a true dysrhythmia; common in younger people","Sinus bradycardia — Under 60 bpm — Regular — Normal; PR interval normal — Narrow — Electrolyte imbalance (esp. high K+), vagal stimulation, digoxin toxicity, late-stage hypoxia, meds (beta blockers, CCBs, amiodarone), MI affecting the SA no…","Sinus tachycardia — 100–150 bpm — Regular — Normal; PR interval normal — Narrow — Exercise, pain, anxiety, fever, fluid volume deficit, epinephrine/albuterol, caffeine/nicotine/cocaine, early-stage hypoxia","PSVT — 150–250 bpm — Regular — Not discernible — Narrow — Re-entry; overexertion, stimulants, digoxin toxicity, rheumatic/coronary heart disease, Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome","PACs — Early beat, otherwise normal — Interrupted by the early beat — Abnormal-looking P wave; PR interval normal — Narrow, follows normally — Generally benign; frequent PACs raise afib risk","Atrial flutter — Atrial up to 250 bpm; ventricular slower — Regular — Sawtooth/\"shark-tooth\" flutter waves instead of P waves — Narrow — Re-entry from the AV node; structural heart disease, cardiomyopathy, valve disease, congenital heart di…","Atrial fibrillation — Ventricular ~100–175 bpm — Irregularly irregular — No discernible P wave; fine fibrillation waves — Narrow — Electrolyte imbalance, hypoxia, \"sick heart\" (CAD, prior MI)","Sinus bradycardia — nursing approach","Symptoms of concern (from decreased cardiac output): lightheadedness/dizziness (especially with exertion), fatigue, syncope or near-syncope, dyspnea, chest pain/discomfort, confusion.","Some people (especially very fit patients) live with a naturally slower rate and are fine — treat only if the patient is symptomatic.","If severe: atropine or a pacemaker.","Sinus tachycardia — nursing approach","Fluid volume deficit is often one of the FIRST signs seen as sinus tachycardia — treat the underlying cause.","For cardiac causes: beta blockers such as metoprolol, carvedilol, or propranolol (see Cardiac Pharmacology).","PSVT — causes & symptoms","Onset and offset are sudden (paroxysmal), and the mechanism is re-entry.","Causes: overexertion, stimulants, digoxin toxicity, rheumatic or coronary heart disease, Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome (which can precipitate PSVT via other rhythm abnormalities and right-sided heart failure).","Symptoms: palpitations, chest pain, fatigue, dizziness, dyspnea, and low blood pressure.","PACs: generally a benign finding on their own, but frequent PACs can signal an increased risk for developing atrial fibrillation — if a patient has frequent PACs, it's worth checking their electrolytes and oxygenation status.","Atrial flutter","Originates from the AV node, effectively overriding the SA node as the pacemaker; mechanism is re-entry.","Regular, sawtooth (\"shark-tooth\") flutter waves in place of P waves.","Atrial rate can be as high as 250 bpm, but the ventricular rate is slower because not every atrial impulse conducts through — described as a conduction ratio (2:1, 3:1, 4:1 = atrial beats : ventricular beats).","QRS stays narrow.","Atrial fibrillation","Multiple chaotic electrical foci fire within the atria at once, producing an \"irregularly irregular\" rhythm.","Ventricular rate is typically ~100–175 bpm; no discernible P wave, only fine fibrillation waves.","The atria quiver instead of contracting in a coordinated way, so the patient loses the \"atrial kick\" (the atria's contribution to ventricular filling) — this costs about 20% of cardiac output.","A key clinical question in managing AFib is whether the ventricular rate is controlled.","Atrial fibrillation — symptoms","Palpitations (\"my heart is racing\"), fatigue, dizziness, chest discomfort, shortness of breath.","How symptomatic a patient is depends on how sick the heart is and how much cardiac output they're getting — a rate of 170 is far more likely to be symptomatic than a rate of 110.","A large portion of patients with AFib are entirely asymptomatic and don't know they have it — AFib is the most common type of treated dysrhythmia overall, but plenty of cases go undiagnosed.","Atrial fibrillation — complications & treatment","Complications: heart failure, and embolus/stroke risk, including pulmonary emboli.","Untreated AFib roughly DOUBLES the risk of cardiac death and increases stroke risk 4- to 5-fold; AFib is present in an estimated 15–20% of all strokes.","Treatment has two parts: rate control (beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, digitalis, or amiodarone) and stroke prevention (anticoagulants or antiplatelets) — see Cardiac Pharmacology for the drugs themselves.","Non-pharmacologic options like ablation or cardioversion are typically handled in more specialized (med-surg/higher-acuity) settings.","Atrial flutter is treated with a similar approach, though it carries a somewhat lower stroke risk than AFib."]},{"s":"Wide-QRS (Ventricular) Rhythms▾","a":"wide-qrs-ventricular-rhythms","k":["Wide-QRS (Ventricular) Rhythms▾","Ventricular dysrhythmias are a higher-acuity/critical-care-level topic. This is also where the QRS first becomes WIDE — a key signal that the impulse is no longer traveling through the normal, fast His-Purkinje pathway.","PVCs — Early beat, comes sooner than expected — Interrupted by the early beat — None associated with the beat — Wide (over 0.12 seconds) — Electrolyte imbalance is the single biggest cause; also stimulants, hypoxia, fever, exercise, emotion…","V-tach — Very fast — Regular — None present — Wide — MI, coronary artery disease, severe electrolyte abnormalities, heart failure, drug toxicity","V-fib — No measurable rate — Chaotic, no coordinated contraction — None present — No organized QRS — Deadly rhythm — no effective cardiac output","PVCs — repeating patterns","PVCs originate from an ectopic focus in the ventricle.","Bigeminy: a PVC every other beat.","Trigeminy: a PVC every third beat.","Quadrigeminy: a PVC every fourth beat.","There is no direct drug treatment specifically for PVCs — the underlying cause is treated instead.","V-tach is a deadly rhythm. Defined as three or more PVCs in a row — an ectopic ventricular focus firing rapidly, regular but very fast, with no P wave present. Requires an IMMEDIATE pulse check: if the patient has a pulse, treatment follows…","V-fib is a deadly rhythm. The ventricles quiver chaotically without any effective, coordinated contraction — there is no measurable heart rate or cardiac output. The nurse must check for a pulse and begin CPR immediately if the patient is p…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week6-peripheral-vascular-disease.html","t":"Peripheral Vascular Disease","secs":[{"s":"Peripheral Vascular Disease","a":"","k":["Week 6 · Topic 4","Peripheral Artery Disease (PAD) — Risk Factors","Other Causes of Arterial Occlusion","Clinical Manifestations of PAD","Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI)","Arterial vs. Venous Disease","Chronic Venous Insufficiency","Treatment — Shared Between PAD and PVD","Pharmacology for Intermittent Claudication","Self-Test Flashcards","Peripheral vascular disease (PVD) is an umbrella term covering two conditions: peripheral artery disease (PAD) and peripheral venous disease (chronic venous insufficiency). They share some risk factors and general treatment principles, but …","Cross-reference: atherosclerosis in the lower extremities is the same underlying disease process as coronary artery disease, just occurring in a different vascular bed — see Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure"]},{"s":"Peripheral Artery Disease (PAD) — Risk Factors▾","a":"peripheral-artery-disease-pad-risk-factors","k":["Peripheral Artery Disease (PAD) — Risk Factors▾","Risk Factors","Smoking — the single biggest risk factor","Uncontrolled diabetes","High cholesterol","Existing heart disease or history of stroke","Age over 50","Affects men and women roughly equally","Smoking is the single biggest risk factor for PAD: more than 80% of PAD patients are current or former smokers, and smoking doubles the risk of amputation."]},{"s":"Other Causes of Arterial Occlusion▾","a":"other-causes-of-arterial-occlusion","k":["Other Causes of Arterial Occlusion▾","Besides atherosclerosis itself, a few other mechanisms can occlude an artery.","Thrombus formation — A blood clot forms within the artery and blocks flow. — Direct mechanical blockage of arterial flow.","Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease) — An autoimmune condition strongly associated with smokers. — Causes permanent arterial occlusion.","Vasospasm (Raynaud's phenomenon) — Sudden constriction/spasm of small arteries. — Associated with scleroderma and pulmonary hypertension, with a genetic predisposition component."]},{"s":"Clinical Manifestations of PAD▾","a":"clinical-manifestations-of-pad","k":["Clinical Manifestations of PAD▾","The hallmark symptom is pain, typically in the calf or buttock, classically described as intermittent claudication — exertional cramping pain that is relieved by rest. It's sometimes called \"angina of the lower extremity\" because the mechan…","Other Manifestations","Non-healing wounds","Diminished sensation","Trophic skin changes — skin becomes shiny and thick, with hair loss on the affected limb","Diminished or absent pulses","Pallor when the limb is elevated","Cyanosis","Dependent rubor — a deep red color when the limb is hanging down","Erectile dysfunction — can occur when pelvic/lower-extremity arterial flow is affected","MNEMONIC — The 5 P's (significant arterial compromise): Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paresthesias, and Paralysis (weakness)."]},{"s":"Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI)▾","a":"ankle-brachial-index-abi","k":["Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI)▾","ABI is the diagnostic test used for PAD: it compares blood pressure at the ankle to blood pressure at the arm.","~1.0–1.4 — Normal ratio.","0.5 or below — Severe arterial disease."]},{"s":"Arterial vs. Venous Disease▾","a":"arterial-vs-venous-disease","k":["Arterial vs. Venous Disease▾","PAD (arterial) and chronic venous insufficiency (venous) are contrasted repeatedly by the same features — pain quality, skin changes, and where ulcers form.","PAD (Arterial)","Intermittent claudication — exertional cramping, relieved by rest","Pallor with limb elevation; dependent rubor when limb hangs down","Diminished or absent pulses","Skin: shiny, thick, with hair loss (trophic changes)","Ulcers typically form at the toes or the top (dorsum) of the foot","Chronic Venous Insufficiency (Venous)","Aching and tiredness in the legs — NOT burning or heaviness","Edema","Skin: leathery, dark/tan discoloration, flaking","Varicose veins commonly associated","Stasis ulcers typically form at the ankles"]},{"s":"Chronic Venous Insufficiency▾","a":"chronic-venous-insufficiency","k":["Chronic Venous Insufficiency▾","Caused by dysfunction of the venous walls and valves, leading to venous stasis — blood pooling in the legs instead of returning efficiently to the heart. It's described as affecting roughly 40% of the U.S. population.","Symptoms","Edema","Aching and tiredness in the legs","Leathery, dark/tan skin discoloration","Stasis ulcers — typically form at the ankles","Flaking skin","Varicose veins are noted as a precursor to, or commonly associated finding with, chronic venous insufficiency."]},{"s":"Treatment — Shared Between PAD and PVD▾","a":"treatment-shared-between-pad-and-pvd","k":["Treatment — Shared Between PAD and PVD▾","General Treatment","Smoking cessation — emphasized repeatedly as the single most important intervention","Increased physical activity — encourages the body to build collateral circulation around blocked vessels","Weight loss","Stress reduction","Controlling diabetes and hypertension","For severe arterial occlusion, procedural options include bypass surgery, balloon angioplasty, or stent placement."]},{"s":"Pharmacology for Intermittent Claudication▾","a":"pharmacology-for-intermittent-claudication","k":["Pharmacology for Intermittent Claudication▾","Cilostazol (Pletal) — Works as both a platelet inhibitor and a vasodilator. Has CYP450 drug interactions. Side effects: headache, dizziness, diarrhea, palpitations, and peripheral edema.","Pentoxifylline — Works by increasing blood flow and oxygen delivery to the affected tissue. Side effects: nausea, vomiting, and dizziness. Dosed TID (three times a day).","Most patients with PAD are also placed on a statin plus an antiplatelet or anticoagulant agent — examples given: aspirin, clopidogrel, warfarin, and apixaban."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week6-valve-disease.html","t":"Valve Disease","secs":[{"s":"Valve Disease","a":"","k":["Week 6 · Topic 5","Valve Anatomy & Blood Flow","Two Categories of Valve Dysfunction","Aortic Stenosis & Mitral Regurgitation","Diagnosis","Infective Endocarditis","Rheumatic Heart Disease","Treatment","Self-Test Flashcards","The heart has four valves, and their job is to keep blood moving forward in one direction. Valve disease happens when a valve's leaflets stop working properly — either they get too tight and restrict forward flow, or they get too floppy and…"]},{"s":"Valve Anatomy & Blood Flow▾","a":"valve-anatomy-blood-flow","k":["Valve Anatomy & Blood Flow▾","Blood flows through the four heart valves in a fixed order.","MNEMONIC: \"TP-MA\" — tricuspid → pulmonic → mitral → aortic. Also remembered as \"tricycle before bicycle\": the first two valves in the order (tricuspid, pulmonic) each have three leaflets like a tricycle's three wheels, and the pattern shift…","Tricuspid — 3 — Atrioventricular (AV) valve","Pulmonic — 3 — Semilunar (ventricular) valve","Mitral — 2 — Atrioventricular (AV) valve","Aortic — 3 — Semilunar (ventricular) valve","The tricuspid and mitral valves are grouped together as the AV valves; the pulmonic and aortic valves are grouped together as the semilunar (ventricular) valves. The tricuspid and aortic valves specifically open during systole.","General mechanisms of valve damage","Normal wear and tear over time","Calcium deposits building up on the leaflets","Problems with the valve annulus (the ring the leaflets attach to)","Leaflet malcoaptation — the leaflets don't fully open or fully close as they should","Endocarditis/infection","Clot formation","Papillary muscle rupture — applies only to the AV valves (tricuspid and mitral), since only they are anchored by papillary muscles/chordae tendineae"]},{"s":"Two Categories of Valve Dysfunction▾","a":"two-categories-of-valve-dysfunction","k":["Two Categories of Valve Dysfunction▾","Stenosis","The leaflets become too tight or stiff.","This restricts forward flow through the valve.","Most commonly seen in the aortic valve.","Regurgitation","The leaflets become floppy or loose and don't close properly.","This allows blood to flow backward instead of moving forward.","Most commonly seen in the mitral valve.","The lecture focuses specifically on left-sided valve disease, because the left side of the heart works against much higher pressure than the right side — this makes left-sided valve problems generally more clinically significant."]},{"s":"Aortic Stenosis & Mitral Regurgitation▾","a":"aortic-stenosis-mitral-regurgitation","k":["Aortic Stenosis & Mitral Regurgitation▾","Aortic stenosis","Classic triad: syncope, lightheadedness, and chest pain.","The chest pain occurs because the coronary arteries — which branch off just past the aortic valve — don't receive adequate forward blood flow when the valve is stenotic.","If it becomes severe, it can progress to pulmonary edema, with crackles and cough.","Mitral regurgitation","Causes fatigue from reduced forward cardiac output — blood is regurgitating backward instead of moving forward efficiently.","Causes pulmonary symptoms/dyspnea from the backward flow of blood into the left atrium and then into the pulmonary veins/lungs."]},{"s":"Diagnosis▾","a":"diagnosis","k":["Diagnosis▾","TTE (transthoracic echocardiogram) — The standard/\"regular\" echocardiogram used to diagnose valve disease.","TEE (transesophageal echocardiogram) — A more invasive alternative to a TTE that provides a closer, more detailed view."]},{"s":"Infective Endocarditis▾","a":"infective-endocarditis","k":["Infective Endocarditis▾","Vegetations are infective masses that form on the valve leaflets. They impair the valve's ability to close properly and can also break off and embolize — travel elsewhere in the body.","Most commonly affected valve — The tricuspid valve — it's the first valve blood encounters when it enters the heart. This makes it especially relevant to IV drug users, since bloodstream infections introduced via IV injection travel through…","Common causative organisms — Staphylococcus aureus and Staphylococcus epidermidis.","Risk groups — Patients with prosthetic (artificial) heart valves, and IV drug users.","Symptoms — Fever, chills, weight loss, inability to eat, muscle and joint pain, and heart murmurs (which can be difficult to hear/auscultate).","Patients with prosthetic heart valves require prophylactic antibiotics before dental procedures. Dental work is a known source of transient bacteremia, which puts a prosthetic valve at risk for infection.","Septic emboli","Septic emboli that break off from vegetations on the tricuspid valve (right-sided) travel to the lungs.","Septic emboli that break off from a left-sided valve travel systemically — to the brain, coronary arteries, spleen, kidney, or bowel.","Roughly 40% of infective endocarditis patients develop neurologic or embolic stroke-type symptoms. Meningitis, seizures, encephalopathy, and brain abscess are also possible complications.","Classic peripheral embolic/immunologic signs of infective endocarditis:","Petechiae — Small red/purple pinpoint spots.","Splinter hemorrhages — Thin, dark red-brown lines under the nail beds.","Janeway lesions — Non-tender, flat spots on the palms or soles.","Osler's nodes — Tender, raised nodules on the fingertips. A helpful distinction: Osler's nodes are tender, while Janeway lesions are not.","Roth spots — Spots seen on the retina during an eye exam."]},{"s":"Rheumatic Heart Disease▾","a":"rheumatic-heart-disease","k":["Rheumatic Heart Disease▾","Another named cause of valve damage, originating from a rheumatic illness in childhood. Noted as especially relevant background for students who may go on to work in cardiovascular ICU or progressive/step-down cardiac care settings."]},{"s":"Treatment▾","a":"treatment","k":["Treatment▾","Valve replacement","The treatment for significant valve disease.","When infection is involved, any active bloodstream infection must be cleared with antibiotics before and after surgery.","Cross-reference: antibiotic considerations around valve infection are covered in more depth in Cardiac Pharmacology"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week6-cardiac-pharmacology.html","t":"Cardiac Pharmacology","secs":[{"s":"Cardiac Pharmacology","a":"","k":["Week 6 · Topic 6","Antianginal Drugs","Heart Failure Drugs","Rate & Rhythm Control Drugs","Self-Test Flashcards","This page pulls together all three cardiac pharmacology voiceovers from this week — antianginal drugs, heart failure drugs, and rate/rhythm-control (antidysrhythmic) drugs — into one section, since the instructor covered them as one continu…","New This Week — Full Detail Below","Nitrates & ranolazine (antianginals)","RAS inhibitors / ARNI (sacubitril-valsartan)","Spironolactone (MRA) in heart failure","SGLT2 inhibitors in heart failure","Digoxin (digitalis)","Amiodarone, atropine, adenosine, dofetilide","Pull-Forward — Review Your Drug Matrix","Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Statins","Aspirin / clopidogrel","ACE inhibitors / ARBs","Warfarin","Cross-reference: cilostazol and pentoxifylline for claudication are peripheral-vascular-specific rather than general cardiac pharm — they're covered on Peripheral Vascular Disease, not repeated here"]},{"s":"Antianginal Drugs▾","a":"antianginal-drugs","k":["Antianginal Drugs▾","For the underlying pathophysiology of stable angina, see Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure. Four drug categories are used for stable angina, each acting on a different piece of the heart's oxygen supply/demand balance.","How Each Category Works","Nitrates — decrease preload via venodilation","Beta blockers — decrease heart rate and contractility (pull-forward — review your drug matrix for full profile)","Calcium channel blockers — decrease afterload and heart rate/contractility (pull-forward — review your drug matrix for full profile)","Ranolazine — improves myocardial energy efficiency (the exact mechanism wasn't detailed further)","Stepped treatment approach for stable angina: start with a beta blocker or a calcium channel blocker plus a nitrate, reassess in about 3 months, and escalate treatment if needed.","Sublingual (Nitrostat) — Used for an active anginal attack. Give every 5 minutes as needed, up to 3 doses total.","Transdermal patch — Applied in the morning and removed in the evening, rotating to hairless application sites.","Topical ointment (Nitro-Bid) — Another topical formulation used for antianginal therapy.","IV nitroglycerin — ICU use only. Requires a glass IV bottle and special (non-standard) IV tubing.","Long-acting oral (isosorbide) — Used for prevention only, not for an active attack. Must be tapered when discontinued to avoid rebound coronary vasospasm. If breakthrough chest pain occurs, the patient should NOT take an extra isosorbide do…","Nitroglycerin — Mechanism & Watch-Fors","Mechanism: venodilation (↓ preload), with some arterial dilation too — across every formulation above","Side effects: headache, hypotension, reflex tachycardia (from the vasodilation itself)","Tolerance: develops rapidly with continued use","Dangerous interaction: severe hypotension with sildenafil, some antihypertensives, or alcohol","MNEMONIC — the nitrate \"3 H's\": Headache, Hypotension, reflex tacHycardia. All three come from the same vasodilating mechanism that treats the angina in the first place.","If chest pain is not relieved within 5 minutes of a sublingual nitroglycerin dose, the patient should call 911 — this raises concern for unstable angina or STEMI, not just stable angina.","Ranolazine (Ranexa) the exception","Oral only — the one antianginal here with no injectable/topical form","Key risk: can prolong the QT interval → risk of lethal dysrhythmias","Unlike the others: does NOT drop blood pressure or heart rate the way nitrates do","Use caution with renal or hepatic impairment","Side effects: headache, dizziness, nausea, constipation","CYP3A4 inhibitor — avoid grapefruit juice and other CYP3A4-interacting drugs"]},{"s":"Heart Failure Drugs▾","a":"heart-failure-drugs","k":["Heart Failure Drugs▾","Heart failure drugs are broadly used across left- vs. right-sided and systolic (HFrEF) vs. diastolic (HFpEF) heart failure, though HFpEF tends to respond somewhat less well to these medications than HFrEF does. For the underlying pathophysi…","THE 4 PILLARS OF HFrEF THERAPY: modern heart failure treatment rests on four drug classes, each doing a different job. Diuretics, nitrates, and digoxin are added on top of these four as needed — they aren't part of the core pillars.","1 — RAS Inhibitor ACEI / ARB / ARNI","The \"unloader\" — ↓ preload & afterload, suppresses aldosterone, interrupts remodeling. Only ONE of the three at a time, never combined.","2 — Beta Blocker carvedilol preferred","The \"calmer\" — counteracts the chronic sympathetic overactivation that drives remodeling.","3 — MRA spironolactone","The \"de-congester\" — suppresses sodium/water retention to offload the left ventricle (not primarily acting as a diuretic here).","4 — SGLT2 Inhibitor the \"-gliflozins\"","The newest pillar — helps \"unload\" the ventricle and supports cardiac metabolism, even in patients without diabetes.","Pillar 1 — RAS Inhibitors (ACE Inhibitors, ARBs, ARNIs)","ACE inhibitors & ARBs are pull-forward drugs — review your drug matrix for full mechanism/side-effect detail.","ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan): an angiotensin receptor–neprilysin inhibitor combo, thought to be the most effective of the three because the neprilysin component adds an extra anti-remodeling effect beyond ACEI/ARB alone. Newer, more expensiv…","A patient is on only one of the three at a time — never combined.","Mortality benefit is clearest in HFrEF; some benefit is seen in HFpEF too.","Titrated to the highest tolerated dose.","Watch for: an ACE-inhibitor-specific cough; elevated potassium (all three); hypotension (some).","MNEMONIC: ACE inhibitors end in \"‑pril\" and cause the classic dry cough; ARBs end in \"‑sartan\" and generally don't. If a patient can't tolerate an ACE inhibitor's cough, an ARB is the usual swap.","Pillar 2 — Beta Blockers in Heart Failure","Carvedilol is used most frequently, because it provides both beta AND alpha blockade.","In heart failure, the sympathetic nervous system becomes chronically overactivated (neurohormonal activation) as a compensatory response that ultimately worsens the disease — beta blockers counteract this.","Help protect against dysrhythmias and interrupt the remodeling process.","Watch for: hypotension — patients are asked to monitor their own BP at home.","Pillar 3 — Spironolactone MRA","Previously learned as a potassium-sparing diuretic — but here it's not primarily used for that diuretic effect.","Suppresses sodium/water retention to offload the LV; goal is keeping patients as fluid-\"dry\" as possible.","↓ hospitalizations and ↓ deaths.","Watch for: hyperkalemia — monitor kidney function closely, since high K⁺ raises dysrhythmia risk in an already \"sick heart.\"","Pillar 4 — SGLT2 Inhibitors the \"‑gliflozins\"","e.g. empagliflozin, dapagliflozin — originally diabetes medications.","Exact HF mechanism isn't fully understood: thought to \"unload\" the ventricle via a natriuresis/osmotic-diuresis-like effect and to support cardiac metabolism.","Can be used even in HF patients without diabetes, for the cardioprotective effect alone.","↓ hospital readmissions, mortality, and morbidity.","Loop Diuretics e.g. furosemide — Symptom Relief, Not Survival","Most HF hospital admissions are from fluid overload — the failing heart can't maintain adequate BP/oncotic pressure.","Most HF patients are on one, usually a loop diuretic — oral at home, or IV/IV drip when hospitalized.","Biggest concerns: potassium imbalance (↑ dysrhythmia risk) and hypotension.","A patient on digoxin who becomes hypokalemic from a diuretic is at high risk for digoxin toxicity — these two drugs are a classic risky combination.","Despite near-universal use, diuretics give NO survival benefit — symptom relief only.","Inotropic drugs try to strengthen the weakened heart muscle itself. Digitalis (digoxin) is the one inotrope covered in depth below. Sympathomimetic inotropes — dopamine, dobutamine, and milrinone — are also IV-drip-only inotropes that incre…","Digoxin (Digitalis) from the foxglove plant","Historically first-line for nearly all heart failure — fallen out of favor (weak survival benefit, high rhythm-disturbance risk) but still used, including for AFib.","Mechanism: inhibits the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump in cardiac cells → calcium accumulates intracellularly → ↑ contractility (a cell-level calcium effect — NOT related to dietary calcium/milk intake).","Also ↑ renal blood flow and ↓ sympathetic stimulation, which helps ↓ heart rate.","Digoxin Toxicity — Who's at Risk","Major risks: cardiac dysrhythmias (V-tach, AFib, flutter, and more) and toxicity itself.","Higher risk: older adults, women, and patients also on diuretics (diuretic-induced hypokalemia predisposes to toxicity — and most hospitalized HF patients ARE on a diuretic).","Digoxin Toxicity — Signs & Prevention","Signs: bradycardia (the hallmark), headache, dizziness, confusion, nausea, visual disturbances.","Prevent: lowest effective dose; weekly digoxin levels (like INR monitoring, not peak/trough); supplement potassium as kidney function allows.","Antidote for severe toxicity: digoxin immune Fab, for significantly symptomatic/unstable patients.","MNEMONIC — digoxin toxicity is \"SLOW & BLURRY\": SLOW (bradycardia — the hallmark sign) and BLURRY (visual disturbances), plus headache, dizziness, confusion, and nausea along the way.","Take the patient's apical pulse for a full minute before administering digoxin, and hold the dose if the pulse is below 60 bpm — then notify the provider and monitor the rhythm. Bradycardia is the hallmark sign of digoxin toxicity."]},{"s":"Rate & Rhythm Control Drugs▾","a":"rate-rhythm-control-drugs","k":["Rate & Rhythm Control Drugs▾","Cross-reference: for the rhythms these drugs are treating, see Cardiac Dysrhythmias","Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers (already-known pull-forward drug classes) are used here too — for example, to treat sinus tachycardia once any underlying cause (fever, volume depletion, etc.) has been addressed and ruled out as t…","Symptomatic sinus bradycardia — Atropine — Blocks vagal slowing of the SA node — only helps if the slow rate is vagally driven","PSVT (narrow, very fast, no visible P wave) — Adenosine — Briefly blocks AV node conduction, interrupting the re-entry circuit","AFib / atrial flutter — rate control — Beta blocker, CCB, digoxin, or amiodarone — Slows the ventricular response so the patient gets usable cardiac output","AFib / atrial flutter — convert & maintain sinus rhythm — Dofetilide or amiodarone — Both can chemically convert and then maintain normal sinus rhythm","Any dysrhythmia, broadly — Amiodarone — The most broadly-used antidysrhythmic — works for atrial AND ventricular rhythms","MNEMONIC: Atropine speeds up a heart that's too SLOW. Adenosine briefly STOPS a heart that's racing too fast and narrow (PSVT). Amiodarone and dofetilide are the \"big guns\" for AFib/flutter conversion and maintenance.","Amiodarone the broad-spectrum antidysrhythmic","Used for: essentially any dysrhythmia — AFib, atrial flutter, ventricular dysrhythmias, and SVT. Effective at converting AFib to sinus rhythm.","Mechanism: prolongs the action potential duration and effective refractory period in all cardiac tissue; blocks both alpha and beta sympathetic receptors.","Contraindicated in severe bradycardia or heart block.","Forms: IV for an acute/emergent situation (e.g. a very tachycardic, symptomatic AFib patient); oral for ongoing maintenance after transitioning off an IV drip.","Interactions: with digoxin (↑ digoxin toxicity risk — typically avoided together); with warfarin (can ↑ INR by 50–100%).","Half-life: very long — remains in the system for days, so adverse effects can take 2–3 months to fully resolve after stopping. Requires substantial ongoing lab monitoring.","MNEMONIC — amiodarone \"TELLS\" you where it can hurt: Thyroid (hypo- or hyperthyroidism, from its iodine content), Eyes (corneal deposits — light sensitivity, dry eye, halos), Lungs (pulmonary toxicity — black box warning, ~10% fatal when it…","Atropine for slow & symptomatic","Given for symptomatic sinus bradycardia only — never for an asymptomatic slow rate.","Anticholinergic/antimuscarinic — blocks the vagus nerve (vagolytic action).","Only works if bradycardia is vagally induced (though it's often tried regardless, since the cause isn't always known immediately) — works in only about 30% of cases.","Dosing: IV push only, 1 mg every 3–5 min, max 3 mg total.","Side effects (anticholinergic): dry mouth, blurry vision, light sensitivity, tachycardia, flushing, hot skin.","Adenosine for fast & narrow","Used for SVT/PSVT (occasionally sinus tach), but really intended for PSVT.","Mechanism: slows AV node conduction.","Extremely short half-life — repeat doses often needed.","Causes a brief, expected pause in rhythm (transient asystole) as it works — shock pads are typically placed as a precaution.","Dosing: IV push only — 6 mg, then up to 12 mg, then another 12 mg if still needed.","Must be given through a three-way stopcock immediately followed by a rapid saline flush, or it won't reach circulation fast enough to work.","Never give atropine without the patient on continuous cardiac monitoring — heart rate and rhythm must be watched closely as it's given. Only give it for a truly symptomatic patient, not simply because a heart rate happens to be low on the m…","Dofetilide convert & maintain","Converts AFib or atrial flutter into normal sinus rhythm and then maintains it — a maintenance drug, unlike acute/one-time adenosine or atropine.","Mechanism: selectively blocks cardiac potassium ion channels.","Major risk: torsades de pointes (a fast, polymorphic form of V-tach — dangerous and potentially deadly; start CPR if it occurs) as well as new-onset SVT.","Side effects: headache, dizziness, chest pain.","Not given to patients with an already-prolonged QT interval or on other QT-prolonging medications.","Because of the torsades de pointes risk, dofetilide is always started in the hospital while the patient is on continuous telemetry monitoring, and it is not given to patients with a prolonged QT interval or on other QT-prolonging medication…","QT-prolonger tie-together: ranolazine (antianginal) and dofetilide (antidysrhythmic) both carry QT-prolongation risk — worth remembering as a pair, since combining QT-prolonging drugs compounds the torsades de pointes risk.","In addition to rate/rhythm-control drugs, patients with atrial fibrillation or flutter also generally need to be on warfarin (or a similar anticoagulant) because of stroke risk — go back and review warfarin's indications and nursing care fr…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week6-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 6","The Instructor's Own Framework, Applied to All Five Topics","The Five Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new — it's a map of Week 6's five topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. The instructor's own opening framework for the whole week was that…"]},{"s":"The Instructor's Own Framework, Applied to All Five Topics▾","a":"the-instructor-s-own-framework-applied-to-all-five-topics","k":["The Instructor's Own Framework, Applied to All Five Topics▾","Five pages is easier to hold in your head than the eleven separate pre-lecture voiceovers they were built from, but they still aren't five unrelated diseases — every one of them is really a plumbing problem, a pump problem, an electrical pr…","Plumbing & Pump — The Heart Itself — Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure — This single page covers both halves of the instructor's framework for the heart itself: coronary artery disease is a plumbing problem (narrowed vessels, reduced b…","Electrical — Cardiac Dysrhythmias — The heart's conduction system — from normal sinus rhythm all the way to V-fib. Explicitly kept separate from the plumbing/pump content, even though ischemia and heart failure are both named causes of spec…","Plumbing & Pump, Beyond the Coronary Arteries — Peripheral Vascular Disease → Valve Disease — Peripheral vascular disease is the exact same atherosclerotic plumbing process as coronary artery disease, just in the leg vessels instead of the …","Cutting Across All Three — Cardiac Pharmacology — Nitrates and ranolazine treat plumbing problems (angina); heart failure drugs treat pump problems; amiodarone/atropine/adenosine/dofetilide treat electrical problems. This page exists becaus…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, work through Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure first (it's the foundation the other pages keep referencing), then Cardiac Dysrhythmias, then the two \"beyond the coronary arteries\" pages …"]},{"s":"The Five Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-five-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Five Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure — CAD narrows coronary vessels until angina or MI results; heart failure follows a predictable chain (contractility down → preload up → afterload up) and splits cleanly into left-vs-right (which bo…","2 — Cardiac Dysrhythmias — Every rhythm answers the same four questions (rate, regularity, P wave, QRS width) — and QRS width is the single biggest fork in the road, splitting the whole list into narrow-QRS (supraventricular) and wide-QRS (…","3 — Peripheral Vascular Disease — PAD (arterial) and chronic venous insufficiency (venous) are near-perfect opposites on every feature that matters — pain quality, skin appearance, and where ulcers form — even though both fall under the sam…","4 — Valve Disease — Every valve problem is either stenosis (too tight) or regurgitation (too floppy), and the left-sided valves get the teaching focus because the left heart's higher pressure makes their failures more consequential — plus a…","5 — Cardiac Pharmacology — Three drug groups (antianginals, heart failure drugs, rate/rhythm-control drugs) built around the same plumbing/pump/electrical split as the rest of the week, plus a running distinction between genuinely new drugs…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content — each idea is fully taught in exactly one place. But they resurface as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why.","Atherosclerosis as the shared root cause — Coronary Artery Disease, Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure — Peripheral Artery Disease — explicitly named as \"the same underlying disease process... just occurring in a different vascular bed,…","Dysrhythmias as both a cause and a consequence of a sick heart — Cardiac Dysrhythmias — Named as a direct cause of heart failure on Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure, and heart failure/digoxin/diuretic therapy are in turn named as risk…","\"Pull-forward\" drugs from a prior pharmacology course — Named throughout Cardiac Pharmacology (beta blockers, CCBs, statins, aspirin/clopidogrel, ACE inhibitors/ARBs, warfarin) — These same drug classes are referenced by name on nearly ever…","Potassium as the recurring dysrhythmia trigger — Three Mechanisms Behind Dysrhythmias, Cardiac Dysrhythmias (inappropriate automaticity) — Comes back as the central safety concern for nearly every heart failure drug on Cardiac Pharmacology …","The \"too tight vs. too floppy\" mechanical split — Stenosis vs. regurgitation, on Valve Disease — A similar too-tight-or-too-loose logic shows up in atrial flutter/fibrillation's re-entry mechanism and in PAD's arterial narrowing vs. chronic…"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","Every danger callout across the five pages, condensed to a phrase. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation — click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure — Call 911 if chest pain isn't resolved within about 5 minutes — the concern point for unstable angina or a full MI.","Cardiac Dysrhythmias — V-tach and V-fib are deadly rhythms requiring an immediate pulse check — CPR immediately if pulseless. Sustained V-tach (>10–15 sec) is a rapid-response emergency even with a pulse.","Peripheral Vascular Disease — Smoking is the single biggest risk factor for PAD, doubling amputation risk — smoking cessation is the single most important intervention for both PAD and chronic venous insufficiency.","Valve Disease — Prosthetic-valve patients require prophylactic antibiotics before dental procedures. Roughly 40% of infective endocarditis patients develop neurologic/embolic stroke-type symptoms.","Cardiac Pharmacology — Hold digoxin and notify the provider if the 1-minute apical pulse is under 60 bpm — bradycardia is the hallmark toxicity sign. Never give atropine without continuous cardiac monitoring. Dofetilide is always started in…","Patterns you'll notice across these rules","\"Wide QRS means it's coming from the ventricle, and that's the deadlier half.\" Every narrow-QRS rhythm on Cardiac Dysrhythmias is manageable or even benign (PACs, sinus arrhythmia); the moment the QRS goes wide (PVCs, V-tach, V-fib), the st…","\"Potassium is the thread connecting dysrhythmias to nearly every heart failure drug.\" High or low potassium can trigger a dysrhythmia on its own, and half of the Cardiac Pharmacology drug list (MRAs, ACE inhibitors/ARBs/ARNIs, loop diuretic…","\"Left-sided problems get more teaching attention because the left heart works under more pressure.\" Left-sided heart failure, left-sided valve disease (aortic stenosis, mitral regurgitation) — the pattern holds across two completely differe…","\"The same disease process shows up in more than one vascular bed.\" Atherosclerosis is the root cause of both coronary artery disease and peripheral artery disease — same risk factors, same mechanism, different vessels and different symptoms…","\"New drug vs. pull-forward drug\" is worth tracking on every pharm page. Cardiac Pharmacology is explicit about which drugs are genuinely new this week (nitrates, ranolazine, ARNI, SGLT2 inhibitors, digoxin, amiodarone, atropine, adenosine, …"]}]},{"f":"week6-comprehensive-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 6 · Topic 1","Part 1 — Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure","Part 2 — Cardiac Dysrhythmias","Part 3 — Peripheral Vascular Disease","Part 4 — Valve Disease","Part 5 — Cardiac Pharmacology","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 6 — ischemic heart disease & heart failure, cardiac dysrhythmias, peripheral vascular disease, valve disease, and cardiac pharmacology — all in one place. It intentiona…"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure▾","a":"part-1-ischemic-heart-disease-heart-failure","k":["Part 1 — Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure▾","Cardiovascular disease breaks into three broad categories: \"electrical\" (dysrhythmias), \"plumbing\" (the vessels — ischemic heart disease), and \"pump\" (the heart muscle — heart failure). This part covers plumbing and pump; electrical is its …","Coronary Artery Disease (CAD)","Driven by atherosclerosis: plaque narrows the coronary arteries, reducing myocardial blood flow. Endothelial dysfunction (damage to the vessel's inner lining) is the earliest step. The left anterior descending (LAD) artery is nicknamed the …","Non-Modifiable Risk Factors","Age","Family history","Gender — estrogen is cardioprotective before menopause","Ethnicity — Black, Hispanic, and Native American populations have higher risk, with a documented death disparity linked to structural racism","Genetics/genetic variants (SNPs)","Modifiable Risk Factors","Hypertension — roughly 2–3x the risk","Smoking","Diabetes","Obesity/physical inactivity","Diet — a DASH-style diet is protective","Hyperlipidemia","Depression/stress/anxiety","Angina & Myocardial Infarction (MI)","Stable Angina","Triggered by exertion, relieved by rest","Lasts 2–5 minutes","Treated with nitrates","Unstable Angina","A more dangerous pattern than stable angina","Not reliably relieved by rest — a step closer to a full MI","MI symptom pattern: pain not relieved by rest (the key distinction from stable angina), pain that radiates, nausea, vomiting, dyspnea, and diaphoresis. Women often present atypically compared to this \"classic\" picture.","Call 911 if chest pain is not resolved within about 5 minutes — the concern point for unstable angina or a full MI.","Heart Failure — Definition & Core Physiology","By the AHA's framing, heart failure (HF) is the heart's inability to pump enough blood to meet the body's needs. CO = HR × SV; stroke volume is determined by preload (fluid volume), afterload (pressure/resistance), and contractility (squeez…","Dilated — Ischemic damage, valve disease, alcohol use, peripartum cardiomyopathy — Leads to HFrEF","Hypertrophic — Related to hypertension — High risk of sudden cardiac death and arrhythmias","Restrictive — Least common; often amyloid-associated — Stiff, non-compliant ventricle; tends to cause right-sided HF","Causes of HF: repeated ischemic episodes/prior MI, papillary muscle rupture, chronic hypertension, COPD (→ right-sided HF), dysrhythmias, valve disorders, pulmonary emboli. Stable angina by itself is NOT a risk factor for HF. Risk factors: …","Left-Sided HF","Pulmonary congestion — cough, crackles, wheezes, frothy/blood-tinged sputum, PND, orthopnea, tachycardia, fatigue, cyanosis","Most common cause: poorly controlled hypertension","Right-Sided HF","Systemic congestion — JVD, dependent edema, weight gain, hepatosplenomegaly, GI distress","Most common cause: COPD","MNEMONIC — \"Left = lungs, right = body.\"","HFrEF (\"Systolic\")","EF under 40%","Impaired contractile function","HFpEF (\"Diastolic\")","EF 40–50% (normal 55–65%)","Ventricles can't relax/fill; associated with older age, female sex, diabetes, obesity, chronic HTN","Ventricular remodeling: angiotensin II, aldosterone, endothelin, TNF-alpha, and catecholamines are inappropriately released, driving apoptosis, hypertrophy, collagen deposits, and myocardial fibrosis — progressively worsening HF. S3 gallop:…"]},{"s":"Part 2 — Cardiac Dysrhythmias▾","a":"part-2-cardiac-dysrhythmias","k":["Part 2 — Cardiac Dysrhythmias▾","Four cardiac cell properties: automaticity, excitability, conductivity, contractility. Depolarization triggers systole; repolarization is the cell recharging. P wave = atrial depolarization, QRS = ventricular depolarization, T wave = ventri…","Sinus arrhythmia — Normal, fluctuates with breathing — Normal — Narrow — Normal variant — not a true dysrhythmia","Sinus bradycardia — <60 bpm — Normal — Narrow — High K+, vagal stimulation, digoxin toxicity, late hypoxia, beta blockers/CCBs/amiodarone","Sinus tachycardia — 100–150 bpm — Normal — Narrow — Exercise, pain, fever, fluid volume deficit (often a first dehydration sign), stimulants, early hypoxia","PSVT — 150–250 bpm — Not discernible — Narrow — Re-entry; overexertion, digoxin toxicity, WPW syndrome","PACs — Early beat — Abnormal-looking; normal PR — Narrow — Generally benign; frequent PACs raise AFib risk","Atrial flutter — Atrial ≤250; ventricular slower (2:1/3:1/4:1) — Sawtooth flutter waves — Narrow — Re-entry from AV node; structural heart disease, hyperthyroidism, electrolytes","Atrial fibrillation — Ventricular ~100–175 bpm — None; fine fibrillation waves — Narrow — Electrolytes, hypoxia, \"sick heart\" (CAD, prior MI)","PVCs — Early beat — None — Wide (>0.12 sec) — Electrolyte imbalance is the single biggest cause","V-tach — Very fast — None — Wide — MI, CAD, severe electrolyte abnormalities, HF, drug toxicity","V-fib — No measurable rate — None — No organized QRS — Deadly — no effective cardiac output","AFib loses the \"atrial kick\" (~20% of cardiac output) and is \"irregularly irregular.\" Untreated AFib roughly doubles cardiac-death risk and raises stroke risk 4–5x (present in ~15–20% of all strokes); treatment is rate control (beta blocker…","V-tach and V-fib are deadly rhythms requiring an immediate pulse check. V-tach with a pulse follows the ACLS/antiarrhythmic pathway; pulseless V-tach or any V-fib means starting CPR immediately. Sustained V-tach (>10–15 sec) is a rapid-resp…"]},{"s":"Part 3 — Peripheral Vascular Disease▾","a":"part-3-peripheral-vascular-disease","k":["Part 3 — Peripheral Vascular Disease▾","Peripheral vascular disease (PVD) is an umbrella term for peripheral artery disease (PAD) and peripheral venous disease (chronic venous insufficiency). Atherosclerosis in the lower extremities is the same disease process as CAD in a differe…","PAD (Arterial)","Intermittent claudication — exertional cramping, relieved by rest (\"angina of the lower extremity\"); femoral artery is the most common site","Pallor with elevation; dependent rubor when hanging down","Diminished/absent pulses; trophic skin changes (shiny, thick, hair loss)","Ulcers typically at the toes/dorsum of the foot","Chronic Venous Insufficiency (Venous)","Aching and tiredness — NOT burning or heaviness","Edema; leathery, dark/tan, flaking skin","Affects ~40% of the U.S. population; varicose veins commonly associated","Stasis ulcers typically at the ankles","MNEMONIC — The 5 P's: Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paresthesias, Paralysis (weakness).","ABI (ankle-brachial index) compares ankle to arm blood pressure: ~1.0–1.4 is normal; ≤0.5 indicates severe arterial disease. Shared treatment: smoking cessation (the single most important intervention), increased activity (collateral circul…"]},{"s":"Part 4 — Valve Disease▾","a":"part-4-valve-disease","k":["Part 4 — Valve Disease▾","Blood flows tricuspid → pulmonic → mitral → aortic (\"TP-MA,\" or \"tricycle before bicycle\"). Tricuspid, pulmonic, and aortic each have 3 leaflets; mitral has 2. Tricuspid + mitral = AV valves; pulmonic + aortic = semilunar valves. Tricuspid …","Stenosis","Leaflets too tight/stiff — restricts forward flow","Most common in the aortic valve","Regurgitation","Leaflets too floppy/loose — blood flows backward","Most common in the mitral valve","Left-sided valve disease is emphasized since the left heart works at higher pressure. Aortic stenosis: classic triad of syncope, lightheadedness, and chest pain (coronary arteries starved of forward flow); can progress to pulmonary edema. M…","Infective endocarditis: vegetations impair valve closure and can embolize. The tricuspid valve is most commonly affected (first valve blood reaches — especially relevant to IV drug users). Organisms: Staph aureus and Staph epidermidis. Risk…","Petechiae — Small red/purple pinpoint spots","Splinter hemorrhages — Thin dark lines under the nail beds","Janeway lesions — Non-tender flat spots on palms/soles","Osler's nodes — Tender raised fingertip nodules","Roth spots — Retinal spots","Rheumatic heart disease (from childhood rheumatic illness) is another valve-damage cause. Treatment for significant valve disease is valve replacement; active infection must be cleared with antibiotics before and after surgery."]},{"s":"Part 5 — Cardiac Pharmacology▾","a":"part-5-cardiac-pharmacology","k":["Part 5 — Cardiac Pharmacology▾","Several drug classes this week are \"pull-forward\" drugs from a prior pharmacology course (beta blockers, CCBs, statins, aspirin/clopidogrel, ACE inhibitors/ARBs, warfarin) — review your existing drug matrix for their full profile. This sect…","Antianginal Drugs","Nitrates decrease preload via venodilation; beta blockers decrease HR/contractility; CCBs decrease afterload and HR/contractility; ranolazine improves myocardial energy efficiency (mechanism not fully detailed). Stepped approach: beta block…","Sublingual (Nitrostat) — Active anginal attack; every 5 min PRN, up to 3 doses","Transdermal patch — On AM, off PM, rotate hairless sites","IV — ICU only; glass bottle + special tubing","Long-acting oral (isosorbide) — Prevention only; taper to avoid rebound vasospasm; use SL nitro (not extra isosorbide) for breakthrough pain","If chest pain isn't relieved within 5 minutes of SL nitroglycerin, call 911 — concern for unstable angina/STEMI.","Nitrate side effects: headache, hypotension, reflex tachycardia; dangerous interaction with sildenafil/antihypertensives/alcohol. Ranolazine (Ranexa): oral only, can prolong QT, CYP3A4 inhibitor (avoid grapefruit juice), doesn't cause the B…","Heart Failure Drugs","Core categories: an ACE inhibitor/ARB/ARNI, a beta blocker, and an MRA — with SGLT2 inhibitors increasingly added as a fourth. Only one RAS-inhibitor type at a time. ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan) is currently thought most effective due to its…","Digoxin (digitalis) — Inhibits the Na+/K+ ATPase pump, raising intracellular calcium and contractility; decreases sympathetic stimulation. Toxicity risk highest in older adults, women, and patients on diuretics (hypokalemia). Hallmark toxic…","Take a full 1-minute apical pulse before giving digoxin — hold the dose if under 60 bpm.","Rate & Rhythm Control Drugs","Amiodarone — Used for any dysrhythmia; prolongs action potential/refractory period, blocks alpha/beta receptors. ~75% adverse-effect rate: thyroid alterations, corneal deposits, pulmonary toxicity (black box, ~10% fatal), hepatotoxicity, pr…","Atropine — Symptomatic sinus bradycardia only; anticholinergic, blocks the vagus nerve; works ~30% of the time. IV push, 1 mg q3–5min, max 3 mg. Requires continuous cardiac monitoring.","Adenosine — SVT/PSVT; slows AV-node conduction; extremely short half-life; can cause brief asystole (shock pads on standby). IV push through a three-way stopcock + rapid saline flush; 6 mg, then up to 12 mg, then 12 mg again if needed.","Dofetilide — Converts/maintains sinus rhythm in AFib/flutter; blocks potassium channels; high torsades de pointes risk — always started in-hospital on telemetry; avoided with prolonged QT.","AFib/flutter patients also generally need warfarin (or a similar anticoagulant) for stroke prevention — review warfarin from prior pharm coursework."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","A trick for tying the whole week together: nearly every topic this week comes down to a pump, an electrical signal, or a valve failing in one of exactly two directions — too weak/slow/floppy, or too stiff/fast/tight.","Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure — MI pain not relieved by rest (call 911 within 5 minutes); acute pulmonary edema/decompensated heart failure.","Cardiac Dysrhythmias — V-tach and V-fib — immediate pulse check, CPR if pulseless.","Peripheral Vascular Disease — Critical limb ischemia (the 5 P's) and amputation risk from continued smoking.","Valve Disease — Infective endocarditis with septic embolization to the brain or lungs.","Cardiac Pharmacology — Digoxin toxicity (hold for HR <60) and dofetilide-induced torsades de pointes."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the five parts above — a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"week7-cancer-fundamentals.html","t":"Cancer Fundamentals","secs":[{"s":"Cancer Fundamentals","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 2","What Is Cancer?","Normal Cells vs. Cancer Cells","Cell Morphology: How Cancer Cells Look","Benign vs. Malignant Tumors","The Cell Growth Cycle","Tumor Grading (Grades 1 to 3)","TNM Classification","Cancer Staging (Stages I to IV)","Self-Test Flashcards","This is the first of several cancer topics. The order of the cancer material is: cancer in general (this page), then the specific cancers you are responsible for knowing, then chemotherapies. This page covers what cancer is, how cancer cell…","Cross-reference: the instructor tells you to recall the cellular changes taught in week 1 of the earlier course, which is the background for this topic. See Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity Review"]},{"s":"What Is Cancer?▾","a":"what-is-cancer","k":["What Is Cancer?▾","Cancer overall is a group of diseases characterized by cells that show cellular transformation, uncontrolled cellular growth, and possible invasion into other organs, with metastasis to other tissues or organs.","Three hallmarks in the definition","Cellular transformation: the example given is a genetic mutation that occurs in a cell.","Uncontrolled cellular growth.","Possible invasion into other organs and metastasis to other tissues or organs.","In general, cancer cells usually lack","Cellular differentiation: they are not as specific as our normal body cells.","Cellular checkpoints in the normal cellular process: they may not have the checkpoint that recognizes DNA errors.","The ability to undergo apoptosis: apoptosis is where a cell self-destructs when it recognizes that it is not doing what it is supposed to do.","Response to signals from other cells: they disregard signals such as \"stop invading my space.\"","The result: the cancer cells continue to proliferate.","The instructor stresses that it is very important to understand what makes normal cells and cancer cells very different. The next section lays out that comparison."]},{"s":"Normal Cells vs. Cancer Cells▾","a":"normal-cells-vs-cancer-cells","k":["Normal Cells vs. Cancer Cells▾","Contact inhibition — Present. \"I'm going to stay out of someone's space that isn't mine and you're going to stay out of mine.\" — Lack contact inhibition; they invade other cells' space.","Cohesiveness — Cells of a similar style stick together (\"stick with me and we'll stay home\"). — Not cohesive; they leave (\"I'm going to leave here. I don't care\").","Cellular signaling / communication — A traditional communication system through cellular signals (the instructor's analogy: \"ask the man next door to help\"). — Do what they want, how they want; they do not communicate or respond to signals …","Proliferation / growth control — Controlled; the goal is zero population growth. — Significantly lack proliferation growth control.","Apoptosis (cell death) — Normal cell death: when the cells recognize that they are no longer needed, they undergo apoptosis. — Sometimes immortal, or they can die unpredictably. They do not follow the normal cellular life span and style.","Proliferation rate — Divide at a normal, expected level that depends on the cell type. Example: skin cells proliferate at more rapid rates than cardiomyocytes. — Very unpredictable. It depends on the level of differentiation (more specific …","HLA antigens — Your body recognizes the HLA antigen as \"this is my cell, don't hurt me.\" — Carry a non-self marker, so attacks may be mustered: the immune system may try to attack cancer cells.","HLA antigens and the immune response","The instructor relates HLA antigens to blood typing, saying blood type is a type of HLA antigen.","Normal cells display the \"self\" marker; cancer cells display a \"non-self\" marker, so the immune system may try to attack them.","How intense the cancer becomes, or where it goes, depends on how successful the immune attack is, what kind of cancer it is, how fast it is proliferating, and other factors."]},{"s":"Cell Morphology: How Cancer Cells Look▾","a":"cell-morphology-how-cancer-cells-look","k":["Cell Morphology: How Cancer Cells Look▾","The instructor goes through a slide picture comparing normal cells and cancer cells. Normal cells have a normal rate of division, while cancer cells are just proliferating. The appearance features below are what her words describe.","Nuclei — Cancer cells have an abnormally shaped nucleus, usually larger than a normal one.","Nucleus-to-cytoplasm ratio — The instructor contrasts the neat normal cell with the cancer cell, noting that the nucleus takes up much more of the cell than the cytoplasm.","Size and shape — A lot of variation in size and shape. Cancer cells can look really different from one another, whereas the cells in the normal area typically do not.","Loss of normal features — Cancer cells may lose some of their normal cellular features. The example is a neuron: normally it has an axon and dendrites, but the cancerous version looks like a \"sick neuron.\"","Arrangement — Disorganized. Normal cells are neatly organized, while cancer cells are stacked on top of each other, not appropriately.","Tumor boundary — Poorly defined."]},{"s":"Benign vs. Malignant Tumors▾","a":"benign-vs-malignant-tumors","k":["Benign vs. Malignant Tumors▾","Starred by the instructor: \"You can put a little star here.\" Know benign versus malignant. You are not asked to tell leiomyoma from leiomyosarcoma, but you are expected to understand the differences between the types of cells at the benign …","Terminology","The terms malignant tumor, malignancy, neoplasm, and tumor are used to indicate cancerous cells.","Tumors, meaning groups of abnormal cells, can be benign (not likely to be associated with cancer) or malignant (associated with cancer).","Benign tumor","Not associated with cancer.","Well differentiated: the cells resemble the tissues of origin in the area, but they are \"just kind of inappropriate.\"","Grow at a progressive or slow rate, closer to a normal rate.","Usually do not invade significantly.","Cohesive cell walls.","Demarcated tumor, often encapsulated, which makes it movable. The instructor's example is a fatty tumor on a dog.","No metastasis is seen.","No necrotic tumor core.","Generally taken to be harmless, which is usually the case.","Malignant tumor","What we associate with cancer; these are cancer cells.","Poorly differentiated: the cells do not resemble the tissue of origin, meaning they are anaplastic, \"completely inappropriate cells for the area.\"","Growth is usually erratic: it can be slow, but it can also be very, very rapid.","Generally invasive: they infiltrate surrounding normal tissues.","Cancer cells that invade surrounding tissues are considered to metastasize, which is frequently seen with malignant tumors.","The tumor core can be necrotic.","Association with cancer — Not associated with cancer — Cancerous","Differentiation — Well differentiated; resembles tissue of origin — Poorly differentiated; does not resemble tissue of origin (anaplastic)","Growth rate — Progressive or slow, closer to normal — Erratic: slow or very rapid","Invasion — Usually does not invade significantly — Generally invasive; infiltrates surrounding normal tissue","Boundary — Demarcated; often encapsulated and movable — Poorly demarcated (see the endometrial example below)","Metastasis — Not seen — Frequently seen","Tumor core — No necrotic core — Can have a necrotic core","A benign tumor can still be lethal. Benign tumors are generally taken to be harmless, but they can be lethal if they grow large enough to mechanically interrupt the normal function of a critical tissue or organ. The instructor's examples ar…","Example: Endometrium (leiomyoma vs. malignant tumor)","The instructor uses a slide picture of the endometrium to contrast a benign tumor with a malignant one. Only her spoken description is captured here.","Benign leiomyoma","Small.","Well demarcated.","Non-invasive.","Non-metastatic.","Well differentiated.","Can usually be removed laparoscopically, or they may not even do that if it is not causing a problem.","Malignant tumor in the endometrium","Invades the tissues of the endometrial section.","Large and poorly demarcated: it is not clear, and there are no nice lines containing it.","Cells generally rapid and growing, with hemorrhage and necrosis of the cells in the surrounding tissues.","Locally invasive.","If it gets into the lymph node area, it can be metastatic.","Often poorly differentiated.","Cross-reference: metastasis is introduced here only as part of the benign/malignant comparison and TNM; it is taught further in Metastasis & Etiology"]},{"s":"The Cell Growth Cycle▾","a":"the-cell-growth-cycle","k":["The Cell Growth Cycle▾","This goes back to biology. Normal cells in our body divide or proliferate in a very controlled and organized fashion, which is regulated by mechanisms and through the cell growth cycle. Cell growth characteristics of normal and neoplastic c…","Generation time","The time it takes to go through the cell cycle is called the generation time.","It can range from hours to days, depending on what kind of tumor is involved.","The phases, as listed in the lecture","G0 — The cell is at rest and not actively engaged in the cell cycle. Most of the human cells in the body exist in this phase right now. G0 is seen in the normal cell cycle but not in the cancer cell cycle in the slide picture. Cancer cells …","G1 — The cells enter the cell cycle and enzymes for DNA synthesis are produced. This is the first post-mitosis phase.","S — Described as \"synthesis of structure.\" The structures move to opposite poles in preparation for division into two separate cells. The 46 chromosomes in our DNA reorganize into two separate sets of 23 pairs at opposite poles. Two nuclear…","G2 — The cells are preparing to divide. This is where RNA and specialized proteins are being created.","M — Mitosis, or division, is completed, and two daughter cells (two other new cells) are created.","Why the cell cycle matters for chemotherapy","Chemotherapy agents can impact specific phases of the cancer cell cycle. Certain chemotherapy medications are specific to certain parts of the cell cycle.","This is why the instructor cares so much about the cell growth cycle. Understanding it will help you understand chemotherapy agents better.","Cancer cells in G0 (the resting phase) are not susceptible to the toxic effects of the cell-cycle-specific drugs.","How the cancer cell cycle differs from normal","Cancer cells do not rest and do not go through restriction checkpoints. They go through cell cycles constantly.","They are actively proliferating without control and without an active restriction point.","Without a restriction point, DNA repairs do not often occur, and the cell can continuously mutate.","This is why we see so many mistakes in cancer cells: they do not have a rest, and they keep mutating even if they are not doing what they are supposed to.","Cross-reference: chemotherapy is taught later in this week, after the cancer topics. See Chemotherapy Principles"]},{"s":"Tumor Grading (Grades 1 to 3)▾","a":"tumor-grading-grades-1-to-3","k":["Tumor Grading (Grades 1 to 3)▾","This is the tumor classification system. Malignant tumors can be graded one through three, based on how the cells from the tumor look under a microscope.","Grade 1 — Cells are well differentiated.","Grade 2 — Cells are moderately differentiated.","Grade 3 — Cells are poorly differentiated or anaplastic.","The instructor also walks through the slide picture mentioned earlier: in grade 1 you may see one or two cells with some abnormal growth and division; in grade 2 it is starting to get bigger; in grade 3 the cells are invading."]},{"s":"TNM Classification▾","a":"tnm-classification","k":["TNM Classification▾","Besides grade, tumors are classified according to size, invasiveness, and spread using the TNM system. The instructor calls this \"very, very common.\"","T — Tumor size, location, and involvement","N — Lymph node involvement","M — Metastasis to different organs","About the TNM system","Used internationally; diagnostic; and very specific.","\"This is what you will see in your patient's charts.\"","It is a series of letters and numbers to describe the cancer.","Cancer therapy is based in part on this classification.","It requires a biopsy; much of it is seen under the microscope.","All of it requires microscopic confirmation of malignancy.","There are many additional subcategories, and the system is much more complex than what is introduced here.","T: the primary tumor","T refers to the primary tumor, the tumor associated with the cancer. There can be other tumors in the body, but they try to find the primary tumor. It is graded T0, Tis, or T1 through T4.","T0 — No evidence of a primary tumor. The instructor says she is \"no cancer expert\" and guesses this may be like what is seen with some blood cancers.","Tis — Tumor in situ: early cancer where the cancer cells are present only in the layer of cells in which the cancer began.","T1 to T4 — Once past tumor in situ, the higher the number after the T, the larger the tumor or the more it has grown into nearby tissues. For this course, associate T1 with about one centimeter, T2 with two, and so on. The \"involvement\" ind…","N: lymph nodes","N0 — No spread to regional lymph nodes.","N1 — Spread to the closest regional lymph node or a small number of regional lymph nodes.","N2 — Spread to the \"most distant or numerous\" lymph nodes.","M: metastasis","The instructor says N is generally related to M. M is simply yes or no.","M0 — No metastasis.","M1 — Metastasis present."]},{"s":"Cancer Staging (Stages I to IV)▾","a":"cancer-staging-stages-i-to-iv","k":["Cancer Staging (Stages I to IV)▾","The simple four-stage system (\"I have stage one cancer, stage two, stage three, stage four\") is a bit more descriptive than TNM alone, and the lecture says it accounts for more tumor variability. The instructor says this is probably the cla…","Stage I — Confined to the organ of origin. Usually very treatable, and usually the best stage to catch it in.","Stage II — Locally invasive.","Stage III — Regional spread.","Stage IV — Spread to distant sites.","What you are responsible for: you do not need to know the specific rates and details of grading. But if the instructor gives you a T and M (TNM) classification, or a stage classification, you must understand what it means for the patient."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-metastasis-etiology.html","t":"Metastasis & Cancer Etiology","secs":[{"s":"Metastasis & Cancer Etiology","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 3","What Metastasis Means","Routes of Cancer Spread","Common Sites of Metastasis","Etiology — Why Do People Get Cancer?","Genetic Mechanisms: Tumor Suppressor Genes vs. Proto-Oncogenes / Oncogenes","Carcinogens","Promoters","What Comes Next","Self-Test Flashcards","This topic covers what the lecture means by metastasis (and what we are worried about when cancer spreads), the difference between a primary and a secondary tumor, the routes cancer uses to spread (seeding, implantation, lymphatic, and vasc…"]},{"s":"What Metastasis Means▾","a":"what-metastasis-means","k":["What Metastasis Means▾","The instructor opens by defining the terms. Malignant cancer cells are invasive, penetrative, and destructive to their surrounding tissues. They generally metastasize from a primary site through the lymph nodes or through the blood.","Establishing a tumor in a target organ","For a tumor to establish itself in a target organ, it must develop its own blood vessels and connect to the pre-existing blood supply.","The reason given: tumors and their cells need oxygen and nutrients to grow.","The instructor later ties this to how fast a secondary tumor grows: it all depends on the angiogenesis rate — is the secondary tumor developing blood vessels (vasculature) to obtain oxygen and nutrients?","Primary Tumor vs. Secondary Tumor","Primary tumor","The location / site of origin — where the original cellular division problem started.","The original site of the cancer.","Secondary tumor","Sometimes there are secondary tumors in addition to the primary.","Generally not the site of origin.","Results from seeding of malignant cells that travel to distant sites — this is due to metastasis.","\"METs\" means a metastasis, or a secondary tumor.","What a secondary tumor looks like under the microscope","Under a microscope, the cancer cells at the secondary site look like the cells from the primary site.","Example from the lecture: a breast cancer that travels to the bone. There may be a tumor in the bone; when it is biopsied, the cells look like the cancerous breast cells seen in the primary breast tumor."]},{"s":"Routes of Cancer Spread▾","a":"routes-of-cancer-spread","k":["Routes of Cancer Spread▾","The instructor lists three words to be familiar with: seeding, implantation, and metastasis (cancer spread). Seeding and implantation are defined first; metastasis then has two primary routes.","Seeding — Tumors erode and shed into the body cavities. — Intraperitoneal seeding: cancer in the colon invades the colon but also puts out \"seeds\" that can implant somewhere else, in other parts of the abdominal cavity.","Implantation — Direct expansion of the tumor into adjoining tissues as it grows. — Prostate cancer that spreads into the bladder — \"directly connected because of close proximity.\"","Metastasis — Malignant cells spread from a primary site through the lymph nodes or the blood. There are two primary routes: lymphatic and vascular. — See the lymphatic and vascular sections below.","The two primary routes of metastasis","Lymphatic spread","Vascular spread (blood drainage)","Which is the \"first stop\"?","Lymphatic spread is generally common; the lymph system is described as the \"first stop in the system.\"","For vascular (blood) spread, the first stop is generally the liver.","Lymphatic Spread","Cancer cells can become trapped in lymph nodes. The instructor says three things can happen to cells in the lymph system:","The cells die — \"Obviously hopeful.\"","The cells go dormant — They sit and wait to grow. You may not know you have cancer at this point.","The cells flourish and proliferate — \"Unfortunately,\" sometimes the cells flourish and multiply.","If the cells survive","Cancer cells that survive get out of the lymph node and into the lymph system.","They can spread through channels to the next set of lymph nodes they encounter.","This is how you get secondary tumor locations, or secondary spread.","Vascular Spread","Vascular spread is spread by vascular (blood) drainage. The lecture describes it as a sequence:","Steps of vascular spread","Cancer cells have to penetrate the blood vessels draining the tumor site — they penetrate these local veins.","Once the cancer cells are in the veins, they travel away from the primary site.","First stop: the liver. The instructor's reason: most of the blood goes through the portal vein before it goes into general circulation.","As cancer cells flow through the liver, they clump and get trapped in the liver capillaries.","They then begin to proliferate in those liver capillaries.","As a result, the liver is often a secondary location when we associate things with vascular spread.","Travels through — Lymph nodes and lymph channels — Veins draining the tumor site (blood drainage)","Where cells get stuck first — Trapped in lymph nodes — Trapped in liver capillaries (after the portal vein)","Then what — Cells die, go dormant, or flourish; survivors move on to the next set of lymph nodes — Cells clump, proliferate in the liver capillaries; the liver is often a secondary location"]},{"s":"Common Sites of Metastasis▾","a":"common-sites-of-metastasis","k":["Common Sites of Metastasis▾","Secondary tumors need nutrients and oxygen, so the site where a secondary tumor appears often depends on the location of the primary tumor. The instructor says the sites depend on how the blood circulation works (the vascular dependence) an…","Lung — Bone and brain","Colon — Liver","Breast — Bone, brain, liver, or lung","Prostate — Vertebrae","Melanoma — Brain","Instructor's emphasis on this slide","\"You should kind of be familiar with this slide.\"","She says she is not going to ask in depth \"in the weeds\" about why melanoma goes to the brain — that is higher level than what you are expected to know for this course.","She attributes the pattern to how blood circulation and vascular dependence work.","Think-about question posed in lecture","The instructor asks: what symptoms would you expect to see with metastatic tumors in the bone, liver, brain, or lung? What would you see in someone who may have METs to the brain or METs to the lungs?","She poses this as a question to think about and does not answer it aloud in this segment, so no symptom list is given here."]},{"s":"Etiology — Why Do People Get Cancer?▾","a":"etiology-why-do-people-get-cancer","k":["Etiology — Why Do People Get Cancer?▾","Etiology means cause. The instructor's honest summary: the etiology of cancer in general is still \"kind of a mystery,\" but we have made a lot of progress over the last several decades, and we have definitely identified many factors that are…","Sun / UV exposure — Risk for skin cancer.","Low-fiber diet — Risk for GI cancers.","Age — For most cancers, the risk increases as we age. Exception: this is not seen with reproductive cancers such as cervical cancer or uterine cancer.","Sex — Lung and urinary cancers are definitely higher risk in males. This could be related to lifestyle and environmental aspects.","Genetic and ethnic factors — Several types of cancer have genetic and ethnic factors. Examples: Burkitt lymphoma is much more common in young African children and children of African descent; nasopharyngeal cancers have a high incidence in …","Cancer-causing (oncogenic) viruses — HPV, HIV, herpes virus, and Epstein-Barr are all associated with various kinds of cancers. These are considered oncogenic viruses.","Occupational and environmental risks — Carcinogens and mutagens are associated with environmental exposure. Example: here in Kentucky, some chemicals in certain factories (she mentions a battery company that used to be around) are associate…","Cross-reference: lung cancer, one of the four highest-prevalence cancers the instructor says come next, is taught on Lung Cancer","Cross-reference: breast, cervical, and colorectal cancer, the other three highest-prevalence cancers, are taught on Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer"]},{"s":"Genetic Mechanisms: Tumor Suppressor Genes vs. Proto-Oncogenes / Oncogenes▾","a":"genetic-mechanisms-tumor-suppressor-genes-vs-proto-oncogenes-oncogenes","k":["Genetic Mechanisms: Tumor Suppressor Genes vs. Proto-Oncogenes / Oncogenes▾","Our bodies have ways of trying to make sure we do not develop cancer: signaling systems and pathways that try to stop cancer cells from proliferating and growing. The lecture describes two major classes of cancer genes / genetic mechanisms:…","Tumor suppressor gene = the brake pedal","Normally keeps the cell from dividing too quickly, just as a brake keeps a car from going too fast.","Normally functions to restrain cell growth.","When something goes wrong with the gene, cell division can get out of control.","They can become defective and lose their ability to inhibit cell growth, thus allowing cancer formation.","One way cancer cells can develop is if they lack this tumor suppressor gene.","Proto-oncogene = the gas pedal; oncogene = a stuck gas pedal","A proto-oncogene normally works much like a gas pedal: it helps the cell grow and divide normally.","Proto-oncogenes are genes that stimulate and regulate a cell's movement through the cell cycle, resulting in cellular growth and proliferation.","An oncogene can be compared with a gas pedal that is stuck down — stuck to the floor, \"stuck in the drive.\"","When a proto-oncogene is mutated (\"messed up\"), it is stimulating constantly: the cell is never in the rest cycle and is constantly reproducing.","Tumor suppressor gene — Brake pedal — Restrains cell growth; keeps the cell from dividing too quickly — Defective; loses its ability to inhibit cell growth, so division can get out of control and cancer can form","Proto-oncogene — Gas pedal — Stimulates and regulates the cell's movement through the cell cycle; helps the cell grow and divide normally — When mutated, it stimulates constantly and the cell never rests","Oncogene — Gas pedal stuck to the floor — (The mutated, constantly \"on\" form) — Cells are constantly reproducing","P53 — the specific gene named","P53 is a specific gene named in lecture.","It is a tumor suppressor gene.","It controls cellular apoptosis — natural cell death — which should lead to the natural death of cells that have damaged DNA."]},{"s":"Carcinogens▾","a":"carcinogens","k":["Carcinogens▾","Why do these genes sometimes get messed up? The instructor says \"I wish we knew,\" but we do know some things that can cause problems. Carcinogens are one of them.","What a carcinogen is and how it works","Carcinogens are substances that cause cancer by altering a cell's DNA — so they can alter the genetic mechanisms described above.","They often require prolonged exposure over many years.","Carcinogens have actually been demonstrated to cause cancer: causation, not just association or correlation.","The ability of some substances to alter DNA is what makes them carcinogens; they can cause cells to mutate and change inappropriately.","Generally the damage is cumulative: the more exposure to the carcinogen, the more likely the initiated cells will undergo further mutation.","Known, Probable, and Possible Carcinogens","Known — Tobacco, asbestos, alcohol, and some viruses such as HPV and HIV — These are ones that \"definitely\" cause cancer; they have been shown to cause cancer in humans.","Probable — Night shift work — It \"should be regarded as a carcinogen\" because of the link found: women who have worked night shift at some point in the past 10 years have a 60% higher risk of developing breast cancer. Why? \"Who knows\" — but…","Possible — Engine exhaust — There is insufficient evidence to conclude; \"in some yes and in some no.\""]},{"s":"Promoters▾","a":"promoters","k":["Promoters▾","Promoters are agents that help mutated genes proliferate. The instructor says this is where \"sometimes the line gets a little messy.\"","Examples of promoters given","Diet — for example, a high-fat diet can stimulate further proliferation of a cancer cell.","Alcoholic beverages","Tobacco","Hormones — \"big promoters\" that can be very important.","Hormones as Promoters — Estrogen Exposure Example","The lecture's reasoning","For women, the more menstrual cycles you experience, the more you are exposed to estrogen, and this can increase your risk of cancer.","That is why starting your period earlier can increase your risk of cancer.","Women at higher risk include those who had their first menstrual period at a young age, had a very late first pregnancy, did not breastfeed, or only breastfed for a few months.","The instructor says it is estimated that in developed countries, we could cut our incidence of breast cancer by half if breastfeeding time increased. She sets aside any breastfeeding argument and says the point is simply that this is an exp…","Promoter vs. Carcinogen","Carcinogen","Alters the cell's DNA.","Can cause cells to mutate and change inappropriately.","Promoter","Helps already-mutated genes / already-damaged cells proliferate.","Example: it is not the alcohol causing the DNA change and mutation, but it can proliferate cells that are already damaged from whatever else has happened.","Things can be both","There is a difference between a promoter and a carcinogen, but you can have things that are both carcinogens and promoters.","Estrogen therapy: it can act as a carcinogen by altering the cell DNA, but it can also help mutated genes proliferate.","Tobacco is another example of something that does both.","What the instructor expects on the exam","She says she is not going to \"get in the weeds\" and ask for an example of a promoter.","She wants you to know what the different definitions are, so that when you read a study describing a cancer risk you can tell whether it describes something associated with promoting development versus something that alters cell DNA.","In short: \"I want you to know the differences between them.\""]},{"s":"What Comes Next▾","a":"what-comes-next","k":["What Comes Next▾","The instructor ends this segment by saying the next voiceovers focus on specific cancers common in Kentucky. The four cancers with the highest prevalence are lung, breast, cervical, and colorectal."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-lung-cancer.html","t":"Lung Cancer","secs":[{"s":"Lung Cancer","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 4","Big Picture: Why Lung Cancer Matters","Late Diagnosis & Survival","Racial, Ethnic & Socioeconomic Disparities","Etiology (Causes)","Pathogenesis","Types of Lung Cancer","Signs & Symptoms","Quick Review: Numbers to Know","Self-Test Flashcards","This is the first of the lecture's \"specific cancers\" topics, focused on cancers seen here in Kentucky. Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related death in the United States and in Kentucky. A major problem is that most patients are…"]},{"s":"Big Picture: Why Lung Cancer Matters▾","a":"big-picture-why-lung-cancer-matters","k":["Big Picture: Why Lung Cancer Matters▾","The instructor opens the specific-cancer section with some context before turning to lung cancer.","Context for the \"specific cancers\" section","Historically these were the four cancers with the highest prevalence (lung, breast, cervical, and colorectal), but through advancement they are not seen quite as much now, especially cervical cancer. Cervical cancer is still mentioned, alon…","The cancer with the highest prevalence is non-melanoma skin cancer. Skin cancer was covered in NUR 325, and the instructor clarifies that the skin cancer she means is non-melanoma skin cancer, \"just to clarify that.\"","This is voiceover 3 of 3, and it is \"really focused on specific types of cancers that we see here in Kentucky.\" The instructor finishes the section in the last voiceover.","Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related death in the United States, and that is also true in Kentucky.","Cross-reference: breast, cervical (including the HPV vaccine), and colorectal cancer are taught in Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer"]},{"s":"Late Diagnosis & Survival▾","a":"late-diagnosis-survival","k":["Late Diagnosis & Survival▾","The central problem in lung cancer (and in cancer in general) is that most patients do not present until the cancer is at an advanced or metastatic stage.","Why lung cancer is caught late","Most patients do not present with cancer, especially lung cancer, until they are at an advanced or metastatic stage.","The lecture says exact percentages vary slightly depending on study and region, but it is \"pretty well documented\" that most lung cancer cases are diagnosed at a late stage, often when the disease has already spread to distant sites.","Two reasons are given: a lack of symptoms in the early stages, and limited screening uptake in the communities.","Early diagnosis and detection significantly improve survival rates.","Five-year survival by stage at diagnosis","Stage 1 — Over 60%","Stage 4 — Drops below 10%","Screening","Screening is done with low-dose CT scans.","The lecture calls this \"very effective for early detection.\"","Age","The average age of diagnosis in the United States is around 70.","So lung cancer is often seen in patients over 65."]},{"s":"Racial, Ethnic & Socioeconomic Disparities▾","a":"racial-ethnic-socioeconomic-disparities","k":["Racial, Ethnic & Socioeconomic Disparities▾","Exam note from the instructor: \"You don't need to memorize these for the exams,\" but it is important for healthcare providers to recognize the disparities. The instructor notes that many chronic conditions show racial and ethnic disparities…","Black men — About 15% more likely to develop lung cancer than white male counterparts.","Black women — Lower incidence than white women.","Black Americans overall — More likely to be diagnosed at later stages, receive less treatment, and have lower survival rates.","Other ethnic groups — This type of inequality in lung cancer diagnosis and treatment is seen in almost every ethnic group, including Hispanic and Asian groups.","Contributing factors the lecture names","Healthcare access: studies have linked the disparities to healthcare access. People of color are more likely to be uninsured, which affects diagnosis, screening, and treatment.","Socioeconomic factors: poverty, education level, and rural residence contribute to worse outcomes. The instructor says this is seen significantly in Kentucky data.","Rural Kentucky: a large portion of the Kentucky population is rural, which limits access to screening. If a patient needs a CT scan, the question is whether there is a CT scanner, a radiologist, and an oncologist available to do this at the…","Systemic issues: biases in healthcare delivery and underrepresentation in clinical trials."]},{"s":"Etiology (Causes)▾","a":"etiology-causes","k":["Etiology (Causes)▾","The instructor reminds you that etiology means cause.","Cigarette smoking: the leading cause","Smoking and lung cancer","The leading cause of lung cancer is cigarette smoking, \"to no one's surprise.\"","80 to 90% of patients with lung cancer are current or former smokers.","Risk increases with the total number of cigarettes smoked. This is expressed as a cigarette pack-year.","Cigarette smoke is both a promoter and a known carcinogen: it causes cancer, and it promotes proliferation of mutated genes that continue to promote the development of cancer.","Pack-year example from the lecture","A 60-year-old who smokes two packs per day and started at age 20 has an 80-pack-year smoking history.","You may hear and see pack-years in practice, and there are calculators that do this.","Cross-reference: carcinogens versus promoters in general are taught in Metastasis & Etiology","E-cigarettes and vaping","Smoking rates in the country are going down, \"which is great,\" but \"we are seeing\" e-cigarette and vape use.","The instructor reiterates that these \"are not good for you.\"","There are no good studies to say that vaping increases the risk of lung cancer. There is some data to suggest it is a little less carcinogenic than cigarette smoking, but \"that data is not great right now.\"","The instructor gets a lot of questions about vaping and e-cigarettes and says she does not have the answers. It is being actively studied.","Other causes and risk factors","Passive smoke (formerly called secondhand smoke) — Being exposed to smoking consistently increases a person's risk of lung cancer by 20 to 30%. This is the big reason for smoke-free laws.","COPD — Chronic obstructive pulmonary diseases, listed as asthma, chronic bronchitis, and emphysema. The lungs are in a chronic inflamed state; this is a promoter and can cause lung cancer as well.","Asbestos — Seen quite a bit in construction workers (the instructor mentions insulation). It is not used now and is seen less, but many buildings still have asbestos. It is a known cause of cancer.","Radon (emphasized) — A chemical that comes from the ground. It is very common in Kentucky to have high radon levels. It is a known promoter and carcinogen. Studies of radon and smoking together show a compounding effect.","Arsenic — Arsenic exposure is a lung cancer etiology.","Genetics — There are many genetic components that may predispose people to getting cancer.","Prior radiation therapy to the chest — A history of cancer that required radiation therapy to the chest can increase the risk of lung cancer. The instructor notes this is also seen with breast cancer.","Radon plus smoking is a \"very dangerous combination.\" Radon and smoking together have a compounding effect, and the instructor says they see this combination quite frequently. High radon levels are very common in Kentucky.","Radon prevention","This combination is a really big reason for pushes to have radon testing of new homes and buildings, so that people know how much radon exposure they are getting.","There are radon mitigation systems that can be used.","Nursing point: take a thorough health history","If you work in the oncology or preventative space, it is important to obtain really good health histories: where the patient works and what their exposures are.","The instructor sees this quite a bit in Kentucky when thinking about carcinogenic chemical exposures, because of the high rate of blue-collar workers in the state.","Early diagnosis is \"definitely key to effective treatment,\" although most patients are not diagnosed until the later stage.","Cross-reference: chest radiation as a risk factor is also noted for breast cancer in Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer"]},{"s":"Pathogenesis▾","a":"pathogenesis","k":["Pathogenesis▾","There are many known causes of lung cancer (smoking and other environmental toxins), and the instructor says we have a pretty decent understanding of what happens, especially with smoking. She calls this one of the best-understood cancers (…","Step-by-step: how lung cancer develops","1. Cilia are paralyzed — Smoke and toxins paralyze the cilia along the respiratory tract. Normally the cilia sweep away carcinogens and bacteria.","2. Carcinogens accumulate — The cilia become less effective, causing an accumulation of carcinogens along the airway tract.","3. Overload plus genetics — The pathogenesis of lung cancer involves an overload of carcinogens along the airway from smoking and environmental toxins, and then usually some kind of genetic predisposition acquired from your parents.","4. Dysplasia and mutations — Lung cancer commonly begins in an area of dysplasia. Wherever the damage from carcinogens occurs along the airway tract, that is usually where the initial mutations are seen. It becomes a thickened area of the b…","5. Proliferation and erosion — The lesion proliferates and can erode the lining of the epithelium.","6. Growth — It grows into the bronchial lumen or becomes a mass within the lung tissue. The cancer destroys and invades surrounding tissues.","Key point stressed twice: smoke and other environmental toxins paralyze the cilia of the respiratory tract. The instructor repeats this and says it is important to recognize.","Cross-reference: dysplasia as a cellular change is covered in Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity"]},{"s":"Types of Lung Cancer▾","a":"types-of-lung-cancer","k":["Types of Lung Cancer▾","Exam scope from the instructor: \"You don't really need to know specifics about types of cancer.\" This lecture stays in broad generalities. The only thing to recognize is that there are two types, non-small cell and small cell, and that non-…","Non-small cell lung cancer","Makes up about 85 to 90% of all lung cancers.","The more common type.","Tends to grow slower than small cell lung cancer.","Growth rates can vary depending on the subtype, such as adenocarcinoma versus squamous cell carcinoma.","Small cell lung cancer","Tends to grow a little more rapidly.","Metastasizes quicker.","Generally responds well to initial treatment, but tends to relapse quicker.","How common — About 85 to 90% of all lung cancers — Less common than non-small cell","Growth — Tends to grow slower; varies by subtype (adenocarcinoma vs. squamous cell carcinoma) — Grows a little more rapidly","Spread — Not stated in lecture — Metastasizes quicker","Treatment response — Not stated in lecture — Generally responds well to initial treatment, but relapses quicker"]},{"s":"Signs & Symptoms▾","a":"signs-symptoms","k":["Signs & Symptoms▾","Why diagnosis is difficult: many of these symptoms coincide with the symptoms of people who are chronic smokers, so it can be very difficult to differentiate. That is why screening and knowing the risk factors are so important. The instruct…","Cough — A cough that does not go away or gets worse. This was called \"a big one.\"","Hemoptysis / sputum — Coughing up blood, or rust-colored sputum (spit or phlegm).","Chest pain — Chest pain that is worse with deep breathing, coughing, or laughing.","Hoarseness — May be a sign if the tumor is compressing the laryngeal nerve.","Loss of appetite — Appetite loss.","Weight loss — Unexplained weight loss.","Shortness of breath — Listed as a symptom.","Fatigue — Feeling more tired or weak.","Recurrent infections — Infections that do not go away, such as bronchitis or pneumonia, that keep coming back.","Wheezing — New onset of wheezing.","Instructor's reassurance about symptoms","Weight loss, weakness, tiredness, and loss of appetite are symptoms that patients get very worried about.","The instructor's rule of thumb: as long as you are not losing weight (not on purpose), can eat, drink, urinate, and have bowel movements, and are not actively having a fever or infection, you are probably doing okay and \"not actively dying.…","She says this knowledge came from learning about cancer etiologies in her cancer lectures."]},{"s":"Quick Review: Numbers to Know▾","a":"quick-review-numbers-to-know","k":["Quick Review: Numbers to Know▾","Five-year survival, stage 1 — Over 60%","Five-year survival, stage 4 — Below 10%","Average age at diagnosis (U.S.) — Around 70 (often over 65)","Black men vs. white men — About 15% more likely to develop lung cancer","Lung cancer patients who are current or former smokers — 80 to 90%","Example: age 60, 2 packs/day since age 20 — 80-pack-year history","Passive smoke exposure — Increases lung cancer risk by 20 to 30%","Non-small cell lung cancer — About 85 to 90% of all lung cancers"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-breast-cervical-colorectal.html","t":"Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer","secs":[{"s":"Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 5","Breast Cancer","Cervical Cancer","Colorectal Cancer","Self-Test Flashcards","This topic finishes the cancer content by covering the last three specific types of cancer: breast cancer, cervical cancer, and colorectal cancer. For each one the lecture walks through who is affected, the risk factors, and what can be don…","Cross-reference: the lecture compares the breast cancer disparities and chest-radiation risk to lung cancer — see Lung Cancer"]},{"s":"Breast Cancer▾","a":"breast-cancer","k":["Breast Cancer▾","Overview & Statistics","Breast cancer is very prevalent and common in the United States.","It does affect men and women, but it is much more likely to affect women, due to the increased amount of breast tissue that women tend to have.","One in eight women in the US will be diagnosed with breast cancer at some point in their lifetime.","It is the most common cancer in the United States. The instructor's comparison: lung cancer tends to kill you sooner, but breast cancer is the most common as of now, outside of the skin cancer already mentioned in lecture.","Lifetime risk in women — 1 in 8 women in the US will be diagnosed at some point in their lifetime","Rank — Most common cancer in the United States (outside of skin cancer)","Who it affects — Men and women, but much more likely in women because of the greater amount of breast tissue","Racial & Ethnic Disparities","As with lung cancer, breast cancer shows a lot of racial and ethnic disparities. The instructor says plainly that you do not need to memorize these statistics for the exam; the point is for healthcare providers to be aware that racial and e…","Average age at diagnosis — Black women: 60. White women: 62.","Death from breast cancer — Black women are 40% more likely to die from breast cancer than white women.","Five-year relative survival — Black women have the lowest five-year relative breast cancer survival rate of any racial or ethnic group.","Triple negative breast cancer — Black women with breast cancer are diagnosed with triple negative breast cancer, which is much harder to treat, at a rate higher than any other racial and ethnic group.","Risk Factors — Non-Modifiable","The lecture says the risk factor lists come from the CDC and the American Cancer Society. Some are non-modifiable and some are modifiable.","Age — People older than 50 are at greater risk of breast cancer.","Reproductive history — Starting menstrual periods before age 12, and starting menopause after age 55. Reasoning given: the longer the span between starting and stopping the menstrual period, the longer the exposure to hormones, which raises…","Obesity — A big risk factor, caused by increased levels of estrogen and fat deposits. The instructor lists it here and also under modifiable, and apologizes for that: you cannot really control the amount of estrogen and fat storage, but the…","Dense breast tissue — Larger breasts or more dense tissue in the breast carry a higher risk of breast cancer.","Family history — A family history of breast or ovarian cancer — either one is a big red flag for providers for prevention and screening.","Radiation to the chest — Similar to lung cancer: if you had radiation to the chest for any reason, you are at a much higher risk for breast cancer.","DES (diethylstilbestrol) — See the DES section below.","BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes — See the BRCA section below.","Risk Factors — Modifiable","Overweight or obesity after menopause — Older women who are overweight or have obesity have a much higher risk of getting breast cancer than those at a healthy weight. (Specifically after menopause.)","Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) — Historically pretty closely linked with breast cancer. As data and science have gotten better, some forms of HRT are connected to breast cancer: both estrogen and progesterone, usually taken during menopa…","Certain oral contraceptives — Hormonal oral contraceptives have also been found to raise breast cancer risk. The instructor notes that not all birth control is hormonal, even the pills.","Pregnancy and breastfeeding history — Having a first pregnancy after 20, not breastfeeding, or never having a full-term pregnancy. The instructor says this is all about hormone exposure, and that the instructor does not like this being on t…","Alcohol — Even just one drink increases risk. The instructor notes this is something most of us will be dealing with.","Night shift work — Found specifically with women and breast cancer. The instructor's reasoning: the instructor assumes it has to do with hormones being out of whack after working night shift long term.","Smoking and other carcinogen exposures — Listed together with night shift work as modifiable risks.","Instructor caution: hormone replacement therapy and oral contraceptives should not be demonized or villainized. They do a lot of good for the people who need them; the link to breast cancer is just something to think about.","DES (Diethylstilbestrol)","DES was a drug given to some pregnant women in the United States between 1940 and 1971 to prevent miscarriage.","Later it was found that women who took DES have a higher risk of getting breast cancer.","Why it still matters: we are in the era where women who took DES would be in their late 60s, 70s, and 80s, or the people who were pregnant might be in their 40s, 50s, and 60s, so we need to keep an eye on that.","DES use is also linked to cervical cancer (see the cervical cancer section).","BRCA1 and BRCA2 Genes","What they are","BRCA1 and BRCA2 are genes; mutations in the genetic code are what carry the risk.","People with BRCA mutations have a much higher risk of breast cancer — a known genetic link.","Other genes are also connected to breast cancer, but BRCA1 and BRCA2 are the most common and well studied.","We all have these genes, but we can test for their mutations.","Cancers linked to BRCA1/BRCA2 mutations","Breast cancer","Ovarian cancer","Colon cancer","Pancreatic cancer","In males with the mutations: an increased risk of prostate cancer","Genetic Counseling, Subtypes & Prevention","A lot of cancers now get genetic counseling, and breast cancer is one of them.","There are many different tags and markers of breast cancer. Triple negative was mentioned in the disparities discussion. The instructor does not go into the different types of breast cancer; just know that they exist.","If you go into oncology you need to spend time in the literature to learn which cancers are common and which treatments are expected with certain types, including whether radiation versus chemo is given. This is covered a little more in med…","If there is a family history, providers often test for the BRCA mutations early.","Women who find they have these mutations often get a preventative mastectomy or an oophorectomy (removal of the ovaries, so you do not get ovarian cancer). The instructor says this is pretty common and that more and more genetic counseling …"]},{"s":"Cervical Cancer▾","a":"cervical-cancer","k":["Cervical Cancer▾","Overview & Prevention","Cervical cancer used to be much more prevalent, in the 80s, 90s, and early 2000s.","Its prevalence has really decreased because of screening with Pap smears and the HPV vaccine.","Essentially, cervical cancer can be prevented with the HPV vaccine and frequent screenings.","Like skin cancer, Pap smears can detect early cancer mutations so they can be found sooner.","The HPV vaccine prevents human papillomavirus (HPV), which is a huge risk for cervical cancer development. By getting the vaccine you prevent HPV and significantly decrease cervical cancer rates.","Almost 100% of cervical cancer patients test positive for HPV, so cervical cancer can be prevented by preventing HPV through the HPV vaccine.","Risk Factors","HPV infection — The big one: almost 100% of cervical cancer patients test positive for HPV.","Smoking — A big risk factor for cervical cancer.","Sexual history and STIs — The earlier, the more frequent, and the more partners, the more likely you are to get HPV — which is how sexual history and STIs relate to HPV.","Other STIs (chlamydia, gonorrhea) — These can increase cervical cancer risk when compounded with HPV.","Immunosuppression — People who are immunosuppressed, such as with HIV or AIDS or some other issues, are more likely to get cervical cancer. Probably because they are more susceptible to HPV.","Genetics and family history — Listed as a risk factor.","DES — DES usage is linked to cervical cancer (DES was introduced under breast cancer above).","The instructor adds that we do not need to demonize sexuality here. It is simply a note that people with these risk factors need more frequent screenings and should be more aware of their risk.","The HPV Vaccine","Effectiveness against cervical cancer — The current vaccine (very different from the vaccine the instructor received in the early 2000s) is 97% effective in preventing cervical cancer and cervical changes, because it is so effective against…","Genital warts — It is 100% effective in treating and preventing genital warts. Use this point if patients are reluctant because they do not think their cervical cancer risk is very high — \"nobody wants genital warts.\"","Safety — Very safe. Decades-long studies show no significant risk or side effects.","Best timing — Best received as an adolescent, around age 11 or 12.","Upper age limit — Can be received up to age 45.","The instructor frames this as a public health message: spread that knowledge."]},{"s":"Colorectal Cancer▾","a":"colorectal-cancer","k":["Colorectal Cancer▾","Overview & Statistics","Colorectal cancer begins anywhere along the colon or the rectum.","It is the fourth most common cancer in men and women.","It is the fourth leading cause of cancer-related deaths.","Males have much higher rates of both incidence and dying from colorectal cancer than females. The instructor says this could be related to some of the risk factors.","Frequency — Fourth most common cancer in men and women","Cancer deaths — Fourth leading cause of cancer-related deaths","Sex difference — Males at much higher rates of incidence and of dying from it than females","Risk Factors","Age — Risk of colorectal cancer increases as you get older.","Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) — Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis highly increases the risk for colorectal cancer.","Family history — Family history of colorectal cancer or of polyps.","Genetic conditions — Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP), or hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer, which the instructor says is called Lynch syndrome. Any kind of genetic mutations.","Lifestyle: activity — Sedentary lifestyle; lack of regular physical activity.","Lifestyle: diet — Diets low in fruits and vegetables and low in fiber, and high-fat diets. There is also some relation to diets high in fat and processed meats (for example deli meat is considered a highly processed meat).","Weight — Overweight and obesity.","Alcohol and tobacco — Alcohol consumption and tobacco.","How genetic syndromes get picked up (instructor's example)","The instructor describes a friend from nursing school whose dad had colorectal cancer very early. He had this kind of genetic syndrome, so all of his children were tested, and they do have it.","Because of that, those children have to have colonoscopies every two years even though they are very young.","The instructor's point: this is generally how these syndromes are picked up — \"how would I even know that?\" — through family history and testing of relatives.","Polyps","Screening for colorectal cancer is significant, and what the screening is often looking for is polyps.","Polyps are non-cancerous growths that can develop into colorectal cancer.","They are usually found during routine screening colonoscopies or flexible sigmoidoscopies — either one.","They are usually asymptomatic: most polyps do not cause any symptoms.","Some may present with rectal bleeding, changes in bowel habits, or abdominal pain, but generally polyps do not cause symptoms, so a lot of the time they are only picked up through screening.","You may see the polyp type on a report from a colonoscopy or flexible sigmoidoscopy.","Adenomatous polyps","The most common type of polyp.","Have a higher potential to develop into cancer.","Hyperplastic polyps","Less common.","Typically do not turn into cancer.","Symptoms of Colorectal Cancer","As with many cancers covered in this unit, the symptoms are nonspecific.","Changes in bowel habits.","Blood in the stool.","Diarrhea.","Constipation.","Feeling that the bowel does not empty all the way — usually more of a rectal cancer symptom. Diarrhea and constipation can be from colon or rectal cancer.","Abdominal pain, aches, or cramping that does not go away.","Weight loss when you do not know why.","The instructor's practical message: as long as you are peeing, pooping, eating, and drinking you are probably okay, but if you notice significant changes in these along with some of the other symptoms, you definitely need to be checked out.…","Screening","The instructor says you will learn more about screening in med-surg and includes it here because it is important enough to reiterate. Screening depends on your risk.","Start age — Screening begins at 45, at regular intervals as discussed with your healthcare provider.","Higher risk — If you are at higher risk, you may begin before 45. Higher-risk examples given: IBD (inflammatory bowel disease), family history, or genetic conditions.","Flexible sigmoidoscopy — Not as intensive, because it examines only one third of the colon.","Colonoscopy — A full colonoscopy. Usually done only every 10 years, depending on whether polyps and such are found.","Instructor's example: why family history and genetics matter","The instructor's husband had a bunch of GI issues and had a colonoscopy in his early 30s, and they found a lot of polyps. He does not have colorectal cancer, but he is at higher risk, so he has to have more frequent colonoscopies.","He is one of four siblings, so all of his siblings were encouraged to get screened earlier than 45, so that higher risk could be determined in the family.","The instructor's point: genetics are obviously going to be a component, because the instructor's husband is very healthy — not sedentary, has a good BMI, and eats pretty healthy. Those kinds of things can change the risk picture a little bi…","Cross-reference: the lecture ends by moving on to the question \"what is chemotherapy\" — see Chemotherapy Principles"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-chemotherapy-principles.html","t":"Chemotherapy Principles & Toxicities","secs":[{"s":"Chemotherapy Principles & Toxicities","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 6","Anti-Neoplastic Drugs & Cytotoxic Chemotherapy","Surgery, Radiation & Chemotherapy","Two Main Groups of Chemotherapy Drugs","Factors That Affect Whether Chemo Works","General Principles of All Chemotherapy Drugs","Toxicities from Rapidly Dividing Cells","Stomatitis & Magic Mouthwash","Myelosuppression","Extravasation & Central Access","Drug Monitoring: No Routine TDM","Contraindications","Fertility & Pregnancy","Quick Recap","Self-Test Flashcards","This is the first of the chemotherapy voiceovers. It is a general overview of the basics, not a drug-by-drug review: what anti-neoplastic drugs are, how chemotherapy fits alongside surgery and radiation, the two big groups of chemo drugs (c…","Cross-reference: the instructor stops this voiceover before covering the specific medications you need to know; those are taught in Antineoplastic Drug Classes"]},{"s":"Anti-Neoplastic Drugs & Cytotoxic Chemotherapy▾","a":"anti-neoplastic-drugs-cytotoxic-chemotherapy","k":["Anti-Neoplastic Drugs & Cytotoxic Chemotherapy▾","Anti-neoplastic drugs","The general term for drugs used to treat cancer.","These are various types of chemotherapy agents that inhibit the growth and the spread of malignant cancer cells.","Chemotherapy (cytotoxic chemotherapy)","A common form of cancer treatment that uses cytotoxic drugs to kill or inhibit cancer cells.","Cytotoxic refers to the ability of these drugs to kill cells.","They particularly target rapidly dividing cells (this idea drives almost everything else on this page).","How much you are expected to know","The instructor stresses that chemotherapy is an ongoing area of research and changes very consistently; it is an ever-evolving field that changes very quickly.","For anyone who ends up in oncology (for example at Markey or another cancer center), the advice is to really know the drugs you are giving and the types of cancers they treat, to take notes, and to stay aware of the changes.","This course only introduces the basics. The instructor explains the drug matrix and what you are expected to know; this page covers the general overview."]},{"s":"Surgery, Radiation & Chemotherapy▾","a":"surgery-radiation-chemotherapy","k":["Surgery, Radiation & Chemotherapy▾","Chemotherapy is one tenet (one pillar) of treating cancer. The other two named in the lecture are surgery and radiation, and these treatments are often used in combination.","Surgery — Generally removes tumors or affected tissues.","Radiation therapy — Uses high-energy radiation to kill or damage cancer cells.","Chemotherapy — What it does depends on the kind of drug, but it also targets the cancer cells.","Choosing the treatment course","A lot of the time these treatments are used in combination, and the type of cancer being treated influences what the normal course of treatment is.","Lots of discussions need to take place before treatment begins: deciding whether to do surgery, radiation, chemo, just one of them, and so on.","The instructor says oncology is really ahead of most of the fields studied so far in shared decision making: oncologists work together with their patients to figure out the best treatment course for what is going on with that patient.","You will learn more about this in your med-surg content."]},{"s":"Two Main Groups of Chemotherapy Drugs▾","a":"two-main-groups-of-chemotherapy-drugs","k":["Two Main Groups of Chemotherapy Drugs▾","Cell cycle nonspecific","Kill cancer cells regardless of what phase of the cell cycle the cell is in.","It does not matter what phase the cells are in.","Cell cycle specific","Only work during certain phases of the cell cycle, like mitosis or DNA synthesis.","Those are the anti-metabolites and the mitotic inhibitors.","The instructor says you do not need to write this down quickly because it will be discussed as the material moves forward.","Cross-reference: the lecture points back to the cancer slides, which discuss what phase the cells are in; see Cancer Fundamentals","Growth fraction","Regardless of the group, these medications work faster on rapidly growing tumors.","Chemotherapy tends to be more effective against tumors that have a high growth fraction.","Reason (instructor's): a high-growth-fraction tumor has more cells that are actively dividing, which makes them more vulnerable to cytotoxic chemotherapy agents."]},{"s":"Factors That Affect Whether Chemo Works▾","a":"factors-that-affect-whether-chemo-works","k":["Factors That Affect Whether Chemo Works▾","The lecture lists these as the factors that determine whether chemo will cure the cancer or work effectively.","Doubling time — Tumors that grow quickly respond better to chemo (as already noted above).","Type of cancer — Some cancers are inherently more sensitive to chemotherapy. Some cancers are less likely to spread, so they are more effectively cured by surgery, and so on.","Efficacy of the treatment plan — Is the correct drug selected? Is it dosed appropriately? Is it scheduled appropriately? There are whole specific oncologists and pharmacists who are dedicated to this area.","Drug resistance — As with other types of medications, cancers can become drug resistant, which can significantly reduce treatment effectiveness over a longer period of time. Example: if a patient got one type of chemotherapy at the beginnin…","General health of the patient — A patient's overall condition affects their ability to tolerate and respond to treatment. Chemo drugs are very harsh medications and are not easy or gentle on the system."]},{"s":"General Principles of All Chemotherapy Drugs▾","a":"general-principles-of-all-chemotherapy-drugs","k":["General Principles of All Chemotherapy Drugs▾","Principles that apply across chemo","No one drug is effective against all types of cancer. (The instructor's point: if there were one, we would simply teach that one drug and move forward.)","Low therapeutic index. There is a fine line between chemotherapy being therapeutic and having toxic effects. Clinicians are monitoring the cancer spread (for example with PET scans) against the adverse or toxic effects of the drugs, and it …","Toxicity comes from affecting rapidly dividing cells. Chemo drugs can kill the harmful cancer cells but also affect normal cells.","Combination therapy usually works better than a single drug. The different mechanisms of action of different cytotoxic medications are maximized when multiples are used. There is some efficacy even as a single drug, depending on the type of…","Avoid overlapping toxicity. When combining drugs, the team is careful that the drugs do not have overlapping toxicity. Example: you would not use two chemotherapy types that both cause cardiotoxicity."]},{"s":"Toxicities from Rapidly Dividing Cells▾","a":"toxicities-from-rapidly-dividing-cells","k":["Toxicities from Rapidly Dividing Cells▾","Chemo drugs affect rapidly dividing cells, so the normal tissues that divide quickly take the damage along with the cancer. Hair follicles, the GI tract, and bone marrow are very commonly affected because they are rapidly dividing. These ar…","Hair follicles — Hair loss","GI tract — A ton of nausea and vomiting (also stomatitis in the mouth, see below)","Bone marrow — Neutropenia, anemia, thrombocytopenia","Cross-reference: neutropenia, the ANC, and the other blood-count changes are covered in Blood Cells & CBC","Timing and other common effects","Nausea and vomiting — Occurs several days after chemo.","Hair loss — Common for many drugs and reversible. Generally begins 7 to 14 days after chemo and starts to regenerate 1 to 2 months after therapy ends.","Fatigue — Extremely common.","Dose-limiting adverse effects — Sometimes patients cannot tolerate the dose increases required to treat the cancer because they are so nauseous and become so ill, or are so anemic, or are so neutropenic."]},{"s":"Stomatitis & Magic Mouthwash▾","a":"stomatitis-magic-mouthwash","k":["Stomatitis & Magic Mouthwash▾","Stomatitis is inflammation of the oral mucosa. It is commonly affected by almost all chemo medications, again because the cells of the mouth are rapidly dividing.","The instructor's way of thinking about it","A blister in your mouth (a canker sore) heals pretty quickly. A blister on your hands takes a little longer to heal.","Skin cells are rapidly dividing too, but they are not as fast as the cells of your mouth or your GI tract.","Magic mouthwash","The instructor says you can put it on your drug matrix if you want; it is a drug you need to be familiar with.","It is a prescription cocktail for stomatitis (a prescription medication).","It is commonly prescribed for stomatitis, and the exact contents vary.","Patients swish, gargle, and spit it out. It can be swallowed if there is thought to be esophageal involvement.","Viscous lidocaine — Numbs the mouth.","Maalox/Mylanta — Listed as a component (no separate role is given in the lecture).","Diphenhydramine — Helps with the inflammation.","Nystatin — The antifungal: prevents antifungal infections, or thrush.","Prednisolone — Listed as a component (no separate role is given in the lecture).","Distilled water — Listed as a component.","Simpler versions and what it can and cannot do","Simple versions may only have lidocaine, an antifungal, and diphenhydramine.","Overall, it numbs the mouth, prevents antifungal infection (thrush), and helps with inflammation.","It does not cure anything and will not fix the stomatitis, but it does treat the symptoms."]},{"s":"Myelosuppression▾","a":"myelosuppression","k":["Myelosuppression▾","What it is","Bone marrow suppression or depression.","The bone marrow is a rapidly dividing area, so it is commonly targeted and affected as an adverse effect of most chemotherapies.","It typically is caused by chemotherapy, but it can also be caused by radiation.","What it causes","Reduced production of white blood cells, red blood cells, and platelets.","This means neutropenia, anemia, and thrombocytopenia.","A very high risk of infection due to the leukopenia (usually neutropenia).","Why neutropenia is dangerous","Neutrophils are critical in fighting bacterial infections, or infections in general.","Their depletion makes patients very vulnerable to infection.","Patients are also vulnerable because they become anemic and thrombocytopenic.","Prophylactic antibiotics: levofloxacin","Because of the neutropenia or leukopenia, it is often recommended that patients get prophylactic antibiotics (the instructor says this will be discussed more in med-surg).","Levofloxacin is very common.","It is recommended in high-risk patients who have prolonged neutropenia, defined in the lecture as an ANC less than 500 for greater than 7 days.","It helps prevent bacterial infections.","Cross-reference: levofloxacin as an antibiotic is covered in Antimicrobials & Antibiotics","The nadir","The nadir is the lowest point of the white blood cells, red blood cells, and platelets. This is when patients are at highest risk. The instructor highlights it as important, and gives the timing in a few slightly different ways while talkin…","First statement — The nadir comes between 7 and 12 days.","Restated right after — \"So 7, 14 days\": the nadir is described as 7 to 14 days.","When counting from the dose — The patient gets the dose at day 0. About two weeks or so later, 10 to 14 days, is when the patient is expected to be at their highest risk for infection, anemia, and bleeding.","The nadir is the time of highest risk for infection, anemia, and bleeding. The instructor calls this a very important teaching point and a point of awareness for nursing interventions: the nadir is the lowest point of white blood cells, red…"]},{"s":"Extravasation & Central Access▾","a":"extravasation-central-access","k":["Extravasation & Central Access▾","Extravasation is a very serious complication of IV chemotherapy. Chemotherapy drugs may leak into the surrounding tissues during IV administration, which can cause severe tissue damage.","Most chemotherapy drugs are given IV, or a lot of them are.","Treatment protocols for preventing and treating extravasation are well established.","Because of this risk, chemo is generally given through a portacath or some other deep tunneled line."]},{"s":"Drug Monitoring: No Routine TDM▾","a":"drug-monitoring-no-routine-tdm","k":["Drug Monitoring: No Routine TDM▾","A common assumption that is wrong","You might expect that, because these drugs have a lot of side effects and a lot of risk, there would be lots of narrow therapeutic monitoring (drug-level monitoring).","The instructor says that is actually not true: most chemotherapy drugs do not require routine therapeutic drug monitoring.","Some do, but it is not as common as you would think."]},{"s":"Contraindications▾","a":"contraindications","k":["Contraindications▾","Usually NOT contraindications","Toxicities, adverse effects, and allergies are not usually contraindications.","There are no absolute contraindications.","Many side effects are expected and managed proactively.","Even allergies can require premedication or a desensitization protocol.","Reasons to delay or adjust chemo","A white blood cell or neutrophil count that is very, very low.","Known active infection.","Significantly decreased nutrition or hydration status.","Organ dysfunction, usually liver or kidney (monitored with kidney and liver labs).","In these situations chemotherapy may be delayed or adjusted, so these can act as contraindications.","Why these are weighed rather than treated as absolute","Low white cell counts and poor nutrition status are really common in patients with cancer.","The healthcare provider weighs the risks and benefits.","The reasoning given: the cancer is not going to stop growing just because the patient is sick."]},{"s":"Fertility & Pregnancy▾","a":"fertility-pregnancy","k":["Fertility & Pregnancy▾","Fertility","A major concern in the instructor's words \"generally\" for female patients, but it can affect males as well.","Any chemotherapy can impair fertility, especially in post-puberty patients.","Fertility preservation options: sperm banking or egg freezing.","This generally should be discussed before treatment; it is something we need to be aware of.","Pregnancy","Most chemotherapy drugs are classified as class D or X.","This means they pose significant risk to fetal development or are generally contraindicated during pregnancy.","Unfortunately, some cancers are more likely to be found during the pregnancy period, which generates scary discussions and conversations that have to be had."]},{"s":"Quick Recap▾","a":"quick-recap","k":["Quick Recap▾","Groups — Cell cycle nonspecific (any phase) vs. cell cycle specific (certain phases such as mitosis or DNA synthesis; anti-metabolites and mitotic inhibitors).","Works best on — Rapidly growing tumors, high growth fraction, short doubling time.","Principles — No one drug for all cancers; low therapeutic index; combination therapy better, without overlapping toxicity.","Toxicities — Hair follicles, GI, and bone marrow (rapidly dividing cells): hair loss, nausea and vomiting, stomatitis, neutropenia, anemia, thrombocytopenia, fatigue.","Magic mouthwash — Prescription cocktail for stomatitis; treats symptoms, does not cure.","Myelosuppression — Reduced WBC, RBC, and platelets; nadir is the highest-risk time; levofloxacin for ANC < 500 for > 7 days.","Extravasation — Leakage into tissue causes severe damage; portacath or deep tunneled line.","Monitoring — Most chemo drugs do not need routine therapeutic drug monitoring.","Contraindications — No absolute ones; very low WBC or ANC, active infection, poor nutrition or hydration, or liver or kidney dysfunction may lead to delay or adjustment after weighing risk and benefit.","Fertility and pregnancy — Any chemo can impair fertility (sperm banking, egg freezing before treatment); most chemo is class D or X."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-antineoplastic-drug-classes.html","t":"Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics","secs":[{"s":"Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 7","What You Are Expected to Know","Overview of the Classes","Cell-Cycle Specific vs Nonspecific Drugs","Cytotoxic Agents in General","Alkylating Agents (Cyclophosphamide)","Antimetabolites (Methotrexate)","Antitumor Antibiotics (Doxorubicin)","Mitotic Inhibitors / Vinca Alkaloids (Vincristine)","Hormonal Agents (Tamoxifen)","Biologics & Targeted Drug Therapies","Master Drug-Class Comparison","Antiemetics for Chemotherapy-Induced Nausea & Vomiting","Self-Test Flashcards","This topic moves from general chemotherapy principles to the specific classes of anti-cancer medication you are expected to know: alkylating agents, antimetabolites, antitumor antibiotics, and mitotic inhibitors (the cytotoxic agents), plus…","Cross-reference: the \"usual\" chemotherapy toxicities referred to throughout this page were covered earlier in Chemotherapy Principles"]},{"s":"What You Are Expected to Know▾","a":"what-you-are-expected-to-know","k":["What You Are Expected to Know▾","Exam expectations stated by the instructor","You will be expected to know the classes of medication: alkylating agents, antimetabolites, antitumor (antibiotics), mitotic inhibitors, plus hormonal agents and some biologics.","Targeted drugs are not covered separately — they generally fall under the biologics.","You do not need to know specific drug names. The instructor will always give you the class (for example, \"this is an antimetabolite drug\" or \"this is a mitotic inhibitor\"), because the class tells you in general about all of the expected ad…","The instructor will not ask you to name only a drug, and will not ask you to pick a drug for a specific cancer (\"this patient has breast cancer — what chemo do you recommend?\" is not a question you will get).","You can put the specific drug types on your drug matrix. If you have questions about what does or doesn't go on your drug matrix, ask the instructor.","The two antiemetics at the end (ondansetron and promethazine) are ones the instructor specifically wants you to know.","Instructor emphasis: know the class, then expect that class's toxicities. The lecture repeatedly ties each class to key toxicities — alkylating agents to hemorrhagic cystitis, antimetabolites to nephrotoxicity/hepatotoxicity and category X,…"]},{"s":"Overview of the Classes▾","a":"overview-of-the-classes","k":["Overview of the Classes▾","The lecture presents a high-level classification of the anti-cancer agents used in clinical practice. Cytotoxic agents remain the backbone of chemotherapy, meaning they are the most commonly used.","\"Chemotherapy\" vs \"anti-cancer\"","Cancer chemotherapy, strictly speaking, refers only to cytotoxic drugs.","Cytotoxic agents are typically the only ones called chemotherapy, whereas all the other drug types can be considered anti-cancer or anti-neoplastic drugs.","The instructor will not get into the weeds on this wording; it is just good to know.","Cytotoxic — Alkylating agents — Cyclophosphamide — Cytotoxic agents as a group are the backbone of chemotherapy (most commonly used)","Cytotoxic — Antimetabolites — Methotrexate — Also used in non-oncology conditions","Cytotoxic — Antitumor antibiotics — Doxorubicin — Derived from Streptomyces bacteria","Cytotoxic — Mitotic inhibitors (vinca alkaloids) — Vincristine — Derived from the periwinkle plant","Cytotoxic (miscellaneous) — Platinum compounds — None named — Mentioned only as \"some miscellaneous things\"","Hormonal — Hormonal agents — Tamoxifen — Commonly prescribed for hormone-sensitive cancers like breast and prostate","Biologic — Monoclonal antibodies and immune therapy agents such as checkpoint inhibitors — None named — Use the body's immune system against cancer","Targeted — Newer class designed to interfere with specific molecular targets involved in cancer growth, such as tyrosine kinase inhibitors — Tyrosine kinase inhibitors — Generally fall under the biologics","These categories reflect current prescribing trends and therapeutic strategies in oncology, and the field is shifting toward more personalized and targeted treatments. Clinicians take the cancer cells, learn all they can about them, and are…"]},{"s":"Cell-Cycle Specific vs Nonspecific Drugs▾","a":"cell-cycle-specific-vs-nonspecific-drugs","k":["Cell-Cycle Specific vs Nonspecific Drugs▾","The lecture reminds you that there are cell cycle phase specific drugs and cell cycle phase nonspecific drugs (the instructor calls it a tongue twister). The big differentiating point is whether the drug can affect cells resting in G0.","Cell cycle phase specific","Only work on a specific phase of the cell cycle.","Examples from the lecture: vinca alkaloids (vincristine) only work during the mitosis phase; methotrexate only works in the S phase.","They cannot interrupt cells that are in G0, the resting phase where a lot of cells tend to sit.","Implication: this kind of medication needs to remain in the blood for extended periods — either by prolonged infusions or by small doses at short intervals over extended times — to optimize dosing.","They do not kill cells in the resting phase.","Cell cycle phase nonspecific","It does not matter where the cells are in the cycle — the drugs are just toxic.","They can even affect cells in the G0 phase; they do kill cells in the resting phase.","Disadvantage: they are not as effective at injuring just the proliferating cells.","Examples from the lecture: cyclophosphamide and doxorubicin.","Combination therapy: because of this difference, drugs are generally used in combination — a cell cycle specific agent plus a cell cycle nonspecific agent. Make sure you understand that one includes cells in G0 and one does not.","The instructor says you do not need to memorize the cell-cycle slide. You should know that vinca alkaloids fall under cell cycle phase specific; that class is discussed specifically below, so if it matters you will see it on that slide."]},{"s":"Cytotoxic Agents in General▾","a":"cytotoxic-agents-in-general","k":["Cytotoxic Agents in General▾","This is a brief overview of the cytotoxic agents as a group; the specific types of drugs can go on your drug matrix.","Common features of all cytotoxic agents","All of them work by disrupting DNA synthesis.","Cytotoxic agents are the largest class of chemotherapy and act directly on cancer cells to cause their death.","They target high growth fraction cells, which are cells that reproduce very rapidly.","All of these drugs commonly affect hair follicles and cause nausea, vomiting, and stomatitis. Myelosuppression is also listed among the usual toxicities (named with doxorubicin).","Handling cytotoxic agents is dangerous. Any cytotoxic agent is very dangerous to handle and there are strict protocols. You need to be certified to deliver cancer drugs. A lot of these are vesicants. They may require double gloving, disposa…"]},{"s":"Alkylating Agents (Cyclophosphamide)▾","a":"alkylating-agents-cyclophosphamide","k":["Alkylating Agents (Cyclophosphamide)▾","The instructor will tell you which class a drug belongs to, so you do not need to remember on your own that cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent.","Example drug — Cyclophosphamide","Cell cycle — Cell cycle phase nonspecific","Clinical uses — A variety of cancers, including breast cancer, lymphoma, leukemia, and ovarian cancer (the instructor will not ask you to match a drug to a cancer)","Common toxicities — Affects hair follicles, nausea, vomiting, stomatitis","Extreme vesicant — Can cause tissue damage pretty extensively if it extravasates","Hemorrhagic cystitis — Often known to cause hemorrhagic cystitis due to a metabolite of the drug. Look for blood in the urine or bladder issues.","Sterility — May lead to sterility, especially if used over a prolonged period; fertility concerns need to be considered","Skin and nail discoloration — Can occur, but less common than the cystitis and sterility issues","Drug resistance — Associated with drug resistance developing over time. It may be used with another drug and may not be used if the cancer relapses.","Key toxicities: hemorrhagic cystitis, sterility, extravasation. The instructor says the exam may ask things like \"these types of drugs are associated with drug resistance over time.\" For alkylating agents, think blood in the urine/bladder i…"]},{"s":"Antimetabolites (Methotrexate)▾","a":"antimetabolites-methotrexate","k":["Antimetabolites (Methotrexate)▾","Example drug — Methotrexate, widely used as an antimetabolite; also a cytotoxic medication","Cell cycle — Cell cycle phase specific — acts primarily during the S phase by inhibiting DNA synthesis","Mechanism — Mimics natural metabolites like folic acid and interferes with folate-dependent enzymes such as dihydrofolate reductase. (The instructor says this level of biochemistry does not mean much to the instructor, who is not a biochemi…","Resistance — Resistance can develop over time (similar to alkylating agents) through increased drug efflux, altered target enzymes, or enhanced DNA repair mechanisms","Nephrotoxicity — Commonly caused, especially at high doses — make sure these patients stay hydrated","Hepatotoxicity — More common with long-term use, and usually more associated with people using methotrexate for non-oncology reasons; something to monitor with liver enzymes","Pregnancy — FDA category X — associated with fetal death and abnormalities; teratogenic and contraindicated in pregnancy","Clinical uses — Usually leukemias and lymphomas most commonly; may also be used in breast cancer, head and neck cancer, and osteosarcomas; also seen a lot for non-oncology uses like rheumatoid arthritis or psoriasis","Category X: pregnancy and fertility counseling. Because methotrexate is teratogenic and contraindicated in pregnancy, the patient may need to agree to some kind of birth control, or there may need to be a discussion about fertility use duri…","In addition to all the usual chemotherapy toxicities, antimetabolites commonly cause nephrotoxicity (so keep the patient hydrated), hepatotoxicity (monitor liver enzymes), and carry category X status."]},{"s":"Antitumor Antibiotics (Doxorubicin)▾","a":"antitumor-antibiotics-doxorubicin","k":["Antitumor Antibiotics (Doxorubicin)▾","Example drug — Doxorubicin, the drug most commonly associated with this class; a widely used antitumor antibiotic","Cell cycle — Cell cycle phase nonspecific — acts at any stage of the cell cycle","Origin — Derived from Streptomyces bacteria, the source of this antibiotic-based chemotherapy","Usual toxicities — Myelosuppression, nausea, etc.","Cardiotoxicity — Highly associated with cardiotoxicity — a major concern, especially with cumulative dosing","Clinical uses — Very effective for a lot of different types of cancers, especially breast cancers, but used in other ones too","Cardiotoxicity is the big concern with antitumor antibiotics. There are maximum doses over time (a maximum lifetime dose). The worry is development of congestive heart failure, which can be fatal. These patients usually require closer monit…","The whole field of cardio-oncology came about because of this drug. Because of its cardiotoxicity there are a lot of issues faced with it, even though it is very effective."]},{"s":"Mitotic Inhibitors / Vinca Alkaloids (Vincristine)▾","a":"mitotic-inhibitors-vinca-alkaloids-vincristine","k":["Mitotic Inhibitors / Vinca Alkaloids (Vincristine)▾","Mitotic inhibitors are also known as vinca alkaloids. The instructor asks you to make sure you know both of those bolded terms. The drug most commonly associated with this class is vincristine.","Example drug — Vincristine","Cell cycle — Cell cycle specific. Vinca alkaloids only work during the mitosis phase.","Mechanism — Inhibits microtubule formation and prevents cell division","Origin — Derived from the periwinkle plant, the natural source of vinca alkaloids","Hallmark side effect — Peripheral neuropathies — the hallmark side effect of this drug, so watch for it; it has a lot of neurotoxic effects","Vesicant — A significant vesicant; can cause significant tissue damage","Bone marrow — Unlike other chemotherapy agents, does not typically cause significant bone marrow suppression. This is a benefit: it is very useful to combine with other drugs that do cause myelosuppression.","Route — Only ever given IV","Clinical uses — More often blood cancers like acute lymphoblastic leukemia and Hodgkin's lymphoma; sometimes neuroblastomas and Wilms tumors (the instructor will not ask you to pick a drug for a specific type)","Vinca alkaloids are given IV only. If this kind of drug is given intrathecally, it will kill your patient. In addition to all the regular toxicities, the hallmark to watch for is peripheral neuropathy, and extravasation is a significant tis…"]},{"s":"Hormonal Agents (Tamoxifen)▾","a":"hormonal-agents-tamoxifen","k":["Hormonal Agents (Tamoxifen)▾","What the lecture says","Hormonal agents are commonly prescribed for hormone-sensitive cancers, like breast and prostate cancers.","One example named is tamoxifen.","You need to know the hormonal agent class as one of the classes on the exam (the instructor gives no specific toxicities for this class in the lecture)."]},{"s":"Biologics & Targeted Drug Therapies▾","a":"biologics-targeted-drug-therapies","k":["Biologics & Targeted Drug Therapies▾","This is the newest class of anti-cancer medication and it is growing rapidly. The instructor thinks this is the future of chemotherapy, although the instructor is not a cancer specialist and notes that many of the cytotoxic drugs work and w…","The instructor will not ask you for a specific type of monoclonal antibody or CAR-T therapy; you just need to understand biologics overall.","Types of biologic therapy","Immune checkpoint inhibitors — Melanoma, small cell cancer, endometrial cancers, specific types of breast cancers. Described as a big area.","T cell transfers (CAR-T cell therapy) — Often used in hematologic malignancies that occur from blood cancers","Monoclonal antibodies — Listed as a type; no specific uses given","Cancer vaccines — Prostate cancer and HPV-related cancers","Immune system modulators — Melanoma and renal cell carcinoma","Targeted therapies such as tyrosine kinase inhibitors — Designed to interfere with specific molecular targets involved in cancer growth","These therapies are often part of personalized medicine strategies and are used alone or in combination with other chemotherapy.","Side effects of biologics","Biologics have common side effects that are different from the cytotoxic toxicities. They do not cause the common toxicities seen with cytotoxic agents in the same way, because they are not that cell cycle specific. They have their own.","Flu-like symptoms — Fever, chills, fatigue, headache, muscle and joint pain","Infusion / injection site reactions — A lot are given IV (the most common route, by infusion); injection site reactions include pain, redness, and swelling","GI issues — Nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea — but not necessarily at the same rate as with the cytotoxic agents; it depends","Immune-related adverse events — Especially seen with the checkpoint inhibitors: skin rashes, colitis, pneumonitis, hepatitis, and endocrinopathies. Many of these end in \"-itis.\" They reflect an overreaction of the immune system, because the…","Neurologic effects — Headaches, dizziness, and neuropathies, especially with the CAR T-cell therapies","Serious infections — These often cause immunosuppression and can increase bacterial, viral, or fungal infection. It happens in a different way than true myelosuppression from cytotoxic therapies, but it is because the immune system is being…","Cytokine release syndrome (CRS) — Seen with CAR T-cell therapy. Can be very life-threatening and often requires very close monitoring. Pain and swelling are mentioned, along with a lot of issues with the cytokine release.","Cytokine release syndrome (CRS) is the life-threatening reaction tied to CAR T-cell therapy and needs very close monitoring.","Routes of administration","The most common route is IV.","Some are available subcutaneously (sub-Q) or IM.","Very few are available orally, or by intrathecal, intravascular, or ventricular routes (as transcribed).","Clinical trials (not exam material)","The instructor showed a slide of ongoing human clinical trials with biologics or targeted therapies as of 2025. This is explicitly not exam material.","Oncology is an extremely fast-growing, advanced field, and the cancer center at UK is involved in a lot of these clinical trials.","This is good news for patients whose cancers are not responding to traditional cytotoxic therapies, or patients who are not going to tolerate some of those therapies well."]},{"s":"Master Drug-Class Comparison▾","a":"master-drug-class-comparison","k":["Master Drug-Class Comparison▾","Everything in this table comes from the lecture. Put it on your drug matrix. Remember the instructor will give you the class, so learn each row from the class column outward.","Cyclophosphamide — Alkylating agent — Nonspecific — Hair follicles, nausea, vomiting, stomatitis; extreme vesicant (extensive tissue damage with extravasation); hemorrhagic cystitis (watch for blood in the urine or bladder issues); sterilit…","Methotrexate — Antimetabolite — Specific (S phase) — Usual toxicities plus nephrotoxicity at high doses (keep hydrated); hepatotoxicity with long-term use (monitor liver enzymes); FDA category X (fetal death and abnormalities, teratogenic, …","Doxorubicin — Antitumor antibiotic (from Streptomyces) — Nonspecific — Myelosuppression, nausea, etc.; cardiotoxicity with cumulative dosing (maximum lifetime dose); CHF that can be fatal; monitor EF and baseline echocardiogram; can occur a…","Vincristine — Mitotic inhibitor (vinca alkaloid; from periwinkle plant) — Specific (mitosis phase); inhibits microtubule formation and prevents cell division — Peripheral neuropathy is the hallmark; significant vesicant; no significant bone…","Tamoxifen — Hormonal agent — Not stated in lecture — For hormone-sensitive cancers like breast and prostate","Checkpoint inhibitors, CAR-T, monoclonal antibodies, cancer vaccines, immune modulators, tyrosine kinase inhibitors — Biologics and targeted therapies — Not cell-cycle-specific cytotoxics (the lecture says they \"aren't that cell cycle speci…","Cell cycle phase specific (do not reach G0 cells)","Methotrexate (S phase)","Vinca alkaloids / vincristine (mitosis phase)","Cell cycle phase nonspecific (can affect G0 cells)","Cyclophosphamide (alkylating agent)","Doxorubicin (antitumor antibiotic)"]},{"s":"Antiemetics for Chemotherapy-Induced Nausea & Vomiting▾","a":"antiemetics-for-chemotherapy-induced-nausea-vomiting","k":["Antiemetics for Chemotherapy-Induced Nausea & Vomiting▾","All of these drugs cause nausea and vomiting, and that is a really big issue for nutrition and hydration in patients receiving chemotherapy. The instructor wants you to know these two antiemetic drugs.","Cross-reference: the general profile of ondansetron and promethazine is covered in GI Pharmacology","Ondansetron (Zofran)","Created specifically for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting; also used in other areas (many of us have probably taken Zofran).","It blocks the type 3 serotonin receptors located in the chemo trigger zone in the brain.","Very effective by itself, but also very effective combined with other drugs, especially steroids, to help decrease nausea.","Given orally or IV.","Most common side effects: headache, diarrhea, and dizziness.","Promethazine (Phenergan)","Used for chemo, post-op, and general nausea and vomiting.","A first-generation antihistamine, which should indicate to you that it will make you very, very tired.","Major adverse effect: drowsiness or sedation.","Can also cause significant respiratory depression, like an opioid.","Lots of drug-drug interactions to be aware of.","Best given by IV piggyback because of the risk of extravasation. Make sure the IV works before IV pushing it, and dilute it down if you can.","Promethazine black box warnings: (1) use in children under 2 years old, and (2) gangrenous extravasation. Be aware of this when pushing the drug: even though it is for nausea and vomiting and is not the chemo itself, it can still be very da…","Class / mechanism — Blocks type 3 serotonin receptors in the chemo trigger zone — First-generation antihistamine","Uses — Created for chemo-induced nausea and vomiting; used elsewhere too — Chemo, post-op, and general nausea and vomiting","Routes — Oral or IV — IV piggyback preferred (confirm IV patency before pushing; dilute if possible)","Side effects — Headache, diarrhea, dizziness — Drowsiness/sedation; significant respiratory depression like an opioid; many drug-drug interactions","Safety warnings — None given in lecture — Black box: children under 2; gangrenous extravasation","Combination — Effective alone or with other drugs, especially steroids — Not stated"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-osteoporosis.html","t":"Osteoporosis","secs":[{"s":"Osteoporosis","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 8","Gender & Bone Mass","Why We Care: Bone Density, T-Scores & DEXA","Who Gets Osteoporosis? Risk Factors","Pathogenesis","Manifestations","Hip Fractures","Pharmacotherapy: Goals & Overview","Calcium & Vitamin D","Drug Table","Bisphosphonates: Alendronate (Fosamax)","SERMs: Raloxifene (Evista)","Calcitonin","Hormone Replacement Therapy","Quick Comparison: Which Drug Reduces Which Fractures?","Self-Test Flashcards","This is the first of several lectures on diseases and problems of the musculoskeletal system. Osteoporosis is low bone density with structural deterioration of the bone, and it matters because of the risk of fractures. This topic covers gen…"]},{"s":"Gender & Bone Mass▾","a":"gender-bone-mass","k":["Gender & Bone Mass▾","The lecture starts with why women are affected so much more than men. Several factors affect bone mass: age, gender, race, genetics, reproductive status, calcium levels, and exercise. The lecture looks particularly at age and gender.","Women","Experience greater bone loss in the early post-menopausal years; after that period of bone loss ends, the loss continues at a more gradual rate.","Also have a lower peak bone density than men.","These factors combine so that women reach a fracture threshold (the point where the risk of fracture significantly increases) much earlier than men.","Over a lifetime, women lose about 35% of cortical bone and 50% of trabecular bone.","Beginning at age 30, the amount of bone resorbed by osteoclasts exceeds the amount formed by osteoblasts, resulting in a steady decrease in bone mass with age.","Men","Lose only about two-thirds of the amount women lose over a lifetime, so men lose about a third less than women.","The same pattern is seen in men: after age 30 the bone-mass curve starts to go down.","Bone mass decreases as a function of age in both males and females, but women lose much more in the immediate post-menopausal period.","The instructor points to a slide graph of bone mass versus age: both curves start to go down after age 30, and women's curve drops steeply in the early post-menopausal years and then continues down at a more gradual rate."]},{"s":"Why We Care: Bone Density, T-Scores & DEXA▾","a":"why-we-care-bone-density-t-scores-dexa","k":["Why We Care: Bone Density, T-Scores & DEXA▾","We care because low bone density means a greater likelihood of breaking bones, and low bone density is considered osteoporosis. Bone density is measured with a T-score. Osteoporosis is defined as a bone mineral density 2.5 standard deviatio…","Normal — −1 or greater (higher than −1) standard deviations — Normal bone density.","Osteopenia — Between −1 and −2.5 standard deviations — Considered low bone mass. The stage before osteoporosis, with thinning of the trabecular matrix of the bone.","Osteoporosis — Less than −2.5 standard deviations — Holey, porous bones that are weaker.","Severe osteoporosis — Osteoporosis (T-score below −2.5) plus a history of a fragility fracture — The diagnosis of \"severe\" is given when there is a history of a fragility fracture.","What is a fragility fracture?","The person fell from standing and broke a bone.","It is not a traumatic accident; the bone broke because it is so porous.","Osteopenia vs. Osteoporosis","Osteopenia is the stage before osteoporosis, with thinning of the trabecular matrix. Trabecular bone is the porous bone. It is seen with a T-score between −1 and −2.5 on DEXA.","Osteoporosis is \"holey\" (porous) bone, reached at a T-score of −2.5. It is obviously a weaker bone.","Osteoporosis is a common yet very serious disease. It is serious because of the risk of fractures, and it is characterized by low bone density and structural deterioration of the bone.","It is most common in the hip, vertebrae, and wrist bones, which the lecture describes as trabecular bones.","Characteristics of the Disease","Low bone mass.","Microarchitectural deterioration of the bone (the lecture shows X-rays/a figure of the changes in bone architecture at a microscopic level).","In the slide image, the osteoporotic bone (on the left) shows clear loss of bone, with large gaps and breaks within the architecture of the bone.","This increases bone fragility and ultimately leads to weak bones and fractures."]},{"s":"Who Gets Osteoporosis? Risk Factors▾","a":"who-gets-osteoporosis-risk-factors","k":["Who Gets Osteoporosis? Risk Factors▾","Aging — Because of increased resorption and bone breakdown.","Female sex, especially post-menopausal women — Post-menopause is related to the drop in estrogen.","Caucasian or Asian race — Caucasians have about a 20% likelihood of getting osteoporosis when they are over 50.","History of a fracture as an adult — Breaking a large bone, or any kind of bone, as an adult increases the likelihood of developing osteoporosis.","Genetics / family history — Genetics play a huge role. A first-degree relative with a fragility fracture is a very high risk factor.","Low body weight; thin, small frame — Listed as risk factors.","Smoking — Identified as a major risk factor (the lecture phrases it as problems with bone healing). It was identified decades ago and there is a direct relationship between tobacco use and decreased bone density. The relationship is actuall…","Alcohol use — Can increase risk.","Long-term corticosteroid therapy — A major risk factor because corticosteroids inhibit osteoblast function (osteoblasts build up bone when there is breakdown).","No weight-bearing exercise — Listed as a risk factor.","Lack of calcium or vitamin D — Listed as a risk factor.","Eating disorders; history of gastric bypass surgery — These are risk factors because of a malnutrition status.","Lack of estrogen or testosterone (low T, low estrogen) — Listed as risk factors.","Excessive caffeine consumption — Listed as a risk factor.","Cross-reference: long-term corticosteroid therapy is a major osteoporosis risk factor; steroids are covered in Stress & Steroids"]},{"s":"Pathogenesis▾","a":"pathogenesis","k":["Pathogenesis▾","Bone is a living tissue. Bone remodeling is the process of resorption and formation. It occurs throughout your lifetime, and about 10% of bone tissue is replaced each year.","Osteoclasts","Cause bone resorption (breakdown).","Stimulate the production of enzymes that dissolve bone.","The instructor's shorthand: osteoclasts \"destroy\" bone.","Osteoblasts","Build up the protein matrix of bone.","Osteoblasts build bone.","MEMORY AID (from the instructor): osteoBlasts Build; osteoClasts destroy. The instructor remembers it with the word \"build.\"","In osteoporosis: increased osteoclast activity and decreased osteoblast activity. We cannot make new bone because we do not have the osteoblast function, and we have too much bone resorption from osteoclasts."]},{"s":"Manifestations▾","a":"manifestations","k":["Manifestations▾","Early","There are really no manifestations early on.","So the approach is risk-factor identification: if someone is high risk, DEXA scans are started earlier.","Later","Fractures","Pain","Loss of height","Stooped posture, like kyphosis"]},{"s":"Hip Fractures▾","a":"hip-fractures","k":["Hip Fractures▾","One of the biggest, most concerning, and most common complications of osteoporosis is the hip fracture.","Epidemiology, Mortality & Outcomes","Osteoporotic hip fractures lead to increased risk of mortality: once you have an osteoporotic hip fracture, the risk of death increases by about 2.5 to 4 times.","Deaths do not occur from the fracture itself, but from complications after it that are related to immobility: sepsis, skin breakdown, immobility, pneumonia, and blood clots.","Much more common in women older than 65, because osteoporosis is much more common in women.","The most common location is the proximal third of the femur.","In addition to the increased risk of death, independent living is usually lost, and these patients often end up in some kind of care facility such as a nursing home.","Presentation","Onset — Elderly patients usually describe the sudden onset of hip pain, either before or after a fall. Which comes first depends on whether it is a fragility fracture or whether they fall and break it or break it and fall.","Walking — They lose the ability to walk, although some patients with a minimally impacted fracture can continue to bear weight for a period of time.","Pain — Severe groin pain is common; a displaced hip fracture usually involves a significant amount of groin pain.","Leg position — The affected leg appears externally rotated and shortened.","Tenderness / bruising — The patients develop tenderness, but there is typically little bruising because the fracture is usually encapsulated. The contrast the instructor gives: a person who breaks an arm skateboarding and the arm breaks ope…","Insufficiency (Fragility) Fractures","There may be no obvious history of trauma.","The patient may complain of vague knee, groin, or thigh pain.","In these cases the bone is so damaged and has lost so much density that it can no longer bear weight, and that is when the fragility fracture develops.","Once the fracture occurs there is typically no difference in the presentation; just know that a fragility fracture may be present in this situation."]},{"s":"Pharmacotherapy: Goals & Overview▾","a":"pharmacotherapy-goals-overview","k":["Pharmacotherapy: Goals & Overview▾","The goal of pharmacotherapy is to reduce fractures. Pharmaceutical companies test drugs that increase bone mineral density, but an increase in bone density does no good if it does not reduce fractures. That is why, when looking at the effec…","Prevention mainly consists of calcium and vitamin D supplementation.","Treatment consists of either promoting bone formation or decreasing bone resorption.","The most common drugs (the ones covered) decrease bone resorption.","At this time there is only one drug that promotes bone formation. It is a new class of drugs that focuses on the parathyroid. The instructor will not cover it because it is still not commonly used.","Drug groups covered: bisphosphonates, selective estrogen receptor modulators (SERMs), and hormone therapy (which the lecturer says includes calcitonin)."]},{"s":"Calcium & Vitamin D▾","a":"calcium-vitamin-d","k":["Calcium & Vitamin D▾","Role — Prevention mainly consists of calcium and vitamin D supplementation. Common especially in post-menopausal women.","Elemental calcium dose — For a true calcium replacement (not calcium carbonate), the amount of elemental calcium is around 1200 to 2000. The instructor says you do not need to memorize dosages, but these are the common ones.","Calcium carbonate (Tums) — Some patients may take much higher doses of calcium carbonate (Tums) as a calcium supplement.","Vitamin D — Increases the absorption of calcium.","Calcium — Helps with bone sturdiness."]},{"s":"Drug Table▾","a":"drug-table","k":["Drug Table▾","The instructor does not ask you to memorize doses. Bisphosphonates are the first-line drugs; know alendronate and raloxifene (the SERM \"you need to know\"). Compare the groups below.","Alendronate (Fosamax) — bisphosphonate — First-line for osteoporosis. Used for both prevention and treatment. — Binds permanently to the surface of the bones and inhibits osteoclast activity, so it decreases bone breakdown. The true mechani…","Raloxifene (Evista) — SERM — Used to prevent and treat postmenopausal osteoporosis only. Bisphosphonates are definitely first line. — Mimics estrogen: binds estrogen receptors and has estrogen-like effects on bone, reducing bone turnover an…","Calcitonin — placed by the lecturer under hormone therapy — Not first line; not used for long-term therapy. Treatment only, not prevention. May be seen used short term. Not as effective as SERMs or bisphosphonates for long-term therapy. — I…","Hormone replacement therapy (estrogen) — Not recommended for osteoporosis. No longer commonly used to treat it. — Post-menopause is related to the drop in estrogen, and estrogen replacement is sometimes used in the post-menopausal period fo…"]},{"s":"Bisphosphonates: Alendronate (Fosamax)▾","a":"bisphosphonates-alendronate-fosamax","k":["Bisphosphonates: Alendronate (Fosamax)▾","Alendronate is \"your first drug\" and the first-line drug class for osteoporosis.","Reduces fractures by about 50%.","Used for both prevention and treatment.","Binds permanently to the surface of the bones and inhibits osteoclast activity, so bone breakdown decreases. The true mechanism of how it works is not really well known.","Adverse effects: GI effects are common; esophageal ulcerations.","It is typically now given only once a week, so patients do not have to think about it every day.","The instructor stresses that the low bioavailability is what causes many of the teaching points.","Safety / administration teaching for alendronate: tell patients they do not want to lie down right after taking it; they want to stay sitting up (standing) for a while, because it can cause esophageal ulcerations. Do not take it with calciu…"]},{"s":"SERMs: Raloxifene (Evista)▾","a":"serms-raloxifene-evista","k":["SERMs: Raloxifene (Evista)▾","SERM = selective estrogen receptor modulator. The one the instructor says you need to know is raloxifene (Evista).","Works by mimicking estrogen: it produces estrogen-like effects on bone by binding to estrogen receptors.","Reduces bone turnover and increases bone density, but not as well as bisphosphonates.","This drug is safer than using estrogen (hormone replacement).","Used to prevent and treat postmenopausal osteoporosis only; not everybody will get it. Bisphosphonates are definitely the first-line drugs.","In one study it reduced the risk of spinal fractures by 50% but not any other types of fractures, including hip.","Side effects: hot flashes and leg cramping, and there is a stroke risk.","You must take adequate calcium and vitamin D replacement for SERMs to work.","Raloxifene clot / stroke risk: there is a stroke risk. Discontinue at least 72 hours before a planned procedure, because the drug mimics estrogen and there is an increased risk of clotting. Any prolonged immobilization period or high risk o…"]},{"s":"Calcitonin▾","a":"calcitonin","k":["Calcitonin▾","The instructor covers calcitonin \"quickly.\" Note that in this lecture calcitonin is described as part of hormone therapy.","Not used for long-term therapy; it is not as effective as SERMs or bisphosphonates for long-term therapy, but you may see it used short term.","Inhibits bone removal by osteoclasts.","Treatment only, not prevention.","May provide an analgesic effect in patients with acute vertebral fractures.","Has been shown to reduce spinal fractures only, and the benefits come only after taking it for 5 years.","It can also reduce pain in someone who has a hip fracture, which is a unique feature compared to the other drugs: you will not see that with a SERM or a bisphosphonate.","Increases spinal bone density, but it is not a first-line drug: it is expensive, and bisphosphonates are much more effective.","It is typically given intranasal, so it can cause nasal irritation."]},{"s":"Hormone Replacement Therapy▾","a":"hormone-replacement-therapy","k":["Hormone Replacement Therapy▾","HRT is no longer commonly used to treat osteoporosis because of its risks: blood clots, breast cancer, and other cancers.","Remember that post-menopause is related to the drop in estrogen, so estrogen replacement is sometimes used in the post-menopausal period because of other issues.","However, estrogen/hormone replacement therapy is not recommended in osteoporosis because those risks are just too high."]},{"s":"Quick Comparison: Which Drug Reduces Which Fractures?▾","a":"quick-comparison-which-drug-reduces-which-fractures","k":["Quick Comparison: Which Drug Reduces Which Fractures?▾","Alendronate (bisphosphonate) — About 50% reduction in fractures.","Raloxifene (SERM) — 50% reduction in spinal fractures in one study; not any other fracture types, including hip.","Calcitonin — Reduces spinal fractures only, with benefit after 5 years of use; can reduce pain in a hip fracture."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-fractures-osteomyelitis.html","t":"Fractures & Osteomyelitis","secs":[{"s":"Fractures & Osteomyelitis","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 9","What Is a Fracture?","Causes of Fractures","Classification of Fractures","What Does a Person With a Fracture Look Like?","Phases of Bone Healing","Complications of Fractures — Overview","Delayed Healing, Malunion & Non-Union","Impaired Bone Growth","Compartment Syndrome","Fat Embolism","Osteomyelitis","Self-Test Flashcards","The lecture has already touched on hip fractures, specifically fragility fractures, in connection with osteoporosis. This topic now covers fractures in general: what a fracture is, what causes one, how fractures are classified, what a perso…","Cross-reference: hip and fragility fractures related to osteoporosis were covered earlier — see Osteoporosis"]},{"s":"What Is a Fracture?▾","a":"what-is-a-fracture","k":["What Is a Fracture?▾","Definition","A fracture is any break in the continuity of bone.","It occurs when more stress is placed on the bone than the bone is able to absorb.","The amount of stress needed to break a bone is relative: weak, brittle bones will not take very much, whereas young, healthy bone can withstand really high forces."]},{"s":"Causes of Fractures▾","a":"causes-of-fractures","k":["Causes of Fractures▾","Traumatic — Example given: a fall.","Indirect — Example given: a massive muscle contraction can break a bone. The instructor notes this would not be very common.","Fatigue — Usually seen with endurance activity — repeated, prolonged stress. Think marathon runners and stress fractures. (The lecture slide's picture is an example of a fatigue/stress fracture.)","Pathologic — The highest-risk population is the elderly. Seen in relation to osteoporosis, bone tumors, or infection — infections that decrease the integrity of the bone."]},{"s":"Classification of Fractures▾","a":"classification-of-fractures","k":["Classification of Fractures▾","Open vs. Closed","Open = Compound","The bone penetrates the skin.","The lecture shows a very extreme example in a picture.","Later in the topic: an open (compound) fracture carries a higher osteomyelitis risk than a closed fracture.","Closed = Simple","The fracture does not break through the skin.","The lecture shows a picture of a closed fracture.","Fracture Orientations","The instructor says she is not going to ask a ton of these. She mentions a few and highlights the ones she feels you should be familiar with, in terms of terminology.","Transverse — A straight line, usually at a 90-degree angle to the length of the bone. — A \"pretty common one\" — the type seen most commonly in traumatic falls.","Spiral — A twisting injury. — The lecture says the fall-on-the-feet/compression type of injury can also be like a spiral.","Comminuted — More than one fracture line and more than two bone fragments. — Example given: people who fall on their feet and break their legs or ankles, described as being like a compression fracture. It can also be spiral, and it can also…","Impacted — Named in the lecture in connection with jumping from heights. — The lecture gives no separate definition for impacted fractures.","Greenstick — An incomplete break where the bone bends. — Flagged by the instructor as \"the other one I want you to be familiar with.\" It is the most common type of break in children.","Stress — Caused by repeated use or fatigue. — Mentioned earlier under fatigue causes (e.g., marathon runners)."]},{"s":"What Does a Person With a Fracture Look Like?▾","a":"what-does-a-person-with-a-fracture-look-like","k":["What Does a Person With a Fracture Look Like?▾","MNEMONIC — \"PED\": Pain, Edema, Deformity.","Pain — Present at the site of the fracture or bone disruption. It is an inflammatory response. You want inflammation to help splint — your body helping to stabilize the area — so in that sense it is a good thing: you do not want to move it.…","Edema — Acts as a natural splint. Swelling is very, very common — the lecture says it \"always\" happens.","Deformity — The person holds the limb in a position of comfort, tries to splint it, and goes to the emergency department. The goal is to keep the part in the most neutral position possible.","Also seen — Loss of function and abnormal mobility in someone with a broken bone."]},{"s":"Phases of Bone Healing▾","a":"phases-of-bone-healing","k":["Phases of Bone Healing▾","The instructor calls this a review from A&P. The three phases are inflammatory, reparative, and remodeling.","1. Inflammatory phase — A hematoma sets (forms) at the fracture.","2. Fibrous cartilage — Fibrous cartilage forms after the hematoma.","3. Callus — A callus forms.","4. Ossification — The callus ossifies.","5. Remodeling — Ultimately the bone remodels.","Why this matters: many fracture complications are related to the healing process. The instructor's examples: maybe the callus did not form, maybe something went wrong with the hematoma, or maybe the callus was not resorbed."]},{"s":"Complications of Fractures — Overview▾","a":"complications-of-fractures-overview","k":["Complications of Fractures — Overview▾","The lecture names four complications at this point: delayed healing, bone growth impairment, compartment syndrome, and fat emboli. It then adds osteomyelitis, which the instructor calls probably the most common complication seen with fractu…","Delayed healing (delayed union) — Bone pain and increasing tenderness when the bone should have healed already; can lead to malunion and non-union.","Impaired bone growth — A pediatric consideration, especially with a fracture through the epiphyseal plate.","Compartment syndrome — Increased pressure within a limited anatomic space; pain out of proportion; treated with fasciotomy.","Fat embolism — Rare; follows a long bone fracture; classic triad of hypoxemia, altered level of consciousness, and petechial rash.","Osteomyelitis — Severe bone infection, usually Staphylococcus aureus; long IV antibiotic treatment."]},{"s":"Delayed Healing, Malunion & Non-Union▾","a":"delayed-healing-malunion-non-union","k":["Delayed Healing, Malunion & Non-Union▾","Delayed Healing (Delayed Union)","What it looks like","Usually identified three months to a year after the fracture.","Bone pain and tenderness are increasing, even though you would expect the bone should have healed by now.","Healing is slow — possibly due to infection or other risk factors such as smoking, malnutrition, and poor circulation.","Delayed healing can lead to malunion and non-union.","Malunion vs. Non-Union","Malunion","Improper alignment.","Occurs with unequal stresses of muscle pull and gravity.","Analogy: you did not put the stick back together straight.","Common in a person who began weight bearing too soon.","Non-union","The fracture has not healed four to six months after the fracture.","No healing at all.","The instructor says this is really particular to people with diabetes and infection in the area.","Risk Factors for Delayed Union / Malunion / Non-Union","The lecture lists these risk factors for delayed union (delayed healing), malunion (improper alignment — the sticks do not align), and non-union (no healing).","Risk factors named","Tobacco use","Older age","Severe anemia","Uncontrolled diabetes","Low vitamin D level","Hypothyroidism","Poor nutrition","Infection","Complicated breaks","Management: ideally you would address or fix the problem when it is only a delayed union or malunion. This is done by following these people frequently and getting frequent extra x-rays as the bone begins to heal."]},{"s":"Impaired Bone Growth▾","a":"impaired-bone-growth","k":["Impaired Bone Growth▾","This is a pediatric consideration.","It is especially common when there is a fracture through the epiphyseal plate.","It can delay future bone growth.","The instructor notes you will talk about this more in your pediatric class."]},{"s":"Compartment Syndrome▾","a":"compartment-syndrome","k":["Compartment Syndrome▾","Another complication from some types of fractures.","Causes / Setups","More common with crush injuries.","People whose casts are put on too tight.","If swelling begins, or bleeding occurs.","If the patient has a cast, you must closely evaluate the development of swelling within the confines of the cast and watch it carefully.","Mechanism","It results from increased pressure within a limited anatomic space.","This causes compression of the internal structures of the limb.","Blood vessels are compressed, which leads to decreased circulation and necrosis.","Nerves are compressed, which can cause pain, numbness, and really uncomfortable sensations.","The result is a tourniquet effect: the edema at the fracture site puts such intense pressure on the soft tissue and circulation that it leads to tissue hypoxia of the muscles and nerves.","Manifestations","Extreme pain out of proportion to the injury — Example: the patient was stabilized, and then begins to have extreme pain again.","Weak or lost palpable pulses — The pulses are very weak; you may need to Doppler them.","Edema — A lot of edema. The instructor stresses this is not pitting edema; it is actual edema. It typically happens very quickly.","Treatment","Compartment syndrome is treated with a fasciotomy. They cut into the muscle space and release some of the edema and tissue swelling. The area is packed and left so the edema can ooze out. The instructor notes these can be done right at the …"]},{"s":"Fat Embolism▾","a":"fat-embolism","k":["Fat Embolism▾","Luckily this is pretty rare, but it is potentially a catastrophic event.","What it is","A fat molecule in the lung following a long bone fracture, usually associated with a large trauma.","It is a type of pulmonary embolism: fat molecules block a blood vessel in the lung.","Fat molecules from the bone marrow or traumatized tissue are released into the bloodstream and travel to the lungs.","The lecture says there are several theories as to why fat emboli occur (not detailed).","Who gets it","The instructor says to be most concerned about who gets this: a long bone fracture.","Think the big bones — the legs are the most common.","Presentation: a very acute onset of pulmonary embolism. The patient may be fine one minute and in respiratory arrest the next minute. The instructor calls it very scary.","Classic Triad","Hypoxemia — Low O2.","Altered level of consciousness — Or a completely decreased level of consciousness.","Petechial rash — The rash is often the last symptom to occur. It results from occlusion of dermal capillaries by fat globules, leading to extravasation of erythrocytes.","Treatment","Treatment is supportive.","The embolism is self-limiting: it will eventually be absorbed by the body (it dissolves) if the person can be stabilized and supported sufficiently until then.","If it is small enough, a ventilator may sustain the patient.","They may need something more advanced, like ECMO (extracorporeal membrane oxygenation). The instructor says she has seen people put on ECMO for a fat embolism."]},{"s":"Osteomyelitis▾","a":"osteomyelitis","k":["Osteomyelitis▾","The instructor says probably the most common complication we would see with fractures is osteomyelitis.","Definition & Organism","The word","-itis = inflammation","osteo = bone","myelo = marrow cavity","A severe infection of the bone and the local tissue that requires immediate treatment.","It can be acute or chronic — a pus-producing infection of the bone.","Typically caused by bacteria found on the skin: Staphylococcus aureus.","The bacteria get into the bone and soft tissue while the area is trying to heal, get trapped, and begin an infection. The infection causes delayed healing.","Who Is Most Likely to Get It","People with very recent trauma","Open (compound) fractures — a higher risk than closed fractures","People with diabetes","People on hemodialysis","IV drug use","People with splenectomies","Routes of Contamination","Direct","Outside bacteria get in through an open wound: an open fracture, a gunshot, a puncture, or surgery.","The instructor says direct contamination is kind of uncommon.","Indirect","Seen commonly.","The person gets an infection in the bloodstream, and it goes to a bone in an area and causes it not to heal.","Example from the lecture picture: a patient with a sternotomy who had a bloodstream infection, probably from an IV line, which then got into the sternal area and became an infection.","The lecture slide also shows common areas you might see this; it occurs from something like a bacterial bloodstream infection.","Why It Is So Problematic","Chain of events","Pressure increases within the bone.","This causes local arteries to collapse.","Without arterial blood supply, there is a decreased supply of oxygen, nutrients, and immune cells.","It is very difficult to get antibiotics to the area.","This leads to impaired healing and makes the infection very difficult to treat.","Manifestations","Local","Local tenderness, warmth, and redness — which is why you are always monitoring those areas.","Infected wound drainage.","More restricted movement than expected, or the person had a level of mobility and then lost it.","If it is from a bacteremia, the person may have a spontaneous fracture.","Systemic (common)","Fever","Positive blood culture","Increased white blood cells","Treatment","1. Culture — Obtain a blood culture to see which antibiotics apply.","2. Empiric start — It is common to start with nafcillin and vancomycin.","3. Narrow — Once the specific culture results are back, you typically switch to an antibiotic specific for that type of bacteria.","4. Duration — IV for a long time: four to six weeks. If the infection gets under control quickly, they may do two weeks of IV antibiotics and two weeks of oral. Often, though, it depends on severity.","Severe cases — Because it is difficult for blood flow to carry the antibiotic to the site, the instructor has seen very severe cases treated with 24-hour infusions of antibiotics in the hospital.","Surgical / local options — Sometimes the wound is opened again and antibiotics are infused into the actual area. Sometimes they open the patient, wash the area out with antibiotics, close, go back again a week later, and do it again.","Osteomyelitis can be cured and fixed, but it is difficult and requires long-standing treatment — typically weeks of IV antibiotics. The instructor stresses it is something to avoid at all costs.","Cross-reference: nafcillin and vancomycin, the antibiotics named for starting osteomyelitis treatment, are covered in Antimicrobials & Antibiotics"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-osteoarthritis-disc-disease.html","t":"Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease","secs":[{"s":"Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 10","Synovial Joint Review","Terminology: Arthropathy vs. Arthritis","Osteoarthritis (OA): Definition & Statistics","Which Joints Are Affected vs. Spared","Risk Factors","Pathogenesis: Step by Step","Manifestations","Management of OA","Degenerative Disc Disease (DDD)","Self-Test Flashcards","Arthritis is a very common disorder seen in many types of patients, but there are different types of arthritis. This topic begins with a quick review of the synovial joint, because most joint disorders (arthritis included) affect synovial j…"]},{"s":"Synovial Joint Review▾","a":"synovial-joint-review","k":["Synovial Joint Review▾","Most joint disorders, arthritis being one of them, affect synovial joints. Synovial joints are typically movable.","Outer fibrous capsule (joint capsule) — The joint capsule connects the bone and the synovial membrane in the lining.","Inner synovial membrane — Inner layer of the joint; the capsule connects it to the bone (no separate role given).","Articular cartilage — Prevents friction between bones; acts like a protective coating.","Synovial fluid — Lubricates the joint.","Because of the smooth surfaces of the articular cartilage and the lubricating synovial fluid, the bones can come together and move easily. The key point of the review: \"unfortunately, sometimes we lose those things.\""]},{"s":"Terminology: Arthropathy vs. Arthritis▾","a":"terminology-arthropathy-vs-arthritis","k":["Terminology: Arthropathy vs. Arthritis▾","Arthropathy","A joint disorder.","The general term for something wrong with a joint.","Arthritis","When the joint disorder involves inflammation of one or more joints, it becomes arthritis.","There are multiple types of arthritis; osteoarthritis is the first one covered, and rheumatoid arthritis follows in the next part of the lecture."]},{"s":"Osteoarthritis (OA): Definition & Statistics▾","a":"osteoarthritis-oa-definition-statistics","k":["Osteoarthritis (OA): Definition & Statistics▾","What OA is","Osteoarthritis is degeneration of joints caused by aging and stress.","The instructor abbreviates it as OA. It occurs with wear and tear on the joints over time and leads to inflammation in the joint.","The inflammation is not systemic, just localized to the affected joint(s).","It is a chronic disease process.","Disability — The most common cause of disability in the United States; also stated as the leading cause of chronic disability in the United States. OA is the most common form of arthritis.","Why incidence is rising — Obesity and longer life expectancy. People are living longer, and age is a big risk factor; people being obese is also causing the incidence of OA to increase.","Projection — By 2040, about 78 million Americans are likely to be affected.","Sex difference — Affects men before the age of 45 more than women, but it is more common in women after 45.","Expected consequence — With obesity on the rise, the instructor expects an increase in joint replacements because of osteoarthritis."]},{"s":"Which Joints Are Affected vs. Spared▾","a":"which-joints-are-affected-vs-spared","k":["Which Joints Are Affected vs. Spared▾","Where do we see these joints locally being affected? Learn both lists.","Joints typically affected","The back, starting in the cervical spine (neck area) or the lumbar-sacral spine (lower back)","Hip","Knee","Hands","Big toe","Joints typically spared","Wrists","Elbows","Ankles"]},{"s":"Risk Factors▾","a":"risk-factors","k":["Risk Factors▾","The instructor's framing: these risk factors lead to the etiology. A lot of stress is applied to a certain joint, and this leads to breakdown or degeneration of its cartilage. Degenerative changes begin in the articular cartilage as a resul…","Aging — \"A big one.\" You begin to lose articular cartilage as you age. This specifically starts over age 40, but the older you are, the more at risk you are.","Obesity — Strains the hips and knees by increasing the load, which increases biomechanical stress and leads to breakdown of cartilage. Being only 10 pounds overweight increases the force on the knee by about 30 to 60 pounds a step.","Team sports — A history of, or participation in, team sports, particularly longer participation. The instructor's example: playing soccer one time as a 12-year-old does not mean you are going to get OA. The risk is wear and tear from overus…","Occupational work — Any repetitive work. This lines up with a history of trauma, overuse, and heavy occupational work. Examples named: gardeners, construction workers, anyone who works outside, heavy lifting, factory work.","Misalignment — Misalignment of the pelvis, hip, knee, ankle, or foot. Women have a built-in misalignment with wider hip joints. One leg shorter than the other can alter the weight balance. If one joint causes pain, the person may shift to c…","Trauma / injury — History of trauma and overuse. Injury during sports play leads to OA of the knees or hips; a previously injured joint under normal loading can degenerate."]},{"s":"Pathogenesis: Step by Step▾","a":"pathogenesis-step-by-step","k":["Pathogenesis: Step by Step▾","The instructor asks \"what is the step-by-step here?\" and walks through the sequence below. Follow it in order.","1. Cartilage wear — Pressure on the joint slowly wears away the cartilage surface, exposing the subchondral bone.","2. Subchondral cysts — This causes cysts to develop in the subchondral area. The articular cartilage erodes from the cysts going through it.","3. Repair attempt by chondrocytes — Chondrocytes synthesize a fluid called proteoglycans in an effort to repair the cartilage.","4. Swelling — This excess fluid, which is supposed to be helpful, is actually what causes swelling of the joint.","5. Inflammation feeds degeneration — Localized inflammation can further degrade the cartilage. The cysts move through the remaining cartilage and destroy the rest, causing more inflammation and more degeneration.","6. Osteoblast activation — Osteoblasts (the cells that build up bone) are activated and build bone spurs. Bone spurs come from osteoblasts trying to fix what is going on.","7. Further inflammation and thickened fluid — The bone remodeling ultimately leads to further inflammatory responses and synovial fluid thickening, which causes decreased movement.","8. Bone on bone — Loss of cartilage narrows the joint space. You lose the synovial fluid (or it becomes thick and \"non-purposeful\"), you get bone spurs, and you get bone on bone.","Osteophytes (bone spurs): an important hallmark","Osteophytes are bone spurs caused by osteoblasts.","The instructor calls them an important hallmark of OA. The remodeling is \"not appropriate\": the body keeps trying to build and repair, but the result is a bone spur.","Bone spurs can be seen on x-ray.","They can actually break off and get into the synovial cavity and cause a lot of problems.","The synovial fluid thickens as cytokines/inflammation occur, leading to even further decreased movement.","The Whole Cycle in One Pass","Exposed bone develops cysts, the cysts move through the cartilage, chondrocytes try to build and repair but cause swelling, inflammation continues to degrade the cartilage, and the body continues to try to remodel the bone but produces bone…"]},{"s":"Manifestations▾","a":"manifestations","k":["Manifestations▾","\"We can probably all understand what someone with arthritis looks like.\" The lecture's picture of the OA patient:","Pain — Deep, aching joint pain, especially with exertion, that is relieved with rest.","Weather — Worse with cold weather. Patients typically feel better in drier climates.","Stiffness — OA typically starts with stiffness but feels better once you get moving.","Crepitus — May have crepitus of the joint during movements.","Altered gait / joint use — An altered joint or altered gait due to pain. If it hurts, the person shifts to minimize pain, which can cause further problems (compare the misalignment risk factor above).","Swelling — Joint swelling from the proteoglycan fluid.","Range of motion — Limited range of motion; you typically lose range of motion.","Deformity and tenderness — May get joint deformities and tenderness, depending on the bone spurs, the bone on bone, and the swelling.","Nodes of the Hands","If OA occurs in the hands, the patient may develop Heberden's nodes or Bouchard's nodes. The lecture distinguishes them by location on the finger.","Heberden's nodes","The distal ones.","Bouchard's nodes","The proximal ones, closer to the hand."]},{"s":"Management of OA▾","a":"management-of-oa","k":["Management of OA▾","Goals","Prevention is the goal: decrease the risk factors and try to help before the problem becomes too severe.","Once OA is here, the aims are to manage pain, maintain mobility, and minimize disability.","Reduce and manage pain and reduce swelling.","Reach out to physical therapy so patients learn exercises to keep the joints flexible.","No drug prevents progression, prevents damage, or reverses the course of arthritis. The lecture states plainly that there are no pharmacotherapies that do this, so the focus is on pain management and the disability aspect.","The Drug Ladder (mild to severe)","Mild to moderate — Acetaminophen (Tylenol) — Usually the starting point.","Mild to moderate — Over-the-counter NSAIDs — Next step after Tylenol. Patients may also be prescribed stronger NSAIDs. Mild to moderate pain: start with acetaminophen or OTC NSAIDs.","Topical — Capsaicin — A topical agent made from hot chili peppers that kind of numbs the top of the area.","Moderate to severe — Prescription-strength NSAIDs — Once pain reaches moderate to severe, prescription-strength NSAIDs are used.","Moderate to severe — Colchicine — \"We may add colchicine.\" The lecture then says that if there is an inflammatory component, \"we'll address that later.\"","Further escalation — Tramadol — May be added.","Further escalation — Opioids — Patients may eventually get to opioids.","Further escalation — Steroid injections into the joint — May eventually be used.","Review your NSAIDs and know the risks of NSAIDs. The instructor says \"I am not going to read through this,\" but wants you to review the NSAID components and know the risk of NSAIDs. Go back to your pain lecture from Nursing 325.","Cross-reference: the lecture sends you back to your pain lecture for NSAIDs and their risks, plus tramadol and opioids. See Pain & Seizures","Beyond Medication","Typically, you may have to replace the joint.","You may get artificial joint fluid.","There are many interventions you will use, such as devices (canes, walkers) and lifestyle interventions.","Other treatment options will be discussed in med-surg. The instructor notes that 327 used to be med-surg, so this will be 334 or 405.","Cross-reference: rheumatoid arthritis, the next type of arthritis in this lecture series, is taught in Rheumatoid Arthritis"]},{"s":"Degenerative Disc Disease (DDD)▾","a":"degenerative-disc-disease-ddd","k":["Degenerative Disc Disease (DDD)▾","The instructor takes a moment to talk about degenerative disc disease. It is the same process just described for osteoarthritis, but specific to the spinal column. The goal of treatment is the same as it was for osteoarthritis.","Where, Who, and How Common","Locations — Occurs in the lumbar or cervical spine. Lumbar means L4, L5, S1. These two regions are the commons.","Typical cause — Typically involves lifting or twisting motions. \"Nurses are notorious for not protecting their back, so very common.\"","Doctor visits — Back pain and degenerative disc disease is the second most common reason people visit their doctor.","Work disability — Back pain is the most common cause of work-related disabilities in people under age 45.","Sex — Affects women and men equally.","Age change — Intervertebral disc compression occurs with age.","Mechanism: Nerve Impingement","The discs lose that \"nice bouncy disc area,\" and then the vertebrae are just on one another.","The nerves then become compressed. This impingement on the nerves is what causes pain, decreased movement, and decreased sensation.","Depending on where the impingement occurs, you can have pain in one leg, one side of the buttocks, or down one leg to certain toes. Nerve pain is very common with degenerative disc disease.","Once you lose the nice, fluffy, healthy disc, it crushes the nerves.","Lumbar vs. Cervical Presentation","Lumbar spine","Lower back pain that often radiates down the back of the leg or the buttocks.","This is often called sciatica.","Worse when sitting, bending, lifting, or twisting.","Better (patients typically say) when walking, changing positions, or lying down.","May get numbness, tingling, or weakness down the leg.","Can cause foot drop.","Cervical spine","Pain can radiate down the shoulders and down the arms.","Numbness or tingling in the arm or hand.","Weakness in the arm or hand.","Herniated and Bulging Discs","DDD typically leads to things like a herniated or ruptured disc, and you can see how it would impinge on the nerves.","With bulging discs, sometimes elongation of the spine can let the disc return into the appropriate space, but sometimes it does not.","There is disc surgery specific to degenerative disc disease that will be covered in med-surg.","OA vs. DDD at a Glance","Where — Back (cervical or lumbar-sacral), hip, knee, hands, big toe — Lumbar (L4, L5, S1) or cervical spine","What is lost — Articular cartilage and synovial fluid; bone on bone — The bouncy disc between vertebrae; vertebrae end up on one another","What develops — Exposed bone, bone spurs, swelling, bone on bone — Impingement and compression of nerves","Treatment goal — Prevention; manage pain, maintain mobility, minimize disability — Same goals as OA; disc surgery covered later in med-surg"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-rheumatoid-arthritis.html","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","secs":[{"s":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 11","What RA Is","Etiology & Risk Factors","Pathophysiology","Manifestations","Systemic Involvement","Management Overview","Glucocorticoids (Prednisone)","DMARD 1: Methotrexate","DMARD 2: Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil)","Biologic Response Modifiers (Biologics)","RA vs OA Side by Side","Self-Test Flashcards","Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is a different kind of arthritis from osteoarthritis (OA), and the lecture stresses that you must understand the distinct differences between the two conditions: they differ pathophysiologically, pharmacologically,…","Cross-reference: RA is taught here as the contrast to OA; the OA side of every comparison is covered in Osteoarthritis & Disc Disease"]},{"s":"What RA Is▾","a":"what-ra-is","k":["What RA Is▾","The lecture abbreviates rheumatoid arthritis as RA, the same way osteoarthritis was abbreviated OA.","RA is a systemic disease versus a localized condition. This is the biggest difference from OA.","It is an autoimmune disease: the body begins to attack itself. In RA, the body attacks the synovial tissue in the joints.","It is classified as a type 3 hypersensitivity. The instructor says that if that term is not familiar, you should go back to the hypersensitivity lecture.","The attack on the synovium develops inflammatory processes and destroys the area of the joint. RA is an inflammatory disease of the synovium.","Cross-reference: type 3 hypersensitivity is reviewed in GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity Review"]},{"s":"Etiology & Risk Factors▾","a":"etiology-risk-factors","k":["Etiology & Risk Factors▾","Etiology","The cause is not well understood. Despite intensive research, the exact cause remains unknown.","Like other autoimmune conditions, it is thought to involve both an environmental factor and a genetic factor. The instructor's way of saying it: there is a genetic link and a triggering event.","You can be genetically predisposed to RA, but just because you have that predisposition does not mean you will develop it.","Environmental factors that play a role include an infection, an injury, or hormonal/reproductive factors.","Risk factors","Genetic link — A significant genetic link, paired with a triggering event.","Age — Most diagnoses occur between 40 and 60. OA is the age-related one that increases with age; RA is the more middle-age disease.","Sex — Women have a 2.5 times higher risk of developing RA than men.","Tobacco use — Linked to RA development. It is thought it may possibly be a trigger, or involve some of the infectious agents from the trigger.","Family history — Pretty significant in RA.","Why the 40 to 60 age range matters: it makes RA all the more destructive, because that is a very active time in people's lives. Patients are mid to late career, still working, and may still have children growing up, so the disease definitel…"]},{"s":"Pathophysiology▾","a":"pathophysiology","k":["Pathophysiology▾","The lecture's sequence, from the immune attack to joint destruction:","Immune complexes (autoimmune complexes) get deposited in the synovial tissue, and immune cells attack that synovial tissue. The instructor says we do not know why they target the synovial tissue.","The activated immune cells are typically B cells (lymphocytes) and macrophages. They secrete cytokines that further attract other white blood cells.","These immune cells produce RF, rheumatoid factor: a specific or special antibody against our own IgG antibodies.","During phagocytosis by these white blood cells, large quantities of lysosomal enzymes are released. These enzymes further damage the tissue and affect the surrounding tissue.","The process is chronic, persistent, and destructive to the joints. Because of the intense inflammatory response, cartilage is destroyed.","As tissue destruction progresses, scar tissue develops. This scar tissue causes a lot of the problem, and in RA joints it is specifically called pannus.","Pannus","Scar tissue in the RA joint.","Very vascularized, because all scar tissue is: it is trying to get nutrients in to heal.","Made up of lots of inflammatory cells: T cells, macrophages, and other inflammatory cells.","Because pannus contains active immune and inflammatory cells, it is very destructive to the surrounding tissues.","End result: you lose the nice synovial tissue and synovial joint fluid, and basically get bone on bone with inflammation.","Diagnosis","Rheumatoid factor (RF) — Can be measured in the serum. This is how diagnosis is confirmed, in addition to symptoms and physical assessment.","CRP or sed rate — Inflammatory lab levels that can also be drawn.","Symptoms and physical assessment — Used together with the RF to confirm the diagnosis."]},{"s":"Manifestations▾","a":"manifestations","k":["Manifestations▾","Internally the joint is being destroyed, but externally RA is an early disease with not a ton of obvious signs. The lecture separates the early vague picture from the later joint picture.","Early disease","Because RA is systemic, there are not a ton of obvious symptoms early on. Maybe some joint pain and discomfort.","Vague systemic symptoms: fatigue, anorexia, weight loss, generalized stiffness.","Later joint manifestations","Eventually joint manifestations appear, and they are symmetrical.","Heat, swelling, tenderness, stiffness.","Stiffness usually starts in the morning and lasts longer than an hour.","Swelling feels spongy, soft, and warm.","Symmetry: a key distinction from OA","In RA the joints are affected symmetrically.","OA is wear and tear on a unilateral joint.","Swelling: RA versus OA","RA swelling is spongy, soft, and warm.","OA swelling is hard. The instructor's example: think of a swollen ankle from spraining it; that is more like OA swelling.","RA swelling is softer and spongier than that.","Subluxation","Joint subluxations may be seen, along with muscle atrophy and tendon destruction.","In subluxation, the bone is displaced from its normal joint position and partially loses contact with the articulating surface.","This is different from a dislocation, where the articulating surface completely loses contact. Subluxation is \"just not aligned perfectly.\"","It decreases range of motion and is very painful.","You cannot fix it, because the tissue is eroded away.","Very common in the hands of RA patients, but can also be seen in the knees.","Deformity: no Heberden's or Bouchard's nodes","RA does not produce the Heberden's or Bouchard's nodes seen in OA.","Instead you get a swan deformity. These are not bony growths but actual curving of the joint because of the inflammation.","This is in the more advanced disease stages, along with the subluxations."]},{"s":"Systemic Involvement▾","a":"systemic-involvement","k":["Systemic Involvement▾","The instructor says multiple times that RA means more systemic involvement: other body systems besides just the joints, even though RA particularly affects the synovial joint areas. When doing a physical assessment of someone with RA, it is…","Fatigue and malaise — An exceeding level of tiredness.","Heart — Can develop pericarditis or myocarditis. The instructor says to assess the heart in particular.","Lungs — Can develop nodules on the lungs. They are typically monitored, maybe through x-rays or CT scans occasionally, to ensure they are not internal.","Sjogren's syndrome — Destruction of moisture-producing glands: the lacrimal glands (eyes) and the salivary glands. Result: dry mouth, dry itchy eyes (dry tongue also shown in the lecture picture).","Rheumatoid nodules — Immune-mediated granulomas. Typically develop around the inflamed joints. Subcutaneous, firm, and sometimes painful. About 25% of people with RA develop them.","What to take away (instructor's emphasis)","You do not need to memorize all of the systemic manifestations. Remember that it is very important to understand the systemic involvement as a whole.","But you should be able to pick up on Sjogren's syndrome and rheumatoid nodules."]},{"s":"Management Overview▾","a":"management-overview","k":["Management Overview▾","Like OA, the goals include relieving pain and swelling.","The difference: with OA, we cannot really do anything to stop or slow disease progression other than encourage weight-bearing activities and proper joint movement and motion. With RA, there are things we can do to actually slow the disease …","Typically there is long-term drug therapy, and it requires a lot of patient adherence.","NSAIDs — Immediate symptom relief. Review your NSAID concerns for patients taking them.","Glucocorticoids (prednisone, hydrocortisone) — Short-term management of severe or acute flare-ups. Review the concerns for patients on steroids in the short and long term.","DMARDs (disease-modifying anti-rheumatic drugs) — The group the instructor adds to the drug matrix. Two older ones are covered: methotrexate and hydroxychloroquine. There are much newer DMARDs in use, often ones you see on TV and in commerc…","Biologic response modifiers (biologics) — Newer drugs. Be aware of them; you do not need them on your drug matrix.","Cross-reference: NSAID concerns to review are covered in Pain & Seizures Review"]},{"s":"Glucocorticoids (Prednisone)▾","a":"glucocorticoids-prednisone","k":["Glucocorticoids (Prednisone)▾","Prednisone is usually the steroid of choice in RA. Prednisone or hydrocortisone are the glucocorticoids named for short-term management of severe or acute flare-ups.","It causes a rapid suppression of inflammation, helping with the inflammatory processes in the joints.","It is used only when symptoms cannot be controlled, meaning the inflammation is not controlled with an NSAID.","There is lots of risk with long-term steroids, so the team tries very hard not to use them.","If a longer term is needed, it is usually in very small doses: less than 10 mg a day, usually as small as 2.5 mg.","Usually used in conjunction with DMARDs, in addition to NSAIDs.","Cross-reference: short- and long-term steroid concerns are covered in Stress & Steroids Review"]},{"s":"DMARD 1: Methotrexate▾","a":"dmard-1-methotrexate","k":["DMARD 1: Methotrexate▾","Methotrexate is one of the first DMARDs. It is still used today as a first-line agent for RA in most patients. It does have a lot of side effects, but it does help. The instructor tells you to put it on your drug matrix, along with hydroxyc…","Methotrexate is given ONCE PER WEEK, never daily. This is a huge safety issue: death can occur if it is administered daily instead of weekly. It is definitely something to teach the patient about.","Mechanism — Interferes with the metabolism of folate. It is an immunosuppressive. It is a chemo drug that attacks the immune system and tells it to stop attacking ourselves.","Routes — Can be given PO. Some people have given it sub-Q or IV.","Other use — Used in smaller doses, it is actually used for cancer therapy.","Frequency — Once per week.","Folate — Because it interferes with folic acid metabolism, patients always need to take a folic acid supplement (folate replacement).","Main side effects — GI: nausea, anorexia, vomiting.","Liver — Metabolized by the liver and pretty toxic to it. Monitor liver enzymes: AST, ALT, alk phos.","Bone marrow — Causes bone marrow suppression, basically because it is a chemo drug. Monitor white blood cells and red blood cells (CBC) to make sure the marrow is not being suppressed.","Black box warnings — There are 11 black box warnings, some of the FDA's strongest warnings against serious life-threatening effects.","Alcohol — Because it is toxic to the liver, no drinking or alcohol.","Pregnancy — Teratogenic. Patients need two forms of birth control. The instructor compares it to some of the other drugs given for acne.","Infection — Very high risk of infection. Teach patients that if they develop a sign of infection, they need to go to the doctor ASAP.","Contraindications — Cannot be given to people with pre-existing liver or kidney conditions.","Aplastic anemia / NSAIDs — As stated in the lecture: there is an aplastic anemia risk using NSAIDs. These patients commonly use NSAIDs, so definitely monitor the white blood cell or CBC count.","Teaching point on life expectancy: people who take methotrexate are shown to have a shortened life expectancy due to cardiovascular disease, cancer, and other conditions that can develop from methotrexate. It is one of those drugs where you…","Putting the risks and benefits together","The lecture admits this all sounds very bad and that it is an intense drug to take.","We are getting better at it: changing the dosing, monitoring closer, and understanding the side effects.","It does help these patients: it slows disease progression, they are able to function in daily life, and they typically can live pretty normally to an extent. They have a lot of things to deal with, but it helps."]},{"s":"DMARD 2: Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil)▾","a":"dmard-2-hydroxychloroquine-plaquenil","k":["DMARD 2: Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil)▾","Not used as frequently for RA. It is actually an antimalarial drug (the instructor notes you probably heard about it during the COVID pandemic).","It can sometimes be used in anti-rheumatic diseases because of its anti-inflammatory effects: it dampens macrophage, T-helper cell, and inflammatory cytokine activity, turning down the inflammatory process.","It can slow the progression of RA, especially in combination with other DMARDs.","Much better tolerated than methotrexate, with fewer side effects.","About a 1% chance of retinopathy, so it is pretty rare. It can lead to blindness.","Teaching: tell patients that if they develop blurry vision or any vision problems, they should contact their health care provider."]},{"s":"Biologic Response Modifiers (Biologics)▾","a":"biologic-response-modifiers-biologics","k":["Biologic Response Modifiers (Biologics)▾","Newer drugs used for really all autoimmune conditions (the lecture notes there are tons of autoimmune conditions, and RA is one of the big ones).","Sometimes just called biologics. They target the part of the immune system that triggers inflammation and causes joint tissue damage. Basically they target the body's own immune system and tell it to stop acting crazy.","Because they are newer, we are less familiar with how they affect patients longer term.","Known risks: increased risk of severe skin or lung infections, skin cancers, and some people can have serious allergic reactions.","There is a ton of them. You do not need to put any on your drug matrix, and you do not even need to know their names, but you should probably recognize many of them from commercials.","They are very expensive, but they work pretty well to keep these autoimmune conditions subdued.","Be aware of them: if you work in areas that care for these patients, you will begin to see them."]},{"s":"RA vs OA Side by Side▾","a":"ra-vs-oa-side-by-side","k":["RA vs OA Side by Side▾","Built only from statements the instructor made in this lecture.","Scope — Localized condition — Systemic disease","Underlying process — Wear and tear — Autoimmune; type 3 hypersensitivity; body attacks the synovial tissue","Age pattern — Increases with age — Most diagnoses between 40 and 60 (middle age)","Joint distribution — Wear and tear on a unilateral joint — Symmetrical","Swelling — Hard (like a sprained, swollen ankle) — Spongy, soft, and warm","Joint changes — Heberden's and Bouchard's nodes (bony growths) — No Heberden's or Bouchard's nodes; swan deformity (curving of the joint from inflammation, not bony growths); subluxations","Other body systems — Not described as systemic — Fatigue and malaise, pericarditis/myocarditis, lung nodules, Sjogren's syndrome, rheumatoid nodules","Can progression be slowed? — Cannot really do anything to stop or slow it other than encourage weight-bearing activities and proper joint movement and motion — Yes: drugs can actually slow the disease process (DMARDs, biologics)","Drug therapy — Treated differently (see the OA page) — Long-term drug therapy with NSAIDs, glucocorticoids, DMARDs, and biologics; requires a lot of patient adherence"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-gout.html","t":"Gout","secs":[{"s":"Gout","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 12","What Gout Is","Where Uric Acid Comes From: Purine","Hyperuricemia: Why It Happens","Risk Factors: Who Gets Gout","Phases of Gout","What the Patient Looks Like: Manifestations","Complications","Drug Therapy for Gout","Self-Test Flashcards","Gout is a disruption in uric acid metabolism in which urate salts (crystals) are deposited in tissues, generally in and around joints. When those crystals inflame a joint, the result is gouty arthritis, an acute and very painful condition. …","Cross-reference: the instructor notes gout has already come up in the kidney stones discussion and says \"we've already talked about this in the renal disorders lecture\" — see Renal Disorders"]},{"s":"What Gout Is▾","a":"what-gout-is","k":["What Gout Is▾","The instructor opens by saying you have heard the word gout many times by now in your nursing career, including when kidney stones and some other conditions were discussed. The lecture separates two terms that are often used together.","Gout","The metabolic problem itself.","A disruption in uric acid metabolism.","Urate salts are deposited in certain tissues, generally in and around joints.","Gouty arthritis","How the gout actually affects the joint, and the inflammation of that joint.","An acute and very painful condition.","The condition is typically referred to as gouty arthritis.","Crystal deposits of uric acid are present in the joint (the lecture slide shows them).","\"The disease of kings\"","Gout is still talked about as \"the disease of kings.\"","This is because it traditionally came from eating foods associated with wealthy people: smoked meats, cheeses, red wine, and beer.","Many old, rich kings had it back in the day, but now many different people get it."]},{"s":"Where Uric Acid Comes From: Purine▾","a":"where-uric-acid-comes-from-purine","k":["Where Uric Acid Comes From: Purine▾","The crystals form because of the breakdown of purine.","The purine-to-uric-acid pathway","Purine is a nitrogen-containing compound; it is the parent compound of nitrogenous waste.","Purine breaks down into uric acid.","Uric acid is excreted in the urine by the kidneys.","Normally, uric acid dissolves in the blood and is excreted by the kidneys with no problem.","In people who develop gout, there is an inability to process it normally and they develop hyperuricemia.","Alcohol, including beer and red wine","Organ meat: liver, kidney, brain","Red meat","Seafood, such as scallops","The instructor's point: there are many things that have a lot of purine in them. The \"disease of kings\" foods (smoked meats, cheeses, red wine, beer) are the traditional examples tied to gout."]},{"s":"Hyperuricemia: Why It Happens▾","a":"hyperuricemia-why-it-happens","k":["Hyperuricemia: Why It Happens▾","Hyperuricemia (high uric acid in the blood) is usually related to two problems working together.","Overproduction of uric acid","Uric acid is overproduced.","This is the situation allopurinol is aimed at (see the drug section).","Underexcretion of uric acid","Not enough uric acid is excreted."]},{"s":"Risk Factors: Who Gets Gout▾","a":"risk-factors-who-gets-gout","k":["Risk Factors: Who Gets Gout▾","Sex — Mostly men.","Obesity — Listed as a risk factor. Obesity now occurs across all different sociodemographics.","Preexisting diseases — Hypertension, diabetes, renal disease, and sickle cell anemia.","Alcohol — People who commonly consume alcohol, especially people with alcohol use disorders.","High-purine diet — People who eat diets high in purine.","Diuretics — The use of diuretics is often associated with gout.","Race — Black or African American people have a higher risk (the instructor corrects herself to say race rather than ethnicity).","The changing stereotype","It used to be that only the rich could eat foods high in purine.","So the stereotypical image of a gout sufferer was a wealthy, overweight male.","Now obesity is seen in all different sociodemographics, so gout is no longer a disease of the wealthy but a disease of anyone who has this kind of diet."]},{"s":"Phases of Gout▾","a":"phases-of-gout","k":["Phases of Gout▾","The lecture numbers three phases and then describes progression to chronic gouty arthritis (shown here as phase 4). Hyperuricemia can persist even when the patient has no symptoms.","1. Asymptomatic — The patient has no symptoms but has elevated serum uric acid levels. If the tissues are tested, crystals can be seen in the tissues. The crystals begin to accumulate, the tissue becomes damaged, and this causes acute infla…","2. Acute flare (attack) — The acute inflammation becomes an acute flare or attack. There is a very high level of hyperuricemia.","3. Clinically inactive (between attacks) — The patient continues to have hyperuricemia, but the inflammation is not there. They may have another flare-up in months or years.","4. Chronic gouty arthritis — Typically, as patients age and the disease progresses, attacks recur closer and closer together until they develop chronic gouty arthritis, with joint pain and other symptoms present most of the time."]},{"s":"What the Patient Looks Like: Manifestations▾","a":"what-the-patient-looks-like-manifestations","k":["What the Patient Looks Like: Manifestations▾","Clinical picture of gout inflammation","Pain is the hallmark sign. It can be excruciating.","Burning pain, redness, swelling, and warmth of the joint.","Fever.","Symptoms are typically present for days to weeks.","It can be difficult to walk, and attacks can happen again.","Most common joint: the big toe","The joint most commonly attacked is the big toe joint, the large metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joint.","About 50% of people with gout get it in that joint specifically.","It can occur in other joints, but the feet and the big toe are very common.","How severe the pain can be","The pain can get so intense that patients cannot even put on socks, and their feet cannot touch the bedsheets.","The instructor recalls seeing this in a real patient: staff could not touch his feet or lay even one thin hospital sheet over his toe. She says she once rolled her eyes learning about this in nursing school, until she saw it herself.","Nursing approach and patient teaching","We treat the symptoms.","We teach patients to stop drinking and stop eating this kind of diet; the hope is they will then be able to control it."]},{"s":"Complications▾","a":"complications","k":["Complications▾","Tophi","Large, hard nodules made of uric acid crystal deposits in the soft tissue.","They may form below the skin, around the joints.","They can cause a local inflammatory response.","If drained, they may drain chalky material.","The lecture slides show pictures of tophi.","Kidney stones","Patients with gout are at high risk for kidney stones.","The instructor notes gout was already mentioned in the renal disorders lecture.","Cross-reference: kidney stones and the earlier gout mention are taught in Renal Disorders"]},{"s":"Drug Therapy for Gout▾","a":"drug-therapy-for-gout","k":["Drug Therapy for Gout▾","First-line for an acute attack: NSAIDs","NSAIDs are typically the drug of choice for acute attacks.","They give anti-inflammatory effects and help reduce symptoms.","Steroids: only in limited situations","Similar to how steroids are used in other conditions, steroids are only given to patients who cannot take NSAIDs or whose gout is not responding to them.","Cross-reference: the same NSAID and steroid theme (steroids reserved for when NSAIDs are not enough) is discussed for RA in Rheumatoid Arthritis","Goals of gout-specific drugs","Decrease the symptoms of an acute attack.","Prevent a recurrence.","Besides NSAIDs, there are three drugs: allopurinol, colchicine, and probenecid.","Drug matrix: the instructor says all three of these drugs (allopurinol, colchicine, probenecid) need to be on your drug matrix.","Three-drug comparison","Role — Prophylactic drug only; not for acute attacks. Taken over a long time. For patients whose gout is related to excess uric acid production, as seen in hyperuricemia. — The oldest available therapy for gout, but second-line treatment. U…","Mechanism — Xanthine oxidase enzyme inhibitor. Xanthine oxidase is required to convert purine into uric acid, so the drug prevents uric acid production. — Inhibitor of cell mitosis. Reduces the inflammatory response to urate crystal deposit…","Adverse effects — Pretty well tolerated. Most common: rash. Chance of agranulocytosis and aplastic anemia (worry about infection). Known to cause Stevens-Johnson syndrome, which can turn into TEN (toxic epidermal necrolysis). — Short-term l…","Interactions — Can increase the effects of hypoglycemic agents (anti-diabetes medicines) and of warfarin. — Not discussed. — Lots of drug interactions; monitor for these.","Onset — Starts to take effect after 2 to 6 weeks; the patient sees the flare start dying down, then continues taking it to prevent future flares. — Not discussed. — Not discussed.","Monitoring — Serum uric acid levels (effectiveness). White blood cells and CBC (agranulocytosis and aplastic anemia risk). Blood sugar if on hypoglycemics. INR if on warfarin. — Watch for GI symptoms and persistent vomiting. — Monitor for d…","Route — Not discussed. — PO only. — Take with food and drink and plenty of fluids.","Report / stop — Not discussed beyond the monitoring above. — Stop immediately if the patient develops GI toxicity symptoms such as persistent vomiting. — Report signs of kidney problems (hematuria, change in the amount of urine, weight gain…","Allopurinol in detail","Prophylaxis only","Allopurinol is a prophylactic drug only; it will not help with acute attacks. Patients take it over a long time.","It is a xanthine oxidase inhibitor. The enzyme is needed to convert purine into uric acid, so blocking it prevents uric acid production.","The instructor says you do not necessarily need to remember the detailed mechanism, but you need to know it is a xanthine oxidase enzyme inhibitor.","It is for patients whose gout is related to excess uric acid production, as seen in hyperuricemia; again, it is a prevention medication.","Onset is 2 to 6 weeks, after which patients see their flare start dying down. They then continue taking it to prevent flares in the future.","Overall it is very well tolerated, but it is not a drug with no issues: it has some interactions and some rare effects of concern.","Allopurinol and skin reactions: the most common side effect is rash, but allopurinol is known to cause Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS), which can turn into toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN). The instructor calls this a fatal skin condition th…","Allopurinol and blood problems: there is a chance of agranulocytosis and aplastic anemia, so we are very worried about infection. Monitor white blood cells and the CBC.","Allopurinol interactions and what to teach","Hypoglycemic agents (anti-diabetes medicines): allopurinol can increase their effects. Examples given: metformin, SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 agonists. It may cause hypoglycemia, so tell these patients to monitor their sugar closely.","Warfarin: allopurinol can increase its effect. Monitor the INR closely to make sure the blood is not getting too thin.","Effectiveness: monitored with serum uric acid levels.","Colchicine in detail","Oldest therapy, second-line","The oldest available therapy for gout, but second-line treatment.","It reduces the inflammatory response to the urate crystal deposits in the joints.","Mechanism: an inhibitor of cell mitosis; it can cause short-term leukopenia and bone marrow suppression.","The instructor says its action is not greatly understood, but it is thought to inhibit the metabolism, mobility, and chemotaxis of polymorphonuclear leukocytes. Basically it decreases inflammation, which is wanted in gouty arthritis, but it…","Usually used for flares; it can be used more short-term for flare-ups. It can also be used for prophylaxis if the patient is not tolerating allopurinol.","It can be given PO only.","Colchicine toxicity: stop immediately. The drug can cause GI bleeding and disrupts cell mitosis in the GI system. If the patient has stomach-flu-type symptoms, this is an early sign of drug toxicity and the colchicine must be stopped immedi…","At this point the instructor also lists any person with severe renal, gastrointestinal, hepatic, or cardiac disorders, or bleeding disorders (the sentence is not finished in the lecture).","Probenecid in detail","Uricosuric agent","A uricosuric agent that effectively reduces serum uric acid levels.","It inhibits the reabsorption of uric acid, so it helps with hyperuricemia.","Can be used alone or in combination with allopurinol.","There are lots of drug interactions with this medication, so monitor for them if you are giving or prescribing it.","Patient teaching for probenecid","Take with food and drink and plenty of fluids.","Report signs of kidney problems: hematuria, change in the amount of urine, weight gain.","Report any signs of liver issues.","Quick distinction of the drugs by job","NSAIDs — Drug of choice for acute attacks; anti-inflammatory effect reduces symptoms.","Steroids — Only if NSAIDs cannot be taken or gout is not responding.","Allopurinol — Prevention only; blocks uric acid production (xanthine oxidase inhibitor).","Colchicine — Oldest, second-line; reduces inflammation to crystals; used for flares; stop immediately if GI toxicity.","Probenecid — Uricosuric; inhibits reabsorption of uric acid; alone or with allopurinol."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-lupus.html","t":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Lupus)","secs":[{"s":"Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Lupus)","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 13","What Is Lupus?","How Lupus Damages the Body","Who Gets Lupus? Risk Factors","What Do Lupus Patients Look Like? Manifestations","Prognosis and Clinical Course","Flares: Warning Signs and Triggers","Pharmacotherapy","RA vs. Lupus","Wrap-Up of the Musculoskeletal Block","Self-Test Flashcards","Lupus is the last topic of the very long musculoskeletal slide deck. It is an abnormal immune reaction of the body against its own cells: a complex autoimmune disease in which autoantibodies (mainly ANA) attack the body's own DNA, forming i…","Cross-reference: the RA-versus-lupus comparison below pairs with the RA lecture, and hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil) was first covered with RA. See Rheumatoid Arthritis"]},{"s":"What Is Lupus?▾","a":"what-is-lupus","k":["What Is Lupus?▾","Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)","The main type of lupus, and the one the lecture covers for the rest of the deck.","It is what people in general mean when they say \"lupus\" in the community.","\"SLE\" and \"lupus\" are used interchangeably; they all refer to the same disease process.","Systemic: it can involve many body systems.","Skin-only lupus","There is a type of lupus that affects only the skin.","The lecture only names it; it is not the version being taught.","The focus is the systemic version (SLE).","Definition","Lupus is an abnormal immune reaction of the body against its own cells, so it is an autoimmune disease.","The lecture calls it a particularly complex autoimmune disease.","It involves the production of autoantibodies.","Major autoantibody — ANA = anti-nuclear antibody. This is the major autoantibody produced in lupus.","Testing — An ANA level can be drawn from plasma.","Target of the antibodies — The body's own DNA. The antibodies are activated against the body's own DNA to form immune complexes.","Immune complexes — They circulate around the body and can target any major organ system: heart, skin, kidneys, brain.","Result — The immune complexes trigger an inflammatory response that destroys whatever organ tissue they landed in."]},{"s":"How Lupus Damages the Body▾","a":"how-lupus-damages-the-body","k":["How Lupus Damages the Body▾","The chain of events, in the instructor's own logic: the disease is \"truly activated by the body's own DNA,\" and because DNA is everywhere in the body, the resulting immune complexes can land almost anywhere.","Step by step","1. The immune system produces autoantibodies; the major one is ANA (anti-nuclear antibody).","2. These antibodies are activated against the body's own DNA and form immune complexes.","3. The immune complexes circulate around the body.","4. They land in an organ (any major organ system can be targeted: heart, skin, kidneys, brain).","5. They trigger an inflammatory response in that organ.","6. That inflammatory response destroys the tissue where the complexes deposited.","Where RA complexes go","In RA, the immune complexes were deposited only in the synovial fluid.","Where lupus complexes go","In lupus they can deposit anywhere, because there is DNA everywhere and the complexes can just land somewhere.","The most common area is the kidney: the kidneys are very commonly affected when complexes deposit in the glomeruli.","The instructor stresses that the glomeruli are so important and can be pretty fragile.","Cross-reference: immune complexes and the hypersensitivity types are reviewed in GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity Review"]},{"s":"Who Gets Lupus? Risk Factors▾","a":"who-gets-lupus-risk-factors","k":["Who Gets Lupus? Risk Factors▾","Genetics — There is definitely an inherited genetic component.","Sex — Females are much more likely to get lupus than males.","Age — Typically affects people aged 20 to 40, so it is a disease process of younger people.","Race — Most commonly affects Black and African American people.","Sun exposure — An environmental trigger thought to trigger a lupus flare-up; it can also be a predisposing factor.","Hormones — Hormones have a significant influence on the immune system and seem to be an important factor in developing the disease (see the two examples below).","Estrogen-containing oral contraceptives — Women who take oral birth control that contains estrogen have a 50% higher risk of experiencing the disease.","Early menarche — Women who started menstruating before age 10 have double the risk.","Smoking — Smoking releases multiple toxins; the instructor says it is essentially a risk factor for everything.","Antibiotic allergies — It is thought there is some relation to having a lot of allergies to certain antibiotics.","Quick picture of the typical patient (from the lecture's list): a younger (20 to 40) female, Black or African American, with a family (genetic) tendency; sun, estrogen-containing birth control, early menarche (before 10), smoking, and many …"]},{"s":"What Do Lupus Patients Look Like? Manifestations▾","a":"what-do-lupus-patients-look-like-manifestations","k":["What Do Lupus Patients Look Like? Manifestations▾","Most common and hallmark features","Fatigue — The most common symptom. Extreme fatigue that can be very debilitating.","Photosensitivity — Sensitivity to light and sunshine.","Butterfly rash — Described as almost one of the hallmark signs. It is bilateral: a red, irritating rash that spreads across the face like butterfly wings (the instructor refers to an image on the slide).","Edema — Usually in the legs or around the eyes.","Fever — Listed with the edema among the common features.","Hair loss — Unusual hair loss.","Raynaud's-type changes","What happens and how it is treated","In the distal capillary regions, the small distal capillaries constrict and reduce blood flow.","It can be very painful.","It is usually triggered by cold.","Treatment: calcium channel blockers.","Organ-by-organ list: lupus can affect every organ system","The instructor warns that the list of manifestations is huge precisely because lupus can affect every single organ system.","Brain — Headache, dizziness, seizures, stroke.","Lungs — Pleuritis, pleural effusion.","Heart — Myocarditis, endocarditis.","Kidneys — About half of patients with lupus develop kidney complications. These can lead to permanent kidney damage and the need for dialysis or kidney transplant.","Blood vessels — Vasculitis.","Blood — Anemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia.","Joints — Arthritis.","Kidney involvement is the big one. The kidneys are the most common site of immune-complex deposition (the glomeruli), about half of lupus patients develop kidney complications, and the damage can be permanent, requiring dialysis or a kidney…"]},{"s":"Prognosis and Clinical Course▾","a":"prognosis-and-clinical-course","k":["Prognosis and Clinical Course▾","Poor prognosis","People with severe multi-system organ involvement (more than one organ involved) tend to have a very poor prognosis.","Good prognosis","The majority of people have mild disease.","They typically have a good prognosis and good survival outcomes with close monitoring and treatment.","The systems are usually not all affected at the same time; involvement can happen at different times.","Lupus has flare-ups, similar to how gout had flares.","The clinical course is exacerbations and remissions."]},{"s":"Flares: Warning Signs and Triggers▾","a":"flares-warning-signs-and-triggers","k":["Flares: Warning Signs and Triggers▾","A flare is an acute exacerbation of the symptoms. The instructor's key point: try to recognize the warning signs and avoid the triggers.","Warning signs of a flare","More severe fatigue","Pain","Headache","The person just becomes more symptomatic in general, with systemic symptoms","Triggers to avoid","Sunlight exposure","Having an infection","Abruptly stopping a medicine","Stress"]},{"s":"Pharmacotherapy▾","a":"pharmacotherapy","k":["Pharmacotherapy▾","The goal is to control symptoms, and the choice of drug depends on which systems are involved.","NSAIDs — For headache, musculoskeletal symptoms, pleuritis, and pericarditis: anti-inflammatories like NSAIDs.","Steroids, high dose — These patients often do need steroids. High dose if there is severe kidney disease or a lot of CNS involvement.","Steroids, low dose — Low dose if the problem is something like arthritis.","Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil) — Discussed earlier with RA. It helps with a lot of the skin and musculoskeletal problems and can prevent kidney and CNS organ damage. The instructor says you will see a lot of people with lupus on hydroxychlo…","Biologics and immunosuppressives — Used if there is multi-organ involvement or if there is concern about progression.","Cross-reference: hydroxychloroquine and the other disease-modifying drugs used with RA are detailed in Rheumatoid Arthritis","Cross-reference: the NSAID drug matrix the instructor says to review is in Pain & Seizures Review","Cross-reference: the steroid drug matrix the instructor says to review is in Stress & Steroids Review"]},{"s":"RA vs. Lupus▾","a":"ra-vs-lupus","k":["RA vs. Lupus▾","The instructor points out a lot of similarities between RA and lupus, and a worksheet on Canvas helps you fill out the distinctions between the two.","Autoimmune? — Yes — Yes","Systemic? — Yes: multiple body systems can be involved — Yes: multiple body systems can be involved","Where immune complexes deposit — Only in the synovial fluid — Anywhere, because there is DNA everywhere (kidney glomeruli most common)","Main area involved — Really only the joints — Any kind of organ can be involved","Systemic fatigue and malaise — Yes (in addition to the joint disease) — Yes: fatigue is the most common symptom","Drug overlap — Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil) was discussed with RA and comes up again for lupus.","The difference to hold onto: in RA it is really only the joints (plus the systemic fatigue and malaise), while in lupus (SLE) any kind of organ can be involved."]},{"s":"Wrap-Up of the Musculoskeletal Block▾","a":"wrap-up-of-the-musculoskeletal-block","k":["Wrap-Up of the Musculoskeletal Block▾","The instructor says most of the musculoskeletal content is pretty straightforward, and the pharmacotherapy is not too hard.","Make sure you review the other drug matrices about NSAIDs and steroids and understand the differences between them.","The lecture ends with encouragement to keep pushing through to the end of the block."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week7-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 7","The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to All Twelve Topics","The Twelve Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new — it's a map of Week 7's twelve topics (plus the Comprehensive Study Guide, which pulls the whole week together on its own page), so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it…"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to All Twelve Topics▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order-applied-to-all-twelve-topics","k":["The Lectures' Own Order, Applied to All Twelve Topics▾","Twelve pages is easier to hold in your head when you see which ones belong together. Each row below is one step in the order the lectures were given.","Cancer in General — What It Is and How It Spreads — Cancer Fundamentals; Metastasis & Cancer Etiology — The lecture's first step. Cancer cells lack differentiation, checkpoints, apoptosis, and response to signals from other cells, so they k…","The Specific Cancers — Lung Cancer; Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer — The four cancers the lecture singles out as the ones it focuses on in Kentucky (the highest-prevalence cancer, non-melanoma skin cancer, was covered in NUR 325). The…","Treatment — Chemotherapy — Chemotherapy Principles & Toxicities; Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics — The general rules first (these drugs hit rapidly dividing cells, so hair follicles, the GI tract, and bone marrow take the damage a…","The Bone Itself — Osteoporosis; Fractures & Osteomyelitis — The first musculoskeletal topics. Osteoporosis is low bone density with structural deterioration (too much osteoclast activity, too little osteoblast activity) and ends in hip frac…","The Joint — Wear and Tear — Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease — Opens with a review of the synovial joint, because most joint disorders affect synovial joints. Osteoarthritis is degeneration from aging and stress, localized to the …","The Joint and the Whole Body — Autoimmune Disease — Rheumatoid Arthritis; Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Lupus) — Both are autoimmune and systemic, and both involve immune complexes. RA is taught as the contrast to OA (the body attacks the s…","The Metabolic One — Uric Acid — Gout — Taught between RA and lupus in the lecture order. Gout is the metabolic problem (a disruption in uric acid metabolism with urate crystals deposited in and around joints); gouty arthritis is the resulti…","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, follow the lecture order within each unit. Read Cancer Fundamentals before the chemotherapy pages (the cell cycle matters for them), the two chemotherapy pages in order, Osteoporosis before F…"]},{"s":"The Twelve Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-twelve-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Twelve Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Cancer Fundamentals — Cancer cells lack differentiation, checkpoints, apoptosis, contact inhibition, and response to signals, so they proliferate without control; know benign versus malignant (starred), the cell growth cycle (it explain…","2 — Metastasis & Cancer Etiology — Cancer spreads by seeding, implantation, and metastasis (lymphatic or vascular, with the liver as the first stop for blood spread); causes include risk factors, tumor suppressor genes (the brake) versus pr…","3 — Lung Cancer — The leading cause of cancer-related death, mostly caught late (stage 1 survival over 60%, stage 4 below 10%); smoking is the leading cause, radon plus smoking is a very dangerous combination, and the only types to recogniz…","4 — Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer — Each is a population-level pattern to know: breast cancer (most common, hormone-exposure and BRCA risks), cervical cancer (almost all HPV-positive and preventable with the HPV vaccine and Pap smear…","5 — Chemotherapy Principles & Toxicities — Chemotherapy works best on rapidly growing tumors and has a low therapeutic index, so the rapidly dividing normal tissues (hair, GI tract, bone marrow) take the damage; know myelosuppression and th…","6 — Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics — The instructor gives you the class, and the class tells you the toxicity: alkylating agents to hemorrhagic cystitis, antimetabolites to nephrotoxicity and category X, antitumor antibiotics to …","7 — Osteoporosis — Low bone density with structural deterioration, measured by DEXA T-scores, caused by increased osteoclast and decreased osteoblast activity; hip fracture is the biggest complication, and the goal of drug therapy is to red…","8 — Fractures & Osteomyelitis — A fracture is a break in the continuity of bone; know the causes, the types, PED (pain, edema, deformity), the phases of healing, and the complications, especially compartment syndrome, fat embolism, and oste…","9 — Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease — OA is localized wear and tear of the joint (cartilage loss, bone spurs, bone on bone) with deep aching pain relieved by rest; no drug stops or reverses it, so management is pain control, mobi…","10 — Rheumatoid Arthritis — A systemic, autoimmune disease in which the body attacks the synovial tissue (symmetrical joints, spongy swelling, pannus, systemic involvement); unlike OA, drugs can slow it, and methotrexate (once a week, never…","11 — Gout — A disruption in uric acid metabolism (overproduction or underexcretion) that leaves urate crystals in and around joints, classically the big toe; NSAIDs are first line for an acute attack, and allopurinol, colchicine, and proben…","12 — Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Lupus) — An autoimmune disease in which autoantibodies (mainly ANA) form immune complexes that can land in almost any organ, most commonly the kidney; know the hallmark signs (fatigue, photosensitivity, bu…"]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content — each idea is fully taught in one place (several in the earlier review pages). But they resurface as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why.","Rapidly dividing cells, and the cell cycle — Cancer Fundamentals (the cell growth cycle and why it matters for chemotherapy); Chemotherapy Principles & Toxicities (toxicities from rapidly dividing cells) — Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Anti…","Carcinogens versus promoters — Metastasis & Cancer Etiology — a carcinogen alters DNA; a promoter helps already-mutated cells proliferate; some things (tobacco, estrogen therapy) are both — Lung Cancer applies it directly: cigarette smoke i…","Smoking, tobacco, and alcohol as risk factors — Lung Cancer (smoking as the leading cause) and Metastasis & Cancer Etiology (tobacco and alcohol as known carcinogens or promoters) — Named again on Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer (alcoh…","Estrogen and hormone exposure — Metastasis & Cancer Etiology — hormones as promoters; more menstrual cycles means more estrogen exposure — Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer (the span between first period and menopause, hormone replacemen…","Obesity — Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer (a risk factor for breast cancer and colorectal cancer) — Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease (obesity strains the hips and knees and is part of why OA incidence is rising) and Gout (a r…","Methotrexate — Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics — the antimetabolite example (S phase; mimics folate; nephrotoxicity, hepatotoxicity, category X) — Rheumatoid Arthritis — the same drug as a first-line DMARD: once per week and never…","Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil) — Rheumatoid Arthritis — the second DMARD for the drug matrix — Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Lupus) — \"discussed earlier with RA\"; helps the skin and musculoskeletal problems and can prevent kidney and CNS or…","NSAIDs — Pain & Seizures Review — the lectures send you back to the NSAID drug matrix and the risks of NSAIDs — Osteoarthritis (acetaminophen or OTC NSAIDs first, prescription-strength NSAIDs for moderate to severe pain), Rheumatoid Arthrit…","Steroids and prednisone cautions — Stress & Steroids Review — the lectures send you back to the steroid drug matrix — Reserved for when other drugs are not enough on Gout (cannot take NSAIDs or not responding) and Rheumatoid Arthritis (pred…","Infection risk and bone marrow suppression — Chemotherapy Principles & Toxicities — myelosuppression, neutropenia, and the nadir; see also Blood Cells & CBC — Biologics can cause serious infections through immunosuppression on Antineoplasti…","Pregnancy and birth control — Chemotherapy Principles & Toxicities — most chemotherapy drugs are class D or X; fertility concerns — Methotrexate is category X on Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics, and teratogenic with two forms of b…","Biologics — Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics (as anti-cancer therapy) and Rheumatoid Arthritis (as biologic response modifiers for autoimmune disease) — the same word, two uses — Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Lupus) — biologics and…","Immune complexes in autoimmune disease — Rheumatoid Arthritis — complexes deposited in the synovial tissue; see also GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity Review — Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Lupus) — the same idea, but the complexes can land i…","Osteoblasts and osteoclasts — Osteoporosis — osteoclasts break bone down, osteoblasts build it — Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease — osteoblasts are activated and build bone spurs, the hallmark of OA, in an attempt to repair the jo…","Kidney involvement — Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Lupus) — the kidneys are the most common site of immune-complex deposition — Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics (methotrexate nephrotoxicity, keep patients hydrated); Rheumatoid Arth…"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and safety statements from across the twelve pages, condensed to a phrase. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation — click through to a topic for the details behind any rule. Some pages…","Cancer Fundamentals — A benign tumor can still be lethal if it grows large enough to mechanically interrupt a critical tissue or organ (a dangerous benign tumor in the brain, or one taking over part of a lung). If you are given a T and M (T…","Metastasis & Cancer Etiology — No danger callout. Know the difference between a carcinogen (alters DNA, causes the mutation) and a promoter (helps already-damaged cells proliferate), and that some things, such as tobacco and estrogen therap…","Lung Cancer — Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related death. Radon plus smoking is a very dangerous combination (a compounding effect). Smoke and toxins paralyze the cilia (stressed twice). Take a thorough health history of work …","Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer — No danger callout. Prevention and screening points: the HPV vaccine (best around age 11 or 12, can be given up to age 45) and Pap smears prevent cervical cancer; family history of breast or ovarian can…","Chemotherapy Principles & Toxicities — The nadir is the time of highest risk for infection, anemia, and bleeding. Extravasation is a very serious complication of IV chemotherapy (central access such as a portacath). Chemotherapy may be dela…","Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics — Cytotoxic agents are dangerous to handle (certification, strict protocols, many are vesicants). Alkylating agents: hemorrhagic cystitis. Antimetabolites: keep the patient hydrated, monitor liver e…","Osteoporosis — The goal of drug therapy is to reduce fractures, not just raise bone density. Alendronate: do not lie down afterward, do not take with calcium, take with one sip of water in the morning (esophageal ulcerations). Raloxifene: s…","Fractures & Osteomyelitis — Compartment syndrome (pain out of proportion, weak pulses, edema) is treated with a fasciotomy. Fat embolism can go from fine to respiratory arrest within minutes (hypoxemia, altered level of consciousness, petec…","Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease — No drug prevents progression, prevents damage, or reverses the course of arthritis, so the focus is pain management and disability. Review your NSAIDs and know their risks.","Rheumatoid Arthritis — Methotrexate is given ONCE PER WEEK, never daily — death can occur if given daily. Folic acid replacement, no alcohol, monitor liver enzymes and the CBC, two forms of birth control, and a patient with signs of infecti…","Gout — Allopurinol: rash can mean Stevens-Johnson syndrome and TEN; agranulocytosis and aplastic anemia risk (monitor WBC and CBC); watch the INR with warfarin and blood sugar with hypoglycemic agents. Colchicine: stomach-flu-type symptoms …","Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Lupus) — Kidney involvement is the big one: about half of patients develop kidney complications, which can require dialysis or a kidney transplant. Flare triggers to avoid: sunlight, infection, abruptly stoppin…","Patterns you'll notice across these rules","\"Rapidly dividing cells take the hit, so watch the blood counts.\" Chemotherapy damages hair follicles, the GI tract, and bone marrow along with the cancer, which is why the nadir, neutropenia, and infection risk are the central safety point…","\"With these drugs, the safety rule is usually how or when you give them.\" Vinca alkaloids are IV only; promethazine is best given by IV piggyback; methotrexate is once a week, never daily; alendronate means one sip of water and staying upri…","\"Every high-alert drug has an early warning sign to teach.\" Blood in the urine with alkylating agents, peripheral neuropathy with vinca alkaloids, persistent vomiting with colchicine, a rash with allopurinol, blurry vision with hydroxychlor…","\"The deadly part is often the complication, not the first problem.\" A benign tumor becomes lethal when it grows large enough to interrupt a critical organ; deaths after an osteoporotic hip fracture come from immobility complications (sepsis…","\"When symptoms are vague or missing, screening and risk factors do the work.\" Lung cancer is usually caught late because early symptoms are lacking (stage 1 survival over 60%, stage 4 below 10%); colorectal polyps are usually asymptomatic; …"]}]},{"f":"week7-comprehensive-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 7 · Topic 1","Part 1 — Cancer Fundamentals","Part 2 — Metastasis & Cancer Etiology","Part 3 — Lung Cancer","Part 4 — Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer","Part 5 — Chemotherapy Principles & Toxicities","Part 6 — Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics","Part 7 — Osteoporosis","Part 8 — Fractures & Osteomyelitis","Part 9 — Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease","Part 10 — Rheumatoid Arthritis","Part 11 — Gout","Part 12 — Systemic Lupus Erythematosus","Whole-Week Quick Reference","Self-Test Flashcards","This page is a full, in-depth walkthrough of everything covered in Week 7 — the cancer and chemotherapy unit (cancer fundamentals, metastasis and etiology, lung cancer, breast/cervical/colorectal cancer, chemotherapy principles, and antineo…"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Cancer Fundamentals▾","a":"part-1-cancer-fundamentals","k":["Part 1 — Cancer Fundamentals▾","The cancer material runs in this order: cancer in general (this part), then the specific cancers you are responsible for, then chemotherapies; this part covers what cancer is, how cancer cells differ from normal cells, benign vs. malignant …","What Is Cancer?","Cancer is a group of diseases characterized by cellular transformation (example: a genetic mutation in a cell), uncontrolled cellular growth, and possible invasion into other organs with metastasis to other tissues or organs. The instructor…","Cancer cells usually lack","Cellular differentiation: not as specific as normal body cells.","Cellular checkpoints: may not have the checkpoint that recognizes DNA errors.","The ability to undergo apoptosis: the cell self-destructing when it recognizes it is not doing what it should.","Response to signals from other cells (e.g., \"stop invading my space\").","Result: they continue to proliferate.","Normal Cells vs. Cancer Cells","Contact inhibition — Present: \"I'll stay out of someone's space and you stay out of mine.\" — Lack it; invade other cells' space.","Cohesiveness — Similar cells stick together (\"stick with me and we'll stay home\"). — Not cohesive; they leave (\"I don't care\").","Cellular signaling — Traditional communication through cellular signals (analogy: \"ask the man next door to help\"). — Do what they want, how they want; do not communicate or respond to signals.","Growth control — Controlled; the goal is zero population growth. — Significantly lack proliferation/growth control.","Apoptosis — Normal cell death when the cell recognizes it is no longer needed. — Sometimes immortal, or die unpredictably; do not follow the normal life span.","Proliferation rate — Normal, expected for the cell type (skin cells divide faster than cardiomyocytes). — Very unpredictable; depends on level of differentiation (more or less specific) and the cancer cell's DNA makeup.","HLA antigens — Body recognizes the HLA antigen: \"this is my cell, don't hurt me.\" — Carry a non-self marker, so attacks may be mustered; the immune system may try to attack cancer cells.","HLA: the instructor relates HLA antigens to blood typing (blood type is a type of HLA antigen). How intense the cancer becomes, or where it goes, depends on how successful the immune attack is, the kind of cancer, how fast it is proliferati…","Cell Morphology: How Cancer Cells Look","From the instructor's slide comparison (normal cells divide at a normal rate; cancer cells just proliferate).","Nuclei — Abnormally shaped, usually larger than normal.","Nucleus-to-cytoplasm ratio — The nucleus takes up much more of the cell than the cytoplasm.","Size and shape — A lot of variation; cancer cells look really different from one another, unlike normal cells in a normal area.","Loss of normal features — May lose normal features (neuron example: normally an axon and dendrites; the cancerous version looks like a \"sick neuron\").","Arrangement — Disorganized; stacked on top of each other, not appropriately (normal cells are neatly organized).","Tumor boundary — Poorly defined.","Benign vs. Malignant Tumors","Starred by the instructor (\"put a little star here\"). Know benign vs. malignant. You will not be asked to tell leiomyoma from leiomyosarcoma, but you must understand the differences between the cell types at the benign and malignant levels.","The terms malignant tumor, malignancy, neoplasm, and tumor indicate cancerous cells. Tumors (groups of abnormal cells) can be benign (not likely associated with cancer) or malignant (associated with cancer).","Cancer? — Not associated with cancer — What we associate with cancer; cancer cells","Differentiation — Well differentiated; resemble tissue of origin but \"just kind of inappropriate\" — Poorly differentiated; do not resemble tissue of origin, i.e., anaplastic (\"completely inappropriate cells for the area\")","Growth rate — Progressive or slow, closer to normal — Erratic: can be slow, but can be very, very rapid","Invasion — Usually no significant invasion; cohesive cell walls — Generally invasive; infiltrate surrounding normal tissue","Boundary — Demarcated, often encapsulated, so movable (example: a fatty tumor on a dog) — Poorly demarcated","Metastasis — Not seen — Frequently seen (the lecture says cancer cells that invade surrounding tissues are considered to metastasize)","Tumor core — No necrotic core — Can have a necrotic core","A benign tumor can still be lethal. Benign tumors are generally harmless, but can be lethal if they grow large enough to mechanically interrupt the function of a critical tissue or organ (examples: a dangerous benign tumor in the brain, or …","Endometrium example (slide, spoken description only)","Benign leiomyoma","Small, well demarcated, non-invasive, non-metastatic, well differentiated.","Can usually be removed laparoscopically, or may not even be removed if not causing a problem.","Malignant tumor in the endometrium","Invades the endometrial tissues; large and poorly demarcated (no nice lines containing it).","Cells generally rapid and growing, with hemorrhage and necrosis of cells in surrounding tissues.","Locally invasive; if it gets into the lymph node area, it can be metastatic.","Often poorly differentiated.","The Cell Growth Cycle","Normal cells divide in a controlled, organized fashion regulated through the cell growth cycle. Growth characteristics of normal and neoplastic cells are similar; both pass through distinct phases. The time to go through the cycle is the ge…","G0 — Cell at rest, not actively in the cycle; most human cells in the body are here right now. Seen in the normal cycle but not the cancer cycle on the slide. Cancer cells in G0 are not susceptible to the toxic effects of cell-cycle-specifi…","G1 — Cells enter the cycle and enzymes for DNA synthesis are produced; the first post-mitosis phase.","S — \"Synthesis of structure\": structures move to opposite poles in preparation for division into two cells; the 46 chromosomes reorganize into two sets of 23 pairs at opposite poles; two nuclear membranes develop around the two sets.","G2 — Cells prepare to divide; RNA and specialized proteins are created.","M — Mitosis/division is completed; two daughter cells are created.","Why it matters for chemotherapy","Chemo agents can act on specific phases; certain meds are specific to certain parts of the cycle.","The instructor cares about this because it helps you understand chemo agents.","Cancer cells in G0 are not susceptible to cell-cycle-specific drugs.","Cancer cycle vs. normal","Cancer cells do not rest or go through restriction checkpoints; they cycle constantly and proliferate without control.","With no restriction point, DNA repairs do not often occur and the cell continuously mutates, hence the many \"mistakes\" in cancer cells.","Tumor Grading (Grades 1 to 3)","Malignant tumors are graded 1 through 3 by how the cells look under a microscope. Slide description: grade 1 might show one or two cells with some abnormal growth/division; grade 2 is starting to get bigger; grade 3 the cells are invading.","Grade 1 — Well differentiated","Grade 2 — Moderately differentiated","Grade 3 — Poorly differentiated or anaplastic","TNM Classification","Tumors are also classified by size, invasiveness, and spread with TNM (\"very, very common\"). It is used internationally, is diagnostic and very specific, and is \"what you will see in your patient's charts.\" It is a series of letters and num…","T — Primary tumor: size, location, involvement (other tumors may exist, but they try to find the primary) — T0: no evidence of a primary tumor (the instructor, \"no cancer expert,\" guesses this may be like some blood cancers). Tis: tumor in …","N — Lymph node involvement — N0: no spread to regional lymph nodes. N1: closest regional node or a small number of regional nodes. N2: \"most distant or numerous\" nodes.","M — Metastasis to different organs (N is generally related to M) — M0: no metastasis. M1: metastasis present (simply yes or no).","Cancer Staging (Stages I to IV)","The simple four-stage system is probably the one we are most familiar with, a bit more descriptive than TNM alone (it \"accounts for more tumor variability\"), and generally based on lymph node spread and metastasis.","Stage I — Confined to the organ of origin; usually very treatable and usually the best stage to catch it in","Stage II — Locally invasive","Stage III — Regional spread","Stage IV — Spread to distant sites","What you are responsible for: you do not need the specific rates and details of grading, but if given a T and M (TNM) classification or a stage, you must understand what it means for the patient."]},{"s":"Part 2 — Metastasis & Cancer Etiology▾","a":"part-2-metastasis-cancer-etiology","k":["Part 2 — Metastasis & Cancer Etiology▾","This part defines metastasis and the routes of spread, then moves to why people get cancer: risk factors, tumor suppressor genes vs. oncogenes, carcinogens vs. promoters.","What Metastasis Means","Malignant cancer cells are invasive, penetrative, and destructive to surrounding tissues, and generally metastasize from a primary site through the lymph nodes or the blood. To establish in a target organ the tumor must develop its own bloo…","Primary tumor","The site of origin, where the original cellular division problem started.","Secondary tumor","Generally not the site of origin; results from seeding of malignant cells traveling to distant sites (due to metastasis).","\"METs\" = a metastasis or secondary tumor.","Under the microscope the cells look like the primary site's cells (example: breast cancer traveling to bone; a bone biopsy shows cells like the primary breast tumor).","Routes of Cancer Spread","Know three words: seeding, implantation, and metastasis.","Seeding — Tumors erode and shed into the body cavities — Intraperitoneal seeding: colon cancer invades the colon but also puts out \"seeds\" that implant elsewhere in the abdominal cavity","Implantation — Direct expansion of the tumor into adjoining tissues as it grows — Prostate cancer spreading into the bladder (\"directly connected because of close proximity\")","Metastasis — Spread from the primary site to distant sites; two primary routes: lymphatic and vascular — See below","First stop — Generally common; the lymph system is the \"first stop in the system\"; cells can become trapped in lymph nodes — The liver, because most blood goes through the portal vein before general circulation","Process — Three fates for trapped cells: die (\"hopeful\"), go dormant (sit and wait to grow; you may not know you have cancer), or flourish and proliferate. Survivors leave the node into the lymph system and spread through channels to the ne…","Common Sites of Metastasis","Secondary tumors need nutrients and oxygen, so the site often depends on the primary location, blood circulation (vascular dependence), and the angiogenesis rate. Built only from what the instructor says aloud over her chart slide.","Lung — Bone and brain","Colon — Liver","Breast — Bone, brain, liver, or lung","Prostate — Vertebrae","Melanoma — Brain","Instructor emphasis: \"You should kind of be familiar with this slide,\" but she will not ask \"in the weeds\" why melanoma goes to the brain (higher level than expected). Think-about question posed (not answered in the lecture): what symptoms …","Etiology: Why Do People Get Cancer?","Etiology means cause. The etiology of cancer in general is still \"kind of a mystery,\" but a lot of progress has been made and many risk factors are identified.","Sun/UV exposure — Skin cancer","Low-fiber diet — GI cancers","Age — For most cancers, risk increases with age. Exception: not seen with reproductive cancers such as cervical or uterine cancer.","Sex — Lung and urinary cancers definitely higher risk in males (possibly lifestyle and environment)","Genetic/ethnic factors — Burkitt lymphoma much more common in young African children and children of African descent; nasopharyngeal cancers high incidence in persons of Chinese descent","Oncogenic viruses — HPV, HIV, herpes virus, and Epstein-Barr are associated with various cancers","Occupational/environmental — Carcinogens and mutagens; Kentucky example: chemicals in certain factories (a battery company that used to be around) linked to many cancers, especially lung","The four cancers with the highest prevalence (and most seen in Kentucky), covered in the next parts, are lung, breast, cervical, and colorectal.","Genetic Mechanisms: Tumor Suppressor Genes vs. Proto-Oncogenes / Oncogenes","The body has signaling systems and pathways that try to stop cancer cells from proliferating. Two major classes of cancer genes/genetic mechanisms. Car analogy: a cell is a car, and there must be ways to control how fast it grows.","Tumor suppressor gene — Brake pedal — Restrains cell growth; keeps the cell from dividing too quickly — Defective, loses ability to inhibit growth, so division gets out of control; one way cancer develops is lacking this gene","Proto-oncogene — Gas pedal — Stimulates and regulates the cell's movement through the cycle; helps the cell grow and divide normally — When mutated, stimulates constantly; the cell is never in the rest cycle","Oncogene — Gas pedal stuck to the floor (\"stuck in the drive\") — The mutated, always-on form — Cells constantly reproducing","P53: a specific tumor suppressor gene that controls cellular apoptosis (natural cell death), which should lead to the death of cells with damaged DNA.","Carcinogens","Why do these genes get messed up? \"I wish we knew,\" but carcinogens are one cause. Carcinogens cause cancer by altering a cell's DNA, so they can alter those genetic mechanisms. They often require prolonged exposure over many years; they ar…","Known — Tobacco, asbestos, alcohol, some viruses such as HPV and HIV — \"Definitely\" cause cancer; shown in humans","Probable — Night shift work — Women who worked night shift at some point in the past 10 years have a 60% higher risk of breast cancer (\"who knows\" why)","Possible — Engine exhaust — Insufficient evidence; \"in some yes and in some no\"","Promoters & Promoter vs. Carcinogen","Promoters are agents that help mutated genes proliferate (\"sometimes the line gets a little messy\"). Examples: diet (a high-fat diet can stimulate further proliferation of a cancer cell), alcoholic beverages, tobacco, and hormones (\"big pro…","Hormone example (estrogen exposure): more menstrual cycles means more estrogen exposure, which can raise cancer risk. Higher-risk women: first period at a young age, very late first pregnancy, did not breastfeed or only a few months. It is …","Carcinogen","Alters the cell's DNA; can cause cells to mutate and change inappropriately.","Promoter","Helps already-mutated/damaged cells proliferate (it is not the alcohol causing the DNA change, but it can proliferate already-damaged cells).","Things can be both: estrogen therapy can alter cell DNA (carcinogen) and also help mutated genes proliferate; tobacco does both. Exam expectation: she will not ask for an example of a promoter, but wants you to know the different definition…"]},{"s":"Part 3 — Lung Cancer▾","a":"part-3-lung-cancer","k":["Part 3 — Lung Cancer▾","Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related death in the United States and in Kentucky; this part covers late diagnosis and survival, disparities, etiology (smoking, radon), pathogenesis, types, and symptoms.","Context for the Specific-Cancers Section","Historically these four (lung, breast, cervical, colorectal) had the highest prevalence, but with advancement they are seen less now, especially cervical cancer (still mentioned, with a little about the HPV vaccine).","The highest-prevalence cancer is non-melanoma skin cancer, covered in NUR 325 (the instructor clarifies she means non-melanoma skin cancer).","Focus is on cancers seen in Kentucky.","Late Diagnosis & Survival","Most patients do not present until the cancer is advanced or metastatic. Exact percentages vary by study and region, but it is \"pretty well documented\" that most lung cancers are diagnosed late, often after spread to distant sites. Reasons:…","Five-year survival, stage 1 — Over 60%","Five-year survival, stage 4 — Below 10%","Screening — Low-dose CT scans; \"very effective for early detection\"","Average age at diagnosis (U.S.) — Around 70; often seen in patients over 65","Racial, Ethnic & Socioeconomic Disparities","Exam note: \"You don't need to memorize these for the exams,\" but providers should recognize the disparities (well studied and prevalent in cancer).","Black men — About 15% more likely to develop lung cancer than white men","Black women — Lower incidence than white women","Black Americans overall — More likely diagnosed at later stages, receive less treatment, lower survival","Other groups — Similar inequality in almost every ethnic group, including Hispanic and Asian groups","Healthcare access: people of color are more likely to be uninsured, affecting diagnosis, screening, and treatment.","Socioeconomic factors: poverty, education level, rural residence contribute to worse outcomes (seen significantly in Kentucky data).","Rural Kentucky: limits screening access (is there a CT scanner, a radiologist, an oncologist able to do this at the needed rates?).","Systemic issues: biases in healthcare delivery and underrepresentation in clinical trials.","Etiology (Causes)","Cigarette smoking is the leading cause, \"to no one's surprise.\" 80 to 90% of lung cancer patients are current or former smokers. Risk rises with total cigarettes smoked, expressed as a pack-year (example: a 60-year-old who smokes 2 packs/da…","E-cigarettes/vaping: smoking rates are falling but vape use is rising; \"not good for you.\" No good studies show increased lung cancer risk; some data suggest slightly less carcinogenic than cigarettes, but \"that data is not great right now.…","Passive smoke (formerly secondhand) — Consistent exposure raises risk by 20 to 30%; the big reason for smoke-free laws","COPD — Asthma, chronic bronchitis, emphysema; chronically inflamed lungs; a promoter that can cause lung cancer","Asbestos — Seen in construction workers (insulation); not used now, but many buildings still have it; a known cause","Radon (emphasized) — Chemical from the ground; high levels very common in Kentucky; a known promoter and carcinogen; compounding effect with smoking","Arsenic — Exposure is a lung cancer etiology","Genetics — Many genetic components may predispose","Prior chest radiation — History of cancer needing chest radiation raises lung cancer risk (also seen with breast cancer)","Radon plus smoking is a \"very dangerous combination\" (compounding effect, seen quite frequently). It is the big reason for pushes to test new homes and buildings for radon; radon mitigation systems exist.","Nursing point: in oncology or preventative care, obtain really good health histories (where they work, exposures); especially in Kentucky given the high rate of blue-collar workers and carcinogenic chemical exposures. Early diagnosis is \"de…","Pathogenesis","Lung cancer (with cervical cancer) is one of the best-understood cancers; knowing the process explains why we avoid carcinogens and lets us educate populations.","1. Cilia paralyzed — Smoke and toxins paralyze the respiratory-tract cilia, which normally sweep away carcinogens and bacteria (stressed twice by the instructor)","2. Carcinogens accumulate — Less effective cilia lead to accumulation of carcinogens along the airway","3. Overload plus genetics — Overload of carcinogens from smoking and environmental toxins, then usually some genetic predisposition acquired from your parents","4. Dysplasia and mutations — Begins in an area of dysplasia, where carcinogen damage occurred along the airway; initial mutations occur here; a thickened area of bronchial mucosa","5. Proliferation and erosion — The lesion proliferates and can erode the epithelial lining","6. Growth — Grows into the bronchial lumen or becomes a mass within lung tissue; destroys and invades surrounding tissue","Types of Lung Cancer","Exam scope: \"You don't really need to know specifics about types of cancer.\" Only recognize two types, non-small cell and small cell, and that non-small cell is more common; no specific questions beyond that.","How common — About 85 to 90% of all lung cancers; more common — Less common","Growth — Slower; varies by subtype (adenocarcinoma vs. squamous cell carcinoma) — A little more rapid","Spread — Not stated — Metastasizes quicker","Treatment response — Not stated — Generally responds well to initial treatment but relapses quicker","Signs & Symptoms","Diagnosis is difficult because many symptoms coincide with those of chronic smokers, so screening and knowing risk factors matter. \"Those are kind of what you'll be responsible for.\"","Cough — Does not go away or gets worse (\"a big one\")","Hemoptysis / sputum — Coughing up blood or rust-colored sputum","Chest pain — Worse with deep breathing, coughing, or laughing","Hoarseness — May be a sign if the tumor is compressing the laryngeal nerve","Other — Loss of appetite; unexplained weight loss; shortness of breath; fatigue (more tired or weak); recurrent infections (bronchitis or pneumonia that keep coming back); new-onset wheezing","Instructor's reassurance: weight loss, weakness, tiredness, and appetite loss worry patients. Her rule of thumb: as long as you are not losing weight (not on purpose), can eat, drink, urinate, and have bowel movements, and are not actively …"]},{"s":"Part 4 — Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer▾","a":"part-4-breast-cervical-colorectal-cancer","k":["Part 4 — Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer▾","The last three specific cancers: breast (statistics, disparities, risk factors, DES, BRCA), cervical (HPV and the HPV vaccine), and colorectal (risk factors, polyps, symptoms, screening).","Breast Cancer: Overview & Disparities","Very prevalent; affects men and women but much more likely in women because of more breast tissue.","1 in 8 women in the US will be diagnosed in their lifetime.","The most common cancer in the US (outside the skin cancer already mentioned); \"lung cancer tends to kill you sooner.\"","Exam note: you do not need to memorize the disparity statistics; providers should be aware of racial and ethnic disparities in diagnosis and treatment and be cognizant of this as we treat our populations and represent the profession.","Average age at diagnosis — Black women 60; white women 62","Death from breast cancer — Black women 40% more likely to die than white women","Five-year relative survival — Black women have the lowest of any racial or ethnic group","Triple negative — Black women are diagnosed with triple negative breast cancer (much harder to treat) at a higher rate than any other group","Breast Cancer Risk Factors","Lists come from the CDC and the American Cancer Society.","Non-modifiable","Age: older than 50.","Reproductive history: periods before age 12 and menopause after age 55 (longer span means longer hormone exposure).","Obesity: increased estrogen and fat deposits; listed under both (the instructor apologizes: you cannot really control estrogen and fat storage, but she considers it more modifiable).","Dense breast tissue: larger breasts or more dense tissue.","Family history of breast or ovarian cancer: a big red flag for prevention and screening.","Chest radiation for any reason (similar to lung cancer).","DES and BRCA1/BRCA2 (below).","Modifiable","Overweight/obesity after menopause: much higher risk than healthy weight.","Hormone replacement therapy: historically closely linked; estrogen and progesterone taken during menopause can raise risk if taken for more than five years.","Certain hormonal oral contraceptives (not all birth control is hormonal, even pills).","Pregnancy/breastfeeding history: first pregnancy after 20, not breastfeeding, or never a full-term pregnancy (all about hormone exposure; she does not like it on this list, but \"you have to take what you can get\").","Alcohol: even one drink increases risk.","Night shift work (specifically women and breast cancer; assumed hormones out of whack long term), smoking, and other carcinogen exposures.","Instructor caution: HRT and oral contraceptives should not be demonized or villainized; they do a lot of good for those who need them; the link is just something to think about.","DES (Diethylstilbestrol) & BRCA1/BRCA2","DES","Given to some pregnant women in the US between 1940 and 1971 to prevent miscarriage.","Women who took it have a higher breast cancer risk.","Still matters: those women would be in their late 60s, 70s, and 80s, or the people who were pregnant in their 40s, 50s, and 60s, so keep an eye on it.","DES is also linked to cervical cancer.","BRCA1 and BRCA2","Genes; mutations in the genetic code carry the risk (a known genetic link; other genes also connect, but these are most common and well studied).","We all have these genes, but can test for mutations.","Raised risk of breast, ovarian, colon, pancreatic cancer; in males, prostate cancer.","Genetic counseling, subtypes, prevention: many cancers now get genetic counseling, breast cancer included. There are many tags and markers (triple negative was named); the instructor does not go into types, just know they exist. In oncology…","Cervical Cancer","Much more prevalent in the 80s, 90s, and early 2000s; prevalence has really decreased because of Pap smear screening and the HPV vaccine; it can essentially be prevented with the vaccine and frequent screening.","Like skin cancer, Pap smears detect early cancer mutations so they are found sooner.","The HPV vaccine prevents HPV, a huge risk for cervical cancer. Almost 100% of cervical cancer patients test positive for HPV.","HPV infection — The big one","Smoking — A big risk factor","Sexual history and STIs — The earlier, the more frequent, and the more partners, the more likely to get HPV","Other STIs (chlamydia, gonorrhea) — Can increase risk when compounded with HPV","Immunosuppression — HIV/AIDS or other issues; probably more susceptible to HPV","Genetics/family history; DES — Listed risk factors; DES usage is linked to cervical cancer","No need to demonize sexuality: people with these risk factors simply need more frequent screenings and awareness of their risk.","Effectiveness — Current vaccine (very different from the early-2000s one) is 97% effective in preventing cervical cancer and cervical changes","Genital warts — 100% effective in treating and preventing genital warts; use with reluctant patients (\"nobody wants genital warts\")","Safety — Very safe; decades-long studies show no significant risk or side effects","Timing — Best as an adolescent, around age 11 or 12; can be received up to age 45","Public health message: \"spread that knowledge.\"","Colorectal Cancer","Begins anywhere along the colon or rectum.","Fourth most common cancer in men and women and the fourth leading cause of cancer-related deaths.","Males have much higher rates of incidence and of dying than females (possibly related to some risk factors).","Age — Risk increases as you get older","Inflammatory bowel disease — Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis highly increases risk","Family history — Of colorectal cancer or polyps","Genetic conditions — Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) or hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer (called Lynch syndrome); any genetic mutations","Lifestyle — Sedentary lifestyle; diets low in fruits and vegetables and low in fiber, and high-fat diets; diets high in fat and processed meats (deli meat is highly processed); overweight/obesity; alcohol and tobacco","Example: a friend's dad had colorectal cancer very early with a genetic syndrome; all his children were tested and have it, so they need colonoscopies every two years even though very young. This is generally how syndromes are picked up: fa…","Polyps","Screening often looks for polyps: non-cancerous growths that can develop into colorectal cancer. Usually found on routine screening colonoscopy or flexible sigmoidoscopy; usually asymptomatic (some may present with rectal bleeding, bowel ha…","Adenomatous polyps","Most common type.","Higher potential to develop into cancer.","Hyperplastic polyps","Less common.","Typically do not turn into cancer.","Colorectal Symptoms & Screening","Symptoms are nonspecific: changes in bowel habits; blood in the stool; diarrhea; constipation; feeling the bowel does not empty all the way (usually more of a rectal cancer symptom; diarrhea and constipation can be colon or rectal); abdomin…","Start age — Begins at 45, at regular intervals discussed with your provider (depends on risk; you will learn more in med-surg)","Higher risk — May begin before 45: IBD, family history, genetic conditions","Flexible sigmoidoscopy — Less intensive; examines only one third of the colon","Colonoscopy — Full colonoscopy; usually only every 10 years, depending on polyps found","Example (family history and genetics): the instructor's husband had a colonoscopy in his early 30s for GI issues and had many polyps; no cancer, but higher risk, so more frequent colonoscopies. He is one of four siblings, so all were encour…"]},{"s":"Part 5 — Chemotherapy Principles & Toxicities▾","a":"part-5-chemotherapy-principles-toxicities","k":["Part 5 — Chemotherapy Principles & Toxicities▾","An overview of what chemotherapy is, the two drug groups, what affects success, general principles, the common toxicities (including stomatitis, myelosuppression, and the nadir), extravasation, and contraindications; the instructor says thi…","Anti-Neoplastic Drugs & Cytotoxic Chemotherapy","Anti-neoplastic drugs: general term for drugs used to treat cancer; chemotherapy agents that inhibit the growth and spread of malignant cells.","Cytotoxic chemotherapy: uses cytotoxic drugs (able to kill cells) to kill or inhibit cancer cells, particularly targeting rapidly dividing cells (this idea drives most of the rest).","Ongoing research and constant change. For those going into oncology (e.g., Markey or another cancer center): really know the drugs you give and the cancers they treat, take notes, and stay aware. This course gives a general overview; the in…","Surgery, Radiation & Chemotherapy","Surgery — Generally removes tumors or affected tissues","Radiation therapy — High-energy radiation kills or damages cancer cells","Chemotherapy — Depends on the kind of drug, but also targets cancer cells","Often used in combination; the cancer type influences the normal course. Many discussions occur before treatment (surgery, radiation, chemo, or just one). Oncology is \"really ahead\" of most fields studied so far in shared decision making: o…","Two Main Groups of Chemotherapy Drugs","Cell cycle nonspecific","Kill cancer cells regardless of cell-cycle phase.","Cell cycle specific","Work only during certain phases (mitosis or DNA synthesis).","These are the anti-metabolites and mitotic inhibitors (no need to write this down quickly; discussed later).","Growth fraction: regardless of group, these drugs work faster on rapidly growing tumors; chemo is more effective on tumors with a high growth fraction, because more cells are actively dividing and are more vulnerable to cytotoxic agents.","Factors That Affect Whether Chemo Works","Doubling time — Tumors that grow quickly respond better","Type of cancer — Some are inherently more sensitive; some are less likely to spread, so more effectively cured by surgery","Efficacy of treatment plan — Correct drug, dosing, and scheduling; dedicated oncologists and pharmacists work in this area","Drug resistance — Cancers can become resistant, reducing effectiveness over time (example: cancer recurs/relapses after initial chemo because cells became resistant to that type)","General health — Overall condition affects tolerance and response; chemo drugs are very harsh, not gentle on the system","General Principles of All Chemotherapy Drugs","No one drug is effective against all cancers.","Low therapeutic index: a fine line between therapeutic and toxic effects; clinicians monitor cancer spread (e.g., PET scans) against the adverse/toxic effects.","Toxicity comes from affecting rapidly dividing cells: kills harmful cancer cells but also affects normal cells.","Combination therapy usually works better than a single drug (different mechanisms of different cytotoxic meds are maximized with multiples); some single-drug efficacy exists depending on cancer type.","Avoid overlapping toxicity: do not combine two chemo types that both cause cardiotoxicity.","Toxicities from Rapidly Dividing Cells","Hair follicles, GI tract, and bone marrow divide rapidly, so these effects are seen with every single chemotherapy.","Hair follicles: hair loss — Common and reversible; begins 7 to 14 days after chemo and starts regenerating 1 to 2 months after therapy ends","GI tract: nausea and vomiting — \"A ton\"; occurs several days after chemo","Bone marrow — Neutropenia, anemia, thrombocytopenia","Fatigue — Extremely common","Dose-limiting adverse effects — Patients may not tolerate the needed dose increases because they are so nauseous and ill, so anemic, or so neutropenic","Stomatitis & Magic Mouthwash","Stomatitis is inflammation of the oral mucosa, commonly caused by almost all chemo meds because mouth cells divide rapidly. Instructor's way to think of it: a mouth blister (canker sore) heals quickly while a blister on the hands takes long…","Magic mouthwash (put it on your drug matrix; know it): a prescription cocktail for stomatitis; exact contents vary (simple versions may have only lidocaine, an antifungal, and diphenhydramine). Swish, gargle, and spit; it can be swallowed i…","Viscous lidocaine — Numbs the mouth","Maalox/Mylanta — Listed; no role given","Diphenhydramine — Helps with inflammation","Nystatin — Antifungal; prevents thrush","Prednisolone — Listed; no role given","Distilled water — Listed","Myelosuppression & the Nadir","Myelosuppression = bone marrow suppression/depression; typically from chemotherapy but can also be caused by radiation.","Reduced production of WBCs, RBCs, and platelets: neutropenia, anemia, thrombocytopenia; very high infection risk from the leukopenia (usually neutropenia).","Neutrophils are critical in fighting bacterial (and other) infections; depletion makes patients very vulnerable, who are also anemic and thrombocytopenic.","Prophylactic antibiotics: often recommended (more in med-surg). Levofloxacin is very common, recommended in high-risk patients with prolonged neutropenia, defined as ANC less than 500 for greater than 7 days.","The nadir is the lowest point of WBCs, RBCs, and platelets, when patients are at highest risk. The instructor highlights it and gives the timing more than one way, recorded as stated:","First statement — Between 7 and 12 days","Restated — \"So 7, 14 days\"","Counting from the dose — Dose at day 0; about two weeks or so later, 10 to 14 days, highest risk for infection, anemia, and bleeding","The nadir is the time of highest risk for infection, anemia, and bleeding (the nadir is the lowest point of white cells, red cells, and platelets). A very important teaching point and a point of awareness for nursing interventions.","Extravasation & Central Access","Extravasation is a very serious complication of IV chemotherapy: drugs leak into the surrounding tissues during IV administration and can cause severe tissue damage. Treatment protocols are well established; because of this risk, chemo is g…","Drug Monitoring, Contraindications, Fertility & Pregnancy","Drug-level monitoring — You might expect lots of narrow therapeutic monitoring, but most chemo drugs do not require routine therapeutic drug monitoring; some do","Not usually contraindications — Toxicities, adverse effects, and allergies; no absolute contraindications; side effects are expected and managed proactively; allergies may need premedication or a desensitization protocol","Reasons to delay/adjust (can act as contraindications) — Very low WBC or neutrophil count; known active infection; significantly decreased nutrition or hydration; organ dysfunction (usually liver or kidney, monitored with labs)","Why weighed, not absolute — Low white counts and poor nutrition are really common in cancer patients; the provider weighs risks and benefits because \"the cancer is not going to stop growing just because the patient is sick\"","Fertility — A major concern (generally female patients, but males too); any chemo can impair fertility, especially post-puberty; options are sperm banking or egg freezing, generally discussed before treatment","Pregnancy — Most chemo drugs are class D or X (significant fetal risk or generally contraindicated); some cancers are more likely found during pregnancy, producing scary conversations"]},{"s":"Part 6 — Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics▾","a":"part-6-antineoplastic-drug-classes-antiemetics","k":["Part 6 — Antineoplastic Drug Classes & Antiemetics▾","The classes you must know (alkylating agents, antimetabolites, antitumor antibiotics, mitotic inhibitors, hormonal agents, biologics), each tied to key toxicities, followed by the two antiemetics the instructor wants you to know.","What You Are Expected to Know","Know the classes: alkylating agents, antimetabolites, antitumor (antibiotics), mitotic inhibitors, plus hormonal agents and some biologics. Targeted drugs are not covered separately; they generally fall under biologics.","You do not need specific drug names: the instructor will always give the class (\"this is an antimetabolite\"), because the class tells you the expected adverse effects and toxicities. She will not ask for a drug name alone or ask you to pick…","Specific drug types can go on your drug matrix (ask the instructor what does or does not go on it).","Ondansetron and promethazine are the two antiemetics she specifically wants you to know.","Instructor emphasis: know the class, then expect that class's toxicities. Alkylating agents: hemorrhagic cystitis. Antimetabolites: nephrotoxicity/hepatotoxicity and category X. Antitumor antibiotics: cardiotoxicity. Vinca alkaloids: periph…","Overview of the Classes","Cytotoxic agents are the backbone of chemotherapy (most commonly used). Strictly, \"cancer chemotherapy\" refers only to cytotoxic drugs; all other types are anti-cancer or anti-neoplastic drugs (the instructor will not get into the weeds on …","Cytotoxic — Alkylating agents — Cyclophosphamide — Backbone of chemotherapy","Cytotoxic — Antimetabolites — Methotrexate — Also used in non-oncology conditions","Cytotoxic — Antitumor antibiotics — Doxorubicin — Derived from Streptomyces bacteria","Cytotoxic — Mitotic inhibitors (vinca alkaloids) — Vincristine — Derived from the periwinkle plant","Cytotoxic (misc.) — Platinum compounds — None named — \"Some miscellaneous things\"","Hormonal — Hormonal agents — Tamoxifen — Hormone-sensitive cancers such as breast and prostate","Biologic — Monoclonal antibodies; immune therapy agents such as checkpoint inhibitors — None named — Use the body's immune system against cancer","Targeted — Newer class interfering with specific molecular targets in cancer growth (e.g., tyrosine kinase inhibitors) — Tyrosine kinase inhibitors — Generally fall under biologics","Cell-Cycle Specific vs. Nonspecific Drugs","The big differentiator is whether the drug affects cells resting in G0.","Cell cycle phase specific","Work only on a specific phase: vinca alkaloids (vincristine) only in mitosis; methotrexate only in S phase.","Cannot interrupt cells in G0 (the resting phase where many cells sit); do not kill resting cells.","Must remain in the blood for extended periods: prolonged infusions or small doses at short intervals over extended times.","Cell cycle phase nonspecific","Cell position in the cycle does not matter; the drugs are just toxic.","Affect cells in G0; do kill resting cells.","Disadvantage: less effective at injuring just the proliferating cells.","Examples: cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin.","Combination therapy: generally a cell-cycle-specific agent plus a nonspecific agent; make sure you understand that one includes G0 cells and one does not. You do not need to memorize the cell-cycle slide, but know vinca alkaloids are cell c…","Cytotoxic Agents in General","All work by disrupting DNA synthesis; the largest chemotherapy class; act directly on cancer cells to cause death; target high growth fraction cells (reproduce very rapidly).","All commonly affect hair follicles and cause nausea, vomiting, and stomatitis; myelosuppression is also among the usual toxicities (named with doxorubicin).","Handling cytotoxic agents is dangerous. Strict protocols apply and you must be certified to deliver cancer drugs. Many are vesicants. Examples given: double gloving, disposal in black bins, many steps before administering (e.g., flushing ab…","Alkylating Agents (Cyclophosphamide)","The instructor will tell you the class of a drug, so you need not remember on your own that cyclophosphamide is alkylating.","Cell cycle — Phase nonspecific","Uses — Variety of cancers including breast, lymphoma, leukemia, ovarian (no drug-to-cancer matching on the exam)","Common toxicities — Hair follicles, nausea, vomiting, stomatitis","Extreme vesicant — Extensive tissue damage if it extravasates","Hemorrhagic cystitis — Due to a metabolite of the drug; look for blood in the urine or bladder issues","Sterility — Especially with prolonged use; fertility concerns","Skin/nail discoloration — Less common than cystitis and sterility","Resistance — Develops over time; may be used with another drug and may not be used if the cancer relapses","Key toxicities: hemorrhagic cystitis, sterility, extravasation. The exam may ask \"these types of drugs are associated with drug resistance over time.\"","Antimetabolites (Methotrexate)","Cell cycle — Phase specific; acts primarily in S phase by inhibiting DNA synthesis","Mechanism — Mimics natural metabolites like folic acid; interferes with folate-dependent enzymes such as dihydrofolate reductase (the instructor, not a biochemist: the key idea is that it mimics folate)","Resistance — Over time, via increased drug efflux, altered target enzymes, or enhanced DNA repair","Nephrotoxicity — Commonly, especially at high doses; keep patients hydrated","Hepatotoxicity — More common with long-term use, usually in those using it for non-oncology reasons; monitor liver enzymes","Pregnancy — FDA category X: fetal death and abnormalities; teratogenic; contraindicated; may need to agree to birth control or discuss fertility","Uses — Leukemias and lymphomas most commonly; also breast, head and neck, osteosarcomas; non-oncology: rheumatoid arthritis, psoriasis","Antitumor Antibiotics (Doxorubicin)","Cell cycle — Phase nonspecific: acts at any stage","Origin — Derived from Streptomyces bacteria","Usual toxicities — Myelosuppression, nausea, etc.","Uses — Very effective for many cancers, especially breast","Cardiotoxicity is the big concern, especially with cumulative dosing: there are maximum (lifetime) doses. The worry is congestive heart failure, which can be fatal. Patients need closer monitoring of EF and baseline echocardiograms. It can …","Mitotic Inhibitors / Vinca Alkaloids (Vincristine)","Know both terms: mitotic inhibitors and vinca alkaloids.","Cell cycle — Specific; works only in the mitosis phase","Mechanism — Inhibits microtubule formation and prevents cell division","Origin — Periwinkle plant (natural source)","Hallmark side effect — Peripheral neuropathies; many neurotoxic effects","Vesicant — Significant tissue damage","Bone marrow — Typically does not cause significant suppression, which makes it useful to combine with myelosuppressive drugs","Route — Only ever IV","Uses — More often blood cancers (acute lymphoblastic leukemia, Hodgkin's lymphoma); sometimes neuroblastomas and Wilms tumors","Vinca alkaloids are given IV only. If given intrathecally, it will kill your patient.","Hormonal Agents (Tamoxifen)","Commonly prescribed for hormone-sensitive cancers, like breast and prostate; one example is tamoxifen. Know the class; the lecture gives no specific toxicities.","Biologics & Targeted Drug Therapies","The newest and fastest-growing class; the instructor thinks it is the future of chemotherapy (though not a cancer specialist, and many cytotoxic drugs work well); where the research world is \"spinning.\" Biologics use the body's immune syste…","Immune checkpoint inhibitors — Melanoma, small cell cancer, endometrial cancers, specific breast cancers; a big area","T cell transfers (CAR-T) — Often hematologic malignancies from blood cancers","Monoclonal antibodies — Listed; no uses given","Cancer vaccines — Prostate cancer and HPV-related cancers","Immune system modulators — Melanoma and renal cell carcinoma","Targeted (e.g., tyrosine kinase inhibitors) — Interfere with specific molecular targets in cancer growth","Often part of personalized medicine; used alone or with other chemotherapy. Routes: IV is most common; some sub-Q or IM; very few oral, intrathecal, intravascular, or ventricular.","Flu-like symptoms — Fever, chills, fatigue, headache, muscle and joint pain","Infusion/injection site reactions — Pain, redness, swelling","GI issues — Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, not necessarily at cytotoxic rates","Immune-related adverse events — Especially checkpoint inhibitors: skin rashes, colitis, pneumonitis, hepatitis, endocrinopathies (many \"-itis\"); an overreaction of the immune system","Neurologic effects — Headaches, dizziness, neuropathies, especially CAR T-cell therapies","Serious infections — Immunosuppression increases bacterial, viral, or fungal infection; different from true myelosuppression of cytotoxics","Cytokine release syndrome (CRS) — With CAR T-cell therapy; can be very life-threatening and needs very close monitoring","Biologics do not cause the usual cytotoxic toxicities in the same way because they \"aren't that cell cycle specific.\" Clinical trials (not exam material): a slide of ongoing human trials as of 2025; oncology is fast-growing and UK's cancer …","Master Drug-Class Comparison","Everything here comes from the lecture; put it on your drug matrix and learn each row from the class outward.","Cyclophosphamide — Alkylating agent — Nonspecific — Hair follicles, N/V, stomatitis; extreme vesicant; hemorrhagic cystitis (blood in urine); sterility; skin/nail discoloration (less common); resistance over time","Methotrexate — Antimetabolite — Specific (S phase) — Nephrotoxicity (hydrate); hepatotoxicity (liver enzymes); category X; resistance; also RA and psoriasis","Doxorubicin — Antitumor antibiotic — Nonspecific — Myelosuppression, N/V; cardiotoxicity with cumulative dosing; CHF can be fatal; monitor EF and baseline echo; acute or years later","Vincristine — Mitotic inhibitor (vinca alkaloid) — Specific (mitosis) — Peripheral neuropathy hallmark; vesicant; little marrow suppression; IV only, intrathecal is fatal","Tamoxifen — Hormonal agent — Not stated — Hormone-sensitive cancers (breast, prostate)","Checkpoint inhibitors, CAR-T, monoclonal antibodies, vaccines, modulators, TKIs — Biologics/targeted — Not cell-cycle-specific cytotoxics — Flu-like symptoms; infusion/injection reactions; GI upset; \"-itis\" events; neurologic (CAR-T); serio…","Antiemetics for Chemotherapy-Induced Nausea & Vomiting","All of these drugs cause nausea and vomiting, a really big issue for nutrition and hydration. Know these two drugs.","Class / mechanism — Blocks type 3 serotonin receptors in the chemo trigger zone of the brain — First-generation antihistamine (so it will make you very, very tired)","Uses — Created specifically for chemo-induced N/V; also used elsewhere — Chemo, post-op, and general N/V","Routes — Oral or IV — Best by IV piggyback (extravasation risk): confirm the IV works before pushing; dilute if you can","Side effects — Headache, diarrhea, dizziness — Drowsiness/sedation; significant respiratory depression like an opioid; many drug-drug interactions","Notes — Effective alone or with other drugs, especially steroids — Black box warnings (below)","Promethazine black box warnings: (1) children under 2 years old; (2) gangrenous extravasation. Be aware when pushing it: even though it is for nausea and vomiting and is not the chemo itself, it can still be very dangerous."]},{"s":"Part 7 — Osteoporosis▾","a":"part-7-osteoporosis","k":["Part 7 — Osteoporosis▾","Osteoporosis is low bone density with structural deterioration of the bone, and it matters because of fracture risk; this part covers gender and bone mass, T-scores and DEXA, risk factors, pathogenesis, hip fractures, and the drug therapy (…","Gender & Bone Mass","Factors affecting bone mass: age, gender, race, genetics, reproductive status, calcium levels, and exercise (the lecture focuses on age and gender). Bone mass falls with age in both sexes, and both curves start down after age 30.","Women","Greater bone loss in the early post-menopausal years, then a more gradual ongoing loss","Lower peak bone density than men","Together these reach the fracture threshold (risk of fracture significantly increases) much earlier than men","Lifetime loss: about 35% of cortical bone and 50% of trabecular bone","From age 30, bone resorbed by osteoclasts exceeds bone formed by osteoblasts, so bone mass steadily decreases","Men","Lose only about two-thirds of what women lose (about a third less)","Same pattern: curve goes down after 30","Women lose much more in the immediate post-menopausal period","T-Scores & DEXA","Low bone density means more likelihood of breaking bones, and low bone density is considered osteoporosis. Osteoporosis is defined as bone mineral density 2.5 standard deviations below peak bone mass, measured with a DEXA scan and reported …","Normal — −1 or greater — Normal bone density","Osteopenia — Between −1 and −2.5 — Low bone mass; stage before osteoporosis; thinning of the trabecular matrix (trabecular = the porous bone)","Osteoporosis — Less than −2.5 — \"Holey,\" porous, weaker bone","Severe osteoporosis — Below −2.5 plus a history of a fragility fracture — \"Severe\" is diagnosed when there is a fragility fracture history","Fragility fracture: the person fell from standing and broke a bone — not a traumatic accident; the bone broke because it is so porous. Osteoporosis is common yet very serious because of fracture risk; it is characterized by low bone density…","Risk Factors","Aging — Increased resorption and bone breakdown","Female, esp. post-menopausal — Related to the drop in estrogen","Caucasian or Asian — Caucasians: about 20% likelihood when over 50","Adult fracture history — Breaking a large (or any) bone as an adult increases likelihood","Genetics / family history — Huge role; a first-degree relative with a fragility fracture is a very high risk factor","Low body weight; thin, small frame — Listed","Smoking — Major risk factor (problems with bone healing); identified decades ago; direct relationship between tobacco use and decreased bone density; a very complicated relationship but a very big risk factor","Alcohol — Can increase risk","Long-term corticosteroids — Major risk factor: inhibit osteoblast function (osteoblasts build bone when there is breakdown)","No weight-bearing exercise; lack of calcium or vitamin D — Listed","Eating disorders; gastric bypass — Because of a malnutrition status","Lack of estrogen or testosterone (low T, low estrogen); excessive caffeine — Listed","Pathogenesis & Manifestations","Bone is living tissue; remodeling (resorption and formation) occurs throughout life, and about 10% of bone tissue is replaced each year. Osteoclasts cause resorption (stimulate enzymes that dissolve bone); osteoblasts build the protein matr…","INSTRUCTOR'S MEMORY AID: osteoBlasts Build; osteoClasts destroy (remember it with the word \"build\").","In osteoporosis: increased osteoclast activity and decreased osteoblast activity. We cannot make new bone (no osteoblast function) and there is too much resorption from osteoclasts.","Early","Really no manifestations","So the approach is risk-factor identification — high-risk patients start DEXA scans earlier","Later","Fractures","Pain","Loss of height","Stooped posture, like kyphosis","Hip Fractures","One of the biggest, most concerning, and most common complications of osteoporosis.","Risk of death increases about 2.5 to 4 times after an osteoporotic hip fracture.","Deaths do not come from the fracture itself but from immobility-related complications: sepsis, skin breakdown, immobility, pneumonia, blood clots.","Much more common in women older than 65; most common location is the proximal third of the femur.","Independent living is usually lost; patients often end up in a nursing home or other care facility.","Onset — Sudden hip pain before or after a fall (depends on whether the bone broke and then they fell — fragility fracture — or they fell and then broke it)","Walking — Lose the ability to walk, though a minimally impacted fracture may still bear weight for a period","Pain — Severe groin pain is common; a displaced hip fracture usually involves significant groin pain","Leg position — Affected leg externally rotated and shortened","Tenderness / bruising — Tenderness, but typically little bruising because the fracture is usually encapsulated (unlike an arm that breaks open from skateboarding)","Insufficiency (fragility) fractures: there may be no obvious history of trauma; vague knee, groin, or thigh pain; the bone has lost so much density it can no longer bear weight. Presentation is typically no different once the fracture occur…","Pharmacotherapy: Goals","The goal of pharmacotherapy is to reduce fractures. Companies test drugs that increase bone mineral density, but a density increase does no good if it does not reduce fractures — look at how well a drug reduces fractures.","Prevention = calcium and vitamin D supplementation. Treatment = promote bone formation or decrease bone resorption; the common drugs covered decrease resorption.","Only one drug promotes bone formation (a new class targeting the parathyroid); not covered because it is not commonly used.","Groups covered: bisphosphonates, SERMs, and hormone therapy (the lecturer includes calcitonin here). Do not memorize doses.","Calcium & vitamin D: common especially in post-menopausal women; true calcium replacement is elemental calcium around 1200 to 2000 (some patients take much higher doses of calcium carbonate, Tums); vitamin D increases calcium absorption; ca…","Drug Table","Bisphosphonates are first line; know alendronate and raloxifene (the SERM \"you need to know\").","Alendronate (Fosamax) — bisphosphonate — First-line; prevention and treatment — Binds permanently to bone surface, inhibits osteoclast activity so less breakdown (true mechanism not well known). Reduces fractures about 50% — GI effects comm…","Raloxifene (Evista) — SERM — Postmenopausal osteoporosis only (prevent and treat); bisphosphonates definitely first line — Mimics estrogen (binds estrogen receptors); reduces bone turnover, increases density, but not as well as bisphosphona…","Calcitonin (placed under hormone therapy by the lecturer) — Not first line; not for long-term therapy; treatment only, not prevention; may be used short term; not as effective as SERMs or bisphosphonates long term — Inhibits bone removal by…","Hormone replacement therapy (estrogen) — Not recommended for osteoporosis; no longer commonly used — Post-menopause is the estrogen drop, and estrogen replacement is sometimes used then for other issues — Blood clots, breast cancer, other c…","Alendronate safety teaching: stay upright and use only one sip of water in the morning — esophageal ulceration risk. Raloxifene: stroke and clot risk; stop at least 72 hours before a planned procedure; not in pregnancy."]},{"s":"Part 8 — Fractures & Osteomyelitis▾","a":"part-8-fractures-osteomyelitis","k":["Part 8 — Fractures & Osteomyelitis▾","This part covers fractures in general (definition, causes, classification, presentation, bone healing) and then the complications the lecture names: delayed healing/malunion/non-union, impaired bone growth, compartment syndrome, fat embolis…","Definition & Causes","A fracture is any break in the continuity of bone; it occurs when more stress is placed on the bone than it can absorb. The stress needed is relative — weak, brittle bone takes little, while young, healthy bone withstands really high forces…","Traumatic — Example: a fall","Indirect — Example: massive muscle contraction can break a bone (not very common)","Fatigue — Endurance activity, repeated prolonged stress — marathon runners, stress fractures","Pathologic — Highest-risk population is the elderly; related to osteoporosis, bone tumors, or infection that decreases bone integrity","Classification","Open = Compound","The bone penetrates the skin","Higher osteomyelitis risk than a closed fracture","Closed = Simple","Does not break through the skin","Orientations — the instructor says she will not ask a ton of these, but to know the terminology of the highlighted ones.","Transverse — Straight line, usually 90 degrees to the length of the bone; \"pretty common\" — the type seen most commonly in traumatic falls","Spiral — A twisting injury; the fall-on-the-feet/compression type of injury can also be like a spiral","Comminuted — More than one fracture line and more than two bone fragments; e.g., falling on the feet and breaking legs or ankles, like a compression fracture; can also be spiral, and can be seen as an impacted fracture from jumping from hei…","Greenstick — Incomplete break where the bone bends; the most common type of break in children (flagged by the instructor as the other one to know)","Stress — From repeated use or fatigue (e.g., marathon runners)","Presentation & Bone Healing","MNEMONIC — \"PED\": Pain, Edema, Deformity.","Pain — At the fracture/bone disruption; an inflammatory response — inflammation helps splint (body stabilizes the area) so it is good (you do not want to move it), but very painful","Edema — Natural splint; swelling is very common (\"always\")","Deformity — Person holds the limb in a position of comfort, splints it, goes to the ED; keep the part in the most neutral position possible","Also — Loss of function and abnormal mobility","Phases of bone healing (review from A&P): inflammatory, reparative, remodeling. Sequence: hematoma forms (inflammatory) → fibrous cartilage → callus → ossification of the callus → remodeling. Many fracture complications trace to healing: th…","Complications Overview","Delayed healing (delayed union) — Bone pain and increasing tenderness when the bone should have healed; can lead to malunion and non-union","Impaired bone growth — Pediatric; especially a fracture through the epiphyseal plate","Compartment syndrome — Increased pressure in a limited anatomic space; pain out of proportion; fasciotomy","Fat embolism — Rare; long bone fracture; triad of hypoxemia, altered LOC, petechial rash","Osteomyelitis — Severe bone infection, usually Staphylococcus aureus; weeks of IV antibiotics","Delayed Healing, Malunion & Non-Union","Delayed healing: usually identified three months to a year after the fracture; bone pain and tenderness increasing when it should have healed; slow healing possibly from infection or smoking, malnutrition, poor circulation.","Malunion: improper alignment from unequal stresses of muscle pull and gravity (\"did not put the stick back together straight\"); common if weight bearing began too soon.","Non-union: not healed four to six months after the fracture; no healing at all; particular to people with diabetes and infection in the area.","Risk factors (delayed union, malunion, non-union): tobacco use, older age, severe anemia, uncontrolled diabetes, low vitamin D, hypothyroidism, poor nutrition, infection, complicated breaks.","Management: ideally fix/address it at the delayed union or malunion stage — follow these patients frequently with frequent extra x-rays as the bone heals.","Impaired bone growth: a pediatric consideration, especially a fracture through the epiphyseal plate; can delay future growth (covered more in pediatrics).","Compartment Syndrome","Setups: more common with crush injuries; casts put on too tight; swelling or bleeding begins. With a cast, closely evaluate swelling developing within the cast.","Mechanism: increased pressure within a limited anatomic space compresses internal structures — blood vessels (decreased circulation, necrosis) and nerves (pain, numbness). A tourniquet effect: edema at the fracture puts intense pressure on …","Extreme pain out of proportion to the injury — E.g., stabilized, then begins extreme pain again","Weak or lost palpable pulses — May need to Doppler","Edema — A lot; not pitting edema; typically happens very quickly","Treatment is a fasciotomy: the surgeon cuts into the muscle space and releases edema and swelling; the area is packed and left so edema can ooze out. Can be done at the bedside or in the ED.","Fat Embolism","Rare but potentially catastrophic. A fat molecule in the lung after a long bone fracture (the legs most common), usually with a large trauma — a type of pulmonary embolism. Fat from bone marrow or traumatized tissue enters the bloodstream a…","Very acute onset like a PE: the patient may be fine one minute and in respiratory arrest the next.","Hypoxemia — Low O2","Altered level of consciousness — Or completely decreased LOC","Petechial rash — Often the last symptom; from occlusion of dermal capillaries by fat globules with extravasation of erythrocytes","Treatment is supportive; the embolism is self-limiting and will be absorbed if the person is stabilized and supported until then. A ventilator may sustain a patient if small enough; may need ECMO (extracorporeal membrane oxygenation) — the …","Osteomyelitis","-itis = inflammation, osteo = bone, myelo = marrow cavity. A severe infection of the bone and local tissue requiring immediate treatment; acute or chronic, pus-producing; typically from skin bacteria, Staphylococcus aureus. Bacteria get int…","Who is most likely","Very recent trauma","Open (compound) fractures — higher risk than closed","Diabetes","Hemodialysis","IV drug use","Splenectomy","Routes of contamination","Direct: outside bacteria via open wound — open fracture, gunshot, puncture, surgery (kind of uncommon)","Indirect: commonly seen; a bloodstream infection goes to a bone and prevents healing (e.g., sternotomy patient with a bloodstream infection, probably from an IV line, that reached the sternal area)","Why it is so problematic: pressure rises within the bone → local arteries collapse → decreased oxygen, nutrients, and immune cells → very difficult to get antibiotics there → impaired healing and hard to treat.","Local signs","Tenderness, warmth, redness (why you always monitor those areas)","Infected wound drainage","More restricted movement than expected, or loss of prior mobility","From a bacteremia: possible spontaneous fracture","Systemic (common)","Fever","Positive blood culture","Increased white blood cells","1. Culture — Blood culture to see which antibiotics apply","2. Empiric start — Common to start with nafcillin and vancomycin","3. Narrow — Switch to an antibiotic specific for the bacteria once culture results return","4. Duration — IV for a long time: four to six weeks; if quickly controlled, may do two weeks IV and two weeks oral (often depends on severity)","Severe cases — Blood flow struggles to carry antibiotic to the site — the instructor has seen 24-hour antibiotic infusions in the hospital","Surgical / local — Wound reopened with antibiotics infused into the area; or open the patient, wash out with antibiotics, close, return a week later and repeat","Osteomyelitis can be cured, but it is difficult and requires long-standing treatment — typically weeks of IV antibiotics. The instructor stresses it is something to avoid at all costs."]},{"s":"Part 9 — Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease▾","a":"part-9-osteoarthritis-degenerative-disc-disease","k":["Part 9 — Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease▾","Most joint disorders (arthritis included) affect synovial joints; this part reviews the synovial joint, then covers osteoarthritis (who, which joints, step-by-step pathogenesis, manifestations, management) and degenerative disc disease, whi…","Synovial Joint Review & Terminology","Synovial joints are typically movable. Parts: outer fibrous (joint) capsule connecting the bone and the synovial membrane; inner synovial membrane (lining); articular cartilage (prevents friction between bones, a protective coating); synovi…","Smooth cartilage plus lubricating fluid let bones come together and move easily — \"unfortunately, sometimes we lose those things.\"","Arthropathy = a joint disorder. When it involves inflammation of one or more joints it becomes arthritis. Multiple types; OA first, RA next.","Osteoarthritis (OA): Definition & Statistics","OA is degeneration of joints caused by aging and stress — wear and tear over time leading to inflammation that is not systemic, just localized to the affected joint(s). It is a chronic disease process.","Disability — Most common cause of disability in the US (also stated as the leading cause of chronic disability); most common form of arthritis","Rising incidence — Obesity and longer life expectancy (age is a big risk factor)","Projection — By 2040, about 78 million Americans likely affected","Sex difference — Affects men before age 45 more than women; more common in women after 45","Consequence — With obesity rising, expect more joint replacements for OA","Joints typically affected","Back — starting in the cervical spine (neck) or lumbar-sacral spine (lower back)","Hip","Knee","Hands","Big toe","Joints typically spared","Wrists","Elbows","Ankles","Risk Factors","Degenerative changes begin in the articular cartilage from excessive loading of a healthy joint or normal loading of a previously disturbed (injured) joint.","Aging — \"A big one\"; articular cartilage loss starts over age 40; older = more at risk","Obesity — Increases load and biomechanical stress on hips and knees; only 10 pounds overweight increases force on the knee by about 30 to 60 pounds a step","Team sports — Longer participation and overuse wear and tear (soccer once at age 12 does not mean OA); athletes commonly get OA of knees or hips from injury","Occupational work — Any repetitive work; trauma, overuse, heavy occupational work — gardeners, construction workers, anyone working outside, heavy lifting, factory work","Misalignment — Pelvis, hip, knee, ankle, or foot; women have built-in misalignment with wider hips; one leg shorter alters weight balance; shifting to compensate for a painful joint increases pressure on other joints","Trauma / injury — History of trauma and overuse; previously injured joint under normal loading can degenerate","Pathogenesis: Step by Step","1. Cartilage wear — Pressure slowly wears the cartilage surface, exposing the subchondral bone","2. Subchondral cysts — Cysts develop in the subchondral area; articular cartilage erodes from the cysts going through it","3. Repair attempt — Chondrocytes synthesize a fluid called proteoglycans to repair the cartilage","4. Swelling — This excess fluid (supposed to help) causes joint swelling","5. Inflammation feeds degeneration — Localized inflammation further degrades cartilage; cysts move through the remaining cartilage, causing more inflammation and degeneration","6. Osteoblast activation — Osteoblasts (bone builders) are activated and build bone spurs trying to fix what is going on","7. Further inflammation — Remodeling leads to more inflammatory responses and synovial fluid thickening (as cytokines/inflammation occur) → decreased movement","8. Bone on bone — Cartilage loss narrows the joint space; synovial fluid lost or thick and \"non-purposeful\"; bone spurs; bone on bone","Osteophytes (bone spurs) are an important hallmark of OA, caused by osteoblasts; the remodeling is \"not appropriate.\" Seen on x-ray; they can break off into the synovial cavity and cause a lot of problems.","Manifestations","Pain — Deep, aching, especially with exertion, relieved with rest","Weather — Worse with cold weather; better in drier climates","Stiffness — Starts with stiffness but feels better once you get moving","Crepitus — May occur with movement","Altered gait — Shifts to minimize pain, which can cause further problems","Swelling / ROM — Swelling from proteoglycan fluid; limited range of motion","Deformity / tenderness — Depends on bone spurs, bone on bone, and swelling","Heberden's nodes (hands)","The distal ones","Bouchard's nodes (hands)","The proximal ones, closer to the hand","Management of OA","Prevention is the goal (decrease risk factors before it is too severe). Once present: manage pain, maintain mobility, minimize disability; reduce pain and swelling; refer to physical therapy for exercises that keep joints flexible.","Beyond medication: may need to replace the joint; artificial joint fluid; devices (canes, walkers) and lifestyle interventions. Other options are covered in med-surg (the instructor says 327 used to be med-surg, so this will be 334 or 405).","No drug prevents progression, prevents damage, or reverses the course of arthritis — so the focus is pain management and disability. Review your NSAIDs and know their risks (go back to the pain lecture from Nursing 325).","Mild to moderate — Acetaminophen (Tylenol) — Usual starting point","Mild to moderate — Over-the-counter NSAIDs — Next step; stronger NSAIDs may also be prescribed","Topical — Capsaicin — From hot chili peppers; numbs the top of the area","Moderate to severe — Prescription-strength NSAIDs; may add colchicine — If there is an inflammatory component, \"we'll address that later\"","Further escalation — Tramadol, opioids, steroid injections into the joint — May be added/eventually used","Degenerative Disc Disease (DDD)","The same process as OA but specific to the spinal column; the treatment goal is the same as OA.","Locations — Lumbar (L4, L5, S1) or cervical spine — the two commons","Typical cause — Lifting or twisting motions; \"nurses are notorious for not protecting their back\"","Frequency — Back pain/DDD is the second most common reason people visit the doctor; the most common cause of work-related disability under age 45","Sex / age — Women and men equally; intervertebral disc compression occurs with age","Mechanism: discs lose the \"nice bouncy\" cushion and the vertebrae end up on one another; nerves become compressed — this impingement causes pain, decreased movement, and decreased sensation. Depending on location: pain in one leg, one side …","Lumbar spine","Lower back pain radiating down the back of the leg or buttocks — often called sciatica","Worse sitting, bending, lifting, twisting","Better walking, changing positions, lying down","Numbness, tingling, weakness down the leg; can cause foot drop","Cervical spine","Pain can radiate down the shoulders and arms","Numbness or tingling in the arm or hand","Weakness in the arm or hand"]},{"s":"Part 10 — Rheumatoid Arthritis▾","a":"part-10-rheumatoid-arthritis","k":["Part 10 — Rheumatoid Arthritis▾","RA differs from OA pathophysiologically, pharmacologically, and in treatment — you must know the distinct differences; the biggest is that RA is a systemic autoimmune disease rather than a localized one. This part covers etiology, pathophys…","What RA Is, Etiology & Risk Factors","Systemic, autoimmune — the body attacks the synovial tissue in the joints; an inflammatory disease of the synovium that destroys the joint area.","Classified as a type 3 hypersensitivity (go back to the hypersensitivity lecture if unfamiliar).","Cause not well understood despite intensive research; thought to involve an environmental factor and a genetic factor — a genetic link and a triggering event. You can be genetically predisposed without developing it. Environmental factors: …","Genetic link — Significant, paired with a triggering event; family history is pretty significant","Age — Most diagnoses between 40 and 60 (OA is the age-related one; RA is middle age); some are diagnosed even earlier. This is a very active time of life (mid-to-late career, children growing up), so it interferes with lifestyle","Sex — Women have a 2.5 times higher risk than men","Tobacco use — Linked to RA; possibly a trigger or involves infectious agents from the trigger","Pathophysiology & Diagnosis","Immune (autoimmune) complexes deposit in the synovial tissue and immune cells attack it (we do not know why they target the synovium).","Activated cells are typically B cells (lymphocytes) and macrophages, which secrete cytokines that attract more white blood cells.","They produce RF (rheumatoid factor) — a special antibody against our own IgG antibodies.","During phagocytosis, large quantities of lysosomal enzymes are released and further damage surrounding tissue. The process is chronic, persistent, and destructive; the intense inflammation destroys cartilage.","As destruction progresses, scar tissue develops — in RA joints called pannus: very vascularized (all scar tissue is, trying to bring in nutrients to heal); made of many inflammatory cells (T cells, macrophages, others); because it contains …","Rheumatoid factor (RF) — Measured in serum; how diagnosis is confirmed along with symptoms and physical assessment","CRP or sed rate — Inflammatory lab levels that can also be drawn","Manifestations","Early: not a ton of obvious symptoms (maybe joint pain/discomfort); vague systemic symptoms — fatigue, anorexia, weight loss, generalized stiffness.","Later: symmetrical joint involvement — heat, swelling, tenderness, stiffness. Stiffness starts in the morning and lasts longer than an hour. Swelling feels spongy, soft, and warm (OA swelling is hard — like a sprained, swollen ankle). OA is…","Subluxation (with muscle atrophy and tendon destruction): bone displaced from its normal position, partially losing contact with the articulating surface (a dislocation is a complete loss of contact; subluxation is \"just not aligned perfect…","Deformity: no Heberden's or Bouchard's nodes (those are OA). Instead a swan deformity — not bony growths but actual curving of the joint from inflammation, in more advanced stages along with subluxations.","Systemic Involvement","RA can affect any and all body systems depending on severity; on physical assessment assess all systems, particularly the heart. The instructor says you do not need to memorize all of these, but understand the systemic involvement as a whol…","Fatigue / malaise — Exceeding tiredness","Heart — Pericarditis or myocarditis","Lungs — Nodules on the lungs; monitored occasionally by x-ray or CT","Sjogren's syndrome — Destruction of moisture-producing glands (lacrimal and salivary): dry mouth, dry itchy eyes (dry tongue shown in the picture)","Rheumatoid nodules — Immune-mediated granulomas around inflamed joints; subcutaneous, firm, sometimes painful; about 25% of RA patients","Management Overview","Like OA, goals include relieving pain and swelling. Difference: in OA nothing can really stop or slow progression beyond encouraging weight-bearing activity and proper joint movement; in RA there are things that actually slow the disease (m…","NSAIDs — Immediate symptom relief; review NSAID concerns","Glucocorticoids (prednisone, hydrocortisone) — Short-term management of severe/acute flares; review short- and long-term steroid concerns","DMARDs — The group added to the drug matrix; two older ones covered: methotrexate and hydroxychloroquine (much newer DMARDs exist, often seen in TV commercials)","Biologics — Newer; be aware, not on the drug matrix","Glucocorticoids: prednisone is usually the steroid of choice; rapid suppression of joint inflammation; used only when inflammation is not controlled with an NSAID; lots of risk long term so the team tries hard not to use them; if longer ter…","DMARD 1: Methotrexate","One of the first DMARDs and still a first-line agent for RA in most patients; many side effects but it helps. Put it on your drug matrix (with hydroxychloroquine).","Methotrexate is given ONCE PER WEEK, never daily. A huge safety issue — death can occur if given daily instead of weekly. Teach the patient.","Mechanism — Interferes with folate metabolism; immunosuppressive; a chemo drug that tells the immune system to stop attacking us","Routes / other use — PO; some sub-Q or IV; in smaller doses used for cancer therapy","Folate — Patients always take a folic acid supplement","Side effects — GI: nausea, anorexia, vomiting","Liver — Metabolized by and toxic to the liver; monitor AST, ALT, alk phos","Bone marrow — Bone marrow suppression (a chemo drug); monitor WBC and RBC (CBC)","Black box — 11 black box warnings — some of the FDA's strongest warnings","Alcohol — No drinking or alcohol (liver toxicity)","Pregnancy — Teratogenic — two forms of birth control (like some acne drugs)","Infection — Very high risk; if signs of infection, see the doctor ASAP","Contraindications — Pre-existing liver or kidney conditions","NSAIDs — As stated in the lecture, an aplastic anemia risk using NSAIDs; these patients commonly use NSAIDs, so monitor WBC/CBC","Life expectancy teaching point: people on methotrexate are shown to have a shortened life expectancy from cardiovascular disease, cancer, and other conditions — monitor risks versus benefits.","Yet it does help: slows progression, lets patients function in daily life and live fairly normally. We are getting better (changed dosing, closer monitoring, understanding side effects).","DMARD 2: Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil)","Not used as frequently for RA; actually an antimalarial (heard about during COVID).","Anti-inflammatory: dampens macrophage, T-helper cell, and inflammatory cytokine activity; can slow RA progression, especially combined with other DMARDs.","Much better tolerated than methotrexate, fewer side effects.","About 1% chance of retinopathy (rare) — can lead to blindness. Teach: report blurry vision or any vision problems.","Biologic Response Modifiers (Biologics)","Newer drugs for really all autoimmune conditions; target the part of the immune system that triggers inflammation and joint damage — tell the immune system to \"stop acting crazy.\"","Newer, so less known about long-term effects. Known risks: severe skin or lung infections, skin cancers, serious allergic reactions.","Do not put any on the drug matrix or learn names (you may recognize them from commercials); very expensive but work pretty well; be aware if you work with these patients.","RA vs OA Side by Side","Scope — Localized — Systemic","Process — Wear and tear — Autoimmune; type 3 hypersensitivity; attacks synovial tissue","Age — Increases with age — Most diagnoses 40 to 60","Distribution — Wear and tear on a unilateral joint — Symmetrical","Swelling — Hard (sprained ankle) — Spongy, soft, warm","Joint changes — Heberden's and Bouchard's nodes (bony growths) — No nodes; swan deformity (inflammatory curving, not bony); subluxations","Slow progression? — Not really (weight-bearing activity, proper joint movement) — Yes — DMARDs, biologics"]},{"s":"Part 11 — Gout▾","a":"part-11-gout","k":["Part 11 — Gout▾","Gout is a disruption in uric acid metabolism with urate salt deposits in and around joints; this part covers gout versus gouty arthritis, purine and hyperuricemia, risk factors, the phases, manifestations, complications, and the drugs (NSAI…","What Gout Is","Gout","The metabolic problem itself","A disruption in uric acid metabolism","Urate salts deposited in tissues, generally in and around joints","Gouty arthritis","How gout affects the joint — inflammation of the joint","Acute and very painful","Uric acid crystal deposits in the joint","\"The disease of kings\": traditionally from foods associated with the wealthy — smoked meats, cheeses, red wine, beer. Many old rich kings had it; now many different people get it.","Purine, Uric Acid & Hyperuricemia","Crystals form from the breakdown of purine — a nitrogen-containing compound, the parent compound of nitrogenous waste. Purine breaks down into uric acid, excreted in urine by the kidneys. Normally uric acid dissolves in blood and is excrete…","High-purine foods/drinks named: alcohol (beer, red wine), organ meat (liver, kidney, brain), red meat, seafood such as scallops.","Hyperuricemia is usually from overproduction of uric acid (allopurinol's target) and underexcretion by the kidneys (probenecid inhibits reabsorption).","Risk Factors","Sex — Mostly men","Obesity — Now occurs across all sociodemographics","Preexisting disease — Hypertension, diabetes, renal disease, sickle cell anemia","Alcohol — Commonly consuming, especially alcohol use disorders","High-purine diet — Diets high in purine","Diuretics — Often associated with gout","Race — Black or African American people have higher risk (instructor corrected \"ethnicity\" to \"race\")","Changing stereotype: only the rich used to be able to eat high-purine foods, so the picture was a wealthy, overweight male; now obesity is across sociodemographics, so gout is a disease of anyone with this kind of diet.","Phases of Gout","1. Asymptomatic — No symptoms but elevated serum uric acid; crystals visible if tissue is tested; they accumulate, tissue is damaged, causing acute inflammation","2. Acute flare (attack) — Acute inflammation becomes a flare; very high level of hyperuricemia","3. Clinically inactive — Still hyperuricemia but no inflammation; may flare again in months or years","4. Chronic gouty arthritis — With age and progression, attacks recur closer and closer together; joint pain and symptoms present most of the time","Manifestations & Complications","Pain is the hallmark sign; can be excruciating. Burning pain, redness, swelling, warmth of the joint; fever; symptoms typically last days to weeks; difficult to walk; attacks can recur.","Most common joint: the big toe (large metatarsophalangeal/MTP joint) — about 50% of people with gout. Can occur in other joints, but feet and big toe are very common.","Pain can be so intense patients cannot put on socks and their feet cannot touch the bedsheets (the instructor saw a patient whose toe could not even have one thin hospital sheet over it).","Nursing approach: treat symptoms; teach patients to stop drinking and stop the high-purine diet, hoping they can control it.","Tophi: large, hard nodules of uric acid crystal deposits in soft tissue, below the skin around joints; can cause local inflammatory response; may drain chalky material if drained.","Kidney stones: gout patients are at high risk (gout was also mentioned in the renal disorders lecture).","Drug Therapy","NSAIDs are typically the drug of choice for acute attacks (anti-inflammatory, reduce symptoms).","Steroids only for patients who cannot take NSAIDs or whose gout is not responding.","Goals of gout-specific drugs: decrease symptoms of an acute attack and prevent recurrence. Three drugs: allopurinol, colchicine, probenecid.","Drug matrix: the instructor says all three — allopurinol, colchicine, probenecid — must be on your drug matrix.","Role — Prophylaxis only; not for acute attacks; taken long term; for gout from excess uric acid production (hyperuricemia) — Oldest therapy but second-line; usually for flares (short term); prophylaxis if allopurinol not tolerated — Uricosu…","Mechanism — Xanthine oxidase inhibitor (the enzyme needed to convert purine to uric acid) — prevents uric acid production — Inhibitor of cell mitosis; reduces inflammatory response to urate crystals; thought to inhibit metabolism, mobility,…","Adverse effects — Well tolerated; most common rash; agranulocytosis and aplastic anemia (worry about infection); Stevens-Johnson syndrome → TEN — Short-term leukopenia and bone marrow suppression; GI bleeding, disrupted cell mitosis in the …","Interactions — Increases effects of hypoglycemic agents (metformin, SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 agonists) and warfarin — Not discussed — Lots; monitor","Onset — 2 to 6 weeks; flare starts dying down, continue to prevent future flares — Not discussed — Not discussed","Monitoring / teaching — Serum uric acid (effectiveness); WBC/CBC; blood sugar closely if on hypoglycemics; INR closely if on warfarin — PO only; watch for GI symptoms/persistent vomiting; stop immediately if GI toxicity — Take with food, dr…","Allopurinol skin and blood risks: rash is most common, but it is known to cause Stevens-Johnson syndrome, which can turn into toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) — a fatal skin condition often considered like an anaphylactic reaction. Agranulo…","Colchicine toxicity — stop immediately: stomach-flu-type symptoms are an early sign of toxicity; persistent vomiting is a major concern. If not stopped, multiple organ failure and death. The instructor also lists severe renal, GI, hepatic, …","NSAIDs — Drug of choice for acute attacks","Steroids — Only if NSAIDs cannot be taken or not working","Allopurinol — Prevention only; blocks uric acid production","Colchicine — Oldest, second-line; reduces inflammation to crystals; flares","Probenecid — Uricosuric; inhibits reabsorption of uric acid"]},{"s":"Part 12 — Systemic Lupus Erythematosus▾","a":"part-12-systemic-lupus-erythematosus","k":["Part 12 — Systemic Lupus Erythematosus▾","Lupus (SLE) is an abnormal immune reaction against the body's own cells — a complex autoimmune disease in which autoantibodies form immune complexes that land in almost any organ; this part covers its mechanism, risk factors, manifestations…","What Lupus Is & How It Damages the Body","Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is the main type (what people mean by \"lupus\"; the terms are interchangeable). A type affecting only the skin exists but is only named.","Production of autoantibodies; the major one is ANA (anti-nuclear antibody), which can be drawn from plasma.","Chain: autoantibodies (ANA) are activated against the body's own DNA and form immune complexes → circulate around the body → land in an organ (any major system: heart, skin, kidneys, brain) → trigger an inflammatory response → destroy the t…","RA vs lupus complexes: in RA, complexes deposit only in the synovial fluid; in lupus they can deposit anywhere because there is DNA everywhere. The most common area is the kidney — the glomeruli (important and pretty fragile).","Risk Factors","Genetics — Definitely an inherited component","Sex / age / race — Females much more likely than males; typically ages 20 to 40; most commonly Black and African American people","Sun exposure — Environmental trigger of flare-ups; can also be predisposing","Hormones — Significant influence on immunity; \"definitely some kind of hormonal interplay\"","Estrogen-containing oral contraceptives — 50% higher risk","Early menarche — Menstruating before age 10 = double the risk","Smoking — Releases multiple toxins; essentially a risk factor for everything","Antibiotic allergies — Thought to be related to having a lot of allergies to certain antibiotics","Manifestations","Fatigue — The most common symptom; extreme, debilitating","Photosensitivity — Sensitivity to light and sunshine","Butterfly rash — Almost one of the hallmark signs; bilateral, red, irritating rash across the face like butterfly wings","Edema / fever / hair loss — Edema usually in the legs or around the eyes; fever; unusual hair loss","Raynaud's-type changes — Distal small capillaries constrict and reduce blood flow; very painful; usually triggered by cold; treated with calcium channel blockers","The manifestation list is huge because lupus can affect every organ system:","Brain — Headache, dizziness, seizures, stroke","Lungs — Pleuritis, pleural effusion","Heart — Myocarditis, endocarditis","Kidneys — About half of patients develop kidney complications; permanent damage, dialysis or transplant","Blood vessels — Vasculitis","Blood — Anemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia","Joints — Arthritis","Kidney involvement is the big one: the most common site of immune-complex deposition (glomeruli), about half of patients, with possible permanent damage requiring dialysis or transplant. The instructor's example: Selena Gomez needed a kidne…","Course, Prognosis & Flares","Poor prognosis: severe multi-system involvement (more than one organ). Good prognosis: the majority have mild disease, with good survival with close monitoring and treatment. Systems are usually not all affected at once.","Clinical course: exacerbations and remissions — flares, similar to gout.","Flare warning signs: more severe fatigue, pain, headache; generally more symptomatic with systemic symptoms. Triggers to avoid: sunlight exposure, infection, abruptly stopping a medicine, stress. Key point: recognize warning signs and avoid…","Pharmacotherapy","The goal is to control symptoms; the drug depends on which systems are involved.","NSAIDs — Headache, musculoskeletal symptoms, pleuritis, pericarditis","Steroids — Often needed; high dose for severe kidney disease or a lot of CNS involvement; low dose for something like arthritis","Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil) — Discussed with RA; helps with skin and musculoskeletal problems and can prevent kidney and CNS organ damage; you will see a lot of lupus patients on it","Biologics and immunosuppressives — Multi-organ involvement or concern about progression","RA vs. Lupus","Many similarities; a worksheet on Canvas helps distinguish the two. The difference to hold onto: in RA it is really only the joints (plus systemic fatigue and malaise); in lupus any kind of organ can be involved.","Autoimmune / systemic — Yes / yes — Yes / yes","Immune complex deposits — Only in synovial fluid — Anywhere (DNA everywhere); kidney glomeruli most common","Main area involved — Really only the joints — Any organ","Fatigue / malaise — Yes — Yes — fatigue is the most common symptom","Drug overlap — Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil) was discussed with RA — Comes up again for lupus","Wrap-up of the MSK block: most of it is pretty straightforward and the pharmacotherapy is not too hard — review the NSAID and steroid drug matrices and understand the differences between them."]},{"s":"Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","a":"whole-week-quick-reference","k":["Whole-Week Quick Reference▾","One must-recognize or emergency point from each of the twelve parts of Week 7, for a last-minute scan before the exam.","Part 1 — Cancer Fundamentals — Benign vs. malignant is starred — and a benign tumor can still be lethal if it grows large enough to mechanically interrupt a critical organ (brain, lung). Know what a given TNM or stage classification means f…","Part 2 — Metastasis & Etiology — Spread is lymphatic or vascular; vascular first stops at the liver (portal vein). Know the common pairs (lung → bone/brain; colon → liver; breast → bone/brain/liver/lung; prostate → vertebrae; melanoma → bra…","Part 3 — Lung Cancer — Leading cause of cancer death in the US and Kentucky because it is diagnosed late (5-year survival over 60% at stage 1 vs. below 10% at stage 4); screen with low-dose CT. Radon plus smoking is a \"very dangerous combin…","Part 4 — Breast, Cervical & Colorectal Cancer — Cervical cancer is preventable: almost 100% of cases are HPV-positive and the HPV vaccine is 97% effective (best at age 11-12, up to 45). BRCA1/BRCA2 mutations raise breast, ovarian, colon, an…","Part 5 — Chemotherapy Principles — The nadir (about 7-14 days) is the time of highest risk for infection, anemia, and bleeding. Extravasation is a very serious IV complication (use a portacath or deep tunneled line). Levofloxacin prophylaxi…","Part 6 — Antineoplastic Drug Classes — Know the class, expect its toxicity: alkylating (cyclophosphamide) = hemorrhagic cystitis; methotrexate = category X; doxorubicin = cardiotoxicity (maximum lifetime dose, CHF, monitor EF/echo); vincris…","Part 7 — Osteoporosis — T-score below −2.5 = osteoporosis; add a fragility fracture for \"severe.\" Hip fracture raises death risk 2.5-4x (deaths from immobility complications). Alendronate: stay upright, one sip of water, once weekly (esopha…","Part 8 — Fractures & Osteomyelitis — Compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion, weak/lost pulses, non-pitting edema (watch casts) — treated with fasciotomy. Fat embolism after a long bone fracture: hypoxemia, altered LOC, petechial rash …","Part 9 — Osteoarthritis & Degenerative Disc Disease — No drug prevents progression or reverses OA — manage pain, maintain mobility, minimize disability; know NSAID risks. Bone spurs (osteophytes) are the hallmark and can break off into the …","Part 10 — Rheumatoid Arthritis — Methotrexate is ONCE PER WEEK, never daily (death can occur); always give folic acid; no alcohol; teratogenic (two forms of birth control); monitor liver enzymes and CBC. Hydroxychloroquine: report vision ch…","Part 11 — Gout — Allopurinol (prevention only, xanthine oxidase inhibitor): rash can progress to Stevens-Johnson syndrome/TEN; monitor WBC/CBC, glucose with hypoglycemics, and INR with warfarin. Colchicine: persistent vomiting or stomach-fl…","Part 12 — Systemic Lupus Erythematosus — Kidney (glomerular) involvement is the big one — about half of patients, possible dialysis or transplant. Hallmarks: fatigue (most common) and butterfly rash. Flare warning signs: worse fatigue, pain…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer. Four cards from each of the twelve parts above — a whole-week spot check."]}]},{"f":"review-pharm-foundations.html","t":"Pharmacology Foundations","secs":[{"s":"Pharmacology Foundations","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Intro to Pharmacology — Definitions, Naming & Regulation","Pharmacokinetics — Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism & Excretion","Pharmacokinetics — Metabolism, Excretion & Half-Life","Pharmacodynamics & Drug Safety","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: pharmacology definitions and drug naming/regulation, pharmacokinetics (absorption through excretion, CYP450, half-life), pharmacodynamics, and medication safety."]},{"s":"Intro to Pharmacology — Definitions, Naming & Regulation▾","a":"intro-to-pharmacology-definitions-naming-regulation","k":["Intro to Pharmacology — Definitions, Naming & Regulation▾","Pharmacology — The study of drugs/chemicals/medications and the changes they cause in the body.","Pharmacokinetics — What the body does to the drug (absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion).","Pharmacodynamics — What the drug does to the body — its mechanism of action and overall effects.","Chemical / Generic / Trade Name — Chemical name: long, complex, used in research (e.g., acetylparaminophenol). Generic name: the one official name, lowercase, used on exams and NCLEX (e.g., acetaminophen). Trade name: the capitalized brand …","Prototype Drug — The first drug in a class, used as the comparison point for effectiveness/side effects of newer drugs in that class (e.g., ibuprofen/Advil is the NSAID prototype).","Therapeutic Effect — The intended effect of the drug (e.g., lowering blood pressure).","Side Effect — An unintended effect of a normal dose.","Toxicity / Adverse Effect — Toxicity: a harmful effect. Adverse effect: an unexpected, dangerous reaction. (Used interchangeably with \"side effect\" at this course level.)","Allergic Reaction — An unexpected, potentially dangerous immune system response — ranges from itching/hives to anaphylaxis.","How New Drugs Are Approved (FDA)","Preclinical: tested on lab animals for therapeutic/adverse effects.","Phase 1: healthy human volunteers — checking for major adverse reactions.","Phase 2: patients with the target disease — identifying rarer/less frequent side effects; the drug may be pulled here if effects are too dangerous.","Phase 3: a vast clinical market — unexpected responses may still lead to withdrawal.","Phase 4: post-market, ongoing FDA monitoring. If a pattern of severe reactions emerges, the FDA can require a black box warning — the strictest FDA warning a drug can carry while staying on the market.","Schedule I — No accepted medical use, very high abuse potential (e.g., heroin, LSD, ecstasy).","Schedule II — High abuse potential but some medical use; severe psychological/physical dependence risk (e.g., opioids, amphetamines). No automatic refills — each fill needs a new prescription.","Schedule III — Moderate-to-low potential for dependence (e.g., ketamine, anabolic steroids).","Schedule IV — Low potential for abuse/dependence (e.g., Xanax, Valium, Ambien).","Schedule V — Very low abuse potential — small amounts of narcotics/stimulants (e.g., cough syrup with a small amount of codeine).","To be sold over the counter (OTC), a drug must be safe (low risk of side effects/abuse) and the consumer must be able to diagnose the condition and monitor effectiveness themselves. Some OTC drugs (e.g., pseudoephedrine — a meth precursor) …","Dietary/Herbal Supplements, Teratogens & Pharmacogenomics","Dietary/herbal supplements can only claim an effect on body structure or function (not that they treat a medical condition) and aren't FDA-evaluated for safety/efficacy before marketing. They can still interact with medications — e.g., gink…","Teratogens (e.g., alcohol, marijuana, nicotine) cause congenital malformations. The FDA's old pregnancy risk categories (A = safest, through X = contraindicated, known fetal risk) have been replaced since 2015 by the Pregnancy and Lactation…","Pharmacogenomics — studying how a person's genes affect their drug response, to tailor medication choice/dosing (already used in some cancer and psychiatric drug selection)."]},{"s":"Pharmacokinetics — Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism & Excretion▾","a":"pharmacokinetics-absorption-distribution-metabolism-excretion","k":["Pharmacokinetics — Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism & Excretion▾","Enteral — Via the GI tract — oral, sublingual, buccal, rectal.","Parenteral — Outside the GI tract — typically injection (intradermal, subcutaneous, intramuscular, intrathecal, intra-articular, intravenous).","Topical — Applied to body surfaces — ointments, creams, pastes, powders, transdermal patches.","Three phases of drug activity: pharmaceutical (the drug dissolves — disintegration breaks a tablet into particles, dissolution dissolves those particles into GI fluid so they can be absorbed), pharmacokinetic (absorption → distribution → me…","Bioavailability — The percentage of an administered drug that reaches systemic circulation in active form. IV drugs: 100% (no absorption barrier). Oral/GI drugs: less than 100%, due to the first-pass effect.","First-Pass Effect — An orally absorbed drug must pass through the liver before reaching systemic circulation, and the liver metabolizes a portion of it to an inactive form — reducing bioavailability. This is why oral doses (e.g., oral morph…","Distribution — Movement of the drug from blood to its site of action — best in highly vascularized areas (heart, liver, kidneys, brain); slower in muscle, skin, and fat, and impaired by conditions like peripheral vascular disease or a poorl…","Blood-Brain Barrier — Tight capillary junctions in the brain that block most drugs — only lipid-soluble drugs or ones with a transport system can cross (e.g., alcohol, glucose). Not fully developed in infants, so extra caution is needed wit…","Protein Binding — Drugs travel partly bound to plasma proteins (e.g., albumin) and partly free/unbound — only the unbound drug is active. Low albumin (malnutrition, liver disease, alcoholism) means more free drug and a higher risk of toxici…"]},{"s":"Pharmacokinetics — Metabolism, Excretion & Half-Life▾","a":"pharmacokinetics-metabolism-excretion-half-life","k":["Pharmacokinetics — Metabolism, Excretion & Half-Life▾","Metabolism (Biotransformation) — Chemically alters the drug into a metabolite — from active/lipid-soluble to inactive/water-soluble, ready for excretion. The liver is the main organ responsible.","CYP450 System — The liver's main drug-metabolizing enzyme system. A drug is a substrate if it's metabolized by a CYP450 enzyme. A CYP450 inducer speeds up metabolism of substrate drugs (less drug in the body, lower therapeutic effect). A CY…","Excretion — Elimination of the drug (mostly via the kidneys, through glomerular filtration, tubular secretion, and reabsorption). Kidney function directly affects how well a drug is excreted.","Half-Life (T½) — The time it takes for serum drug concentration to fall by 50%. After about 5 half-lives, ~97% of the drug is eliminated. It also takes about 4–5 half-lives to reach steady state — where the amount of drug eliminated equals …","Half-life guides dosing intervals. With a drug like morphine (half-life ~2–4 hours), levels drop meaningfully between doses — around-the-clock dosing (a scheduled baseline dose plus PRN doses for breakthrough symptoms) is used, especially f…"]},{"s":"Pharmacodynamics & Drug Safety▾","a":"pharmacodynamics-drug-safety","k":["Pharmacodynamics & Drug Safety▾","Receptor — A reactive site on/inside a cell (usually a protein) that a drug binds to, forming a drug-receptor complex that stimulates or inhibits a response.","Agonist — Binds a receptor and initiates the desired therapeutic effect (e.g., a beta-1 adrenergic agonist causing vasodilation).","Antagonist — Blocks/inhibits a natural substance from binding and acting (e.g., diphenhydramine/Benadryl blocking histamine at the H1 receptor).","Receptor-less Activation — Some drugs act through direct chemical interaction rather than a receptor (e.g., antacids neutralizing stomach acid, or magnesium sulfate acting as a laxative via an osmotic effect).","Therapeutic Index — The ratio of a drug's toxic level to its therapeutic level. A narrow therapeutic index (e.g., warfarin, digoxin) means a small margin between helpful and toxic — needs closer monitoring. A wide/high index (e.g., amoxicil…","Drug Interactions","Additive effect: two drugs with a similar mechanism of action are given together on purpose, for a stronger combined effect (e.g., two antibiotics for a complicated infection).","Synergism/potentiation: two drugs with different mechanisms combine for an effect greater than either alone (e.g., Coumadin plus aspirin).","Antidote: a drug given to antagonize another drug's toxic effects (e.g., naloxone/Narcan for opioid overdose).","Interactions can also decrease therapeutic effects — CYP450 inducers speeding up elimination, impaired GI absorption (e.g., after intestinal surgery), or activated charcoal intentionally blocking absorption.","Polypharmacy raises drug interaction risk — a special concern in older adults, who also have slower hepatic metabolism, reduced circulation/distribution, decreased GI absorption, and reduced renal excretion, raising risk for adverse reactio…","Medication safety practices: tall-man lettering for look-alike names (e.g., \"heparin\"), barcode scanning and automated dispensing (e.g., Pyxis), no trailing zeros (write \"1 mg,\" not \"1.0 mg\") and always a leading zero (\"0.25 mg,\" not \".25 m…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-cellular-adaptation-infection-immunity.html","t":"Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity","secs":[{"s":"Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","What Pathophysiology Is","Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors","Identifying, Describing & Staging Disease","Cellular Adaptation — Atrophy to Neoplasia","Necrosis & Gangrene","Inflammation Basics","Adaptive Immunity — B Cells, T Cells & Antibodies","Infection Basics — Microorganisms, Transmission & Portals of Entry","Stages of Infection & the Infectious Process","Diagnosing Infection & Healthcare-Associated Infections","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: pathophysiology basics, causes and staging of disease, cellular adaptation, necrosis/gangrene, inflammation, adaptive immunity, and infection."]},{"s":"What Pathophysiology Is▾","a":"what-pathophysiology-is","k":["What Pathophysiology Is▾","Breaking Down the Word","Physiology is normal functioning — how the body works when there aren't any problems, at the molecular, cellular, and whole-organ-system level.","Pathology is the study of disease and injury — what abnormal processes are happening to disrupt the body from normal functioning.","Pathophysiology combines the two: the mechanisms of disease — how and why changes in body structure and function happen, and how those changes produce the signs and symptoms of a given disease.","Disease is a disruption in homeostasis — \"homeo\" (same) + \"stasis\" (balance/stability). This disruption can be physical (e.g., heart failure — the heart can't pump adequately, so the body doesn't get the blood/oxygen it needs), mental (e.g.…","Temperature Rises — Blood vessels dilate (flushed/red skin) and sweat glands secrete fluid — both mechanisms lose heat to the environment, cooling the body back toward normal.","Temperature Drops — Blood vessels constrict (pale skin) and sweat glands stop producing fluid, conserving heat. Shivering — involuntary muscle contraction — generates additional heat."]},{"s":"Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors▾","a":"causes-of-disease-intrinsic-extrinsic-factors","k":["Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors▾","Intrinsic Factors — Come from inside the body: genetics/genetic mutations, immune status (immunocompromised patients are more susceptible to disease), age, sex, autoimmune disorders (the body attacking itself), and over- or under-production…","Extrinsic Factors — Come from outside the body: pathogens (bacteria, viruses, parasites), environmental exposures (toxins, pollutants), lifestyle/behaviors (diet, exercise, smoking, alcohol), and stressors that overwhelm the body's defenses…","The Epidemiologic Triangle (Epi-Triad)","Pathogen (agent): the causative factor responsible for disease — a bacteria, virus, or parasite.","Host: typically the human — susceptibility depends on genetics, age, and immune status.","Environment: the external conditions that influence how the pathogen and host interact (e.g., some bacteria can't survive heat, which is why certain foods are cooked to specific temperatures).","Disease risk emerges when all three components come together."]},{"s":"Identifying, Describing & Staging Disease▾","a":"identifying-describing-staging-disease","k":["Identifying, Describing & Staging Disease▾","Signs — Objective data — what can be seen, heard, or touched (e.g., wheezes on auscultation, a strong or weak pulse).","Symptoms — Subjective data — what the patient reports (e.g., pain, nausea, dizziness).","Diagnosis — Putting a name to the disease (e.g., pneumonia, heart failure, hypertension).","Etiology — The cause of the disease — not always known.","Prognosis — The expected disease course or outcome — the likelihood of returning to baseline.","Exposure — Contact with the agent or pathogen that sets off the disease cascade.","Sudden Onset — Signs/symptoms start quickly (e.g., a myocardial infarction, an asthma attack).","Insidious Onset — Slow and gradual — patients often adjust to symptoms until the disease has progressed significantly before it's caught (e.g., a slow GI bleed).","Latent Onset — The disease process is present but inactive, and can become active later (e.g., the chickenpox virus lying dormant before reactivating as shingles).","Prodromal — Early, nonspecific signs that a disease/condition is beginning (e.g., chills or a mild headache before a cold or flu fully sets in).","Remission / Convalescence — Remission: the disease is no longer active. Convalescence: recovering from the disease, back toward baseline.","Idiopathic — The cause of the disease is unknown (e.g., idiopathic cardiomyopathy).","Iatrogenic — Caused by the healthcare field itself — a treatment, drug, or action (or inaction) caused the disease (e.g., a colon perforation during a colonoscopy).","Exacerbation — A worsening of disease — often an acute decline in a chronic condition (e.g., an asthma attack triggered by allergen exposure).","Prefixes/suffixes worth knowing: hypo- (low) vs. hyper- (high); -penia (too few) vs. -cytosis (too many); -osis (a process/condition); -itis (inflammation, e.g. hepatitis); -pathy (disease, e.g. myopathy). Same root, opposite direction is a…"]},{"s":"Cellular Adaptation — Atrophy to Neoplasia▾","a":"cellular-adaptation-atrophy-to-neoplasia","k":["Cellular Adaptation — Atrophy to Neoplasia▾","Cells adapt to survive stress or injury. Some adaptations are normal/physiologic (muscle growth from exercise, uterine growth in pregnancy); others are pathologic, usually triggered by things like radiation, medications, or lack of oxygen.","Atrophy — Cells shrink in size; the whole organ can become atrophic. Physiologic atrophy is a developmental problem at birth (an organ/gland that never develops properly). Pathologic atrophy comes from decreased workload, poor blood supply,…","Hypertrophy — Cells get bigger (not more numerous), usually increasing function too. Typically from mechanical stimuli — repetitive stretching, chronic pressure, prolonged volume overload (e.g., quad muscle growth from repeated exercise is …","Hyperplasia — An increase in the number of cells (cell size stays the same) — only cells capable of dividing can do this (commonly skin, intestinal epithelium, glandular cells). Usually pathologic — most often cancers or abnormal hormonal s…","Dysplasia — Abnormal changes in the size, shape, or organization of mature cells — often follows atypical hyperplasia. Classified mild/moderate/severe, and is reversible (e.g., tobacco-related mouth/mucosal cell dysplasia can normalize afte…","Metaplasia — A reversible replacement of one mature cell type with another, less-differentiated type — related to chronic irritation/inflammation, and more strongly associated with cancer risk than dysplasia alone. It's an adaptation that h…","Neoplasia / Anaplasia — Cellular changes that no longer respond to normal regulatory processes — usually from a gene mutation. Anaplastic cells are cancer cells that have differentiated back to immature/embryonic forms; seeing \"anaplasia\" s…","Benign Tumors","Differentiated cells, less anaplastic (more mature).","Reproduce faster than normal cells, but cannot metastasize — stay contained and grow slowly.","Usually encapsulated.","Mainly a problem if located somewhere sensitive to compression, like the brain.","Malignant Tumors","Very anaplastic, non-differentiated, immature cells.","Reproduce rapidly with abnormal cells.","Metastasize — spread to other sites, near or distant, depending on proximity to lymphatic/blood systems.","Typically not encapsulated."]},{"s":"Necrosis & Gangrene▾","a":"necrosis-gangrene","k":["Necrosis & Gangrene▾","Necrosis — Cell death from disease, injury, or failure of blood supply — irreversible, with inflammation, swelling, redness, and cell bursting in the area.","Ischemic Necrosis — Necrosis from infarction and prolonged ischemia — an absence of blood supply to the area.","Liquefactive Necrosis — Dead cells/tissue are digested by enzymes and become liquefied, often forming an abscess with a liquid center. Typical in organs with a lot of lipids (lungs, brain) or where there's an abscess with heavy acute inflam…","Necrotic tissue becomes a breeding ground for bacteria — when bacterial invasion follows disruption of a major blood supply, that's gangrene. Most common in the lower extremities (toes, feet) of patients with poor circulation, such as those…","Dry Gangrene — Minimal bacteria, minimal inflammatory response. Tissue turns black, dry, and wrinkled. Spreads slowly (weeks to months); a clear demarcation line separates dead from healthy tissue.","Wet Gangrene — Liquefactive necrosis with extensive bacterial/WBC damage producing a liquid wound. Skin is moist, dark, cool, pulseless, and swollen, with blisters and a foul odor. No demarcation line; spreads much faster than dry gangrene …","Gas Gangrene — A specialized, severe form of wet gangrene caused by Clostridium perfringens (an anaerobic, spore-forming organism found in soil), typically from severe trauma or a compound fracture. Destroys connective tissue and cellular m…"]},{"s":"Inflammation Basics▾","a":"inflammation-basics","k":["Inflammation Basics▾","What Inflammation Is","Occurs with cell injury, regardless of cause — initially a protective mechanism that begins healing, working to destroy invading/harmful agents, limit their spread, and prepare damaged tissue for repair.","The five cardinal signs: redness, swelling, heat, pain, and loss of function.","Exogenous causes come from outside (surgery, trauma, burns); endogenous causes come from inside (e.g., tissue ischemia).","Acute inflammation lasts less than 2 weeks and is helpful. Chronic inflammation extends much longer, can form scar tissue, and is no longer a helpful process.","Mechanism: tissue injury releases chemical mediators that cause vasodilation and increased vascular permeability, letting white blood cells (mainly neutrophils and monocytes) move out of the blood vessel and into the injured tissue — a recr…","Serous — Mild inflammation — watery, low protein (e.g., clear fluid leaking from a healing surgical incision).","Serosanguineous — Similar to serous but pink-tinged, with small amounts of RBCs — normal immediately post-op.","Purulent — Severe inflammation, typically bacterial infection — thick, sticky, high in neutrophils/protein/debris, often discolored and foul-smelling; may need drainage (an abscess).","Hemorrhagic — Large amounts of RBCs — the most severe type, from badly damaged/necrotic blood vessels. Signals active bleeding, not just a wound.","If inflammation isn't contained, it can become systemic — driven by cytokines (interleukin-1, interleukin-6, TNF-alpha). Systemic inflammation presents as fever, an elevated white blood cell count, fatigue, and muscle catabolism (breakdown)…"]},{"s":"Adaptive Immunity — B Cells, T Cells & Antibodies▾","a":"adaptive-immunity-b-cells-t-cells-antibodies","k":["Adaptive Immunity — B Cells, T Cells & Antibodies▾","Self vs. Non-Self","The major histocompatibility complex (MHC), also called the human leukocyte antigen (HLA) in humans, is a gene cluster on chromosome 6 that makes \"self\" proteins displayed on nearly every cell surface.","Specific adaptive immunity recognizes foreign invaders, destroys them, and remembers the encounter for a faster response next time.","B cells and T cells are lymphocytes responsible for this memory immunity. B cells provide humoral immunity (antibodies in body fluids); T cells provide cell-mediated immunity (recognizing antigens on cell surfaces).","An antigen is essentially a \"name tag\" on a foreign/bad cell (cancer, bacteria, fungal, inflammatory) that marks it for attack.","Memory B cells remember a prior antigen exposure and respond rapidly on re-exposure. Plasma cells are short-lived B cells that secrete antibodies against the triggering antigen.","IgG — Most prevalent (~75–80% of all antibodies). Easily crosses into interstitial fluid; produced later in an infection (or after a vaccine) — protects against bacterial and viral infection.","IgM — ~10% of antibodies. First produced after exposure/immunization, activating complement — a signal that something is newly/acutely happening.","IgA — Found in saliva, tears, secretions, breast milk, GI, and GU tract — protects at secretory/excretory sites.","IgD — Trace amounts in serum and on B cell surfaces — stimulates B cells to multiply, differentiate, and secrete other immunoglobulins.","IgE — Plays a major role in immunity against parasites and in allergic reactions (works with mast cells).","Passive Immunity — Transfer of antibody-containing plasma from a protected person to one who isn't — e.g., mother-to-fetus (IgG crosses the placenta; IgA is passed via breast milk), or injection of antibodies/immune globulins (not a vaccine…","Active Immunity — The result of an active infection or a vaccine — the body creates its own antibodies (usually via memory B cells), typically much faster on a second exposure, which is how vaccines prevent illness.","Traditional — An inactivated or killed part of the virus/bacteria/organism.","Attenuated — A weakened, still-live version — should not be given to immunocompromised patients or those with conditions putting them at risk for complications.","Toxoid — An inactivated toxin that stimulates antitoxin production (e.g., tetanus vaccine).","Conjugate — A protein/toxoid from one organism attached to a disease-causing organism to stimulate a response (e.g., Hib).","mRNA — Uses a snippet of the virus's genetic code to teach the body's antibodies to respond."]},{"s":"Infection Basics — Microorganisms, Transmission & Portals of Entry▾","a":"infection-basics-microorganisms-transmission-portals-of-entry","k":["Infection Basics — Microorganisms, Transmission & Portals of Entry▾","Virus — DNA or RNA with a protein shell; requires a host cell to replicate. Responsible for COVID-19, influenza, chickenpox, and more.","Bacteria — Larger than a virus, at least single-celled, usually with one strand of DNA; can reproduce inside or outside cells and suppress the body's defenses (e.g., strep throat, TB, UTIs).","Fungal — Spore-forming organisms (e.g., yeast infections).","Protozoa — Typically live in water environments (e.g., malaria).","Helminths — Parasitic worms (roundworms, hookworms).","Prions — Misfolded proteins that attach to other proteins, even smaller than viruses — cause devastating diseases like Creutzfeldt-Jakob (mad cow) disease.","Direct Contact — Kissing, sex, contact with contaminated soil/vegetation, or droplet spread (short-range aerosols from sneezing/coughing, e.g., pertussis, meningitis).","Indirect — Airborne — Droplet nuclei stay suspended in air (e.g., measles).","Indirect — Vehicle-Borne — Infectious material via food, water, or blood (e.g., hepatitis A from contaminated food).","Indirect — Vector-Borne — Carried by another organism — mosquitoes, fleas, ticks (e.g., malaria, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, Lyme disease).","Portals of Entry","Respiratory (airways/lungs) and GI (via eating/the stomach).","Genitourinary tract (e.g., STDs, catheters).","Skin — normally a strong barrier (tight cell junctions), but skin integrity issues are a major vulnerability. Translocation is the movement of bacteria across the intestinal lining (most often the peritoneal cavity) when the gut becomes \"le…","Blood — transfusion contamination or needle sticks.","Maternal-fetal transmission — some organisms (e.g., Zika, Listeria) cross the placental barrier or transmit during childbirth."]},{"s":"Stages of Infection & the Infectious Process▾","a":"stages-of-infection-the-infectious-process","k":["Stages of Infection & the Infectious Process▾","Incubation — Time from pathogen entry to first symptoms — no symptoms yet, just growth/multiplication.","Prodromal — Onset of nonspecific symptoms (mild fatigue, low fever) — not yet fully symptomatic.","Acute Stage — Full-blown, specific signs/symptoms of the illness.","Convalescence — Symptoms improving, illness disappearing.","Resolution — The pathogen is completely eliminated. Some pathogens never reach this stage (e.g., chickenpox virus staying dormant for life).","The 6-Stage Infectious/Inflammatory Process","1. Injury — the initial insult to the area.","2. Brief vasoconstriction — stops bleeding, limits organism movement.","3. Vasodilation — blood flows freely into the area, bringing the five cardinal signs of inflammation.","4. Increased permeability — fluid leaks from vessels into tissue.","5. Emigration of leukocytes & phagocytosis — neutrophils (plus eosinophils, NK cells, monocytes) migrate in and engulf invaders, producing exudate.","6. Systemic symptoms (if the process doesn't stay localized) — a total-body response driven by the hypothalamic fever set point, which is ultimately protective (helps conserve heat, boosts immune cell/antibody function, and slows some bacte…"]},{"s":"Diagnosing Infection & Healthcare-Associated Infections▾","a":"diagnosing-infection-healthcare-associated-infections","k":["Diagnosing Infection & Healthcare-Associated Infections▾","Colonization — A pathogen inhabits a body site without causing signs/symptoms of infection (e.g., asymptomatic staph on the skin). Can turn into an active infection with immune compromise or tissue disruption.","Gram Stain — Identifies bacteria as gram-positive or gram-negative and their shape/arrangement. Gram-negative bacteria are more dangerous — an outer membrane/capsule hides their antigens, making them harder for the body to fight.","Sensitivity — Takes 24–72 hours; identifies the exact bacteria and which antibiotics it's sensitive/resistant to.","Urinalysis — Normal Values — pH 5–9; nitrates negative (positive suggests bacterial infection); leukocyte esterase negative (positive suggests infection); RBCs less than 5 (more suggests infection); epithelial cells suggest a contaminated s…","Nosocomial (Healthcare-Associated) Infections","Acquired in the hospital, not present at home — typically more virulent and more likely to be drug-resistant.","MRSA — methicillin-resistant Staph aureus. CRE — carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae (resistant to an entire antibiotic class). MDRO — multi-drug-resistant organism (resistant to multiple drug types).","Superinfection: a new infection that develops during treatment for another one — broad-spectrum antibiotics kill off normal flora that normally keep opportunistic pathogens in check.","C. diff (Clostridium difficile) — Normal intestinal flora killed by antibiotics lets C. diff overgrow — causes frequent, watery (often orange-tinged) diarrhea and abdominal cramping, sometimes months after treatment. Diagnosed by stool PCR …","Candidiasis — A yeast/fungal overgrowth after antibiotics kill normal skin/mucosal flora — thrush (oral), or infections in moist skin folds (under the breast, etc.). Untreated oral thrush can descend into the esophagus. Prevented/treated wi…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-hypertension-hyperlipidemia.html","t":"Hypertension & Hyperlipidemia (HTN/HLD)","secs":[{"s":"Hypertension & Hyperlipidemia (HTN/HLD)","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Cholesterol Basics — Lipoproteins & Where Cholesterol Comes From","Diagnosing Hyperlipidemia — Screening, Targets & Familial Hypercholesterolemia","Atherosclerosis — How Plaque Forms","Atherosclerosis — Consequences, Blockage Severity & Related Terminology","Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Statins (HMG-CoA Reductase Inhibitors)","Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Ezetimibe & Secondary Causes","Blood Pressure Regulation — The RAS System & Other Mechanisms","Hypertension Basics — Classification, Primary & Secondary Causes","Hypertension — Long-Term Complications & Hypertensive Crisis","Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Diuretics","Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Sympatholytics","Antihypertensive Pharmacology — RAS Blockers, Calcium Channel Blockers & Vasodilators","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: cholesterol and lipoprotein basics, diagnosing hyperlipidemia, atherosclerosis, lipid-lowering pharmacology, blood pressure regulation (the RAS system), hypertension classification and complications, and antihypert…"]},{"s":"Cholesterol Basics — Lipoproteins & Where Cholesterol Comes From▾","a":"cholesterol-basics-lipoproteins-where-cholesterol-comes-from","k":["Cholesterol Basics — Lipoproteins & Where Cholesterol Comes From▾","Cholesterol is essential — it's part of every cell membrane, a building block for hormones (estrogen, testosterone, cortisol) and vitamin D, a key ingredient in bile acid, and it helps the skin retain water. It's highly insoluble, so it has…","Exogenous Cholesterol — From diet — only about a quarter of the body's cholesterol. The 2015 USDA Dietary Guidelines dropped the old 300 mg/day dietary limit after the AHA/ACC found cholesterol intake itself isn't a nutrient of concern for …","Endogenous Cholesterol — Made by the liver — about three-quarters of the body's cholesterol, via the HMG-CoA reductase pathway. The liver responds to diet: more dietary cholesterol only minimally raises levels, but more dietary saturated fa…","Liver cells have LDL receptors. LDL from the blood binds these receptors, and HMG-CoA reductase uses it to produce cholesterol. This is a negative feedback loop: high LDL/cholesterol turns down HMG-CoA reductase production; if cholesterol p…","HDL (High-Density Lipoprotein) — The \"good\" cholesterol — more protein, less cholesterol. Higher HDL decreases risk of adverse cardiovascular events.","LDL (Low-Density Lipoprotein) — The \"bad\" cholesterol — less protein, more cholesterol (\"L for lousy,\" or \"L for lower\"). Increases atherosclerosis risk.","VLDL (Very-Low-Density Lipoprotein) — Mostly triglycerides, so it isn't measured directly — high triglycerides suggest high VLDL, which also raises heart disease risk."]},{"s":"Diagnosing Hyperlipidemia — Screening, Targets & Familial Hypercholesterolemia▾","a":"diagnosing-hyperlipidemia-screening-targets-familial-hypercholesterolemia","k":["Diagnosing Hyperlipidemia — Screening, Targets & Familial Hypercholesterolemia▾","Hypercholesterolemia (too much cholesterol) is also called hyperlipidemia (high lipid levels, the term you'll hear most) or dyslipidemia (a lipid problem generally). Diagnosed via a fasting blood draw (fasting overnight — a recent meal can …","Total Cholesterol — Below 200. Calculated as HDL + LDL + triglycerides ÷ 5.","HDL — Above 55 (females), above 45 (males); above 60 is optimal for both.","LDL — Below 130 generally, below 100 for higher-risk patients (diabetes, other risk factors), and as low as below 70 for the highest-risk patients.","Triglycerides — Below 160.","NCLEX-style exams provide normal lab ranges as a reference — you don't need to memorize exact numbers, but you should recognize what an abnormal cholesterol panel looks like.","Familial Hypercholesterolemia","A genetically-linked, familial pattern of high cholesterol caused by a defect in liver LDL receptors — the liver can't efficiently pull LDL out of the blood, so cholesterol stays elevated despite diet, exercise, and a healthy lifestyle.","Suspect it in children or people in their early 20s with high cholesterol, especially with a strong family pattern.","Inheriting the genetic variant from both parents can cause severe hypercholesterolemia in young children, including heart disease or heart attacks.","Because the defect is in the LDL receptors themselves, this group responds poorly to medications that work by increasing LDL receptors (like statins).","General (non-familial) risk factors: age, family history, tobacco use, hypertension, diabetes, physical inactivity, and a poor diet. Age and family history can't be modified — the goal is addressing what can be controlled to lower overall c…"]},{"s":"Atherosclerosis — How Plaque Forms▾","a":"atherosclerosis-how-plaque-forms","k":["Atherosclerosis — How Plaque Forms▾","Atherosclerosis is a buildup of cholesterol in the vessels. Arteriosclerosis — thickening/hardening of the arterial wall — is often used interchangeably, since the arteries (which carry oxygenated blood to tissues) are the vessels of greate…","The Plaque-Formation Sequence","1. Endothelial injury. The endothelium (the vessel's inner lining) is damaged — by cigarette/tobacco use, chronic hemodynamic wall stress (hypertension), or hyperglycemia (long-term high blood sugar).","2. Increased permeability. The normally tightly-bunched endothelial cells loosen, letting LDL molecules pass under the vessel wall.","3. Inflammation & foam cells. Macrophages arrive and engulf the trapped lipids, becoming foam cells that embed in the vessel wall and appear as fatty streaks.","4. Plaque development. Inflammatory mediators, accumulated lipids, and foam cells form a lipid core that expands into the vessel and can impede blood flow.","There's no simple blood test for atherosclerosis itself — confirming it invasively (fluoroscopy with injectable dye) is highly invasive. As a proxy, clinicians check C-reactive protein (CRP), a nonspecific marker of systemic inflammation re…"]},{"s":"Atherosclerosis — Consequences, Blockage Severity & Related Terminology▾","a":"atherosclerosis-consequences-blockage-severity-related-terminology","k":["Atherosclerosis — Consequences, Blockage Severity & Related Terminology▾","Large plaques with a large lipid core are prone to rupture. A rupture triggers platelet aggregation and clot (thrombus) formation, which can block the vessel entirely — anything downstream loses oxygen and nutrients. In the heart, that's a …","30–90% Blockage — Blood can still get through; the patient may be asymptomatic, especially if sedentary or if the blockage is in a smaller vessel.","~99% Blockage — Severe flow constriction — typically chest pain (angina) with minimal exertion, like getting up and walking to a chair. Needs immediate intervention; this is life-threatening.","Overlapping terminology: coronary heart disease (CHD), coronary artery disease (CAD), ischemic heart disease, and atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) all describe the same underlying problem — plaque buildup in the coronary arter…"]},{"s":"Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Statins (HMG-CoA Reductase Inhibitors)▾","a":"hyperlipidemia-pharmacology-statins-hmg-coa-reductase-inhibitors","k":["Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Statins (HMG-CoA Reductase Inhibitors)▾","Before medication, clinicians try 6 months of diet and physical activity modification (unless the patient already has a history of MI, stroke, or other atherosclerosis-related organ damage). If that isn't enough, statins are first-line. Sta…","Statins reduce fatal and non-fatal atherosclerotic cardiovascular events (heart attacks, strokes), reduce disability after a non-fatal stroke, and are used for both primary prevention (before any event) and secondary prevention (after one, …","Drugs (know these) — Atorvastatin (the prototype), simvastatin, rosuvastatin.","Typical Effect — LDL decreases ~21–63%, HDL increases ~5–22%, triglycerides decrease ~6–43%.","Dosing Timing — Take at night — the liver makes more cholesterol at night, so this is when the drug is most effective. Required for simvastatin and rosuvastatin (shorter half-life); atorvastatin has a longer half-life so timing matters less…","Interactions — Avoid grapefruit — it interferes with statin metabolism and raises statin levels. Avoid alcohol (liver risk). Avoid other drugs that raise myopathy/rhabdomyolysis risk (azoles, HIV protease inhibitors, macrolides, cyclosporin…","Adverse reactions: myopathy (muscle weakness — ask patients about this), rhabdomyolysis (muscle fiber breakdown whose byproducts can cause acute kidney failure), and hepatotoxicity (liver damage with a year or more of treatment — monitor AS…"]},{"s":"Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Ezetimibe & Secondary Causes▾","a":"hyperlipidemia-pharmacology-ezetimibe-secondary-causes","k":["Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Ezetimibe & Secondary Causes▾","Ezetimibe (Zetia) — Cholesterol Absorption Inhibitor","Blocks the small intestine's ability to absorb cholesterol, from both dietary intake and bile.","A second-line therapy to statins — sometimes given in combination (a combined statin + ezetimibe pill exists, though it's typically expensive); combination therapy produces a greater LDL decrease than either drug alone.","Diet — Especially saturated/trans fat intake.","Medications — Diuretics, glucocorticoids, cyclosporine, and amiodarone can raise cholesterol.","Disease — Bile Duct Obstruction — Cholesterol is normally secreted into bile; a blocked/obstructed bile duct raises cholesterol levels.","Altered Metabolism — Hypothyroidism, obesity, and pregnancy can all raise LDL and triglycerides.","Other second-line agents you don't need to know in detail: bile acid sequestrants, niacin (sometimes combined with statins), fibric acid derivatives, and fish oil (available OTC — worth asking patients about). Statins remain the gold standa…"]},{"s":"Blood Pressure Regulation — The RAS System & Other Mechanisms▾","a":"blood-pressure-regulation-the-ras-system-other-mechanisms","k":["Blood Pressure Regulation — The RAS System & Other Mechanisms▾","The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAS) is the primary hormonal driver of blood pressure, with two endpoints: sodium/water retention and vasoconstriction. It's a normal, appropriate physiologic response to a drop in blood pressure/vo…","The RAS Cascade","1. Decreased renal perfusion (low blood volume/pressure) triggers the kidney to release renin.","2. Renin converts angiotensinogen (released by the liver) into angiotensin I.","3. Angiotensin I travels to the lungs, where ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) converts it to angiotensin II — a potent vasoconstrictor.","4. Angiotensin II acts on the adrenal glands to release aldosterone, which causes the kidney (nephron) to retain sodium and water — \"salt sucks,\" water follows salt — raising blood volume and blood pressure.","Chronic stress, hypersensitivity to angiotensin II, or being a high renin-secretor can push this system into an inappropriate, pathologic overactivation. Other blood-pressure mechanisms: arterial baroreceptors (carotid sinus, aorta, left ve…"]},{"s":"Hypertension Basics — Classification, Primary & Secondary Causes▾","a":"hypertension-basics-classification-primary-secondary-causes","k":["Hypertension Basics — Classification, Primary & Secondary Causes▾","Hypertension = high (hyper-) blood pressure (tension). It affects about 1 in 3 people in the US and is often called the \"silent killer\" because it's usually asymptomatic. The 2019 AHA guidelines lowered treatment thresholds after evidence s…","Normal — Below 120 and — Below 80","Elevated — 120–129 and — Below 80","Hypertension Stage 1 — 130–139 or — 80–89","Hypertension Stage 2 — Above 140 or — Above 90","Hypertensive Crisis — Above 180 and/or — Above 120","Watch the \"and\" vs. \"or\": Normal and Elevated require both numbers to qualify. Stage 1 and Stage 2 only need one number to qualify — e.g., a systolic of 120 (which alone reads as Elevated) paired with a diastolic of 85 makes the patient Sta…","Primary (Essential) Hypertension","~90–95% of cases; no single known cause — usually idiopathic, from a complex mix of genetics and environment affecting the sympathetic nervous system, the RAS system, and natriuretic peptides.","Risk factors: smoking, excess sodium, sedentary lifestyle, hyperlipidemia, stress, family history/genetics, obesity, age (over 55 for males, over 60 for females), insulin resistance, high alcohol intake.","Secondary Hypertension","Has a known, identifiable cause — renal disease, adrenal cortical or medullary tumors (pheochromocytoma), or drug-induced (oral contraceptives, corticosteroids, antihistamines, cocaine, amphetamines).","Treated by treating/removing the underlying cause."]},{"s":"Hypertension — Long-Term Complications & Hypertensive Crisis▾","a":"hypertension-long-term-complications-hypertensive-crisis","k":["Hypertension — Long-Term Complications & Hypertensive Crisis▾","Hypertension is usually asymptomatic until there's end-organ damage — this is why it's screened for routinely rather than waiting for symptoms.","Heart — Left ventricular hypertrophy (from the increased workload), increased myocardial oxygen demand, accelerated atherosclerosis, increased risk of aortic aneurysm.","Kidneys — A primary cause of end-stage renal disease.","Brain — Higher risk of stroke, aneurysm, and hemorrhage.","Eyes — Retinopathy, blindness.","Lower Extremities — Gangrene, intermittent claudication.","Hypertensive crisis (systolic ≥ 180 and/or diastolic ≥ 120) splits into two severities. Hypertensive urgency: no signs of end-organ damage — treat with oral medication, lowering blood pressure gradually over hours to days. Hypertensive emer…"]},{"s":"Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Diuretics▾","a":"antihypertensive-pharmacology-diuretics","k":["Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Diuretics▾","Diuretics lower blood pressure by increasing urinary output, decreasing circulating volume, and decreasing arterial resistance — decreasing that volume decreases cardiac output. They're inexpensive and commonly first-line.","Thiazide Diuretics(hydrochlorothiazide/HCTZ) — First-line for mild hypertension. Works on the distal convoluted tubule, inhibiting sodium/potassium/chloride reabsorption and relaxing arterioles (decreasing peripheral vascular resistance). P…","Loop Diuretics(furosemide/Lasix) — Inhibits sodium reabsorption at the loop of Henle — a much stronger diuretic effect than thiazides. Also used for fluid volume overload unrelated to blood pressure (common in the ICU), and works even with …","Potassium-Sparing Diuretics(spironolactone/Aldactone) — An aldosterone antagonist — blocks aldosterone's action, so sodium/water are excreted but potassium is retained (spared). A mild diuretic on its own, often added to a thiazide/loop reg…","Nursing considerations across diuretics: monitor potassium levels closely (critical for cardiac rhythm — both hypo- and hyperkalemia can cause dysrhythmias); with thiazides/loops, consider potassium supplementation and potassium-rich foods …"]},{"s":"Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Sympatholytics▾","a":"antihypertensive-pharmacology-sympatholytics","k":["Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Sympatholytics▾","This class works by blocking the sympathetic nervous system, decreasing vasoconstriction and peripheral vascular resistance.","Beta Blockers(metoprolol, propranolol, carvedilol) — Suffix \"-olol.\" Decrease heart force, heart rate, and renin secretion; increase nitric oxide (vasodilation). Beta-1 receptors are cardioselective (heart — \"1 heart\"); beta-2 receptors are…","Alpha-2 Agonists(clonidine) — Centrally acting — decreases sympathetic outflow to both alpha and beta receptors, causing bradycardia, decreased cardiac output, and vasodilation. Not first-line (high side-effect profile); given PO or transde…","Alpha-1 Blockers(doxazosin) — Directly blocks the sympathetic nervous system, decreasing peripheral vascular resistance (venous and arterial dilation). Not first-line. Side effects: hypotension, dizziness. (Tamsulosin/Flomax is also an alph…","Never stop beta blockers or clonidine abruptly — doing so can cause rebound hypertension, a critical, dangerous rise in blood pressure with high risk for stroke or death. Wean the dose down gradually instead. Also: always take blood pressur…","Other notes: beta blockers can mask hypoglycemia (tachycardia is a common hypoglycemia symptom, but a beta blocker keeps heart rate normal) — use cautiously in diabetes. Beta blockers are also used for angina, arrhythmias, heart failure, mi…"]},{"s":"Antihypertensive Pharmacology — RAS Blockers, Calcium Channel Blockers & Vasodilators▾","a":"antihypertensive-pharmacology-ras-blockers-calcium-channel-blockers-vasodilators","k":["Antihypertensive Pharmacology — RAS Blockers, Calcium Channel Blockers & Vasodilators▾","ACE Inhibitors(captopril, lisinopril) — Suffix \"-pril.\" Block angiotensin-converting enzyme, so angiotensin II (a vasoconstrictor) and aldosterone (water retention) are both reduced. First-line for hypertension, also used in heart failure; …","ARBs(losartan) — Suffix \"-sartan.\" Block angiotensin II's action at its receptor (rather than blocking its production) — vasodilation, decreased peripheral vascular resistance, increased sodium/water excretion, without affecting heart rate.…","Renin Inhibitor(aliskiren) — Directly inhibits renin, causing vasodilation, decreased blood volume, decreased sympathetic activation. PO only. Long half-life — takes several weeks for full effect. Not for use in pregnancy; watch for hyperka…","Calcium Channel Blockers(nifedipine, amlodipine, nicardipine, verapamil, diltiazem) — Block calcium channels, causing vasodilation of arterial (not venous) smooth muscle. Nifedipine/amlodipine/nicardipine are used mainly for hypertension (n…","Vasodilator(hydralazine) — Works directly on arterial and venous smooth muscle, causing relaxation and decreasing systemic/peripheral vascular resistance. PO or IV (IV for emergencies or when PO isn't tolerated). Side effects: hypotension, …","ACE inhibitors carry a risk of angioedema — swelling of the deep skin layers, around the eyes/lips, and critically the larynx, which can close the airway. This is rare overall (0.1–0.7%) but occurs more often in African American/Black patie…","ACE inhibitor side effects to also expect: first-dose hypotension (a 15–20% BP drop in the first 6–8 hours — advise taking the first dose at home, not before driving or operating machinery), a dry, persistent, non-productive cough (a common…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-diabetes-metabolic-syndrome.html","t":"Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome","secs":[{"s":"Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Glucose Regulation Basics — Carbs, the Liver & the Pancreas","Insulin Deficiency — The Self-Feeding Cascade","Diabetes — Symptoms & Diagnostic Criteria","Type 1 vs. Type 2 Diabetes","Acute Complications — DKA, HHNS & Hypoglycemia","Chronic Complications — Microvascular Disease","Chronic Complications — Macrovascular Disease & Infection Risk","Insulin Pharmacology — Types, Onset, Peak & Duration","Insulin Administration, Regimens & Technology","Insulin Complications — Hypoglycemia, Somogyi Effect & Dawn Phenomenon","Oral & Injectable Antidiabetic Agents","Obesity — Adipose Tissue, Distribution & Measurement","Obesity — Adipokines, GI Hormones & Pharmacology","Metabolic Syndrome","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: glucose regulation and the insulin-deficiency cascade, diagnosing and classifying diabetes, acute and chronic complications, insulin pharmacology and administration, oral/injectable antidiabetic agents, obesity, an…"]},{"s":"Glucose Regulation Basics — Carbs, the Liver & the Pancreas▾","a":"glucose-regulation-basics-carbs-the-liver-the-pancreas","k":["Glucose Regulation Basics — Carbs, the Liver & the Pancreas▾","Carbohydrates are broken down into glucose, raising blood glucose; insulin is secreted in response and lowers it back to baseline. The liver regulates glucose storage and release: glycogenesis converts excess blood glucose into stored glyco…","The pancreas is both an exocrine gland (secretes digestive enzymes — amylase, lipase — through the pancreatic duct, not the bloodstream) and an endocrine gland (secretes hormones directly into the blood). The islets of Langerhans are the pa…","Alpha Cells — Secrete glucagon in response to low blood sugar — triggers glycogenolysis, raising blood glucose.","Beta Cells — Secrete insulin (and amylin) — lowers blood glucose by promoting cellular glucose uptake in skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and adipose tissue, and promotes glucose storage as glycogen in the liver. Also inhibits fat breakdown…","Glucose counter-regulation is the body's defense against hypoglycemia — a negative feedback loop where insulin (lowers glucose) works against a group of counter-regulatory hormones that raise it: glucagon, epinephrine, glucocorticoids, and …"]},{"s":"Insulin Deficiency — The Self-Feeding Cascade▾","a":"insulin-deficiency-the-self-feeding-cascade","k":["Insulin Deficiency — The Self-Feeding Cascade▾","When there isn't enough insulin, glucose can't get into cells even though blood glucose is high (hyperglycemia) — cells are starving despite the excess sugar circulating around them. The body compensates with two other processes, both of wh…","Lipolysis (Fat Breakdown)","Fat is broken down for energy instead of glucose, releasing free fatty acids.","Unused free fatty acids convert to cholesterol/phospholipids (contributing to atherosclerosis) or are broken down to acetyl CoA, which the liver turns into acetoacetic acid → ketone bodies.","Rising ketones cause ketosis, which can progress to severe metabolic acidosis and coma.","Proteolysis (Protein Breakdown)","Protein synthesis stops and protein catabolism increases, releasing amino acids the cells use for energy.","Results in muscle wasting, multiple organ dysfunction, amino acidemia, and an increased BUN.","More common in type 1 diabetes (complete insulin deficiency) than type 2.","Osmotic diuresis & dehydration: excess blood glucose raises serum osmolality, pulling water out of cells and into the bloodstream (intracellular dehydration). Once blood glucose exceeds the renal threshold (~200 mg/dL), the kidneys can't re…"]},{"s":"Diabetes — Symptoms & Diagnostic Criteria▾","a":"diabetes-symptoms-diagnostic-criteria","k":["Diabetes — Symptoms & Diagnostic Criteria▾","Diabetes is characterized by hyperglycemia from an absolute or relative insulin deficiency. Clinically, this presents as the classic three P's: polyuria (excessive urination, from osmotic diuresis), polydipsia (excessive thirst, from dehydr…","Hemoglobin A1c — ~5–5.5% — 5.7–6.4% — ≥ 6.5%","Fasting Plasma Glucose — ≤ 99 mg/dL — 100–125 mg/dL — ≥ 126 mg/dL","Oral Glucose Tolerance Test — Measures blood glucose at set intervals after a large glucose load — most often used to screen for gestational diabetes.","A1c reflects average blood glucose over the last 2–3 months (the percentage of hemoglobin with glucose attached) — a better long-term picture than a single fasting glucose value. The three main types of diabetes are type 1, type 2, and gest…"]},{"s":"Type 1 vs. Type 2 Diabetes▾","a":"type-1-vs-type-2-diabetes","k":["Type 1 vs. Type 2 Diabetes▾","Type 1 Diabetes","Most common pediatric chronic disease, usually diagnosed around age 12; adult-onset type 1 exists but is often misdiagnosed as type 2.","Usually autoimmune (T cells destroy the beta cells that make insulin), sometimes idiopathic; genetic predisposition + environmental triggers; slowly progressive.","Beta cells cannot regenerate — once destroyed, they're gone. About 80–90% of beta cells must be destroyed before insulin secretion noticeably declines.","Complete lack of endogenous insulin. ~5–10% of U.S. diabetes cases. Previously called insulin-dependent or juvenile-onset diabetes.","Symptoms: the three P's, plus weight loss/fatigue more than in type 2.","Type 2 Diabetes","Attributed to genetic and environmental factors; strongly associated with long-term obesity.","Primarily insulin resistance (cells/insulin receptors lose sensitivity, so glucose can't enter despite insulin being present), with possible decreased insulin secretion as beta cells become exhausted over time.","Risk factors: age, obesity, hypertension, poor diet, physical inactivity, family history.","Symptoms are vague/nonspecific — recurrent infections, visual changes, prolonged wound healing, fatigue. The three P's are less common than in type 1.","ADA recommends screening all adults ≥ 35 regardless of risk factors (earlier if risk factors present).","In type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance triggers compensatory hyperinsulinemia (the body makes more insulin to try to overcome the resistance) — those high insulin levels stimulate lipogenesis (fat creation) and inhibit fat breakdown. The li…"]},{"s":"Acute Complications — DKA, HHNS & Hypoglycemia▾","a":"acute-complications-dka-hhns-hypoglycemia","k":["Acute Complications — DKA, HHNS & Hypoglycemia▾","Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) — Most common acute complication in type 1 diabetes. Severe hyperglycemia (blood sugar in the 400–600s), acidosis from ketone bodies, ketonuria, and possible coma. Can be fatal — treated as an emergency.","Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic Syndrome (HHNS) — Primarily in type 2 diabetes. Extremely high hyperglycemia (blood sugar in the 600–800s) causing severe osmolality-driven fluid shifts. Results from a less profound insulin deficiency than DKA, b…","Hypoglycemia — Occurs in both types — typically blood sugar below ~55–60 mg/dL, usually from too much insulin/medication relative to food intake. A patient chronically running high (e.g., ~250 mg/dL) can feel symptomatic at a much higher gl…","Hypoglycemia is a true emergency. Signs: cold sweats, pallor, tachycardia, hunger, restlessness, anxiety — and if untreated, it can progress to seizures or coma. If a patient shows these symptoms, check the blood sugar immediately — it's ea…"]},{"s":"Chronic Complications — Microvascular Disease▾","a":"chronic-complications-microvascular-disease","k":["Chronic Complications — Microvascular Disease▾","Chronic complications result from poorly controlled diabetes — mainly chronic hyperglycemia, which produces advanced glycation end products, free fatty acids, ketone bodies, and abnormal amino acid pathways that damage tissue. Microvascular…","Diabetic Neuropathy — Most common microvascular complication. Progressive peripheral nervous system disorder — ischemia, inflammation, and demyelination cause delayed nerve conduction, sensory/motor loss (pain, temperature, vibration sensat…","Diabetic Retinopathy — Leading cause of blindness worldwide; common in both types. Risk rises with duration of diabetes and poor glucose control, and with comorbidities like hypertension/dyslipidemia. Damaged retinal vessels → hemorrhage an…","Diabetic Nephropathy — Diabetes is the leading cause of chronic kidney disease/end-stage renal disease worldwide. Hyperglycemia injures the glomeruli, progressively thickening the glomerular basement membrane and allowing protein to leak in…"]},{"s":"Chronic Complications — Macrovascular Disease & Infection Risk▾","a":"chronic-complications-macrovascular-disease-infection-risk","k":["Chronic Complications — Macrovascular Disease & Infection Risk▾","Macrovascular disease involves lesions in large/medium arteries — common in type 2 diabetes (but possible in type 1). Thought to result from oxidative stress, endothelial dysfunction, altered mineral metabolism, and increased cytokine produ…","Diabetes and infection are a dangerous combination — people with diabetes are more prone to cystitis, enteric infections, skin/soft tissue infections, appendicitis, pneumonia, and ear infections, due to immune dysfunction, decreased blood s…"]},{"s":"Insulin Pharmacology — Types, Onset, Peak & Duration▾","a":"insulin-pharmacology-types-onset-peak-duration","k":["Insulin Pharmacology — Types, Onset, Peak & Duration▾","The overall goal of diabetes pharmacology is to prevent hyperglycemia and reduce long-term microvascular/macrovascular complications, by maintaining tight glycemic control (target blood glucose ~80–140 mg/dL, A1c < 6.5%) and healthy lipid l…","Rapid-Acting(insulin lispro) — 15 min — ~1 hr — 2–4 hr — Given with meals to control postprandial glucose rise — administer as the patient starts eating (giving it earlier risks hypoglycemia if the meal is delayed). Often paired with an int…","Short-Acting(insulin regular) — 30–60 min — 2–6 hr — 3–8 hr — Given before meals for postprandial control. The only insulin that can be given IV (bolus or infusion) or IM — used in insulin pumps and IV infusions (e.g., DKA, tube feeding) wh…","Intermediate-Acting(NPH / insulin isophane suspension) — 2–4 hr — 4–10 hr — 10–20 hr — A cloudy suspension — agitate/roll gently before drawing up. Typically given twice daily with meals. Often combined with regular/rapid insulin in the sam…","Long-Acting(insulin glargine / Lantus) — ~70 min — No peak — 24 hr — Once-daily dosing, often at night, for steady all-day coverage. Never mix with other insulins in the same syringe.","Clear before cloudy: when drawing up rapid/short-acting insulin (clear) together with NPH (cloudy) in the same syringe, always draw the clear insulin first, then the cloudy — this prevents contaminating the short-acting vial with longer-act…"]},{"s":"Insulin Administration, Regimens & Technology▾","a":"insulin-administration-regimens-technology","k":["Insulin Administration, Regimens & Technology▾","Insulin is dosed in units, not milligrams or milliliters, and must be drawn up with an insulin-specific syringe — mixing up a standard syringe with an insulin syringe is a known, serious medication error (1 mL of U-100 insulin = 100 units).…","Before giving any insulin, get a current blood sugar — not one from an hour ago or earlier in the day, but immediately before the dose (finger-stick/FSBG). This is one of the most important safety rules in diabetes nursing care.","Sliding Scale — Regular insulin given with meals and at bedtime (charted as AC and HS) — dose is based on the blood glucose reading at that moment, per a provider-defined scale (e.g., 2 units for BG 150–200, 8 units for BG 303). Often combi…","Insulin Pumps — Computerized devices delivering a continuous basal infusion of regular insulin, plus patient-triggered bolus doses calculated from carbohydrate intake. Mostly used in type 1, but also in type 2. Many still require a finger-s…","Continuous Glucose Monitors (CGM) — Sensor-based, often paired to a phone, giving real-time glucose readings without repeated finger-sticks.","Certified diabetes educators are a valuable resource for teaching patients — especially children — about insulin regimens like sliding scale."]},{"s":"Insulin Complications — Hypoglycemia, Somogyi Effect & Dawn Phenomenon▾","a":"insulin-complications-hypoglycemia-somogyi-effect-dawn-phenomenon","k":["Insulin Complications — Hypoglycemia, Somogyi Effect & Dawn Phenomenon▾","Signs of hypoglycemia from too much insulin: tachycardia, sweating, nervousness, headache, drowsiness, and — if severe — decreased level of consciousness. Causes beyond a straightforward insulin overdose: skipped/reduced meals, GI illness (…","Somogyi Effect","Rebound hyperglycemia in the morning caused by excessive insulin at bedtime, which triggers nocturnal hypoglycemia.","The body senses the low blood sugar and releases stored glucose (counter-regulatory response) — overshooting into hyperglycemia. Severe cases can trigger ketosis.","Usually a sign of poor diabetes management (too much basal/bedtime insulin) — needs patient education and dose adjustment.","Dawn Phenomenon","Hyperglycemia in the early morning from the body's natural release of cortisol and growth hormone.","Cortisol raises blood sugar — a normal physiologic pattern, not an insulin-dosing error.","Usually less extreme than the Somogyi effect.","Other insulin complications: rare allergic reactions (less common now that insulin is chemically synthesized), and lipodystrophy/lipoatrophy — skin depression, hardening, or discoloration at injection sites from repeated use of the same spo…"]},{"s":"Oral & Injectable Antidiabetic Agents▾","a":"oral-injectable-antidiabetic-agents","k":["Oral & Injectable Antidiabetic Agents▾","Sulfonylureas(glipizide, glyburide) — Suffix \"-ide.\" First-line oral agent. Closes potassium-ATP channels in beta cells, stimulating insulin secretion (requires some remaining beta cell function — type 2 only), increases insulin sensitivity…","Biguanides(metformin) — First-line for type 2 diabetes (FDA-approved 1994). Stops hepatic conversion of glycogen to glucose and enhances glucose uptake/use by muscle. Does not stimulate insulin release, so it doesn't typically cause hypogly…","DPP-4 Inhibitors (\"Gliptins\")(linagliptin, saxagliptin, sitagliptin) — Suffix \"-gliptin.\" Inhibits DPP-4, the enzyme that breaks down incretin hormones (GLP-1, gastric inhibitory peptide) — letting them last longer, which increases insulin …","GLP-1 Receptor Agonists(dulaglutide, exenatide, semaglutide) — Suffix \"-tide.\" Act on the incretin (GLP-1) receptor: stimulate insulin secretion, reduce postprandial glucagon, slow gastric emptying, increase satiety. Usually adjunct therapy…","SGLT2 Inhibitors(dapagliflozin) — Suffix \"-flozin.\" Blocks the kidney's SGLT2 transporters, which normally reabsorb ~90% of filtered glucose — blocked glucose is excreted in urine instead, lowering blood glucose. Oral, type 2 only. Side eff…","Glucagon — The hypoglycemia antidote. Oral tablet preferred for an awake, swallowing patient; IM/IV/subcutaneous for decreased level of consciousness. Activates liver glucagon receptors, triggering glycogenolysis. Short half-life (25–35 min…","GLP-1 = Gila monster: GLP-1 receptor agonists (the \"-tide\" drugs) were derived from a peptide in Gila monster saliva — after clinicians noticed people bitten by the venomous lizard became hypoglycemic. Gila starts with G, just like GLP-1.","Other considerations: steroids raise blood sugar — a diabetic patient started on steroids (e.g., for a sinus infection) may need their insulin regimen increased. Illness raises blood sugar (stress response) and can push a patient toward DKA…"]},{"s":"Obesity — Adipose Tissue, Distribution & Measurement▾","a":"obesity-adipose-tissue-distribution-measurement","k":["Obesity — Adipose Tissue, Distribution & Measurement▾","About 74% of U.S. adults ≥ 20 are overweight or obese (42% obese, 10% severely obese) — a costly problem (over $147 billion in obesity-related medical costs in 2008) associated with increased risk across most major disease categories. Adipo…","Subcutaneous (Peripheral) — Just under the skin — the \"pinchable\" fat. Considered the healthier storage type; less inflammatory.","Visceral — Stored between abdominal organs (e.g., the omentum). More hormonally active than subcutaneous fat — associated with impaired lipid/glucose metabolism, insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome, increased cancer risk, and cardiovascu…","Android obesity (\"apple\" shape, more common in males) concentrates fat in the abdomen — more likely visceral fat, carrying higher risk of coronary artery disease, hypertension, stroke, and diabetes. Gynoid obesity (\"pear\" shape, more common…","BMI (adults) — Weight (kg) ÷ height² (m), or weight (lb) ÷ height² (in) × 703. Obesity = BMI > 30 kg/m².","BMI (children) — Obesity = BMI ≥ the age/sex-specific 95th percentile on growth charts.","Body Fat % — A more precise gauge than BMI alone (e.g., accounts for a muscular person with a high BMI but low body fat). Ideal: women ≤ 30%, men ≤ 25%.","Obesity is the fifth leading cause of death in the U.S. and a risk factor for type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, cancer, hypertension, GERD, arthritis, infection, and sleep apnea. It's polygenic (multiple genes involved — e.g., leptin…"]},{"s":"Obesity — Adipokines, GI Hormones & Pharmacology▾","a":"obesity-adipokines-gi-hormones-pharmacology","k":["Obesity — Adipokines, GI Hormones & Pharmacology▾","Adipose tissue functions as an endocrine organ, secreting adipokines — cell-signaling proteins regulating appetite, energy expenditure, lipid storage, insulin secretion/sensitivity, and coagulation.","Adiponectin — A \"good\" adipokine — inversely related to body fat (decreases as adiposity increases, especially visceral fat; resistance can also develop). Increases energy expenditure, improves insulin sensitivity, has anti-inflammatory and…","Leptin — A \"good\" adipokine made by adipocytes — rises with fat mass (positive relationship). Signals satiety and increases energy expenditure after eating; low levels (fasting) stimulate intake and reduce expenditure. With obesity, chronic…","Ghrelin, GLP-1 & Peptide YY — GI-derived hormones that normally promote satiety/fullness (ghrelin also rises with fasting). All three are decreased in obesity, contributing to reduced satiety signaling.","Cholecystokinin (CCK) — Stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme release; increases satiety, reduces food intake, slows gastric emptying. Research on its level in obesity is mixed (traditionally decreased, some newer studies…","Obesity also causes a chronic low-grade inflammatory state — immune cells infiltrate adipose tissue and release cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-6) that circulate systemically, contributing to insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome.","Orlistat — Pharmacologic Treatment for Obesity","Over-the-counter. Binds/blocks gastric and pancreatic enzymes, reducing dietary fat absorption by up to 30%.","Black box warning: liver injury — avoid in patients with existing liver disease.","Side effects (from blocked fat absorption): oily spotting, flatulence, fecal incontinence — advise reducing fat intake to < 30% of daily calories to help. Also decreases fat-soluble vitamin absorption (A, D, E, beta-carotene) — patients nee…","Takes about 3 months to see effect; minimally effective without diet/exercise; significant weight rebound after stopping — it's a long-term treatment, not a short course.","Drug therapy is generally reserved for BMI > 30, or > 27 with risk factors (after ~6 months of unsuccessful diet/exercise alone). Other FDA-approved obesity medications exist (phentermine, liraglutide, naltrexone/bupropion) but aren't detai…"]},{"s":"Metabolic Syndrome▾","a":"metabolic-syndrome","k":["Metabolic Syndrome▾","Metabolic syndrome is a cluster of risk factors that together substantially raise the risk of stroke and cardiovascular disease. A patient is considered to have metabolic syndrome with three or more of: elevated waist circumference (> 40 in…","Management treats each component individually (antihypertensives for high blood pressure, lipid-lowering agents for high triglycerides, antidiabetic therapy for high glucose) alongside counseling on weight loss, exercise, healthy diet, and …"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-coagulation-dvt.html","t":"Coagulation & DVT","secs":[{"s":"Coagulation & DVT","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Blood Basics — Plasma, Platelets & Hemostasis","The Coagulation Cascade — Clot Formation & Breakdown","Anticoagulant vs. Antiplatelet Therapy — Overview & Bleeding Surveillance","Heparin & Low-Molecular-Weight Heparin (Enoxaparin)","Warfarin (Coumadin)","Direct Factor Xa Inhibitors — Apixaban & Rivaroxaban","Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Aspirin","Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Clopidogrel, Ticagrelor & Direct Thrombin Inhibitors","DVT & VTE — Terminology and Virchow's Triad","DVT & VTE — Signs, Symptoms & Complications","DVT & VTE — Prevention & Treatment","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: blood/plasma basics and platelets, the coagulation cascade and hemostasis, anticoagulant pharmacology (heparin, enoxaparin, warfarin, factor Xa inhibitors), antiplatelet pharmacology, and deep vein thrombosis/venou…"]},{"s":"Blood Basics — Plasma, Platelets & Hemostasis▾","a":"blood-basics-plasma-platelets-hemostasis","k":["Blood Basics — Plasma, Platelets & Hemostasis▾","Whole blood is about 55% plasma and 45% formed elements (red blood cells, white blood cells, platelets) — and blood itself is only about 8% of total body fluid. Plasma is about 92% water, plus plasma proteins — most of which are made in the…","Albumin — ~57% of plasma proteins — a carrier molecule whose most important role is regulating water/solute movement through capillaries (drives oncotic pressure).","Globulins — Includes HDL, prothrombin (a clotting factor), and hormone-transport proteins.","Clotting Factors — Fibrinogen is the most plentiful. Serum = plasma minus clotting factors.","Formed elements include erythrocytes (RBCs, carry oxygen), leukocytes (WBCs — neutrophils for early inflammation/phagocytosis, eosinophils for allergic/parasitic response, basophils for allergic reactions, monocytes/macrophages for phagocyt…","Platelets","Not true cells — anucleate cytoplasmic fragments made in the bone marrow (with additional storage in the spleen).","Normal count: 150,000–400,000. Thrombocytopenia is generally < 100,000 — high bleeding risk.","Platelet activation: a resting platelet is smooth; on sensing vessel injury, it transitions to a spiky (\"dendritic\") shape, becomes sticky, and adheres to the injury site — then platelets aggregate with each other and with the vessel wall, …"]},{"s":"The Coagulation Cascade — Clot Formation & Breakdown▾","a":"the-coagulation-cascade-clot-formation-breakdown","k":["The Coagulation Cascade — Clot Formation & Breakdown▾","The coagulation cascade (clotting system/coagulation system — same thing) is a group of proteins that activate in sequence to form a blood clot. Platelets are the primary initiators of the cascade. The cascade can be triggered through the e…","Antithrombin III — A circulating inhibitor of thrombin — stops thrombin from converting fibrinogen to fibrin, halting the clotting process.","Tissue Factor Pathway Inhibitor (TFPI) — Produced by platelets and endothelial cells — inhibits factor Xa, stopping further clot activation.","Deficiencies in these natural stop mechanisms can cause bleeding disorders. Once the vessel has healed, the clot itself is removed by fibrinolysis (\"lysis\" = breakdown), carried out by the fibrinolytic system: tissue plasminogen activator (…"]},{"s":"Anticoagulant vs. Antiplatelet Therapy — Overview & Bleeding Surveillance▾","a":"anticoagulant-vs-antiplatelet-therapy-overview-bleeding-surveillance","k":["Anticoagulant vs. Antiplatelet Therapy — Overview & Bleeding Surveillance▾","Anticoagulants","Inhibit the action/formation of clotting factors.","Prevent a clot from forming, or prevent an existing clot from extending — but have no direct effect on an existing clot (don't break it up).","Antiplatelets","Inhibit platelet aggregation, preventing the platelet plug from forming.","Best for preventing heart attack and stroke.","Both classes are considered antithrombotic (prevent clot/thrombus formation), just by different mechanisms. Common indications: atrial fibrillation, heart disease/heart failure, history of DVT, prolonged immobility (e.g., ortho surgery), an…","The universal concern with anticoagulant/antiplatelet therapy is bleeding — internal (brain bleed, GI bleed — may not be obvious until it's an emergency) or external (prolonged bleeding from a cut, gum bleeding with a hard-bristle toothbrus…"]},{"s":"Heparin & Low-Molecular-Weight Heparin (Enoxaparin)▾","a":"heparin-low-molecular-weight-heparin-enoxaparin","k":["Heparin & Low-Molecular-Weight Heparin (Enoxaparin)▾","Heparin — Activates antithrombin, which indirectly inactivates both thrombin and factor Xa, inhibiting fibrin formation. Given IV or subcutaneously. Derived from pig lung/intestinal mucosa (may raise kosher/halal considerations; pig livesto…","Enoxaparin (Lovenox) — Low-molecular-weight heparin — cleaved into smaller fragments than heparin, giving greater factor Xa affinity, better bioavailability, and a longer half-life. Inactivates factor Xa only (not thrombin). Subcutaneous on…","Shared adverse effects: bleeding and thrombocytopenia (including HIT — see below). Use both cautiously with spinal/epidural anesthesia (hematoma risk — monitor for neurologic changes); enoxaparin carries a black box warning for spinal hemat…","Heparin safety: if giving protamine sulfate (the heparin/enoxaparin antidote — binds heparin, inactivating its anticoagulant effect) IV, push it slowly — too fast can cause profound hypotension. Also watch for heparin-induced thrombocytopen…"]},{"s":"Warfarin (Coumadin)▾","a":"warfarin-coumadin","k":["Warfarin (Coumadin)▾","Warfarin is a vitamin K inhibitor — it blocks vitamin K's role in synthesizing several coagulation factors, so those factors aren't made. Given PO only, once daily, typically in the evening. Onset is delayed ~24 hours after the dose, but du…","Adverse Effects — Bleeding, lethargy, muscle pain, and — notably — purple toes.","Antidote — Vitamin K (IV) — restores the substrate warfarin blocks. Fresh frozen plasma or whole blood can also replace the missing clotting factors directly.","Lab Monitoring — PT/INR. A person not on warfarin has an INR around 1 or less; the therapeutic range on warfarin is about 2–3.5 (goal varies by indication). Checked at baseline, then monthly once therapeutic; dose adjusted based on trend.","Pre-Op Holding — Held before surgery (including dental procedures) — anywhere from ~72 hours up to 2 weeks depending on the procedure and the patient's INR.","Significant interactions: many common medications (including OTC cimetidine) raise or lower INR — patients need to disclose all medications/supplements. Alcohol also thins the blood. Since warfarin blocks vitamin-K-dependent clotting factor…"]},{"s":"Direct Factor Xa Inhibitors — Apixaban & Rivaroxaban▾","a":"direct-factor-xa-inhibitors-apixaban-rivaroxaban","k":["Direct Factor Xa Inhibitors — Apixaban & Rivaroxaban▾","Apixaban (Eliquis) and rivaroxaban (Xarelto) directly inhibit factor Xa. Used preemptively before knee/hip replacement, to prevent stroke in atrial fibrillation, postoperatively for thromboprophylaxis, and to treat DVT/PE. Side effects: ble…","Interactions — St. John's wort, rifampin, and phenytoin decrease effectiveness. Both are CYP substrates — grapefruit juice and CYP inhibitors increase drug levels/bleeding risk. Don't combine with other anticoagulants.","Monitoring — No routine coagulation lab monitoring needed (a major selling point vs. warfarin) — but monitor liver function, since these drugs can affect it.","Antidote — Andexanet alfa (Andexxa) — recombinant factor Xa; a newer, extremely expensive antidote (early doses ran roughly $50,000), so it's used only when truly needed."]},{"s":"Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Aspirin▾","a":"antiplatelet-pharmacology-aspirin","k":["Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Aspirin▾","Aspirin is classified as an NSAID but is the only NSAID used for its antiplatelet effect: it blocks prostaglandin synthesis via the COX pathway, which also blocks platelet aggregation, making platelets less sticky. Used to prevent/treat MI …","For an acute MI, the patient chews a baby aspirin rather than swallowing it whole — and it must be a non-enteric-coated (plain, white) tablet, not the orange enteric-coated pill, since enteric coating is meant to survive intact to the intes…","Side effects: bleeding, nausea/vomiting, drowsiness, confusion. Enteric-coated/extended-release formulations reduce GI upset for routine (non-emergency) use — but never crush an enteric-coated tablet. Daily aspirin for primary cardiovascula…"]},{"s":"Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Clopidogrel, Ticagrelor & Direct Thrombin Inhibitors▾","a":"antiplatelet-pharmacology-clopidogrel-ticagrelor-direct-thrombin-inhibitors","k":["Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Clopidogrel, Ticagrelor & Direct Thrombin Inhibitors▾","Clopidogrel (Plavix) and ticagrelor (Brilinta) are ADP inhibitors — they alter the platelet membrane so it doesn't receive the aggregation signal, decreasing platelet accumulation. Used to reduce stroke risk, for TIA prophylaxis, and post-M…","Clopidogrel — Black box warning: certain genetic variants reduce conversion to the active metabolite, raising cardiovascular event risk — ask about genetic/family history before starting. Effectiveness is reduced by amiodarone, calcium chan…","Ticagrelor — Black box warning: increased bleeding risk with aspirin doses over 100 mg.","Direct thrombin inhibitors — argatroban and bivalirudin — irreversibly bind and inactivate thrombin, so it can't convert fibrinogen to fibrin. Given IV only; used to treat HIT and in patients undergoing PCI who are at high HIT risk from hep…"]},{"s":"DVT & VTE — Terminology and Virchow's Triad▾","a":"dvt-vte-terminology-and-virchow-s-triad","k":["DVT & VTE — Terminology and Virchow's Triad▾","A thrombus is a blood clot still attached to the vessel wall. Once it detaches, it's a thromboembolus; if it originated in the venous system, it's a venous thromboembolism (VTE). Thrombi form more often in veins than arteries because venous…","Virchow's Triad — Three Factors Promoting Venous Thrombosis","Venous stasis — slow blood flow. Risk: older age, immobility (hospitalized/post-op patients), heart failure.","Venous endothelial damage — injury to the vein lining, from trauma, certain IV medications, or surgery.","Hypercoagulable states — inherited clotting disorders, malignancy, pregnancy, oral contraceptives/hormone replacement, recent surgery, and genetic factors (prothrombin/antithrombin mutations).","Additional risk factors: ortho surgery (especially hip/knee replacement, hip fracture — high immobility, sometimes in casts), spinal cord injury, OB/GYN conditions, obesity, smoking, certain cancers, inflammatory bowel disease, prior person…"]},{"s":"DVT & VTE — Signs, Symptoms & Complications▾","a":"dvt-vte-signs-symptoms-complications","k":["DVT & VTE — Signs, Symptoms & Complications▾","Classic DVT findings: pain, redness, warmth, and swelling in the affected extremity (most often a lower extremity) — always compare the affected limb to the other side. Some patients are entirely asymptomatic. Significant obstruction to ven…","Pulmonary Embolism (PE) — A thrombus lodges in the pulmonary circulation, blocking gas exchange.","Chronic Thromboembolic Pulmonary Hypertension — Rare — repeated pulmonary clots raise pulmonary circulation pressure over time; presents with shortness of breath.","Post-Thrombotic Syndrome — Affects 8–70% of patients — chronic inflammation and venous hypertension from the thrombus blocking flow and damaging the vein wall. Presents with pain, aching, fatigue, swelling sensation, cramps, itching, heavin…","Phlegmasia cerulea dolens (\"painful blue inflammation\") — rare, but a true emergency: major leg veins become totally or near-totally occluded, causing sudden massive swelling, intense deep pain, and severe cyanosis. Left untreated, it can p…"]},{"s":"DVT & VTE — Prevention & Treatment▾","a":"dvt-vte-prevention-treatment","k":["DVT & VTE — Prevention & Treatment▾","Anticoagulation Therapy — For active DVT/VTE — see the coagulation modifier drugs above.","Mechanical Prevention — Sequential compression devices (SCDs) and TED hose (thromboembolism-deterrent stockings); aggressive/early mobilization of hospitalized and post-op patients.","Lab Monitoring — PT/INR and other bleeding-time labs for at-risk patients.","IVC Filter — An inferior vena cava interruption device — mechanically catches traveling clots before they reach the heart, lungs, or brain.","Embolectomy — Surgical removal of the embolus — effective, but a higher-risk procedure not commonly used."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-blood-cells-cbc.html","t":"Blood Cells & CBC Interpretation","secs":[{"s":"Blood Cells & CBC Interpretation","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Red Blood Cells, Hemoglobin & Hematocrit","White Blood Cells — Types & Function","Neutrophils & Lymphocytes — Shift to the Left","CBC with Differential — Interpreting Percentages vs. Absolute Counts","Leukocytosis, Leukopenia & Neutropenia","Infectious Mononucleosis","Myelodysplastic Syndrome","Leukemias — Acute vs. Chronic, Lymphocytic vs. Myelocytic","Malignant Lymphomas — Hodgkin vs. Non-Hodgkin","Multiple Myeloma","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: red and white blood cell basics, CBC/CBC-with-differential interpretation, leukocytosis/leukopenia/neutropenia, hematopoietic growth factors, and white blood cell disorders (mononucleosis, myelodysplastic syndrome,…"]},{"s":"Red Blood Cells, Hemoglobin & Hematocrit▾","a":"red-blood-cells-hemoglobin-hematocrit","k":["Red Blood Cells, Hemoglobin & Hematocrit▾","Erythrocytes (RBCs) are the most abundant blood cell, shaped like a biconcave disc (thick outer rim, thin center). They carry hemoglobin, which binds oxygen and delivers it to tissues — RBCs are the primary cell responsible for tissue oxyge…","RBC Count — Total erythrocytes in the blood. — ~4.2–6.2 million/mm³","Hemoglobin (Hgb) — Amount of hemoglobin in the blood — reflects oxygen-carrying capacity. Low with bleeding, folate/B12 deficiency, certain cancers, kidney/liver disease. High with polycythemia, COPD, high altitude, heavy smoking. — Males 1…","Hematocrit (Hct) — The percentage of blood volume made up of packed RBCs. Low with anemia, bleeding, or fluid overload (dilution). High with polycythemia, COPD, dehydration, shock, congenital heart disease — since hematocrit is a percentage…","Other RBC labs exist (mean corpuscular volume/MCV — RBC size; mean corpuscular hemoglobin/MCH — hemoglobin amount per RBC by weight) but are used less at the bedside — when an abnormal RBC lab comes back flagged, look it up and connect it t…"]},{"s":"White Blood Cells — Types & Function▾","a":"white-blood-cells-types-function","k":["White Blood Cells — Types & Function▾","Leukocytes (WBCs) exist to protect the body — immune function and inflammation. There are five types, and their percentages always add up to 100%.","\"Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas\" — Neutrophils, Lymphocytes, Monocytes, Eosinophils, Basophils: the five types of white blood cells.","Granulocytes","Have a nucleus with several lobes and granules that release inflammatory/immune mediators.","Neutrophils — most prevalent at a fresh injury/infection site; the first responders.","Eosinophils — role in phagocytosis tied to antigen-antibody reactions; rise with allergic reactions and parasitic infections.","Basophils — role in phagocytosis; rise with allergic/hypersensitivity and inflammatory reactions.","Agranulocytes","Fewer granules, don't release the same inflammatory mediators — but still participate in immune/inflammatory processes.","Lymphocytes (T-cells, B-cells) — primary immunity cells, usually reside in lymphoid tissue rather than circulating blood.","Monocytes/macrophages — potent phagocytosis, clean up cellular waste/debris; monocytes still secrete some cytokines despite being agranulocytes.","Eosinophils increase with \"worms, wheezes, and weird diseases\" — parasitic infections, allergic/asthma-type reactions, and other hypersensitivity conditions."]},{"s":"Neutrophils & Lymphocytes — Shift to the Left▾","a":"neutrophils-lymphocytes-shift-to-the-left","k":["Neutrophils & Lymphocytes — Shift to the Left▾","Neutrophils come in two forms: segs (mature, \"segmented\" neutrophils) and bands (immature neutrophils). Neutrophils rise with acute bacterial infection and trauma — in the acute stage, the bone marrow releases more immature bands than usual…","Lymphocytes rise with chronic bacterial infection and acute viral infection — in chronic bacterial infection, T- and B-cells act as memory cells; in acute viral infection, they act as active fighters. Think: neutrophils for something acute …"]},{"s":"CBC with Differential — Interpreting Percentages vs. Absolute Counts▾","a":"cbc-with-differential-interpreting-percentages-vs-absolute-counts","k":["CBC with Differential — Interpreting Percentages vs. Absolute Counts▾","A standard CBC reports WBC count, RBC count, hemoglobin, hematocrit, and platelets. A CBC with differential (\"diff\") goes further, breaking the total WBC count into the proportion of each of the five WBC types — those five percentages alway…","Leukocytosis — Total WBC count above normal (normal ≈ 5,000–10,000/mm³).","Leukopenia — Total WBC count below normal.","Neutropenia — Low neutrophil count specifically, measured by the absolute neutrophil count (ANC).","Because the \"diff\" percentages are relative to one another, a rising percentage in one WBC type mathematically pushes another type's percentage down — even if that second type's absolute count hasn't changed at all. Example: WBC count 10,00…"]},{"s":"Leukocytosis, Leukopenia & Neutropenia▾","a":"leukocytosis-leukopenia-neutropenia","k":["Leukocytosis, Leukopenia & Neutropenia▾","The total WBC count reflects how intense an immune/inflammatory process is — a large elevation suggests a bigger process than a modest one. Fever often accompanies infection and is a protective mechanism (not a disease itself) — it can slow…","Neutropenia (ANC < 1,000, most often from cancer treatment — chemo or radiation) leaves a patient with little to fight infection, making even a routine infection potentially life-threatening. Neutropenic precautions: meticulous hand hygiene…","Hematopoietic growth factors (granulocyte colony-stimulating factors, G-CSF) treat significant leukopenia/neutropenia: filgrastim (Neupogen) is a leukopoietic growth factor made via recombinant DNA technology that promotes granulocyte proli…"]},{"s":"Infectious Mononucleosis▾","a":"infectious-mononucleosis","k":["Infectious Mononucleosis▾","A self-limiting, lymphoproliferative infection of B lymphocytes, most often caused by Epstein-Barr virus (EBV). Most common in adolescents/young adults (college students) — about 1 in 4 teens/young adults who get EBV develop mono. Spread ma…","Clinical Manifestations — Lymphadenopathy (swollen neck/axillary nodes), hepatitis, splenomegaly, extreme fatigue, fever, sore throat, body aches.","Diagnosis — Elevated WBCs, ~95% lymphocytes.","Course — Acute phase ~2–3 weeks; full resolution takes 2–3 months. Avoid contact sports during this time (splenic rupture risk from splenomegaly).","Treatment — No specific treatment — supportive care (antipyretics, rest, hydration). No vaccine; risk reduction means not sharing food/drinks, kissing, or personal items like toothbrushes with an infected person."]},{"s":"Myelodysplastic Syndrome▾","a":"myelodysplastic-syndrome","k":["Myelodysplastic Syndrome▾","A group of related hematologic disorders — also called bone marrow failure disorder — where blood cells made in the bone marrow fail to mature properly, accumulate abnormally in the marrow, or have a shortened lifespan. Most common in older…","Hallmark finding: cytopenias (low counts across blood cell types), which can produce anemia, infection, and spontaneous bleeding/bruising. Diagnosed via labs and bone marrow biopsy. Treatment depends on severity — mild/chronic low-grade ane…"]},{"s":"Leukemias — Acute vs. Chronic, Lymphocytic vs. Myelocytic▾","a":"leukemias-acute-vs-chronic-lymphocytic-vs-myelocytic","k":["Leukemias — Acute vs. Chronic, Lymphocytic vs. Myelocytic▾","Leukemias are malignant neoplasms of immature, undifferentiated cells derived from a single hematopoietic cell line — these leukemic cells proliferate in the bone marrow, circulate in the blood, and infiltrate the spleen/lymph nodes. Classi…","Acute Lymphocytic Leukemia (ALL) — Most common childhood leukemia (~80% of childhood acute leukemias); peak age 3–7. 5-year survival: ~71.7% overall, ~91.9% in children.","Acute Myelocytic Leukemia (AML) — Primarily an adult disease (median age 64; ~80% of adult acute leukemia cases). 5-year survival: ~29.4% adults, ~68.7% children.","Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) — Most common leukemia in older adults; ~30% of all U.S. leukemia cases. Signs: fatigue, weight loss, anorexia, recurrent infections (leukemic B cells don't make normal antibodies). 5-year survival: ~88.2%…","Chronic Myelocytic Leukemia (CML) — ~15% of U.S. leukemia cases; typical onset age 40–50 (can occur in children). High granulocyte count and splenomegaly; signs include fatigue, weight loss, diaphoresis, bleeding, abdominal discomfort. 5-ye…","Acute leukemias have a sudden, \"stormy\" onset with signs tied to decreased mature WBCs/RBCs/platelets — bone pain, bruising, fever, fatigue, infection, unexplained pain (in children, sometimes a sudden refusal to walk). Diagnosed when immat…","Treatment aims for remission: cytotoxic chemotherapy first (to destroy rapidly dividing cells), followed in some cases by bone marrow/stem cell transplant (donor types: volunteer donor, identical twin, or the patient's own cells; typically …"]},{"s":"Malignant Lymphomas — Hodgkin vs. Non-Hodgkin▾","a":"malignant-lymphomas-hodgkin-vs-non-hodgkin","k":["Malignant Lymphomas — Hodgkin vs. Non-Hodgkin▾","Lymphomas are neoplasms of cells derived from lymphoid tissue.","Hodgkin Disease (Hodgkin Lymphoma)","~30% of malignant lymphomas; occurs across all ages.","Painless, progressive, rubbery enlargement of a single lymph node or group — most often cervical (neck).","Diagnostic hallmark: Reed-Sternberg cells (originate from B cells) on biopsy.","Spreads/grows in a predictable pattern — this predictability supports a relatively good prognosis.","Other findings: fever, night sweats, weight loss, malaise. Treated with chemotherapy, radiation, or stem cell transplant.","Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma","No Reed-Sternberg cells. Can arise from lymph nodes or any lymphoid tissue; most common in older adults.","Spread is unpredictable and often early to liver, spleen, bone marrow — less certain prognosis, often diagnosed at a later, more widespread stage.","Painless lymph node enlargement plus extranodal involvement (skin, GI tract, bone/bone marrow), fever, night sweats, weight loss, malaise, itchiness.","Treated with chemotherapy, radiation, stem cell transplant (refractory cases), or immune-modulating drugs (e.g., rituximab)."]},{"s":"Multiple Myeloma▾","a":"multiple-myeloma","k":["Multiple Myeloma▾","Also called plasma cell myeloma — a malignant disorder of mature, antibody-secreting B lymphocytes (plasma cells). Produces abnormal immunoglobulins/fragments, including a monoclonal antibody (M protein), and increases osteoclast activity, …","Epidemiology — Typically diagnosed at age 65+; more common in African American/Black patients than white/Caucasian patients. Unknown etiology.","Presentation — Slow, insidious onset. Bone pain is usually the first symptom (sometimes discovered via a fracture or back pain workup); also anemia, recurrent infections, and bleeding (bone marrow depression); hypercalcemia (from bone break…","Diagnosis — Labs, imaging, bone marrow exam. Findings: monoclonal antibody protein in serum/urine, pancytopenia (all blood counts low), hypercalcemia, Bence Jones protein (urine), elevated serum creatinine (kidney involvement), and osteolyt…","Treatment — Ranges from watchful waiting to steroids, chemotherapy, biologic therapy, stem cell transplant, and bisphosphonates; ensure adequate hydration."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-fluid-electrolytes.html","t":"Fluid & Electrolytes","secs":[{"s":"Fluid & Electrolytes","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Fluid Compartments, Movement & Pressures","Electrolyte Basics","Sodium Imbalances — Hyponatremia & Hypernatremia","Sodium Correction & Sodium Bicarbonate","Potassium Imbalances — Hypokalemia & Hyperkalemia","Potassium Replacement — Oral & IV","Hyperkalemia Treatment","Magnesium Imbalances & Replacement","Calcium Imbalances","Calcium & Phosphorus Treatment","Phosphorus Imbalances","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: fluid compartments and fluid movement (diffusion, osmosis, colloids, hydrostatic/oncotic pressure), electrolyte basics, and imbalances/replacement for sodium, potassium, magnesium, calcium, and phosphorus."]},{"s":"Fluid Compartments, Movement & Pressures▾","a":"fluid-compartments-movement-pressures","k":["Fluid Compartments, Movement & Pressures▾","About 50–60% of adult body weight is water — fluid containing dissolved/suspended substances like glucose, electrolytes, and proteins. It's stored in two spaces: intracellular (inside cells — about 70% of body fluid) and extracellular (outs…","Osmosis — Water, across a semi-permeable membrane. — Low solute concentration → high solute concentration, until concentrations equalize (or hydrostatic pressure opposes further movement).","Diffusion — Molecules/solutes. — High concentration → low concentration, until concentration is equal. Liquid volume stays the same.","The body constantly works toward homeostasis — balancing solvents (fluid) and solutes (electrolytes/particles). Osmotic pressure drives water across the cell membrane toward the more concentrated side: if solutes build up on one side of a m…","Colloids","Substances that raise colloid osmotic pressure (also called oncotic pressure) and pull fluid from the interstitial compartment into the plasma/blood compartment.","Three primary colloids: albumin (the main one), globulin, and fibrinogen — measured together as total protein level (a measure of oncotic pressure, not an indicator of dietary protein intake).","Colloid oncotic pressure decreases with age and with malnutrition; can be replaced with colloid replacement products (more detail in Med-Surg/critical care).","Hydrostatic Pressure — Blood pressure, at the capillary level. — Pushes fluid out of the vascular system into the interstitial space, supplying nutrients to tissues/cells.","Oncotic (Colloid Osmotic) Pressure — Plasma colloids (mainly albumin). — Pulls fluid into the vascular space from tissue — plasma proteins attract water, which helps remove metabolic waste from cells/tissues."]},{"s":"Electrolyte Basics▾","a":"electrolyte-basics","k":["Electrolyte Basics▾","Electrolytes are substances that carry an electrical charge in solution. They influence fluid balance, acid-base balance, nerve impulses, muscle contraction, heart rhythm, and many other cell functions — and because electrolytes work togeth…","Intracellular — Potassium, magnesium, phosphorus.","Extracellular — Sodium, chloride, bicarbonate, calcium.","Sodium, potassium, calcium, and magnesium are positively charged (cations); phosphorus, chloride, and bicarbonate are negatively charged (anions). Positive and negative charges balance each other out — a process called electroneutrality — w…","Memory hooks for which body system each major electrolyte imbalance tends to hit first: sodium → brain (confusion, altered LOC), potassium → heart (cardiac rhythm disturbances), magnesium → muscle (\"M for magnesium, M for muscle movement\"),…"]},{"s":"Sodium Imbalances — Hyponatremia & Hypernatremia▾","a":"sodium-imbalances-hyponatremia-hypernatremia","k":["Sodium Imbalances — Hyponatremia & Hypernatremia▾","Normal sodium: 136–145. Below 136 is hyponatremia; above 145 is hypernatremia. Sodium is the main extracellular cation and the primary driver of fluid balance — \"where salt goes, water follows.\" Sodium governs osmolality (concentration in b…","Hyponatremia (< 136)","Causes: GI losses (diarrhea, vomiting, fistulas, NG suction), renal losses (adrenal insufficiency, diuretics), skin losses (burns, wound damage), fasting/not eating or drinking, polydipsia (water intoxication), or excess hypotonic fluid.","Signs/symptoms: confusion and altered LOC (cells swell as water shifts in — brain cells are especially sensitive), anorexia, muscle weakness; if severe/untreated, seizures and coma. CNS symptoms appear first.","Hypernatremia (> 145)","Causes: essentially never dietary intake (near-impossible to eat enough salt) — instead IV fluids/tube feeds high in sodium, saltwater near-drowning, or inadequate water intake/excess water loss (severe diarrhea, high fever, heat stroke, pr…","Signs/symptoms: altered LOC, confusion, seizure, coma, extreme thirst, dry/sticky mucous membranes, muscle cramps — cells shrink as water shifts out.","Hyponatremia specifically breaks down into two causes with opposite fluid pictures, both showing the same CNS symptoms:","Dilutional Hyponatremia — Too much water taken in — sodium is diluted. — Hypervolemia: increased blood pressure, weight gain, rapid/bounding pulse, increased urine specific gravity.","Depletional Hyponatremia — An actual loss of sodium and fluid. — Hypovolemia: decreased blood pressure, tachycardia, dry skin, weight loss, decreased urine specific gravity."]},{"s":"Sodium Correction & Sodium Bicarbonate▾","a":"sodium-correction-sodium-bicarbonate","k":["Sodium Correction & Sodium Bicarbonate▾","Sodium imbalances must be corrected slowly — think sodium, think brain, think slow. Correcting hyponatremia too fast (especially one that's lasted > 48 hours) causes brain dehydration and risks osmotic demyelination syndrome, which can lead…","Depletional Hyponatremia — Sodium replacement (PO or IV — often normal saline); withhold diuretics until the imbalance is fixed.","Dilutional Hyponatremia — Fluid restriction instead of sodium replacement — fluid levels come down and sodium concentration corrects.","Hypernatremia (sodium excess) — Remove sodium, via fluids.","Hypernatremia (water loss) — Add water.","Sodium bicarbonate dissociates to provide bicarbonate ions, neutralizing acid concentration and raising blood/urine pH — its main indication is metabolic acidosis, though it also raises plasma sodium concentration. It's reserved for people …"]},{"s":"Potassium Imbalances — Hypokalemia & Hyperkalemia▾","a":"potassium-imbalances-hypokalemia-hyperkalemia","k":["Potassium Imbalances — Hypokalemia & Hyperkalemia▾","Normal potassium: 3.5–5. Below 3.5 is hypokalemia; above 5 is hyperkalemia. Potassium is an intracellular cation — 98% of the body's potassium lives inside cells, maintained by an ATP-driven active transport pump. It's the main driver of ca…","Hypokalemia (< 3.5)","Causes: renal/GI losses (diarrhea, vomiting, ileostomy drainage, diuresis/furosemide), acid-base disorders (alkalosis shifts potassium from extracellular into intracellular space). Rarely from inadequate dietary intake alone.","Signs/symptoms: cardiac rhythm disturbances (can be lethal if severe), muscle weakness, leg cramps, decreased bowel motility → constipation, nausea, ileus.","Hyperkalemia (> 5)","Causes: decreased potassium output (renal failure, low urine output), massive cell injury releasing intracellular potassium (burns, crush injuries, sepsis), or certain drugs (potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs).","Signs/symptoms: peaked T waves on ECG, muscle weakness, cramps, abdominal cramping, diarrhea, vomiting."]},{"s":"Potassium Replacement — Oral & IV▾","a":"potassium-replacement-oral-iv","k":["Potassium Replacement — Oral & IV▾","Potassium chloride (KCl) is given to treat/prevent potassium depletion when dietary measures aren't enough — there's no real \"mechanism of action\" beyond replacing what's missing. Oral forms (powder mixed into liquid, or liquid) taste awful…","IV potassium must always be diluted and can never be pushed IV — undiluted IV potassium can cause ventricular fibrillation, a lethal rhythm incompatible with life. It must be given slowly (usually over an hour or more): max 10 mEq/hr periph…"]},{"s":"Hyperkalemia Treatment▾","a":"hyperkalemia-treatment","k":["Hyperkalemia Treatment▾","Kayexalate (Sodium Polystyrene Sulfonate) — A cation exchange resin — binds potassium in the digestive tract and swaps it for sodium, lowering serum potassium by about 0.5–1 mEq/L over 4–6 hours. Given PO, or rectally as a powder/suspension…","D50 + Regular Insulin — A combination (10 units regular insulin + 1 ampule of 50% dextrose) that shifts potassium temporarily from outside cells to inside — used in urgent/emergent situations (e.g., cardiac dysrhythmia from hyperkalemia) fo…","Insulin's most important safety rule — always check a current blood sugar before the dose — applies here too. With the D50/insulin cocktail, recheck blood sugar about 5 minutes after giving the D50 (or sooner if hypoglycemia symptoms appear…"]},{"s":"Magnesium Imbalances & Replacement▾","a":"magnesium-imbalances-replacement","k":["Magnesium Imbalances & Replacement▾","Normal magnesium: 1.3–2.1. Below 1.3 is hypomagnesemia; above 2.1 is hypermagnesemia. Magnesium and potassium are closely linked — magnesium blocks/controls potassium's movement out of cardiac cells, stabilizing cardiac and smooth muscle.","Hypomagnesemia (< 1.3)","Causes: diuresis, GI/renal losses, limited intake (starvation, fasting), alcohol abuse, pancreatitis, hyperglycemia.","Signs/symptoms: overactive-muscle picture — hyperactive reflexes, confusion, cramps, tremors, seizures, and nystagmus (rapid, uncontrolled eye movement — side to side, up/down, or circular; usually bilateral).","Hypermagnesemia (> 2.1)","Causes: increased intake accompanied by renal failure (e.g., milk of magnesia in a patient with chronic renal failure — think elderly patients).","Signs/symptoms: opposite of low magnesium — lethargy, floppiness, muscle weakness, decreased reflexes, flushed/warm skin; very high levels can drop pulse/blood pressure.","Magnesium sulfate (IV) or magnesium oxide (PO) replace magnesium — one of the few electrolytes that can be given IV push if absolutely necessary, though IV administration can burn. Beyond replacing a deficiency, magnesium sulfate is also us…"]},{"s":"Calcium Imbalances▾","a":"calcium-imbalances","k":["Calcium Imbalances▾","Normal calcium: 9–10.5. Below 9 is hypocalcemia; above 10.5 is hypercalcemia. About 99% of body calcium is stored in bone (thyroid/parathyroid hormones control release from and absorption into bone); of the small remainder, about 40% is pro…","Hypocalcemia (< 9) — Inability to mobilize calcium from bone (hypoparathyroidism, hypomagnesemia), increased renal loss (renal failure), increased binding, decreased intake/absorption or vitamin D, acute pancreatitis, thyroid/parathyroid su…","Hypercalcemia (> 10.5) — Hyperparathyroidism, certain cancers (lung, breast, hematologic), immobility, antacid overdose (e.g., Tums). — Acts as a sedative: fatigue, lethargy, confusion, weakness; very high/prolonged levels can cause seizure…","Two Classic Signs of Low Calcium","Trousseau's sign — inflate a blood pressure cuff to ~20 mmHg above the patient's systolic pressure for about 3 minutes; a resulting carpal (wrist/hand) spasm is a positive sign.","Chvostek's sign — gently tap the facial nerve just in front of the ear; ipsilateral twitching of facial muscles is a positive sign."]},{"s":"Calcium & Phosphorus Treatment▾","a":"calcium-phosphorus-treatment","k":["Calcium & Phosphorus Treatment▾","Hypocalcemia — IV calcium chloride (ionized, preferred form) or calcium gluconate — both are vesicants, given via central line. Oral calcium (elemental calcium, calcium carbonate/Tums) is also used. May also need vitamin D — an active form …","Hypercalcemia — Adequate hydration (3–4 L/day) to increase urine output, promote renal calcium excretion, and reduce kidney stone risk; diuretics (sodium excretion is accompanied by calcium excretion); dialysis if the patient has renal fail…","Phosphorus has an inverse relationship with calcium — low calcium accompanies high phosphate, and vice versa. Normal phosphorus: 3.0–4.5 (below 3.0 is hypophosphatemia, above 4.5 is hyperphosphatemia). About 85% of body phosphorus is in bon…"]},{"s":"Phosphorus Imbalances▾","a":"phosphorus-imbalances","k":["Phosphorus Imbalances▾","Hypophosphatemia (< 3.0) — Decreased absorption (antacid overdose), severe diarrhea, increased kidney elimination, malnutrition (alcoholism, TPN use, recovery from malnutrition — re-feeding syndrome, where reintroducing nutrition too fast i…","Hyperphosphatemia (> 4.5) — Kidney failure, phosphate-containing laxatives/enemas, massive trauma or heat stroke (intracellular phosphate shifts out), hypoparathyroidism. — Usually asymptomatic on its own — symptoms seen are actually from t…","Treatment: for low phosphate, IV (given slowly over a long period) or oral replacement — use oral intake cautiously in chronic kidney disease/hypocalcemia (risk of phosphate calcifications in joints). For high phosphate, treat the cause — c…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-pain-seizures.html","t":"Pain & Seizures","secs":[{"s":"Pain & Seizures","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Pain Basics — Definition, Neuroanatomy & Nociceptors","The Physiology of Pain — Four Phases & Nerve Fiber Types","Pain Threshold, Tolerance & the Gate Control Theory","Pain & Inflammation — Chemical Mediators","Classifying Pain — Nociceptive vs. Neuropathic, Referred & Phantom Pain","Atypical Analgesics — Tramadol, Gabapentin & Pregabalin","NSAIDs — COX-1 vs. COX-2 Mechanism","NSAIDs — Individual Drugs, Black Box Warnings & Reye Syndrome","Acetaminophen — Pharmacology, Dosing Limits & Toxicity","Opioid Basics — Classes, Assessment & Respiratory Depression","Individual Opioids — Morphine, Hydromorphone & Fentanyl","Individual Opioids — Meperidine, Codeine, Oxycodone, Hydrocodone & Methadone","Naloxone, the Opioid-Naive Patient & Common Opioid Side Effects","Seizure Terminology, Pathogenesis & Classification","Antiepileptic Drug (AED) Therapy — Principles","Individual AEDs & Rapid Seizure Management","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: pain pathophysiology (nociception, pain classification), non-opioid and adjuvant analgesic pharmacology (NSAIDs, acetaminophen, tramadol, gabapentin/pregabalin), opioid pharmacology (morphine, hydromorphone, fentan…"]},{"s":"Pain Basics — Definition, Neuroanatomy & Nociceptors▾","a":"pain-basics-definition-neuroanatomy-nociceptors","k":["Pain Basics — Definition, Neuroanatomy & Nociceptors▾","As a nurse, pain is whatever the patient says it is — a complex experience shaped by physical, cognitive, spiritual, emotional, and environmental factors, plus previous experience and genetics. Acute pain is protective: it promotes withdraw…","Three parts of the nervous system are involved in pain: afferent pathways (begin in the peripheral nervous system, travel to the spinal cord and up into the cortex), interpretive centers (cortical/subcortical brain areas that interpret the …","Nociceptors","Pain receptors — free nerve endings in the afferent peripheral nervous system. Stimulation causes nociceptive pain.","Respond to mechanical (pressure/distortion), thermal (heat/cold), and chemical (acids, inflammatory chemicals) stimuli — but only once a stimulus reaches a certain intensity/threshold.","Unevenly distributed — dense in skin, dental pulp, meninges, and some internal organs (more acute pain felt there); sparse in the brain, alveoli, and deep tissue (less acute pain felt there)."]},{"s":"The Physiology of Pain — Four Phases & Nerve Fiber Types▾","a":"the-physiology-of-pain-four-phases-nerve-fiber-types","k":["The Physiology of Pain — Four Phases & Nerve Fiber Types▾","Neurotransmitters modulate pain transmission — excitatory ones (norepinephrine, acetylcholine, dopamine, serotonin) enhance/transmit pain signals and sensitize nociceptors; inhibitory ones (GABA — gamma-aminobutyric acid) suppress it. Endor…","Transduction — A painful stimulus is converted to an action potential at the sensory receptor. Chemical mediators (e.g., prostaglandins) released with injury/inflammation lower the pain threshold.","Transmission — The pain impulse conducts along A-delta and C fibers into the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, then up through the brain to the thalamus.","Perception — The message reaches the somatosensory cortex — conscious awareness of pain's location/intensity. Influenced by attention, distraction, anxiety, fear, fatigue, prior experience, genetics, age, and culture.","Modulation — Synaptic transmission of the pain signal is amplified or dampened — e.g., by endorphins closing spinal cord \"gates,\" similar to how opioid analgesics work.","A-delta Fibers","Small diameter (not the smallest); transmit sharp, well-localized, \"fast\" pain (stinging, cutting, pinching).","Can trigger a spinal reflex withdrawal before pain is even perceived (e.g., a needle stick).","C Fibers","Even smaller diameter, far more numerous; found in muscles, tendons, organs, and skin.","Transmit dull, aching, burning, poorly-localized pain — often constant."]},{"s":"Pain Threshold, Tolerance & the Gate Control Theory▾","a":"pain-threshold-tolerance-the-gate-control-theory","k":["Pain Threshold, Tolerance & the Gate Control Theory▾","Pain Threshold — The lowest intensity of pain a person can recognize. Has \"perceptual dominance\" — intense pain at one site can raise the threshold (mask pain) elsewhere. Raised by stress, exertion, acupuncture, and sexual activity.","Pain Tolerance — The greatest intensity of pain a person can handle — varies widely between and within people. Decreased by repeated pain exposure, fatigue, anger, boredom, apprehension, sleep deprivation. Increased by alcohol, persistent o…","Opioid Tolerance — An adaptation from opioid exposure that changes drug receptors over time — does not by itself indicate addiction.","The gate control theory: stimulating large-diameter nerve fibers (A-alpha, A-beta) via touch, massage, distraction, or activity can \"close the gate\" on pain signals traveling through the smaller A-delta/C fibers, attenuating the pain signal…"]},{"s":"Pain & Inflammation — Chemical Mediators▾","a":"pain-inflammation-chemical-mediators","k":["Pain & Inflammation — Chemical Mediators▾","Tissue injury triggers chemical mediators (from plasma) that produce inflammation's signs — pain, swelling, redness, warmth, immobility. Key mediators: histamine and arachidonic acid metabolites (prostaglandins, leukotrienes). Prostaglandin…","Acute Pain","Sudden onset; resolves once the injury heals or mediators clear. Lasts seconds to under 3 months.","Stimulates the autonomic nervous system: increased heart rate/blood pressure, sweating, anxiety, dilated pupils.","Chronic Pain","Lasts > 3–6 months, beyond expected healing time; often out of proportion to observable tissue damage. Can be ongoing or intermittent (e.g., migraines).","Does not produce the same ANS response as acute pain, but causes significant psychosocial/emotional burden. Linked to CNS/peripheral nervous system sensitization changes."]},{"s":"Classifying Pain — Nociceptive vs. Neuropathic, Referred & Phantom Pain▾","a":"classifying-pain-nociceptive-vs-neuropathic-referred-phantom-pain","k":["Classifying Pain — Nociceptive vs. Neuropathic, Referred & Phantom Pain▾","Nociceptive — Cutaneous/Somatic — Pain receptors in/around skin, joints, tendons, musculoskeletal system. — Aching, constant; can be sharp/well-localized (A-delta) or dull/less-localized (C fibers, e.g., a broken bone).","Nociceptive — Visceral — Organs, often with inflammation; transmitted via C fibers. — Poorly localized, deep, diffuse; cramping, splitting, nausea/vomiting, diaphoresis. Often produces referred pain.","Neuropathic — Originates within the central nervous system — a lesion/injury to the nerves themselves; more associated with chronic pain. — Shooting, burning, electric-shock, sharp, numbness, motor weakness; poorly localized. Often does not…","Referred pain is felt at a distance from its actual source, since the brain can't always distinguish the origin — classically, chest/jaw/neck/shoulder pain during a myocardial infarction. Phantom pain originates at the site of an amputated …"]},{"s":"Atypical Analgesics — Tramadol, Gabapentin & Pregabalin▾","a":"atypical-analgesics-tramadol-gabapentin-pregabalin","k":["Atypical Analgesics — Tramadol, Gabapentin & Pregabalin▾","Tramadol (Ultram) — A \"miscellaneous analgesic\" — binds weakly to mu opioid receptors and inhibits norepinephrine/serotonin reuptake. PO only (plain, combined with acetaminophen, or extended-release). Used for moderate-severe pain. Mild sid…","Gabapentin (Neurontin) & Pregabalin (Lyrica) — Technically anticonvulsants, used for pain management — specifically neuropathic pain. Mechanism not fully known; thought to suppress neuronal firing. Complement/enhance opioid analgesia and ca…"]},{"s":"NSAIDs — COX-1 vs. COX-2 Mechanism▾","a":"nsaids-cox-1-vs-cox-2-mechanism","k":["NSAIDs — COX-1 vs. COX-2 Mechanism▾","NSAIDs are anti-prostaglandins — they block the cyclooxygenase (COX) enzyme needed to produce prostaglandins. Used for mild-moderate pain (headache, myalgia, arthritis, post-op pain), inflammation, and fever.","COX-1 — Protects gastric mucosa; needed for thromboxane synthesis (platelet aggregation) — largely beneficial, \"housekeeping\" prostaglandins. — Loses GI protection and platelet function — the source of NSAID GI/bleeding side effects.","COX-2 — Responsible for inflammation and fever. — Stops inflammation/fever — the therapeutic effect, without necessarily disrupting COX-1's protective roles.","Non-selective NSAIDs (aspirin, ibuprofen, naproxen, ketorolac) block both COX-1 and COX-2 — effective, but with the full GI/bleeding side-effect profile. Selective COX-2 inhibitors (celecoxib/Celebrex) block only COX-2, preserving COX-1's G…"]},{"s":"NSAIDs — Individual Drugs, Black Box Warnings & Reye Syndrome▾","a":"nsaids-individual-drugs-black-box-warnings-reye-syndrome","k":["NSAIDs — Individual Drugs, Black Box Warnings & Reye Syndrome▾","Aspirin — Non-selective, irreversible. Combined with caffeine = Excedrin; enteric-coated forms exist (never crush). Antithrombotic (more so than ibuprofen/naproxen). Not part of the NSAID cardiovascular black box warning (unlike the others)…","Ibuprofen — Non-selective — easiest on the stomach of the non-selective NSAIDs; the most commonly used.","Naproxen — Non-selective — hardest on the kidneys, but longer-acting than ibuprofen (once-daily dosing).","Ketorolac (Toradol) — Non-selective — the most potent NSAID, given IV/IM (rarely PO). Analgesic effect comparable to morphine without the addiction/respiratory-depression risk — useful for opioid-avoidant or opioid-addicted patients. Very h…","Celecoxib (Celebrex) — Selective COX-2 inhibitor — less GI toxicity than non-selective NSAIDs (not zero), higher cardiovascular thrombotic risk.","Since 2006, the FDA requires NSAID black box warnings for cardiovascular risk (all except aspirin) and GI risk (bleeding/ulceration, can be fatal — watch elderly patients and long-term users especially). Aspirin also carries its own risk of…","Reye syndrome: never give aspirin to children — especially children under 15 with a viral/flu-like illness. It's rare but causes severe swelling of the liver and brain, with a high mortality rate. Acetaminophen and ibuprofen are the appropr…"]},{"s":"Acetaminophen — Pharmacology, Dosing Limits & Toxicity▾","a":"acetaminophen-pharmacology-dosing-limits-toxicity","k":["Acetaminophen — Pharmacology, Dosing Limits & Toxicity▾","Acetaminophen (Tylenol/APAP) is not an NSAID — no anti-inflammatory activity, but does have analgesic and antipyretic effects (acts on the hypothalamus; heat dissipates via vasodilation). Mechanism is largely unknown, thought to decrease CN…","Acetaminophen overdose causes hepatotoxicity — a toxic metabolite is normally detoxified by liver glutathione stores; an overdose depletes glutathione, letting the toxic metabolite accumulate and cause hepatic necrosis. Adult limit: 4 grams…"]},{"s":"Opioid Basics — Classes, Assessment & Respiratory Depression▾","a":"opioid-basics-classes-assessment-respiratory-depression","k":["Opioid Basics — Classes, Assessment & Respiratory Depression▾","All opioids are high-alert drugs, derived originally from the opium poppy; today mostly synthetic. Three chemical classes: morphine-like, meperidine-like, and methadone-like — a patient's allergy to one class doesn't necessarily apply to an…","Respiratory depression is the biggest concern with any opioid — it's what kills in an overdose. If respiratory rate is < 10 breaths/minute, assess sedation level and use physical stimulation to try to arouse the patient; if significant stim…"]},{"s":"Individual Opioids — Morphine, Hydromorphone & Fentanyl▾","a":"individual-opioids-morphine-hydromorphone-fentanyl","k":["Individual Opioids — Morphine, Hydromorphone & Fentanyl▾","Morphine — The prototype opioid — a mu-receptor agonist mimicking endogenous endorphins. Schedule II, high abuse potential. Given PO, IV, epidurally, or rectally; used for moderate-severe pain. MS Contin = extended-release (12–24 hr), good …","Hydromorphone (Dilaudid) — Semi-synthetic, similar to morphine but 7× more potent (1 mg IV hydromorphone ≈ 7 mg IV morphine — typical doses 0.2–1 mg). Schedule II; PO or IV; used for severe pain. Same adverse effect profile as other opioids…","Fentanyl — Fully synthetic, extremely potent (0.1 mg IV fentanyl ≈ 10 mg IV morphine — dosed in micrograms). Used for moderate-severe (especially severe) pain, surgical induction, and chronic pain (transdermal patch, worn ~72 hr — not for o…"]},{"s":"Individual Opioids — Meperidine, Codeine, Oxycodone, Hydrocodone & Methadone▾","a":"individual-opioids-meperidine-codeine-oxycodone-hydrocodone-methadone","k":["Individual Opioids — Meperidine, Codeine, Oxycodone, Hydrocodone & Methadone▾","Meperidine (Demerol) — Synthetic, Schedule II, weaker/shorter-acting than morphine with less respiratory depression — but rarely used today. A toxic metabolite accumulates with repeated dosing and can cause seizures, so it's not recommended…","Codeine — Schedule II alone, Schedule III combined with acetaminophen. Used for mild-moderate pain, but more often as an antitussive (cough suppressant). PO only. Not given to children under 18 (life-threatening breathing problems). Strong …","Oxycodone — Schedule II, semi-synthetic derivative of codeine, ~10× more potent — PO only, for moderate-severe pain. High abuse potential (OxyContin = time-release form). Combined with acetaminophen = Percocet.","Hydrocodone — For mild-moderate pain and cough relief — about 6× more potent than codeine (less potent than oxycodone), usually combined with acetaminophen (Lortab, Norco, Vicodin). PO only.","Methadone — Fully synthetic, Schedule II — the opioid of choice for opioid-addiction detoxification/treatment. Long half-life (dosed q12–24h vs. q4–6h for most opioids); still addictive, but with less euphoric \"high.\" PO."]},{"s":"Naloxone, the Opioid-Naive Patient & Common Opioid Side Effects▾","a":"naloxone-the-opioid-naive-patient-common-opioid-side-effects","k":["Naloxone, the Opioid-Naive Patient & Common Opioid Side Effects▾","Naloxone (Narcan) is an opioid antagonist — it binds mu receptors without activating them, blocking other opioids from doing so. No analgesic effect, and doesn't itself cause respiratory depression, which is why it's the drug of choice to r…","Constipation — Very common — proactively give stool softeners, encourage hydration/nutrition. Untreated, it can progress to paralytic ileus (especially post-op).","Nausea/Vomiting — Common enough that some patients refuse opioids over it — may need a concurrent antiemetic.","Care should be collaborative — some patients decline opioids due to personal or family history of substance use disorder, fear, or simply disliking the effect; respect those preferences and consider adjuvants/alternatives. These side effect…"]},{"s":"Seizure Terminology, Pathogenesis & Classification▾","a":"seizure-terminology-pathogenesis-classification","k":["Seizure Terminology, Pathogenesis & Classification▾","Seizure — A brief episode of abnormal electrical activity in brain neurons — can have motor, sensory, and/or cognitive manifestations.","Convulsion — A more severe seizure with involuntary spasmodic muscle contractions.","Epilepsy — A disease involving recurrent, paroxysmal seizure activity — requires more than one seizure with no evidence of a reversible metabolic cause.","Myoclonic Seizure — Brief, shock-like jerks of a muscle/muscle group, often occurring in a row — easily mistaken for a tic, tremor, or clumsiness.","A seizure starts at the seizure focus — a group of neurons (often near scar tissue/gliosis) firing abnormally and autonomously, which can \"recruit\" nearby neurons and spread the seizure locally or globally. About 50% of seizures are primary…","Partial Seizure — Focal Seizure (one area of the brain)","Simple Seizure — Aware (patient's awareness intact during the seizure)","Complex Seizure — Impaired Awareness","Grand Mal Seizure — Generalized Onset, Tonic-Clonic","Classification uses three features: where it originates (focal, generalized, or unknown), the patient's level of awareness, and other features like motor involvement. Generalized onset seizures involve both brain hemispheres at once. Subtyp…","Status epilepticus — a series of seizures without a postictal recovery period, or a single seizure lasting 30 minutes or more. A true emergency, especially with tonic-clonic seizures: it can progress to respiratory arrest, hypoxia, permanen…"]},{"s":"Antiepileptic Drug (AED) Therapy — Principles▾","a":"antiepileptic-drug-aed-therapy-principles","k":["Antiepileptic Drug (AED) Therapy — Principles▾","AEDs (also called anticonvulsants) aim to control/prevent seizures while maintaining quality of life — a constant risk/benefit balance, since these drugs carry serious side effects. About 70% of patients become seizure-free on a single AED;…","Never abruptly stop an antiepileptic drug — doing so will cause seizures. AEDs also carry a black box warning for increased risk of suicidal ideation, worsening depression, and unusual mood changes — educate every patient starting one, and …","Therapy typically starts as monotherapy, slowly titrated with serum drug level monitoring to find the therapeutic range that balances seizure control against toxicity — providers switch to a different single drug rather than stacking multip…"]},{"s":"Individual AEDs & Rapid Seizure Management▾","a":"individual-aeds-rapid-seizure-management","k":["Individual AEDs & Rapid Seizure Management▾","Phenytoin (Dilantin) — First-line for tonic-clonic and focal seizures. PO usually, IV for emergent use. Highly protein-bound (use cautiously with malnutrition, CKD, low albumin) and a CYP450 inducer, so many drug interactions. Therapeutic r…","Valproic Acid (Depakote) — Works for essentially every seizure type (generalized, absence, myoclonic, tonic-clonic, and focal) — unusual versatility. Also used for bipolar disorder. PO or IV, highly protein-bound. Contraindicated in liver d…","Topiramate (Topamax) — Adjunct for focal and secondary generalized tonic-clonic seizures. Mechanism poorly understood (structurally similar to fructose). PO only. Watch for visual changes (can cause acute angle-closure glaucoma); can intera…","Levetiracetam (Keppra) — Adjunct for focal seizures (with or without generalization) — widely used inpatient, less so outpatient. Mechanism not well understood, generally well tolerated. PO or IV.","Rapid seizure management: most seizures stop spontaneously within about 2 minutes without intervention. For an actively epileptic or seizing inpatient, IV access is recommended for rapid medication administration; the inpatient gold standar…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-dementia-depression-sud.html","t":"Dementia, Depression & SUD","secs":[{"s":"Dementia, Depression & SUD","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Delirium — Hyperactive vs. Hypoactive","Delirium vs. Dementia & Delirium Treatment","Dementia — Pathophysiology & Types","Alzheimer Disease","Vascular & Frontotemporal Dementia","Dementia Medications — Donepezil & Memantine","Neurotransmitters in Mood & Anxiety Disorders","Depression Pathophysiology","Panic Disorder & Generalized Anxiety Disorder","PTSD, Social Anxiety Disorder & OCD","SSRIs & SNRIs","TCAs & MAOIs","Atypical Antidepressants & Benzodiazepines","Substance Use Disorder — Pathophysiology, Risk & Protective Factors","SUD Treatment, Screening & Medication-Assisted Treatment","Withdrawal Syndromes — Opioids vs. Benzodiazepines/Alcohol","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: delirium and dementia (Alzheimer's, vascular, frontotemporal) and dementia pharmacology, depression and anxiety pathophysiology (panic, GAD, PTSD, social anxiety, OCD), depression/anxiety pharmacology (SSRIs, SNRIs…"]},{"s":"Delirium — Hyperactive vs. Hypoactive▾","a":"delirium-hyperactive-vs-hypoactive","k":["Delirium — Hyperactive vs. Hypoactive▾","Delirium is a transient disorder of cognitive function, consciousness, and perception — onset can be sudden or gradual. Causes include drug/alcohol intoxication or withdrawal, hypoglycemia and other metabolic derangements (liver/kidney dysf…","Hyperactive Delirium — Autonomic nervous system overactivity; develops over 2–3 days. Seen in the ICU, post-surgery, during withdrawal, and in hospitalized elderly patients. Risk factors: benzodiazepines, narcotics, acute infection/sepsis, …","Hypoactive Delirium — Right-sided frontal/basal ganglion disruption; more common with metabolic disorders (liver or kidney failure). — Decreased alertness, attention span, and perception; forgetful, apathetic, slow speech, frequent sleep. E…"]},{"s":"Delirium vs. Dementia & Delirium Treatment▾","a":"delirium-vs-dementia-delirium-treatment","k":["Delirium vs. Dementia & Delirium Treatment▾","Onset — Acute — hours to days. — Chronic — slow, progressive over years.","Course — Usually reversible once the cause is treated. — Irreversible once symptoms present; no cure.","Typical Setting — Hospitalized, often elderly, patients. — Long-term, community or care-facility living.","Dementia patients are at higher risk for delirium, so consistent routines are crucial for a dementia patient to help prevent it. Delirium treatment centers on identifying and removing the cause and risk factors — using the lowest dose and s…"]},{"s":"Dementia — Pathophysiology & Types▾","a":"dementia-pathophysiology-types","k":["Dementia — Pathophysiology & Types▾","Dementia is an acquired deterioration and progressive failure of cerebral functioning — impaired intellectual processes, memory, language, judgment, orientation, and decision-making. Patients are often not cognitively aware of their own dec…","Pathophysiology involves neuron degeneration, brain tissue compression, atherosclerosis of cerebral vessels, brain trauma, infections, and inflammation, and CNS infections (HIV, Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease) causing atrophy — plus a genetic pr…","Types of Dementia","Dementia is an umbrella term — mixed dementia (more than one type at once) is common.","Alzheimer's disease — the leading cause of severe cognitive dysfunction in the elderly (detailed next).","Vascular dementia — the second most common type (detailed next).","Lewy body dementia — named as a type in this lecture but not detailed here.","Frontotemporal dementia — formerly Pick disease (detailed next)."]},{"s":"Alzheimer Disease▾","a":"alzheimer-disease","k":["Alzheimer Disease▾","Alzheimer disease affects about 6 million people in the U.S., about two-thirds of them women. The cause is unknown. Early-onset Alzheimer's has a more common genetic link; late-onset (sporadic) Alzheimer's is far more common but has no spec…","Risk Factors","Age over 65","Family history","Existing mild cognitive impairment","Down syndrome","Unhealthy lifestyle","Head trauma","Social isolation","Pathophysiology","Neuritic/amyloid plaques build up between neurons, and neurofibrillary tangles of tau protein accelerate degeneration.","Changes are centered in the cerebral cortex and hippocampus — the brain's memory areas.","Loss of synapses, acetylcholine, and other neurotransmitters drives memory/cognitive decline and brain atrophy.","These brain changes occur decades before symptoms appear. Initial symptoms are subtle — progressive forgetfulness. Progression is variable, and the disease ends in a non-verbal, vegetative state, then death."]},{"s":"Vascular & Frontotemporal Dementia▾","a":"vascular-frontotemporal-dementia","k":["Vascular & Frontotemporal Dementia▾","Vascular Dementia — The second most common type of dementia — related to cerebrovascular disease (large artery disease, cardioembolism, small vessel disease, stroke), causing cerebral hypoperfusion. — Diabetes, hyperlipidemia, hypertension,…","Frontotemporal Dementia — Formerly called Pick disease; rare. Familial when onset is under age 60, from mutations in tau-encoding genes. Three syndromes: behavioral variant (personality/judgment), progressive nonfluent (language/writing), a…"]},{"s":"Dementia Medications — Donepezil & Memantine▾","a":"dementia-medications-donepezil-memantine","k":["Dementia Medications — Donepezil & Memantine▾","Alzheimer's medications target three problems: decreased acetylcholine, the buildup of plaques and tangles, and excess NMDA receptor stimulation.","Donepezil — A cholinesterase inhibitor — increases acetylcholine by inhibiting the enzyme (acetylcholinesterase) that breaks it down. Used for mild-moderate Alzheimer's. — Mild side effects overall — GI (ulcer/GI bleed risk, avoid NSAIDs) a…","Memantine — The only NMDA receptor antagonist on the market — blocks excess glutamate/NMDA stimulation, decreases calcium influx, and normalizes nerve transmission to prevent further neuronal damage. Used for moderate-severe Alzheimer's; ca…"]},{"s":"Neurotransmitters in Mood & Anxiety Disorders▾","a":"neurotransmitters-in-mood-anxiety-disorders","k":["Neurotransmitters in Mood & Anxiety Disorders▾","Over 200 neurotransmitters exist, but depression and anxiety pathophysiology centers on four:","Dopamine — Attention, motivation, pleasure, reward. — Deficit → Parkinson's-like symptoms, apathy, anhedonia.","Norepinephrine — Alertness, energy, fight-or-flight. — High → anxiety, stress, hyperactivity. Low → lack of focus, energy, motivation.","Serotonin (5-HT) — Involved in obsessions/compulsions. — Deficit → OCD-like symptoms, increased impulsivity, suicide risk, aggression.","GABA — The principal inhibitory neurotransmitter — promotes relaxation. — Dysfunction linked to anxiety/panic disorder and major depressive disorder."]},{"s":"Depression Pathophysiology▾","a":"depression-pathophysiology","k":["Depression Pathophysiology▾","Depression affects more than 17 million people in the U.S. — about twice as common in women (men underreport it) — and is more common in white people and people who have never married. It's associated with cardiovascular disease (a 64% grea…","Types","Major depressive disorder","Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia)","Premenstrual dysphoric disorder","Substance/medication-induced","Due to another medical condition","Postpartum","Symptoms","Anhedonia","Fatigue or restlessness/irritability","Poor concentration","Low self-esteem, negative thinking","Sleep disturbances (either direction)","Appetite disturbances (either direction)","Diagnosis requires multiple symptoms, intense and persistent enough to impair psychosocial functioning. Treatment combines medications, psychotherapy/CBT, psychoeducation/support groups, and — for refractory cases — brain stimulation therap…"]},{"s":"Panic Disorder & Generalized Anxiety Disorder▾","a":"panic-disorder-generalized-anxiety-disorder","k":["Panic Disorder & Generalized Anxiety Disorder▾","There are many distinct types of anxiety disorder, each treated differently. Panic disorder involves two psychological symptoms: anticipatory anxiety (fearful expectation that panic will start) and avoidance anxiety (personal strategies use…","Panic Disorder — Palpitations, chest pain, smothering feeling, dizziness, nausea, fear of losing control, tingling, hot/cold flushes. — 1st line: CBT + antidepressants (SSRIs/SNRIs/TCAs/MAOIs). 2nd line: benzodiazepines, for breakthrough on…","Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) — Chronic, lasting more than 6 months — excessive, uncontrolled, unrealistic worry, muscle tension, autonomic hyperactivity, exaggerated startle, poor concentration. Risk factors: substance use, genetics, …"]},{"s":"PTSD, Social Anxiety Disorder & OCD▾","a":"ptsd-social-anxiety-disorder-ocd","k":["PTSD, Social Anxiety Disorder & OCD▾","PTSD — Three core symptoms: hyperarousal, avoidance of reminders, and re-experiencing events — a chronic stress-response activation from exposure to a life-threatening event. Vivid flashbacks, nightmares/night terrors, emotional blunting, i…","Social Anxiety Disorder — Intense fear of criticism, humiliation, negative evaluation, or embarrassment in social situations, leading to withdrawal/avoidance or intense discomfort. Caused by inherited traits, amygdala structural issues, and…","OCD — Obsessions (repetitive unwanted thoughts, e.g., contamination) plus compulsions (repeated rituals, e.g., cleaning, or checking that a stove is off — a harm obsession). Time-consuming and distressing to the patient and to those around …"]},{"s":"SSRIs & SNRIs▾","a":"ssris-snris","k":["SSRIs & SNRIs▾","Despite the name, antidepressants treat both depression and anxiety. SSRIs and SNRIs are used first-line because they're the safest class. All antidepressants carry a suicide risk that must be monitored before, during, and throughout treatm…","SSRIs — Block serotonin reuptake at the presynaptic nerve ending, leaving more serotonin available in the synapse. Exam drug: fluoxetine (Prozac). Over 20 SSRIs exist (all second-generation); no common suffix. Therapeutic benefit takes abou…","SNRIs — Block reuptake of both serotonin and norepinephrine. Exam drug: venlafaxine (Effexor). No common suffix; contraindicated with MAOIs. — Similar to SSRIs, plus sweating, blurred vision, and increased LFTs — watch liver function tests …","Serotonin syndrome: risk increases when a patient takes two serotonergic drugs together (especially an SSRI/SNRI with an MAOI). Symptoms — including altered mental status and confusion — appear 2–72 hours after the interacting dose. Always …","Abruptly stopping an SSRI or SNRI can cause withdrawal syndrome: dizziness, headache, sensory disturbances (patients may describe their hands or feet as \"on fire\"), shaking, tremors, anxiety, and dysphoria. This is a major teaching point — …"]},{"s":"TCAs & MAOIs▾","a":"tcas-maois","k":["TCAs & MAOIs▾","Tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) and MAOIs are older drug classes, prescribed less often than SSRIs/SNRIs because of more intense interactions and side effects.","TCA anticholinergic effects mnemonic: \"hot as a hare\" (overheated), \"dry as a bone\" (dry mouth/eyes/secretions), \"blind as a bat\" (blurry vision), \"red as a beet\" (flushed), and \"mad as a hatter\" (confusion).","TCAs — Blocks reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin. Exam drug: amitriptyline (Elavil). Used for 50+ years — good efficacy, tolerable side effects, inexpensive; also used for neuropathic pain, nocturnal enuresis, and TMJ. — The anticholi…","MAOIs — Inhibits the monoamine oxidase enzyme that normally converts norepinephrine, serotonin, and dopamine to inactive products — making more of each available. Exam drug: phenelzine (Nardil). Used less often, generally for refractory or …","TCA overdose can be fatal. This is a major concern precisely because TCAs are prescribed to patients with depression/anxiety who already carry a higher suicide risk — and the medication itself can sometimes increase suicidal thoughts. Scree…","MAOI + tyramine-rich foods → hypertensive crisis — the most serious MAOI risk. Tyramine is found in aged cheese, smoked meats, yeast/bread, and red wine; patients on an MAOI must avoid these entirely. The same hypertensive crisis risk appli…"]},{"s":"Atypical Antidepressants & Benzodiazepines▾","a":"atypical-antidepressants-benzodiazepines","k":["Atypical Antidepressants & Benzodiazepines▾","Bupropion (Wellbutrin) — Amphetamine-like structure with a stimulant effect and decreased appetite; unclear mechanism of action. Faster onset than most antidepressants (1–3 weeks). Also used for smoking cessation, ADHD, and neuropathic pain…","Ketamine — Traditionally used for extreme pain control. Low-dose intranasal ketamine now treats refractory/extreme depression with a rapid effect — useful for actively suicidal patients. Adverse effects: dissociation/hallucinations (a \"K-ho…","Trazodone — A second-line agent for depression that blocks serotonin reuptake, but with minimal antidepressant effect. More commonly used for anxiety or as a sleep aid.","Benzodiazepines (\"benzos\" — historically nicknamed \"the lams and the pams\" for their common -lam/-pam suffixes, though not universal) enhance GABA's inhibitory effects in the CNS. Indicated for GAD and panic disorder, short-term only — they…","IV benzodiazepines as the inpatient gold standard for active seizure management — covered in Pain & Seizures","Adverse Effects — CNS depression/decreased LOC (respiratory depression risk, especially IV), addictive with a withdrawal risk, memory loss.","Nursing Considerations — Contraindicated in pregnancy (teratogenic); Schedule IV with moderate abuse risk; short-term prescriptions only (e.g., 10 pills, no automatic renewals); avoid grapefruit/fatty foods (CYP450 interaction).","Antidote — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine effects.","Never combine a benzodiazepine with another CNS depressant — Benadryl, alcohol, opioids, and barbiturates — the combination risks fatal respiratory depression."]},{"s":"Substance Use Disorder — Pathophysiology, Risk & Protective Factors▾","a":"substance-use-disorder-pathophysiology-risk-protective-factors","k":["Substance Use Disorder — Pathophysiology, Risk & Protective Factors▾","Substance use disorder (SUD) is a disease — a complex interaction of environmental, biological, genetic, and brain-chemistry factors. It is explicitly not a personal failure or weakness, despite that being a common and outdated misconceptio…","Dopamine drives the reward pathway: substance use causes a large, rapid dopamine surge that greatly exceeds the normal pleasure response. Over time, the brain's normal dopamine pathways undergo adaptive downregulation, requiring more freque…","Risk Factors","Aggressive childhood behavior","Lack of parental supervision","Poor social skills","Drug experimentation","Drug availability at school","Community poverty","Protective Factors","Good self-control","Parental monitoring/support","Positive relationships","Good grades","School anti-drug policies","Neighborhood resources","These risk and protective factors are proxies for larger social determinants of health. Addiction risk also depends on the substance itself: type, route of administration, effect, age of first use, availability, and cost."]},{"s":"SUD Treatment, Screening & Medication-Assisted Treatment▾","a":"sud-treatment-screening-medication-assisted-treatment","k":["SUD Treatment, Screening & Medication-Assisted Treatment▾","Treatment is highly individualized and therapy-centered — CBT, contingency management, motivational enhancement, family therapy, and 12-step programs — alongside pharmacological interventions, addressing the whole person rather than the sub…","Methadone — A full synthetic opioid analgesic and mu agonist, used for opioid use disorder (including heroin). Occupies opioid receptors to reduce withdrawal symptoms and cravings. Lower abuse potential than opioids generally, but given via…","Buprenorphine + Naloxone (Suboxone) — Buprenorphine is a partial opioid agonist that blocks receptors and prevents cravings (alone, it's called Subutex, and can be used standalone). Naloxone (Narcan) is an opioid antagonist. Combining an ag…"]},{"s":"Withdrawal Syndromes — Opioids vs. Benzodiazepines/Alcohol▾","a":"withdrawal-syndromes-opioids-vs-benzodiazepines-alcohol","k":["Withdrawal Syndromes — Opioids vs. Benzodiazepines/Alcohol▾","Withdrawal timelines are explicitly testable. Opioid withdrawal (including heroin) begins about 12–24 hours after the last dose, peaks around 72 hours, and lasts 5–10 days. It feels miserable — flu-like symptoms including nausea, vomiting, …","Benzodiazepine and alcohol withdrawal both act on GABA receptors and share similar signs, symptoms, and treatment. Non-dangerous symptoms: sleep disturbances, dry retching, irritability, nausea, increased tension, weight loss, anxiety, palp…","Unlike opioid withdrawal, benzodiazepine and alcohol withdrawal can be life-threatening. Dangerous symptoms include seizures, hallucinations, delirium tremens (DTs) — hallucination, disorientation, tachycardia, hypertension, fever, agitatio…","Opioids — 12–24 hours after last dose — ~72 hours — 5–10 days","Benzodiazepines — 6–12 hours to 1–4 days after last dose — ~week 2 — Subsides after peak; commonly abused benzos include Xanax, Klonopin, Valium, Ativan, and Ambien.","Alcohol — ~8 hours after last drink — 1–3 days — Tapers over 1 week, up to several weeks"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-gi-gu-skin-hypersensitivity.html","t":"GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity","secs":[{"s":"GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Fungal Skin Infections — Transmission & Classification","Tinea Infections — Pedis, Capitis & Versicolor","Candidiasis & Systemic Fungal Infections","Antifungal Pharmacology","Pigmented Lesions — Melasma & Vitiligo","Herpes Zoster (Shingles)","Bacterial Skin Infections — Impetigo, Abscess, Furuncle/Carbuncle & Cellulitis","MRSA","Skin Cancer — Precancerous Lesions & ABCDE Screening","Skin Cancer — Basal Cell, Squamous Cell & Melanoma","Eczema & Psoriasis","Constipation & Diarrhea Pathophysiology","GI Pharmacology — Antidiarrheals","GI Pharmacology — Laxatives","Hypersensitivity Basics & Type I (IgE-Mediated) Reactions","Anaphylaxis, Epinephrine & Hypersensitivity Pharmacotherapy","Type II (Cytotoxic) & Type III (Immune Complex) Hypersensitivity","Type IV (Delayed) Hypersensitivity","Urinary Tract Infections — Pathophysiology, Risk Factors & Pharmacology","Overactive Bladder & Urinary Incontinence","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: skin disorders (fungal, viral, and bacterial skin infections; skin cancer; eczema and psoriasis) and antifungal pharmacology, basic GI pharmacology (constipation and diarrhea), hypersensitivity reactions (Types I–I…"]},{"s":"Fungal Skin Infections — Transmission & Classification▾","a":"fungal-skin-infections-transmission-classification","k":["Fungal Skin Infections — Transmission & Classification▾","Fungi are spore-producing organisms that live naturally on skin, in soil, on plants, and on household surfaces — usually harmlessly. Fungus becomes a problem three ways: implantation (entering through broken skin after an injury), inhalatio…","Mycosis — A fungal infection in general — dermatophytes cause superficial mycoses (affecting only the surface layers of skin).","Tinea — Mycosis caused by dermatophytes — fungi that love keratinized tissue (hair, skin, nails). Also called \"ringworm,\" not because a worm is involved, but because it can cause a ring-shaped rash.","Candidiasis — Mycosis caused by Candida albicans, a yeast.","Tinea and candidiasis are the two most common fungal infections. Superficial fungal infections are typically treated with topical antifungal creams/ointments; severe, extensive cases need systemic (PO or IV) antifungals."]},{"s":"Tinea Infections — Pedis, Capitis & Versicolor▾","a":"tinea-infections-pedis-capitis-versicolor","k":["Tinea Infections — Pedis, Capitis & Versicolor▾","Tinea Pedis (Athlete's Foot) — Fungal infection of the feet, typically starting between the toes and spreading to soles/toenails. Dry, scaling, itchy lesions; can see maceration, fissures. Spread by contact with infected skin/fungus in the …","Tinea Capitis (Cradle Cap) — Affects the scalp, eyebrows, eyelashes — scaly red lesions and hair loss (can cause permanent alopecia, though hair often regrows). The most common dermatophyte infection in the pediatric population. — Topical a…","Tinea Versicolor — Affects the upper back, chest, and arms — caused by an overgrowth of yeast that normally lives on the skin (not contagious). Risk factors: hot climate, heavy sweating, oily skin, weakened immune system. Produces bleached,…"]},{"s":"Candidiasis & Systemic Fungal Infections▾","a":"candidiasis-systemic-fungal-infections","k":["Candidiasis & Systemic Fungal Infections▾","Candidiasis (thrush, yeast infection) is caused by Candida albicans, normally found on mucous membranes, skin, the GI tract, and the vagina — it becomes pathogenic particularly in critically ill and immunocompromised patients. Risk factors:…","Candidiasis affects only the outer layers of mucous membranes and skin — typically the mouth, vagina, an uncircumcised penis, nail folds, and large skin folds (intertriginous areas). Presents as white lesions in the mouth or beefy-red spots…","Systemic Fungal Infections","Require aggressive oral or IV antifungal treatment and typically infect internal organs — intestines, urinary tract, lungs, brain.","Common lung infections: histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, pneumocystis.","Cryptococcus affects both the lungs and meninges."]},{"s":"Antifungal Pharmacology▾","a":"antifungal-pharmacology","k":["Antifungal Pharmacology▾","Nystatin — A polyene, used to treat superficial candidiasis (thrush, oral mucosa, vagina, skin). Available as cream, powder (skin folds), or liquid (swish-and-spit or swish-and-swallow). Too toxic to give IV. — Topical route: mild skin irri…","Amphotericin B — A polyene — the agent of choice for systemic mycoses. Binds ergosterol in the fungal cell membrane, making it leaky and destroying the fungal cell wall. Given PO or IV, typically every other day for several months (can take…","Flucytosine — A pyrimidine that inhibits fungal DNA synthesis. Rarely given alone — almost always paired with amphotericin B to allow a lower amphotericin B dose (and fewer side effects). — —","Fluconazole (an Azole) — Interferes with ergosterol synthesis, disrupting fungal cell wall integrity. Used for superficial and less-serious systemic (e.g., pulmonary) fungal infections. Rapidly/completely absorbed orally, reaching bone, CNS…","Griseofulvin — An older, inexpensive drug that inhibits fungal mitosis by binding keratin (doesn't affect the cell wall/membrane). Used for dermatophyte infections of the scalp, skin, or nails resistant to other treatment. — Bone marrow sup…","Remembering azole toxicity: azoles have an \"L\" in the name — for Liver toxicity. Monitor AST/ALT.","Fluconazole nursing considerations: needs an acidic stomach environment to absorb properly, so separate it by at least 2 hours from anything that reduces stomach acidity (antacids, PPIs). Never mix IV fluconazole with other IV medications. …","Amphotericin B is a high-alert drug — it must be diluted and infused slowly. It's hard on the kidneys (monitor BUN/creatinine) and can cause cardiac dysrhythmias (put the patient on a cardiac monitor during IV administration)."]},{"s":"Pigmented Lesions — Melasma & Vitiligo▾","a":"pigmented-lesions-melasma-vitiligo","k":["Pigmented Lesions — Melasma & Vitiligo▾","Melasma — Dark macules on the face, more common in women — especially during pregnancy (the \"mask of pregnancy\") or with oral contraceptive use. A dermatological nuisance, not a health problem. — Avoid sun exposure, bleaching creams, retino…","Vitiligo — An acquired condition — abnormalities in melanin production cause pigment to disappear from patches of skin. Usually appears around age 21 or earlier, can appear suddenly. May be associated with autoimmune conditions (pernicious …"]},{"s":"Herpes Zoster (Shingles)▾","a":"herpes-zoster-shingles","k":["Herpes Zoster (Shingles)▾","Shingles is caused by reactivation of the varicella zoster virus (the chickenpox virus), which lies dormant in a dermatome after a chickenpox infection. Reactivated by immunosuppression, significant stress, or illness — most common in peopl…","Key Features","Follows dermatomal lines but is asymmetric and does not cross midline — appears on only one side of the body.","Vesicles are extremely painful; the rash clears in about 2–3 weeks.","Most contagious when vesicles are weeping — can transmit chickenpox (not shingles) to someone who has never had chickenpox or the vaccine.","Treated with antivirals. A notable complication is postherpetic neuralgia — persistent, indefinite pain in the same area — affecting about 13% of patients over 60, which is why the shingles vaccine is recommended starting around age 50–60."]},{"s":"Bacterial Skin Infections — Impetigo, Abscess, Furuncle/Carbuncle & Cellulitis▾","a":"bacterial-skin-infections-impetigo-abscess-furuncle-carbuncle-cellulitis","k":["Bacterial Skin Infections — Impetigo, Abscess, Furuncle/Carbuncle & Cellulitis▾","Impetigo — Acute, contagious infection caused by staphylococcus/streptococcus (carried in the nose) — common in children, spreads in daycares/schools. Vesicles and pustules with a honey-colored crust on a red base. — Topical antibacterial (…","Abscess — Inflamed, red skin with a collection of pus underneath — raised, palpable borders, tender, warm, may fluctuate (feel fluid-filled) with purulent discharge. — Incision and drainage (I&D) plus antibiotics (PO or IV); drainage may be…","Furuncle vs. Carbuncle — Furuncle: bacterial infection of a single hair follicle (an \"ingrown hair\"). Carbuncle: deeper, more painful swelling involving several hair follicles. — I&D plus antibiotics, same as an abscess.","Cellulitis — Bacterial infection of the skin and surrounding tissue (usually staph/strep), often starting from an injury or animal/insect bite, common on the lower extremities. Swollen, red, warm, tender; can blister if severe. Not contagio…"]},{"s":"MRSA▾","a":"mrsa","k":["MRSA▾","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) is a staph strain resistant to methicillin and the penicillin family as a whole.","Hospital-Acquired MRSA","Associated with invasive procedures — surgery, IV tubing, central lines, artificial joints.","Treated with IV vancomycin or linezolid.","Community-Acquired MRSA","Often begins as a painful boil; transmitted person-to-person. Highest risk: high school wrestlers, childcare workers, crowded living conditions.","Treated with Bactrim or dicloxacillin.","MRSA presents with warm skin, purulent drainage, possible fever, and can develop an abscess quickly — it can burrow deep into tissue and spread to bones, joints, surgical wounds, the bloodstream, heart valves, and lungs. It's not limited to…"]},{"s":"Skin Cancer — Precancerous Lesions & ABCDE Screening▾","a":"skin-cancer-precancerous-lesions-abcde-screening","k":["Skin Cancer — Precancerous Lesions & ABCDE Screening▾","Actinic Keratosis — Benign, precancerous lesion from sun/UV damage — rough, scaly, red plaques. More common in light-skinned people.","Solar Lentigo — Benign, precancerous \"liver spots\"/\"age spots,\" also from sun exposure.","Both are watched, measured, and followed over time to catch malignant transformation early. Skin cancer disproportionately affects Caucasians (20× more likely to be diagnosed than African-Americans) — but African-Americans are 4× more likel…","ABCDE melanoma screening: Asymmetry, Border (uneven, notched, crusty), Color (variation — white, brown, blue), Diameter (larger than a pencil eraser, >6mm), Evolving (changing size, shape, or color; new bleeding/scabbing)."]},{"s":"Skin Cancer — Basal Cell, Squamous Cell & Melanoma▾","a":"skin-cancer-basal-cell-squamous-cell-melanoma","k":["Skin Cancer — Basal Cell, Squamous Cell & Melanoma▾","Basal Cell Carcinoma — Most common skin cancer, least malignant — usually doesn't metastasize, slow-growing, most curable. Increased risk with increased sun exposure. — Small, flesh-colored/pink dome-shaped bump that progresses to a translu…","Squamous Cell Carcinoma — Second most common — curable with early treatment, but can metastasize to lymph nodes/organs if not caught early. — Redness and scaling, slightly elevated, irregular border, shallow chronic ulcer.","Melanoma — Rare but the most deadly skin cancer — cancer of melanocytes, spreads both radially (wide) and vertically (deep) into the dermis, and can occur in the eyes and under nails. Highest rate among white males. Risk factors: family his…"]},{"s":"Eczema & Psoriasis▾","a":"eczema-psoriasis","k":["Eczema & Psoriasis▾","Eczema (Atopic Dermatitis) — Not contagious; thought to be caused by an overactive immune system, with a hereditary tendency — more common alongside asthma or hay fever. Itchy rash on the face, backs of knees, wrists, hands, feet; dry, thic…","Psoriasis — Chronic condition beginning in young adulthood (18–24); not contagious. Thought to involve an overactive/autoimmune immune response; linked to obesity and cardiovascular disease (connection not fully explained). Skin cells grow …"]},{"s":"Constipation & Diarrhea Pathophysiology▾","a":"constipation-diarrhea-pathophysiology","k":["Constipation & Diarrhea Pathophysiology▾","Constipation is small, infrequent, or difficult bowel movements — a general guideline is fewer than 3 bowel movements per week. Common causes: a low-fiber diet, lack of exercise (exercise stimulates peristalsis), slowed peristalsis (especia…","Diarrhea is increased frequency and fluidity of bowel movements — often the first sign of a larger GI disorder. Acute diarrhea can result from infection (viral/bacterial), emotional stress, or medications (antibiotics, antacids, chemotherap…","Osmotic — Increased osmotically active solutes (e.g., magnesium sulfate) pull sodium and water into the gut. — Common with concentrated tube feedings.","Secretory — A bacterial toxin increases secretion and inhibits reabsorption. — Vibrio cholerae, Staphylococcus aureus.","Exudative — Active bowel inflammation exudes mucus, blood, and protein into the stool, pulling water in with them. — Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis.","Motility Disturbance — Decreased small-intestine absorption delivers an abnormally large fluid volume to the colon. — Dumping syndrome (post-gastrectomy), irritable bowel syndrome.","Diarrhea's consequences go beyond the underlying cause: skin breakdown (especially in hospitalized or low-motility patients), fluid/electrolyte imbalance, and nutritional concerns from poor absorption — all bigger risks in the very young, v…"]},{"s":"GI Pharmacology — Antidiarrheals▾","a":"gi-pharmacology-antidiarrheals","k":["GI Pharmacology — Antidiarrheals▾","Antidiarrheals are chosen based on the specific cause when known, and are always given orally (no IV options). Three groups: adsorbents, anti-motility drugs, and probiotics. (Opioids have anti-motility properties too, but are rarely used fo…","Adsorbents — Bismuth subsalicylate (Pepto-Bismol, an aspirin derivative) and activated charcoal (also used in overdose). Coats the GI tract wall and binds the causative agent. — Increased bleeding time, constipation. Harmless: dark stools/t…","Anti-Motility — Loperamide (Imodium) — an anti-peristaltic agent. Diphenoxylate with atropine (Lomotil) — anticholinergic/antispasmodic, slows peristalsis and dries gastric secretions; prescription-only due to anticholinergic effects (loper…","Probiotics — Lactobacillus-based products (the organism making up most normal gut flora, often destroyed by antibiotics) and Saccharomyces boulardii (Florastor, used specifically for C. diff). Replenish gut flora and suppress diarrhea-causi…"]},{"s":"GI Pharmacology — Laxatives▾","a":"gi-pharmacology-laxatives","k":["GI Pharmacology — Laxatives▾","Laxative choice is individualized by the patient's age, constipation severity, and contributing factors, alongside dietary fiber and increased activity. Laxatives are among the most misused OTC medications — inappropriate use can cause depe…","Bulk-Forming — Psyllium (Metamucil) — Works like dietary fiber — absorbs water, increases stool bulk, distends the bowel to trigger motility. No systemic effects. Must be taken with liberal water (prevents esophageal obstruction/impaction).…","Emollient — Docusate sodium (Colace), mineral oil — Docusate lubricates fecal material/intestinal walls and softens stool — used for fecal impaction and to prevent opioid-induced constipation. Mineral oil lubricates the intestines (PO or PR…","Hyperosmotic — Glycerin, lactulose, polyethylene glycol (GoLYTELY) — Draw water into the colon. Glycerin is mild, often given as a suppository in children. Lactulose also reduces blood ammonia — used in hepatic encephalopathy. Polyethylene …","Saline — Magnesium salts (mag citrate, milk of magnesia, Epsom salts), sodium salts (fleet enema) — Draw water into the colon via osmotic pressure — produces a watery stool within 3–6 hours. Use magnesium salts cautiously with renal insuffi…","Stimulant — Bisacodyl (Dulcolax), senna — Induces intestinal peristalsis directly; effect is dose-proportional. The class most likely to cause dependence. Bisacodyl can be given PO or PR."]},{"s":"Hypersensitivity Basics & Type I (IgE-Mediated) Reactions▾","a":"hypersensitivity-basics-type-i-ige-mediated-reactions","k":["Hypersensitivity Basics & Type I (IgE-Mediated) Reactions▾","A hypersensitivity reaction is a normal immune response inappropriately triggered, excessive relative to the trigger, or producing effects damaging to the body. Triggered by a specific antigen-antibody or antibody-lymphocyte reaction. Types…","Memory aid: B cells make antiBodies (Types I–III); T cells provide long-Term, delayed immunity (Type IV).","Hypersensitivity reactions are antigen-specific and typically don't occur on first exposure — the first exposure sensitizes the patient (antibody production begins), and the reaction happens on a later exposure.","Mechanism — First exposure to an antigen (e.g., pollen) triggers a B cell to mature into a plasma cell, which produces IgE antibodies. These IgE antibodies attach to mast cells and wait. On re-exposure, the antigen links to the mast-cell-bo…","Effects of Mediators — Vasodilation (drop in BP), laryngeal edema/swelling (the most severe reaction — anaphylactic shock), urticaria (hives), increased vascular permeability (fluid leak, worsening hypotension), bronchial smooth muscle cons…","Common Triggers — Environmental (pet dander, pollen, dust, mold), foods (nuts, shellfish, eggs — most are proteins), medications (penicillin, contrast dye). Note: a shellfish/seafood allergy is a red flag for a possible contrast dye allergy…","Etiology — Genetic component — 30% risk if one parent is allergic to an antigen, 50% if both. Theorized to be an inherited overactive immune system.","Type I reactions can be local (e.g., allergic rhinitis — about 50% of the US population reacts to one of the ten most common allergens) or systemic (anaphylaxis — a medical emergency, covered next). An allergy shot works by building toleran…"]},{"s":"Anaphylaxis, Epinephrine & Hypersensitivity Pharmacotherapy▾","a":"anaphylaxis-epinephrine-hypersensitivity-pharmacotherapy","k":["Anaphylaxis, Epinephrine & Hypersensitivity Pharmacotherapy▾","Systemic Type I reactions release chemical mediators throughout the body and are considered a medical emergency — laryngeal edema can make breathing difficult or impossible, and widespread vasodilation causes vascular collapse with severe h…","Anaphylaxis is life-threatening — laryngeal edema can obstruct breathing, and widespread vasodilation causes vascular collapse. Treat with antihistamines, glucocorticoids, and epinephrine. Educate patients to wear a MedicAlert bracelet, car…","Epinephrine is a sympathomimetic (mimics fight-or-flight): a vasopressor, bronchodilator, anti-asthmatic, and vasoconstrictor, acting on both alpha (arteries) and beta (heart, lungs, arteries, skeletal muscle) receptors. It inhibits mast ce…","Epinephrine concentration matters — mixing it up can be fatal. Anaphylaxis dosing uses a 1:1,000 concentration (adult dose 0.2–1 mg; pediatric 0.01 mg/kg); cardiac arrest uses a more dilute 1:10,000 concentration. Giving an IM-concentration…","Immunosuppressants","Decrease immune response — used in transplant patients to reduce rejection of foreign tissue.","Increases infection risk.","Antihistamines","Reduce allergic symptoms by blocking histamine's mediator activity — don't stop the underlying reaction."]},{"s":"Type II (Cytotoxic) & Type III (Immune Complex) Hypersensitivity▾","a":"type-ii-cytotoxic-type-iii-immune-complex-hypersensitivity","k":["Type II (Cytotoxic) & Type III (Immune Complex) Hypersensitivity▾","Type II — cytotoxic reaction (memory aid: what happens if a patient gets the wrong blood type). IgG/IgM antibodies bind directly to antigens on a foreign cell's surface, forming an antibody-antigen complex that attacks and lyses the target …","During a blood transfusion, watch for fever/chills, flushing, changes in heart rate or blood pressure, chest or back pain, restlessness, anxiety, and headache — if any of these appear, stop the transfusion immediately. Take the patient's te…","Type III — immune complex reaction (memory aid: rheumatoid arthritis). Antigen-antibody complexes (IgG/IgM) form in circulation and deposit into tissue, where the resulting inflammation — not the complex itself — causes tissue damage. Neutr…"]},{"s":"Type IV (Delayed) Hypersensitivity▾","a":"type-iv-delayed-hypersensitivity","k":["Type IV (Delayed) Hypersensitivity▾","Type IV — delayed hypersensitivity (memory aid: poison ivy, a positive TB skin test, a jellyfish sting, a jewelry reaction, Crohn's disease). Unlike Types I–III, this involves no antibodies — it's mediated by T lymphocytes, and reactions ar…","Mechanism — A small incomplete protein (hapten) attaches to a human protein to form a complete antigen. An antigen-processing cell presents this to a T lymphocyte, which — after a delay — recognizes and attacks. Cytotoxic (CD8) T cells atta…","Common Antigens — Plant oils (poison ivy/oak/sumac), adhesives, cosmetics, clothing, nickel alloys (jewelry), tuberculin antigen (the Mantoux/TB skin test), organ transplant/skin graft tissue, and gluten in Crohn's disease.","Timing — Clinical manifestations generally peak 48–72 hours after antigen exposure — this is why a TB skin test has to be \"read\" 48–72 hours after placement. The two most common Type IV reactions are contact dermatitis and tuberculin hypers…","Organ transplant/graft rejection specifically can be a Type I, II, or IV reaction depending on timing and mechanism."]},{"s":"Urinary Tract Infections — Pathophysiology, Risk Factors & Pharmacology▾","a":"urinary-tract-infections-pathophysiology-risk-factors-pharmacology","k":["Urinary Tract Infections — Pathophysiology, Risk Factors & Pharmacology▾","UTIs are more common in females due to a shorter urethra and incomplete bladder emptying. E. coli is the most common causative organism. Bacteriuria (bacteria in the urine) is colonization and does not automatically mean infection; urethrit…","Risk Factors","Catheterization (CAUTI)","Female anatomy (shorter urethra, perineal irritation)","Older age (up to 50% of nursing home residents have bacteriuria)","Pregnancy; spermicide/diaphragm/condom use","New sexual activity (~25% of females get a UTI within their first sexually active year)","Urinary obstruction/reflux, immobility, incontinence, decreased cognition, hygiene-access barriers","Protective Factors","Acidic urine","Prostatic secretions (males) and urethral gland secretions (females) — prostatic secretions are more protective","Unidirectional urine flow and the bladder's one-way valve","A functioning immune system","Males are less likely to get a UTI, but more likely to have recurrent UTIs (bacteria can hide deep in the prostate); BPH also raises urinary retention risk. Atypical presentations: children often present with fever, irritability, poor appet…","Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Bactrim) — First-line UTI antibiotic — avoid in patients with sulfa allergies.","Ciprofloxacin — Alternative for patients with sulfa allergies.","Nitrofurantoin — Used for recurring lower UTIs.","Phenazopyridine (Pyridium) — A urinary analgesic — relieves UTI pain but does not treat the infection itself (the antibiotic does). Harmless but notable side effect: turns urine a reddish-orange color.","Key teaching point for any antibiotic course: finish the entire prescription, even after symptoms improve."]},{"s":"Overactive Bladder & Urinary Incontinence▾","a":"overactive-bladder-urinary-incontinence","k":["Overactive Bladder & Urinary Incontinence▾","The bladder is smooth muscle that normally holds about 300–500 mL before signaling the need to urinate. Overactive bladder occurs when bladder muscle contracts involuntarily even at low urine volumes, creating urgency. Causes: neurological …","Mirabegron is an antispasmodic that selectively stimulates beta-3 adrenergic receptors to relax bladder smooth muscle (PO, 25–50 mg/day). Most serious adverse reaction: hypertension (monitor BP; use cautiously in patients who already have h…","Urge — Involuntary urine leakage immediately after a sudden urge — no time to reach the toilet. Caused by an overactive detrusor (bladder smooth) muscle; frequency increases with age. Contributing factors: bladder infections, outlet obstruc…","Stress — Involuntary urine loss with increased intra-abdominal pressure (sneezing, coughing, lifting) — from loss of pelvic floor muscle/fascial support (often from childbirth trauma or pelvic surgery), which disrupts the normal bladder-ure…","Mixed — Combination of stress and urge incontinence — common together, especially in older females.","Overflow — The bladder becomes so full it leaks/overflows — more common in males with an enlarged prostate obstructing outflow.","Functional — The urinary system itself works fine, but a physical condition, mobility limitation, cognitive disorder, or lack of toilet access prevents timely toileting.","Transient — Sudden and reversible — often related to a UTI, constipation, or fecal impaction.","Incontinence is not a normal part of aging, though age-related lower urinary tract changes can contribute. It carries psychosocial impact plus real skin risk — acidic urine causes inflammation/breakdown, and damp, warm, damaged skin becomes…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-stress-steroids.html","t":"Stress & Steroids","secs":[{"s":"Stress & Steroids","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Homeostasis vs. Allostasis","Stress, Stressors, Eustress & Distress","Factors Influencing Coping & Adaptation","Alarm Stage — Fight-or-Flight & the Two Activation Pathways","SNS vs. PNS — Fight-or-Flight vs. Rest-and-Digest Effects","Catecholamines — Norepinephrine & Epinephrine","HPA Axis — Cortisol & Aldosterone","Resistance Stage","Exhaustion Stage & Allostatic Overload","Chronic Stress — Signs, Symptoms & Disease Associations","Cortisol's Metabolic Effects — Glucose, Protein & Fat Redistribution","Steroid Pharmacology — Indications & Routes","Steroid Replacement Dosing Strategies","Glucocorticoid Drugs — Hydrocortisone, Prednisone & Dexamethasone","Corticosteroid Side Effects — Acute & Long-Term","Corticosteroid Patient Education","Mineralocorticoid Replacement — Fludrocortisone","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: homeostasis and allostasis, stress versus stressors, General Adaptation Syndrome (alarm, resistance, exhaustion), the sympathetic/adrenergic and HPA-axis stress pathways, allostatic load and overload, chronic stres…"]},{"s":"Homeostasis vs. Allostasis▾","a":"homeostasis-vs-allostasis","k":["Homeostasis vs. Allostasis▾","Homeostasis — From Greek homeo (same/similar) + stasis (stable/standing still). The state where all body systems are balanced around an ideal set point (temperature, vital signs, blood pH). Each person's normal set point falls within a rang…","Allostasis — From Greek allo (variable/different) + stasis (stable/standing still). The dynamic process the body uses to achieve and return to a steady state — the body's overall attempt to get itself back to homeostasis. Allostasis recogni…","Word roots: homeo-stasis = staying the same (the balanced set point itself); allo-stasis = the variable, active process that gets the body back to — or adjusts — that set point.","Examples of homeostasis in action: blood pH depends on CO₂ concentration, which the respiratory system regulates; core temperature is hypothalamus-mediated; and blood pressure is sensed by stretch receptors called baroreceptors, which trigg…"]},{"s":"Stress, Stressors, Eustress & Distress▾","a":"stress-stressors-eustress-distress","k":["Stress, Stressors, Eustress & Distress▾","Stressor — The actual force or challenge causing stress (e.g., an exam).","Stress — The physical, chemical, or emotional tension the body and mind feel in response to a stressor (e.g., how you feel in your body before that exam).","Eustress — Positive stress — feeling happy, accomplished, excited, or nervous in a good way. Perceived as within one's coping ability; can motivate, sharpen focus, and improve performance.","Distress — Negative stress — causes anxiety and concern, can be short- or long-term, and decreases performance. Perceived as outside one's coping ability.","The stress response is meant to be helpful in the short term — its job is to get the body through the stressor and back to homeostasis. Problems develop when the stress response is activated repeatedly or does not let up: chronic stress las…"]},{"s":"Factors Influencing Coping & Adaptation▾","a":"factors-influencing-coping-adaptation","k":["Factors Influencing Coping & Adaptation▾","What shapes a person's coping ability","Genetics — some inherent difference in how efficiently a person relaxes and responds to stress.","Culture and environment — different values, viewpoints, and surroundings shape how stress is handled; a child raised by a parent who panics under stress can learn to panic too.","Prior experience — a bad past experience (e.g., at the dentist) creates anticipatory stress at future encounters.","Pre-existing health status — an existing acute or chronic disease adds extra allostatic demand, pushing a person closer to the limit of what they can manage.","Ability to manage stress — support systems, coping mechanisms, and logical thinking that help a person work through stress.","Coping mechanisms are strategies directed at the stressor that help a person withstand it; a coping strategy is effective if it resolves the situation and the feelings around it. Healthy examples: meditation, exercise, social support, talki…"]},{"s":"Alarm Stage — Fight-or-Flight & the Two Activation Pathways▾","a":"alarm-stage-fight-or-flight-the-two-activation-pathways","k":["Alarm Stage — Fight-or-Flight & the Two Activation Pathways▾","General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS) is the body's similar physiologic response to a stressor regardless of what the stressor actually is (physical, emotional, or psychological) and unfolds in three stages: alarm, resistance, and exhaustion. I…","The alarm stage is the initial burst of arousal and energy that helps the body evade or confront a stressor — the \"fight-or-flight\" response. The hypothalamus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), which activates two pathways simu…","Sympathetic (adrenergic) pathway — CRH activates the sympathetic nervous system (SNS) → SNS stimulates the adrenal medulla → adrenal medulla releases catecholamines (norepinephrine and epinephrine) — the \"adrenergic response.\"","HPA axis — CRH stimulates the anterior pituitary → anterior pituitary releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) → ACTH stimulates the adrenal cortex → adrenal cortex releases glucocorticoids (cortisol)."]},{"s":"SNS vs. PNS — Fight-or-Flight vs. Rest-and-Digest Effects▾","a":"sns-vs-pns-fight-or-flight-vs-rest-and-digest-effects","k":["SNS vs. PNS — Fight-or-Flight vs. Rest-and-Digest Effects▾","Sympathetic (SNS) — \"Fight or Flight,\" Adrenergic","Pupils dilate (better see the threat)","Salivation stops (dry mouth)","Heart rate increases","Bronchioles dilate (more oxygenation to muscles)","Peristalsis and gastric secretions decrease (digestion isn't needed right now)","Liver breaks down glycogen for glucose (glycogenolysis) — muscles need fuel","Bladder contraction is inhibited","Parasympathetic (PNS) — \"Rest and Digest,\" Cholinergic","Saliva production stimulated","Bronchioles constrict","Peristalsis picks back up","Bile is released","Bladder contracts"]},{"s":"Catecholamines — Norepinephrine & Epinephrine▾","a":"catecholamines-norepinephrine-epinephrine","k":["Catecholamines — Norepinephrine & Epinephrine▾","Norepinephrine — Primary constrictor of smooth muscle in blood vessels — regulates blood flow distribution to organs and maintains blood pressure via vasoconstriction. Dilates pupils (improves night and far-distance vision). Decreases gastr…","Epinephrine — Enhances heart contractility, increases heart rate and venous return (raising cardiac output). Relaxes bronchial smooth muscle to dilate airways. Increases glycogenolysis (glucose release from the liver) and inhibits insulin s…","Together, norepinephrine and epinephrine also influence the immune system through cytokine production."]},{"s":"HPA Axis — Cortisol & Aldosterone▾","a":"hpa-axis-cortisol-aldosterone","k":["HPA Axis — Cortisol & Aldosterone▾","The HPA axis (hypothalamus → anterior pituitary → adrenal cortex) works on a negative feedback loop that normally keeps cortisol within a normal range. The adrenal cortex releases both glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids in response to A…","Cortisol (primary glucocorticoid; the body's endogenous steroid) — Works with catecholamines to maintain normal blood pressure and cardiac output. Inhibits reproductive hormones (male and female) — in times of stress, the body shunts energy…","Aldosterone (primary mineralocorticoid) — Acts in the kidney to reabsorb sodium and excrete potassium and hydrogen ions; water follows sodium, so aldosterone promotes fluid retention, raising extracellular volume and blood pressure.","Aldosterone's role in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and its blocking drugs — covered in Hypertension & Hyperlipidemia","Glucocorticoids and catecholamines also work together in the brain to help form memories of a stressor — useful because it teaches the brain how to respond to that stressor in the future, but this same mechanism can create a traumatic memor…"]},{"s":"Resistance Stage▾","a":"resistance-stage","k":["Resistance Stage▾","In the resistance stage, stress is ongoing and the body keeps trying to cope and adjust — but this response takes a lot of energy and is not an unlimited supply. One of two things happens:","Stressor resolves","The parasympathetic nervous system takes over — a cholinergic response replaces the adrenergic one.","Heart rate and blood pressure lower; immune function, sleep, and digestion improve.","Hormone levels still take time to drop back to normal — a person may feel jittery or anxious for a short while afterward (e.g., after narrowly avoiding a car accident).","Stressor continues","The body starts running out of supplies — blood sugar runs low, and norepinephrine, epinephrine, and cortisol stores are depleted.","The body can no longer sustain the same response and moves toward exhaustion."]},{"s":"Exhaustion Stage & Allostatic Overload▾","a":"exhaustion-stage-allostatic-overload","k":["Exhaustion Stage & Allostatic Overload▾","In the exhaustion stage, the body is no longer able to return to homeostasis. Constant HPA-axis stimulation can cause the adrenal cortex to hypertrophy, while the underused lymph tissue atrophies — raising infection risk. There are no energ…","Chronic stress (living in a war zone, ongoing racism or discrimination, poverty, or being a caregiver for a sick family member) can drive the body to this exhaustion point. If stress continues unrelieved for long enough, the adrenal cortex …","Note: it was once believed that stress directly causes bleeding ulcers. That's not accurate — stress can worsen an existing ulcer, but it does not cause bleeding ulcers to develop in the first place.","Allostatic Load vs. Allostatic Overload","Allostatic load — the normal wear-and-tear demand that daily life and stress place on the HPA axis, sympathetic nervous system, and immune system as they work to maintain the body and adapt to stressors.","Allostatic overload — when those systems are chronically over- or under-activated beyond their capacity, reaching a point of exhaustion that damages organs and tissues and allows stress-induced disease to develop."]},{"s":"Chronic Stress — Signs, Symptoms & Disease Associations▾","a":"chronic-stress-signs-symptoms-disease-associations","k":["Chronic Stress — Signs, Symptoms & Disease Associations▾","Physical signs & symptoms of allostatic overload","Hair loss; tension and stiff neck; tension headaches","Asthma triggers; palpitations; tics (involuntary movements)","Digestive complaints — upset stomach, nausea, diarrhea, constipation, loss of appetite","Irritable bladder; acne; irregular menstrual cycles or impotence","Sleep disturbances (either insomnia or excessive sleep)","Sleep deprivation — the inability to get quality sleep — itself increases stress levels, and it is common in hospitalized patients (a noisy, interrupted, privacy-poor environment). It can cause irritability, apathy, difficulty with memory, …","Chronic overstress is strongly associated with higher rates of coronary artery disease, hypertension, stroke, tension headaches, rheumatoid arthritis, other autoimmune disease, irritable bowel syndrome, ulcers, sexual disorders, and type 2 …"]},{"s":"Cortisol's Metabolic Effects — Glucose, Protein & Fat Redistribution▾","a":"cortisol-s-metabolic-effects-glucose-protein-fat-redistribution","k":["Cortisol's Metabolic Effects — Glucose, Protein & Fat Redistribution▾","Glucose metabolism — Cortisol raises blood glucose — the opposite of insulin. It promotes gluconeogenesis, decreases peripheral glucose utilization, inhibits glucose uptake by muscle and adipose tissue, and promotes glucose storage as glyco…","Protein metabolism — Breaks down muscle tissue (muscle wasting), breaks down skin integrity (skin tears more easily), and breaks down bone tissue (fragile bones/osteoporosis).","Fat redistribution — Breaks down fat from the extremities and redistributes it to the face and trunk. With chronic cortisol exposure, this produces a characteristic \"moon face\" and \"buffalo hump.\""]},{"s":"Steroid Pharmacology — Indications & Routes▾","a":"steroid-pharmacology-indications-routes","k":["Steroid Pharmacology — Indications & Routes▾","We can also give steroids as medications — systemic corticosteroids given orally or IV, or inhaled steroids for a more localized effect with little to no systemic action.","Replacement — The adrenal gland isn't producing enough on its own — either from primary adrenal dysfunction or because it can no longer keep up after prolonged stress.","Anti-inflammatory — Very effective at decreasing inflammation — used for systemic inflammatory responses, allergies, asthma (typically inhaled, for a localized lung effect), COPD exacerbations (often IV, transitioned to oral), chronic infla…","Immunosuppression — Smaller doses given to intentionally reduce immune response — e.g., after organ transplant to help prevent rejection. Patients are typically weaned off if no rejection issues develop."]},{"s":"Steroid Replacement Dosing Strategies▾","a":"steroid-replacement-dosing-strategies","k":["Steroid Replacement Dosing Strategies▾","Steroid replacement is dosed to mimic the body's normal, physiologic pattern of cortisol release, which peaks in a burst around dawn with several smaller bumps through the day. Common approaches include alternate-day therapy, multiple small…","The body's natural early-morning cortisol/growth hormone surge and its effect on blood glucose (dawn phenomenon) — covered in Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome","Sick-day dosing: a patient on long-term steroid replacement needs an increased dose during illness, surgery, or any major physiologic stress — their own adrenal glands cannot ramp up cortisol production the way a healthy body would. Failing…"]},{"s":"Glucocorticoid Drugs — Hydrocortisone, Prednisone & Dexamethasone▾","a":"glucocorticoid-drugs-hydrocortisone-prednisone-dexamethasone","k":["Glucocorticoid Drugs — Hydrocortisone, Prednisone & Dexamethasone▾","Hydrocortisone — PO or IV (parenteral) — Biochemically identical to endogenous cortisol — a blood test cannot distinguish a patient's own cortisol from the hydrocortisone given as a drug. Has some mineralocorticoid (aldosterone-like) activi…","Prednisone — PO — Typically dosed around 5–10 mg/day for chronic disease states (e.g., long-term low-dose use in COPD).","Dexamethasone — PO or IV — Used to assess adrenal function — unlike hydrocortisone, dexamethasone does not cross-react with the cortisol lab assay. Any cortisol measured in a patient taking dexamethasone reflects only their own (endogenous)…","Rule of thumb: give hydrocortisone to replace cortisol (it looks identical to the body's own on a lab test); give dexamethasone to test the adrenal gland (it's invisible to the cortisol assay, so any result reflects only the patient's own p…"]},{"s":"Corticosteroid Side Effects — Acute & Long-Term▾","a":"corticosteroid-side-effects-acute-long-term","k":["Corticosteroid Side Effects — Acute & Long-Term▾","Acute Effects","Increased intraocular pressure — a particular concern in patients with glaucoma","Fluid retention and hypertension — a concern in patients with heart failure or other fluid-sensitive conditions","Mood swings","Weight gain with central fat deposits (abdomen, face, back of the neck)","Increased hunger (from cortisol's catabolic state)","Long-Term Effects (months to years of use)","Cataracts (clouded lens)","Hyperglycemia — can worsen or unmask diabetes","Increased infection risk (immunosuppression)","Osteoporosis and fractures","Suppressed adrenal gland hormone production — causes fatigue, appetite loss, nausea, and muscle weakness","Thin, papery skin with easy bruising and delayed wound healing"]},{"s":"Corticosteroid Patient Education▾","a":"corticosteroid-patient-education","k":["Corticosteroid Patient Education▾","Never stop long-term corticosteroids abruptly. This is why patients are placed on a taper — a gradually decreasing dose over time. Long-term steroid use suppresses the body's own adrenal hormone production, and abruptly withdrawing the drug…","Additional teaching points for long-term steroid therapy","Take the medication at the same time each day — consistency helps mimic the body's natural hormonal pattern.","Wear a medical alert bracelet.","Monitor weight and blood glucose regularly, since both can increase with extended steroid use.","Report illness or major stress promptly so the dose can be adjusted (see sick-day dosing above)."]},{"s":"Mineralocorticoid Replacement — Fludrocortisone▾","a":"mineralocorticoid-replacement-fludrocortisone","k":["Mineralocorticoid Replacement — Fludrocortisone▾","Mineralocorticoids aren't often replaced on their own, because hydrocortisone already carries some aldosterone-like activity. But prednisone and dexamethasone do not have that aldosterone effect — so patients on those drugs can develop salt…","Fludrocortisone is the only mineralocorticoid replacement available — it's the aldosterone substitute, used to augment glucocorticoid replacement (with prednisone or dexamethasone) when salt wasting develops."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-intro-to-pathophysiology.html","t":"Intro to Pathophysiology","secs":[{"s":"Intro to Pathophysiology","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","What Pathophysiology Is","Homeostasis & Compensation","Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors","Identifying & Describing Disease","Disease Onset & Course","Prefixes & Suffixes Worth Knowing","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"What Pathophysiology Is▾","a":"what-pathophysiology-is","k":["What Pathophysiology Is▾","Breaking Down the Word","Physiology is normal functioning — how the body works when there aren't any problems, at the molecular, cellular, and whole-organ-system level.","Pathology is the study of disease and injury — what abnormal processes are happening to disrupt the body from normal functioning.","Pathophysiology combines the two: the mechanisms of disease — how and why changes in body structure and function happen, and how those changes produce the signs and symptoms of a given disease.","Disease is a disruption in homeostasis — \"homeo\" (same) + \"stasis\" (balance/stability). This disruption can be physical (e.g., heart failure — the heart can't pump adequately, so the body doesn't get the blood/oxygen it needs), mental (e.g.…"]},{"s":"Homeostasis & Compensation▾","a":"homeostasis-compensation","k":["Homeostasis & Compensation▾","The Body's Steady State","The body operates best within a narrow normal range for any given process — heart rate (typically 60–100 bpm) and temperature (around 98.5°F, with some individual variation) are two easy examples.","When something pushes a value outside that normal range, the body activates processes to bring it back toward homeostasis.","Disease manifests when the disruption is prolonged, or exceeds the body's ability to compensate.","Temperature Rises — Blood vessels dilate (flushed/red skin) and sweat glands secrete fluid — both mechanisms lose heat to the environment, cooling the body back toward normal.","Temperature Drops — Blood vessels constrict (pale skin) and sweat glands stop producing fluid, conserving heat. Shivering — involuntary muscle contraction — generates additional heat."]},{"s":"Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors▾","a":"causes-of-disease-intrinsic-extrinsic-factors","k":["Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors▾","Intrinsic Factors — Come from inside the body: genetics/genetic mutations, immune status (immunocompromised patients are more susceptible to disease), age, sex, autoimmune disorders (the body attacking itself), and over- or under-production…","Extrinsic Factors — Come from outside the body: pathogens (bacteria, viruses, parasites), environmental exposures (toxins, pollutants), lifestyle/behaviors (diet, exercise, smoking, alcohol), and stressors that overwhelm the body's defenses…","The Epidemiologic Triangle (Epi-Triad)","Pathogen (agent): the causative factor responsible for disease — a bacteria, virus, or parasite.","Host: typically the human — susceptibility depends on genetics, age, and immune status.","Environment: the external conditions that influence how the pathogen and host interact (e.g., some bacteria can't survive heat, which is why certain foods are cooked to specific temperatures).","Disease risk emerges when all three components come together."]},{"s":"Identifying & Describing Disease▾","a":"identifying-describing-disease","k":["Identifying & Describing Disease▾","Signs — Objective data — what can be seen, heard, or touched (e.g., wheezes on auscultation, a strong or weak pulse).","Symptoms — Subjective data — what the patient reports (e.g., pain, nausea, dizziness).","Occurrence — How often and when signs/symptoms happen (e.g., indigestion that occurs only after meals).","Diagnosis — Putting a name to the disease (e.g., pneumonia, heart failure, hypertension).","Etiology — The cause of the disease — not always known.","Prognosis — The expected disease course or outcome — the likelihood of returning to baseline.","Working up a symptom means asking detailed, specific questions. For a cough: is it productive (and if so, what does the sputum look like)? When does it happen — morning, after eating, with a choking/aspiration risk? Is it acute or chronic? …"]},{"s":"Disease Onset & Course▾","a":"disease-onset-course","k":["Disease Onset & Course▾","Exposure — Contact with the agent or pathogen that sets off the disease cascade — a chemical, temperature, or organism exposure.","Sudden Onset — Signs/symptoms start quickly (e.g., a myocardial infarction, an asthma attack).","Insidious Onset — Slow and gradual — patients often adjust to symptoms until the disease has progressed significantly before it's caught (e.g., a slow GI bleed).","Latent Onset — The disease process is present but inactive, and can become active later (e.g., the chickenpox virus lying dormant before reactivating as shingles).","Prodromal — Early, nonspecific signs that a disease/condition is beginning (e.g., chills or a mild headache before a cold or flu fully sets in).","Manifestations — The signs and symptoms related to the disorder.","Remission — The disease is no longer active.","Convalescence — Recovering from the disease, back toward baseline.","Idiopathic — The cause of the disease is unknown (e.g., idiopathic cardiomyopathy — thickened heart muscle with no confirmed cause).","Iatrogenic — Caused by the healthcare field itself — a treatment, drug, or action (or inaction) caused the disease or complication (e.g., a colon perforation during a colonoscopy, a hospital-acquired infection, a medication error).","Exacerbation — A worsening of disease — often an acute decline in a chronic condition (e.g., an asthma attack triggered by allergen exposure in a patient with chronic asthma)."]},{"s":"Prefixes & Suffixes Worth Knowing▾","a":"prefixes-suffixes-worth-knowing","k":["Prefixes & Suffixes Worth Knowing▾","Hypo- — Under, below, low (e.g., hypotensive = low blood pressure).","Hyper- — Above, over, high (e.g., hypertensive = high blood pressure).","-Penia — A lack of or deficiency (e.g., leukopenia = low white blood cell count).","-Cytosis — An increase, usually referring to cells (e.g., leukocytosis = excess white blood cells, commonly seen with infection).","-Osis — A process or condition — production, increase, invasion, or infection, generally (e.g., osmosis = movement of fluid through a cell membrane).","-Itis — Inflammation (e.g., hepatitis = inflammation of the liver).","-Pathy — Disease or suffering (e.g., myopathy = a disease of the muscles).","Opposites worth pairing up: hypo- (low) vs. hyper- (high); -penia (too few) vs. -cytosis (too many). Same root, opposite direction — a common exam trap when the terms are swapped."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-module1-foundations.html","t":"Module 1 — Foundations","secs":[{"s":"Module 1 — Foundations","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","What Pathophysiology Is","Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors","Identifying, Describing & Staging Disease","Cellular Adaptation — Atrophy to Neoplasia","Necrosis & Gangrene","Inflammation Basics","Adaptive Immunity — B Cells, T Cells & Antibodies","Infection Basics — Microorganisms, Transmission & Portals of Entry","Stages of Infection & the Infectious Process","Diagnosing Infection & Healthcare-Associated Infections","Intro to Pharmacology — Definitions, Naming & Regulation","Pharmacokinetics — Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism & Excretion","Pharmacokinetics — Metabolism, Excretion & Half-Life","Pharmacodynamics & Drug Safety","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: pathophysiology basics, cellular adaptation, inflammation & immunity, infection, and intro pharmacology/pharmacokinetics/pharmacodynamics."]},{"s":"What Pathophysiology Is▾","a":"what-pathophysiology-is","k":["What Pathophysiology Is▾","Breaking Down the Word","Physiology is normal functioning — how the body works when there aren't any problems, at the molecular, cellular, and whole-organ-system level.","Pathology is the study of disease and injury — what abnormal processes are happening to disrupt the body from normal functioning.","Pathophysiology combines the two: the mechanisms of disease — how and why changes in body structure and function happen, and how those changes produce the signs and symptoms of a given disease.","Disease is a disruption in homeostasis — \"homeo\" (same) + \"stasis\" (balance/stability). This disruption can be physical (e.g., heart failure — the heart can't pump adequately, so the body doesn't get the blood/oxygen it needs), mental (e.g.…","Temperature Rises — Blood vessels dilate (flushed/red skin) and sweat glands secrete fluid — both mechanisms lose heat to the environment, cooling the body back toward normal.","Temperature Drops — Blood vessels constrict (pale skin) and sweat glands stop producing fluid, conserving heat. Shivering — involuntary muscle contraction — generates additional heat."]},{"s":"Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors▾","a":"causes-of-disease-intrinsic-extrinsic-factors","k":["Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors▾","Intrinsic Factors — Come from inside the body: genetics/genetic mutations, immune status (immunocompromised patients are more susceptible to disease), age, sex, autoimmune disorders (the body attacking itself), and over- or under-production…","Extrinsic Factors — Come from outside the body: pathogens (bacteria, viruses, parasites), environmental exposures (toxins, pollutants), lifestyle/behaviors (diet, exercise, smoking, alcohol), and stressors that overwhelm the body's defenses…","The Epidemiologic Triangle (Epi-Triad)","Pathogen (agent): the causative factor responsible for disease — a bacteria, virus, or parasite.","Host: typically the human — susceptibility depends on genetics, age, and immune status.","Environment: the external conditions that influence how the pathogen and host interact (e.g., some bacteria can't survive heat, which is why certain foods are cooked to specific temperatures).","Disease risk emerges when all three components come together."]},{"s":"Identifying, Describing & Staging Disease▾","a":"identifying-describing-staging-disease","k":["Identifying, Describing & Staging Disease▾","Signs — Objective data — what can be seen, heard, or touched (e.g., wheezes on auscultation, a strong or weak pulse).","Symptoms — Subjective data — what the patient reports (e.g., pain, nausea, dizziness).","Diagnosis — Putting a name to the disease (e.g., pneumonia, heart failure, hypertension).","Etiology — The cause of the disease — not always known.","Prognosis — The expected disease course or outcome — the likelihood of returning to baseline.","Exposure — Contact with the agent or pathogen that sets off the disease cascade.","Sudden Onset — Signs/symptoms start quickly (e.g., a myocardial infarction, an asthma attack).","Insidious Onset — Slow and gradual — patients often adjust to symptoms until the disease has progressed significantly before it's caught (e.g., a slow GI bleed).","Latent Onset — The disease process is present but inactive, and can become active later (e.g., the chickenpox virus lying dormant before reactivating as shingles).","Prodromal — Early, nonspecific signs that a disease/condition is beginning (e.g., chills or a mild headache before a cold or flu fully sets in).","Remission / Convalescence — Remission: the disease is no longer active. Convalescence: recovering from the disease, back toward baseline.","Idiopathic — The cause of the disease is unknown (e.g., idiopathic cardiomyopathy).","Iatrogenic — Caused by the healthcare field itself — a treatment, drug, or action (or inaction) caused the disease (e.g., a colon perforation during a colonoscopy).","Exacerbation — A worsening of disease — often an acute decline in a chronic condition (e.g., an asthma attack triggered by allergen exposure).","Prefixes/suffixes worth knowing: hypo- (low) vs. hyper- (high); -penia (too few) vs. -cytosis (too many); -osis (a process/condition); -itis (inflammation, e.g. hepatitis); -pathy (disease, e.g. myopathy). Same root, opposite direction is a…"]},{"s":"Cellular Adaptation — Atrophy to Neoplasia▾","a":"cellular-adaptation-atrophy-to-neoplasia","k":["Cellular Adaptation — Atrophy to Neoplasia▾","Cells adapt to survive stress or injury. Some adaptations are normal/physiologic (muscle growth from exercise, uterine growth in pregnancy); others are pathologic, usually triggered by things like radiation, medications, or lack of oxygen.","Atrophy — Cells shrink in size; the whole organ can become atrophic. Physiologic atrophy is a developmental problem at birth (an organ/gland that never develops properly). Pathologic atrophy comes from decreased workload, poor blood supply,…","Hypertrophy — Cells get bigger (not more numerous), usually increasing function too. Typically from mechanical stimuli — repetitive stretching, chronic pressure, prolonged volume overload (e.g., quad muscle growth from repeated exercise is …","Hyperplasia — An increase in the number of cells (cell size stays the same) — only cells capable of dividing can do this (commonly skin, intestinal epithelium, glandular cells). Usually pathologic — most often cancers or abnormal hormonal s…","Dysplasia — Abnormal changes in the size, shape, or organization of mature cells — often follows atypical hyperplasia. Classified mild/moderate/severe, and is reversible (e.g., tobacco-related mouth/mucosal cell dysplasia can normalize afte…","Metaplasia — A reversible replacement of one mature cell type with another, less-differentiated type — related to chronic irritation/inflammation, and more strongly associated with cancer risk than dysplasia alone. It's an adaptation that h…","Neoplasia / Anaplasia — Cellular changes that no longer respond to normal regulatory processes — usually from a gene mutation. Anaplastic cells are cancer cells that have differentiated back to immature/embryonic forms; seeing \"anaplasia\" s…","Benign Tumors","Differentiated cells, less anaplastic (more mature).","Reproduce faster than normal cells, but cannot metastasize — stay contained and grow slowly.","Usually encapsulated.","Mainly a problem if located somewhere sensitive to compression, like the brain.","Malignant Tumors","Very anaplastic, non-differentiated, immature cells.","Reproduce rapidly with abnormal cells.","Metastasize — spread to other sites, near or distant, depending on proximity to lymphatic/blood systems.","Typically not encapsulated."]},{"s":"Necrosis & Gangrene▾","a":"necrosis-gangrene","k":["Necrosis & Gangrene▾","Necrosis — Cell death from disease, injury, or failure of blood supply — irreversible, with inflammation, swelling, redness, and cell bursting in the area.","Ischemic Necrosis — Necrosis from infarction and prolonged ischemia — an absence of blood supply to the area.","Liquefactive Necrosis — Dead cells/tissue are digested by enzymes and become liquefied, often forming an abscess with a liquid center. Typical in organs with a lot of lipids (lungs, brain) or where there's an abscess with heavy acute inflam…","Necrotic tissue becomes a breeding ground for bacteria — when bacterial invasion follows disruption of a major blood supply, that's gangrene. Most common in the lower extremities (toes, feet) of patients with poor circulation, such as those…","Dry Gangrene — Minimal bacteria, minimal inflammatory response. Tissue turns black, dry, and wrinkled. Spreads slowly (weeks to months); a clear demarcation line separates dead from healthy tissue.","Wet Gangrene — Liquefactive necrosis with extensive bacterial/WBC damage producing a liquid wound. Skin is moist, dark, cool, pulseless, and swollen, with blisters and a foul odor. No demarcation line; spreads much faster than dry gangrene …","Gas Gangrene — A specialized, severe form of wet gangrene caused by Clostridium perfringens (an anaerobic, spore-forming organism found in soil), typically from severe trauma or a compound fracture. Destroys connective tissue and cellular m…"]},{"s":"Inflammation Basics▾","a":"inflammation-basics","k":["Inflammation Basics▾","What Inflammation Is","Occurs with cell injury, regardless of cause — initially a protective mechanism that begins healing, working to destroy invading/harmful agents, limit their spread, and prepare damaged tissue for repair.","The five cardinal signs: redness, swelling, heat, pain, and loss of function.","Exogenous causes come from outside (surgery, trauma, burns); endogenous causes come from inside (e.g., tissue ischemia).","Acute inflammation lasts less than 2 weeks and is helpful. Chronic inflammation extends much longer, can form scar tissue, and is no longer a helpful process.","Mechanism: tissue injury releases chemical mediators that cause vasodilation and increased vascular permeability, letting white blood cells (mainly neutrophils and monocytes) move out of the blood vessel and into the injured tissue — a recr…","Serous — Mild inflammation — watery, low protein (e.g., clear fluid leaking from a healing surgical incision).","Serosanguineous — Similar to serous but pink-tinged, with small amounts of RBCs — normal immediately post-op.","Purulent — Severe inflammation, typically bacterial infection — thick, sticky, high in neutrophils/protein/debris, often discolored and foul-smelling; may need drainage (an abscess).","Hemorrhagic — Large amounts of RBCs — the most severe type, from badly damaged/necrotic blood vessels. Signals active bleeding, not just a wound.","If inflammation isn't contained, it can become systemic — driven by cytokines (interleukin-1, interleukin-6, TNF-alpha). Systemic inflammation presents as fever, an elevated white blood cell count, fatigue, and muscle catabolism (breakdown)…"]},{"s":"Adaptive Immunity — B Cells, T Cells & Antibodies▾","a":"adaptive-immunity-b-cells-t-cells-antibodies","k":["Adaptive Immunity — B Cells, T Cells & Antibodies▾","Self vs. Non-Self","The major histocompatibility complex (MHC), also called the human leukocyte antigen (HLA) in humans, is a gene cluster on chromosome 6 that makes \"self\" proteins displayed on nearly every cell surface.","Specific adaptive immunity recognizes foreign invaders, destroys them, and remembers the encounter for a faster response next time.","B cells and T cells are lymphocytes responsible for this memory immunity. B cells provide humoral immunity (antibodies in body fluids); T cells provide cell-mediated immunity (recognizing antigens on cell surfaces).","An antigen is essentially a \"name tag\" on a foreign/bad cell (cancer, bacteria, fungal, inflammatory) that marks it for attack.","Memory B cells remember a prior antigen exposure and respond rapidly on re-exposure. Plasma cells are short-lived B cells that secrete antibodies against the triggering antigen.","IgG — Most prevalent (~75–80% of all antibodies). Easily crosses into interstitial fluid; produced later in an infection (or after a vaccine) — protects against bacterial and viral infection.","IgM — ~10% of antibodies. First produced after exposure/immunization, activating complement — a signal that something is newly/acutely happening.","IgA — Found in saliva, tears, secretions, breast milk, GI, and GU tract — protects at secretory/excretory sites.","IgD — Trace amounts in serum and on B cell surfaces — stimulates B cells to multiply, differentiate, and secrete other immunoglobulins.","IgE — Plays a major role in immunity against parasites and in allergic reactions (works with mast cells).","Passive Immunity — Transfer of antibody-containing plasma from a protected person to one who isn't — e.g., mother-to-fetus (IgG crosses the placenta; IgA is passed via breast milk), or injection of antibodies/immune globulins (not a vaccine…","Active Immunity — The result of an active infection or a vaccine — the body creates its own antibodies (usually via memory B cells), typically much faster on a second exposure, which is how vaccines prevent illness.","Traditional — An inactivated or killed part of the virus/bacteria/organism.","Attenuated — A weakened, still-live version — should not be given to immunocompromised patients or those with conditions putting them at risk for complications.","Toxoid — An inactivated toxin that stimulates antitoxin production (e.g., tetanus vaccine).","Conjugate — A protein/toxoid from one organism attached to a disease-causing organism to stimulate a response (e.g., Hib).","mRNA — Uses a snippet of the virus's genetic code to teach the body's antibodies to respond."]},{"s":"Infection Basics — Microorganisms, Transmission & Portals of Entry▾","a":"infection-basics-microorganisms-transmission-portals-of-entry","k":["Infection Basics — Microorganisms, Transmission & Portals of Entry▾","Virus — DNA or RNA with a protein shell; requires a host cell to replicate. Responsible for COVID-19, influenza, chickenpox, and more.","Bacteria — Larger than a virus, at least single-celled, usually with one strand of DNA; can reproduce inside or outside cells and suppress the body's defenses (e.g., strep throat, TB, UTIs).","Fungal — Spore-forming organisms (e.g., yeast infections).","Protozoa — Typically live in water environments (e.g., malaria).","Helminths — Parasitic worms (roundworms, hookworms).","Prions — Misfolded proteins that attach to other proteins, even smaller than viruses — cause devastating diseases like Creutzfeldt-Jakob (mad cow) disease.","Direct Contact — Kissing, sex, contact with contaminated soil/vegetation, or droplet spread (short-range aerosols from sneezing/coughing, e.g., pertussis, meningitis).","Indirect — Airborne — Droplet nuclei stay suspended in air (e.g., measles).","Indirect — Vehicle-Borne — Infectious material via food, water, or blood (e.g., hepatitis A from contaminated food).","Indirect — Vector-Borne — Carried by another organism — mosquitoes, fleas, ticks (e.g., malaria, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, Lyme disease).","Portals of Entry","Respiratory (airways/lungs) and GI (via eating/the stomach).","Genitourinary tract (e.g., STDs, catheters).","Skin — normally a strong barrier (tight cell junctions), but skin integrity issues are a major vulnerability. Translocation is the movement of bacteria across the intestinal lining (most often the peritoneal cavity) when the gut becomes \"le…","Blood — transfusion contamination or needle sticks.","Maternal-fetal transmission — some organisms (e.g., Zika, Listeria) cross the placental barrier or transmit during childbirth."]},{"s":"Stages of Infection & the Infectious Process▾","a":"stages-of-infection-the-infectious-process","k":["Stages of Infection & the Infectious Process▾","Incubation — Time from pathogen entry to first symptoms — no symptoms yet, just growth/multiplication.","Prodromal — Onset of nonspecific symptoms (mild fatigue, low fever) — not yet fully symptomatic.","Acute Stage — Full-blown, specific signs/symptoms of the illness.","Convalescence — Symptoms improving, illness disappearing.","Resolution — The pathogen is completely eliminated. Some pathogens never reach this stage (e.g., chickenpox virus staying dormant for life).","The 6-Stage Infectious/Inflammatory Process","1. Injury — the initial insult to the area.","2. Brief vasoconstriction — stops bleeding, limits organism movement.","3. Vasodilation — blood flows freely into the area, bringing the five cardinal signs of inflammation.","4. Increased permeability — fluid leaks from vessels into tissue.","5. Emigration of leukocytes & phagocytosis — neutrophils (plus eosinophils, NK cells, monocytes) migrate in and engulf invaders, producing exudate.","6. Systemic symptoms (if the process doesn't stay localized) — a total-body response driven by the hypothalamic fever set point, which is ultimately protective (helps conserve heat, boosts immune cell/antibody function, and slows some bacte…"]},{"s":"Diagnosing Infection & Healthcare-Associated Infections▾","a":"diagnosing-infection-healthcare-associated-infections","k":["Diagnosing Infection & Healthcare-Associated Infections▾","Colonization — A pathogen inhabits a body site without causing signs/symptoms of infection (e.g., asymptomatic staph on the skin). Can turn into an active infection with immune compromise or tissue disruption.","Gram Stain — Identifies bacteria as gram-positive or gram-negative and their shape/arrangement. Gram-negative bacteria are more dangerous — an outer membrane/capsule hides their antigens, making them harder for the body to fight.","Sensitivity — Takes 24–72 hours; identifies the exact bacteria and which antibiotics it's sensitive/resistant to.","Urinalysis — Normal Values — pH 5–9; nitrates negative (positive suggests bacterial infection); leukocyte esterase negative (positive suggests infection); RBCs less than 5 (more suggests infection); epithelial cells suggest a contaminated s…","Nosocomial (Healthcare-Associated) Infections","Acquired in the hospital, not present at home — typically more virulent and more likely to be drug-resistant.","MRSA — methicillin-resistant Staph aureus. CRE — carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae (resistant to an entire antibiotic class). MDRO — multi-drug-resistant organism (resistant to multiple drug types).","Superinfection: a new infection that develops during treatment for another one — broad-spectrum antibiotics kill off normal flora that normally keep opportunistic pathogens in check.","C. diff (Clostridium difficile) — Normal intestinal flora killed by antibiotics lets C. diff overgrow — causes frequent, watery (often orange-tinged) diarrhea and abdominal cramping, sometimes months after treatment. Diagnosed by stool PCR …","Candidiasis — A yeast/fungal overgrowth after antibiotics kill normal skin/mucosal flora — thrush (oral), or infections in moist skin folds (under the breast, etc.). Untreated oral thrush can descend into the esophagus. Prevented/treated wi…"]},{"s":"Intro to Pharmacology — Definitions, Naming & Regulation▾","a":"intro-to-pharmacology-definitions-naming-regulation","k":["Intro to Pharmacology — Definitions, Naming & Regulation▾","Pharmacology — The study of drugs/chemicals/medications and the changes they cause in the body.","Pharmacokinetics — What the body does to the drug (absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion).","Pharmacodynamics — What the drug does to the body — its mechanism of action and overall effects.","Chemical / Generic / Trade Name — Chemical name: long, complex, used in research (e.g., acetylparaminophenol). Generic name: the one official name, lowercase, used on exams and NCLEX (e.g., acetaminophen). Trade name: the capitalized brand …","Prototype Drug — The first drug in a class, used as the comparison point for effectiveness/side effects of newer drugs in that class (e.g., ibuprofen/Advil is the NSAID prototype).","Therapeutic Effect — The intended effect of the drug (e.g., lowering blood pressure).","Side Effect — An unintended effect of a normal dose.","Toxicity / Adverse Effect — Toxicity: a harmful effect. Adverse effect: an unexpected, dangerous reaction. (Used interchangeably with \"side effect\" at this course level.)","Allergic Reaction — An unexpected, potentially dangerous immune system response — ranges from itching/hives to anaphylaxis.","How New Drugs Are Approved (FDA)","Preclinical: tested on lab animals for therapeutic/adverse effects.","Phase 1: healthy human volunteers — checking for major adverse reactions.","Phase 2: patients with the target disease — identifying rarer/less frequent side effects; the drug may be pulled here if effects are too dangerous.","Phase 3: a vast clinical market — unexpected responses may still lead to withdrawal.","Phase 4: post-market, ongoing FDA monitoring. If a pattern of severe reactions emerges, the FDA can require a black box warning — the strictest FDA warning a drug can carry while staying on the market.","Schedule I — No accepted medical use, very high abuse potential (e.g., heroin, LSD, ecstasy).","Schedule II — High abuse potential but some medical use; severe psychological/physical dependence risk (e.g., opioids, amphetamines). No automatic refills — each fill needs a new prescription.","Schedule III — Moderate-to-low potential for dependence (e.g., ketamine, anabolic steroids).","Schedule IV — Low potential for abuse/dependence (e.g., Xanax, Valium, Ambien).","Schedule V — Very low abuse potential — small amounts of narcotics/stimulants (e.g., cough syrup with a small amount of codeine).","To be sold over the counter (OTC), a drug must be safe (low risk of side effects/abuse) and the consumer must be able to diagnose the condition and monitor effectiveness themselves. Some OTC drugs (e.g., pseudoephedrine — a meth precursor) …","Dietary/Herbal Supplements, Teratogens & Pharmacogenomics","Dietary/herbal supplements can only claim an effect on body structure or function (not that they treat a medical condition) and aren't FDA-evaluated for safety/efficacy before marketing. They can still interact with medications — e.g., gink…","Teratogens (e.g., alcohol, marijuana, nicotine) cause congenital malformations. The FDA's old pregnancy risk categories (A = safest, through X = contraindicated, known fetal risk) have been replaced since 2015 by the Pregnancy and Lactation…","Pharmacogenomics — studying how a person's genes affect their drug response, to tailor medication choice/dosing (already used in some cancer and psychiatric drug selection)."]},{"s":"Pharmacokinetics — Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism & Excretion▾","a":"pharmacokinetics-absorption-distribution-metabolism-excretion","k":["Pharmacokinetics — Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism & Excretion▾","Enteral — Via the GI tract — oral, sublingual, buccal, rectal.","Parenteral — Outside the GI tract — typically injection (intradermal, subcutaneous, intramuscular, intrathecal, intra-articular, intravenous).","Topical — Applied to body surfaces — ointments, creams, pastes, powders, transdermal patches.","Three phases of drug activity: pharmaceutical (the drug dissolves — disintegration breaks a tablet into particles, dissolution dissolves those particles into GI fluid so they can be absorbed), pharmacokinetic (absorption → distribution → me…","Bioavailability — The percentage of an administered drug that reaches systemic circulation in active form. IV drugs: 100% (no absorption barrier). Oral/GI drugs: less than 100%, due to the first-pass effect.","First-Pass Effect — An orally absorbed drug must pass through the liver before reaching systemic circulation, and the liver metabolizes a portion of it to an inactive form — reducing bioavailability. This is why oral doses (e.g., oral morph…","Distribution — Movement of the drug from blood to its site of action — best in highly vascularized areas (heart, liver, kidneys, brain); slower in muscle, skin, and fat, and impaired by conditions like peripheral vascular disease or a poorl…","Blood-Brain Barrier — Tight capillary junctions in the brain that block most drugs — only lipid-soluble drugs or ones with a transport system can cross (e.g., alcohol, glucose). Not fully developed in infants, so extra caution is needed wit…","Protein Binding — Drugs travel partly bound to plasma proteins (e.g., albumin) and partly free/unbound — only the unbound drug is active. Low albumin (malnutrition, liver disease, alcoholism) means more free drug and a higher risk of toxici…"]},{"s":"Pharmacokinetics — Metabolism, Excretion & Half-Life▾","a":"pharmacokinetics-metabolism-excretion-half-life","k":["Pharmacokinetics — Metabolism, Excretion & Half-Life▾","Metabolism (Biotransformation) — Chemically alters the drug into a metabolite — from active/lipid-soluble to inactive/water-soluble, ready for excretion. The liver is the main organ responsible.","CYP450 System — The liver's main drug-metabolizing enzyme system. A drug is a substrate if it's metabolized by a CYP450 enzyme. A CYP450 inducer speeds up metabolism of substrate drugs (less drug in the body, lower therapeutic effect). A CY…","Excretion — Elimination of the drug (mostly via the kidneys, through glomerular filtration, tubular secretion, and reabsorption). Kidney function directly affects how well a drug is excreted.","Half-Life (T½) — The time it takes for serum drug concentration to fall by 50%. After about 5 half-lives, ~97% of the drug is eliminated. It also takes about 4–5 half-lives to reach steady state — where the amount of drug eliminated equals …","Half-life guides dosing intervals. With a drug like morphine (half-life ~2–4 hours), levels drop meaningfully between doses — around-the-clock dosing (a scheduled baseline dose plus PRN doses for breakthrough symptoms) is used, especially f…"]},{"s":"Pharmacodynamics & Drug Safety▾","a":"pharmacodynamics-drug-safety","k":["Pharmacodynamics & Drug Safety▾","Receptor — A reactive site on/inside a cell (usually a protein) that a drug binds to, forming a drug-receptor complex that stimulates or inhibits a response.","Agonist — Binds a receptor and initiates the desired therapeutic effect (e.g., a beta-1 adrenergic agonist causing vasodilation).","Antagonist — Blocks/inhibits a natural substance from binding and acting (e.g., diphenhydramine/Benadryl blocking histamine at the H1 receptor).","Receptor-less Activation — Some drugs act through direct chemical interaction rather than a receptor (e.g., antacids neutralizing stomach acid, or magnesium sulfate acting as a laxative via an osmotic effect).","Therapeutic Index — The ratio of a drug's toxic level to its therapeutic level. A narrow therapeutic index (e.g., warfarin, digoxin) means a small margin between helpful and toxic — needs closer monitoring. A wide/high index (e.g., amoxicil…","Drug Interactions","Additive effect: two drugs with a similar mechanism of action are given together on purpose, for a stronger combined effect (e.g., two antibiotics for a complicated infection).","Synergism/potentiation: two drugs with different mechanisms combine for an effect greater than either alone (e.g., Coumadin plus aspirin).","Antidote: a drug given to antagonize another drug's toxic effects (e.g., naloxone/Narcan for opioid overdose).","Interactions can also decrease therapeutic effects — CYP450 inducers speeding up elimination, impaired GI absorption (e.g., after intestinal surgery), or activated charcoal intentionally blocking absorption.","Polypharmacy raises drug interaction risk — a special concern in older adults, who also have slower hepatic metabolism, reduced circulation/distribution, decreased GI absorption, and reduced renal excretion, raising risk for adverse reactio…","Medication safety practices: tall-man lettering for look-alike names (e.g., \"heparin\"), barcode scanning and automated dispensing (e.g., Pyxis), no trailing zeros (write \"1 mg,\" not \"1.0 mg\") and always a leading zero (\"0.25 mg,\" not \".25 m…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Five Clusters, Not Eleven Separate Topics","The 11 Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new — it's a map of the other 11 Pathopharm Review pages, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. A handful of big ideas (a few key hormones, immune…"]},{"s":"Five Clusters, Not Eleven Separate Topics▾","a":"five-clusters-not-eleven-separate-topics","k":["Five Clusters, Not Eleven Separate Topics▾","Eleven pages is a lot to hold in your head at once. Most of them naturally fall into one of five groups — knowing the group can matter more than memorizing all eleven titles.","Foundations — Pharmacology Foundations; Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity — The vocabulary and basic mechanisms (PK/PD, cellular injury, inflammation, immunity) that every other topic quietly assumes you already know.","Cardiometabolic & Stress Hormones — HTN/HLD; Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome; Stress & Steroids — All three run on the same hormonal wiring — the RAS system, cortisol, and catecholamines regulate blood pressure, blood glucose, and the stress re…","Blood & Fluid Balance — Coagulation/DVT; Blood Cells/CBC Interpretation; Fluid/Electrolytes — The lab-value-and-safety-rule-heavy cluster — clotting factors, blood cell counts, and electrolyte levels, with a lot of \"check this number before…","Neuro & Psych Pharmacology — Pain/Seizures; Dementia, Depression & SUD — Both live in the CNS — shared neurotransmitters (norepinephrine, serotonin, GABA), shared drug-interaction dangers (CNS depressant combinations), and both loaded with …","Multisystem — GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity — The broadest single page on the site — it pulls in immune-system basics from Foundations and steroid pharmacology from Cardiometabolic, on top of its own skin/GI/GU content.","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, working through the clusters in this order touches every recurring theme once before moving to the next, instead of jumping between unrelated systems."]},{"s":"The 11 Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-11-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The 11 Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Pharmacology Foundations — The core vocabulary: PK/PD, drug naming, FDA approval, controlled substances, routes, and how drugs move through (and interact within) the body.","2 — Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity — What disease actually is at the cellular level, plus the building blocks of inflammation, immunity, and infection that every later topic assumes.","3 — HTN/HLD — Cholesterol, atherosclerosis, and blood pressure regulation (the RAS system), plus the drug classes used to manage both.","4 — Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome — Insulin, glucose regulation, type 1 vs. type 2, DKA/HHNS, and the full range of diabetes and obesity pharmacology.","5 — Coagulation/DVT — How clotting works, the anticoagulant/antiplatelet drugs that interrupt it, and DVT/VTE.","6 — Blood Cells/CBC Interpretation — Red and white blood cells, reading a CBC with differential, and the blood cancers/disorders that show up on one.","7 — Fluid/Electrolytes — Fluid compartments and the five major electrolyte imbalances, with the IV-safety rules that go with correcting them.","8 — Pain/Seizures — Pain pathophysiology and pharmacology (non-opioid through opioid), plus seizure pathophysiology and antiepileptic drugs.","9 — Dementia, Depression & SUD — Delirium vs. dementia, the mood/anxiety neurotransmitters, depression/anxiety pharmacology, and substance use disorder/withdrawal.","10 — GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity — The broadest page on the site: skin infections and antifungals, basic GI pharmacology, all four hypersensitivity types, and an intro to urologic problems.","11 — Stress & Steroids — How the body responds to stress (General Adaptation Syndrome, the HPA axis) and the corticosteroid drugs that mimic or replace that response."]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content — each idea is fully taught in exactly one place. But they resurface as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why.","Cortisol & the HPA axis — Stress & Steroids — Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome (cortisol as a counter-regulatory hormone; the dawn phenomenon), HTN/HLD (cortisol mentioned as a cholesterol-derived hormone)","Aldosterone & the RAS system — HTN/HLD — Stress & Steroids (aldosterone as part of the adrenal cortex's stress response; salt wasting without it)","Potassium regulation — Fluid/Electrolytes — HTN/HLD (potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitor hyperkalemia risk), Stress & Steroids (salt wasting/hyperkalemia without aldosterone effect)","Glucose regulation & hyperglycemia — Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome — Stress & Steroids (cortisol raises glucose; illness/steroids as a hyperglycemia trigger), Fluid/Electrolytes (D50/insulin used to treat hyperkalemia)","Immune system & inflammation basics — Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity — Blood Cells/CBC Interpretation (the white blood cells that carry it out), GI/GU/Skin/Hypersensitivity (the four hypersensitivity types), Stress & Steroids (co…","Catecholamines (norepinephrine/epinephrine) — Stress & Steroids — Dementia/Depression/SUD (norepinephrine as a mood neurotransmitter), Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome (epinephrine as a counter-regulatory hormone), GI/GU/Skin/Hypersensitivity (e…","Corticosteroid pharmacology — Stress & Steroids — GI/GU/Skin/Hypersensitivity (steroids used to treat anaphylaxis and inflammatory skin conditions)"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","Every danger callout across all 11 pages, condensed to a phrase. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation — click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","HTN/HLD — Hypertensive emergency (BP ≥180/120 + end-organ damage) needs IV meds now. Never stop beta blockers/clonidine abruptly — rebound hypertension. ACE inhibitors can cause angioedema, which can close the airway.","Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome — Hypoglycemia is a true emergency — check blood sugar immediately. Always get a current blood sugar before giving insulin, not one from earlier.","Coagulation/DVT — Push protamine slowly — too fast causes profound hypotension; watch for heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT). Phlegmasia cerulea dolens is a true emergency that can progress to amputation.","Blood Cells/CBC Interpretation — Neutropenia — treat any infection as potentially life-threatening; use neutropenic precautions.","Fluid/Electrolytes — Correct sodium imbalances slowly — too fast risks permanent neurologic damage. Never IV push undiluted potassium — can cause fatal ventricular fibrillation.","Pain/Seizures — Never give aspirin to a child with a viral illness — Reye syndrome. Acetaminophen max 4 g/24 hr (2 g if liver disease/alcohol use). Opioid respiratory rate < 10 needs immediate assessment. Status epilepticus is a true emerge…","Dementia, Depression & SUD — Serotonin syndrome risk when combining serotonergic drugs. TCA overdose can be fatal — screen suicide risk first. MAOI + tyramine-rich food → hypertensive crisis. Never combine a benzodiazepine with another CNS …","GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity — Amphotericin B is high-alert — nephrotoxic and cardiotoxic. Anaphylaxis is life-threatening. Epinephrine concentration mix-ups (1:1,000 vs. 1:10,000) can be fatal. Stop a blood transfusion immediately at th…","Stress & Steroids — Increase steroid dosing during illness or major stress (sick-day dosing). Never stop long-term corticosteroids abruptly — risk of adrenal crisis.","Patterns you'll notice across these rules","\"Never stop this abruptly\" shows up three separate times — beta blockers/clonidine (HTN/HLD), antiepileptic drugs (Pain/Seizures), and corticosteroids (Stress & Steroids). Different drugs, same underlying principle: the drug has been suppre…","\"Get a current number immediately before dosing\" appears for both blood sugar before insulin (Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome) and blood pressure/heart rate before a beta blocker (HTN/HLD) — the same instinct to verify the current state rather …","\"This can look mild at first but escalates fast\" is really the same warning behind anaphylaxis, a transfusion reaction, ACE inhibitor angioedema, hypertensive crisis, and status epilepticus — five different diseases teaching the same nursin…"]}]},{"f":"topic-template.html","t":"Topic Page Template","secs":[{"s":"Topic Page Template","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1","Glossary Table","Checklist Table","Callouts","Comparison Cards","Lecture Diagram","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Glossary Table▾","a":"glossary-table","k":["Glossary Table▾","First term — The definition, straight from the lecture. — The consequence, complication, or clinical significance.","Second term — Keep the wording close to how the instructor said it. — If the lecture didn't say it, it doesn't go here.","Third term — Three to six columns works; more than that gets cramped on a phone. — Tables stack into cards below 640px automatically."]},{"s":"Checklist Table▾","a":"checklist-table","k":["Checklist Table▾","Do the first thing — Because of this reason.","Then the second thing — Because of this other reason."]},{"s":"Callouts▾","a":"callouts","k":["Callouts▾","MNEMONIC (blue): an acronym, a memory hook, or a rule of thumb. This is the \"here's the trick for remembering it\" box.","DANGER (red): a true emergency, a priority action, or a hard safety rule. Reserve it — if everything is red, nothing is.","Cross-reference (accent): this topic is covered again here — review in Must Know if needed","A plain note. Use it for a short framing sentence under a heading, or a caveat that isn't worth a callout."]},{"s":"Comparison Cards▾","a":"comparison-cards","k":["Comparison Cards▾","Option A","First distinguishing feature","Second distinguishing feature","Third distinguishing feature","Option B","How it differs on the first point","How it differs on the second point","How it differs on the third point","Full-Width Single List","A lone card outside a .two-col fills the width.","Use this instead of a one-column table — a table with a single column wastes a lot of horizontal space."]},{"s":"Lecture Diagram▾","a":"lecture-diagram","k":["Lecture Diagram▾","Images live in images/<topic>/ and never appear inline — they open in a lightbox from a pill button. Uncomment the block below once you have a real image."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]}];
